   153	It began there, between the smith's and a chandler's, at a kerb where the cobbles of the open place gave way to setts that had been laid aslant for the grip of hooves. It went straight up. He could follow it with his eyes on the ground: two hundred yards to the blue coats, and through, and on under slate roofs, growing narrower, to a second arch where the coats were a paler colour. Beyond that he did not follow it.
   154	
   155	An old woman sat on the kerb at his elbow, with a carrying-frame beside her and her boots off.
   156	
   157	"Boards are down the street," she said. She had looked at his neck, where the strap had been. "There's nothing going up before the second bell."
   158	
   159	"I'm not after work. I was looking for where the registry is."
   160	
   161	"That's the top." She pointed with a bare heel, without troubling to get up. "Straight up. Through the blue, and through the buff, and then you're in the square. You go across it. It's the door that faces the steps." She spat, neatly. "I've carried paper to it for thirty years. You'll not get that far in those boots without a card."
   162	
   163	"Across the square."
   164	
   165	"There's no other way to a front door, is there? It's a square. It's got the one thing on one side of it and the other thing on the other, and you walk between."
   166	
   167	He thanked her. She had shut her eyes already.
   168	
   169	So that was how it lay. He stood with the toes of his boots against the kerb, and looked at the first of the slanted setts, a hand's breadth in front of them.
   170	
   171	He had written two sentences in a book, at a fire, for a healer who would not take his word. They had been easy to write. The square had been two days off, in a town that he had never seen, and a thing which is two days off costs nothing to forgo. He had not known that it would stand at the head of the only street; that the record he had walked here for would be kept behind a door on its far side; or that the two of them would face one another across forty yards of paving, like a pair of people who have been set to keep each other honest.
   172	
   173	He could break it by walking. That was all that the promise was made of. There were four hundred yards of hill, and two gates that had not seen him, and a paved place.
   174	
   175	He did not put his foot on the sett.
   176	
   177	It was not a hard thing to refrain from, today. He had no card, and a blanket hung over a window for him in a hayloft. He thought that it would be harder on the day when he had a card. He turned his back on the street, and went down past the trough, where a brown mule that was not theirs lifted her head and looked through him.
   178	
   179	---
   180	
   181	Vastin had sat down.
   182	
   183	He had done it in the lower room of the inn, which opened off the yard: a long low place with one table down the middle of it and a bench on either side, a hearth at the far end, and a hatch through which bowls came. He had sat on the bench's end with the bound leg laid along it. There was a post behind him, and a strap hung from the post, and he had marked both before he let himself down. He would be able to get up by them. He had sat twice now in two days, and he noted, with the interest that he gave to any finding, that the second time had cost him less than the first.
   184	
   185	He had come in by a yard.
   186	
   187	He turned that over. In forty years he had entered some hundreds of houses on the seat's business, and he could not call to mind one in which he had seen the back of the kitchen door. He saw this one now. It had been mended with a board of a different wood, and somebody had cut a tally into the frame with a knife, in fives.
   188	
   189	Every district that he had ever inspected had been told that he was coming.
   190	
   191	He had known it, of course. An officer of his grade did not arrive; he was announced, by a form, four days before, and the form went to the very people whom he came to look at. So he had ridden for forty years through streets that had been swept the day before, past whitewashed kerbs, to offices where every book lay squared on its desk and a man stood at the sill with his cap in his hand. He had allowed for it, as a careful officer allows for the known error of a scale, and had supposed that he could subtract the whitewash and arrive at the street.
   192	
   193	He looked through the open door, and across the yard, and out under the carriage-arch, where a strip of the street went by in the sun.
   194	
   195	It was filthy. It was loud, and exact, and nobody in it had been told anything. A boy went past with a yoke of slops. Two porters were quarrelling over a card, with their foreheads almost touching. A woman had been turned away from the painted door ten minutes since; he had heard the whole of it, *that side's for names, mistress, you want the arch*, and had heard her come in by the arch, grumbling, and pay.
   196	
   197	It was the first honest district that he had ever been in.
   198	
   199	He had certified this town on four occasions. He had put his name to the soundness of its station, and the order of its gates, and the keeping of its books. He had ridden over a bridge without knowing that it had piers. It had taken a stick, and a brown coat, and a place in the count of a keeper's thumb, to show him what any carter saw from his seat.
   200	
   201	There was a keeper at the hearth.
   202	
   203	He was off his watch, by the unbuttoned collar; but he had kept the blue coat on, and he sat in the one chair that had a back. A bowl had come to him through the hatch before he asked for it. He had not paid. The carters along the bench had moved up for him as water moves for a boat, without looking round, and the bench had closed again behind him.
   204	
   205	Vastin's right hand left his knee.
   206	
   207	*Irregular. Subsistence taken in a licensed house by an officer of the gate, without payment; to be drawn to the notice of—*
   208	
   209	It was half way to his breast before he saw it go. He looked at it. It was an old man's hand, with a strip of linen round the palm, and it had been about to write a district's keeper into a report for a bowl of barley.
   210	
   211	A small grey man had told him at a spring what that hand was. He had not much cared to be told, and it had been true. He laid the hand on his knee again, palm down, and he looked at the room and did nothing to it.
   212	
   213	A keeper was eating, and the bench had made room. That was all that was in front of him.
   214	
   215	There was a paper under his hand, where it had stopped, and it was not the only one. He carried, in oilskin, three sheets under one seal, and they were what he had told a loft that he would not show; and beside them lay a receipt in a small upright hand, unsealed, which said that certain things had been read in part and understood in no part. He had read that sentence a good many times on the road, and he thought it the more exact document of the two.
   216	
   217	And he carried one thing that was not paper.
   218	
   219	A boy had given it to him on a bank, at dusk, across a yard of turf. He had it by heart, as he had always had a finding: there was a place, which a healer had found; it had been read twice in a day; nothing had come back. He did not know what had read it. A man of his training could set out the possibilities, in their order, and on the road that morning he had begun to; and he had reached the second, and seen that it was a draft, and stopped.
   220	
   221	He was not accustomed to holding a thing that he had not been given the whole of. He found that he could do it. It was like the leg.
   222	
   223	Out under the arch, a clerk in an apron had come to the bench across the way. Four people got up. A card went into a cap, and the four went off up the hill with frames on their backs, and not one of them had been asked so much as a name.
   224	
   225	He watched them out of sight. The lowest thing in that district went through its walls on the least ink. He set that beside the keeper's bowl, as a fact about a mechanism, and could not have said why he had troubled to keep it.
   226	
   227	A bowl came down on the table in front of him.
   228	
   229	It was the large young man who had set it there, and he had six others on a board. Vastin felt for a copper. He was told, as he had been told on four evenings running, to put it away.
   230	
   231	"I had not known," he said, "that a man could be fed for four days and still be owed nothing."
   232	
   233	"You've not been fed. You've been counted." Brom set the seventh bowl at the table's end, by itself. "It's different. Eat it while it's hot."
   234	
   235	---
   236	
   237	Seln came up the ladder after dark, with dust to the knees.
   238	
   239	Brom had begged a lantern of the woman at the half-door, and it hung from a rafter in the middle of the loft, lit at the kitchen fire with a spill. The six sat or lay in the round of it. Nobody had gone to sleep.
   240	
   241	"I did not go above the trough," said Seln. He sat down on the ladder's head with his feet on the second rung, which was as near as he came to resting. "I hired a frame for a copper, and I sat for three hours on the porters' bench at the top of the street, and was twice offered work. The man beside me has carried to the registry's yard for eleven years, and he likes to be listened to. I have four things from him, and one difficulty. I shall give them in his order."
   242	
   243	Karis had her pencil. She did not write; she held it.
   244	
   245	"The first is the place. The old town stands upon a hole: before there was a wall on that bluff, somebody had quarried the crown of it from the inside, by galleries, and had gone. The town that came afterward kept its records down there, since it was dry; and when the Compact took the town, it built its registry across the quarry's mouth, and has never troubled to bring the old records up. Its people call that the lower archive, and they do not go into it."
   246	
   247	"What stone?" said Karis. "Did he say what stone the galleries are cut in?"
   248	
   249	"He carries to a yard; he has not been below the door, and he knows nobody who has." He inclined his head to her, by a very little. "There is one door, in the undercroft beneath the yard, and it is of iron. He says that the registry's wall is built round its frame, and he meant it as a complaint against the masons."
   250	
   251	Her breath went in, and she said nothing more.
   252	
   253	"The second is the leave. I have seen that form, in another house: it is the oldest provision in the registry's book, and the shortest. A practitioner who is registered under historical studies has standing entry to a lower archive, by the week, renewed at the yard door, and he may have matter brought down to him for study. For the practitioner, that is the whole of it."
   254	
   255	"And there's one," said Cael.
   256	
   257	"There is one. He has gone down by that door in every week for longer than my porter has carried. The porter has never spoken to him."
   258	
   259	"Which Path?" said Karis.
   260	
   261	"Rune Path. Bronze."
   262	
   263	The word was strange to Cael. He had stood on floors against a good many Paths, and had the pieces of ten of them in him, and he had never met that one on any list of bouts; it was not a thing that a person fought with, then. He lay and turned it over, and it told him nothing.
   264	
   265	"He comes up at noon to eat," said Seln, "at the cook-house which is built against the wall, beside the blue gate; he stands at its counter, and goes down again within the hour. He has done so on every day that my porter can remember, and he is said to dislike being spoken to."
   266	
   267	"The third," said Brom, who had three fingers ready.
   268	
   269	"The third is the clerk. One man keeps the provision's book, at the yard door, and has kept it for many years, and in all that time there has been a single name in it. My porter does not know what he is called, and I did not ask."
   270	
   271	"And the fourth is how we get in," said Lira.
   272	
   273	"The fourth is what the form asks, when matter is brought." He drew his hands into his sleeves. "It was drawn a long while ago, when a scholar's bundles came with whoever happened to be carrying them, and nobody thought such people worth a line apiece. So it has a line for the matter, which is described. It has a line for the matter's bearer, who is one. And it has a line for hands."
   274	
   275	[[TIER-B: "Four hands." / "I've two." "Entered by count. That's the door." — coordinator to supply protected text]]
   276	
   277	Nobody spoke at once. Below them a horse blew through its nose, and stamped.
   278	
   279	"So it wants a figure," said Brom slowly. "For the ones that carry. It doesn't want to know who."
   280	
   281	"It doesn't even want to know what," said Lira. She had sat up, with her arms round her knees. "That's the whole trick of this town, and it's taken me a day to see it. If you walk in on your own paper they write you down, because you're somebody. If you walk in under a load you're a stroke of a pen. There's nothing to write." She looked across the lantern. "He's spent his whole life being a name with a word after it. And the only way they'll let him into the one place that might know what the word means is as a number."
   282	
   283	Cael had got there a breath before her, and it did not feel any better for being said aloud.
   284	
   285	He had been filed in every registry that had ever had him in reach. He had learned to read upside down so that he might know what was in the file. Now he lay in a hayloft under the oldest registry on the Line, and learned that it had one lawful door which a person of no tier could go through; and the door did not ask for his name. It did not ask for anything that he had spent his life refusing to give. It wanted to know how many of him there were.
   286	
   287	---
   288	
   289	The lantern had burned down a finger's breadth, and Brom had carried seven bowls back across the yard and come up again, before anybody asked for the rest.
   290	
   291	"There was a difficulty," said Karis.
   292	
   293	"It is the exposure. I cannot shorten it." Seln spoke as he would have priced a wall. "The form does not require the clerk to ask who a bearer is, or a hand, and it does not forbid him. There will be a moment at that yard door when a pen is held over a line, and a man is counting; and if he is a curious man, or only a careful one, he will say *and you are?*, to pass the time. I have known a hundred clerks. Eighty of them would."
   294	
   295	"And then?"
   296	
   297	"And then somebody must answer, with a name or a lie; and I will not have a lie told at a registry's door by anybody who means to go on standing on this side of the Line." His eyes went, briefly, to Lira, and to Brom. "We go in on a count, or we do not go in. I cannot tell you which of the hundred he is."
   298	
   299	"There's the other thing," said Cael.
   300	
   301	"The square."
   302	
   303	"A porter told me, at the trough. It goes across."
   304	
   305	"It is the only way to the front door, and a load does not go in at a front door. There is a yard, and a yard has a gate, and carts must reach it by some road which is not a paved square with a station's steps on it." Seln got up off the ladder's head. "I have not found that road. I shall find it tomorrow; and tomorrow at noon somebody will stand at a cook-house counter with a sheet in her hand that a certain man cannot look away from. That is as far as I have got."
   306	
   307	Karis laid her pencil down on the flat. Her hand was not quite steady.
   308	
   309	Brom turned the lantern low. The blanket hung over the gable, with a faint paleness round its edges that did not flicker; and Cael lay on his back, with his eyes on the slates a yard above him, and spoke to them.
   310	
   311	[[TIER-B: the S8 close — "Last time we walked into a place like this," … "it took four years and a hearing to walk out." / "Last time we didn't know what we were looking for." / "Do we now?" / "A name and a reason," … "Older than the walls, under our feet, and nobody's read it because nobody's had a reason to look and a person who could read it in the same lifetime." … "That's us. That's the whole reason it's still buried. We're the first ones who are both." — coordinator to supply protected text]]

