     1	# Chapter 26 — The Iron Door
     2	
     3	The lane ran under the third wall for two hundred paces, and they were the longest that Cael had walked since a cut on a moor.
     4	
     5	It was a narrow way, cobbled, with the wall's inner face on his right hand and the backs of old houses on his left. Nothing in it was meant to be looked at. There were water-butts, and ash-heaps, and a line of washing; and every forty paces, between two houses, there was an alley.
     6	
     7	He had not been told about the alleys.
     8	
     9	Each was no wider than a door. Each ran straight, from the lane, away to his left, between blind walls; and at the far end of each, a hundred yards off, there stood an upright strip of white. It was the same white that had lain on the setts at the head of the street. The square was at the end of every one of them. It lay along that side of the lane, the whole way, like a lit room beyond a row of keyholes.
    10	
    11	He kept his eyes on Brom's heels.
    12	
    13	It was harder than the rise had been. On the rise the thing had been a thumb's breadth of light at a day's distance, and he had only needed to turn his head. Here it came at him sideways, at the edge of his sight, a bar of brightness that swung across the cobbles in front of his boots at each opening and was gone. He did not need to look. He had only to walk through it. He found that the muscles of his neck had set like a rope with a load on it.
    14	
    15	At the first alley Lira's shadow fell across the brightness before it reached him.
    16	
    17	She had come up on his left at the gate, and she had stayed there. She was a hand shorter than he was, with a box that stood up above her head; and at every opening she was between it and him for the two strides that it took to pass. She did not look down the alleys. He could see the side of her face, and it was turned to the lane ahead, and her chin was level. She went by each strip of white as she would have gone by a dog that she did not mean to notice.
    18	
    19	Nobody had asked it of her. He had not asked it on a road, or in a loft where a blanket hung on two nails, and he did not ask now. He counted the alleys instead, since he counted anything. There were four.
    20	
    21	At the third there was a sound.
    22	
    23	It came down the alley from the white end, small and clear in the quiet: a broom, going on stone, in long even strokes. Somebody was sweeping a great many steps. The keeper at the arch had said that they were swept. Under the broom, once, he thought that he heard a child's voice, high up and a long way off, counting.
    24	
    25	He did not turn his head. He went through the bar of light on the cobbles, and through the shadow that a girl laid over it; and the broom went on behind him, stroke after stroke, and grew faint.
    26	
    27	"The lane is not on the plan," said Vastin.
