    40	
    41	Then the cobbles tipped, and the lane began to go down.
    42	
    43	---
    44	
    45	It turned as it fell. The houses ended on the left hand, and the wall on the right dropped to a parapet no higher than his waist, where the crown of the bluff broke off; and for the length of ten yards there was nothing on that side but air.
    46	
    47	Lira stopped.
    48	
    49	She set her box down on the parapet's top, and held it there by one becket, and looked.
    50	
    51	The whole town lay under them. They had come round to the bluff's northern edge, and the ground fell away so steeply below the parapet that Cael could have dropped a stone onto a slate roof. He saw the second wall from above, going round the hill's waist like a belt, with the blue gate in it no bigger than a keyhole. He saw the roofs change below that from slate to tile and thatch. He saw the outer wall, and the long shed of the rope-walk laid against it, and the river coming round under the quay; and far out, small and dark across the brown water, a bridge on five piers.
    52	
    53	It went down in three steps. She had said so, from a rise, three days ago. *They've built the ladder out of stone.*
    54	
    55	He watched her, and not the town.
    56	
