    58	
    59	He waited for her weight to go forward.
    60	
    61	It did not. She stood flat on both heels, with one hand on a box, and she looked the whole of it over, from the parapet to the bridge, slowly, with her lips moving. She had looked at a factor's fence so, on a hillside, when she was asked what it would take to mend.
    62	
    63	"Two gates," she said at last. "And a view."
    64	
    65	Nobody spoke.
    66	
    67	"That's all that it is. I've been trying to see what else there was since the first one." She did not take her eyes from it. "It's three walls, one inside the next. You go through a gate and the roofs get better. You go through another, and there's a tap that you needn't pay at. And when you're right at the top, what you've got is this. You can see everybody who isn't."
    68	
    69	"It's a good view," said Brom.
    70	
    71	"It's a very good view. I'd have given everything I had for it. I very nearly did; I'd my foot on it." Her left hand had come out of its pocket, and she looked at that for a moment, the long thin fingers that had been in wool for the better part of half a year, and shut them. "And I'm stood on it, and it doesn't want anything of me. I thought that it'd pull. I've been waiting for it since the rise. I had it all ready, what I'd say to myself when it did."
    72	
    73	"And it doesn't," said Cael.
    74	
    75	"It's a hill with a tax on it." She said it with a kind of wonder. "You can't want a place the same once you've been its porter. I don't think you can, anyway. I've carried fifty-two sheets up the inside of it, and seen who fetches its eggs."
    76	
    77	She swung the box off the parapet, and got her arms through the beckets, the right and then the left.
    78	
    79	"It's worth the look," she said. "I've looked."
    80	
    81	Karis had turned to her, with the flat under one arm. She did not say anything, since she never did when a thing was finished. She only moved aside, so that the girl with the box should go first into what lay beyond the parapet.
    82	
    83	It was the cut.
    84	
    85	It opened in the wall of the last building, a slot a stride and a half wide, and it went down. Cael saw the first flight: five steps of dark worn stone, and a landing, and the head of the next. The walls went up on either hand, three times the height of a man, without a door or a window or so much as a drain. The sky was a strip at the top. It was like looking down a well that had been laid on its side.
    86	
    87	Brom went in first, and his box all but brushed both walls.
    88	
    89	"I shall go before you," said Cael.
    90	
    91	The stick had stopped at the slot's mouth. The old man was looking down the first flight, and at his right leg, and he was doing a sum.
    92	
    93	"You are behind me, in the order."
    94	
    95	"I'm going two steps below you. If the knee goes, it goes onto a box, and the box is full of paper."
    96	
    97	"You are proposing to stand where I should fall."
    98	
    99	"Yes."
   100	
   101	"That is not a thing that I can properly require of a person who is carrying the weight that you are."
   102	
   103	"You've not required it. I've said where I'm walking."
   104	
   105	The grey eyes rested on him. Then Vastin shifted the case higher on his shoulder, and set the stick's foot on the first tread.
   106	
   107	"So you have," he said.
   108	
   109	He went down as a man goes down who has thought it through. The stick went first. Then the bound leg was swung out and lowered, quite straight, like a plank let down on a rope; and then the sound one bent, and took the whole of him, and followed. He did that for every step. Cael went backward below him, with a hand on either wall and his heels feeling for the treads, and the box dragging at his shoulders.
   110	
   111	Lira counted aloud, from the landing beneath. Five. Nine. Thirteen.
   112	
   113	It was on the fourth flight that the stick slipped.
   114	
   115	The tread there had been worn into a dish by more feet than anybody could have reckoned, and it was wet. The ash skidded an inch. The straight leg came down with nothing to steady it, and the whole long weight of the man tipped forward into the slot.
   116	
   117	Cael had both heels down before he knew that he had set them. Compression had come up into them of its own accord, as it did when a load was on its way, and his frame creaked like hemp. He told it to wait.
   118	
   119	The load did not come. A raw left hand, which had not opened flat since a ferry's strap, struck the wall and held there; and the old man hung on it, with his shoulder against the stone and his breath gone, a foot above the box.
   120	
   121	"Seventeen," said Lira's voice, from below, a little higher than before.
   122	
   123	"Seventeen," said Vastin. He got the stick under him again. "I had it as seventeen."
   124	
   125	He did the last five without a word. At the bottom he stood with both hands on the ash, quite grey, and looked back up the slot at the strip of sky.
   126	
   127	"Twenty-two," he said. "The man can count. I shall tell him so."
   128	
   129	---
   130	
   131	The cut let them out through an open gate into a yard, and the yard was full of carts.
   132	
   133	It was a long paved place, sloping, shut in on three sides by sheds and on the fourth by the back of a very large building. A wagon of split logs was being unloaded at a dock. There was a beam on a post by the lower gate, with a pan hung from it and a man beside the pan holding a slate. Nobody looked round at four backs and a small pale woman coming out of a hole in the wall. Loads came into that yard all day.
   134	
   135	Cael looked at the building, since it was the only thing there that he had come for.
   136	
   137	He looked at it from the ground upward. It was of the pale stone, mortared, and it went up five storeys, with a row of small barred windows to each. It had no ornament on this side. A registry does not trouble about its back.
   138	
   139	Karis had stopped again. He saw where her eyes were, and followed them down.
   140	
   141	The lowest three courses were dark.
   142	
   143	They ran the whole length of the wall's foot, under the pale, close-jointed and without a thread of mortar; and where a pale block had been set upon a dark one, the mason had cut it to lie snug, one stone at a time. She did not say what it was. She had said it once already, on a river bank. Her gloved hand went out, and came back, and closed on the flat.
   144	
