editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-13.md
     1	# Chapter 13 — Ours
     2	
     3	The page lay where it had lain all night, on its square of oilcloth at the bare end of the deal, and the first grey light out of the one small window showed Cael that nobody had moved it.
     4	
     5	He had slept beside it, after a fashion, on the meal-sacks. It was one line out of an old register, copied by a man who had then walked three hundred miles to lay it on that table, and it sent whoever read it to a town. He had got up twice in the dark to see that it was there, which was foolish, since a sheet of desk paper does not walk; and each time he had stood over it, keeping both hands by him, and had gone back to the sacks.
     6	
     7	Karis came in before the smith's first hammer, with her gloves on already.
     8	
     9	"I am packing," she said. "I said that I should count in daylight, on a table without elbows, and I have been awake since the middle of the night working out where such a table is to be had. It is not in this inn. So everything goes into the flat, in the order that he gave it, and the flat goes where I go."
    10	
    11	She folded the oilcloth over the page along its own creases, and did not look at him while she did it. The broker's sheet went under the sixth row, and the straps drew down across a red stamp which said that the thing was to go by hand.
    12	
    13	"You have still not touched it."
    14	
    15	"No."
    16	
    17	"I am not asking you to. I only enter what I see." She put the flat under her arm. "It is a hand's width that you have kept, all night. I should not have had the patience."
    18	
    19	He followed her out through the tap-room, where the keeper was raking his ashes, and into the yard.
    20	
    21	It was the two hundred and seventy-eighth morning past the Line, and cold, with a white mist on the river that hid the cable's middle. Under the long eave, on twelve feet of tarred oak, Pike had his book open and his stone on the leaf. He was driving a tack with the butt of his penknife into the last clear space at the plank's right hand.
    22	
    23	"That came over in the bag," he said, without turning. "I had it yesterday, with eleven pieces to give out and a boat that nearly went down to the mill. I don't pin by lamp."
    24	
    25	Five of them read it together from the left-hand post, since Lira and Brom had come round from the well, and Seln had been standing there already, with no wand under his arm. It was a strip of bark, like every other slip on that oak, in the square black hand. Beside the words Pike had drawn a little road going between two heaps, and on the road something with four legs.
    26	
    27	*Clearance. The cut below the shoulder hold, on the border road, two days west.*
    28	
    29	"It's twenty," said Pike. "At the hold's own gate, on the factor's thumb, when the factor's walked it. That's a hold that's lost carts. It doesn't say what to. They never do; they'd have to pay more."
    30	
    31	Nobody answered him at once.
    32	
    33	Cael read the slip a second time, as he read anything, and it was the last five words that he read. Two roads left the western landing. One ran away to the south and west for three hundred miles, to a ford and a station and a house with a seal, and an old man had come down it on a stick. The other was the border road. It went up over a moor to the Line by the short way, and Cael knew what stood at the end of it, since a small pale woman had laid her palm on a strip of oilcloth and told him; and last night he had read it again, in a square black copy, under a title.
    34	
    35	"Two days west," said Lira, with her lips. She did not say the rest.
    36	
    37	At the far end of the eave, beyond the wash-house door, the keeper had a settle against the wall for carters to wait on. Vastin sat there now, at the door end, with his stiff leg out before him and both hands on the head of the ash stick. He was on no slip on that oak, and on no line of that book. He had read the new one as soon as it was pinned; Cael saw his eyes go to it once and then away to the river, as a guest's go from a letter that is not his.
    38	
    39	"Ours," said Karis.
    40	
    41	She had said it small and clear, with the flat under her arm. Cael had known her since a library table in Ostrand, and in all that time he had never once heard her claim a job. She had put her hand up for fences and for roofs when hands were asked for, and had gone where the count sent her; she had never looked at a slip and wanted it. Pike's pen stopped over the horn.
    42	
