   247	Cael looked at his own two hands, which lay on either side of a heap of paper. It was a cord that he was thinking of, run out three times across an inn yard, with a slip of ash laid down at its end and a scratch on a stone trough under the middle of the slip. It was a hair, in a gap that had been measured for it. He had been one short then. He had held the thing exactly as four other people held it, and it had come out a piece under what it was.
   248	
   249	He had thought that paper would be different, since a sheet either lay in a heap or did not.
   250	
   251	"Count it for me," he said. "With your own hands. I'll not touch it."
   252	
   253	Karis had begun to rise from her stone. She had the look that he had seen on her in a store behind a chimney, with her cheek almost on the deal, and it was not unkind; it was a great deal worse than that. It was careful.
   254	
   255	A sleeve came past his shoulder.
   256	
   257	He had not heard anybody cross the turf. A small dry hand laid a single sheet down on the rock in front of him, between his two hands, and squared it with one finger, and went away again. It was thin stock, and newer than the rest, and it had been folded once. It had a printed head. At its foot a bar of black lay across the paper, as broad as a thumb, cracked like mud in a rut.
   258	
   259	It had not been laid at the foot of the second row, where it belonged, and it had not been given to Karis, who was the keeper of it. It had been set before him. And the man who had set it there did not say one word.

