     1	# Chapter 18 — A Year and a Day
     2	
     3	Seln had taken the sheet an hour before, from the foot of the second row, while a small pale woman was reading four words aloud from the foot of another; and it had troubled him a little that it was still so easy.
     4	
     5	He had lifted papers out of heaps under their owners' eyes for the better part of his working life. One did not snatch. One was seen to be handling the heap for an honest reason, as he had been, reading six rows for what they left out. One's left hand squared the sheets while the right went on turning them. The sheet was in his sleeve before the pebble was back on its corner. Nobody at the slab had looked up. They were the only six people living from whom he had never before taken anything.
     6	
     7	He stood at the basin now, with his back to the rock, and held the thing low against his body, where the wind could not get at it.
     8	
     9	He had read it once already, on the night of the two hundred and seventy-seventh, over a shoulder, by one lamp. He had not meant to read the date at all. Karis's pencil had come to it, and written a year of the registry's and a day in that year, in a clerk's figures, exactly as they stood; and a man who has spent fifteen years among the seat's paper reads such a date as a carter reads a load, whether he chooses to or not. He had read it before he knew that he was reading.
    10	
    11	He had known that day. He had written it.
    12	
    13	It stood at the head of a file. It was the first line of the first sheet, after a name and before a word in square brackets, where the registry sets down the day on which a person was classed. The file had lain open on a good many desks at a house above Ostrand, in the years when he was employed there to carry such things from one desk to another, and to be overlooked. He had copied that line twice with his own hand, for two different officers. A clerk forgets a face in a week. He does not forget a line that he has copied twice.
    14	
    15	So he had stopped. He remembered it without any pride: his hands, and his breath, and the eyes that he had not been quick enough to take off the pencil. The boy had seen. He saw most things, though he was slow to say so. Seln had said that he had read it, which was true, and had gone to a window and looked at nothing for the rest of a lamp.
    16	
    17	He had not been certain. That was the reason that he had given himself, and it had been a good one for two days.
    18	
    19	A date read once, by lamplight, over another person's shoulder, in another person's pencil, at the end of such a day, by a man who would have paid a year's wages to find it otherwise, was not a finding. He might have taken one figure for another. So might she, for all that she never did. He might, after fifteen years of files, have laid the wrong day at the head of the right one. There were three places where it could have slipped, and he had spent two days on a road going over each of the three.
    20	
    21	He read it again now, properly.
    22	
    23	He had the sheet itself this time, and daylight, and nobody at his shoulder. He read the technician's date a figure at a time, with his thumb moving under it, as he had not read anything since he was a junior: the year first, and then the day. He turned the sheet over, and looked at the brush's work on its back, and turned it again, and read the date a second time from its last figure to its first. Then he shut his eyes. He set the other date beside it, the one that he had copied twice, and went along the two of them together, as a clerk goes down a copy against its original.
    24	
    25	They agreed. They had agreed by the lamp, and they agreed now.
    26	
    27	He stood for a while and watched the water run over the basin's lip.
    28	
    29	He did not ask himself what it meant. He had a rule for that. It was older than the one that Karis kept, and a good deal less handsome. A clerk who guesses at a file in front of its subject has written the subject's finding for him. He had seen it done to this boy since his Kindling, by men who were better paid than himself and no wiser, each of whom had looked at an empty column and found it unbearable, and had put a word in it. He did not know what a sequence was. He could not have said what it was for one to be initiated, or what lay under a plate, and neither could the man who had carried the sheet three hundred miles. He knew that two papers agreed about a day. He had no more than that, and he did not intend to have more.
    30	
    31	There remained the question of what a person did with it.
    32	
    33	---
    34	
    35	His training allowed three things to be done with a fact, and he went through them in their order, since it was the only order that he had.
    36	
    37	A fact could be filed. One kept it, and said nothing, and let it lie against the day when it should be useful; and he had kept a great many so. It was what a desk did. He had cut a hundred and forty-seven notches in two hazel wands to count what a desk would do with a clerk's packet, and had been wrong about the manner of it; but he had not been wrong about desks. There was an office somewhere that had held six years of sheets where nobody would receive them. He had been reading its work all morning. He did not care to find, at his age, that he had become a small copy of it, with a locked case for a drawer.
    38	
    39	He did not look down at the strap across his chest. He had carried that case for twelve years, shut, and it was shut now. He had found before today that he could not hold two closed things up to the light in one morning. He made no ruling upon it.
    40	
    41	A fact could be reported. That was the second way, and it was the one that an honest man would be expected to take. He had only to walk back to the rock and say eleven words.
    42	
    43	He considered it with care, since he distrusted his own distaste. If he said it at the slab, seven people would have it in the same breath. Karis would be obliged to enter it; she entered everything. She would then be obliged to refuse the sentence that followed from it, aloud, with the boy standing there, and he had watched what that refusal cost her once already today. A man who had certified readings for forty years would hear a day named, and his mouth would open on an order before he could prevent it. A healer would hear it from the basin. And the boy would be told one more thing about himself, in public, by a clerk.
    44	
    45	He could take the boy aside. That was better. But it would still be Seln's mouth, and Seln's choice of words, and Seln's face to be read while he chose them. Afterward the boy would have to decide what to tell the others with somebody standing by who already knew. He had been handled so since he was a child. People had found things out about him and then come to tell him what they had found.
    46	
    47	There was the third way. Seln had used it oftener than the other two together, and never once on a friend.
    48	
    49	One placed a thing. One did not say it. A paper was left where the right person would come upon it, at a time when he could read it unobserved. He was let to make of it what he would, and was never afterward asked. It was how a man was turned, in the old trade, or warned off, or ruined. It was done so that the subject should believe that the knowledge was his own. Seln had been excellent at it.
    50	
    51	He looked at that squarely, standing over a spring on a moor with a sheet in his sleeve.
    52	
    53	The kindest thing that he knew how to do for the boy was, in every particular that could be seen from outside, the same act as the cruellest thing that he had been trained to do to a stranger. He could find no way round it. The difference lay wholly in what he wanted. He had been taught, by people who knew their business, that what a man wanted could not be seen and ought never to be trusted.
    54	
    55	He wanted the boy to have it, and to be free of him in the having.
    56	
    57	That was all. The boy could read a registry date; he had read them upside down on other people's desks for as long as Seln had known him. He knew this one a great deal better than Seln did, since every paper that had ever been served on him began with it. If the sheet lay before him he would see what it was, or he would not look. If he did not look, it was not yet his to carry. If he did, then he might write it or burn it, and tell five people or none. Nobody alive would have the right to ask him what he had made of it. Seln would least of all, having said nothing.
    58	
    59	It had a cost, and he made himself price it, as that large young man would have.
    60	
    61	A thing given so cannot be shared. He would never know for certain that it had been read. He could not stand beside the boy in it, or be asked what he thought, or say that he was sorry. He could not be thanked, which he did not mind; he could not be blamed either, and he found, with some surprise, that he minded that. He would be handing over the heaviest paper that he had ever carried, and he would have to do it as a man slides a bowl along a board, and look away.
    62	
    63	He thought that it was probably what the boy would have done for him.
    64	
    65	"Cael," said Karis's voice, behind him.
    66	
    67	He heard the boy give a figure. He heard a pencil stop. *Again*, said Karis, quite gently; and then the boy's voice, a little too level, saying that he had done it twice, from the front and from the back.
    68	
    69	"Then you are one short of me."
    70	
    71	Seln turned round.
    72	
    73	He had not thought of it. He had taken one sheet from the foot of one row, to be sure of a thing before he did anything with it. He had forgotten that she never trusted a count until a second person had made it. She had put that row into the one pair of hands in which a cord had read short. He saw those hands now. They lay flat on the rock, on either side of the heap, and they had gone the colour of the paper. The boy was looking at them as a man looks at a tool that has failed him twice.
    74	
    75	Seln had been wrong about a desk, after a hundred and forty-seven days of counting it. He had been wrong about this by the width of a slab of rock, and it had taken him less than an hour.
    76	
    77	He crossed the turf, and made no more noise than he ever did. He set the sheet down between those two hands, with its head away from the boy, as every paper had lain before him in his life. He squared it with one finger. Then he went back to the water, and stood with his face to the mule. He counted the sound of the spring, since a man who has placed a thing does not watch it found.
    78	
    79	He minded that a great deal more than he had allowed for.
    80	
    81	---
    82	
    83	It lay the wrong way up.
    84	
    85	Cael saw that before he saw anything else, and for a breath it steadied him. A clerk who set a paper before a person turned it to face him. This one had been turned the other way, with its printed head toward the far side of the rock, as papers had lain on a registrar's counter at Denvash, and on a magister's desk, and on a table on a bluff. It was how he had learned to read. Somebody had noticed that, and had laid the sheet so on purpose.
    86	
    87	He was not one short. That came next, like the blood coming back into a hand. There had been nothing the matter with his counting. A sheet had been out of the row, in a quiet man's sleeve, and here it was.
    88	
    89	Then he understood that it had not been given back to the row.
    90	
    91	He looked along the slab. Karis had sat down again on her stone. She had seen the sleeve, and she was writing something very small beside a figure, with her head bent; she did not look at him. Seln was at the basin with his back turned. Nobody else was nearer than Brom, who was frowning at the third row.
    92	
    93	Cael read the sheet.
    94	
    95	He had heard it read, by a lamp, in Lira's voice, from the far end of a table. There was the printed head. There were four short lines in a quick hand that leaned to the right, as a man writes on his knee. He knew what the first of them said, and the third, and where the fourth broke off. They had gone round in him for three days, with the stripe that lay across the rest.
    96	
    97	The second line he had never seen. It was a date. There was a year of the registry's in it, and a day in that year, in a clerk's figures.
    98	
    99	He read it as he would have read any other, and went on to the line beneath. Then his eyes came back. He had read a great many such dates, upside down, on a great many desks, and they were all of them much alike; but this one he seemed to have met, as a person seems to have met a face in a crowd.
   100	
   101	He did not trust that. He did it as a sum is done.
   102	
   103	There was one date that he knew as he knew his own name. He set it beside the technician's, in his head, and went along the two of them a figure at a time: the year, and then the day. He did it without moving his lips, with his two hands flat on the rock. He did not believe what he had, and he did it again from the far end, backward, from the last figure to the first.
   104	
   105	They were one date.
   106	
   107	He knew the day. It was written in his own file, on the first line of the first sheet. It came after his name and before a word in square brackets, where the registry puts the day on which a person was classed. He had first read it at fourteen, the wrong way up, across a counter, while a woman with a pen made a small mark beside it. Every paper that the Compact had ever served on him had begun with it: a summons, and a challenge under a seal, and an order for a room and an hour. It was the day on which he had stood in a windowless building and put his hand where he was shown. A plate of brass had taken eleven seconds over him.
   108	
   109	He had been waiting for it, he found, without knowing that he waited. A quiet man had stopped like a clock over that date, by a lamp. There was only one day in the registry's whole count that could have stopped him so on Cael's account.
   110	
   111	*Deep layer*, the sheet said, in the first of its lines; and then two words; and then that day.
   112	
   113	He stood very still.
   114	
   115	He had two things. One was a line in a technician's hand, about something under a plate that nobody could open. The other was a date, and the date was his. He could feel the place where a verb would go between them. He could feel a dozen that would have fitted, each of them ready, as the ten were always ready, leaning toward the first stride.
   116	
   117	He had learned from a small pale woman what a verb cost. She had sat up over one in a store behind a chimney, with a cord that read true and a hair in a fold of paper, and had refused to let it finish itself in her own head. He had not understood until now how hard that was.
   118	
   119	He told it no.
   120	
   121	A great many people were classed in a year. On that same morning, in other districts, other children had stood on other plates; a station's man who went below one, somewhere, might have found a thing beginning and written down the day. He did not know that it was his plate. He did not know what a sequence was, or what it meant for one to be running. The sheet did not say what it went with. It had begun to say, and somebody had laid a brush across the rest.
   122	
   123	It was true, every word of it. It did not help at all, and he held to it regardless, as he would have held a rope.
   124	
   125	His left hand had gone into his coat.
   126	
   127	It had Hesk's book half out before he knew what it was about. He had written down everything that had ever happened to him. It was how an old man had taught him to keep from being told what he was: one noted the day, and the count, and the trend. He had the pencil. He had the place, under yesterday's line.
   128	
   129	He looked at the blank beneath it for as long as he could have held his breath, and it frightened him more than the date had.
   130	
   131	If he wrote the two days side by side, he would have drawn the line himself. It would not matter that he added no verb. A sum set down with its answer showing is a sentence. Anybody who opened that book would finish it for him: Lira, who had read it, or a healer, who had made him swear by it once. And it was not his alone. A man at a basin had carried it for three days, and had put it into his hands without a word, so that it need never be said.
   132	
   133	He put the book back.
   134	
   135	He lifted the second row with both hands, and laid the technician's sheet in at its foot, where an office had filed it, and squared the heap upon it.
