   137	"It's whole," he said. "The row. It was only out for a while."
   138	
   139	"I saw where it was." Karis had not looked up; and then she did, and her pale eyes went over his face once, carefully, as they went over a seal. "You read it."
   140	
   141	"I've read it."
   142	
   143	She had been given those words before, in a store, by another man, about the same sheet. He saw her hear them. She did not ask a second time. She wrote nothing, and tied the tape across the second row with a knot that she pulled very tight.
   144	
   145	Lira was looking at him across the rock, with her chin up. She had a look for when he had put a thing away where she could not get at it, and he had seen it perhaps four times in his life. She gave it to him now, and held it, and let it go.
   146	
   147	He had entered every day that had happened to him since an old man put a pencil into his hand, and he supposed that he would go on doing so. There was one line now that Hesk's book would never have. He would always know the place where it was not.
   148	
   149	---
   150	
   151	"That is all six," said Karis. "Counted twice, by two people, and agreed."
   152	
   153	She had the rows back in the leather in their order, and the flap down. Nobody had spoken for some time. The sun had gone a little past its height, and the shadow of the outcrop had begun to creep out over the slab's western end.
   154	
   155	Vastin had stood through the whole of the morning. He was standing now, a yard from the rock, with both hands on the head of his stick and his eyes on that flat of waxed leather. Cael, who had had reason to watch faces that day, saw something gather in his.
   156	
   157	"There are places in that country," the old man said slowly. He was not speaking to anybody. "They have been measured for six years by an office that told no other office. They are classed, by a mark that the survey's own people carry on their iron. A surveyor who formed a view of them had the space for his view struck off the form. And a return upon the deepest thing that the Compact owns has been laid among their sheets, for want of a shelf, with a brush drawn through it." His voice had quickened, and gone level. "This has to go up."
   158	
   159	Nobody answered him.
   160	
   161	He heard it then. Cael saw him hear it: the four words, in a voice that had said them from a desk every working day for forty years. He stood with his mouth a little open. His right hand had left the stick's head, and it hung in the air a foot from his coat, on its way to a pen.
   162	
   163	"Up," he said, in another tone altogether. "To whom?"
   164	
   165	It was Seln who spoke. He had come back from the basin when the tapes were tied, and had taken his place at the end of the slab, hands in his sleeves.
   166	
