   176	
   177	Cael had known that voice for a long while. He had heard it give an inventory, and a warning, and a count of days; he had heard it refuse a good many things. He went back over all of that, standing by a rock on a moor, and he could not find one occasion on which it had told a person how a thing was done.
   178	
   179	Seln kept doors. He had seen two posts a hand out of true, and had told one man, in eight words, and let him mend them. He had stood with his back to a shed through a whole cold term so that he could never be asked what was in it. He knew more of how to be overlooked than any of them; and when an Archmarshal had answered a hand aloud on a road, and stood like a gatepost at a ditch, it had been a girl who walked backward in front of him and beat it out. Seln had watched. He had not said a word.
   180	
   181	He had said seven now. It was the first thing that he had ever taught anybody, in Cael's hearing. He had chosen for it the one man there who had once had the power to file him.
   182	
   183	"The tell," said Vastin.
   184	
