editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:85:Last term he had stood up in a room full of people and read their own schedule back to them, from the first page to the last, and he had won because he had not reached past the text by a single line. If he let himself start wanting this clause to be alive, he would end by reading it the way the Compact's clerks had read his own file for two years: hoping, and writing the hope down as though it were a finding, and never once noticing the difference.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:7:The notice lay on the desk where he had left it, folded on its crease, and he did not unfold it. He knew what it said. Somewhere inside the next month, on a day he did not choose, a man he had never met would watch him in a room full of instruments and write down what he saw, and every half-year after that somebody would lay the new figures beside the old and look for the difference. It was the only paper he would ever sign that could not be amended, Karis had said, and she had been right, and he had lain awake a good part of the night turning that over without getting anywhere at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:37:From above, the floor looked like a pale lake. The boards ran the long way of the hall, oak, close-laid and polished by feet to a dull shine, and when the cohort came in and began to warm up he could feel it through the bench: a faint give, a faint return. He had written that down on his first morning on the bluff without knowing why it mattered. He wrote it again now, beside the date, with a line under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:155:By then Ephram had a page to himself in the notebook and was well into a second. He had placed him on the Iron board the first afternoon and checked him against the registrar's table on the second: Blade Path, Iron-tier, Rank Six, at the top of the Iron column, which at Halcenvane was the same as saying he was the best competitor on the bluff. Fourteen of the hill's students were Silver and one fellow was Gold, yet the cohort spoke of none of them the way it spoke of Ephram, because no column in the yard carried their names. Here, *best* meant a place on a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:229:He was thinking of Wray on the last morning, standing on her stone with her slate under her arm. *Find somebody there who'll break your count on the third pass.* He had been on the bluff a month, and he had watched every hall and walked every corridor and filled a section of the notebook, and nobody on this hill had broken his count on any pass at all, because nobody on this hill was teaching him anything. They were measuring him. That was a different thing, and he had not until now let himself notice how different.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:235:*Brom has a man who'll make him worse on purpose and tell him so in advance. I have a provision, a panel, and a man with instruments. Nobody here is going to make me worse for a season so I can be better after it. That's mine to do, if it's done.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:5:He had walked past it most days for a month, on the path behind the assessment wing that ran down to the low hall, and every time he had looked at it and every time he had left it alone. It hung beside the door on a square iron nail at the height of his shoulder, a plain black key on a loop of leather gone shiny with handling, and the door it belonged to was locked from the outside. He had written both facts down on his first morning on the bluff, and underlined them, and had not been able to think of a single reason for either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:19:The porter looked up at him for perhaps the first time since Cael had come to the bluff, briefly, with a kind of patient pity.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:29:The porter grunted and went back to his rag without asking what an eight might be. He had the incurious patience of a man who had carried every trunk on the bluff and had stopped wondering, years ago, what was in any of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:51:The low hall was one room, low-ceilinged as its name, with its windows set very high along one wall, so the light came down in long slanting bars and lay across the floor in stripes. The floor was plain stone, swept bare. Two pupils were standing in the middle of it, a fifth-year girl with her hair in a tight knot and a younger boy, and both of them had stopped where they were when the knock came, and had not moved since. Along the back wall a row of pegs held nine grey practice smocks, one for each student of the Path on the bluff, with a name stitched over each peg in blue thread. Below them the stone had been scored with fine straight lines, dozens of them, crossing and recrossing, like the marks a joiner leaves on a bench where he has measured the same cut a thousand times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:87:A thing had been built eighteen inches from his skin that could have shut off two of his ways out, and the instrument he had trusted for two years above his own eyes had not stirred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:117:"Every other Path on this bluff is a push of some kind. Force is a push. Blade is a push sharpened. Stone is a push that refuses to move." Her hands still hung quite motionless at her sides. "A frame doesn't push. It forbids. There is nothing moving in it for you to feel. You may as well listen for the weight of a law."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:139:Outside, the noon light was very bright after the striped dimness of the low hall. He hung the key on its nail and stood with his hand still on the leather loop, looking at it. A month he had walked past it, and a month it had been hanging there, at the height of his shoulder, offering him the plainest piece of information on the whole bluff, and he had left it alone because he could not read the invitation. The invitation had been perfectly simple. *Stop. Knock. Wait.* Caution had hidden it from him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:157:*Nine people on this bluff build frames. And half the Paths this hill teaches I've never been close to. Some of them it can't see. I won't know which until I'm beside them, and perhaps not even then, unless somebody tells me something's there.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:229:*Lira: nine bouts, nine wins, in a register half the bluff reads with a pencil in hand, and still nothing in it can climb as high as her certificate.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:233:*Me: a named category, the finest boards I've stood on, half the bluff's Paths still out of reach, a read caught lying this week, and no line anywhere.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:7:It was generous. He understood that at once and was grateful for it. He could stand on any floor the office sent him to, under any instructor it named, and run whatever the record allowed; he could sit at the back of any lecture on the bluff, on any Path; he could be present at anything Halcenvane did in public, and Halcenvane did nearly everything in public. At Greyvane he had begged for half of this and been given a quarter. Here it came folded once, in a clerk's hand, without his asking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:29:He had known his name would not be there. A ladder is fed by certificates, and no certificate anywhere carried a tier against his name for it to be fed. The porter wiping the boards had taught him that much. Knowing it and standing over a book of names with his hand on the page were different things.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:31:No line. No bracket, so no seed. No session pairings, no challenge windows, no slow climb of four rungs at a time. No crown, however well he fought and however many years he stayed. He could go down into the sunk floor tomorrow and put every practitioner on the bluff on the boards, one after another, with eleven hundred people watching, and not one stroke of chalk on any of the four walls would move. There was no place for it to move to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:37:At Greyvane he had made a discipline out of this, over a year, and called it a vantage point. If you could not compete, you could watch, and nobody measured the one in the corner, and the corner saw everything. He had believed it. He had built a good part of himself on it. Now he would learn, on this bluff, how much of that belief had been real and how much had only been comfort.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:49:*Filed. Working on it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:181:He did not know anybody else on the bluff who had made that choice where he could see them make it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:201:"Because of the sweep." Karis sat at last. "Clause six of the committee's charter. When a cycle closes, whatever a student has made in the library gets swept up into the library's own holdings. Catalogued, shelved, opened to any reader on the bluff, or on the coast, or anybody who writes in with a fee." She tapped the third term. "Institutional work held by the registrar isn't. It stays in his cabinet, under his key, read by whoever he says may read it. It's a filing distinction."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:23:He thought instead of a question she had asked him months ago, in the week after the match with Karis. She had asked it not for the file but for herself, and he had written it in the binder the same night because he had not known the answer. Whether he could *not* do it. Whether, in front of people who wished him harm, with everything lined up the way it had lined up once in a formal yard, he could hold still and keep his hands to himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:107:At Greyvane there had been no choosing, because nine people had been the most on any floor and he had held every one. Here he would have to choose, and choosing meant putting down on paper, privately, his own ranking of who on this bluff was going to matter, which was the same as deciding which nine-tenths of the people around him would be weather.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:123:"He knows where everybody sleeps, and why. He knows which trunks go to which quadrangle before the trunks do." Cael considered it. "He might be the best-informed man on this bluff, and nobody's ever going to ask him anything."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:137:He kept the Crown yard back until the sixth morning on purpose, because he wanted the whole bluff understood first, so that when he finally stood in the middle he would know what it was the middle of.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:169:"Five tiers if you count the Gold, and she doesn't count." The porter jerked his head toward the north halls. "One Gold on the bluff. She's not on any board. Finished her coursework two years back and stayed on, under some fellowship the chancellor's office drew up. Doesn't fight on the ladder. Nobody within two tiers of her to fight." He started on the Copper board. "Two hundred and nine Copper. Hundred and thirty-eight Iron. The fifty-one and the fourteen. That's your whole school."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:173:Until that moment he had not known how many people the bluff held. Standing there he saw he still did not. What the porter had given him was a tally of papers. The Gold fellow was not in it, because the brackets had run out of room above her. And he was not in it either. The brackets had never had a column for him at all. There were two people on this bluff that the boards could not describe, one at the top and one off the side, and neither of them appeared on any wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:197:"Her certificate says Copper." The porter wrote *COPPER* in big square capitals across the top of the clean board. "The board doesn't care what you can do, lad. It reads what's on your certificate and puts you where that says. It's not unkind. It's a board."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:219:They walked back to the bridge in silence. Halfway across, at the crown of the middle span, Brom stopped at the parapet where Lira had stopped on the first day, and looked up at the bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:229:"It changes into everything else," said Cael. "Rooms. You saw the trunks. The instructor's eye, who gets a word after the drill and who doesn't. Which cohort you're put in, and who you train beside. And Karis says the standings feed something bigger, something the academy wants more than any of that." He looked up at the dark hollow of the Crown yard, just visible over the wall at the top of the bluff. "Nobody hands you a purse at the end, because the prize is how they treat you for as long as you're here. Every bout is paid for in where they let you stand next year."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:239:*They spend on the yard, and the boards, and the rooms that go to whoever's seeded. They spend on a walled wing full of instruments under cloths. Nobody's paid in coin here. They're paid in standing, and standing pays in everything.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-09.md:5:She never once glanced at the page. She stood at the table with her hands behind her back and went down the list in the order the names had gone onto it, with a line for each: which hall, which Path, how they had walked up to the table and how they had walked away. Two hundred and nine Copper practitioners on the bluff, and something over sixty had signed by then, and she had been on the rim or in the tiers for every one of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-09.md:15:"Transfer," he said, reading off the sheet clipped beside it. "Wind Path. Copper, Rank Two, formal. Re-certification." He looked up. "You understand how the seeding goes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-09.md:133:"It goes one way," he said. "Certificate to bracket. Nothing comes back up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-09.md:243:Then she looked at Cael, and Cael looked at the notebook on his knee, at the question he had underlined twice on the thirty-fourth evening, with the empty space under it. The space under it stayed empty, since this was not an answer yet. He wrote it on the next page instead, and dated it, and put Lira's name beside it, and then, because it had been his sentence and he wanted it honestly filed, his own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:85:He had come to this bluff braced for the kind of opposition that showed in a body, the kind that set its shoulders before it spoke and came at him across a floor, and he knew how to fight that kind. This was not that kind. Rooke had written down a price in public, before it fell due, so nobody could ever say afterward it had not been named. Cael had done the same thing with his own life every night for two years, in a binder, with nobody watching.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:115:The last line of the second appeal asked for gifts *toward the instruments by which a school is known.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:139:There was a case of glass on a stone plinth along one wall, and in it three crowns on three dark cushions with a brass plate under each. The Copper was the plainest of them, and the darkest, and worn bright along its top edge where a great many hands had lifted it. Its plate had been lengthened twice with new brass riveted on below the old.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:157:The right column was what he would bring her: a name among the registry's clerks for being the school whose paperwork held when the hardest case on the continent leaned on it, and a serious practitioner, which was worth having on any hill whatever his file said. Last came something to prove things with, since she had a great deal to prove and, at a school of her size, very little to prove it with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:171:Below the tall windows the Crown yard lay empty in the afternoon. The session bell went faintly, a long way off across the bluff. Neither of them moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:173:"I'll tell you how rare," said Withrow, still to the case. "I could count the times on one hand and have fingers left, and I filed the other kind by the hundred. I put sound law in drawers for twenty years because it was inconvenient to the people above me. I was good at it." She turned round. "Nobody can make me put anything in a drawer now. And I mean to have done something with this chair before I hand it on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:225:*Not settled. Filed. Look again in a year.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:229:He closed the binder. Across the river the waterfront lamps had come up along the whole length of Ostrand, and under the lamp at the ferry landing the two new grey coats had settled in for their four hours. Behind the four of them, in the dark hollow at the middle of the bluff, the boards stood on their wall with last season's names wiped clean, and nobody yet had chalked up this season's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:29:It wanted his name as the registry carried it, and his number, and the station where he had Kindled, and the date, and the designation the instrument had returned, written exactly as the station had written it, brackets and all. It wanted the name of the household he had been registered under and how that name was spelled. It wanted to know whether he had ever, at any academy, been enrolled under a category whose terms he could not honestly meet, and if so, under which category, and what had been done about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:33:*Caelen Hesk-ward. 41-7843-V.* He wrote the number slowly, digit by digit, and checked it against the copy of the ruling in the binder, because he had learned that the number you were surest of was the one you were likeliest to get wrong. He wrote *Denvash* and the date. He wrote the designation in brackets, *[SHATTERED]*, as the station had written it, and under it a line saying that the panel at Greyvane had found it did not appear anywhere in the enumerated schedule, with the page of the ruling where it said so. He wrote *Hesk*, and then, because Bracken had asked how it was spelled, he wrote it again in capitals, and then felt foolish, and left it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:39:He wrote *Yes.* Then he wrote the name of the category, *unclassified observer*, and the term he had been enrolled under it, and that the form had been defensible on its face and had been challenged by the Compact and held. And then, because a file that left out the uncomfortable part was only half a file, he wrote one more line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:75:"I'm a seventeen-year-old with a notebook." Karis's voice was not quite even. "He's a registrar. He doesn't have to agree with me. He doesn't have to do anything but answer the letters he wants to answer and file the rest." She laid the three pages square on the table. "Agreement is cheap. People agree with you because they like you, or because they're tired, or because they want you to go away. This is worth more than agreement. He got there by himself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:149:"We filed in week sixteen," she said. "Brom and Karis and me. Transfers. Did you think we'd let you walk four days to a new gate on your own and find out from a letter which of us had come?" She speared a piece of turnip. "Karis wrote to the registrar. He sent the forms in the fourteenth letter. He said he'd assumed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:159:Lira had wanted, for as long as Cael had known her, one thing that nobody had ever been willing to give her. It was not a rank, though she would take the rank. It was not a crown or a purse or a better room. It was to stand in front of the instrument that had read her at fourteen and set her down as a Copper practitioner, and to make it look again. She had said so once, on a road, in the dark, and never again, and he had not forgotten it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:35:He was looking past all of it, across the water, at the far bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:37:Halcenvane stood along its top. The first thing he noticed was that nothing in it had been inherited. Every school he had ever seen had been made out of something older: a road-house, a granary, a lord's barn with a floor laid in it. This one had been built to be what it was, all at once, by somebody who had drawn it first. The roofs all ran at the same pitch. The stone was the same pale stone from end to end. Along the bluff's north lip ran three long halls with their windows set high under the eaves, all on the side the sun came up. One heavy-roofed building had walls thick enough to carry a great weight of something, and he thought it must be books. A separate block sat behind a low wall of its own. And in the very centre, where everything else on the bluff seemed to lean toward it, there was a great hollow ringed on three sides with tiered stone seats.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:53:The road up the far side climbed the bluff in two long switchbacks, and it had passing bays.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:79:"Dellenmoor. Standard. Research standing pending; the library committee writes to you. Brom. Standard. Ninth day, first bell, east hall; the Blade cohort wants a look at you. Lira. Transfer, re-certification. Copper bracket. You register for the ladder by the fourteenth or you start the season on the bottom rung and climb from there." He licked a finger and turned the page. "Cael. Assay-provision enrollment. Straight to the registrar. Assessment wing, ground floor, the door with the brass plate. He's cleared the whole morning, which he does not do, so go now and look at the view later."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:115:Cael could not hear all of it from the bench, but he could see it, and he could read Lira standing at an official table with her arms folded better than he could read most people's faces. Her clerk was a grey-haired woman with spectacles on a cord. Lira's file had come up from Greyvane ahead of her: the old Fenmark record, the acknowledgment of standing she had signed before any of them knew her, and, pinned at the back, the sheet Greyvane had given her. It was the flag asking the registry to look at her tier again, under Greyvane's seal, the provost's whole two terms of watching folded into one line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:167:In the first stack lay the clause, copied out of three founding-era compilations, every copy certified, every one tagged with where its book stood and which clerk had sworn to it. Bracken read out each mark. Cael, because the index was in his bag, took it out and found each one in Prynn's small upright hand and read her cross-reference back. Two of the compilations were ones Karis had worked from. The third Greyvane had never owned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:169:On the second certified copy, Cael stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:171:"The date on the clerk's certificate," he said. "It's a year after the date in the compilation's own colophon. But the shelf-mark is for the first printing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:175:"The copying clerk's slip," he said. "He took the shelf-mark off the first printing and the certificate off the second, because they stand side by side. The text's the same in both. I checked that myself. But the sheet says two things at once, and anybody who wanted to could pull on that thread." He set the sheet aside on a pile of its own. "It'll be redone this week. That's what I asked you here for. Thank you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:179:"Most people would leave the second sentence out," said Bracken. "It looks like weakness. It's the strongest line in the file." He tapped it. "Never let a document," he said, "claim more than it proves. If it claims one line more, that one line is where it gets attacked. Then people stop reading, and the whole file goes into the fire with its true lines and its false one together."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:201:This one was about him. It did not say what he could do. It was careful not to, and he could see now that the not-saying was what made it strong. It said only how he and the academy stood to each other. Here was a person whose capability no registry line could give, so it would be shown, in a room, before instruments, every semester, for as long as he was here. Nothing in it was hopeful. Nothing in it was kind. Every word of it was so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:225:"Twelve years I've kept these rolls," he said. "In twelve years, every record I've filed has been true because nobody ever had cause to look at it closely. Yours had to be true *because* I did the work properly. One day somebody will be paid to hunt for the flaw in it." He looked up. "Do you know how long I've waited for a file like that?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:231:It ran along the edge of the bluff behind the second quadrangle, chest-high, the same pale stone as everything else, and beyond it the ground fell away in rough terraces to the water. Ostrand lay across the river with the late light going gold along its roofs. Down on the wharves somebody was lighting lamps, one after another, and the lamps ran out along the waterfront like a line of stitches.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:255:"Two terms of putting Iron-equivalents on the floor. An academy's seal on a flag. A queue that's answered that woman three times in two years." She ate the bread. "And they read the number on my certificate, and they stop reading. They always have."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:287:He closed the binder. Down on the water the last lamp on the last wharf came alight. Lira bumped her shoulder against his, once, the old knock, and stayed leaning there. Brom passed the butter. Karis, beside them, was already writing her own day into her own book, small and fast. In the middle of the bluff behind them, in the hollow of stone seats he had seen from the bridge, nobody had lit a lamp at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-15.md:9:He had begun it in his second week on the bluff, for a reason that embarrassed him slightly and which he had written down anyway. He wanted to know when the staring would stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-15.md:29:Seven times within a few paces of the man who sat beside the calendar on which every semester evaluation of the assay provision would be written, and not once had the man lifted his eyes. There had been no glance, and none of the small twitch of attention a person spends on a door when it opens, and none of the half-turn of the head that every second student on the bluff gave him without thinking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-15.md:127:The fourth was the first one with the right idea in it. It gave up the man altogether and drew the bluff, every hall and passage and stair as a box with a letter in it, so that each sighting could be filed under its room. It took him two evenings. And it was wrong in a way he could not see until he had poured three days of shorthand into the boxes and found the shorthand would not go in. The man was never in a room. He was always on the edge of one. Rails, landings, the top of a stair, the middle of a knot of five people on a tier: the seams between one space and the next, where a person can stand still for a long time without anybody feeling that he has stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-15.md:139:"It's the bluff."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-15.md:141:"It's a very bad bluff," said Brom kindly, and withdrew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-15.md:149:For a week he had been asking where the man was. And for a week the bluff had given him the same answer: somewhere sensible, on an errand. A teaching assistant carried paper, and paper had business in every passage on the hill. There was nowhere on the bluff that a man with an armful of forms would look out of place, so there was nowhere that finding him proved anything. It was beautifully done, Cael thought, and he had been staring at it for a week like a fool at a conjurer's empty hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-15.md:155:He turned to a clean page and started again, and this time he left the man out entirely. He took each hour of his own week, every session and lecture and meal and practice afternoon, and worked out the one place on the bluff from which a watcher would see the most of it. It was a sum he could do. In the Ironyard he had done harder ones under fourteen lamps with a crowd shoving at his back, hunting the angle from which a fighter's off hand could not hide behind his own shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-15.md:187:Floor times on the bluff moved by paper and by nothing else, so the trade went to the assessment office before noon on a half-sheet in Cael's hand, countersigned for Brom's cohort. Cael took it there himself. The desk clerk read it, initialled the corner, and passed it across to the copying table. There the man with the steady pen read the front, turned it, read the back, wrote both changes into the ledger, and laid the sheet on its pile, without a glance at either the paper's owner or the paper's subject.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:19:It was not the room he feared; he had stood before two hundred people with the Compact's warden at the other table and read a schedule back to them page by page, and no room of instruments could be worse than that. It was the arithmetic: every practitioner on every board had been set down by an Arbiter at fourteen and handed a number. He was being handed a pen, and whatever he drew with it on the day would go into a folder in Gault's cabinet and stay there. One day an inspector would take it out, with a grudge or only with a schedule, and read it on a table, or aloud in a hearing room. *Choose with that in mind,* Bracken had said, holding out the tag on its cord. Cael had nodded at the time. He was only now beginning to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:29:"It's comfortable," he said. "That's the trouble with it. Comfortable for years, and then one day not, and all at once." He held up a finger. "Three costs. One: the line's a document. It goes into the file in Gault's hand, and one day an inspector opens the file. Bracken's warned me twice, and Withrow wrote *sooner* on her sheet with her own pen. Find a feeble line in there and you've found proof the provision lets feeble people in. Somebody uses that against the provision, and I've lost the only floor I stand on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:43:"And it's a lie," said Cael quietly. "Written down. In the one file I went into a hearing room to get made honest."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:59:He could have recited it. Fourteen of Greyvane's monthly sittings had been read into the hearing as exhibits, one after another, and every one of them, under three assessors' names, had said the same two things about him: that he could get out of the way with the Wind, and that he could feel a weight coming before it came. A court had looked at those two and filed them where anybody could find them. Nothing else about how he was made had ever been put where the world could read it, except the Ember, and that only in a single late exhibit, half a page, so thin that showing it on Gault's floor would not confirm anything. It would announce something. Of Pressure and Compression it said nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:127:"The Arbiter took about a minute and a half." Her eyes were on the wall's dark bulk, not on him. "It came, and I saw it, and it looked at me, and then the station clerk wrote *Copper* on a sheet and *Rank One*, and that was me. A minute and a half. Two years now I've been trying to get somebody to look longer than that." She was quiet a moment. "That man tomorrow has a room built for nothing but looking. A dozen instruments. He's got a clerk to time your blinks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:147:"I can't come in tomorrow," she said, sitting down on the step where Lira had been. "Closed panel. I asked Bracken. He said I could read the note afterward, when it's filed, like everybody else, and then he looked at me as if I'd asked to borrow his spectacles."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:167:He walked Cael across the bluff in the early light without saying anything at all, which was what Cael wanted. They went by the long way, round the rim of the Crown yard, where the porter was already out with his pail chalking the practice rings, and past the north hall, where somebody was running a drill alone on the big stone floor, the same somebody perhaps who had been running one there on Cael's first morning. Brom ate his bread. Cael carried his and did not eat it, and Brom noticed and said nothing about that either. The assessment wing's low wall came up ahead of them, pale and new, with its single gate standing open and nobody in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:175:The demonstration room was the most expensive place on the bluff, and he stopped just inside the near door for a moment longer than he had meant to, looking at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:179:Fifty feet by thirty, the room was floored in oak laid on bearers like the east hall's, and the boards answered his first step. Brass ran through the boards in a grid, finger-wide strips set flush a third of a pace apart, wall to wall in both directions. Nobody at the long table would need to stand to see where a foot had come down. He had grown up at Hesk's bench, watching an instrument-maker scribe a line on brass and check it three times before he cut, and he knew what it cost to set a true line over fifty feet, in any material at all. Whoever had laid this had done it by survey and not by eye. At the far end, where a careless setter's work always wandered, these ran true. The crossings were as sharp at the back wall as at his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:183:Two weeks of asking had got him the names of nine pieces out of the twelve. First a drop-frame: a trigger, a rail, a weight to run down it, and four more weights racked underneath; someone at the far end tested the pawl, and it gave one soft click. A pendulum escapement in a glass case, ticking a steady interval that he found himself breathing in time with before he noticed and stopped. Nine shutters in a board, opened in an order a drum behind them decided; the drum's ratchet made the noise of a thumbnail going slowly down the teeth of a comb. Three spring plates on braces, each with a date of calibration painted on its housing. For footprints, a shallow tray of chalk dust. And at the very end, a thing like a tall brass funnel over a cup, which dripped into the cup with a small bright sound every few seconds, and which he could not name at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:49:Gault turned his head an inch toward the clerk, whose pen was already lifted. "Put this down. Enrollee gives a working ceiling of four on sprung timber without being asked, and says what is wrong with the sixth before the instrument does." He turned back to Cael. "Since the brass went into this oak, nobody standing on it has said a more useful sentence. A man's best afternoon tells me very little. His honest ceiling tells me where to stand. Go on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:113:"No getting out of this one," said Gault. "Too close, too heavy. Catch it on the plate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:115:The clerk brought the plate across on its brace and put it into Cael's hands: a round of steel with a spring behind it and a small dial in the housing, made to show how much of a blow had been held and how much had gone on through.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:123:He stood with the plate and the reaching in his arm for two breaths, which was one breath longer than he had meant to, and he saw Gault see the extra breath and say nothing. Somewhere the pendulum ticked. He thought of Lira on the wash-house step: *make him look.* He thought of Karis in carrel eleven: *it was mine before it was yours.* He thought of half a page of transcript, late and thin. Then he let the reaching go, as he had let it go on a thousand other floors, and his arm was only his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:125:He braced the plain way: feet wide; the plate held out on an arm loaded from the shoulder; everything down through his legs into the oak. It was how a body with no Path takes a knock, and how he had taken every knock in his first winter on the Ironyard's planks. The pawl let go, and the weight came down the rail and met the plate, and the shock went up the bones of his forearm and stopped in the shoulder like a nail driven into a joist. Nothing in him carried it anywhere else; the shoulder had all of it. Behind the plate the spring hit the end of its travel with a dull clank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:149:The Ash instructor's book lay shut in front of her, with a finger in it at a place she had long since stopped keeping. The Mire instructor no longer looked resentful; she looked thoughtful, which on her face was nearly the same expression turned the other way up. And Gault had watched him; Cael understood it only now. All through the morning, every run and every drop and every clank of the plate, the heavy still man in the middle chair had watched him, start to finish, and looked away only to write.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:155:Then he lifted the top sheet. By charter the note had to be read in the enrollee's hearing. Gault read it, and Cael heard the words that would stand for him on this bluff for as long as he stayed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:159:Four short sentences, against three bad nights and an hour of held breath and a shoulder that would complain until the week was out. He waited to be let down by them, and was not. There was nothing wrong in any of them. *Consistent across trials* was the only good word the instrument knew how to say, and it had said it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:167:He wrote the plate up that evening at the desk by the window, with his arm on a folded shirt because it did not like to hang.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:191:"I don't need to know what you are, Enrollee. I need my instruments and your consistency. Give me the second; the first is not my mandate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:193:Cael did not move from the doorway. He held the sentence carefully. He had the sense that it was the most useful thing the bluff had handed him yet, and that it could be taken two ways, and he did not want to walk off with the wrong one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:199:"It's where I stop. And the stopping is for your sake." He said it simply. "The moment an assessor settles what a practitioner is, the measuring stops. Everything after that is him proving himself right. I have seen that finish men's careers. I saw it finish one man altogether. He was a better assessor than I shall ever be. He decided early what a girl in front of him must be, and he spent four years building trials that showed he was right, and they all did. A man's instruments will agree with him if he wants it badly enough. He was simply wrong."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:211:Under the plate that night he added the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:215:*About the man he spoke of. Instruments agree with whoever wants them to: that isn't a story about instruments. It's a story about me, if I let it be. Every column I keep, every third entry I've been hoping for since the first. I've been the one holding the instrument all my life. I'd better be Gault's kind and not the other.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-20.md:7:Copper ran to ninety-one names. Two hundred and nine practitioners on the bluff could have put their names to it, and a few more than half had looked at what a season cost them in hours and bruises and missed lectures, and decided not to pay. Iron ran to sixty-three of its hundred and thirty-eight. The last sheet, headed Silver, held nineteen, gathered from the Bronze and Silver tiers together, all that the top of Halcenvane had been willing to put on a wall. The thinness of that last sheet, Cael thought, was the whole reason a school like this one stayed a school like this one. By the second bell the figures were in the binder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-20.md:11:They came in ones and twos at first, and then in a steady stream, between the bells. Every one of them did the same thing first. He saw ninety-odd people do it that morning and not one failed to: they found their own name before they looked at anything else, with a small fierce hunt of the eyes, as if somebody might have left them off. Then, having found it, they did one of three things, and the three things told him more about the bluff than a month of corridors.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-20.md:21:Another had been there before him, at the board's left end, quite still, her hands clasped at the small of her back. She went down the Copper names slowly, line by line, the way a quartermaster goes down a list of stores. Force Path. Copper Rank Eight. Fiske. Her own name stood at the very top of the column, with two seasons of results behind it in Bracken's files, and beside it, alone among the hundred and seventy-three names on all three sheets, a small brass stud pressed into the sheet like a button.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-20.md:23:Cael had asked Bracken about that stud in his second week. Every other mark on the bluff's paper explained itself; that one said nothing, and he had not liked not knowing what it said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-20.md:39:Cael stood and did that sum twice. Nearly every name that had not fought last season was a first-year, and nearly every first-year carried a fresh Arbiter's Rank One. Lira's paper said Rank Two. The one figure on her certificate that had ever helped her had lifted her over seventy people at a stroke and set her directly beneath the last of the old names.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-20.md:41:Her paper and her place were two different measures, and only one of them would outlast the season. Copper Rank Two was her classification, written on her certificate by an Arbiter and changed only by an advancement evaluation. Twenty-second was her seed, a standing the ladder could move every six days and would wipe clean at the season's end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-20.md:59:Cael had Nyle already. Everybody on the ladder was in the binder by now, one page each, and Nyle's was one of the fuller ones because Cael had watched him twice on practice afternoons out of pure liking. He was a third-year, Copper Rank Five, broad through the shoulders with a settled, comfortable stance, a dozen wins across two seasons in Bracken's files.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-20.md:61:Stone was a slow Path to gather, and Nyle had learned, the way the best Stone fighters did, to make the slowness look like patience, so that an opponent waited for him when he ought to have been moving. A Stone fighter's weight could not bluff. It was the working itself, so whatever it promised, it meant, and the craft of the Path lay entirely in timing: bring it down at the instant that hurt most, and never mind who saw it coming. He breathed out through the gather, low, so that you could hear it from the tiers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-20.md:77:It was correct. It was what every Stone fighter on the bluff would have done against a smaller, quicker opponent: start the weight moving early, slow and heavy, and make her choose a side before he had to. Whichever side she chose, he would land on it. The yard had seen that gather beat the Copper column a dozen times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-16.md:9:He had worked it out on the walk back from the counter the day before, while his face was still warm from the floor trade. Anything written went through the office. Anything that went through the office reached the copying table. So the next test could not be written, or asked for, or filed, or entered. It had to be a thing he simply did, on an ordinary morning, with no paper behind it at all. And the only thing he could do without paper was turn up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-16.md:19:There were always people to stay among. Cael had never once trained in the east hall without a gallery; he was the thing on the bluff people climbed stairs to look at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-16.md:31:He came along it at a clerk's pace, neither quick nor slow. He passed the turned post, where he had stood on every one of Cael's mornings in this hall for nine days, and he did not slow at it. He did not glance at it. Most of all, and this was the thing that went through Cael like cold water, he did not look down. Below him, alone in an empty hall at an hour when nothing was scheduled, sat the most stared-at student on the bluff. Every porter and lecturer and first-year who had ever crossed that gallery would have glanced down at him from pure habit, the way anybody glances at the one lit window in a dark street.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-16.md:63:It took him across the shin. It did not hurt much. It told him a great deal. A slow instrument was a nuisance. A slow instrument he had started guessing ahead of was a danger, because a guess felt exactly like a reading from the inside, right up until it was wrong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-16.md:137:That night, at the desk by the window, he did not write the shorthand first. He did not write anything at first. He sat with the binder open at a clean page and the pen in his hand and the river sliding by in the dark under the bluff. The sentence came to him from wherever such sentences waited, and came out whole, on the first try, without one word crossed through, which almost never happened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-16.md:187:She waited. Below them a barge went downstream with its sweeps shipped, and its master blew his horn, the long note and the two short, and somewhere under the bluff another horn answered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-16.md:195:*Havel watched me and watched himself watching, both at once, and it leaked out of him at every seam. The coats watch me so that I will watch them; their whole craft is being looked at. The third does not watch me at all. He watches whatever I happen to be standing in, a hall, a yard, a stair, and lets me be the reason it is worth his while. I have been looking for his eyes. His eyes were never the instrument. His feet are.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-18.md:3:The bluff road came down to the river in four long bends, and at the foot of the last one, beside the toll board, two men in grey coats stood exactly where somebody had told them to stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-18.md:75:And under that stillness the air of the bluff had changed for him in a way it had taken him the better part of a fortnight to put a word to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-18.md:87:Fifteen years say the file's question is the wrong question. The interesting question is who taught him to watch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:1:# Chapter 21 — Instruments
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:63:There was another way to read the same sheet, from the top down, and that was the way the rest of the bluff read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:69:"No," said Bracken. He laid his pen down square to the desk's edge. "They are evidence. They are admissible. They are not a verdict. I would be grateful if nobody in your hearing confused those three words. Nor a standing with a rank. A standing is this season's chalk. A rank is what the registry has written on a certificate, and the registry does not read our wall." He folded his hands. "Next year this academy will contest the qualifying season for the Continental. The chancellor has said so in an open address; it is on the record, so I may repeat it. A squad will be chosen. The people choosing it will read this season's closing standings among a great deal else. A strong season will not put a name on that squad. A weak one will very probably keep a name off it." He picked his pen back up. "Your friend from the north stands near the foot of a column she plainly does not belong in. If she climbs, it will be noticed. If she does not, that will be noticed too. That is all I may tell you, and rather more than I ought."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:77:She was the best Copper fighter Cael had ever seen. Her certificate said Copper Rank Eight, and had said Rank Eight for two years. Rank, like tier, was a word an Arbiter wrote on a certificate after an advancement evaluation; no bout on any ladder could add a number to it, and while her paper said Copper, the Copper bracket was the only bracket she could fight in. No registry had written a word about Fiske in two years, because nobody had asked one to. Lira had worked out in a wash-house, over a pair of ruined shirts, why nobody had asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:89:He would not have called it a court. He came to the yard when the ladder told him to, fought, and won, without making anything of it; he was Iron Rank Six and the highest-ranked enrollee on the bluff, and he carried it like a man carrying an umbrella on a day it was not raining. But between his bouts he stood at the lower rail on the west side, and five or six of Rooke's cohort stood round him. When a bout on the floor ended he took it apart for them, exchange by exchange, in a low even voice he never raised, and the circle of people within hearing grew a little every afternoon, because people had learned to stop talking when he started.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:91:On the second of those afternoons, at the outer edge of the circle, Cael saw a small wiry figure in a Current smock with her hair dried into a wild bush. It was the first-year from the third ring, seventy-fourth on the Copper sheet, standing on her toes behind two Blade fourth-years to hear what the best fighter on the bluff thought about a bout she would never be in. Nobody had told her she could not reach the final. Nobody had told her she was allowed to listen to Ephram, either. She had simply come, and was listening, with her lips moving. Cael found, to his surprise, that he was glad.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:103:A school like this one lived by turning bruises into names on a wall, and names on a wall into a reputation, and reputation into money, and money into halls and brass grids and, next year, a qualifying berth on a continental stage. The Crown yard was the one place on the bluff where that whole long chain came out into the light where people could see it. Withrow had told him on his nineteenth day what she meant to have done with her chair before she gave it up. Here she was, doing it, in the plain open air, with a river-trade woman at her elbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:111:He would come through the north arch into the yard, or up the gallery stair, or out onto the second landing of the lecture range, and somewhere in the time it took him to count four, he would know. He did not look. The agreement said not to, and he had found he no longer had to. He asked himself the question he had carried onto every floor for three years, *where would I stand?*, and the bluff gave him one answer, and the answer always had a man in it. In the thick of a knot of people. High on the west. Never on yesterday's seat. Never at the bare end of a bench, where a body on its own is a thing the eye goes to. Never once alone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:125:Walking back along the coping afterward, Brom stopped to listen to a barge going down under the bluff, the long note and the two short, and the shorter answer from a landing somewhere below.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:139:The Log got its season entry late on Seventh-day. He wrote the inventory first, as he had opened every season he had ever logged, with the shutter back and the river noisy under the bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:145:*Pressure-adjacent. Unused this term. Withheld at the baseline plate. On the record as not shown.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:159:*Season's open. Lira's going to win the wrong crown. Brom's going to lose the right fights. And the Compact's man corrects my paperwork now. Everyone in this building is being measured by an instrument that doesn't fit them. I need to understand why the instruments are built this way. That's the semester's real question.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:173:"And then they'll write *Copper* on my certificate the morning after, same as the morning before." She was quiet. "You've written down that it won't mean anything."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:185:Cael sat for a while. Then he got up and went to the window and leaned on the sill with the river going by in the dark below, its lamps and its barges and somewhere under the bluff a horn saying *heard you*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:187:Every door on the bluff had its card, in a little brass frame at the height of a man's eye: name, Path, tier, the ledger hung up where people walked. He went round some of them in his head, one by one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:195:*Seln. Shadow. Bronze.* At the far end of the staff corridor. Bronze, because Bronze was easy to forget; and behind the card, work no certificate anywhere had a name for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:199:Every one of them weighed wrong. Every one of them weighed carefully, by honest people doing honest work, on instruments that did not fit. And instruments did not grow out of the ground like grass on a bluff. They were made.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:203:*Somebody built these instruments. Somebody chose what each one would look at and what it would look past. Nobody stumbles into a thing that exact. That was a decision.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-22.md:3:Karis wrote notes, and everybody on the bluff who had dealings with her learned it inside a fortnight; most of them learned to be grateful for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-23.md:23:"He isn't a friend," she said. "He isn't even a stranger. He's a man who was offered a job, and the job is Cael, and he said yes. Nobody carried him up the bluff road in a sack. Any morning he likes, he could walk down to the ferry and go home, and every morning he gets up instead and goes to stand wherever he can do the most damage. On the circuit at Fenmark, if a man came at you with a thing, you didn't owe it him back. You took it off him if you could. Nobody there would have called that stealing. They'd have called it learning to stay alive."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-23.md:59:"You've been talking all night about Karis's yard, where she put her Path out on the chalk and meant him to have it." He shook his head slowly. "Nobody's giving Cael anything here. That man is spending his Path declaration on Cael every hour of every day, a little at a time. The toll on the eyes. The posts. The walking past the empty hall without looking down. He's spent it on Cael more times than any of us could count, and he spent it to fill a file."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-23.md:175:The wash-house behind the second quadrangle had a stone floor that sloped to a gutter by the door, a copper in the corner, a long trough under the window, and a drying rack slung from the beams on two ropes and a pulley. Nobody came into it before the fourth bell, when the laundry woman arrived to light the copper. Cael had known that since his third week on the bluff, because in his third week on the bluff he had needed somewhere to drill a thing nobody had ever seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-23.md:189:He took them four breaths apart, the full run. In his third week on the bluff, rested, he had taken every one of them clean.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-19.md:121:"You're the only person I've ever met who'd file a bruise," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-19.md:139:The office numbered its halls on paper, as offices do, though nobody on the bluff ever said the numbers aloud. Hall one was the north hall, on stone. Hall three was the east, on timber. And his Sixth-day slot, the coming Sixth-day, the eightieth, which had been hall three every week since the allocation began, said hall one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-19.md:167:The brackets posted the next morning, and for a day and a half the whole bluff thought about nothing else, and Cael thought about it with the rest of them, and the floor issue sat folded in his coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-19.md:177:Nobody had complained. He was certain of that, because the only person on the bluff with a reason to complain was himself, and he had been nowhere near the counter for three days. The clerk who made up the week's issues had not caught it; that clerk had made the mistake in the first place. Somebody else had sat down with the ledger in one hand and Cael's issue in the other and seen the single week's trade carried into a week where it did not belong. That somebody had known it was wrong, and had known that Cael would know it was wrong, because Cael read every line of every sheet as surely as the man at the copying table read the backs. And he had known that Cael had let it stand for three days without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-19.md:245:"I'm asking now, because it's going to annoy you." She tapped the binder on his knee, the shorthand section. "All of this. The galleries, the stair, the rail on the covered walk, the curve. It's beautiful work. It's the best thing you've ever done. And every line of it is the same instrument. Where a man stands, written down by you. You've measured it from a lot of different places, and on different days, and you've had me hold the pencil once or twice while you counted. But two hands on one instrument is still one instrument." She sat back. "You need a second leg under it. Something that doesn't come from where he stands."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-19.md:249:"I know you wrote it down. I'm saying it out loud, so it's true in two places." Karis smiled. "That's what you've been teaching all of us, isn't it? Lira's been at you for a week about it. Say it, don't just file it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:1:# Chapter 14 — Filed
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:3:The panel had been closed. By the fourth bell that afternoon, the whole bluff knew what had happened in it, and none of what the bluff knew was so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:7:*Entry demonstration, assay-provision enrollee: satisfied. Baseline established and filed.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:9:Nine words. They said nothing at all about what he had done. Inside four hours, every one of the eleven stories that had followed him round the bluff since the gate had rushed in to fill that nothing, until a reader could no longer make out the wall behind the slip for the crowd of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:11:He found it oddly hard to be angry with anybody about it. The slip was accurate. As charters went, it was a good one. The people who had filled the space were only doing what people always did with a space, and he had watched them do it at Greyvane, and on the road, and in the stew line on his thirteenth evening here. It was only that he had hoped, without quite admitting it to himself, that a room full of instruments might be the one thing on the bluff a rumour could not reach.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:21:"Held a plate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:41:That night it went into the binder beneath the entry for the plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:63:"He sent five up the bluff this year," said Cael. "Three went to faculty. One's in the library. Karis asked him who had the fifth, and he wouldn't say."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:181:"A while," said Seln, in the stale voice, and looked at his plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:199:By the end of the hour there was the old flat greyness behind his eyes that came from holding a room's attention away from himself. It would be there in the morning. However long he slept, the morning kept it. The Path cost him his own watching; to keep the room from noticing him he had to keep noticing the room, without a pause, and four hours of that wore a man thin. Instruments were beyond it entirely; a pendulum, a counting-board, a ledger had no attention for anyone to steer. And it was useless, entirely and at once, against the rare kind of watcher who went looking for a hole where a man should be instead of the man. He had met two such people in his life. Both had been very good. A provincial academy seemed an unlikely place for a third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:207:He walked the bluff for three days with a satchel of paper on his shoulder, and he never once looked at it the way a visitor looks at a place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:211:In the middle lay the sunk yard, ringed with stone tiers except where the faculty stand took up one whole side. Room for more than eleven hundred. The top tier gave a man the whole floor and half the ways in and out of it, which made it the best place on the bluff to watch from. Everybody on the bluff knew it, so the top tier was where eyes gathered, and a place where eyes gathered was a place where a watcher got noticed, which made it also the worst.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:237:Each door on the bluff had its card, eye-high in a little frame of brass, giving name, Path and tier. Greyvane would never have hung such cards; it had been built on the suspicion that papers lied about people, and you do not fix to a door a figure you suspect. Halcenvane put the number on every door because here the number ran everything, the ladder and the brackets and the rooms and the seeding, and a corridor of cards was simply the ledger with its pages hung up where everybody walked. Cael's own card read *Cael. Assay provision. Evaluation by demonstration.*, in letters squeezed smaller than any other on the corridor so that the words would fit. Lira's read *Wind. Copper.* Brom's read *Iron Skin. Copper.*, and Brom tapped it with a knuckle every night on his way in, as if to remind it he was there. Karis's read *Ember. Iron.* It was the one card she said she would have bet her supper on, and she used it to measure all the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:275:*New TA in Gault's office. Bronze-tier, Shadow Path per the residence card. Doesn't look at me. Note: at Halcenvane, everyone looks at me. Filed.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-24.md:15:He sat back. The river was loud below the window, high with two days of rain. A barge went down it with its lamps swinging, giving the long note and the two short, and somewhere under the bluff another horn answered *heard you*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-24.md:23:*Karis on the stair afterward, when the others had gone up: "An instrument doesn't change sides. It changes readings."*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md:89:There had been a hall at Fenmark with a long table and a woman behind it who had spent about a minute and a half on her, and at the end of the minute and a half had written a word and a number: Copper, Rank One. That was three years ago. Since then Lira had fought on a foundry floor and on the Ironyard's planks and in a formal yard at Greyvane, and in front of eight hundred people here, and she had beaten fighters whose paper said Iron. She had a flag on her own file, put there by somebody who had once looked at her properly, and the flag had gone into a queue, and the queue was where it still was. And every morning at the fifth bell the clerk chalked the same word beside her name as he had chalked the morning she arrived.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md:99:She did not turn round. She did not need to. The third step from the top of that stair had a board that complained under any weight, and every person on the bluff trod on it and made it complain, except one. He stepped over it. He had stepped over it since his first week, without thinking, the way he stepped over everything he had once noticed, and so she had heard the second step and the fourth step and no third. Then nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md:127:A name four places down had sent its challenge up to her for the eleventh session, and the name was one he knew well, because he had been writing it down for most of the season. A Shield Path fifth-year, Copper Rank Six on his card. Bracken's files gave him forty-one wins across five seasons on the Copper sheet. Nobody on the bluff could remember him losing a bout that mattered, and nobody could remember him winning one beautifully either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md:261:Bracken's half-sheet on the top line went up on the board two days later. It was the only piece of paper anybody on the bluff read that week more often than the challenge lists.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-34.md:23:Nobody at the table asked whether he was going. Karis talked about the transitional article of a directive nobody else had read. Brom described exercise thirteen, which involved a door frame and was, he said, the worst so far by a distance he could not express in feet. Lira ate with her eyes on her plate and said almost nothing. When they got up she walked beside him as far as the foot of the residence stair, and stopped there, and looked him over once, top to bottom, as she looked over the standings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-34.md:43:He had come up the lower flight by the count, stepping over the ninth without thinking, and found the corner by touch without having to look for it. That frightened him a little. Three nights, and the stair had gone into him like the bluff road, one more place his feet knew without asking him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-34.md:49:The building made its night noises round him, and on the third night he knew them all. The stone ticked as it gave up the day's last warmth, a small dry sound somewhere in the wall at his back, like a clock that had lost interest in keeping time. High up in the archive a shutter that had not been properly fastened knocked against its frame whenever the wind came round to the east, three or four soft knocks and then silence for a long while. Once, far off across the bluff, a dog barked twice and was told to be quiet. And under all of it, the whole time, came the river, never stopping, so steady that he only heard it when he listened for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-34.md:91:He had not been there. For over an hour Cael had been turning every instrument he owned on the head of that flight, and it had been black and empty. Now there was a man in the doorway, and he was the most ordinary thing in the building: a plain wing coat buttoned to the collar, hands loose at his sides, neither tall nor short. He stayed in the doorway. He did not move at all. Nothing about him had *come*; there had been no coming for any eye to catch. He was simply there, as if he had been standing in that doorway since the stones were laid and the light had only now got round to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-34.md:152:*Bronze.* Every other time, in that place, it had said *unknown*, and he had built a whole small private theory on the word: that whatever wrote these lines could tell what kind of thing it had given him but could not weigh it, and that weighing was some other instrument's business, in some registry, in somebody else's hand. Tonight it had weighed. It had set down a tier as plainly as Bracken's sheet set down *Copper*. Whatever wrote these lines had held the scale all along, it seemed, and had only now decided he was owed a number.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-34.md:200:*First try: total. Nothing, twice. Then the pain behind the eyes, at about what the read charges for six breaths at full stretch. Then the one man on this bluff paid to look into corners looked into mine, and asked how I was. In five months nobody on the fire-watch has so much as looked at me.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:17:In fifteen years he had never seen anybody get anywhere that fast, at that size, in that direction, toward the danger and not away. And then the boy's arm had taken the return the way a stone quay takes a barge coming in too fast: it had not given, and it had not broken, and the force had gone on down through the boy into the boards. Two things, at the least, and the file's classification had a name for neither.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:19:His brief had categories, as every brief did. The second of them was the one the whole posting existed to fill, if it ever needed filling: *observed capability materially in excess of the subject's documented baseline.* Seln had filed under it seven times in fifteen years. Seven times he had been right. Seven people had gone, afterward, into files further up the coast that he had never been shown and had never asked to see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:23:*Hall three (east), north bay, sixth bell, Third-day. Apparatus failure during a supervised floor hour: the retaining pin of a resistance post sheared under load at the seventh setting, locking the core during a Force Path third-year's fourth declaration of his third set and returning the delivery off the pad. Subject was at the bay's edge on an adjoining footwork drill, signed for by the Ash Path instructor, and intervened. The third-year was not struck. No injury requiring the infirmary. The academy's floor warden attended within the quarter-hour, withdrew all four posts on the bluff from use, and has recommended inspection and replacement of every retaining pin in the academy's apparatus; her draft and the wing's log are enclosed. Response consistent with documented baseline.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:33:Gault's office had filed the baseline in the boy's first weeks, and it had been copied into the product soon after. It gave a practitioner of non-standard architecture who executed consistently. It gave a displacement framework, measured to a working ceiling on sprung timber. And it gave a plate trial at the frame's heaviest weight, in which the enrollee had taken the blow on a braced arm and kept his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:47:It lingered on the shear and the setting, the core and the collar, the warden coming within the quarter-hour, every post on the bluff withdrawn, every pin in the academy to be inspected and replaced. It was warm about the institution, and the young man would have nodded at that. Naturally the writer cared about the institution. It was what a man in an assessment wing was best placed to see. And an academy finding a flaw in nine years of its own apparatus was the weather the analysts liked best.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:67:Out by the wing's side door, with his own key. The dark length of the north quadrangle. A nod to the night porter at the lodge, returned without the porter's eyes leaving his newspaper. Then the four long bends of the bluff road, down toward the lamps of the river.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:81:The whole wing was silent. Far below the bluff, the river was going about its business.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:83:He filed the report. Then he sat for a while with the file drawer open, not filing anything else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:119:Karis, when he found her in carrel eleven the next morning, did not wait for him to finish, and by noon she had a box: a plain pasteboard document box from the stationer's in Ostrand. Into it, in order, went every sheet the provision had ever produced about him: the enrollment record, Gault's baseline note, every drill sheet he had built and every countersignature on each, every floor issue, wrong and revised, every supervised hour since his first week on the bluff. Each was copied in her small hand where the original could not leave the wing, and each copy was dated and initialled and cross-referenced to the original's place in the wing's own files.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:127:The incident report itself came up through the wing three days later. By then the warden's account ran to four pages. The wing made two copies: faculty, and file. Cael, who read everything on the bluff with his name in it, asked at the counter for the copy he was entitled to, and read it standing at the end of the passage.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:129:It named the pin, the collar, the setting, the nine years, the inspections passed. Its last paragraph asked for every retaining pin in every apparatus on the bluff to be drawn and replaced. The warden wrote the way she spoke, without one spare word, and he copied two of her sentences into the observation notebook to learn from.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:137:Nobody asked him a single question. Not the warden, not the wing, not Gault. The Ash instructor added a note of three lines to say the enrollee had done well and quickly, and that was true, and it asked nothing. Of all of it, that was what he could not put down. He had spent the whole of his life being asked. On the one afternoon he had done a thing worth asking about, the bluff had written it down in eleven words and turned the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:175:"I'm not calm. I've only got one instrument, and it's slow. Shouting at it won't make it quicker."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:177:On the fifth day the minutes of the faculty's session went up on the board by the theater door, as they always did, and Cael read them because he read everything. Under *apparatus*, Rooke had moved that the floor warden's schedule for replacing every pin on the bluff be adopted in full and at once. The bursar had proposed phasing it over a year. Rooke's ground was minuted in his own words: *the next pin to fail will not wait for the bursar's year*. The motion was carried. Under it, in Bracken's own hand, was a note that the registrar's office would find the money from the reserve within a fortnight and would account for it at the next session.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:217:Eight days after the pin came the fifteenth session, and for a week beforehand there was nobody on the bluff who did not know what it meant.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:239:He had watched Fiske, Force Path, Copper Rank Eight on a certificate two years old, fight four times since Lira's decision, as Lira had wanted him to. Four pages. On every one of them the same thing: a minute and a half of collecting, two bad openings, and then the floor closing behind her opponents like doors in a long corridor, one after another, until there was nowhere left to stand. Fiske never struck anybody. She put her force into the boards where they were about to be, and they arrived on it, and stumbled, and found her hand on them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:5:He had read the boy's slate every morning since his second week on the bluff. It hung by the covered walk's north arch, too high for its keeper, and at the first bell the boy climbed onto an upturned bucket and chalked up the night's reckoning with the tip of his tongue showing at the corner of his mouth. He was perhaps ten. He belonged to the fire-watch, two men who walked the bluff all night with a lantern on a pole, out from the residence quadrangles along the administration range to the assessment wing and back again, noting the hour at which each building put its lamps out. The boy kept their count. At noon a bursar's clerk copied it onto a quarter-sheet and took it away, and at the month's end the bursar and the chandler sat down with the quarter-sheets between them and fought over the oil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:7:Nobody else on the bluff had ever, as far as Cael could see, stopped to read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:9:He had started for a reason that was all his own. In his second week he had been trying to chart where a man might walk at night without being noticed, and a man walks where the dark is. The slate told him, building by building, when the dark began. So every morning he had copied it into the shorthand, *hall three, last bell; lecture range, last bell*, and long after the chart was finished and filed he had gone on reading it, the way you go on glancing at a clock in a room after you have stopped needing the time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:27:Then Cael did a thing he had never done on the bluff before. Under the slate, on its nail, hung yesterday's quarter-sheet, waiting for the bursar's clerk. He lifted it off the nail and folded it into his coat. It was a list of oil. It was almost certainly theft. He did it anyway, and went to find Karis, and found her before he had gone twenty paces, coming the other way down the covered walk from the registrar's door with a sheet of Bracken's paper in her hand and her coat flying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:45:"The enrollment section. These are the file drawers. Bracken went in this morning at first light and looked at them along the line, the way you'd look along a row of teeth. Drawers that have been pulled out and pushed back by somebody who's being careful don't go all the way home. They stop a hair short. You can't see it face on. You can see it from the end of the row." She shaded one square, very neatly. "Every drawer in the row sat flush but this one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:49:"Yours. It's the provision drawer. There aren't a dozen provision files in the whole place, back nineteen years. Nine are closed: people who finished, or left, or died. One belongs to a fourth-year woman, Silver, who's here on a medical provision. And yours." She put the pencil down. "Bracken looked yesterday as well, after the first slate. It sat proud yesterday, too. He'd pushed it home himself. It came out again in the night."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:55:"Through the door. With a key or something as good as one." She tapped the end of her drawn room. "The inner door has a counting lock. The Compact makes every chartered records office on the continent fit one. Each time the bolt goes across, it knocks a little brass plate inside the case, and the registrar reads the plate. Bracken reads his every Fifth-day, never oftener, because a registrar seen reading his lock plate on an odd day is a registrar who's telling the building he expects a thief. He read it yesterday anyway. Two marks. This morning, four." She held up four fingers, then folded two of them down, then the other two. "In and out. In and out. Two nights."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:59:"Nothing." She said it with the small extra weight she put on words she meant to build on. "Not a sheet, Cael. Nothing torn, nothing moved, nothing missing. Somebody came in twice, read your file, put it back, and left. And Bracken would never have known, if his drawer hadn't sat a hair proud and his building hadn't burned two extra gills of oil."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:61:He looked at the shaded square and thought about it from the reader's side, which was the only side that ever told him anything. A thief took a file and left a hole that somebody would one day find. A reader took nothing. He went away with the whole of it in his head or his bag, and the file stayed in its drawer looking as innocent as ever, and nobody knew that it had been had. Somebody had paid for the second thing, not the first. Somebody wanted to know what was in that drawer and wanted nobody to know they knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:71:"I built that file," she said. "Every sheet in it. Bracken's chain, the clause, the citations, the transcript extracts, the cross-references to the founding schedule. Eleven weeks of my life are in that drawer, and somebody's been sitting in the dark reading them by a shuttered lamp and I don't even know whether they understood the footnotes." She picked the pencil up again and put it down again. "I'd like to know what they thought of it. Isn't that ridiculous? Somebody's robbing you and I want their review."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:101:He sat there with his back to the wall for a quarter of an hour, in daylight, learning what the courtyard sounded like: a cart on the bluff road, the refectory's pans, the chapel clock on the lecture range a little behind the bells. At night most of that would be gone, and what was left would be what mattered. He wanted to know what had been taken away when the time came, so that he could hear what remained.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:185:*Going out tonight, to watch the records hall. Observation only. I will not interfere with whoever is reading the provision files, and I will not try to stop them, and I will not go inside the building. If anybody on the staff finds me there at that hour, it costs me the enrollment. That record is the one true thing ever set down about me by anybody.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:207:"Because somebody is reading my file in the dark," he said. "Somebody who isn't the Compact, or isn't the Compact's normal way, because the Compact can read my file whenever it likes; it's got a man in the wing with a key. Somebody's money pulled that drawer out at the fourth hour. And Bracken's copy lay on his desk at the ninth bell yesterday. He knows. Nobody but Karis knows that drawer better." He stopped. "I want to see what he does about it. I want to see it with my own eyes, once, instead of reading it afterward in an incident record in eleven words. That's the want. I've written it down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:9:There were four resistance posts on the bluff, two in the north bay of the east hall and two in the north hall, and the assessment wing kept their ledgers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:11:Each was a stout padded column standing about as high as a man's breastbone, set into an iron floor plate bolted through the oak to the joists beneath. Inside the padding, on a spindle, sat a heavy iron core.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:13:Behind the core sat a drag, set by a wheel with nine notches. At the first a child could have turned it. At the ninth no Copper practitioner on the bluff had ever made the dial move more than a finger's width. A Force practitioner declared into the pad; the core took what was delivered and spun with it, and the drag ate the spin; and on the far side of the column a needle swung round a brass face and settled on a number. The number went into the ledger at the wall table. Over a term the numbers made a line, and the line showed a Force fighter's delivery climbing or sagging in a way no instructor's eye could have measured.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:21:Each post was meant to be that something. Bolted to every plinth, in letters a quarter of an inch high, was a brass plate saying what happened if they ever failed to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:93:Jask sat down on the floor plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:109:She did not ask Jask anything at first. She took his right hand in both of hers and said, "Squeeze." He squeezed. "The other." He squeezed with the other. "Look at my finger." She moved it slowly left and right in front of his face, and his eyes followed it, and some of the stiffness went out of her back. Then, "Tell me today's date," and he told her, a day wrong, and she nodded as if a day wrong was about right for a man who had just sat down on a floor plate, and only then asked whether anything hurt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:113:One second-year was off toward the warden's shed before the instructor had finished the word *warden*. The porter, sent to the wing for the duty clerk, went with his broom still on his shoulder. She moved everybody back out of the north bay with a single flat word, and nobody touched the post. One of the second-years, coming back, stood in front of it and read the brass plate aloud, every word, in a voice that was not steady, as though reading it might still help. Nobody had the heart to stop her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:175:That night he could not write properly. His left hand would not keep the paper still and his right hand had picked up a shake from somewhere, so he did a thing he had not done since the spider-map of the bluff: he drew before he wrote.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:211:*And I'll watch the coats. Two at the ferry, two at the road's foot, every change of every four hours. They're the one instrument I've got that reads his people from outside. If the coats don't change, nothing came down the road. Any change, four days should show it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-31.md:7:He read it from the observation notebook, word for word, because he had written it word for word on the walk back from the wing before it could soften in his head. He gave them the sheet and the chained pen, and *Your training requirement will take coursework*, and Gault and the unit and the back half of term, the room in the wing and Rooke's boards; then *Who takes it?* and *I do*, and the file sentence, and his own three words, and the board where his name would have to go up among everybody else's. He read the half-second at the door as well, since to skip it would have made the evening's first lie. Then he turned the page and read them the other entry, the one about his feet in hall three, and what he wanted, in his own hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-31.md:19:"Right. The other side." She looked at the ceiling. "He's offering it in public, on a board, with Gault's name on the syllabus and every second-year who wants to be an assessor in the same room. You can't be quietly anything in a room full of second-years. If he wanted you on your own, that's the last place he'd put you." She stopped. "And you'd learn something from the only man on the bluff who could teach it, which isn't nothing. I'd have walked a long way at your age for a teacher like that." She looked down at Cael. "I still think no. I just can't make it as big as I'd like it to be."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-31.md:33:"Position: it breaks nothing in the minute, because you didn't arrange it and you didn't go near him. The offer came over a counter, by the route any clerk would use, and the right it rests on is printed in your own file. Every rule you wrote is standing." She paused. "Now why I don't trust that. I want it. Not on your account; on mine. I've wanted to sit in a room with a working Shadow practitioner at a table's length since the first page you wrote about him, and I want it the way I want a missing volume, which is badly and for my own sake. And I caught myself, about halfway through you reading it out, building the case for yes. I'd got two good reasons laid out before I noticed the want they were standing on." She turned one hand over. "So whatever I say tonight, mark it down. Mark it down a long way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-31.md:93:"An instrument doesn't change sides. It changes readings." She said it the way she must have said it to herself a thousand times, flatly, as a thing she had built her working life on. "I told you that before the season. It was a warning then, and it's been the first article of my trade for as long as I've had one." She squared nothing in particular on the table. With Karis, that stood in for a sigh. "Eleven days isn't a finding. I know that better than anybody in this room. But this is the first thing I've ever put in a notebook that I can't make go into that sentence, turn it how I like. I hate that. I'd rather tell you tonight, while it's still sticking out, than after I've sanded it down to fit."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-31.md:185:*He watched me stop Iron-tier force with a Copper baseline and his desk stayed quiet. Eleven days of the same two coats at the landing say so. This morning he offered, at a counter, to teach me his own Path across a table. Either it's the best trap the Compact ever built, or the man they sent to watch me has started watching them. Karis says instruments don't change sides. Karis has never met this instrument.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-31.md:225:"She's drawn an eye," said Brom, reading it over his shoulder with a plate in one hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-31.md:271:"I know. I read it on your face at supper." She paused in the wash-house door with the lamp held up and the light running along the three hanging sandbags. "It's a good unit, Cael, whatever else it is. Observation, position, presence. Somebody ought to teach it properly, and nobody on this bluff ever has." She considered. "Mark that down a long way too."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-33.md:21:He had not brought anybody here. A man with a copying kit had brought himself, twice, on somebody's money, and the slate had told the bluff about it in chalk. He had not arranged the hour, or the door, or the drawer that sat proud. He had not told anybody in the wing he was coming, or let anything be seen that would draw the man out. He was sitting in the cold to watch a thing happen that was going to happen whether he sat here or not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-33.md:39:At every doorway the lantern reached, the boy's head turned toward it: a window, a drain, a door, a cat that shot out across the light and was gone. The boy looked at all of it. The younger man looked at about half. The older man's head did not turn once that Cael saw. Thirty years on the same round had taught him which doorways on this bluff were worth a turn of the head, and on this range there were none.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-33.md:87:*I measured this Shadow declaration in the covered walk weeks ago, at the rail, standing in daylight: a Bronze-tier man paying a tax on every look. It isn't hiding. People still look. But the look lands, finds nothing worth a second one, and lets go. Tonight I saw it working on the two men on this bluff whose whole job is to look into doorways at night, and it worked better than I've ever seen it work, at a rate I wouldn't have believed from the rail.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-33.md:115:Empty buildings were bad for anyone with a drawer to read in the dark. That had surprised him when he first saw it, and then it had not surprised him at all. Where nothing moves, one footstep is an event. One door opening at the fourth hour, with nothing else moving anywhere, would be the loudest thing on the bluff to anybody lying awake. What a man like that wanted was a building with a little life in it still: enough coming and going that one more door was nothing worth lifting a head for, and not so much that anybody was standing about in the passage when it opened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-33.md:175:He had made his argument. It was made by sitting where he was sitting, at the hour he was sitting there, in the one place on the bluff that a person who had understood the third hour would choose. There was nothing else to say. He let his eyes rest on the window's grey, and listened to the building, and to the river, which was quieter in here, through stone, and to his own breathing, which he did not count.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-35.md:3:"Bracken's clerk came and banged on my door at the third hour," said Karis, before she had quite reached him, "in his nightcap, which I shall never be able to stop seeing. That file is half mine. I built every sheet in it. So I've been in the records hall for most of an hour with my coat over my nightgown and my bare feet in my shoes, and I'd be grateful if you walked me home, because I can no longer feel them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-35.md:81:*Sixth fragment. Bronze — the notice finally said a tier out loud, like the architecture decided I'd earned a number. Acquired from a man who doesn't know, in defense of a file with my name on it, during an operation he let me watch. I drew the line in advance and I stayed on my side of it. It cost anyway. Write that down too.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-35.md:85:*The price, in order. Three seconds, maybe four, of the very thing I'd sat three nights in the cold to see, gone while it happened above me. That's the dearest thing I spend, and I spent it without being asked. Then the pain behind the eyes, at once and heavy. Then two dead tries in four minutes, and a fire-watch man asking if I was all right. Last, in no column yet, what I owe the one man on this bluff who has never once sent me a bill.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-35.md:91:*I don't know what that means. Filed.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-35.md:95:At the fourth bell the deferral went into the wing in his own hand, on the wing's own form: *training deferral requested, eight days, on grounds of accumulated fatigue.* It was true: seven hours' sleep in three nights, and anybody could have signed for it from his face. The desk clerk read it, and looked at him over the top of it with real kindness, and told him he looked dreadful and should go to bed. Then she initialled it, and stamped it, and dropped it in Gault's tray. By noon it was back on the residence board with Gault's initial and the purple ring of the stamp, approved, as the wing approved four such forms a week. It was the only mark the whole business would ever leave in any office on the bluff, and it said, correctly, that a boy was tired.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-35.md:171:"Mine, then," she said. "Write it in. Nothing at full until there've been three clean days in a row. Not with me, not with Brom, and not for any office on this bluff, whatever they write on a form."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-35.md:247:The chart stayed in carrel eleven, under an inkpot. The notebook stayed in his coat. The Log stayed where the Log had always lived. Gault's brass and dials would read what was put before them and report it honestly. The assay file would keep its three words and add none. Nobody's coursework, no supervision note, no reason written on a deferral would ever carry so much as a hint of a sixth thing. At the semester's measuring he meant to stand before the panel and show five things, the five the record already knew, and every one of them true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-17.md:33:"I said it was a bad bluff," he said. "I didn't know it was a bad bluff with somebody standing in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-17.md:41:"The assistant in Gault's office is a watcher. He's good. He's very good. Shadow Path, Bronze tier, by the classification on his own card, and I believe the card. He's been on the bluff about four weeks. He has never once looked at me, because looking is the one thing that would give him away, and he stands every day where he can see everything I do without being seen to see it." He paused. "I can't prove whose he is. I don't know of anybody on this continent who'd pay a Bronze to copy forms in an assessment wing, except the people who already pay for those coats at the ferry."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-17.md:97:She took her hand back. "Tell Withrow, and by the end of the week he's gone. And then, inside a month, somebody else is in that chair, or another chair, or a room in Ostrand with a window on the bridge. Somebody we haven't found. Somebody who's been told in detail how the last man was caught, and who will not be caught that way, because that's what being told is for." She looked at Lira properly. "We'd be trading an instrument we can read for one we can't even find. I'm not saying I like it. I'm saying it's arithmetic."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-36.md:27:He had a column for this. He had kept it all season, since the first week, in the back of the observation notebook: how many people on the bluff looked at him, and where, and how hard. The fame tally, Karis called it. It had been one in three in the passages, the whole of the first month, and it had gone down a little as the bluff got used to him, and it had never once in all those weeks gone to nothing. On a stair, at a pace and a half, in full afternoon light, a man coming round a turn should have seen him as surely as he saw the rail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-36.md:45:Three in three days. Since the bridge, he had been looked at by every soul on this bluff. He had measured it, in passages and on stairs and at counters, to the second decimal, because it was the weather he lived in, and he had kept a column of it and filed it in the back of the notebook. Three times in three days, three different people had failed to see him from a yard away, in good light, while he stood in front of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-36.md:115:People looked at the ladder. He could feel it: eyes coming up to the long swinging shape and following it, judging whether it would clip a head, making sure its foot was clear of their own. A Current boy stepped aside for it with a little bow. Two girls ducked under its end and laughed. Not one of them, as far as he could tell, so much as glanced at the face of the person underneath it. He walked forty paces across the busiest yard on the bluff in full afternoon light, and for the first time since the bridge he was not being looked at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-36.md:119:Since the stair he had been trying to make himself less, to turn the hand down on purpose, and it had come to nothing. Here, without trying anything at all, forty people had looked past him, because he had been carrying something more interesting than himself. Nothing at his edges had stirred the whole way across; the fragment had done nothing, and this was only the plain trick every porter on the bluff lived by, ordinary attention going to the more interesting thing. That was not a Path. It was a ladder. But it was the same shape, he thought, standing in the arch with the yard going on round him. You did not have to be nothing. You only had to give the eyes somewhere better to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-36.md:131:Brom had offered to be the instrument before Cael finished asking. Then he improved on his own offer, as he always did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-36.md:203:"Then once upon a time he couldn't either," he said, through his teeth. "A year of it, I'd guess. Before he was a man in a doorway, he was somebody like you on a walk, glowing." He let the shift go. "You always do this. You find somebody's finished thing and you stand next to it and measure yourself and come up short and write it down like news. You never once did it to me. When you found my seam, you didn't write me off. You went and found out the bluff had a Rooke."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-36.md:272:*Filed under debts, no current mechanism of payment,* he wrote under the columns. Then he sat looking at it. Cold and correct, and he would not let it be the page's last line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-30.md:3:The conclusion had come to Seln on the bluff road, on the ninth night after the pin, somewhere on the third bend going up. He had just watched the courier's clerk drop his third report of the fortnight into the canvas sack by the fish steps, and he was climbing home in the dark with nothing in his satchel at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-30.md:11:First step: the service depended not on its officers being honest but on its files being complete, because officers were posted, promoted and buried while the file outlived them all, and a gap in it was therefore a slow trap. One day a reader with better access would come at that stretch from another side, see one man's product go quiet just where things got interesting, and ask him why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-30.md:59:One was from his first month on the bluff. The rest were the three he had walked to the fish steps since the pin. He laid them in a row on the floorboards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-30.md:77:He had filed them anyway. He knew that as a fact, and it was true, and he went on. Then he turned the lamp out and slept without trouble until the second bell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-30.md:87:He had promised himself a change would show in four if it was going to. It had not shown in four. It had not shown in eight. At the foot of the bluff road it had been the same, on the same bell. One of the coats coming on there walked with a stiff left knee that Cael remembered from the morning of the pin, so even the roster was unchanged. There had been no new face in the residence quadrangle and no extra pair on the covered walk. Bracken would have heard of any visitor to Withrow's door by supper and told Karis by breakfast, and Bracken had heard of none.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-30.md:149:"Your training requirement will take coursework," he said, to the sheet, in the level voice the counter used for everything at that hour. "Under the clause. Supervised coursework counts as evaluable contact. It's written in your file. I processed it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-30.md:167:He said it to the sheet. "Your file says you watch things well. The course would tell us if the file's right."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-30.md:169:The wing's carbon sets came in threes, held at the corner with a pin: a top sheet for whoever asked, a second for the wing's own drawer, and a third, faint and grey, for the file that went away. Cael had handled a hundred of them. He read the sentence the same way, top to bottom, in about two seconds, before the clerk's newspaper turned again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-30.md:175:The third sheet was the faint one, and it was the one that mattered. Seln knew precisely what eight feet of him was worth; nobody alive could have priced it better. He had spent fifteen years making himself a man no one could get near. Now, in the dullest hour of the day, he had drafted a lawful pass into his own range, made out to the single person on the bluff who could find him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-30.md:197:A thing that was going to be decided was read to all three at once, with the door shut, before anything was done about it. Breakfast was a long table and sixty people. So he ate, and listened to Brom describe exercise nine in a voice of deep injury, and said nothing. Karis looked at him once across the bread and then down at her plate, and he saw her decide not to ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-30.md:225:They stood along the north wall where they had always stood, four iron columns with their padded heads, and the new retaining pins shone in the collars like fresh nails in an old fence. The chains were gone. The floor warden had fitted every pin herself over two long evenings, so the Ash instructor told him at the bay's edge, and had made each Force third-year who used the posts that week watch her do it, and then made them each say the plate aloud before she let them near a dial.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-30.md:249:*Before I tell them, the want, in my own hand, so it can't come back later in a hat: I want that room. I want it more than I've wanted anything on this bluff since the gate list. My feet went looking for the place a man by the door would see last, in hall three, in the middle of a set, without asking me. That is what wanting it looks like from the outside, and I would rather the three of them heard it from me than worked it out from where I stand.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md:103:That was the rule he had made for the week, and it was a rule about sameness, not about bouts. Every ladder session of the season he had sat a third of the way up the east tier, in the same place, from before the first bout until after the last. If he stopped now, with eleven strangers on the bluff, his absence would be the first new thing anyone had seen him do in a month.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-40.md:61:The bluff's temperature went up for three days, and it went up in small ugly ways, as the temperature of a place does when it is frightened and cannot say so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-40.md:79:"No," he said. "Not by a delegation and not by the chancellor. An enrollment stands until a finding is filed against it and answered in the proper forum, and then it stands until that forum rules. That is the law, and you may cite me." He made a small note on a slip, which might have been anything. "Is there something else you'd like to ask me?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-40.md:95:Cael had lived with it since the bridge. Every person on the bluff had come up the road already carrying some version of him: the boy from the transcript, the boy who had beaten the registry, the boy whose people had given him up. Not one of those versions had ever been tested against anything that mattered. Now something that mattered was coming up the road in carriages, and all over the bluff the versions were being taken out and turned over in people's hands, to see whether they would hold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-40.md:99:Gwen, alone of everybody on the bluff, took it at face value.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-40.md:153:The hall had benches for six hundred, and Cael guessed five hundred in it: faculty in the front rows, then every year of enrollee in rising tiers behind them, more people than he had ever seen in one room on the bluff. He stood at the back by the east door with Lira and Brom, where the latecomers stood, and he had decided beforehand not to read the room. A hall of five hundred was the kind of crowd that pulled all of him outward into the reading of it, and he had a hold to keep. So he gave himself one thing to watch, and chose the clock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-39.md:15:"You write a very fair hand," he said. "And there is nothing in that room I can let you touch. Not one sheet." He folded the rag. "Every document on that table is about you. If a delegation counsel ever finds that the enrollee carried his own file, or copied a line of it, she will spend a whole morning asking what else he carried, and she'll be right to. Your hand on any of it makes all of it weaker."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-39.md:19:"Be dull," said Bracken. "Go to your lectures. Train in your hours and sign for them. Hand your work in on the day it's due, and not a day early, because early is interesting. Don't put your name to a single sheet the calendar didn't ask for." He leaned his forearms on the counter. "Strong files are made of dull months, Enrollee. Give me a dull month."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-39.md:31:"An argument persuades a person. A file survives a person leaving the room." He said it plainly, as he might have said what bell it was. "Miss Karis persuaded me. My counsel persuaded the chancellor. Both of those were people. In a week there will be a room full of strangers on this bluff, none of whom were persuaded of anything, and some of whom would rather not be. A file has to work on them. It has to work if Miss Karis is in her bed with a fever and I'm at the bottom of the river. That's what's being built in there." He nodded at the door. "Not a better argument. Something that stands up with nobody holding it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-39.md:39:Her chain of proof ran through thirty-odd volumes, from the founding schedule that held the clause to the charter article that took it into Halcenvane. At Greyvane she had built it out of her own transcriptions, careful ones, in her small upright hand, and Bracken would not use a single line of them. For every link in the chain a clerk went to the actual volume, in whatever library held it, and copied the passage whole. Then the librarian who kept that volume read the copy against the page, word by word, and sealed the foot of it. Where the volume was not on the bluff, a letter went down the river by the first coach to the house that held it, asking for a copy under that house's seal. A dated note of the asking went into the box in its place, so that even the gap was on paper. That protected against the first thing a hostile reader would say, which was that the whole chain rested on one girl's handwriting. By the end of the third day there was not a word of Karis's handwriting anywhere in it. She had stood at the table and watched the last of it go, and Cael had watched her face, and it had not looked like loss.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-39.md:53:The third decision was about the standardization directive, all forty-one pages of the order that had swept away the old terminology. It protected against a reader who knew only the four pages everybody quoted, and would say that a repeal might be hiding in the rest. So the whole of it went into the file, all forty-one pages, copied and sealed. The four passages where the old words were replaced by new ones were tabbed with green paper. At every place in the text where a clause repealing the assay provision would have had to stand, if one existed, a narrow white slip was pinned with a line in Bracken's hand saying that nothing stood there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-39.md:57:It was a numbered list of every sheet in the file. Beside each number stood one line, never more, saying what that sheet proved and nothing else. There were no adjectives anywhere in it, and nothing that argued. Cael read the first page of it over the counter, upside down. With it, a delegation counsel with a sore back and no patience could find the sheet that carried the whole matter inside a minute and a half. She would not have to ask anybody on the bluff for anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-39.md:59:On the fifth afternoon Karis tested it. She did not ask anyone's leave. She simply took the clerk with the cold, who had never seen the file's contents and had only searched for the absence of one thing, and sat him down at the long table in front of the first box with the index on top. Then she turned over the little sand-glass she used for timing her own reading. "The question," she said, "is whether this enrollment rests on a clause that has been repealed. Find me the sheet that answers it." The clerk ran his finger down the index, stopped, turned to the number, lifted out a sealed copy of a schedule with a white slip pinned to its margin, and laid it in front of her. Karis looked at the glass. A little under half of it had run. She turned it back over and wrote the figure in her notebook, and did not say anything at all, and Cael, from the doorway with his hands in his pockets, thought it was the most satisfied silence he had ever heard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-39.md:63:*How the registrar fights: he takes everybody who built the file out of it, me included, and builds for whoever walks in tired.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-39.md:83:"Same nothing." She let out a long breath at the ceiling. "Do you know what that file is now? It isn't mine. That's the whole point of it. At Greyvane it was mine. I'd dug it up, and people believed it partly because I was standing there ready to answer for it. Take me away and you took half the reason they believed. Bracken's spent four days taking me out of it. One link at a time. Every place my hand was, there's a seal now, and a librarian's name, and a clerk who never met me." She opened her eyes. "I could fall off the bluff tomorrow and the enrollment would hold without me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-39.md:93:"Of course he wouldn't." Karis sounded faintly scandalised. "You're the subject. The subject doesn't carry his own file. That's the first thing anybody learns." Then, more gently, still with her eyes shut: "You're doing the other half. The dull half. You'll have done the harder one, if you manage it. I couldn't be dull for fifteen days if you paid me. I'd find something."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-39.md:155:It was not a thin file. A thin file is a shape; it tells you that someone was there and did little. This had no shape at all. No panel had ever been printed with him on it. He had signed nothing the library owned: not a finding, not an opinion, not so much as a note on procedure or a circular to the house.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-43.md:7:It had sounded like kindness, and Cael had carried it about for two days as kindness. It was only at breakfast, watching Brom eat porridge with the steady attention of a man stoking an engine, that he turned it the other way up and saw the shape underneath. If Cael sat somewhere else, Brom would look up at the usual place before the word and not find him there, and then he would look for him. He would look for perhaps two breaths, perhaps four, and every one of those breaths would be a breath in which Brom was thinking about the tier instead of the boards. Merrick had not offered advice. Merrick had offered a feint, and offered it to the one person on the bluff who would carry it straight to Brom without knowing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-43.md:19:So he sat where he always sat, a third of the way up the east tier, with Karis on his right and the space on his left empty, because Brom was on the boards today and not beside him. Lira had refused to stay in bed. She sat on the bottom step of the tier, below them, with the leading leg straight out in front of her along the stone and a folded coat under the knee, and nobody on the bluff was foolish enough to say anything about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-43.md:57:It was the stall. It was the old two-year stall, the gap between taking and going that every good fighter on the bluff had found in him, the gap Rooke had spent a season and nineteen exercises shrinking until Cael could barely see it from the gallery. Brom had found a use for it. He let the stall run exactly as long as Merrick's tell, and then went through the place where the pane was about to be, before it was. The pane came up a moment later, flat and bright and perfectly placed, behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-43.md:219:"Five." He said them as she wrote. "The Wind. The Pressure. The Iron read. The Compression. The Ember. Not all on the same paper, though." He made himself go through it sheet by sheet, the way she would have. "The transcript anybody can buy holds two of them, the Wind and the read, and that one thin Ember exhibit at the back that nobody ever reads. Gault's baseline holds the Wind numbers and the read, and the plate, where I held the Pressure back, so that line's flat, and a Compression line he ringed because the floor never gave him a fair test of it. And Vell copied the circuit record at Ardenmere. Every bout I fought is in it, the Pressure I used in public and the Compression that finished Reydan."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:3:On the tenth of Reaping, the morning after the file was closed, the woman who kept the wing's instruments came into the wing's outer office with her oil can and her brass rag at the third bell, as she did on the posted day of every month, and checked the instruments.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:9:That was how the days went, from the closing of the file on the ninth to the eve of the twelfth. Cael watched them the way he watched a bout from the tier, without blinking at the wrong moment.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:27:Havel rode up the bluff road in the second carriage, facing backward, with the counsel's knees an inch from his own and the counsel talking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:33:The carriage leaned into the first bend of the bluff road, and Havel looked out of the window at the river falling away below.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:35:He did it properly. He had been trained to arrive at a place properly, and he did it now out of a habit so old it no longer felt like a decision. First the road behind them: the bridge with its toll board, the town, the ferry landing on the near bank with its lamp post and two men in road-grey coats standing under it. He noted the two men, his own service's coats, briefly and without staring, and looked away again, because a records officer who stares at a rotation from a carriage window is a records officer who will be asked by somebody, later, why. Then the road ahead: the bends, the wall, the gate. Then the slope of the hill itself, the roofs above the wall, the long range of grey stone along the top of the bluff with its tiers of seating, open to the sky, standing up against the morning like the side of a ship.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:57:Some things on the boy's file stood above his clearance, and he did not think about those in carriages. He had a notebook with a cardboard cover in his inside pocket, and in it, among dates that meant nothing, there were three short entries in pencil that he had written over two years and did not read in front of anyone. He did not take it out now, since there was nothing to add to it this morning. There might never be. A careful man kept a notebook like that for the reason he kept a spare button sewn inside his coat: not because he expected to need it, but because when you needed it, there would be no time to sew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:95:In a good many years of arriving at institutions he had met a great many registrars, and nearly all of them had told him first, at the door, how difficult the records were to find. He had never before been told at the door where a file was and how many boxes it filled, by a man who seemed to think that was the ordinary thing to say. It was the manner, he thought, of somebody who had built a thing for strangers and was quite content to let strangers have it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:99:Inside, under the stone hood, the hall was cool and dim and smelled of old paper and lamp oil, and his footsteps went ahead of him along the flags. He let himself think, once, about the high tier above the courtyard behind him, and the east end of it with the sun on it, and who was standing there. He did not turn round. There would be days on this bluff for looking. This was not one of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:105:He did it at the rail of the Crown yard's upper tier, at the east end, with the morning sun warm on the back of his neck and the whole of the bluff road's last bends laid out below him like a ribbon dropped on a hill. He had written it at the head of the plan the night before, ruled off from everything under it: *Hold. First.* So before he looked at anything, before he let himself begin to read, he took one small hard corner of his attention and gave it to the half-yard of air round his own body, and told it to stay there, as a man sets a stake in a field before he lets the horses out. The rest of him could go where it liked. That corner was not for reading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:115:Karis herself was down in the records hall with Bracken. She had said long ago that she would sooner read what a delegation wrote than look at what it wore, and nothing on the bluff could have shifted her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:147:Lira thought about that, and did not ask him to explain it. He was glad, because he was not sure he could have. He only knew that the upper tier was the one place on the bluff from which a person could see the whole courtyard without being in it. A careful man who had once refused a pear would have worked that out on the road. And a man who works out where you will be, and then keeps his eyes off it, has decided something. He did not know what, and he wrote nothing down about it. Some things you do not put in a notebook until you know what column they go in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:229:Then he sat a while longer by the low lamp. Below the bluff the river went on, and at the fourth hour the two grey coats at the ferry landing changed under their lamp, on the bell, as they had changed every night since the bridge. The delegation was inside. Somewhere in the long building under the stone hood, a small woman who read everything to the end was reading. The final was in seven days, the evaluation in eight. And the one man on the bluff he could not read had looked at him for exactly as long as he had looked at an empty bench, and gone indoors.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-26.md:115:Brom had opened the sheet. Cael, from his end of the bench, could see close columns in Rooke's narrow writing and a list of numbers down the left side, and at the bottom, alone, a date. He knew the date, as everybody on the bluff knew the date.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-48.md:5:The counter was the plainest place on the bluff. It was a long scrubbed board with a brass rail along the front, a chained pen, a pot of sand, and a slot for slips. Behind it the desk clerk sat on his high stool with the wing's ledger open. Behind him, at the copying table under the window, the teaching assistant, Seln, was writing out somebody's timetable in the round copying hand. He did not raise his head when the door opened. He never did. Cael had a whole term of that, on a page that said almost nothing, and the page had not needed changing all week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-48.md:51:"The weighbeam." She pulled the strap again, gently. "At the foundry, before every pour, the master had them prove the beam. There was a set of brass weights in a box with a lock on it, and only he had the key, and before anybody put a single ingot on the beam he'd hang the brass on it and see what it said. My second year there, a beam had been filed. Just a little, on the pivot. Some man had been selling us short for months, and nobody knew, because everybody trusted the beam." She shifted the leg and winced and went on. "After that the master wouldn't believe any number at all until he'd watched the beam proved. Not even his own."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-48.md:55:He thought of every hour he had worked under the wing that term, and of every figure the quiet woman had sent across to the registrar in her neat hand on the same afternoon, and each one of those figures had been made on some post at some setting. He thought of his baseline in the second month, the entry demonstration that had set the measure for his whole provision, taken against plates with their dates of calibration painted on the housings. He thought of the twentieth, when Gault's panel would lay his new numbers beside his old ones and say whether he had grown. And he thought of the posts in hall three, every one of them re-pinned this term after a pin had sheared in the north bay at the seventh setting, with Cael's own forearm the first thing it hit. Was a post with a new pin the same post? Did it read the same? Who said so?
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-48.md:57:The instrument woman said so. A woman with an oil can, who came on the posted day of every month and initialled a yellowed card by Gault's door that nobody had ever told her to keep. And this morning she had stood outside her own shut store, not on her posted day, to make sure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-48.md:155:Gault sat in the very last chair on the academy's side, by the door, and he had brought nothing. There was no folder in front of him, no sheet, not so much as a pencil. Cael read that at once. It was a man saying with his chair what he would not trouble to say aloud: that the wing had nothing riding on the argument about paper, and would answer for its instruments and nothing else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-48.md:185:"Two copies, certified," said Bracken. "One taken from this library's own holding. The other came up from a house on the coast, four hundred miles off, under that house's seal, in answer to a letter from this office. The letter's dated, and it's in the box. The two librarians who certified them have never written to each other in their lives. Then I gave both copies to a clerk who had never read either, and had him go through them side by side, line against line. What he found is on his sheet, which is document fourteen. Three places where they differ. All three are spelling. None of the three is in the clause."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-48.md:205:That was the second time. The first had been ten minutes before, while Bracken described how the two copies had been compared: by a clerk who had read neither, line against line. Not when the second certified copy came out of the box, which was the stronger point. Not at the long silence while the counsel read. Only at how the comparing was done. And now, only at the counsel saying the index was sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-48.md:231:"Is a possibility," said Withrow. "Which is why the file answers it. Document sixty-two, please."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:17:She heard him and turned her head, and looked at him with the level, unhurried attention she gave the boards before a bout. Then she nodded, once, as one person on the bluff who got up too early nods to another, and turned back to the board. He nodded back, and went on to breakfast, and did not write it down, because there are things a person does at a board at dawn that are nobody else's business.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:31:On this day of the year, the bluff had two lectures and a coursework deadline and nothing else at all. Nobody had marked this date in front of him since he left Fen Street, and somewhere along the road he had stopped being the kind of person who noticed. On the morning of his fifteenth he had written the date at the top of a page and gone on to the next line, and it had seemed to him then the right and grown-up thing to do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:39:The long table had been cleared to the wood. Four places were laid at the far end of it, close together, with the residence's chipped blue plates and four cups that very nearly matched. And in the middle of the table, lit, its glass chimney polished until it shone, stood the good lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:63:He sat at the end of the table with his coat still half on and his hands flat on the wood either side of an empty blue plate, and found that he could not say anything at all. It was not that he had nothing to say. It was that everything he had to say had arrived at once, at the door of his mouth, and stuck there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:77:Brom set the basket on the table and took the cloth off it with a little flourish and began to lay out what was inside on the blue plates, one thing at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:79:A round loaf came first, its crust still faintly warm, from no oven Cael had ever seen on the bluff. Then a crock of butter, and a wedge of hard pale cheese with a black rind, and a cold game pie whose glazed lid somebody had decorated with a single pastry leaf. Then a jar of pickled walnuts. Karis, coming in at that moment with her grey notebook under her arm, looked at the jar and said that pickled walnuts tasted exactly like ink, and then sat down beside it and ate four. Last, from the very bottom of the basket, wrapped one by one in paper, came six pears, golden and freckled, of a kind Cael had not seen anywhere on the bluff since the bridge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:133:"My sister had been looking at that fork all the way through dinner. And when her pear came, she put the fork down on the cloth, very neatly, right beside her plate, where everybody could see it. And she picked the pear up in her hand. Like this. And she bit it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:155:It was steel, and it was an instrument-maker's pen, not a stationer's. The barrel was plain and round, with no ornament on it anywhere. When Cael lifted it out and laid it across his fingers he found that the balance had been set a little forward of the middle, so that it settled down into the curl of his hand of its own weight, as though it had been made for that particular hand. It had. The nib had been ground to a fine, even line, and a spare nib lay in a slot cut into the underside of the lid. Near the point, just where the fingers held it, a narrow band of the steel had been left unpolished, so that in the dark the fingers would always know where to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:193:He thought about where he had been at the start of this half-year: across a desk from Bracken at intake, while the registrar took ninety-four minutes over his papers and then wrote, at the end of it, the first true sentence anybody had ever written about him. Since then Lira had climbed a ladder that had been built for somebody else, Copper Rank Two on a certificate that would have put her at Iron in any honest registry, and stood on its top line. Brom had been taken apart on purpose, and had come out the far side of nineteen dull exercises and gone round again from the first. Karis had found a clause, and a chart, and a way of saying *I don't know* that was harder to argue with than most people's certainty. And a quiet man at a copying table had stood in a doorway in the light and spent everything he had, and never once looked down a flight of stairs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:197:It ran better than any pen he had ever held, and he set that down first, since it was true, and Hesk would want to hear it. *Balance forward of the middle. The hand doesn't have to hold it; it holds itself. I could write till morning.* Then he thought of Hesk at the bench, with a file and a lamp and a scrap of steel, setting the balance for a hand he had not seen in a long while, from memory, and getting it right. He wrote that down too: *He set it for my hand without my hand there.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-42.md:27:"The rest," he said, "is that neither of us is needed now. Miss Karis found the clause. I filed it. Neither of those things carries any weight now. The shelf carries it." He straightened the four boxes again, though they had not moved. "That's what a defense is, Enrollee, when it's finished. It isn't a speech. It's a thing that goes on being true in a room where nobody who built it is standing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-42.md:33:He had been fourteen in the Denvash certification hall. A clerk behind a long counter there had written a word about him on a form, in about the time it takes to sneeze, and the word had followed him into every room he had entered since. It had stood there talking about him long after the clerk who wrote it had gone home to his supper. That was what files had always been, to him: true in rooms he was not in, kept by people who had long since gone home, and impossible to question or to stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-42.md:41:He did not write it. He wrote, that evening, *Bracken closed the file. Karis has something in her eye from the wax*, which was not the same thing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-42.md:185:She came straight, as she had come at the end of their first bout: one plain heavy delivery, built and committed, the Force that every other fighter on the sheet used, coming across her own slick on feet that knew where the oil lay. There was nowhere to go from it. Lira could not step back; behind her was slick. She could not step aside; slick. She could burst, and land, and stand still in front of the one woman on the bluff who hit still things best.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-47.md:51:Karis's marbled watcher log lay on the sill where she had left it for him, open at the day's page, as it had been open every evening since the bridge. *Ferry landing, two. Road's foot, two. Changed on the bell. Nothing new.* Eleven strangers had come up the bluff, and the four grey coats below it had gone on changing every four hours as they had before anybody arrived, as if the delegation were weather that did not concern them. He found that steadying, and did not quite like finding it so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-47.md:67:He slept the nine hours and woke a little before the bell with his head clear and the rent behind his eyes no bigger than a coin. He ate at the second bell, early, at a time when nobody would remark on it, and went to his lecture and sat in his usual place. On the way he passed the wing, where the door onto the covered walk was shut and the young clerk stood outside it with two black boxes in his arms, waiting to be let in. Beside him, a little apart, stood the instrument woman with her oil can and her brass rag, looking at the shut door of her own document store with the patient face of somebody who has been told to come back later and fully intends to. It was not her posted day. She had come anyway, to make sure, and Cael liked her for it more than he could have explained.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-47.md:115:Under the clause, the institution had to keep a record, term by term, of the work that led up to each semester's panel. The panel would not sit until the twentieth, and nobody could inspect a panel that had not yet sat. What could be inspected today was the hand that kept the record of the term. A record was as good as the hand that kept it and not one grain better. She had spent a long career finding handsome files built by careless hands, and a few plain ones built by careful hands, and she had learned to look at the hand first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-47.md:131:Years ago, at a district station two days' ride from anywhere, she had watched a beautiful four-year observer's log bring a practitioner's whole file down, for no worse reason than that it had no holes in it; honest records are full of small holes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-47.md:189:He had made every stroke of it with one hand tied behind his back, under seven people who had come all the way up the bluff to see what he was. Not one of them would ever know, which was all he had wanted from the afternoon, and now that he had it, it felt strange in his hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-47.md:217:He sat looking at that while the wind came up off the river and found its way under his collar. Nobody came. Below the bluff the water went on as it always went on, with nobody watching it and nobody needing it to be anything except water. After a while the shake in his hands eased, and the other shake under it eased a little too, and he got up and went to find the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-47.md:221:He went to the library to look for them, and Karis found him there first. She came in with her supper on a plate, which was against the library's rules, and which the library had stopped trying to enforce on Karis some time in the first month. She sat down opposite him at the long table under the north window.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-47.md:235:"Anywhere. Three to Gault, in the wing, this morning; the clerk doesn't know what. Then nothing. Not in the lecture range at the second bell. Not in the residence quadrangle at noon, or the fire-watch's store, or any of the three floors." She looked at him over the plate. "He went into every room they went into, and stood in it, and he asked nobody anything at all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-47.md:243:He turned the notebook round and showed her the last two lines. She read them, and then she put her fork down on the plate, very quietly, and did not pick it up again for a while.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:15:None of it was the first task any more. That had changed in a corridor two days ago, with Brom standing square across the flagstones like a post. Reading the delegation was the second task. The first was to stay as thick as an ordinary boy for every hour of every day they were on the bluff, and the first task did not care how interesting the second one got.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:31:In six days she would walk out onto the oak for the Copper final, Copper Rank Two on her certificate and Iron-equivalent by every honest ledger on the bluff, on a leg the safety instructor had not cleared, against the one fighter in the bracket who knew it best.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:125:The order was the panel's. It was the order the panel had been given the matter in, and so it cost the most to read, and he had paid that price on every file for most of his life.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:147:And the third stood at the foot of the record's first sheet, beside two dates the Compact's own clerk had entered there: the day the challenge was filed, and the day the first sitting was called. He had drawn two lines under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:157:So there were two ways to read it. Either the three weeks were wrong in the record, which happened, quietly and for years. Or something in the two years before them had made a boy who could do that much with three weeks. And nowhere in the file, in two years of district filings or in any page of the warden's long reports, did a single sentence say what. There was a great deal about what the boy had done. There was nothing about what had made him able to do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:169:He had read files in the panel's order ever since, and grown no theories he could help.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:177:The notice that had sent him here was a true notice. It named an academy that had done an unusual thing lawfully, and an inspection of the ordinary kind, and nothing else. He had filed no question. He knew perfectly well that not one person on this hill would believe it. Years ago he had given up trying to close the space between his work and the work people imagined for him. It was a useful space. It showed you what each of them feared he had come for. But what it showed was theirs, not his, and he left it to them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:181:This house had answered the notice by changing nothing, and it had not troubled to hide that it was doing so. He had seen it in the courtyard yesterday. There was a gutter mended badly three times and left that way. The cobbles had not been swept for visitors. And the registrar had told the first man through his door where the file stood and how many boxes it filled, instead of how hard it would be to find. A house that changed nothing for an inspector was either very sure of itself or very well advised. Either was worth knowing. Neither was a finding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:191:But three doors that had stood open all season were shut now, from the second bell until the fourth. One was the records hall's back room, where Bracken had built the file. One was the long upper room over the lecture range, where the faculty argued on Fifth-days. The third was the wing's document store behind Gault's office. Cael had never before seen it closed. He had always known it by the instrument woman's oil can, standing on its sill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:215:"Her talking and her reading keep to different rooms in her head," he said. "She can argue with you and read your file at the same time, and she won't lose her place in either."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:227:"Bracken says the file has to work on strangers."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:283:Nine pages, one line, and one name under it. The eleventh he left alone. To try it he would have to stand near the man, and nothing on the bluff that morning had given him an honest reason to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-29.md:93:Cael felt all four go in his own leading hip, forty feet up. Four held breaths, not one returned; the last she paid for with breath borrowed from tomorrow. Four landing beats, four half-breaths locked in place, every one of them standing still in front of the one woman on the bluff who could hit a still thing before it knew it was still. And the hip. From the tier he watched the leading side take the load, and take it, and take it again, and the line it was drawing down her thigh was one he had drawn in himself at two bursts and never dared carry to four.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-29.md:109:Fiske could not read the girl any more. Whatever she had been reading had stopped meaning what it said, and she knew it. Cael watched the knowledge settle into the line of her mouth as she walked back to her chalk. So at the word she did the only thing left to a champion whose instrument has been broken over her head in front of her whole hill. She stopped placing. She built, and committed, and came straight at Lira with a plain heavy delivery, the Force that every other Force fighter on the sheet had always used.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-29.md:155:"There's a form," said Fiske. "For an advancement evaluation. The registry sends an Arbiter if the form goes up. It wants one signature that isn't mine." She turned the cup in her hands. "Two seasons I've sat on top of that sheet, with Rank Eight on my certificate. Two crowns. Every bout on this hill worth having. The porter polishes the stud beside my name on Fourth-days. And in all that time nobody has picked up the pen. Not because anyone decided not to. Because nobody ever had a reason to. I was winning. Why would anybody send the winner away?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-29.md:199:"You can write it down exactly if you never call it *the file*. Not once. Not to anybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-29.md:211:"You beat her," said Brom. "And then she told you something true. That's her being a good sort. You don't have to file it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-29.md:213:"Karis is going to file it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-29.md:215:"Karis files everything. That's Karis being a good sort." He set his heel down hard and shifted his weight. "Thirty-two. Feed me, Cael, before my arms forget what they're for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-29.md:253:*What the third one cost her, in my own hip's language. Four bursts, all borrowed against tomorrow. Four half-breaths stood still in front of the one fighter on the bluff who could have used any of them. A hip I'd guess at three days before it lets her walk, and then the semifinal on top of it. A left shoulder that took the edge of a Force delivery so she wouldn't have to give up a hand's breadth of distance.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:33:Her own question was a long way off by now. Four days ago she had filled a form in eleven minutes and laid it in a tray, and since then it had gone down the bluff in a courier's satchel and up the river, one level higher than the last, to a room she would never see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:35:She had put it down when she filed it. She did not pick it up again here, on a warm stone bench, with a contest going on below her that had nothing to do with it. A woman who carried her filed matters about with her into every room soon stopped being able to see the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:91:The glass was half run before the yard caught on. Cael heard the moment it did, because the noise did not rise. It sank, and thickened, and turned careful, like a crowd watching somebody carry too many plates.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:161:The sound that came down off the tiers was one Cael had no instrument for, and he had spent two years building instruments for rooms.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:189:"Wind, Copper, Rank Two. Seeded the twenty-second. Opening bout drawn by lot, and won. Top line from the fifteenth session. Ten bouts unbeaten. None lost. Certified at the first bell this morning."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:197:Ilsev sat very still. A record certified in the morning, before a result anybody could know; and the result added in the afternoon, at the foot, in the same hand, before a thousand witnesses.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:267:A half-sheet, folded once, in the wing's trained hand. *The assessment office files at the seventh bell, in the Magister's office, an application of the second class. The office invites the senior evaluation seat of the visiting delegation to attend as witness, at her discretion. — Gault.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:293:Gault read the application out. Paper of that class had to be heard by its witness as well as signed by her, and he read it in the voice he used for a column of instrument checks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:299:Then the ground, in the form's printed words, *advancement demonstrated, inconsistent with tier of record*. Then what stood behind it, in Gault's hand. The season, certified; the final, before a witnessed panel; and the panel's own account of what it had seen, attached.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:307:"The season record," she said. "Certified when? Before the final, or after?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:317:Those were the only two answers that mattered. A record certified after its result has been told the result. An account written by a clerk is an account of what the clerk was told.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:343:All term Gault had said the same thing in nearly the same words. Instruments and consistency; nothing else, to anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:89:Today the second-years had done a different sum. They had come an hour early with bread in their coat pockets and climbed to the back, because a full yard would shut the late half of the bluff out in the covered walk, and the back rows were the ones nobody could take from you.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:105:"Copies," Karis said. "The sealed originals sit under the glass down there. He certified both their season records at the first bell this morning, and sealed them, and filed them, and then he walked them down here himself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:125:Two years the Copper Crown had sat on her shelf. Within the hour somebody else would be holding it in front of the whole bluff. She sat down in the front row with her hands loose in her lap and the face of a woman who had made her peace with that a long time ago, perhaps on the very afternoon she won it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:143:For nine days he had kept the read at its thinnest layer, to spare the hold. This afternoon, though, no one on the bluff would spare him a look. Every eye in the yard, the Archmarshal's among them, would be on the oak.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:173:The east tier he left alone, by rule. There was a boy somewhere over there whose file he had carried up a road, and he would not be seen looking for him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:177:He knew her only from paper, and from corridors. On paper she was Wind, Copper, Rank Two, on a certificate three years old that nobody had looked at since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:181:Now she was the top line of the Copper column, and the whole bluff had come to see her fight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-58.md:9:He filed it in the requisitions book, in its place, and blotted the entry. Anyone watching would have seen that and nothing more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-58.md:11:The file itself had come home ten days before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-58.md:35:Of the form's five parts, four were furniture, and he had filled them so often that his hand did them without much help from him. Coverage held from the first day, by way of nothing but the posting's ordinary work. The subject's days, set out in full across a page and a half: floor hours under supervision, lectures, the library, the ladder from its gallery, the wing's own unit, a habit of private writing, a handful of letters posted in the open to people already on file. No one had ever written down the boy's days more truly, and none of it would tell a reader anything. Associations unchanged since the bridge. The inspection, the delegation, the sittings, the observed evaluation, all entered in a voice a harbour clerk keeps for tides.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-58.md:55:There was a reason he had written it, and it was not on the form, or on any page anywhere: not in his own cipher, not in the small case, not in a word to anybody on the bluff. He had made nobody a promise and no arrangement with a living soul. For a term he had gone on setting down true sentences and had stopped setting down some others, and that was all, except for one slip of wing paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-58.md:63:Four lines, in the fifth hand, the shorthand that had grown out of the cramped one. The date the carriages left. The serial of the reserve frame, copied from the wing's maintenance book. The date the file had come back, the fourteenth, and the docket's date beside it. And one line under them that was not a fact at all, of the kind he let himself write perhaps twice a year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-58.md:65:*unmarked file. one of three. probably the third.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-58.md:105:He sealed it and took it to the courier post himself in the morning, and walked back up the bluff road in a thin wind past the two men at the foot of it, who changed on the bell as he went by and did not look at him at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-58.md:209:"No. Not one." Cael sat. "That's his language. It's the only one he owns. A man who can be read loses his post, so he talks the one way nobody can read: like a clerk passing on a timetable. Tonight, then, he picked the one person on this bluff who'd be finished if those papers were read, recited her a timetable, and stood aside while she carried them up a stair."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-58.md:271:"I sat here and told you that an instrument doesn't change sides; it changes readings. That was in this room. I said it with my hands on this table, more than once, as if it closed the matter." Her eyes stayed on the table. "It's true. It's a true thing about instruments. I was applying a correct principle to the wrong class of object. He isn't an instrument. He's a man who's been used as one, for years, by an office that will drop him the day he stops working for it. And I drew him, the whole of him, from the way he was used and not from what he is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:29:Chest heaving in the chalk, Cael felt a thing his ledger had no column for: relief, so plain it was nearly comic. *On the number.* Weight four, clear eleven times in twelve, with his best instrument turned down to almost nothing: it was inside Karis's brackets, a call above the floor of them, which was where a term should put it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:35:The heaviest weight, the fifth, went onto the rack under the Mire instructor's hands. The clerk rubbed out the chalk square and drew it again one pace from the rail, then fetched the plate on its brace and handed it over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:41:He had carried it for three years, ever since a bout he had lost honestly, and it had always lived close under his skin. It did not keep a blow. It gathered a blow, and turned it, and sent it on somewhere else. The thing in his hands was what it had been made for. If anybody had been marking answers right and wrong, it was the right one, and the little dial in the plate's housing would have swung round to a figure that nobody in the room had ever seen it show.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:45:It was written down elsewhere. Vell's copy of the Ardenmere circuit record held the bouts in which he had used it in public, years ago, before he knew enough to keep it back. But Gault's book had no line for it. Gault's book had a plate held low four times, with the shoulder taking everything, and a boy saying so before anybody asked. To bring the other thing out now would be a second surprise, on a third instrument, in a place the baseline had never pointed at, and a slip of wing paper had told him how many surprises a sitting could afford.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:49:His brace was the plain one: wide feet, the plate out on an arm loaded from the shoulder, and everything sent down through the legs. The pawl let go and the weight came down the rail into the plate, and the shock ran up the bones of his forearm and stopped in the shoulder, all of it, like a nail going into a beam. Behind the plate the spring hit the end of its travel with a dull clack.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:61:Two sheets went down side by side under Gault's hands, the baseline's plate and this morning's, and his eyes went from one to the other. Line for line they matched, with not a graduation between them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:67:"I'll say the useful thing about that before somebody else in this building says the other," he said, to the room at large. "A man who comes back after a single term better at every instrument I own is either a wonder or a man whose evaluator should go and look at his instruments, and I look at mine on Fourth-days, and they were looked at yesterday. So far this morning I've measured four things, and one of them hasn't moved. That is what a real semester looks like," said Gault.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:85:At the baseline this weight had been too near and too heavy to escape, and they had made him stand under it with the plate. Now the clerk chalked a square only a pace and a half out from the rail, and along its landing side he laid the long shallow tray of chalk dust and drew a ruler across it to smooth it flat. The wing read that tray standing, at once, before the enrollee had stepped out of it, so that nobody's memory could tidy up what the dust said, and so the clerk took his place at the tray's far end with his ledger open along his forearm, near enough to read a heel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:149:He kept his face turned from the east wall, but the oldest instrument he owned had started counting without being asked. It was the tally of glances he had built in the first weeks of term on the stairs of this very building, and it counted now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:159:You look twice at a thing you did not expect. That isn't manners or temper or training; it's what eyes are for. The first look tells you that something has happened and the second tells you what, and six people in that room had needed the second look. The fourth chair had not. One look had given him the whole of what happened on the oak. He had weighed it on the spot and filed it, and Cael held nothing further that another look could fetch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:179:"Growth against baseline: confirmed across all demonstrated capabilities. Ceiling on the board raised from four to six, which the enrollee gave before the board gave it, now as then. The combined trial bettered, at the heavier weight. Ignition point shown, and met to the wing's standard; the earlier exhibit thin. One measure unchanged, the plate, and so entered. Trial five void for a fault in the apparatus, and run again on the reserve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:195:Odder words had never been said of him, nor duller ones, and both remained true. It had only been said this time with every instrument the system owned pointed at the saying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:197:The east wall emptied. The counsel's chair scraped first, and she went out re-tying her ribbons and already talking. Havel tucked his board under his arm with the log sheet squared on the file beneath it and followed her with his eyes on the floor. Ilsev picked up her case and stopped for a breath or two beside the copper pot, where its thread of colour had sunk back to the first line, before she went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:235:They went back across the quadrangle at the slowest pace the bluff had ever seen, two people with two good legs between them, one each, and took the residence stair a tread at a time. Brom was on the landing, a bag of ice ready in his strapped hand, and he held it out to Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:279:*Thirty-five days since the stair. It wakes when I do. Three hours under the Compact's four best chairs, and it stayed in. Cost: one interval and one headache. The Pressure stayed in my arm. Gault wrote "no change" about the plate and then stood up for me over it, which I didn't earn and can't put right.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:281:*I've been keeping things back since before I had a word for it. Today the keeping went under instruments for three hours, and it held. I'm going to stop calling it a habit.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:23:"Their counsel proposed one of theirs at the foot of her request, and I objected in writing, on both sides of a sheet." He set the pen down. "It took me a quarter of an hour to draft and her four minutes to accept, and I'd like you to know it was done, because you'll never see it anywhere else. It'll be in the file. Nobody reads that part of a file."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:33:Cael looked at the shut door, and at the clerk, and understood that he was being treated exactly as the delegation was being treated, to the minute and to the word, and that it was the fairest thing anybody on the bluff could have done for him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:69:"If he asks about anything that's already written down somewhere, I tell him all of it, straight. If he asks about anything that isn't, I tell him I won't answer, out loud and in those words, so that the clerk can put down that I said no." He looked at the face-down sheet. "That's the change. In every room before this one, when there's been a thing I couldn't say, I've said something true with a corner cut off it: on Gault's plate yesterday, at the baseline, at Greyvane. Each time it worked, and each time it cost, and each cost went in the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:71:"It won't work on him. Hand him a true sentence with a corner cut off and he'll see the cut. He won't mention it. He'll simply know. Then the finest reader the Compact has will be holding a map of my walls." He turned his hands over. "A flat no only tells him that there's something I won't say, which he knows already, as does the whole bluff. A cut corner points his eye at the cut."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:75:"Then when you say no, put it down like a full plate," he said. "Both hands, all the way to the table, and no rattle."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:79:"Let me name the danger over the porridge, so I'm not naming it to the ceiling in the small hours. Picture it on paper: the assay boy declines to answer the Compact's own officer, mid-inspection, into the book. Into the inspection file it goes, and the first readers will be the ones hunting for a reason. You could hardly hand them anything better to quote."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:87:"Nine days he's been on this hill, and not a soul has played to him. Withrow stood still on purpose. Bracken's file stood up without anybody holding it. Rooke signed his sheets and went back to his cohort. Yesterday Gault ran three hours of true instruments and wrote down a flat line and stood up for it. The whole house has spent nine days being exactly what it is, as hard as it can." He spread his hands on the table. "So picture me walking in there and playing to him. Then I'm the only performer on the whole bluff, and I've spoilt the single thing this house has managed to let him see."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:163:"For three years I've tried every week to make that half-breath shorter on purpose, and I've never managed it; I tried a month ago on the flagstones and failed." He kept his eyes on the man's face and did not try to make the answer look better than it was. "Yesterday a frame failed by nobody's design. It happened once. How, I can't tell you. Promise you a second time and I'd be selling a skill I haven't got. What I own is one accident and a bruise the size of a plate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:165:Two breaths went by while the man weighed it, unhurried, like a figure from a new instrument he was still learning to believe.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:201:"Sixth. You keep records of your own; a report on your file has a phrase for it, *extensive private notation habit*." The phrase came out like a borrowed cup set gently down. "What are they for?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:247:"Your two friends fought on the house's ladder this season, and the registry hasn't looked at either of their certificates in years. Did the brackets measure them?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:249:"No." Cael did not soften it. "The ladder sorts people by their paper. It was built for practitioners who'd be exactly what their certificates say on the morning they're seeded, and stay so till the season ends. My friends spent the whole year in a cage built for people who did not exist. One of them took the whole cage outright. That's how you know it was one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:259:"No," said Cael. "He favours his instruments. He's never once told me what he thinks I am; he says that's where his work stops, and it stops there, and if his needles said I'd gone backward he'd write it down in the same hand he wrote yesterday's note in." He thought about it. "I'd rather that than favour. Favour can change its mind."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:277:"The Compact filed no question," he said. "My being here carries nothing. I have no other errand and nothing written in my pocket. Your counsel will have said as much. She was right. Of everything true you've heard this month, I expect it's the hardest to swallow." He let that sit. "I came to see whether the record and the practitioner match."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:295:"Then we're finished, at twenty-nine minutes." He rose. The chair stayed where it was, out from the table. A man of his rank had perhaps never needed to tuck one in, or perhaps the thought had never crossed his mind. "Counsel, I'm obliged. Magister, I'm obliged. No paper on this bluff is better kept than the card on your wall, and I've seen most of them." He turned to the corner. "Clerk."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-38.md:13:After breakfast he went to the covered walk's east end, where the rail looked down over the last bend of the bluff road, and stood there through the change of the second bell with a lecture's notes open in his hands. It was a good place to be ordinary in. People streamed past behind him in both directions, going to the lecture range and coming from it, and none of them needed him for anything, and he gave every one of them the ordinary amount of room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-38.md:17:The man came round the last bend on foot, alone, walking fast, with a satchel strapped flat across his chest so that it could not swing. That alone was wrong for the hour. The bluff's morning traffic came up slowly and in company: carters with flour, a guild factor with a clerk, instructors back from the town with parcels under their arms, all of them stopping at the bend to look back at the river. This man did not look at anything, and he climbed like somebody whose day was measured from the moment he set off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-38.md:21:The watchers at the road's foot wore grey, and Cael had spent a season learning their grey: road grey, the grey of dust that has been rained on, a colour chosen so that nobody would remember it. This was the other grey: cold and faintly blue, like slate after rain, the grey of the registry. He had seen it on the clerks behind the long counter at the certification hall in Denvash, and once on a young assessor standing in front of a pump step, in boots too new to have creased. He had never once mistaken it for anything else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-38.md:25:He did not follow the man up to the gate. He wanted to, and he noticed himself wanting to, and stayed where he was at the rail with his notes open, because a boy who leaves his place to trail a courier has told every watcher on the bluff that couriers matter to him. Instead he watched the satchel go in under the arch, and counted twelve breaths. Then the porter came out of his lodge at a trot, which the porter never did, and went across the first quadrangle toward the chancellor's door with the man in grey a pace behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-38.md:33:By the third bell there was nobody on the bluff who did not know that a sealed thing had come up the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-38.md:35:Nobody had read it, and nobody needed to. Withrow's runner had gone from her door at a run, which runners on the bluff did not do, and had knocked at four other doors in turn. Four people had come out of those doors and crossed the second quadrangle toward the records hall with the particular walk of people who have been asked to bring nothing and come now. Bracken was one, and Gault another, carrying a folder. Rooke came last, with his hands in the pockets of his dark coat, and stopped in the middle of the quadrangle to say something to a cohort boy who ran off toward the halls. By the time the lecture range let out, the second-years were telling one another it was a death in the chancellor's family, and the third-years were telling one another it was money. A Current boy on the lecture stair was telling anybody who would stand still that it was war.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-38.md:119:"So we'll be inspected in the ordinary way," said Withrow. "Bracken, the documentary work begins this afternoon. I don't want it built to win an argument. I want it built to be filed and to sit in a cabinet and be true. Magister Gault—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-38.md:171:The common room after supper was a different instrument altogether.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-38.md:197:Cael thought about it honestly, because she had asked honestly. "That he'd decided before he came into the room. He wasn't telling the table anything; he was telling me. The evaluation is the evaluation. Same floor, same instruments, same standard. Whoever's sitting at the rail." He turned his cup on the arm of the chair. "It was the kindest thing anybody said in there, and he said it with his eyes, so it can't go in a minute."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:21:Two at the ferry landing. Two at the bluff road's foot. The change on the bell, every four hours, and a small tick for every change she had seen with her own eyes from the library's top window. There was a line for every day since the delegation came up the hill, and every line was the same as the one above it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:31:"I file things under what they are."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:57:She did it in the records hall, at the long table, to nine people. Withrow sat halfway down one side as she always did, with Bracken next to her and, at the corner, his inky-knuckled clerk holding a pen he had been forbidden to use. Gault had come, and Rooke had come and was standing, and the Ash instructor sat at the far end because she had signed the evaluation and liked to know what became of her signatures. Karis had a chair because the provision's file began with her name. Cael was there because the provision was his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:185:He did it under the records-hall porch, out of the rain, with the list on a board against his forearm and the chests set down in a row in front of him on the flags. There were four of them, and they had come up the bluff road under four seals apiece, and they would go down it under four new ones, pressed that morning at the delegation's working table in the counsel's wax and the senior seat's. His list had sixteen lines, one for each seal, and beside each line the impression's number and the hand that had pressed it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:219:Havel had sat at the foot of the long table on the fifteenth while that man's index was turned over by the best counsel the delegation had, sheet by sheet, for most of a day, and had watched it find every paper she asked for inside a minute and a half. He had never been in a position to tell a registrar that his file was good, and he was not in one now. He could hand back a book with his initials in the right column and his eyes on the right line, and so he did that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:233:The carriage moved, and facing backward, Havel had the whole bluff in his window as they went down it: the gate, the wall, the long grey range with its tiers open to the rain. It drew away from him a bend at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:235:His eyes went down to the list in his lap, where every line had its tick. He went down it once more anyway, from the top, line by line, until the road had turned enough that the bluff was behind the shoulder of the hill and there was nothing in the window but rain and the river and the ferry landing, where two men in road-grey coats stood under a lamp just as they had stood on the morning he arrived.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:255:In a quarter of an hour the carriages were off the bluff, and the road below the gate was empty except for the rain. Cael lingered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:285:*Count the rooms. Coss asked it with his whole coat. Quenna asked once, never again, and taught me anyway. Gault won't ask it, says so to my face, and keeps saying so. Ephram turned it into a promise to wait, out loud, on the yard. This bluff has answered it among itself at supper more ways than I've bothered to write down.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:291:*If you don't know what a thing is, you ask. If you think you already know, you don't ask. You go and look at the paper, and then at the thing, and see whether whoever kept the paper kept it properly. That's what he did. He came to audit a file. The file was about me; that's all I was in it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:303:*But look at what a gap gets. Nothing. Nobody watches a gap. Nobody writes a designation for one and files it where no name comes back. Nobody sends the best evaluator they own to sit four feet from a gap and then leave its biggest question alone on purpose.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-54.md:27:"Seated, against a wall, playing clerk, and three times it slid off me regardless." Cael turned the mug in his hands. "This is six standing, under Gault's instruments."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-54.md:31:"Here's what I think, then. Today the work that counts has no instrument pointed at it. Every one of them will be looking at the brass and the frame and the figures and thinking that's where the work is, and nobody in that room will ever know what the corner cost you." He reached down and knocked twice on the top of Cael's head with the knuckles of his good hand, softly. "I'll know. That's the best I've got."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-54.md:81:The delegation's counsel had the nearest, and she was untying the ribbon of a second folder with her teeth because both her hands were full of the first. Next to her Ilsev was sharpening a pencil with a small knife, carefully, into a fold of paper on her knee, so as to leave no shavings on the wing's floor. Beyond Ilsev, Havel was writing the date at the head of a ruled sheet on a board across his knees, with the bound file under the board. As Cael came in he looked up for about four-tenths of a second, level and without expression, and then down again to finish his date.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-54.md:87:Between door and crossing lay thirty feet of oak, and by the end of them Cael had it. Everybody else on that wall had brought the tool of their trade, because people do. The counsel lived by paper and had brought paper, Ilsev had brought a point for marking, and Havel had brought the record, since keeping it was what he was for. The highest rank in the building had come with empty hands. Either he meant to do nothing, or the instrument he worked with was already sitting in his chair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-54.md:153:"Working ceiling given by the enrollee: six. Baseline: four. Seven runs within the baseline's spread, with a shorter interval. The ceiling given before the instrument gave it, as before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-54.md:185:Cael had looked at it every time he came into this room and had never once got a name for it. Squat, copper, three short legs, a little larger than two fists: that was the pot. Out of its lid stood a glass straw scored with lines as close as a comb's teeth. A small brass plate on its belly carried a date of calibration in paint, and the date was this month's. The clerk set it on its legs on the oak a pace from the crossing and stepped back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-54.md:199:Under his hand the metal warmed, and a hot band two fingers broad spread under its skin and passed inward to the water, and the thread in the glass straw began to rise. It rose slowly. Slowness was the instrument's whole virtue and the reason Gault trusted it, because nothing quick can fool water, and the thread climbed past one line and the next and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-54.md:217:"That is a better instrument reading than the Greyvane exhibit supports," he said. "The exhibit calls it marginal."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-41.md:33:He thought of it as a ledger, because he had been trained on ledgers, and on one side stood the subject. In a single year the boy had produced an adjudication, a transcript that half the continent had paid six silver marks to read, and a designation on his file graded well above that of the man sent to watch him. On the other side stood a full term of embedded coverage by an officer with a clean record. And at the foot, where the total went, there was nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-41.md:41:He looked at the six for the space of a few breaths, while the desk clerk murmured *forty-one* and wrote it down, and then he made up the file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-41.md:47:After supper he read the floor allocations on the covered walk's board, which he had written himself, as if they were news, and went up the staff stair at the pace of a man going to bed. There was nothing for the small case tonight. He sat on the bed's edge for a while, listening to the river under the bluff, and then he put the lamp out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-41.md:67:The Blade second-year fed him short strikes, cheap ones, the kind Merrick had always fed him, and Brom stood under them and did not turn. He took them on the forearm and the shoulder, square, with his weight set, and the instant each one landed he went forward through it, hard, while the struck side was still hard. That was Rooke's repair, the one the nineteen exercises had been built to make: stop finishing the absorption. Drive while you're still iron. For two years Brom had taken a strike, finished taking it, softened, and only then countered, and in the gap between the softening and the counter there had been a stall that every good fighter on the bluff could find.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-41.md:205:*Bracken closes the file in the morning, Karis says. Four boxes. She's going to watch him tie the last string and she says she won't cry, and she's lying.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:1:# Chapter 49 — Live on an Active File
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:5:People talked in recesses. They stood at windows with cups and told each other what the morning had meant, and by the time the sitting began again, half of them had agreed on a meaning that nobody had read anywhere. She had seen it happen in a hundred halls, and so when the counsel rose and went out talking, with two of the clerks trailing after her, and the academy's people drifted to the far windows to stretch, Ilsev stayed in her chair and opened the file she had asked for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:9:The routing file was a long narrow ledger bound in grey, every request and transfer that had ever touched the subject's file entered one to a line, in a dozen hands over two years. She went down it from the top. A district office and a first filing. A records request in an autumn, with the designation in its priority field, two grades above anything its signatory could have entered. Greyvane: her own query, the return, her referral. A transfer to this house. And at the very end, the requisition for the wing's filings, and their return to the wing the evening before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:11:Behind the ledger, in a pocket of the back board, was the file's correspondence. Most of it was ordinary paper. At the very back, under a brass clip, lay a single folded sheet with the registry's stamp on it. Pinned to it was a narrow docket in Havel's hand: *Correspondence in. Delegation's courier, up. Eighth bell.* It had come up the bluff the evening before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:19:*Determination: no originating authority of record. Designation predates file creation. No action available at this clearance.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:37:The first witness said that nobody had done it. A mark in the registry was not rain; it did not fall on a file out of the sky. Somebody entered it, and the whole reason the registry existed was so that an entry could be traced to a hand. Every finding she had ever put her name to stood on that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:39:The second witness said the mark was older than the file it was written in, and that was the one she could not get round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:41:Ink goes onto a sheet. The sheet does not grow up round the ink, so either the file had been made over at some time, and the mark carried across from an older file, in which case the older file was the true record, and her question had been sent to the wrong door twice. Or else the second witness meant precisely what it said. There had been a value in that field before there was any field to hold it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:59:It was a well-made sentence. She could admire it as work, even while it was being done to her. It was courteous and final, and it cost nothing to accept, and it had plainly been made to be accepted. Most people who received it would nod and lay it in the file, and by every rule she knew they would be right to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:63:It left her the same choice it left everybody. She could take it as her answer, note it in the file, and go on with the afternoon. Or she could send it up again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:71:*A determination that no originating authority exists is not a determination as to the designation's validity, and the designation remains live on an active file.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:81:Then she put the matter down, on purpose. A form was a place to leave a weight. That was what forms were for, whatever the people who printed them believed, and a woman who could not leave a filed thing where she had filed it would sooner or later file badly, just to be rid of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:83:Across the hall, against the far wall, the enrollee sat on his hard chair among the registrar's clerks, with a floor sheet on his knee and his eyes on nothing in particular. The designation she had just sent up for the third time sat on that boy's file, and had sat there since before the file was made. She did not look at him for longer than it took to see where he was. He was not her question, any more than he had been in hall three. The paper was her question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:85:Cael had spent the recess on that hard chair, because a clerk would have, and because getting up to walk about was the kind of small change he had promised himself at dawn not to make. Of the whole delegation only two had kept their places: the Archmarshal, with his folder shut and his pen across it, and Ilsev, who had read something from a file and then written on a form for about eleven minutes, by Cael's count of his own breaths, and laid it in the records officer's tray, and drunk a cup of tea in four swallows. He did not know what it was. It was not his to know, and he did not write it on the back of his floor sheet. He only noticed that whatever she had read had not changed her face at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:91:The courier had come up the bluff the evening before on a tired horse, and Havel had signed for the satchel himself and entered every paper in the day-book before he opened it, as the rule said. The third was a single folded sheet with the registry's stamp, addressed to the senior evaluation seat. He had known what it was before he unfolded it, and had unfolded it all the same, to enter it properly, and had read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:97:There were two sheets in it, and he knew them both. He logged them at his place at the foot, with the brass glass lying on its side, in four minutes, because that was how long it took to do it properly. The return had come in the night before, and he had entered it then; but it had moved today, from the file to the senior seat's hand and back, and a thing that moves gets a line. Then the new referral, out, one level higher. He gave the new referral a reference to the return, and the return a reference to the Greyvane referral, and the Greyvane referral, already in the ledger from a year ago, a reference forward to both. Three sheets, each pointing at the other two, so that whoever lifted any one of them would be led to the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:103:Today she had not read it a third time. She had read it once, and filed again inside the recess, and drunk her tea cold. And Havel, at the foot of the table with his head bent over the ledger, found that it moved him more than the third reading had. The third reading had been a person puzzled. This was a person deciding to go on being puzzled, out loud, in ink, at the next level up, where people with better lamps would have to read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:113:He dated the fourth and wrote it in the same dry hand as the others, because the sameness of the hand was the point. It was one line long: the Greyvane referral returned from the chain above, eleven months on, and refiled a level higher inside the recess, both sheets logged and cross-referenced in his own hand. It was true, and it said no more than he knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:203:"The clause is unrepealed. It appears in two independently certified copies of the founding schedule, is incorporated by the receiving institution's charter under a standard article, and is not among the eleven clauses the standardization directive repealed by name in a schedule its drafters had open in front of them. The directive's transitional article presupposes enrollments made under it. The enrollment before us conforms to the clause's language in its basis, its ongoing requirement, and its recording. Whatever else the delegation reports, the basis is sound law."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:211:The Archmarshal's pen lay crosswise on the closed folder, where it had lain since Gault's last line. It did not rise. It had not risen for the counsel's best argument either, nor for Withrow's answer, nor for the second certified copy coming out of the box, and it did not rise now, when a senior evaluator of the Compact stood up and said, into the record, that the boy on the hard chair by the wall was enrolled on sound law.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:237:*He is calibrating the instrument. I am the reading.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:51:*Filed under debts, no current mechanism of payment. Second entry. Same file. Same nothing.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:63:They had the long table nearest the hearth. The counsel talked, as she had talked the whole road from the coast, about inns: this one's mutton, which was good, and its beds, which had been aired. Then she told of a magistrate she had once watched eat a whole game pie at this very table so that he would not have to answer a question put to him between courses. The records officer handled his fork like an instrument under calibration. The assessor ate a little bread and went up before the pudding. Vastin asked the counsel what the question had been, which pleased her a great deal, and listened to the answer, and did not say one word about Halcenvane.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:69:He laid the brass watch on the table, and beside it, in a row, four pencils of four colours, though he did not expect to use them. Then the file in its three tapes: grey round the record, red round what had been tendered in it, string round the rest. Last of all, from the inside pocket of the coat on the nail, the small calf-bound notebook.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:83:They were correct, and he read them once and set them to one side. They answered every question the notice had asked, and none of the questions he had brought up the bluff in his own head. He had said as much to the boy's face, across a whitewashed table, and meant it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:93:The record had the interval. *At the enrollee's request: fatigue affecting response quality.* The clerk had set the minute beside it, and then, in a smaller hand, squeezed in under the line, three words of his own: *before instruments indicated*. No one had asked him to. He had seen that the boy asked before any needle had a reason to move, and had decided that a record which left it out would be a less true record. Vastin made a mark against the clerk in his head, the kind of mark he made perhaps four times a year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:119:It was small, its calf gone soft at the corners from years of coat pockets, a third of its leaves used and the rest blank. His coat had carried it to the last inspection; it would carry it to the next. Nothing in it ever went into a file. Proof was no part of its purpose. He kept it so that he could set a thought down while it was warm and come back to it cold, and see whether it had survived the cooling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:153:Sixteen years old. Mid-payment. Under every instrument and every pair of eyes that would ever sit in judgment on his life. And met with a hazard nobody had rehearsed him for, he had answered it *smaller*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:161:The notebook went down by the file. The pencils stayed in their row.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:165:He went on through the rest of the file at the pace of the lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:177:The boy kept records. That fact sat in every monitoring filing on the file and in the Greyvane transcript besides, and every officer who had ever read the folder had passed over it as the dullest line in it. Vastin had asked what the records were for and been told, and had believed what he was told: *So I can't lie to myself later.* The next question he had let lie. It was the obvious next question. Withrow's counsel had drawn breath to hear it. Gault, at the boy's elbow, had gone as still as a post. Any capable officer would have put it, and any poor one would have put it badly, and he had looked at it for less than a second and not put it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:181:The mandate on that bluff had been the record against the practitioner, and a private notebook was not in the record. An officer who reaches past his mandate because the reach happens to be open to him has stopped evaluating. He has begun to collect. And a collector at Vastin's height, carrying Vastin's authority, is the worst thing the Compact is able to make.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:189:The wing's six filings were not in the folder. He had read them on his first morning on the bluff, the thirteenth, before the first bell, and put them where they belonged in his own reckoning, a good way under the record: a capable officer's honest account of the chair he had been given and the list he had been handed. There had been nothing in them he needed twice. He had tied the pink tape again knot over knot, as it had come, and set the case by the door for the clerk to carry home to the wing, and he did not think of it now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:191:Last, before the note, he went through the enrollment file one more time, with his finger running down Bracken's numbered index.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:193:He had handled more enrollment files than he could count, and this one was the best-made of any that had crossed his table in years. Each sheet carried one proof and said so in one line, and nothing argued, and nothing was ornamented. The delegation's counsel, who had wanted very badly to find a weak sheet, had found the load-bearing one in under a minute and a half on her first attempt and had looked, for a moment, as though she might clap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:195:Good work should be told it is good by somebody who knows the difference, while the man who did it is still in his chair. So he drew a sheet of the house's paper from the table drawer and wrote to the registrar at Halcenvane, privately, four sentences long. What the file had been tested for, he left unsaid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:197:*Registrar. Your enrollment file on the assay provision is the best-made I have handled in some years. Each sheet proves one thing and says which, and my own counsel, who wished to find a weak one, found the load-bearing sheet on her first attempt, which is the only real test of an index. I thought you should hear it from somebody who has no reason to say it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:205:A finding is read once, by the people it concerns, and filed. A file note goes on being read, by every officer who ever lifts the folder down, for as long as the folder exists. Whoever came to this folder after him would come to it through his note, and would read the rest in its light, whether they meant to or not. He had spent his life refusing to let other men's digests do that to him. He had no wish to do it to anyone else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:219:The second got shorter shrift. Within a month it would put a warden on the bluff road. And a boy who had already sat through one adjudication would answer it with just the behaviour that turns a monitoring file into a stage play, because a boy who knows why he is watched begins to perform for the watcher, and nothing true has ever been got out of a performance. He tore it across. The halves lay one on the other by the lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:223:It was the kind reading, and half the continent had reached it already. A small house in the hills had staked a year on it, and he did not think less of them. It said the file was a mistake. Some clerk at some station had made one wrong mark at a boy's Kindling, and everything since, two academies and a hearing and a provision and a stamp, had been the long labour of picking out one bad stitch. It was comfortable because it asked nobody to change their mind about anything: the machine was sound, it had caught its sleeve once on one boy, and decent people had spent a year working the sleeve free.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:227:It was not the boy's gifts. The registry swallowed a few remarkable children every year without so much as a hiccup; finding them a shelf was what it had been built for. What decided him was an absence: no carelessness anywhere. A slip leaves fingermarks. He had hunted this file for them for eleven days and four nights and found not one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:229:Nobody had decided anything about the boy when the designation went onto his sheet. The Kindling had entered it, in the first minutes of the file's life. It had stood ever since. Three houses, a public adjudication, and a registrar's index that left the other side almost nothing to say: it had come through all of them. And through all of it nobody, at any height, had put forward another classification, or amended the entry by a word, or written so much as a sheet suggesting it be looked at again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:235:He did not write that. He had no evidence for it. It lay a very long way outside his mandate. And an officer of his grade who puts a guess into a file has arranged for some junior man, three years on, to act on the guess without its history and without its doubt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:5:*Caelen Hesk-ward, 41-7843-V, enrolled practitioner; see demonstration provision, enrollment basis, filed.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:7:The entry instrument lay open on the records-hall counter with its four companions stacked beside it, and Bracken had turned it round so that Cael could read it right way up, which he did not need. It was the fourth day of term, a Fifth-day, in the hour after the morning's session let out, and the stone hall was empty except for the three of them and the clerk at the far desk, who was copying something with his tongue between his teeth. Bracken had sent a boy to the residence with the first bell, carrying a half-sheet that said *Records hall, the hour after session. Bring nothing.* Nobody else had been asked. Withrow had declined to come down, and had said so to Bracken in Cael's hearing in the covered walk the afternoon before, in five words: "It's a filing. File it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:15:He had seen the name before, of course. It was on the registry's own extract in the enrollment file, and on the Greyvane transcript, and on the summons that had brought him up the bluff road in the first place. But it was not a name anybody had ever called him. It was the name a clerk at Denvash had built out of a boy and a workshop on the morning of his Kindling. The given name had been made formal, the household he came from had been turned into a word with a hyphen in it, and a number stood after it that meant nothing to anyone but the registry. Seeing it here, in a list of five, under Lira and Brom and Karis and Ephram, was like hearing his own voice played back from the far side of a valley.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:19:"Good." Bracken took the instrument back. "The schedule office will cut it down to something it can print. They always do. That's their business, and none of mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:21:Under the instrument lay the fee note, with the house's seal on it. Under that lay the acknowledgment of the meet calendar, initialed. And under all of them, thicker than the other four together and bound in a grey card cover with a tape round it, lay the document the charter really required: a certified copy of Halcenvane Academy's enrollment record, every name on it, current to the first of the month.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:27:*I certify this record complete and true, containing every practitioner enrolled at this institution on the date given, without omission.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:39:"This morning I have decided nothing about you." Bracken said it to the tape, knotting it. "I wasn't asked to. Your name is on that record because you're enrolled, on a lawful basis, adjudicated in public, and renewed by a panel in front of the Compact's own chairs. The charter says the record goes, so the record has gone. A registrar who decides things is a registrar who will one day be asked, under oath, why he decided them. In twelve years nobody has asked me that question, and nobody is going to begin with this file." He looked up then, over the counter, and there was something under the dryness of his face that Cael had seen there only once before, on the step of a grain factor's counting house with his hand flat against a door. "When somebody comes to look, and somebody will, they'll find a clerk's routine with nobody's fingerprints on it but the form's."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:53:"Once it's in the courier's bag it belongs to somebody else," she said on the records-hall steps. "I've been minding it for twelve weeks. I'd like to watch it go." She said it the way she said anything about her own feelings, as a finding she had checked and was now reporting, and Cael went down the bluff road with her as far as the first bend. A stone bench stood there under a thorn the wind had bent half over, and the whole valley lay open below it, river and bridge and town and the long brown fields going away to the hills.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:77:"Then the registrar signs a certificate that says *without omission* over a record with an omission in it." She looked at him. "Do you want to be the one to write down that you'd have preferred Bracken to lie? Over his name? In ink?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:105:They walked back up together. Behind them the ferry grounded on the far bank, and a speck on a grey went up the road beyond the river toward the hills, and the morning's work was out of every hand on the bluff for good.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:119:He did it the same way for everybody. He took the form. He read it, every line, at an even pace, from the name to the signature. He stamped the received line, tore off the copy-slip along its rule, and gave the slip back. The Stone first-year got what the Mire third-year got, and the Mire third-year what the first-year got, down to the second it took to read the reason for the hours. Cael had worked out in his second month on the bluff what that was for. A man who read every form from every hand was a man nobody remembered reading anything in particular.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:161:"With the same instruments?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:163:"With instruments to the same standard, checked by me on the day, the way I check these on Fourth-days." Gault's face did something that in another man would have been a grin. "I have been issued a case. For the seals and the record books and the calibration weights. It is a very solid case." He paused. "It has a handle."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:179:"At every meet." Gault sobered, a little. "The tournament charter has the registry validate every tier result. A Copper win goes onto a Copper fighter's record only once a Compact officer has watched it and signed that the tier was what the paper said. So there will be a validation seat at every floor, every meet, a man or a woman with a badge and a book." He reached for the next form on his tray, and then did not pick it up. "Nobody will have to come up this bluff road to look at you this year, enrollee. You are going to them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:183:Since Ardenmere the arrangement had run in one direction, and he had never once seen it run the other. He stayed where he was, and the apparatus travelled to him. Wardens had come to a market town. Assessors had come to a circuit hall. A delegation with an Archmarshal at its head had come up this very road in three carriages and stayed eleven days. And all that time two coats stood at the ferry landing and two at the road's foot, because the bluff was where he was, and somebody paid by the year to have it watched.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:225:He kept it short. Hesk did not like long letters; he said a long letter was a man who had not yet decided what he meant. So Cael wrote what had happened, in the order it had happened, and stopped. The roster had been filed at noon by the registrar, with every name on the enrollment record, his own among them in the registry's spelling. The courier had gone over on the ferry with a cart of turnips. Gault had been issued a case with a handle and was unreasonably pleased about it. The first meet was five weeks out in a wool town two days to the south.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:237:*Roster filed at noon. Eleven names on the travel sheet. Five on the entry. Mine the fifth, in the registry's spelling.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:5:Cael wrote that down first, standing in the doorway of a chandler's that had not yet opened, on the fifth of the new month, with a fine rain deciding whether to begin. Two storeys of brown stone. A doorstep hollowed like a bowl by a hundred years of grain buyers coming in to argue. Beside the door, a brass plate about the size of a book, which said what the building was in letters too small to read from the far side of the street. A rope-maker's on one side of it, with coils of new rope hung in the window like sleeping snakes, and on the other a shuttered house with a cat on its sill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:21:Nobody had asked who would come. At first light all four of them had simply been at the residence door with their coats on, and nobody had said anything about it, and they had gone down the bluff road together in the grey. At the road's foot the two men in grey coats had been changing on the bell. One of them had looked at Lira's kit bag for just as long as a man looks at a bag, and then at the river. In the town Karis had bought four hot rolls from a baker's hatch and handed them round without asking who wanted one, and they had eaten them walking, and Brom had paid the bridge toll for all of them, as he always did, before anybody else could get a coin out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:23:Bracken had come too. He wore his own plain coat instead of the registrar's gown, and he carried a leather satchel with Halcenvane's sponsorship file in it: the certified season record, the witnessed final, Gault's filing on the registry's long form with Ilsev's line under it, and the season's sheets behind them in his numbered order. He had carried it down the hill himself and would not let Brom take it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:25:"Ninety seconds," he said at the curb. "That's my part. I put the file on a desk and say what it is. Then I'm furniture with a bag. The rest is hers and the frame's, and I'd thank you all to remember whose it is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:41:She watched him do it. She had seen him behind his own counter for most of a year, and she had never seen him on the wrong side of one. He did it the same way. He took the file out in its order and laid it square to the desk's edge. He set it facing the clerk. He put his finger on the first sheet, the filing itself, and then on the line under it where the season record was named, and then on the witness's line, and said the name of each in a voice that did not rise or hurry. The clerk read along behind his finger and nodded at each one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:49:"She is," said Bracken, and took his hand off the file, and stepped back from the desk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:55:The clerk gathered the file and took it through the far door, and the door shut behind him, and Lira sat down on the bench to wait.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:61:Every morning since she came to the bluff, at the fifth bell, the porter had climbed his little ladder at the board in the Crown yard and chalked the same word beside her name. He did it carefully, in a round hand, and polished the letters with his cuff when he was done. Six letters. He had chalked them beside her name on the morning she arrived and on the morning after the final, when she was the champion of the bracket the word belonged to, and he would chalk them tomorrow unless somebody gave him a reason not to. Nothing she did on a floor could reach that chalk. She had known that all season. Fiske had said it to her at the board, flatly, two years into knowing it herself: win it, and then make them look at you.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:77:"Practitioner Lira," he said. "Wind Path. Copper, Rank Two, on her present certificate. Filed for advancement by an assessment office on the record of a season." He did not look at the file. He had read it already. "We'll run the standard evasion sequence first. Then the load figures. Then the frame." He tipped his head toward the wall without turning. "Have you run the sequence before?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:117:A rope off a drum, a sprung plate on the far wall, a measured rod along the floor: how far the burst carried, how much it could carry with it, how fast she was off the line again after the landing. Five runs at each. The woman read the figures aloud as the man called them, so that both of them would have heard every one. Lira did not listen to them. She knew what they were. They had been Iron since Vell kept them in her ledger at Ardenmere, and everyone who had stood at a rail and watched her fight had known it for two years. The only thing that had ever been missing was a station.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:119:So she gave the station its numbers. She took the rope off the drum in her right hand and burst against its pull, and felt the drum fight her and lose. She put her shoulder to the sprung plate at the end of a burst and felt it give and come back. She ran the measured rod five times, landing and leaving, landing and leaving, and on the fourth landing the hip sent up a single bright complaint, like a nail in a boot, and she let it complain and went on. On the fifth she was off the line faster than on the first. The woman read the figure out twice, in case she had misread it, and the man said, "Yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:133:It took four seconds. Nobody had ever told her that, either: that it was not a flash. Something went down into her from the palm, and down, the way cold water goes down when you drink it too fast, and it *read*. It went through the hip and the old foundry burns on her forearm and the place under the ribs where Brom had hit her in the rain at Ardenmere, and through all the floors she had fought on since, and it did not hurry. For four seconds there was nothing in the whole world but a system deciding what she was. There was not one thing she could do about it while it happened. She had known that before she put out her hand. She had decided, on the road down the bluff, that she would not try to do anything, and she kept to it. She stood with her palm on the cold metal and her breath even and let it look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:141:She had thought, on the road down, that when she heard it she would feel something enormous, and that she would have to stand very still to keep it from showing. She did not feel anything enormous. She felt the cold of the frame going out of her palm a little at a time, and a small sore place at the back of her heel where her boot had rubbed on the bluff road, and the bench-hard ache in the hip. Ninety breaths at Fenmark had made her Copper. Four seconds in a grain factor's counting room had unmade it. It seemed to her that there ought to be more to it than that, and that there was not, and that this was exactly right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:149:It took ten. A clerk came in from the front office with a second book, thicker than the first, and sat at the end of the table, and entered the supersession in it, line by line, with a pen that scratched. While he wrote, the woman explained to Lira that her Fenmark certificate would not be destroyed. It would remain in the registry as a historical entry, marked superseded, so that the line of her record was unbroken, and any reader in future would be able to see where she had stood before and on what date she had been moved. The prior classification had to be superseded on the same instrument that made the new one, the woman said, or it stayed live underneath, and that caused trouble later.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:181:Cael had seen her not open a book once before, on the morning of the twentieth, in the common room, with a page that had not turned between the second bell and his coming home. That had been twice now. He filed it, with some care, as the nearest thing to a tell that Karis had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:195:The certificate was printed. Somebody had filled in the blanks in ink.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:233:She started walking again. The rain had set in now, small and steady. At the foot of the street the houses stopped and the wharfs began, and beyond them the river lay wide and grey and full of barges. A mile upstream the bluff rose out of the water with Halcenvane strung along its top, small from here, and steady, and wet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:239:She did not look at him. She looked at the bluff, and at the river, and at the certificate in her hand, which she had still not folded again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-50.md:13:"I'll tell you a thing I didn't know until today," said the registrar. He was holding a volume up to the lamp to read its board. "Twelve years I've had this office, and I've always believed the best day a file can have is the day you close it. You tie the last box. You write the count on the lid. It's done, and it's right, and you go home." He slid the volume into its place and squared it with one finger. "It isn't. The best day a file can have is the day somebody who wishes it ill opens every box, with a good mind and a whole morning, and can't find anything wrong."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-50.md:61:"*Shattered* was written in at that table, by somebody holding a pen, over an older word. And the older word meant unbound. Four articles along, somebody took the old system's only way of judging a person by what that person could actually show, instead of by which box they would go into, and turned it into a road out of itself, and put a sign up telling everybody to use the road." Her hands had tightened on the wood. "And the old way works. That's what I keep coming back to. It isn't a museum piece. It's working tonight, in this house, on the record, with seals on it. I sat at that table from the second bell to the seventh and watched it bear weight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-50.md:63:*Shattered* was the word in brackets on Cael's own registry sheet, beside his number, in the Denvash station's hand. He waited until he was sure she had finished.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-50.md:151:*1. What held. Sound law, said standing up, into their own record, by the one person on their side of the table who reads to the end. A year of work. Two academies. Four grey boxes and three hundred and eleven sealed sheets. Bracken's index. Withrow's twenty years. And Karis, who spent eleven weeks of our last term at Greyvane trying to prove that nothing had happened to a clause, and proved it, and then let Bracken take every trace of her hand out of the file so that the file could stand without her. That's what today was for.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-50.md:157:He stopped there with the pen lifted. Down the bluff, the dark river kept on going. At the hour, down at the ferry landing, the two grey coats changed under their lamp on the bell, as they had changed every four hours since the bridge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:5:Halcenvane stood along the top of a bluff above the river Ost, a long grey academy of halls and tiers and covered walks, and for twelve weeks three parts in four of it had been empty. The Crown yard at that hour belonged to nobody. The porter's boards stood bare along the long wall, wiped down for the recess, with the ghost of last season's chalk still grey in the grain of the wood. Mist lay on the tiers. At the far end, by the wash-house, Brom had his shirt off in the cold and was working through his sheet at the striking post, slowly, just as Rooke had written it for him: nineteen exercises, round three, four mornings in the week. He had not missed a morning since the recess began, and he would not have mentioned it if he had been paid to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:21:The burst was hers, in a way. Lira's Path was Wind, and she had been bursting across floors in front of him for three years, since long before either of them had heard of this bluff. Cael had no Path at all that the registry could name. What he had instead was a short list of things he had watched other people do until, at a cost, they had become things he could do too, and her burst had been the first of them. He had paid for it ever since, every time, in the same coin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:99:*The wing's counter: open every working day of the recess, with the same clerk's name against every day on the sheet. Nobody on this bluff has said a word about that sheet. Everybody I live with has read it to the bottom.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:101:*Bracken: three hundred season sheets certified across the recess, every one in his own hand. "That's the house's year," he said over the last of them. "Strangers will read it now."*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:111:*The push I took from the Bronze at Ardenmere. Never shown on this bluff; flat at every plate they've put me on. Four blows a sitting, paid in the right shoulder.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:161:"Four. There are four meets in this region. Every standing earned at any of the four is added to the house's total, and the houses whose totals cross the line go on to the finals. This cycle the finals are at Norhold, a crossroads city about four times the size of the town under this bluff, which once in every three years hosts the oldest contest between chartered houses on this continent.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:163:"Eighteen." She said it with no weight on it at all. "This house has not been there in eighteen years. I have read the file on the last side we sent. It was made of the people the house liked best, and the continent did not like them nearly as much. I don't intend to repeat the experiment."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:175:"Inside the week," said Karis, following his eyes. "He'll file inside the week. The regional office takes entries until the close of the next, and Bracken would sooner swallow his own pen than file on a closing day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:199:*Cael. See demonstration provision, enrollment basis, filed.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:225:"For Ephram, that was singing." She turned back to the board, and her eyes went to her own line, *Lira, Wind, Iron Rank One*, and stayed there a moment with her hand on her coat over the place where the certificate had lived since the anteroom. Then she looked away from it on purpose, before anybody could catch her admiring it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:229:Karis was reading the selection standards again from the top, and her pencil had stopped two-thirds of the way down. The line under it said that the registrar would file the house's entry, together with a certified copy of its enrollment record, complete and current, not later than the close of intake.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:239:Lira's name went up at the foot of the Iron column, under a column of strangers and over nothing, with the short new word beside it in the porter's round hand. Brom's went up in Rooke's cohort's column at the end of the Blade board, third, where the certified standings had put it at the green table on the last morning of term. Fiske's went up near the head of the Copper with the same six letters it had always had. Cael watched Lira's name until the porter had moved his ladder twice more, and then went on watching the porter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:245:On the way back he went past the assessment wing. The sheet that had hung beside Gault's door all recess, the one with the same name against every working day, was gone. In its place hung the term's counter rota, five clerks' initials spread across the week in the usual way, the teaching assistant's among them on three days out of seven. The recess sheet would be in a file somewhere now, in the wing's own order, where it would never be read again by anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:67:"Tomorrow you're going to walk into Gault's room and be careful for three hours, and you've never once not been careful in all the time I've known you, and I doubt you've the least idea how it shows from across a floor. It shows like that. So don't hide what it costs. Let it cost you. Breathe hard after the board, and shake the arm out after the plate where they can watch you shake it, and let your fourth run be a fourth run, a little worse than the first, the way fourth runs always are. Pull together for the fifth, and make the pulling visible. Any trainer alive knows a working body when he sees one. Give them that, and they'll stop looking for the other thing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:79:"The wing posts its order for a semester sitting beside the Magister's door, under the calibration card, and I'm fairly sure I'm the only person who has ever read it from top to bottom, the Magister included." She wrote as she spoke. "One, the board. Two, the frame, read only, with your back to the rail. Three, read and move together. Four, the plate. The last two are the first two over again, with an hour of oak in your legs. That's what you did at the baseline. This time the read stops being a trial of its own and gets carried inside the third and the cold runs, which leaves the second slot empty, and the posted sheet says so in so many words: *Two, at the enrollee's placing.* Anything already on your record that you choose to show goes there, or nowhere." She chalked a short dash beside the figure two. "The fifth slot is new: the heaviest weight on the rail, your square brought in close, and a tray of chalk dust beside it so that the clerk can read your feet from the dust instead of from your account of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:81:"The weight I took on the plate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:85:Next to each row went the baseline's figures, small. Four free on the board, the fifth billed, the sixth wide and slow. Eleven calls in twelve off the frame. Seven clear in eight on the combined, at weight three. The plate held low four times, *and not eight*. Then she drew two chalk brackets down the right-hand side of the slate, one above the other, with a gap between them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:95:"The width isn't the hard part. The hard part is doing it on every instrument at once, for a man who's told you to your face that he'd rather have six runs that agree than one wonder and five that don't, while all the time you carry something nobody else in the room knows about." She looked at him. "For three hours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:103:Karis chose to rehearse the plate first, out of the posted order, because it was the one line on the slate that was not allowed to move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:105:There being no plate in the common room, she handed him the residence's book of hall rules, which was bound in boards nearly an inch thick, and had him hold it out at shoulder height on his right arm, braced the plain way. Brom hit it from the floor, a short heavy sitting blow that went through the book and up the arm and into the shoulder and stopped there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:121:"I hadn't seen that coming, and it would have had you." Her chalk rapped the lower bracket. "For two weeks you've planned against the roof. Don't go over; don't become a question. Now look where you just went. Under. That plate line sits in Gault's own log in Gault's own hand with *four, not eight* beside it, because you said it before he asked, and anybody in that room can lay tomorrow's line over it in the time it takes to blink. Go in frightened and clamp down on everything by feel, and you won't merely scrape the floor; you'll go clean through it. Then the record says that a boy got worse over a whole supervised term while the Compact sat and watched it happen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:125:"So it was never *hold back*," Karis said. "It's *land on the number*. The plate doesn't move by so much as a graduation, and everything else moves exactly as far as a term would move it. That's a great deal harder than holding back, and it's the actual job."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:155:"Tomorrow I hold from waking, the read drops to its stub, and that run can't happen." He was glad his voice stayed even. "Whatever I put on Gault's oak in the third trial, I'll be putting there with my best instrument turned nearly off. He'll write it down. It'll be honest, and it'll fall short of what I've got."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:223:"That's half. The other half's less welcome. Tomorrow's about being measured right, and everything tonight has been about that, and I've helped, and it's right. But you fight, Cael. Whatever they lay their instruments against had better still be you. Clamp everything down and give Gault a careful boy sitting neatly on Karis's floor, and yes, you'll pass. You'll also come out of that room a little less. Then you'll do it again next half-year, and the half-year after that, and every time a little less."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:269:Only one thing he owned met all three. It was the Ember, Karis's ignition point, which sat in a single thin half-page at the back of the Greyvane transcript, where anybody who paid six marks could read it and nobody ever had. No instrument in the wing had measured it and no panel on the bluff had seen it, and on paper it was the least remarkable thing he possessed: a point of fire at contact, gone in the instant it was made. The wing put its instruments on whatever an enrollee laid before it, and the placing was his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:61:Cael had read it in the profiles stack, and Brom had read it twice. *Rank Four on paper. Fights like a Seven. Buried.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:237:They were not there for his rank. Iron Rank Six was a good line on a certificate, and near the top of the seeding. But there were other Sixes in the bracket, and a Seven, and nobody stood in a knot at their rope. The knot was there for how the work looked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:279:The inn had given the delegation the long table under the stair, and the house had filled it in the order it always did. Rooke sat at the end with the coaching file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:289:"Meaning it's honest." Brom tore the loaf in two and passed half to Lira without looking. "Half the bracket's in the wrong column. The fifth man's in Copper because his house needed a fifth man. She's in Copper because somebody wants her there. I'm in Copper because nobody's filed for me." He chewed. "Everybody on that floor knows it. Nobody pretends. I like a room where nobody pretends."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:303:"He wasn't coaching," he said at last. "He was keeping. She's worth more to him as a Four who wins than a Seven who wins, because a Four who wins every fair-day purse in the hills is a living, and a Seven just gets matched against other Sevens." He picked up the bread again. "I'd like to know who files for her. I'd like it to be somebody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:305:Rooke, at the end of the table, took out his pencil and wrote something in the margin of the coaching file, and then, after a moment, wrote something else under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:307:Cael thought about the sheet Rooke had marked in the profiles stack: *Somebody's keeping her low on purpose.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:5:Karis had spread the charter's three copies down its length in a row, open at three different places, with the certified transcription at the head of the row like a magistrate at the head of a bench. Beside them lay a stack of clean foolscap, four pencils sharpened to the same length, the memorandum with its short-inked type, and the grey notebook, open at the page where the clause had been copied out on the afternoon before the recess with one word beside it. Bracken had brought the copies down himself after the last bell, had set them out in the order she asked for, had told the night porter that the hall was in use and why, and had gone home. He would come back, he said, when there was something to check. He did not like to watch people draft. It made him want to correct them before they had finished being wrong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:35:Cael heard her before he saw her. Nobody on the bluff had ever heard Karis run; she walked everywhere at the same even pace, as though the distance between two places were a sum she had already done. But there were feet on the residence stair taking it two at a time, and a door banging on the floor below, and Brom's voice on the landing saying "What—" in the tone of a man flattening himself against a wall. Then she was in Cael's doorway with Bracken's transcription held against her chest in both arms and her hair come half out of its pins.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:97:It said that the enrollment record was true, and had been certified so, without omission, as the charter required. It said that the house stood ready to comply with the charter's mandatory language in every particular. And it said that if the regional registrar should determine that a true enrollment record must be altered before it could be accepted, the house respectfully requested that instruction in writing, over the registrar's name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:119:A long desk bare of everything but the work in hand. A wall of grey file cases, each labelled in the same hand. Above the cases, framed and glazed and hung at the height of a person's eyes, the house's own charter, the founding one, its ink gone brown, hung there not as an ornament but where she could point at it. One window looked down the bluff to the river. The lamp on the desk was already lit, and the folder with the four pages lay beside it, open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:147:He looked up at the framed charter above the file cases, the brown ink and the old seal, and Withrow saw him look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:151:Withrow sat back in her chair. The lamp made a small bright room of the desk and left the file cases in shadow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:193:The response had gone down the bluff on the morning courier of the Sixth-day. After that nothing came back up, and the regional registrar did not write. The memorandum's fourteen days went on running down on the calendar behind Bracken's counter, one day a morning, each crossed off in his small neat stroke. In its turn the fourteenth was crossed off like the rest, and nothing went back down the hill to meet it. The house had answered once, in four pages, and Bracken did not resubmit; a true record, he told Brom when Brom asked, did not get any truer for being sent twice. The coats at the ferry landing and the road's foot changed on the bell, two and two, and Karis's marbled book got a line a day, and every line was the line above it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:197:Rooke did not post the first meet's bout targets. Every other week on the calendar board had its targets pinned beside it in Rooke's hand, and the first meet's week had a clean space the size of a sheet where they would go. Bracken's clerk opened a travel file for the delegation and wrote out its cover in full, every name and wagon and stable fee, and left the date line blank. The wagoner came up from the town to look at the axles and went down again without being told which morning to come back. And at the counter on the Fifth-day, when Cael brought the week's coursework, the teaching assistant took it, read the head of it, and laid it in the tray face down, square to the others, and said "Mm," and that was all, as it should have been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:233:"Who found the schedule? Who sat up three nights in a stone hall with the porter bringing her tea? Who certified the record, and sanded it, and put it in a wallet, and watched the courier take it?" She took one hand out of her coat and held it up, empty. "Who decided to put Halcenvane in a tournament in the first place, a year before you'd ever heard the word *clause*?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:5:It was pinned to the top of the file with a brass pin, a narrow strip of the Compact's buff paper with a printed column of offices down its left side and a ruled space beside each for a clerk's initials. *Received. Logged. Assessed. Referred for advisory.* Against the first three stood three sets of initials he knew, the ordinary clerks of the registry floor below his. Against the fourth, in a narrow, sloping hand he did not know at all, stood two letters he could not place, and after them, written out in full, his own name and title.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:9:His room was high in the Compact's house, up a stair that the builders had made too steep and the clerks had worn too smooth. Its one window gave on a court where a man in a porter's apron fed pigeons every morning at the same hour from the same paper bag. Vastin had come to regard the pigeons and the porter as the most reliable instrument in the building. The room held a table, two chairs, and four shelves of files that were his only in the sense that he was the officer who would be asked about them. It was a little after the second bell. His left hand, which had begun some years ago to dislike mornings, lay on the table beside the bundle and declined to be hurried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:13:Then he did a thing he had done all his working life with a file that had come up from below: he read the defence before the charge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:21:He had seen perhaps five such paragraphs in forty years. It asked for nothing. It offered compliance and requested, if compliance should require a true record to be made false, that the instruction come in writing over a name. A document that asked for nothing was a document whose author had already counted every door out of the room and shut each one, gently, without locking it. He did not know whose paragraph it was. He thought he might guess. A registrar at the top of a bluff above a river had kept an index that his own counsel had wished to find fault with and had not, and he had written to that registrar privately afterward, in four sentences, because good work should be told it is good. This paragraph had the same bones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:31:He knew the enrollee. That was the plain fact of it, and he did not pretend otherwise to himself. He had sat four feet from the boy in a room twelve feet by fourteen, some months back, and put eleven questions to him, and left a twelfth where it lay. He had watched him on a failing frame. He had written a line in his own hand into the file the Compact kept on him, and signed it with his name entire. *Classification error is unlikely. Continue observation.* Anybody above him who read that file had read that line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:53:He pinned the advisory to the file, put the struck draft in his own drawer, and took the bundle down the too-steep stair to the dispatch room himself, before the noon post closed. The dispatch clerk logged it and gave him a slip. He put the slip in his coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:61:The ordinary silence after an advisory had a shape he knew as well as he knew the porter and his pigeons: an acknowledgment from the receiving office, a file marked closed somewhere below with a clerk's initials, a line in the weekly digest. He had read that silence ten thousand times. This was not it. The advisory was received. He knew it was received, because the dispatch slip in his coat said so. After that, nothing. No acknowledgment came back up the chain. The file did not reappear in the digest. The two letters on the fourth line of the routing slip were not on any other slip that crossed his table in the next nine days, and he looked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:63:On the tenth day, among the morning's papers, he found a printed meet card from a regional host, filed for information, routinely, as all such cards were filed. He read down it until he came to the fifth line of one house's block. Then he laid it on the table beside the routing slip, and sat with his left hand open on his knee, watching the porter in the court below feed his pigeons from the same paper bag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:71:On the Fifth-day the answer came up the bluff, and it did not look like an answer at all. It looked like a timetable.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:95:"So we were printed," said Bracken. "Which in my trade is better." He laid the card in a clean folder and wrote on the folder's spine in his small upright hand. "Somebody upstream wrote something very short and very good to make that happen. I'd have liked to file it." He shut the folder. "A month's pay to read it. Two to know who sent the question to the man who wrote it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:107:Cael saw it in the order he had watched it stop. Rooke's first-meet targets went up on the calendar board in the clean space that had waited there for a fortnight, a full sheet in his small hard hand with a line for each of the five, and the line against Cael's name still said nothing but a dash. Bracken's clerk took the delegation's travel file out of its drawer and wrote a date on the cover sheet, at last, and blotted it, and showed it to Bracken, who looked at it and nodded as though a date were a thing that might still be got wrong. The wagoner was sent for, and came up from the town in his leather apron, and was told which morning, and went down again whistling. In the wing a new stack of the buff travel forms appeared at the counter's end, already filled in, five of them, in the round copying hand, each waiting only for a competitor's signature at its foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:193:"I've been in this trade a long time," he said. "I've seen floors bought. I've seen brackets drawn by men who owed money to the houses they were drawing. I've seen a certificate say Copper about a girl who could take any Iron in her region apart in four exchanges." Lira did not move. "This thing I've never seen lean. Not once. Rich houses have tried, for as long as anybody's kept books. Chief Adjudicator Umber keeps the calibration, and has done since before most of you were Kindled, and I've never heard a word against him that wasn't sour grapes from a house that lost." He tapped the table with one knuckle. "There are perhaps three honest instruments on this continent, and you're looking at one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:219:He could not. Not because he did not know what he could do; he knew to the burst. Because he did not know which of the things he could do he would let five strangers see. Execution, for a boy who ran the public suite at its documented rates and not one burst past them: clean, probably. Control: that depended on the man in front of him, and on whether the read, which looked from the stand like peculiar timing, would look to a trained panel like command. Effect: what the performance did. He could make a performance do almost anything a single exchange needed, if he spent what he owned. He would not spend it. So the figure in each box was not his; it was a choice. He sat with the pen over the grid and found he could write any number he liked in it, within a band, and that the band was his to choose, and that the instrument would never know.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:223:Then, because the habit was older than the doubt, he sat on the bench and priced the others. Lira, from what he had watched on the oak all recess: execution near nine, the cleanest bursts on the hill; control eight, nine against anyone slower than her; effect nine, because she made people spend. Twenty-six, perhaps. A Silver-touched figure for a girl whose certificate had said Copper not long ago. Brom: execution seven, because he would never look quick; control nine, because nobody moved Brom anywhere he did not agree to go; effect eight. Twenty-four. Ephram he had been rating in his head for a fortnight, and came out at twenty-four too. He did not price Karis. He did not know yet what an Ember fighter's effect looked like to a panel trained to Silver Five, and he found, to his surprise, that he wanted to be surprised by it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:233:*And I'll only ever let it see what I choose to show it. I ruled a grid on the back of Rooke's card tonight and found I could fill it in with any figure in a band, and that the band was mine to pick. The fairest instrument on the continent, and I've already decided how to manage it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:45:He had six free on the sprung oak. This floor was not the oak, it was the second floor's boards, a little stiffer, a little less forgiving, and he knew from a month of mornings that six here was really five. The record said he had a burst that carried twenty feet and a read that looked like peculiar timing. The record had nothing in it about a burst shut off halfway through its paying, or about a contact that took a blow and did not give it back, or about a plate. So tonight, in front of a panel of five, he would fight with what the record held, at the rates the record held it, and not one thing more. It was the doctrine, and he had chosen it, and he had never before had to keep to it in front of people holding pencils.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:183:"You're Copper on your certificate," said Rooke. "You rated twenty-four tonight against an Iron Rank One with a panel of five, two of whom have never once said a kind word about anybody." The reserves did not look at each other. "The Standard doesn't read certificates. That's the whole of what I've been telling you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:225:Par was not a number, though he had thought it was one when he sat in the map room and copied out the bands: thirty, twenty-eight, twenty-two. It was a way of moving, a thing you could see from a bench if you knew how to look: clean, and in command, and doing something to the man in front of you. Lira had been near it tonight. Brom had been nearer than his certificate would ever admit. And Cael had been at a table's distance from it all evening, on purpose, choosing his distance mark by mark, while five people wrote down honestly what he had chosen to give them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:227:The instrument had not been wrong about him. That was what sat in his stomach. It had been perfectly right about everything he showed it. It had measured, to two-thirds of a point, a boy who was not there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:233:Karis sat over the tournament charter every night that week, late, in the small common room. She always read a governing document before it governed anybody. Cael sat across the table from her with the observation notebook and Rooke's forty-one profiles, which he was learning like coursework.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:235:It was the night after the mock bouts, and five days remained. The fire was down to embers. Brom had gone to bed and Lira had gone to bed after him, threatening to come back down if anybody laughed. Karis had a lamp to herself and the certified transcription open in front of her. She was well into the back of it, past the schedules where she had found the exhibition provision, into the oldest parts of all: the founding articles, printed in a type nobody used any more, in the vocabulary of a century nobody alive had seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:241:He did not look up. He counted instead, from habit, while he turned one of Rooke's profiles over and read the back of it, which was blank. Twenty. Thirty. Forty. At forty he heard her pencil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:259:He sat a while longer with Rooke's profiles in front of him and did not read them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:267:He had not argued for it, or asked for it. Other people's honest words had carried him to it: a clerk's record, a dead board's rule, a girl who read schedules, a registrar's mirror and a chancellor's seal. And somebody, somewhere above all of them, had written an answer to a question and let it go through without a word to anybody on the bluff. He did not know who that was. He would very likely never know. Some silence up there had shaped itself round his name, and he could feel the edges of it, a draught from a door he could not see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:269:And he had an instrument waiting for him now that he wanted honestly, more than he had wanted any number in his life, and that he had already decided, in a cold room with a borrowed card, to manage.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:271:He opened the window. The fountain went on below in the court, the one continuous sentence it had been saying all recess. Beyond the residence roofs the bluff fell away into the dark. The valley lay under no moon, and the town's lamps had gone out street by street until only the ferry lamp was left, small and steady at the landing, with two grey coats under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:5:It was not a large thing: a plain circlet of beaten copper on a brass mount, dented once on the rim where some champion long ago had dropped it, with a plate riveted to the mount that had been riveted on twice because the first rivets had pulled. The porter polished it every term whether anybody had won it or not. Since the final it had acquired a ring of dried tea on its mount where Brom had put a cup down without looking, and a smear of chalk from Lira's thumb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:7:The evening of the fifth found Lira at that table, the Iron certificate folded square under her hand, studying the crown.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:39:"Where else? It's not mine to keep in a box. I've left that bracket, and it won't see me again." She did not turn round. "But nothing else I own says it out loud, with a plate riveted on: the bracket had me wrong. You don't put a thing like that in a drawer. Prizes go to winners. That's what I got for having to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:47:*It was a plan. It took a whole engine of parts: a season on a public board, a final in front of witnesses, a registrar willing to certify the record before the result was known, an officer of record willing to file, an assessor at his elbow to witness the filing, and a Compact evaluation seat sitting in the second row of the north tier while it all happened. Six pieces. Fiske had drawn all six for her in four sentences at a standings board. She had been two years in the same box. And she handed the plan to the one fighter she thought could use it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:67:Beside the board, by the second bell, somebody from the registrar's office had pinned up a copy of the certificate, as the house did with every change of tier, so that anyone who wanted to know why a name had moved could read the reason.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:157:"Don't." He took the accession book from his clerk and ran an eye down the column. "It's filing. I've three hundred season sheets to certify before the selection file can be opened, and a recess to do them in, and every one of them will be read by strangers. Correct filing is all I have to offer anybody this side of the river." He handed the book back. "Carry on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:167:The first part of it was the ordinary business of shutting a house for the recess, and she gave it the attention of a woman counting linen. The roof over the north hall was poor and would be seen to. The wing's frame was being rebuilt, and the man who built frames had been written to, at length. The porters were thanked one by one, by name, and the porter of the Crown yard went very red. Brom shifted on the bench. Lira, beside Cael, sat with one hand inside her coat, resting flat over the pocket where the folded certificate lived, as though it might otherwise get up and leave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:171:"Here is what the Compact found," said Withrow. "It was filed in the first days of this month and reached the registrar on the ninth. I will read you its four lines, because they are short, and because you earned them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:195:"Two: every sheet written about you this year, the ladder's, the season's, the certified results, goes into the file the selectors read, and strangers will read it, and a season chalked on a board goes into the record, whether or not you lived as though it would.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:209:Lira shook her head. "It goes in as a final. Against the champion. Lost by one beat in the fourth exchange on a mended seam." Her palm settled over the certificate in her coat. "If I were a stranger reading it, I'd want to know who you were."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:197:*Note for the season. There's more than one instrument on a meet floor. The panel's is only the one that writes things down. Every man who stands across the rope from me is measuring too, and most of them only measure. The good ones read back.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:223:He declined all six. He gave the panel what it needed to rate: clean feet, a read that looked like decent timing, two touches on the right lines.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:239:*Margin note. On the night of the map room I wrote, in this book, that what I wanted most was to stand in front of a fair instrument and be told the truth by it. Today I stood in front of it twice, and both times I decided what it would be allowed to see. I filled in every box before the panel picked up its chalk.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:259:He stopped, and looked down at the coaching file in his hands. His own exhibition notes were clipped inside its cover, a single sheet in his small hard hand, and he looked at that sheet for longer than he had looked at anything else that evening.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:265:"Twenty years of coaching," said Rooke, "and that's the first time I've had to say it to anybody. I don't know yet if it's praise. I don't know what a coach is for, in a sentence like that." He shut the file. "Wagons at first light."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:273:The road behind was full. There were other houses' wagons, three of them, in loose file, and a carter, and a lantern bobbing at the back of the line where somebody was walking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:283:*First meet. The companions are winning in brackets with their names on them. I'm winning in a category invented before the categories. Everyone's being measured. I'm being — accommodated. Filed under: things the season will have to answer.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:37:The delegation had sorted itself between the two wagons without anyone giving an order. Rooke rode up beside the wagoner of the first, with the profiles in a satchel on his knees and his hat tipped down over his eyes, and did not appear to sleep. Seln sat behind him on the box of files.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:83:"Certified," said the clerk. "She checked." He slid the credential across. "Next house."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:89:*A girl I'll never meet paid her own money for a copy of my hearing and read it aloud at supper. Nobody carried that story into this town by talk. It came in on certified paper, through a copying office, at the public rate. People say rumour runs ahead of a man. Mine doesn't. My record does.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:103:Seln had the delegation's credential file. It was his work on the roster: *records and floor scheduling*. He laid the file open on the green cloth in front of the senior officer, a woman with grey hair cut short and a pencil she held like a scalpel, and stepped back half a pace.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:105:Cael stood at his shoulder, because the file was partly about him and Rooke had said he should see it handled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:107:She checked the first four names against her list. She did it fast, with the pencil's tip moving down the extract's columns and back up to the file, a tick in her margin for each. Lira, a tick. Brom, a tick. Karis, a tick. Ephram, a tick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:117:Seln already had it. The certified extract of the enrollment record was in the file's front pocket, with a thin paper tab at the provision's page. He drew it out and put it into her hand, and stepped back again, and his face did nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:127:"You'll carry that to every table of the season," she said. "Every one. Don't let it leave the file. They won't be quicker about it at the next meet than I was at this one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:139:Seln put the extract back in its pocket, tab outward, and closed the file. On the way back along the porch he walked a half-pace ahead with the file under his arm, and did not look round. At the porch steps he went one way, toward the stewards' table and the floor schedules, and Cael went the other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:157:"Demonstration performances," he said. "Scheduled into the gaps in the card. Opponents by challenge, filed here, voluntary on both sides. Rated by the standing panel of the floor against the Standard, figure only, entered in the meet record. Five exchanges at most, which is the old form; two touches ends it; short of two, the one with more; level, the figure. No standing. No points. No weight in the house total." He lowered the slip. "So you fight whoever asks. And nobody wins anything."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:161:"Two." The steward sniffed. "The ganger filed his while your wagoner was still taking the horses out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:191:He did not know yet how the ganger fought. Rooke's forty-one profiles did not run to the quarry guild's fair-day men, and the form told him only that the man could write and wanted to see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:203:Rooke had given her the opening slot's terms at the meal. The draw had done what he had said it would do and put the foot of the Iron seeding against its head: the river academy's first entry, an Iron Rank Seven, a Stone Path, broad and patient, whose margin on the profiles sheet said *honest; slow to read; slower to change*. She had read it twice and handed it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:273:The Stone stood on his chalk, breathing, looking at the floor in front of him. Then he did what Rooke's profile had said he was slow to do. He changed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:17:"Every bracket at every meet is seeded off paper," he said. "Your formal tier puts you in a bracket. Your formal rank puts you on a line in it. The host sets the draw so the top rank meets the bottom rank first and the middle meets the middle, and nobody at the host's table ever looks at you, only at the line on your certificate." He looked at them now, one at a time. "So the paper puts Lira at the foot of the Iron seeding, because she's Rank One, and the first thing the draw will give her is somebody from the top of it. The paper puts Karis in the middle. It puts Ephram near the top, which is where he belongs. And it puts Brom in a Copper bracket, which is where he doesn't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:119:At the water break the squad drifted to it, and Rooke lifted the stone off the stacks and let them look. Profiles. Forty-one of them, one to a sheet, a fighter to a page: every name entered at the first meet in the Iron and Copper brackets, with a Path, a rank, a house, a record where the house had one, and down the margin in Rooke's hand a verdict of three words or fewer. *Quick hands, slow feet.* *Honest; telegraphs left.* *Lose to her once and she studies you.* *Hits above his rank.* One sheet had only a question mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:123:Cael read the Copper stack over Brom's shoulder before he read his own. Halfway down it was a sheet that Rooke had marked more heavily than the others. A fair-day fighter from a market house in the hills, Iron Skin like Brom, Copper Rank Four on her certificate and a record of exactly four bouts, every one of them won, every one of them short. Rooke's margin said: *Rank Four on paper. Fights like a Seven. Buried.* And under it, in smaller letters, as if Rooke had come back to it later: *Somebody's keeping her low on purpose.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:149:Fenmark: the house that had Kindled her at fourteen and read her Copper and sent her out into the world with six letters on a certificate. It had a fighter in the region's Iron bracket this year, a second-year, and Rooke had given him four words: *Good. Schooled. Expects respect.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:5:It was a courier on a grey, with a leather satchel strapped behind the saddle. He carried the wool town's meet record to every house that had fought there, and he was in a hurry to be rid of it. He came into the inn's front room with the cold still on him and asked for the Halcenvane delegation, and the innkeeper pointed at the long table by the fire. The courier did not go to Rooke. He went to the end of the table where the files were, because the satchel's tag said *records*, and laid a sealed packet in front of Seln.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:13:"The town's Bronze filed a protest," she said. "This morning. Before our wagons had crossed the second ferry."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:43:"Good." She handed the ruling back along the table to Seln, who took it without looking up and laid it in the travel file. Then Karis wrote one line in the grey notebook, short, and turned the page before Cael could read it upside down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:53:Rooke had said nothing at all since asking for the ruling to be read. He sat at the end of the table with the coaching file closed under his hand and his eyes on the fire, and when the talk had moved on to other things, Cael saw him open the file, find a page near the back, and write something on it that was longer than one line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:61:The instrument had not been fooled, quite. It had measured what it was shown, and then the people around it had looked up from the slates and noticed what they had not been shown. *Appears to have won without extending himself.* An adjudicator did not write *appears* by accident. She had chosen the word as carefully as the validator had chosen hers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:89:"Nothing torn." Rooke took the coaching file out from under his arm and opened it against the door frame. "Everything taxed. Four bursts in three exchanges, one over the rate, against a man who made you work for each of them. That's what the extra one cost you. It's the bill for unpicking his wall." He wrote. "Two days at half work. Then you'll be sound for the mill town."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:93:"Half work. Properly. I'll know if you cheat it." He shut the file. "One extra burst is a fair price for a wall like that. Two in one bout is a habit, and you can't afford the habit yet. I'll tell you when you can."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:199:Gault offered him the annotated manual on the third evening, holding it out across a waystation table. Cael thanked him and did not take it. He wanted to watch the instrument work, first, with his own eyes, before he read how it was supposed to. That was the order he trusted. Gault seemed to understand this perfectly, and put the book back in the case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:3:The circular came up the bluff road on the morning courier, two days after the wagons got home, and Rooke chalked it onto the cohort board in the covered walk before the second bell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:67:Brom laughed, and so did Cael. Ephram came in late with his new file book under his arm and wanted to know what was funny, and was told, and did not think it was funny at all, which made Brom laugh again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:201:Bracken was in the records hall when Cael went to collect the meet records for the travel file. He stood behind the long counter in his black coat with his sleeves held back by bands, sanding a page, and he finished sanding it before he looked up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:207:He took a canvas pouch from under the counter and laid it on the wood. It was grey and worn soft, with the academy's seal stamped on the flap and a brass ring for the lock. "Certified copies of anything the meet's clerks want from us. The chancellor's letters. And yours, if anybody writes to you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:227:She had the page open on the common-room table that evening when he came in with the meet records for her to check against the travel file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:267:The wagons went down to the ferry at first light on the Seventh-day, and the two coats at the landing watched them go, as two coats had watched everything go from that landing for longer than anybody on the bluff liked to count.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:289:That evening at the waystation the talk at supper was about the horses and the ice, and whether the hill coach would truly find the quarry town, and whether Ephram's new file book would survive the damp. It was cheerful talk and a little louder than usual, and Cael listened to it through the whole meal and did not hear the name once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:303:Seln was sitting in the second wagon with the travel file open on his knees, as he had sat for ten days, copying something from one sheet to another in a hand too small to read from the tailboard. He did not look up at the hill or at Lira. Once, when the wagon jolted, he lifted the pen clear of the page and waited for the wheel to find the road again, and then went on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:3:Hesk's answer came up the bluff on the twelfth, by the evening coach, which was fast even for Hesk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:13:Hesk could not know what any Archmarshal had written. Nobody on the bluff knew that, and nobody in Hesk's workshop at the far end of the country was going to know it either. What Hesk had was Cael's own letter: the stamp, Gault's last lines copied out, and one paragraph about a room twelve feet by fourteen and a man with eleven questions who had walked up to the edge of a twelfth and left it alone. Out of that, at a bench, with his spectacles on his forehead, Hesk had guessed what a man like that would put on paper afterward. It was a grandfather's guess. It was not evidence of anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:21:It was in a hand he knew from a circuit rating card at Ardenmere, small and square, a ledger-keeper's hand. The cover had been readdressed twice: once from Greyvane, where the letter had gone first and missed him, and once by somebody at the Greyvane gate who had crossed out the old line and written *Halcenvane, the bluff above Ostrand* in a careful clerkly print. Most of a year had gone by on its road to him. He carried it unopened as far as the covered walk and stood under the arches with it a while before he broke the wafer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:23:*Read your account three times. Filed it with your record, where it belongs. The offer of a proper session stands whenever the road runs through Ardenmere again. The ledger doesn't forget. Neither do I.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:43:The wing was half packed. The instrument woman had sheeted the gauges, and the frame stood bare with its weights racked and padlocked for the recess, and somebody had taken the pendulum off its hook so that it would not swing all recess for nobody. Gault's door stood open. Through it Cael could see him at his desk with a new calendar spread in front of him, ruling lines on it with a straightedge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:55:Cael read it the way he read every notice, all the way down and back up, and let nothing reach his face. A wing with no students on its floors would need its allocations made all the same, for the cohorts coming back and the sittings already booked, and somebody would have to sit at the counter through the empty weeks and make them. Nobody on the bluff would find anything to remark in it. Of all the notices on that wall it was the dullest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:63:"Next term's sittings." Gault held the calendar out for him. Every half-month had its line, and on the line for the provision's half-yearly evaluation a date had been entered in Gault's hand, and beneath it: *Panel of record: Gault, with two. Measured against: baseline (entry), and the note of the twentieth of Reaping.* "Same rules as before. Same instruments, checked on Fourth-days. The frame will have a new second notch, and the man who builds them is coming upriver in person to fit it, which I'm told he finds humiliating." The calendar went back to the desk. "I'll measure you against yourself again. That's all the provision asks, and it's all I'll do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:95:*This year: held back at the plate both times. It's on the Ardenmere circuit record, not on this wing's, and to show it would have been a second surprise. Cost: one flat line on a semester note read in front of the Compact. Gault put his own name behind that flat line with a delegation listening, and never knew what was under it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:128:*Here is what I hold toward the next question, all of it. A gap on Prynn's shelf where a volume never came back. One retired word, copied into two notebooks, that neither of us can read. A designation on my file that nobody I've ever asked can trace to a desk or a hand. And, somewhere over all of it, one note in one file, in a hand I'd wager signs its whole name.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:130:*Whatever it believes it has settled about me, and the reason it was built to settle such things. That's next year's question. I came up the bluff road with a smaller question than this. This one I came by honestly, and I've paid for all of it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:142:A table had been carried out under the north arch and set with a green cloth, and Bracken sat at it in his gown with the season's ladder book open in front of him and a box of blank certificate sheets at his elbow. The porter stood at the long board with his ladder. He read each column aloud from the top, name and standing and record, in his round slow voice, and at the table Bracken checked every line against the book, and wrote it fair on a clean sheet, and signed the foot of the sheet and pressed the registrar's seal beside his name. Then the porter wiped that column off the board with his rag, and moved his ladder, and started on the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:146:"Every one," said Karis. "They go into the selection file. Withrow said so. The registrar certifies them before they go in, so that nobody can say afterward they were copied wrong." She watched Bracken's pen. "Three hundred sheets, near enough, across the recess. He'll do them all himself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:190:At dusk on term's last evening the four of them climbed to the wall behind the second quadrangle. They had first sat there in the third week. Below the coping the bluff fell away, and the valley lay open under a long low light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:202:Lira's legs hung out over the drop. She sat with her boots in the air above a hundred feet of nothing and her hands flat on the stone either side of her, the hip quiet at last, and she was Iron-tier now, by the account of an instrument that had taken four seconds to give it. Half a mile behind her a copper crown stood retired on a shelf, between a lamp and a pile of drafts that must on no account be thrown away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:204:Brom sat beside her, turning a pebble over and over in the fingers of the hand that had been strapped. He was Copper on his certificate and would be until somebody filed for him. Rooke's cohort, quicker than any other on the river, had him third. One beat had cost him the final; a season and two losses had paid for mending the seam behind that beat. None of it showed on him. He was watching a barge come down toward the bend with the face of a man estimating its load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:228:They let that be for a while. Out on the water the barge made the bend, and somebody on its deck gave two short notes on a horn, *heard you*, to nobody any of them could see; a moment later, from the far bank, somebody answered. The sun went down behind the far hills and the river took the colour of it, first gold and then pewter, and the bluff's long shadow went out across the water toward the ferry lamp. It was the kind of light such evenings end in, at the end of such years: years that had asked more of each of them than any of them had known to expect.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:242:He said, "And somewhere in a file, the most careful man I've ever met wrote that I'm not an error."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:250:The bluff held. The stamp was real.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:9:The landlord signed. The figure went into the travel file in Seln's small, square hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:53:By the time the alehouse put its lamps out, Seln knew that there was a file in the town on the provision boy from Halcenvane. He knew that it was the current edition, made since the mill town, and that a commercial shop that sold such files across half the continent had made it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:93:He had it from four things, and he had filed none of them. There was the post locking in the east hall, with the dial sheets against his chest, and two things the boy's record said he did not own arriving inside one breath. There was Jask, a third-year, telling the refectory queue for a week that the boy was *quick off a read*; the file that came home from the guest floor with nothing written on it, and his own line under it, *unmarked file. one of three. probably the third.*; and one slip of wing paper, cut small, in a hand that was nobody's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:99:A file is made the way a wall is built. You cannot see the order of the work when it is finished, unless you know where to look. Seln knew where to look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:101:Every commercial file he had ever read on a fighter began with the fighter's bouts, because bouts were what the buyers paid for. This one did not. This one began with the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:103:It began with the enrollment basis: the clause, the citations under it, the long chain of verification by which a registrar on a bluff had shown that a boy with no Path on his papers could stand on a floor at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:105:Only after the door had been set out, carefully, did the file come to the boy's record. And only after the record did it come to the boy himself, and after the boy, to the people round him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:107:Door, record, man, company. It was a strange order for a fight file. It was an order a buyer would never ask for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:123:He held the stair and the file's order in his head together, and let them sit until they agreed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:139:Seln did not keep it. He had not copied a line of it. A file that stays bought is a file somebody can trace, and a man who copies what he has bought has made two things to be found instead of one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:147:He walked back along the quay at a clerk's pace in the thin morning light, with the travel file under his arm, and closed the matter in the only ledger he had never had to lock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:155:Now the file sat on shelves for anybody's money. It would go on being sold, edition after edition, for as long as the boy fought anywhere a gallery could see him. Any fighter who mattered would walk out to meet him having met him already, on paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:163:He spent the rest of the morning at the exchange, sorting the travel file, and in the afternoon he copied the bracket figures off the board as they went up, in a neat column, without any appearance of interest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:167:He would say it to the table as a file. He would use the office's voice: plain and dry, with nothing in it that a hostile clerk could hold up afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:175:Cael watched him do it, and watched him wait until the plates were gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:183:"The office has closed last term's records-hall matter," he said. "The man on the stair. His job was bought by a commercial shop that makes files on fighters and sells them up and down the continent. It was bought their way. What they were buying it for is on sale in this town tonight: a file on the enrollee, made since the mill town." He poured himself tea. "The office's finding: private interest, confirmed — the market, not the Compact. Who first paid for the job can't be found from here. In the office's view, it no longer matters."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:193:"The office would put its name to it before any panel you care to choose." He answered in his counting voice, flat as a column. "The shop builds its files in a particular order, and the job on the stair was the first piece of that order. And it pays its readers in a particular way. In advance. On paper, unsigned. Through a box near water, let by the month and given up, so that no box is used twice and no reader ever has a name to sell. The readers in this town complain of it; they would like somebody to shout at." He moved the milk jug an inch to the left. "The man on the stair was paid through a box by the river, let for one month. Every buyer has a hand, as every clerk has one. This shop has written its orders in the same hand since before any of us were born. It doesn't change its hand for one stair."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:197:"And there is an absence that confirms it," he said. "Suppose the client had been an office. The Compact, or the registry, or any body with a seal and a standing. An office that wants a thing and fails to get it asks again, louder, on better paper, with a name at the bottom. Nobody asked again. Instead, the shop went round to the public hearing and built the door from that, more cheaply, and sold the file anyway." He set the cup down. "Offices insist. Shops make do. This one made do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:207:"One thing left," said Karis, with her pen still on the page. "If there's a file on Cael in every meet town, then everybody at Norhold has read him before they meet him. All of them. And Auremont's had scouts at this meet. They won't be buying their file from a shop."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:221:"They'll prepare for the file," he said. "The file is the record. I've lived inside the record since Ardenmere. Everything in it is true, and everything in it is what I chose to show." He set his cup down. "So Auremont is going to spend the whole cycle drilling its fighters against a thing I made to be drilled against. I'd rather fight people who prepared for the wrong me than people who prepared for nothing." He looked up. "A man who's prepared has committed. And I can read a man who's committed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:297:He wondered what page three of his own file said, and who was going to come and stand at it, and whether he would know the door when they did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:5:The waystation sat at the foot of the hill country, where the road came down off the last of the slate and ran out level across a country of hedges and frozen ditches. Behind it the hills stood up grey in the first light. They had been two days coming down out of them. Ahead, by Rooke's reckoning, lay another week of flat road and then the bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:41:It came to him, standing in the straw with the arm hanging easy at his side, that he had priced every bout of the season to the touch and had not once priced a floor until a floor sent him its bill. He knew the oak because he lived on it. Every other surface he had walked onto as a guest, trusting it to behave like home until it showed him otherwise. For the first time he understood that the floor had been in every bout he fought this year, an opponent that filed no challenge and shook no hands, and that it had been keeping its own score the whole time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:81:He did it sitting up straight, with both hands flat on the table on either side of his bowl. He kept them shut for one breath, a long one, in and out, while the fire cracked and the innkeeper's girl clattered plates behind the counter and nobody at the table moved at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:149:*Qualified. Rooke read it at a waystation table with the bread going stale beside it, and shut his eyes for one breath, and then told us not to get careless. Seln had known since breakfast. Gault filed it in his case and then thought better of it and gave it to Rooke.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:159:They came up the bluff road on the Third-day evening in a thin dry snow that did not settle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:161:The coats were on the bell. Two stood at the ferry landing when the wagons crossed, stamping on the boards, and two more at the foot of the bluff road in the lee of the wall. Cael counted them out of habit. Karis wrote the time in the marbled log without looking at them, and nobody else looked at them either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:189:Ephram copied the season's records into his file book until there was nothing left to copy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:201:The two sheets rode in the delegation's file all that time, folded together in Rooke's coat with the training sheets: Zerin's times and Marek's notations. Nobody mentioned them. Nobody had to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:205:The confluence was two days north of Ostrand, at the place where the river that ran under the bluff met a bigger one coming down out of the east.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:211:The town stood on a tongue of land between the two rivers, built up to the water on both sides. Both rivers were iron-grey and running hard, with ice along their edges in plates and shelves, and the place where they met was a long rough seam of broken water that never froze, even in the deep cold, because the two currents never agreed with each other long enough to let it. Barges lay up at the quays three deep, roped together, their masts down. The hall where the meet was held stood at the very point of the tongue, with water on three sides of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-11.md:7:He had the observation notebook open on his forearm and a pencil, and he had told nobody what he meant to do, because he did not quite know himself. He knew only that he had been rated three times now, once in a mock ring by people who loved him and twice at the wool town by strangers, and that he had never once watched the thing happen from the side where it happened. He had read about it, and Rooke had shown him a yellow card and drawn him a table. But the instrument itself he had only ever seen from the floor, which was the one place from which you could not see it at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-11.md:61:Five judges marked each of the three. Each judge was trained to one picture of par and tested against it on old bouts before every cycle, and struck from the lists if he drifted. The picture was a practitioner at Silver tier, fifth rank, on an ordinary good day. That fighter's performance was ten on each mark. That was the reference profile, the Silver Standard, sealed at the start of the cycle and opened by nobody until it closed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-11.md:145:She took her hand back and walked off the floor, and Rooke met her at the rope and said nothing at all. But that evening, in the coaching file, under the bout, Cael saw a new line in Rooke's small hard hand: *Pillar corner. Seen the first afternoon; kept two days, unspoken; spent once, at the end, when he'd stopped expecting anything.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-11.md:301:Afterward the steward brought out the certificate. It was a stiff card with the meet's seal on it, printed with the year's date and the words *Iron, upper draw*, and a space where the steward wrote Ephram's name in his best hand and blotted it. He handed it over at the rope. Ephram held it in both hands and looked at it, turning it a little toward the window, as if he were not quite sure which way up it went. He did not say anything for a while.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-11.md:309:"It's a figure of speech." Ephram put the certificate inside his coat, carefully, against his chest. "I'm not giving you half of it. I'm telling you that you're owed. It's different." He looked toward the arch, where the noise from the main hall told them that Lira's final had begun. "I think I'd like to do that again. Win something." He seemed to listen to the sentence after he had said it, and to find it strange, and then to decide he liked it. "I think I'd like to get used to it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-18.md:11:She had not had it by morning, after all. She had told Ephram at breakfast what she did have, which was that the Stone's touch against her had come near the stove end, that he had twice tried to take her there, and that he seemed to like it. Ephram had nodded and written it in his file book: *Stay away from the stove end.* It was a sensible note, and it was the wrong one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-18.md:87:He shook the Stone's hand at the centre of the floor and meant it. Then he walked back to the delegation's rope, took his file book out of his coat pocket before he had put the coat on, and stood at the rope in the cold, writing, while the panel's slates went face down and came up again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-18.md:99:Ephram found her at the coat-rack an hour before it. He had a page in his hand, torn out of the file book, and he held it out to her without a word, as stiffly as a clerk handing over a bill he is not proud of.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-18.md:129:Cael found it the best bout of the meet to watch, and nobody else in the gallery seemed to know it. The boy came in behind a high probing line, slowly, testing, finding out what was there. He did not go near Brom's left side, because nobody had told him to. He went at Brom's right instead, the side the file had called the strong one, because he had looked at it for himself and thought it might not be as strong as all that. He was wrong about that, but he was wrong honestly, with his own eyes, and he learned something from every touch he did not land.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-18.md:163:"They had," said Karis. "They'd all read the same file."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-18.md:175:He did it standing, as Lira had, with his file book open, and he read it out item by item in the flat voice of a carter reading a manifest at a toll gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-18.md:183:Nobody laughed this time. Rooke had come back from the exchange while Lira was doing the board-man and had been sitting at the far end of the table by the fire with his coat still on. He took his own file out of his coat and wrote one line in it, and Cael, who could read Rooke's hand upside down at twenty feet, read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-18.md:201:*A betting house has checked Seln's arithmetic on its own account and come to the same answer. The file on me calls my margin to the exchange, and the town bet on it, and the town was right. A man whose whole trade is not knowing has looked at me and found nothing he doesn't know. He meant it as a joke, and it isn't one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-18.md:211:She did not read them the market's file again. They had all seen what it held, and where every line of it came from: meet records, the gallery, the hearing at the copying rate. "The walls hold," she said. "Two rules will keep them holding."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-18.md:227:Seln, by the door, had copied the rules down as Karis gave them, on the back of a sheet from the travel file, under a heading in his small square hand: *Delegation: correspondence and floor discipline.* When the parlour broke up he filed the sheet and went up first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-18.md:237:The challenge had been filed on the first afternoon by a Blade from the slate-country house, an Iron Rank Five who fought on that floor every week and had seen the boy from the provision on two meet records and wanted to see him on a third. He was a striker who liked to commit, and to commit early, and to make the other man answer him. It was a style Cael liked, on a floor he had come to like: true and fast and honest, the kind of slate that told you everything a body was doing in the moment it did it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-18.md:273:His second challenger of the day had filed late the evening before, and was waiting for him at the west floor after the noon bell. He was a quarry foreman, and he was a grappler.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-16.md:7:Cael knew him slightly from Rooke's profiles. His house was small and poor and well run, and it had lost regional finals to richer houses for a long time without ever seeming to hold it against anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-16.md:33:It was a good file. That was the first thing, and nobody enjoyed it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-16.md:57:Karis held out her hand for the file. Brom gave it to her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-16.md:245:*The registry's instrument said she was Iron. That last touch said something the instrument hasn't got a word for.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-16.md:347:When supper was over and the plates were being cleared, the door from the kitchen passage opened and Seln came in from the quay. He had his coat on and the travel file under his arm, and his face was the same as it always was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-16.md:351:"When the plates are gone," he said, to the table in general, "the office has a matter to put to the table. It won't take long."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:9:And Ephram, regional champion of a day and a half's standing, sat across the table from Karis with Rooke's coaching notes on the bout spread in front of him, copying them out into a book of his own in his careful hand. Rooke had told him that afternoon that champions kept their own files. Ephram had gone straight out and bought the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:51:The meet record was posted on the hall board at the first bell of the last morning, and Seln was in front of it at the first bell and a quarter, with the travel file under his arm and a pencil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:53:Copying it was his work. It was there on the roster against his name: *records and floor scheduling.* The delegation's travel file needed the meet's figures in it before the wagons left, and the clerks of the mill guild, who were slow, would not have the certified copies ready until noon. So Seln stood in the cold hall before the board, in the grey light from the loading doors, and copied the figures out by hand in a column on a fresh sheet, as he copied everything, neatly and without any appearance of interest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:61:Then he took out of the travel file the copy of the wool town's record that the courier had brought to the waystation, and laid the two sheets side by side on the closed file, and looked at them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:73:That was what fighters looked like on paper: they wobbled. They had bad mornings and good ones. They over-reached at home and under-reached in the cold, and they met styles that suited them and styles that did not. Across a season their figures spread like shot from a fowling-piece, ten points, twelve. That was not a fault in the instrument; it was the instrument working. Five honest hands, struck at the extremes, reading five honest days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:89:Seln squared the two sheets together and put them in the travel file and closed it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:91:Then he went to the document table at the end of the hall, by the arch, where the delegations' papers were stacked for collection, and began to sort Halcenvane's into the order the travel file required. He did it slowly, and it took him some time. At a quarter past the second bell the enrollee came through the arch from the guest house on his way to the second room, limping a little on the left, alone, and stopped at the table to ask whether the certified copies had come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:93:They had not. Seln said so, to the file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:97:Seln did not look up. "There is a matter of arithmetic," he said. "The office has noted it. Six exhibition figures this season. Two towns, two panels, six opponents at four levels. From the highest of the six to the lowest is three points." He laid a sheet on the stack. "Every bracket fighter in this hall runs across ten points in a season, or twelve. Yours spreads three. An honest instrument scatters. When it stops scattering, the cause is not usually the instrument." He laid another sheet on top of the first. "The office draws no conclusion. It notes only that pencils are cheap, and that other people own them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:99:He closed the file. Then, in the voice of a clerk reading out the small print, he added one sentence more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:103:He picked up the file and went out through the arch toward the guest house without waiting for an answer, and the enrollee did not give one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:109:Cael timed it anyway, out of habit, on the travel clock Hesk had sent him. Hesk, who had raised him in a workshop at the far end of the country, made instruments that measured true, and this one ran an hour fast in tournament weeks because Cael set it so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:111:The committee room had a long table and a portrait of a dead miller over the fireplace, and the panel sat along one side of the table with their backs to the portrait. It was the panel of record Gault had written into his calendar at the end of last term, in his ruled lines: *Gault, with two.* The two were a registry officer from the district seat, a careful dark man who had plainly read the whole file in the coach on the way and had the page numbers ready, and the host region's senior examiner, a stout grey woman who had sat on the meet's own panels all week and who said very little.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:127:It was what Gault had promised at the end of last term and every time since: the enrollee measured against himself, and nothing else. *Measured against: baseline (entry), and the note of the twentieth of Reaping.* The basis of the enrollment was read into the minute and found unchanged. The term's supervised floor hours were certified from Rooke's sheets, which Gault had brought in the case, signed. The two meet records were laid on the table as evidence of performance under the provision, and the registry officer read the exhibition leaves with great attention and asked four questions about the documented ceilings, all of them competent, and Cael answered each one in the language of the documents themselves. The burst's distance; the burst's rate on sprung oak and on other floors; the landing beat; the read that looked like timing. All of it was in the record, and all of it was true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:129:Then Gault took the plate out of the case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:131:It was the travelling plate, the small twin of the one in the wing at home, a brass-faced disc on a short arm with its dial set into the housing. It had come upriver in a felt bag in the bottom of the case, under the seals. The guild had lent the panel its proving frame, an iron thing the millwrights used to test shafts, with a weight that ran down a rail on a pawl, and the guild's own millwright stood by to work it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:133:Cael set his feet, and took the plate on his forearm, and braced it from the right shoulder, as he had braced it in the wing every time he had ever been tested.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:135:The pawl let go. The weight came down the rail into the plate, and the shock went up the bones of his forearm and stopped in the right shoulder, all of it, with nothing taken and nothing turned. The dial in the housing swung a little way, and stopped where it had stopped at the baseline, and where it had stopped on the twentieth of Reaping.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:137:"No change," said Gault, and wrote it. "The plate flat, and so entered."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:145:"Routine," said Gault to the officer, putting the plate away in its felt bag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:151:*Two instruments, one year, both managed.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:153:*Yesterday I chose a figure for five judges, and they wrote it down in good faith: twenty-four, a careful distance under par. Today three officers measured me against myself on a borrowed plate, and found me exactly as unchanged as I'd decided to be. One instrument reads a performance. The other reads a file. Neither has ever once touched the thing they're measuring.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:155:*Inventory, while I'm here. Wind: five of five on hard boards yesterday, which is all the day sells free on that floor. Four went to the yard-master and one to the academy Irons, and the Bronze got a step. The hip after; the left calf tight from the exchange I held. Pressure: flat at the plate again, the right shoulder paying. The cap is four blows a sitting, and today took one. Compression: held at a sleeve's width from the ganger's hand. Not on any record here. Ember: none spent; nobody here asked for it. Shadow: deployment none. Still.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:157:*At the end the man from the district seat said "routine," and Gault said "routine" back, and they shook hands on it. Not long ago a file like mine went upstairs at a run. Today it's a word two officers trade over a sealed minute while a christening waits on the stair.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:165:He did it at the stove, with the coaching file open on his knee and the meet records clipped into its back, while the others were at supper in the next room. Cael had come back for his coat. Rooke kept his eyes on the file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:171:"Six figures." Rooke turned a page of the file with one finger. "I've had them up beside each other since noon. They keep time like a metronome." He closed the file. "Twenty years of coaching, and I've never turned one out before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:173:He stood up, and put the file under his arm, and went in to supper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:179:Where the figures sat was not the trouble. Strong Iron, low in its range, a careful boy with good feet: that was what he had built, and it was holding. What would give him away was how close together they sat. A real Iron who fought six different people in two different towns would wobble. He would have a bad morning against a style that did not suit him. He would over-reach in front of his own house and under-reach on a cold floor. Six bouts, and he had stamped out the same figure every time, like coins from one die. He had been so careful to keep the instrument from seeing too much that he had shown it something else instead, and the something else was the care.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:197:It offended something quite deep. He was sitting by a stove planning his own bad mornings. He was going to stand in front of an honest instrument and lose points on purpose, and lose them in a pattern designed to look like no pattern, so that nobody would ever be able to say he had chosen them. Whatever had winced in him at page thirty-one winced again now, and worse. It was not fear. It was something more like a craftsman being told to file a joint badly, by hand, so that it would look as if it had been done by somebody less careful.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:11:He had filed the challenge late the evening before, in a big careful hand, and in the space for a reason he had written *my lads asked me to*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:277:*Of what the panel will score today, two shares were mine by plan and one was the floor's. I meant to spend two points. I've spent two points and a shoulder. Rooke's file gets the true account. The panel's record has its wobble. My shoulder has four days.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:297:There were certified copies for the meet's clerks, a letter from the chancellor for Rooke, and a packet of forms for Gault that Gault received with deep suspicion.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:307:*For the Wind girl, if you think she'd take it from me: I have spent my life on instruments that other men gave up on, and I'll tell her what my trade knows and doesn't say in front of customers. When a place throws out what it doesn't know how to set right, the fault's in the workshop, never the instrument. The Wind girl has shown them that already, on paper with a stamp on it, which is the only paper such places read. Your grandmother proved a version of it once with a door. That's a tale for another day. Ask me when the road runs home.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:319:"The fault's in the workshop," she said quietly. "Never the instrument."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:365:These were Wind times, and they were not Iron times. The gap between one burst and the next was shorter than anything in Rooke's file. The figures for a long sequenced run belonged further up the page by two whole tiers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:431:The Copper pages were thin. Nobody spent much money on Copper files at the continental level, Karis said from the other side of the table, and you could see where they had saved it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:457:Seln copied it in a neat column on a fresh sheet, with the travel file under his arm and the cold coming in through the great door behind him. Cael stood beside him with his arm in Gault's scarf and read the exhibition leaf for himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:473:Brom read the Copper leaf with his head on one side and said, "Same line again," with deep satisfaction. Ephram copied the whole record into his file book, both brackets and the exhibitions and the house totals, standing up, in his careful hand, and when Karis asked him why, when Seln was already copying it, he said that a champion of the upper Iron kept his own files and that he did not intend to stop being one just because he had lost a semifinal. Gault picked up his case by its handle and held it a little higher than he needed to, so that the registry clerk at the next table could see the stitching.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:485:He sealed it and handed it to the courier who carried the region's sealed mail north. He watched the man ride out along the quay toward the hill road until the road took him. Then he went back inside, picked up the travel file, and went to stand in front of the board with his pencil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-22.md:213:He had beaten the eighth seed, and the top seed, and Karis. He had his title from the mill town in his file book, and his account of the quarry town, and a season of supervised sessions with Cael on the oak. And he had watched Lira for a whole year, from closer than anybody but Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-22.md:291:He did not go to the stove this time. He walked back to the delegation's rope with his chin up, took his coat from Gault, and put it on. Then he took out the file book and wrote in it standing, as he had at the quarry town.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-30.md:173:He thought of Bracken at his green table, certifying a list without omission because a true record was the only kind there was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-30.md:229:He found he was not thinking about whoever might file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-30.md:263:Withrow had come with them, in her dark coat, and Bracken with her, and Seln with the travel file open on his arm, already copying each of the house's lines into it as the boards went up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-30.md:271:Then her eyes went on down, past the quarterfinal, to the line she had worked out on the back of her hand on the bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-30.md:347:They were blank. The category did not draw. It waited for filings, and nobody had filed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md:43:Karis spoke at last. "The fairest instrument on the continent has exactly one blind spot, and you live in it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md:57:That, he thought, was the most frightening thing about a really good instrument. It knew exactly where it could not see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:33:"Rhagen," said Karis. "Doctrine. Everything they do is written down first and taught second. Marek's from there. So is whatever they send to look at you." She considered the slip. "Rooke's right about one thing. If a doctrine house files on you, it'll be to find out what you are, not to beat you. They won't care about the touches. They'll care about the survey."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:39:She needed a host rule about delegation quarters. She went to the shelf for the certified transcription of the tournament charter and took it down and opened it at the back, where the schedules were, and turned pages until she found the rule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:55:"I know you didn't. I said it to me." She shut the transcription and put it back on the shelf. "I keep deciding it, every time I go past. It's very tiring. The season first. Then the other thing." She came back to the map and picked up another pin. "It isn't going anywhere. It's been in that book longer than the house has been on this bluff."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:57:Below the window, at the foot of the bluff road, two coats stood in the snow in the lee of the wall, and two more stood at the ferry landing on the far side of the river, small and dark against the white, where Cael could see them from here as he could see them from every high window on the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:93:The block's inventory went into the Log that night, with the ferry landing white and empty below except for its two coats. Most of it said what it always said, so he wrote those lines short: the oak's six free a day and the landing beat no shorter; the right shoulder unasked for anything since the mill town's plate; Compression shown nowhere, not even for the barge-master's fall; Shadow's rent from waking, and one drift on the stair. Then he wrote the two lines that were new.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:135:He signed the report, sealed it with the office's plain seal, and put it in the pouch for the north. The pouch would go down the bluff on the morning coach, past the two coats at the foot of the road. They would not look at it. Nobody ever looked at the pouch; that was what a pouch was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:169:The snow had stopped. The sky had cleared and was full of stars, and the river below was loud and dark and fast, with ice going down it in plates that knocked together in the current with a sound like somebody far off moving furniture.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:199:Cael woke to the sound of water. It was running everywhere, off the roofs and down the gutters and along the covered walk, and in the morning the snow on the bluff had gone grey and soft and was sliding off the tiles in long wet sheets. The river under the wall had risen a man's height overnight and ran brown and heavy and very fast, carrying whole trees down past Ostrand toward the confluence, and the bluff road was a stream for a day, and then deep mud, and then, on the morning of the Second-day, a road again, or near enough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:205:Seln had a corner of the first wagon's tailboard with the travel file on his knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:213:The wagons went out through the gate and down the bluff road in the thin early light, with the mud sucking at the wheels and the whole valley below them steaming in the first sun.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:221:Nobody had arranged that either. It was market day, and the town was out, and somebody had seen the wagons coming down the bluff. By the time they reached the bridge there were perhaps two hundred people standing along it in the mud, with more coming down the lanes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:223:There were the baker's boys and the ferry crew and the women from the fish steps, and the man who sold hot chestnuts at the bottom of the hill. There were children on their fathers' shoulders. A great many of them had never set foot on the bluff in their lives.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:235:Above it the bluff stood up dark against the morning, with the house on top of it, and the wall where Lira had sat a few nights before, looking north, in the starlight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:245:The watchers grew smaller behind them, two and two, standing in the melt on the bell, and then the road bent and the bluff went behind a line of willows, and they were gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:255:*In the file on Rooke's knee: a city we've never seen, two sheets we haven't solved, and a Shield I think I have.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-15.md:23:Ephram wrote the inn's name in his file book under the date and looked at it with satisfaction. Gault set his case on the bench beside him and kept one hand on its handle through the whole meal, as if the hill might try to take it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-15.md:27:Seln did not stay for the end of supper either. He finished his soup and folded his napkin square and told Rooke, to the table in general, that he would see to the inn's account with the landlord before the man went to bed, so that the travel file would be straight in the morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-15.md:29:Then he went out through the door to the kitchen passage, with the file under his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-15.md:349:"Of people." She handed the chart back. "They buy what other people see. A steward's notes. A regular's opinion. Anybody with a seat that has a view, they'll buy the view off him for a cup of something. Then they put it all together into a file on a fighter, and they sell the file to anybody who pays the fee. Houses buy them to get ready for a bout. The betting houses buy them to set a price. Families buy them so they can feel they know something."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-15.md:391:*Her next file on me will say I watch the watchers. It'll be right. Whoever pays for that page will have one more fact about me than they had before, and it's a good fact, and I gave it to her free.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:7:Cael, at the rail, knew exactly where he had read about it. There was an old edition of Brom's file in circulation, the first one the shops had made, four close pages with the seam laid out on the third. The shops had sold a later one since. This young man had bought the cheap one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:123:Ephram took the first exchange two to one with the plain entry he had kept all year, and the heel behind it, and the late half-turn of the front foot that had won him the mill town. The coast Blade had read none of it in any file. He met it honestly and lost honestly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:163:Ephram studied his plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:169:Ephram wrote it down. Cael saw him do it, in the file book, under the table, and saw what he wrote: *Two points for a stride. Karis read it. Cheap.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:187:"Think of a fighter's standing as money in a bank," she said. "All year, anybody with a rank who fought you was taking his money out and putting it on a table with nothing on the other side. If he beat you, he'd beaten a boy with no tier, and the sheet couldn't hold it. If you beat him, he'd lost to a boy with no tier, and the sheet held that very well indeed. So nobody with a rank worth having ever filed, and the only volunteers were curious men and quarrymen." She tapped the back of the sheet. "That held while you were something the regions talked about. Here the city's telling a story, and you're in the middle of it. Now the story takes in whoever stands across a ring from you, whether he wins or not. Rhagen gets its doctrine argued over on every broadside in the city. The lake man gets eight thousand people watching him do the one thing he loves." She took her hand off the sheet. "Everybody else here is fighting for a place on a board. People fight *you* for meaning."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:201:"Well, at least one of them is honest," she said. "File it, would you? Under correspondence. I should like it kept."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:203:Bracken filed it, there and then, in the pouch he never let out of his reach, and wrote the date on the back in his small upright hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:207:"Rhagen first, because Rhagen's the one that matters." He laid the first page down. "Every house on this continent owns a copy of Rhagen's book. Their doctrine is written down, every piece of it, and sold, and argued over in every coaching room I've ever sat in. By tomorrow night I'll have that man's whole structure laid out for you to the half beat. He knows that. He knows we'll have read it." Rooke looked round the table. "And he's filed anyway. So he isn't coming to win, and he isn't coming to surprise anybody. He's coming to see whether the book holds. He'll run the whole of it at you, every layer, and watch what happens to each piece." He searched for the word and found it, and made a face at it. "It isn't a bout. It's a *survey*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:211:"Rhagen. Then everybody who buys what Rhagen writes afterward, which is everybody." Rooke turned the page. "The lake man's simpler. He's a showman. A showman's timing is built for the back row of the benches, so it's half a beat too slow for the man in front of him. Make him fight the man in front of him, and not the back row, and you'll find the half beat." He shut the coaching file. "Four days to the first and seven to the second. We'll show Rhagen the ground we want drawn on their map, and not a yard more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:221:At the far end of the table Seln had been sorting the evening's papers into the travel file throughout. He spoke without looking up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:223:"Two items for the record. Rhagen's head of delegation has put his own name under the captain's. It is the house's filing, then, and not the man's." He fitted a schedule under its clip. "And the west tower has given both bouts to the most senior panel it keeps for exhibitions." He squared the clip against the edge of the file with one finger. "Scheduling is opinion."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:227:Then Brom said "Mm," in precisely the voice Seln used across his counter at home, and Lira laughed, and Seln looked up from the file for the first time that evening. Something happened, very briefly, at the corner of his mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:291:And all through it, in every queue and tavern door and printed sheet, the city was telling itself the story of this cycle out of whatever it had to hand. There was the favourites' house and its Gold. There was the house from the bluff, back after eighteen years. There was the wall. And there was the boy with no tier, who had pulled two Silvers out of a fossil in an evening.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:295:"It's reading me. It's only polite to read back." Below them a printed sheet went from one hand to another, and Cael saw his own woodcut face go past upside down. "The coats at home were reading my file. They wanted something out of it. These people only want a good afternoon, and I'm in it. Nobody down there cares how anything works. They want to see what happens next."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:335:He looked along the row. He had known it for four days by face and by seat. Ilsev had the first seat, the senior evaluation seat, with her files set on her lap-desk at an angle that had not shifted by a hair since the opening. He remembered that exact, unhurried care from a hearing room and from the bluff, and in every act of hers that he knew of she had been perfectly correct. Havel had the second. He was the quiet records officer from the bluff, older than Cael remembered him. Cael had hardly exchanged a word with him, and yet he had turned up at the edge of Cael's life at Ardenmere and at Greyvane and on the bluff, watching and writing. The other four observation seats had changed on the rotation every day, badged men and women he did not know. Past them sat two district officers, a liaison man, and two officials of the host city who came and went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:337:Every one of their chairs had a brass plate on its back with a name cut into it. He had read the plates from the floor with a borrowed glass on the first day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:341:He had the borrowed glass again now, and he put it to his eye. The chair was the same as the others, dark wood with a cane seat, and the registry's grey rail-cloth ran on in front of it without a break. There was a clean pale square on its back where a plate would have been screwed, and the screw holes were there, empty, from some earlier sitting. Somebody had sat in it once, at some other sitting, under some other plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:343:He looked at it for a long time. A plate, a steward and a brushed seat were all a chair could tell anybody from a public gallery, and this one had told him two of the three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:347:*Compact row, seat twelve. Held, unplated, empty, and guarded. Three times now a steward has cleared it, the last time of a man with a pie.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:349:*Every other chair in that row has a brass plate on its back. Twelve has a pale square where a plate used to be, two empty screw holes, a brushed seat, and a steward at the head of the stair. No name.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-33.md:11:Her opponent was a Force fighter from one of the inland houses, a broad, unhurried man with a heavy jaw and thick wrists. His regional file called him a counterpuncher. Karis had put it more plainly at breakfast, over the bread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-33.md:63:*The demonstration-exhibition category,* it said, *requires the main floor and the senior exhibition panel. Both are required this week by the bracket semifinals and the Copper final. The demonstration ring will therefore be raised when the brackets have cleared the main floor, and the bouts filed in the category will be heard in the order of their filing as soon thereafter as the floor allows. Houses concerned are notified accordingly.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-33.md:73:Nobody laughed, exactly. But Brom put his head down on his folded arms, and Lira looked hard at the ceiling, and Ephram made a small noise into his cup. Seln's face did not change at all. He took the notice back, made a note in the travel file, and fitted the sheet under its clip with one finger, and gave no sign at all of having spoken.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-33.md:79:All year he had been the thing in the middle of every floor. He had been charted and priced, protested and filed, sold in woodcut on street corners and argued over in taverns, and every hall he had walked into since the wool town had turned its head to watch him come in. He had grown so used to the weight of it that he had stopped noticing he was carrying anything at all. Now a clerk in the west tower had set it down for him, for a week, for reasons that had nothing to do with him, and he felt his shoulders come down a whole pack's weight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-33.md:89:"You've been the best scouting I've had all year," he said at last. "I haven't said so, because you had bouts of your own to think about, and I don't ask a man to carry two sacks up one hill." He nodded at the travel file, where the notice had gone. "Seems you've only the one this week. Carry it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-33.md:97:*For one week I'm an instrument and not a specimen. I was an instrument long before I was anything else. I'd like to find out whether I'm still any good at it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-33.md:213:"She shortened the count," said Ephram. He was still breathing hard, and his face was red, but his voice was perfectly steady. "I found her wheel in one exchange, and she changed it in the next. Fifteen years of doing it one way, and she changed it in a breath because a boy from the bluff had found it." He looked down at his hands, half unwrapped. "I couldn't hit a pane before it was there. Nobody could. Not with an edge."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-33.md:273:"What does her file say about before? About where she came from?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-33.md:277:"Then I want the rest of it." She stood up. "Rooke's got the long one, the whole commercial file. I'll ask him for it in the morning." She looked down at him, and he saw that she was not discouraged in the least. She was the other thing, the thing she turned into when a problem finally showed her its face. "You found a country nobody's mapped. I'll go and live in it. I've lived there before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-33.md:281:It came to him slowly, sitting there, that what he had found was not really about Zerin at all. A file was made out of what a fighter had been asked to do. Every page of Zerin's was full, because she had been asked for everything a program could think of and had given all of it, and the one blank country on her map was only the one place nobody had ever thought to send her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:5:*Continental finals, Norhold. Observation support. Seat two of six.* There was a date for the coach and a list of what to bring, and at the foot of the list, in the same type, *Read the qualifying files before departure*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:9:There were three qualifying files from the western region, one for each house. He read the two older houses' files first, completely, because they were first in the bundle, and then he read Halcenvane's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:11:He had read it before. He had read it at Ardenmere, before it was a file, when it was a form with four questions on it and a boy on a pump step. He had read it at Greyvane across the end of a hearing table, and on the bluff with the rain coming down on the canvas. Now it had a season grown onto it: four meets, thirteen exhibitions, a column of figures, a house first in its region. It was thicker than any file on the boy had ever been, and in the administrative layer underneath it, where nobody but a records officer ever looked, the same mark stood where it had always stood, as plain and as orphaned as on the day he had first found it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:13:He did not stop at it. He read past it at the pace he read everything, and finished the file, and squared it, and set it with the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:37:He did not write this one down. There was no fifth entry. The rule of the notebook was that it held what had happened and nothing of what he made of it. He had broken that rule once, on the bluff, and did not mean to make a habit of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:121:The reserves were pressed into service as Zerin's furniture. The Shield reserve held one end of the counting-string and the Stone reserve the other, and they stood at the chalk marks like a pair of gateposts while Lira ran between them. Brom kept the count on the bench with a spoon against his mug, because he said it was the only instrument in the house he trusted, and Gault, passing through, stopped to watch and went away again looking pained.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:177:"He'll find every join I've got," said Brom. "That's what he does. So I've been asking myself where my joins are." He pointed, without looking, at his own left side. "That's the one everybody will tell him about. Page three. The old seam. It's mended, and everybody who's bought my file knows it's mended, and they'll tell him anyway, because it's the only thing on page three worth the price."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:207:Ephram stood at the back of his own squad with his file book in his hand and nothing in his face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:223:He stood at the back with the file book shut and watched the first three breaths of the drill without saying anything at all, and then said one word, quite quietly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:237:"You don't need more," said Ephram. He did not look at any of them, but his voice was perfectly steady. "You know what to do. You all do. You only need to know which way the rest of us are going before you go." He opened the file book and wrote something in it. "I was telling you how to fight. You don't need that. You need to be told where the floor is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:243:By the end of the block Ephram could call the floor in a dozen words a drill, and the squad moved to them without hearing them. He could tell, too, from the back, a breath before anybody else, when a drill was going wrong. Cael watched him do it on the last morning, standing at the back of the squad with the file book closed under his arm, looking at the floor much as Rooke looked at it, already several minutes ahead of everybody on it and waiting, with some patience, for them to catch up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:33:But he thought about it afterward, drying his face on the gallery stair, longer than a joke deserved. All year he had been something that other people wrote down. Clerks had written him on rosters and judges on slates, compilers in their files, and the registry in a single bracketed word. Every one of them had put him on paper for a purpose of their own, and he had never once been asked whether he would like to add anything. The boy had asked. It was a cheap printed face that looked nothing like him, but at the bottom of it, for the first time, there was a mark in his own hand that said he had been there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:79:There was one of Lira, called *LIRA OF THE BLUFF*. It made her a head taller than she was and very much angrier, with her hair streaming and her fists clenched, against a background of lightning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:83:And there was the sixth, the newest, the ink still smelling. It was called *THE FIVE FROM THE BLUFF*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:91:Cael had read the compilers' files on the five of them, all four pages of Brom's and every shop's careful guess at his own. None of them had Seln in it. Every list had him on its last line, and every file took its cue from the lists. Then a woodcutter who had never read any of them had looked up from his bench as they went by his shop, and cut six figures into the block instead of five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:105:Seln had come to the table last, with the travel file under his arm, and he had not looked at any of the sheets until the others had finished. Now he looked at the group sheet. He looked at the lean figure at the end of the line for what seemed to Cael a very long time. His face did not change at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:109:He returned to his place at the far end and drew the travel file toward him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:127:*And one of all of us. It took a man with a knife and a block of pearwood, who has never read a file in his life, to see where the sixth of us stands.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:129:*Every compiler on the circuit missed him, because the compilers start from the rosters and the rosters put him on the last line, as staff. The woodcutter started from his window. Instruments measure what they were built to measure. A woodcutter only has his eyes, and uses them.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:215:The Archmarshal Vastin read the Norhold files on a Fourth-day evening, in his room in the Compact's house, a week before the opening.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:221:He had read a great many files in forty years, and most of them told him more about the office that had made them than about the thing they described. These were no different. Their courtesies told him which officers liked one another and which did not. Their dates told him that somebody had been in a hurry in the cold term, and then had not. He noted that, and did not make it carry more than a date can carry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:227:It was a plain document, a plan of twelve seats drawn in a clerk's neat hand, with a name-plate entered against each. The six observation seats came first, then the district officers, then a liaison seat, then two left for officials of the host city. He read along them in order. One, two, three. Eleven.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:231:There was no plate entered against it. Where the name should have been there was a short instruction in the same clerk's hand. *Seat twelve: held. No plate. Stewards to keep the seat clear for the length of the sitting.* Beneath that, in the column for the grade of the officer for whom a seat was held, the clerk had written one word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-23.md:21:He was an Iron Rank Nine of the Force Path, the steward said, and had worked the barges on these two rivers since he was a boy. In the deep cold, when the barges lay up, he fought. He had fought at the confluence every year for longer than the steward had kept the list. The gallery loved him, and it was easy to see why, because he was what the whole town was made of: the rivers, and the barges, and the long hard trade of the deep cold, when the ice came down in plates and a man who misread the water for one moment lost a boat and everything in it. When he raised one hand to the beams, the beams roared back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-23.md:85:He had grown up in Denvash, inland, with no river worth the name. But he had stood on the ferry landing under the bluff for a year now, and watched the water come down past Ostrand, and he had watched the barges here at the quays since he arrived. A river's surface shifted and swirled and went every way at once. Underneath, it went one way. It went the way the bed went, because the bed was stone and the water was only water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-23.md:229:Along the delegation's bench nobody moved, because Rooke had told them at breakfast that nobody would. But Cael felt Brom, on his left, let out a long slow breath through his nose. On his right Lira's knee began to jog up and down under her coat and would not stop, and down at the end of the bench Ephram had taken out his file book and was writing the total in it, very small, in the margin of a page that was already full, so that he would have it in his own hand before the regional office had printed it in anybody else's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-23.md:237:She sat on the front bench of the east side, between Bracken and Rooke, with her hands folded on the rug in her lap. Her name was not read. The name of her house was, once, in four syllables, in a voice that gave it nothing. It was the name she had carried up and down the bluff for eighteen years in front of a house that had not qualified once in all that time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-23.md:245:It came out at the long table in the inn that evening. The innkeeper had given them his long room at the back, with the fire built up to roaring and the framed ruling taken down off his wall and propped on the mantel, where he said it belonged for the night. The company had eaten, and the plates had gone, and everybody had settled back in the warmth, half expecting something. Rooke had the look of a man who has been warned he may be called on, and has a few words ready, and hopes not to need them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-28.md:19:Cael had never seen her away from the bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-28.md:21:On the hill she was the house, the way the long wall was the house, or the bell; she moved through it at its own pace and was part of its furniture. Here she seemed smaller and quicker, and a good deal younger. She laughed once at something Ephram said, a short surprised laugh that kept Ephram's eyes on his plate for the rest of the meal.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-28.md:35:She looked down the table, at each of them in turn. She took the reserves in, and Gault, and Seln at the far end with his file, and Rooke, and then the five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-28.md:107:He had spent the year among rules that somebody had written: a registrar's certificate, a dead board's clause, a schedule nobody had read in forty years. Nobody had written this one, and nobody enforced it. Somebody had done it once, and somebody else the cycle after, and now a man with wet legs and his hat in his hand stood on a verge a long way from any office and kept it, because it was the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-28.md:227:Cael looked at it for a long time as the wagons crawled up the processional way. Somewhere in that building was the other thing with the same name, the reference profile in its sealed case. He thought about the two of them, the cloth outside and the tables within, and how the city had hung the one where everybody could see it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-28.md:249:He went alone, with the travel file under his arm and the roster in a folder of its own, and he was back before Rooke had finished arguing with the keeper about the beds. He had the credentials for all eleven of them, threaded on a cord. He had the floor schedules and the warm-up allotments for the whole of the first week, and a printed map of the Concourse with the delegation's rooms marked on it in ink.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-28.md:257:He was. At dusk the hill house's clerk came up the stair looking grey and beaten, with half his papers. He stopped on the landing outside Halcenvane's door and looked in at Seln, who was sitting at the end of the table with a cup of tea, writing in the travel file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:51:He had started it at the beginning of the year with a few lines about an instrument case and a frame and a man who marked what the manual said. Since then it had filled up with small things, one at a time, until it no longer described anything like an instrument. He wrote *Fourth, continental Copper. Boots in a ford. Plodding.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:53:Then he sat and looked at the page, and thought that he had once filed this man under machinery, and had been usefully wrong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:73:Two days later, on the First-day, the wagons climbed the bluff road at dusk, with the snow coming down thick and soft and the lamps already lit in Ostrand behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:77:Then the wagons began the long climb, and at the first bend Cael saw that something was wrong with the top of the bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:217:He made himself price it first, as he priced any advice before he gave it. At the beginning of the year an advisory of three sentences had cost him nothing, because it had only declined to misread a charter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:257:He filed the half-sheet with the query and his answers, in the drawer where he kept the struck draft of his advisory from the beginning of the year. Then he went home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:265:It ordered the observation files for the continental finals at Norhold to his desk. It wanted all of them: the seating of the Compact's row in the Concourse gallery, the rotation of the observation officers, the liaison papers between the observation office and the tournament's adjudication office, and every instruction that had gone out under any of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:267:Nothing in his grade required him to see those files. Nothing in his duties touched them. The finals lay a long road and two offices away from anything he was answerable for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:269:His clerk read the direction twice, entered it in the day book, and went to fetch the files. If he wondered about it, he did his wondering in the corridor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:275:He let that stand. A man who keeps an honest instrument must sometimes write down a reading he cannot yet account for, rather than leave it out until he can. He wrote nothing about it anywhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:277:The files came up the stair in the afternoon in two boxes. He had them set on the chair beside the table, where he could see them while he worked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-31.md:135:It ran under the north tiers, wide and dim and lit with lamps, and all fourteen houses waited in it in their files, one behind another, with their colours on staves at their heads.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-31.md:143:It meant the crowd read the file from its smallest end to its largest, and kept the best of it for last, like a good storyteller.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-31.md:153:Three of them had stood in the tunnel with a list while the early houses filed out, arguing in low voices. Every so often one of them had looked at Cael and then back at the list.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-31.md:157:At last the eldest of them had taken him by the elbow, quite kindly, and walked him to the very back of Halcenvane's file, a stride behind Lira, and left him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-31.md:161:Then the file in front moved, and they walked out of the dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-31.md:175:A movement ran along the tiers ahead of Halcenvane's file, faster than the file was walking, people leaning to their neighbours and pointing, and inside it the roar broke into separate voices, and some of them came down to him through the stone. *That's the clause house. At the back. The one with no tier. That's him, the last one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-31.md:209:Far behind her, at the tail of the file, the colour-guard kept its slow march, and she kept the same pace, no faster and no slower.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-31.md:223:The noise in that bowl had bent him when he came out of the tunnel. He had seen it bend every fighter in every file that morning, one way or another, into swagger or stiffness or a careful ignoring of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-31.md:225:It did not bend her. She walked through it at the head of a file that ended in the most loved object on the continent, with eight thousand people on their feet, and her body carried on as though she were crossing an empty yard in the rain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-31.md:309:His regional file said he was good and no more. On the board at the end of the hall he was a line of figures that would not have kept a semifinalist awake.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-34.md:11:Karis had laid out her work as she always did. Her notebook lay open at a fresh leaf headed *QUARTERFINAL* and ruled into her four columns. Beside it lay her own chart of the two bouts Ivenne had fought at Norhold, taken from the gallery. Beside that was a thin packet of four pages in a russet paper cover, bought that afternoon from a shop in the delegation quarter. Every house's file on every other house could be had somewhere in this city for the price of a dinner, and Ternhall's was no different.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-34.md:181:Lira had gone to bed in the afternoon on Rooke's orders and slept through the supper bell. When she came down, the others had kept her place and her plate, and Brom had kept the end of the loaf, which in Brom was close to a declaration.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-34.md:187:He set the jug down. "Then one week you stopped coming at me. You went round. You made me chase you all over the oak until I was blowing like a carthorse, and you touched me on the back of the neck, and I sat down on the floor." He went back to his own plate. "You beat me the times you made me play yours. Don't play hers."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-34.md:237:Rooke had given Lira the whole commercial file on Zerin in the morning, seven pages bound in grey board with the shop's stamp on its cover. She had read the first six at the rail between sessions. The seventh she had kept back for the little room, and for him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-34.md:241:It was the page that every file in that shop's style kept for its end, the page about where a fighter came from, and she read it aloud to him in a low voice, with the window open on the dark courtyard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-34.md:257:Cael sat with that while the lamp hissed. He caught himself trying, without meaning to, to imagine his own other road, the version of him somebody might have been supposed to build. There was nothing there to imagine. No program on the continent had a page for a boy like him, and no shop sold a file that began where his began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-34.md:273:"Do me a chart," she said at last. "Her against me. Properly. No coaching. Not what you hope for me. What the instrument says."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:11:"*Auremont went home with the bout. The bluff went home with the first thing anybody has taken off Auremont all week, and there is no column in the standings to put it in.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:17:Brom ate his breakfast in silence. He ate all of it, and then he ate the end of the loaf, which nobody contested, and then he sat with his hands on the table on either side of his plate and looked at nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:23:"I know. I've been thinking about the second half." He looked round at them. "Plan to have fewer. That's not plan to have none. You can't have none. Nobody can. Rooke knows that better than anybody." He stood up and began to clear his own plate, which he never did. "So the question isn't how many I've got. It's which ones he gets to keep."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:71:Across the floor, Marek looked at where Brom was standing, and then down at the seam in front of him, and then at Brom again. Cael was watching his face. He saw nothing on it at all, except that the man had read something, and filed it, and gone on to the next line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:73:High on the east side, over the far rail, the Compact's row was full except for one chair. Ilsev sat in the first seat with her lap-desk on her knees. Havel was in the second. At the end of the row, behind the grey rail-cloth, the twelfth chair stood empty, with its brushed seat and its pale square where a plate should have been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:91:Marek stepped back over the seam onto the stone. He did not look at the left side again for the rest of the morning. One contact had told him everything the dossier was worth, and he had closed that page and put it away, and Cael saw the craft-readers in the tiers see it too. In the space of a single touch, the old edition of Brom's file had stopped being worth the paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:123:The second floor's whole gallery had stood up for Lira yesterday. The main floor did not stand. It went quiet instead, every bank of it, the quiet of a great many people who had just watched the best defensive fighter in the bracket taken apart in a single exchange in front of them, every piece of him labelled and filed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:185:He went looking for new structure, fresh gaps nobody had priced, in light, quick, unflagged contacts all over Brom's body. But every question asked of a wall that hits back has to be paid for, and Brom was hitting back. Every light contact Marek made bought a heavy one in return. And the heavy ones had been adding up since the first exchange. Cael could see it in him now. He was slower by a hair. His reset came a shade late. His step away was half a step short. All the precision of him, the whole fine instrument, was running now against a cost that rose with every contact, and the cost was beginning to show.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:325:*I sat at the rail today and thought about my own record,* Cael wrote that night, after the ceremony and the letter and everything else. *Every figure on it is a price I set. Every exhibition I've fought this year, I've fought so that the paper would read a certain way. Brom forged one line, once, in the open, for one bout, and then shook the man's hand and told him exactly what he'd done. Mine is the whole file, every day, and I can't tell anybody.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:327:*Marek said he'd never had a page forged on him before, and sounded pleased. I don't think anybody who reads my file is ever going to get to say that.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:33:Cael watched the column draw away up the valley, the matched greys pulling steadily into the rain, until a fold of the land took it. He found, a little to his surprise, that he did not mind the look. A year ago he would have counted it and filed it and lain awake over it. This one had come from somebody who knew exactly what she was looking at, and had looked at it properly, and then gone back to her business without wanting anything from him except to know.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:47:By then the road had found its shape. They rose before light, ate standing in the yard with their breath smoking, and were moving by the first bell. Rooke rode up front and slept, or let it be thought that he slept. Seln kept his corner of the first wagon's tailboard with the travel file open on his knees, and at every halt he wrote in it, small and neat, and showed it to nobody. Gault sat with one hand resting on the case. The reserves took turns walking beside the wheels to keep their feet warm, and the Shield reserve kept his note in his coat and added to it whenever he believed nobody was watching him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:67:Gault thought about it with the care he gave every question, as though it might later have to be certified.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:101:The others drifted to the wall to watch. Ephram had his file book out. Karis had nothing in her hands at all, which meant she intended to remember every second of it. Seln was at the yard gate with the travel file under his arm, on his way to the stable to see about the horses, and he stopped there and did not go on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:225:"No." Karis wrote nothing down, but Cael saw her put it away somewhere behind her eyes, in the place where she kept other people's methods, filed and squared. "No, I don't suppose it will."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:229:At supper Lira ate with her left hand flat on the table beside her plate, and every so often she glanced down at it. Ephram bore this for as long as he could.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:281:Seln had come round from the stable with the travel file under his arm. He stopped beside the wagon and looked up at the pole just long enough to read it, and then made a small mark in the file, and Cael, who could see the page upside down, watched him write the crossroads, the day and the hour in his narrow upright hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:285:"The office logs the road." Seln closed the file. "The road now has a flag on it. It would be an odd record that left it out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:43:He stood and looked at them for a while. All season long, at every meet in the region, the men who filed against him had been men with nothing to lose by it: quarrymen and bargemen and curious Bronzes who wanted to see. At Norhold two Silvers had filed in one evening, and the whole city had boiled over. He had supposed, if he had thought about it at all, that more would come. Here, in the biggest city on the continent, with the best fighters of fourteen houses walking its streets with nothing to do between their brackets, somebody else would want to try.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:49:"Rhagen will sell its chapter in a week," she said. "Any Silver who files now files against a boy who beat Rhagen's captain on his own crown. If he wins, he's beaten a boy with no tier. If he loses, he's the second Silver to do it, in front of eight thousand people, with Rhagen's book in his hand." She put her salved hands into her coat pockets. "That was a hard sum before yesterday. Yesterday made it a worse one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:51:"So nobody files."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-41.md:279:"The captain's a chapter," said Karis. "Rhagen sells its book. Whoever files next will have read today before he steps through the gate, and whoever files after him will have read two days like it." She looked round the table. "I don't think the doctrine's failing. It held. It held perfectly. But every time it holds, it costs a little more to hold it, and there's a little less room." She took her hand off the page. "The doctrine isn't failing. The doctrine is *filling*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:11:Twenty-five was a strong Iron figure. Everybody on the floor who could read a board knew it, and so did she. It sat in the same band as half the Iron semifinalists of the cycle. And the semifinal before it had been twenty-four, against a fighter the gallery-sheets called buried, and a bout in the second round had gone five exchanges, and the line on the young man's certificate, the line the registry had written and the registry stood behind, said Copper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:23:Along the row to her left, Havel was writing something small in the margin of his attendance sheet. Beyond him the other seats were emptying for the midday interval. At the end of the row the twelfth chair stood empty behind the grey rail-cloth, as it had stood every day of the sitting, with its pale square and its two empty screw holes. Ilsev did not look at it. She had a referral of her own still pending somewhere above her desk, the second she had ever filed on a single matter, and it had taught her a great deal about the difference between what a record said and what a record was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:31:Cael had never seen a house do it for a quarterfinal. Houses brought their delegations to finals, and to the Gold, and on the last day they all came to everything. For the bracket rounds they sent a coach and whoever was free. But at the third bell on the ninth day, while Karis was still warming up in the hall behind the second floor, Ternhall's whole delegation came up the stair in russet and filed onto the tiers behind the western rail, forty or more of them, and sat down together in a solid block.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:73:The first exchange belonged to the file. It is very hard to beat somebody who has read your answers before you have written them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:91:Cael wrote one word in the margin of his chart. *Flawless.* He meant Ivenne, and the file, and the two of them together, and he meant it as the most honest word he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:93:Karis walked back to her mark. She did not settle her shoulders. She did not reset her stance or run her hands down her wrists or do any of the small things Ternhall's paired students did between exchanges, any of the things that were in the file. She stood on her mark in the moving light, quite still, with her eyes half shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:95:Cael knew what she was doing, because he had done it himself a thousand times. She was filing. Behind her eyes, he knew, a notebook lay open, and what had just happened to her was going into it, not as a loss but as a page of measurements of the instrument that had just been used on her. She was finding out exactly how good Ternhall's file was, by having it used on her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:103:The Halcenvane rail went quiet all along its length, because they knew what they were watching, and nobody else in the hall did. The file had a line in it about this, the cruellest line on the third page, and Ivenne had written it. *Pressed for time, she goes back to shapes she has already written down. She does not invent well, knows it, and declines to try.* Ivenne was standing twenty feet away, waiting on that line. If that sentence was still true, she had already lost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:109:Her counters went where the file sent them, on time, every one, to the places where the file's Karis would be. The Karis on the floor was not in any of them. She had built nothing, so there was nothing to arrive inside before it was finished. She had anchored nothing, so there was no planted foot to catch. She went about the floor plainly on her feet, and when she made a contact she made it in passing, on the move, with no frame round it at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:123:The motion she was using was Lira's: never a step that bought only one thing, every yard of floor paid for twice. Her open gaps were Brom's. She was leaving herself unguarded in three places on purpose, and putting a price on each, so that when Ivenne's file sent a counter to take an opening, the opening cost more than it gave. And underneath it all was the thing she had watched the whole circuit do all year, from a hundred gallery rails, and charted, and never once done herself: holding nothing back. Not a contact. Not a step. Not a plan.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:139:She put the file down. Cael saw her do it. She came off her mark for the third exchange and her counters stopped going to the places on Ternhall's map. She began instead to read the woman in front of her from nothing, like a stranger met on a floor in a strange town. It was the right fight. It was the only fight left. And Ivenne was good at it, far better than Cael had feared, because Ternhall's paired doctrine was a doctrine of reading, and its best student could read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:141:She forced two real contests in the first minute. Both of them were honest, and both of them cost Karis. The first was a counter Karis had to take instead of getting ahead of, and Cael saw her left wrist turn under it, badly, and saw her hold the hand close for a breath afterward. The second was a feint that would not have fooled the Karis in the file and nearly fooled this one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:157:Ivenne had spent one exchange on the file and one on finding out that the file was wrong. She had three left, at most.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:195:Ivenne saw it at once. Every line of the file came back into her body at the sight of it, and Cael saw her counter commit, fast and sure and full, toward the third anchor, toward the flank, toward the place where the file promised the frame would close.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:201:Cael saw it happen and could never afterward have said how long it took. It was less than a beat. In the middle of her own doctrine, in the middle of a stroke she had made a thousand times, Karis felt the file fighting with her, felt Ivenne's counter arriving at the place her own body was about to take her, and broke the stroke off. The third anchor never went down. The frame was left standing open with two sides of it built, like a sentence broken off before its last word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:257:*She did in one exchange this afternoon what I've done every day since I can remember,* Cael wrote, when the others had gone up. *She went out on a floor as somebody no file had a page for. It took everything she had. Both palms and her left wrist and the last of whatever it is that lets a person make things up as they go.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-35.md:17:Twice somebody called Lira's name from a doorway. Once a girl of about ten ran alongside them for half a street, shouting *Bluff! Bluff!*, until her mother caught her by the collar and hauled her back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-35.md:175:She had come to Norhold with everybody's files in her head and had needed none of them. Now she had found something she had no file for. So she was writing one, standing there, in the space of a single interval.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-35.md:225:Cael felt it go wrong in his own instrument.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-35.md:299:He thought about his own file, the one nobody had the whole of. Nobody had ever got him there either. He was not sure, standing at the rail, whether that was a strength or only a place nobody had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-40.md:135:"I have," she said. "It's called having a house." She put her good hand on his arm for a moment. "Go and eat something. Brom's ordered for you. He hasn't told anybody, but there's a plate in the common room with your name on it in gravy."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:21:*The push I took from the Bronze at Ardenmere. Flat on two plates on the bluff and one in a felt bag at the mill town. Four blows a sitting, paid in the right shoulder. Not asked for since the mill town's plate.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:71:"No price on the fifth man of the bluff! None! The house won't quote him! Come and see the thing we can't price!"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:77:Nobody else said what everybody in the court was thinking. Auremont had not filed its squad yet. When it did, whatever else the list held, it would hold the Gold, and there was no rule anywhere in the trial's pages to keep her off the floor. A scenario floor had no tiers on it. It had ground, and the people each house sent out to stand on the ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:97:"Filed," she said. "Go to bed. All of you. Rooke wants you at the second bell." She looked at Cael. "And happy birthday."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:109:"Withrow's filed," he said. "Somebody in the west tower will decide in the morning whether they like it. I'm not going to spend a night waiting to find out what a clerk thinks. We'll plan it as filed, and if they say no, I'll have wasted an evening, and I've wasted worse."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:171:"The Halcenvane squad, Chief Adjudicator," said the clerk. He was a tall careful man with ink on his second finger, and he held the slip as if it might go off. "Filed at dusk with the chancellor's signature. Five names and the rule's number. Nothing else."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:185:"The rule says roster," he said. "A house's roster is whatever its enrollment book says it is. That book was certified at the start of this year by the house's own registrar. A regional office questioned it, was answered, and printed it." He laid the slip on the open page. "There is nothing to rule on. There's only a list."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:205:It was one sheet, sealed, and it said that the Halcenvane squad was approved as filed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:207:"Within the hour of opening," said Bracken. "I'm told the Chief Adjudicator read the rule aloud once and approved it without a hearing." He sat down, which he rarely did at their table, and took a piece of bread, which he more rarely did. "I'm also told he was relieved. The trial office wants it known that it was not relieved, being an office, but that he was." He buttered the bread with great precision. "I don't mind telling you I was relieved myself. I've been sleeping badly on that clause for a year. Last night I slept like a quarry stone." He considered. "That's twice since the bluff. I may be getting used to it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:209:By the third bell the notice was on the boards in the outer court. HALCENVANE — SQUAD AS FILED.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:3:Brom's quarterfinal took nine minutes in the hour after noon, against a valley house's Stone who had read both editions of his file and had sensibly decided to trust neither. It did the Stone no good at all. Brom won in three exchanges without once doing anything that anybody in the gallery would still remember by supper, and walked off the floor looking pleased about that too.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:7:Cael knew her before she had her coat off. She had been a sheet in Rooke's profiles stack in the first week of the year, marked more heavily than the sheets round it: a fair-day fighter from a market house in the hills, Iron Skin like Brom, Copper Rank Four on her certificate, with four bouts on her record and every one of them won and every one of them short. *Rank Four on paper. Fights like a Seven. Buried.* At the wool town she had walked Brom backward round his own chalk by the arm in front of a muttering gallery, and he had beaten her two touches to one in the fourth exchange, and the figures had come up twenty-three to twenty-two.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:25:Cael had never seen him fight like it, and it took most of the first exchange to understand what he was looking at. Brom did nothing new. He did not funnel anybody anywhere. He did not leave a door open on purpose or rent anything out at a discount. He stood in the middle of the floor and fought her with the plain heavy things the whole circuit already knew he had: the drive, the forearm, the left door that changed hands in the turn. Every one of them was on somebody's page already, and every one of them he did exactly as the page said he would, as if he were reading his own file aloud to the man in the third row and adding nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:77:Brom thought about that. "She earned the semifinal. I only gave her the rating." He folded the wraps over his arm. "Somebody ought to file for her. Somebody ought to have done it a year ago, at the wool town, and I'd like to know why nobody has."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:85:Rooke did not wait for the plates to be cleared.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:87:He sat at the head of the long table with the coaching file open beside his cup and his supper going cold, and as soon as Lira had sat down he turned to her and read her the exchanges, her side first, as he read every result to every one of them, in full.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:91:Then he closed the file and put his hand on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:93:"Four to nine. And a twenty-seven to her twenty-nine. You know what those numbers are? They're a measurement. They're an honest one, of how far you are from Silver, and nobody's handed you one before." He tapped the file. "A tenth on every window. Two points on the figures. That's the distance. That's all it is, and now you know it to the inch. I've had fighters go their whole careers and never once find out how far off they were. They'd lose to somebody better and tell themselves it was luck, or the floor, or the panel, and they'd never know the size of the gap well enough to start closing it." He looked at her. "You know the size of it now. That's what you bought this morning."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:107:Lira looked down at her plate, which was full and which she had not touched. Then, slowly, she picked up her fork and began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:113:Cael read it, and then read it again, and understood the last two words. Once in the standings, where it would be printed and posted and filed with everybody else's result. And once by Withrow herself, in whatever ledger she kept that nobody else would ever see. It was perfectly plain which of the two she thought would last longer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:121:He reached behind him for the Rhagen file. It was thicker now than it had been at the start of the week, with Cael's charts of Marek's bouts folded in among the shop's pages. He laid it in front of Brom and put one finger on the cover.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:163:Lira thought about that for the length of a street. At the guesting-house the common room was still lit, and the others were in it in their usual places: Brom at the table with the Rhagen file, Karis in the corner with her notebook open and nothing written in it, Ephram reading Rhagen's book aloud upside down to make Brom laugh, and Gault asleep in the good chair by the fire. Cael sat down on the bench by the hearth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:211:"The instrument's answer. No coaching. The same as you gave Lira."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:219:"The file is accurate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:221:"The file is *finished*," said Cael. "You're not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:223:He watched her take the sentence apart, turning it over in her hands to find where it joined. He saw her find the join. A file was closed on the day it was written. The person it was written about went on living after the ink was dry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:239:"Ternhall's file will fight the woman who left. Halcenvane sends the one who arrived."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:45:At the end of the row was the twelfth chair. It had been held, unplated, for thirteen days before anybody sat in it, and had been empty again since the day of the duelist. There was a man in it now. He wore the plain grey of an inspecting officer and nothing on his collar. Seln said he had come in by the east stair an hour before the brackets began, alone, with no case and no clerk, and had sat down, and had not moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:57:Cael had read the crews' certificate the night before, pinned in the tunnel with the foreman's name and Auremont's risk officer's beneath it. The floor was true to the specification. Every bolt had been driven and every peg checked. It was written down and signed. Umber had signed it too, as the office required. And here he was in front of eight thousand people, testing it again with his own foot, as if the signatures were the beginning of knowing a thing and not the end of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:59:Cael watched him all the way round, and something in his chest that had been held tight since he woke let go a little. Whatever this man's instrument said this afternoon, it would be what the instrument said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:109:Then the protocols were read, and then they were read again. Every bout above Silver had them read twice, Seln had said, so that nobody could claim afterward not to have heard. The referee and the panel each held the power to stop the bout, either without the other, and nobody could appeal it. There were two healers at each fighters' gate. If fire went where nobody meant it to go, the masts would take it, and the cables would carry it down into the trench, and the trench would carry it off toward the east break, out of the bowl. On the second reading Cael listened for the one thing he had marked on the first. Every line of it was measured in tiers. Every threshold in it, for when a healer should stand and when a bout should stop, was set against a fighter's tier. There was one fighter in the bout to whom none of it could be set.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:151:And as he read her he understood what she was doing. Her lanes were not full. He had seen her full lane on the trial floor and these were not that. They ran a little short, and a little shallow, and each one came at a slightly different speed from the one before, as if she were turning a dial. She was not trying to finish him. She was finding out where he stopped. It was what Gault did with a plate before he measured anything against it: take the baseline first, honestly, so that every reading after it would mean something. She was taking his. And then she would fight a little above it, so as not to waste either of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:5:He had not told anybody he was coming, because nobody had asked, and because he had not been sure himself until the morning he left. The west tower's notice about the exhibition ring had come to his desk among the liaison papers, copied by fast post as everything about Norhold was copied to him now, since he had ordered the files. *The ring to be raised when the brackets clear the main floor. Bouts to be heard in order of filing.* He had read it at his table with the pigeons outside, and turned it face down, and turned it up again. Then he had written in his day book *Norhold, on observation. Not requested by any office,* and told his clerk he would be away for a week, and packed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:21:He followed her along the back of the gallery, past the public benches and behind the row's plated chairs, to the end, where the grey rail-cloth ran on in front of a chair with a pale square on its back and two empty screw holes. She lifted the cloth for him and let it fall again when he had sat down, and went back to her stair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:61:He had answered them at length, in his own language, and been told his answers had been entered. He thought about the digest, and the row's report, and the files in their two boxes, and the seating plan with his grade on it in a stranger's hand. Paper about paper. Some of it competent and some of it careful and all of it written for somebody to read somewhere else, by people who had been told what it was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:75:He went along the back of the row without hurrying, behind the plated chairs, past Ilsev, who did not turn, and past Havel, who did. He nodded to Havel. He went down the east stair, where the young steward stood aside for him with her list against her chest, and out by the side door into the street, where the afternoon was going gold over the roofs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:95:At the far end of the table Seln had been squaring the evening's papers into the travel file, as he did every night. He finished. He fitted the last sheet under its clip. Then, instead of closing the file, he laid one hand flat on top of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:103:Seln closed the file and took his hand off it, and went back to being a man at the end of a table who kept the papers. The table let him. It sat for a while with what he had said, and then, because it was supper and there was bread to pass, it began to talk again about other things, a little more quietly than before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:149:On the corner of the fish market there was a public house with two doors. The front one was propped open on a room full of wharfmen and smoke. The other, round the side, was shut, with a small brass plate screwed to it at the height of a man's eyes. Cael stopped and read the plate. It said that the back room was kept for ranked custom, and it gave the rank in a form of words that the registry had stopped using before Cael was born. Nobody had changed the plate. Nobody had needed to. Everybody who used that door knew what it meant.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:165:Cael bought it. Hesk would want it. Hesk would say something withering about the eyes and keep it in the workshop drawer with the good files for the rest of his life.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:167:The likeness-seller took the copper and made change and handed him the sheet rolled up, and said "Good luck to the bluff," because the sheet had the bluff's name on it, and did not look at his face at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:171:At the third wharf a woman was playing a fiddle for the holiday crowd with her case open at her feet. She was good. She played as people play who have done it on cold wharves for twenty years and are paid in coppers and do it well anyway. Cael stopped, as Hesk had taught him to stop for anybody who knew an instrument, and listened through to the end of the tune, and put a coin in the case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:7:There was not much. A bundle for Rooke from a coaching house in the south. A thin packet for Withrow, sealed, which went into the inner pocket of the travel file unopened. A card for the boy in a plain hand he knew from a Greyvane file. An envelope for the boy with the Ardenmere circuit ledger's stamp on the flap, which could only be one person.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:21:He put Rooke's bundle and Withrow's packet into the travel file and locked the file in his room. He took off the coat he wore for the office, the dark one with the deep pockets, and hung it on its peg, and put on the plain brown coat that he wore for nothing in particular. He put the card and the envelope in its pocket. He put the parcel under his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:29:Through the nearest window he could see the long table at the back, and the five of them round it, and the plates. He could also see, along the side of the room, the counter, where a man could sit on a stool with a cup in front of him and his back to the wall and see the whole room and everybody in it, and not be at anybody's table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:61:Brom was opposite him, with his wrapped forearms resting on the table and a plate of something in front of him that had certainly not been there when the proprietor opened her door. As Cael sat, a girl from the kitchen came and put down a basket of bread between them. Then she brought a pot of mussels in a dark, buttery broth, and a dish of potatoes cut thick and salted with grey salt, and went away again. Nobody had asked her for any of it. Brom looked at the mussels with mild interest, as a man looks at weather he had expected.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:77:She had them under her on the bench, palms down, both of them, and when she saw him see it she went faintly pink and took them out and put them on the table, and they lay there, empty. There was no notebook beside her plate. There was no document wallet at her feet or pencil behind her ear. She had come to the harbour with nothing whatever to write on, and she had found, by the look of her, that her hands did not know what to do about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:93:"It's balanced," he said, sitting down, still breathless. "The whole gate. It weighs as much as this building and it's balanced so exactly that one man can move it with one hand, if he moves it at the right time. If he moves it at the wrong time, nothing on earth can shift it." He unfolded the paper and laid it among the plates. "He gave me this."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:109:He crossed the shop to the long table. He put the parcel down in front of Cael, and laid a card and an envelope on top of it, and said, "The pouch. This morning's," in exactly the voice he used across the counter at home on the bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:115:Brom saw it too. Without looking up from his plate he put out one wrapped hand and moved the pot of mussels six inches along the table, to the empty end of the bench beside him, where nobody was sitting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:121:Then it went on, and five people managed it without a word between them. Lira, in the middle of telling Ephram that he would not be allowed to wind anything else today, reached down the table without looking and set a clean cup in front of Seln and filled it from the cider jug, and went on talking. Brom went back to his plate. Ephram turned round and asked Seln, quite seriously, whether the office happened to know what the harbour's lock gates were made of, because the lock-keeper had said oak and Ephram thought he had seen iron, and Seln said, "Both," and Ephram said, "I knew it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:129:Cael ate his fish. He kept his face on his plate. He put the moment away very carefully, in the place where he kept the few things he meant to have for the rest of his life.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:183:"The office," said Seln, "was once required to travel nine days by coach in the company of a clerk who whistled. The clerk whistled one tune. It had four bars. The office counted the repetitions on the seventh day as a matter of record, and stopped counting at nine hundred, and has never since been able to hear the tune without wishing to file a complaint." He picked up his cup. "That is all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:193:It was a brown honey-cake of the northern kind, sticky on top and plain underneath. Lira borrowed a knife from the next table and cut it into six pieces, and looked at them, and moved one by a hair, and served them round. Brom did not look up from his plate. Lira looked at him down the whole length of the table while she did it, steadily, until Karis laughed out loud at the two of them, which happened about once a season.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:199:*The broadsides come even this far now. Two Silvers filed, I read, and one of them from Rhagen. I once asked a boy whether he could hold back what he had in front of people who meant him harm. These are people who mean you well, and I suspect that is the harder room. You will manage it. — Q.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:217:*Seventeen. My trade says an instrument is finished when it measures true — not when it looks done. You measure true. Let the rest of them look. — H.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:221:Lira reached across and took the note out gently from under his hand. She read it to herself. Then she read it aloud, quietly, to the table, and nobody said anything after she had finished. Every one of them had been weighed on instruments that looked finished and were not, at one time or another, by people who had been quite certain of the weight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:243:"The office has signed for a good many parcels since the bluff," he said. "Kit. Floor declarations. Two crates of the chancellor's papers and one of Gault's instruments, which he would not let out of his sight on the coach. The office does not as a rule mind handing parcels over." He looked at the book on the table in front of Cael, and then, for a moment, at Cael. "That is the first one it has been sorry to hand over."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:7:The opponent had been built for her out of three senior practitioners from the program's own staff. The design staff had drawn the scenario from her projected profile for the next rank up, and they had drawn it well. She could see the care in it the moment the three came on: the way they spread, the way the middle one kept a half-step back so that either of the others could fall on him and find him there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:19:Under the chart, in the senior designer's hand, was the note. *Opponent insufficient for profile. Revise upward.* She had a small drawer of those at home, all in the same hand, all saying the same thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:45:"I know it was right." Zerin looked at the stacked staves. "I've never once gone round anybody in my life. Not since I was twelve. Everybody I meet, I find out how much they cost and I pay it or I don't. I couldn't find out." She set the cup down on the bench between them. "The broadsides say nobody's going to file on him now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:79:That night Daeva read the file again, the third time since the trial.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:81:She had bought it herself on the evening of the trial, from a shop in the delegation quarter where nobody from the program would think to look for her. She had gone in her plain cloak with the hood up and put a copper on the counter and asked for the enrollee's file, the full one, and the shop's clerk had wrapped it in brown paper and handed it over without once raising his eyes. Nine pages in grey board. She had carried it home inside her coat like something stolen and read it twice through at the table before she slept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:85:It gave his name, and his house on the bluff above some northern river, and the clause of an old charter that had obliged the house to enter him. It gave fifteen exhibition figures, with a note that he had lost none of the bouts behind them. It gave a circuit record from early on in his fighting, in a city called Ardenmere, and the circuit record had losses in it, which the shop printed without comment, as if it could not decide whether they mattered. It used the phrase *peculiar timing* four times. And it had a new last leaf, sold separately for a copper, which said in italics that he had survived her lane at trial speed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:91:She did not try to work out from the file what he was. She caught herself not trying, and wondered at it, and decided after a while that she did not want to learn it from paper. She had stood within a pace of him on a floor. If there was anything to find out, she would find it out the way she had found out everything worth knowing in her life, standing up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:95:It never did, anywhere. Every room she had slept in since she was sixteen had a single pane of thick leaded glass, with a small brass plate on the sill to say what it was rated for, and the rating was always her own assessed ceiling and a margin. The program's facilities office ordered them. She had stopped trying latches years ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:99:She stood there a long time, with the file shut on the table behind her. Then she went to her travelling case and put her hand into its lining.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:163:He had been out in the middle near the round of old oak, and she had laid the lane at him, and when it closed he had been two steps to one side of it. Not a burst. Two steps on his own feet, taken before the lane came. Nobody had ever been early on one of her lanes. The file could not tell her how he had done it and she found she did not want to guess. Guessing was what people had done to her all her life.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:175:She had not filed that night. She had stood at this same glass and decided to wait.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:247:"I won't read you the objection," said the head at last. "You'll have read it. You read everything." He looked at the painted founder rather than at her. "I'll only say that it was written by people who are fond of you, in a hurry, and that every line of it is true. A filed bout was never meant for this. The underwriters have no table for it. There's no bout like it on any record anywhere. I'd hoped that would be enough." He breathed out. "And in a few days you are to stand at the closing in front of every house on this continent with our banner behind you. That's all. That's the whole of what the program has to say."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:11:Cael stood at the west gate with the others and listened to the roar and read it, because reading things was what he did when he was frightened. Under it he could hear two hopes at once, the holders' and the bluff's, shouted by the same people in the same breath. The holders against the house with the blank. The Gold against the boy nobody could rate. He did not write any of it down. There would be no time for writing on that floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:57:Cael ran the middle. He had no platform of his own. He stood a little north of the barrier between the two northern ramps, where he could see across the open ground, and he read the traffic. He read weight and direction and where every fighter on the floor was going a fraction before they went, the ordinary read, the thing his file called peculiar timing, and he gave it out to the others in single words. The five of them had been growing those words for a year at tables and on floors, until they were almost a language of their own. *Left. Back. Two coming. Gap.* Nobody else in the building could have understood half of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:133:Two exchanges. The marshals had chalked them both up beside the bluff's name, in a sanctioned book, in front of everybody who mattered in the business of fighting, and no clerk alive could rub them out again. It had been counted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:145:Nine of the columns had a tier at the head of them. The tenth had the registry's word for him, in its square brackets, as it was printed on every paper about him in the Compact's files.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:147:She had written the registry's word herself on her own forms more times than she could count. She had written beside it, every time, on every form she had ever filed concerning him, the same short note about the watch level on his sheet. *Authorizing office: not traced.* She had written it on a referral, once, and then on a second referral, and the second was still pending somewhere above her desk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:245:Nobody ever had. In a year of wardens and evaluators and brass plates and panels, everybody who looked at him had been looking for a line to fill in or a figure to post. Nothing in her face was going to be written down anywhere. She meant to find out what he was before she went on, because he was on none of her tables.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:271:*An economist,* Cael thought, *who won't buy what she can't price.* He filed it. It was the first thing Auremont had ever written about him in real time, and it said: *unknown cost, go round.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:289:Two Silvers had filed. There would not be a third from anybody who still had a tier to lose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:301:"I want that understood before you get to the stair, because by the stair you'll all have started blaming yourselves, and I won't have it." He looked from one of them to the next. "Nothing a squad does answers the third. Nothing a bracket fighter does, either. I've stood at a great many rails, and I've never once seen one fighter take a whole scenario floor away from nine others, and nobody else in this building has either. Every coach up there is writing that down right now in his own hand." He shifted the coat on his arm. "So nobody reviews it. Not you, and not me. The first two, and the fourth, we'll take apart tonight, and you'll be proud of every piece. The third goes in the file as weather."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:317:Rooke read all five lines, slowly, at the head of the table. He did not say anything about any of them. He folded the sheet into the coaching file, and closed the file, and sat with his hand flat on the cover of it for a moment longer than he needed to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:337:"I wrote most of that on the road to the mill town," he said. "It's been in my file book ever since, waiting for an afternoon to go with it. It had four more sentences and Lira cut them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:349:*She stopped mid-trial to read me. Gold Rank 3, nineteen years old, and the first person at this tournament whose attention feels like mine. Filed under: things that are going to happen whether I schedule them or not.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:11:Brom walked out across the grey stone to the table. His forearms were still wound in linen from wrist to elbow, and he carried them a little out from his sides. The clerk lifted the plaque off the grey cloth and gave it to him. It was a plain board of dark wood with a brass plate let into it, the tournament's bar-and-ring cut at the top and a line of small type underneath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:17:Cael had seen Lira hold the Copper Crown like that, once, on the bluff. He had not expected ever to see it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:21:He carried it up the guesting-house stair the same way and set it on the long table beside his plate, upright, against the water jug, where it stayed all through supper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:31:Then Seln read it, once, and laid it in front of Karis. "The office notes," he said, "that the review is mandatory. It does not note that anyone at the registry has yet read the bout." He went back to his file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:49:When he came back he had the envelope folded small in his fist. He went to his kit against the wall, opened it, and put the envelope in at the very bottom, beside nothing else Cael had ever seen him keep there. Then he came back and sat down in front of his plate and the plaque.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:67:*The registry wants to sit him down and ask how it priced him wrong. A house in Velmere has already worked out the answer in ten words. Two notices in one evening, one from each instrument, and he didn't have to think for a moment about which one to trust.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:71:Withrow came up the stair late that same evening, when the plates had been cleared, which she seldom did. She sat down at the end of the long table with a cup of the guesting-house's poor wine and listened to Brom's plaque being discussed for a while, and said nothing until it had been discussed enough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:89:Ivenne spoke first, without turning her head. "Ternhall's file on you is the best document I've ever worked from." She paused. "It was perfect through the first exchange."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:109:The first was the bill. She had told the rail she would pay in full and itemise it, and Karis always kept her word to an instrument.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:115:*A file is a likeness of a person on the day the file is finished. Ternhall's was a good likeness, and I beat it because I have gone on living since it was drawn. That is not a method. I can't teach it to myself, and I can't do it twice on purpose.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:117:*The second finding is the one that matters. I have charted Cael at every rail since Greyvane. Today was the first day I understood what it costs him that nobody can study him back. I left my own file behind for an exchange and a half, and it emptied me to the bottom. He has no file to leave. Every time he walks onto a floor he walks out as somebody nobody has ever written down, and he does it alone, and in all this time he has not once said in my hearing that it was hard. I have written "unreadable" in my notes about him as though it were luck. It is labour. Every day, unpaid, and with nobody to check the work. I ought to have seen it sooner.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:121:He had been studied all year. He had been charted and priced and protested and filed, compiled in shops and bought and sold in every meet town on the circuit, and none of it had ever touched him, because none of it had ever been right. He had come to think of that as the whole of his safety.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:133:Her opponent was Auremont's third Iron entry, a heavy, patient Force fighter whom the circuit called the lattice-breaker, because taking apart fighters who built things was what Auremont had made him for. He was very good at it. Auremont's own file on him said so, and Karis's map said so, and so did the record of every caster he had met that cycle. Each of them had built something in front of him and stood and watched him take it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:189:"All of it," said Rooke. "On one floor. On the same day." He folded the sheet into the coaching file. "So eat your supper. Rest those hands, and that shoulder, and those arms." He looked down the table at what was left of his squad: one shoulder in a heat-wrap, two forearms in linen, two palms in salve, and one Blade with nothing whatever wrong with him. "I sent four of you onto three floors this week and I've got four of you back. That's all I ever ask of a week."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:209:He had been at the rail all week. He had watched three people he loved walk onto three floors and be read by the best files on the continent. Lira had met the fighter the program built and learned, to a tenth, exactly how far she was from Silver. Brom had let the finest reader at his tier take him apart line by line, on purpose, so that he could sell the one false line that mattered. Karis had met the woman who wrote her down and walked straight out of the writing. All three had been studied as hard as anybody could be studied. All three had come off the floor larger than they went on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:211:He had been the instrument for all of it. For a week nobody had looked at him at all, and he had liked it, and he had learned more about being studied in that week than in the whole year of being studied before it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:235:*Three floors this week,* he wrote, before he slept. *Three of us read by the best files on the continent. Lira lost to the program by a tenth and came home wanting a bigger thing than she went out for. Brom let the best reader alive read every line of him and won on the one line he'd forged. Karis walked out of her own file in front of the people who wrote it, and it cost her both hands.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:25:By the time he reached the outer court somebody had already been up the ladder at the great board with a brush. *Filed; acceptance pending* had been painted out. In its place, in fresher white, was a single word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:119:It was a copy of the category's printed form, sent up from the west tower by the observation row, because anything that touched the enrollee's file now came to him without waiting for the weekly bag. The challenger's name. Her house. Her tier. And one word at the foot, in a firm upright hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:141:The sixth line asked for purpose. He had filled that line more times than he could count, always with the exact word for what he meant to do. *Evaluation. Station review. Certification dispute.* For this journey there was no exact word. The form's rules allowed an officer of his grade, travelling on his own authority, two that were not quite a reason. He wrote them. *Official interest.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-53.md:69:Most of the door was about Auremont. Auremont let nobody copy a bout of the girl's and never had. The Storm Path kept its workings sealed with the registry, as a potter keeps the recipe for a glaze. Every shop's file Karis had bought on her in three weeks had been ceremonies with adjectives. What the four of them actually had was the trial, from four places on one floor, and a walk round an empty ring.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-53.md:157:"Keep it shut. That's the case. You go out tomorrow and fight inside your rates, as you've fought every floor this year, and she stops you. Early. One exchange, two. Nobody in that bowl thinks you're meant to win. Nothing new goes on any record. Every file on you stays wrong exactly the way we've built it to be wrong." She set her pen down. "I believed that, the morning the filing went up. I carried it round all day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-53.md:169:"And what it costs." He did not look away. "Every plate anybody's ever set in front of me read me against those rates. Every shop's file was written against them. They're why nobody's ever quite known what to ask of me. After tomorrow whatever I show is in the book, and nobody will ever again measure me against less. There's no getting it back into the box." He put both palms down on the table. "A rate you have shown is a rate you have shown forever."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-53.md:243:*Quenna asked if I could not-do it in front of people who wish me harm. I learned to. It's under everything else I know how to do. It's held in front of wardens and panels and men with plates, and in front of a bowl of eight thousand people on their feet, and I never once had to wonder.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-52.md:43:"Third on the continent, by the instrument's own arithmetic."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-52.md:201:"On the trial floor. You were off the line before it closed. Half a second, less. You had my lane before there was a lane." Her eyes did not leave his. "That isn't in your file."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-52.md:203:"And yours says nobody's ever early on it," said Cael. "Both files are having a difficult season."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-52.md:237:"You've lost bouts. It's in the file. On a circuit, when you started." She paused. "What is it like?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:13:Bracken took its head, nearest his counter, with the registry code's three bound volumes stacked at his elbow, spines toward him, and the four bound rulings from the press beside them. Karis took the near side, with her copy of the instrument open and her notebook open beside it and her pens in a row along the table's edge like a surgeon's knives. Her assistant sat at her elbow writing whatever she was told until the tenth bell. Just before she was sent to bed she put up her hand, as if the long table were a lecture hall, and asked the registrar a question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:19:"Because the men who write codes can't see every case coming," he said, "and they know they can't. *Discretion* is the room they leave for the cases they couldn't see. Most nights it's the kindest word in the book." He looked down at the instrument. "It depends who's standing in the room."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:27:She had come up the bluff road at the ninth bell in a hired chaise from Ostrand, with one bag, and Cael had never seen her before. The inspection had brought a counsel up the hill once, a woman who talked by every account from the bridge to the gate and signed for her chests with a face like a cat in a bath; she had been the Compact's. This was the house's own. In eleven years nothing she had advised the house had ever carried her name; that was her price, and the house had paid it gladly. People on the bluff called her counsel and nothing else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:55:For two years his whole place on the bluff had lived because somebody kept writing it down: a stamp, a renewal, a line in the enrollment book in Bracken's upright hand, every one of them a thing an officer was obliged to do on a day the code named. The provision was alive because it had dates in it. Part two had none. It was the same machine run backward, a thing that stayed done because nobody was ever obliged to undo it. Cael realized he had never once thought of a date as a kindness, and that it was the kindest thing on any form he had ever signed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:59:Cael had counted the instrument at the start of the night, while the others were still arranging their papers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:67:Schedule two was a list of himself, and he read it slowest. It was laid out in the grammar of declarations, as every practitioner's file on the continent was, with a heading for Path, a heading for tier and a heading for rank. Under Path stood the one bracketed word the frame at Denvash had given him. Under tier, a ruled line with nothing above it. Under rank, the same. It was a form built to hold an answer, holding the shape of a question instead.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:69:The file's standing entries ran down beneath, in order: his Kindling at Denvash, the Greyvane hearing and its finding, the provision with the stamp and its renewals, the tournament's entry with all eleven of its words. And between the hearing and the provision, given its full title as if it were the most ordinary line on the page, sat the one he had known was on his file since the inspection year and had never once seen written down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:87:She turned the instrument face down, turned it over again from the back, and laid her rule under the first line of the thirty-seventh paragraph. Then she climbed. Thirty-six, thirty-five, the rule moving up the page a line at a time like a man going up a ladder he does not trust, and twice she stopped and made a note. When she reached the first leaf she closed the paper and set the rule across it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:115:"So they didn't go at it." The counsel turned the instrument to its first leaf. "And they told no story at the front. Four sentences of recital, on a paper this heavy. I'd expect forty. The front is where a drafter usually gives something away without noticing, and in twenty-six years I've won most of what I've won there." She laid the rule down. "This one knew somebody would read him from the back. He left me nothing to find."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:119:Karis took the first finding standing, as she took anything that was going to be minuted, with the instrument's second paragraph under one hand and the Greyvane transcript open under the other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:139:She did not read it out. She turned the instrument round on the table so that it faced the rest of them, put her finger on the paragraph, and took the finger away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:175:"There's a great deal more of it," said Karis, and turned the instrument back round to face herself, and found the ninth paragraph with her finger. "And none of it gets any better."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:3:The file reached Ilsev's desk a little before noon, in the observation office's own case, with the west tower's seal on its cord.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:9:Then she began on the routing, which was her work and had been her work since before most of the clerks in the building were born: a minute on the front sheet to say what the file was and whom it went to next, and why, and under what article. She knew the article without looking. She wrote it in her small upright hand and blotted it, and reached into the case for the dispatch slip that would send it on up the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:15:The designation in the enrollee's file had been her question for a long while. She had first sent it up from Greyvane, plainly, asking who had entered the mark and on whose authority. Twice it had come back down to her with the same two sentences, *no originating authority of record*, and the second time a third sentence latched on behind them, saying the same nothing more firmly. So last year at Halcenvane, on the fifteenth of Reaping, she had sat at the long table in the emptied hall and filled in a second referral, fuller, every date and reference in its order, and marked it a level higher than the office that had answered. In all her years she had never before filed twice on one matter. A question nobody answered was a question to be asked again, through the next door up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:25:Every reader of records learns early that the registry can say no in a hundred ways. *Not of record. Not found. No file.* The registry was full of honest holes, places where something had once been written down and later lost, or never written down at all. She had filed hundreds of those holes in her time without losing a night's sleep over any of them. A hole was only a hole. You marked it, and went round it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:29:And it had come to her desk in the same case as a file from the west tower asking the Compact to look closely at the same boy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:37:What she wrote, nobody ever read, because it was not for any file and not addressed to anybody, and it was the first thing she had written in all her years of service that was not meant to be read by somebody else. She wrote it, and read it through once, and folded it twice, and put it inside her coat, against her chest, where she had never in her life carried anything that belonged to the Compact.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:39:Then she finished the routing minute on the front sheet, correctly, under the right article, and sent the file on up the line as the charter required.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:71:"Nothing written," she said. "Think about what that means for us. This whole house runs on one rule, and it's kept you alive since the bluff. If it's on paper, we can survive it. Every word of the hearing was written down, and that's why we could fight it. The provision's on paper. The roster was on paper and that's the only reason you were on a floor at all." She touched the note with one finger. "He's asking you to walk out of the one place we've ever been safe. Whatever's said up there, afterwards there's his memory of it and yours, and his comes with a grey coat and a seal."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:83:"I asked for a witness once," he said, "with a warden across a table from me. I was right to. That man was a piece of the machine, and you don't sit down with a piece of the machine without somebody to say afterward what was said." He looked at the note. "This one's different, and I've had a year to learn how. Eleven questions in a room on the bluff. Three sentences of advisory. A chair held for him that he never asked for, two notes, and a walk out before the figure." He turned the note over. "Every move he's ever made toward me he's made in ink, and signed with his whole name. This is the first thing he's ever asked for that he can't sign. A man like that doesn't give up his ink to set a trap. He'd use the ink." He folded it. "I want to know what's worth that much to him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:103:*Exhibition, finals day, against the filed Gold challenger of Auremont. Subject fought four exchanges. Capabilities exercised: as in the public record attached, entire. Result and entry: as attached. Observation in this instance is complete, the event having been public.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:143:His eyes went over Cael once. They did it as they had on the bluff, without any hurry and without seeming to do it at all. They rested a moment where the strapping showed under the shirt, and a moment on the wrapped forearm, and on the care with which Cael lowered himself into the chair across the table. Vastin said nothing about any of it. Cael saw him take each thing and put it away, and understood that this was a kind of good manners. In his trade you did not comment on what you found. You kept it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:149:"No. Thanks are the wrong instrument. You owe me nothing, and I didn't ask you here for anything of yours. I asked you here for something of mine." He laid his hands on the table, the right flat, the left as flat as it would go. "I would rather we began straight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:155:"The keeper of this room," he said, "can tell you how wrong each of these is. To the half minute. He doesn't throw them out for it. He knows each one's error, and he corrects for it, and so between them they tell him the time very well." He brought his eyes back. "That has been my work for forty years. Not finding the instrument that is right. Learning exactly how each one is wrong."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:163:"I'm going to give you eight words," said Vastin. "Four, and then four. I have watched them come across my table this year in letters about your file, the first four in the early part of it, the second four since. I want you to hear them in the order they came." He said the first four slowly, each one by itself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:199:"If I gave you names I would be guessing, and I don't guess." He did not say it to be clever. He said it as Gault said a plate would not read past its last notch. "I can tell you what my year has looked like from my chair. A year ago I was asked what you are. This year I am asked what can be done about you. I answer every one of those letters by saying, again, what you are. Each answer goes up the stair. Each one is entered and filed. I am fairly sure none of them is read." He turned his left hand over, and back. "My office still works. It works very well. It has simply stopped mattering. When the questions stop being questions, an evaluator knows the decision has been taken in some room he was not shown."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:207:"The designation in my file. The old one, with no office against it. Is that part of what's coming?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:215:That was all. But it was a great deal. He had not said *I don't know*, which would have been true and easy. He had looked for whoever set that line in the file, as Ilsev had, and found nobody; and it troubled him. It troubled him more than anything else in the hour, more than the eight words, more than the warning. He had just said a thing in this room that could end forty years if anybody heard it, and he would not say this. Cael understood that it was not the record he was wary of. It was the blank in it. A man who has spent his life learning exactly how each instrument is wrong does not like an error that belongs to no instrument at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:229:"With the wrong instruments, for three days," he said. "I came up to the bluff with a file and a plate and a set of questions, and all three told me nothing. A man in my trade has one great temptation, and I gave in to it. I thought the nothing was something being hidden from me." He moved the left hand. "It is the oldest mistake there is. When an instrument won't read, you'd sooner believe it's broken than believe you've pointed it at a thing it was never made to measure."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:233:"Then you answered my fourth question. In full, truthfully, every word of it. And I learned nothing whatever from the answer." Something that was nearly amusement moved in his face. "I have done that to other men for forty years. Nobody had ever done it to me. From that question on I stopped measuring what you could do and started watching how you went about it." He looked at Cael steadily. "The method is not a boy's. That is my whole opinion of you. It's in no file. There is no line on any form where it would go."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:261:Nothing to file. No copy, no witness, no line in any book on the continent. The whole hour was in two heads now and nowhere else. One of those heads belonged to a man who had spent forty years making very sure nothing of the kind would ever happen to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-47.md:21:Brom had not said anything. He had a folded paper of his own beside his plate, and he waited until the others had finished with the broadsides before he unfolded it, because Brom read things at the table or not at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-47.md:23:It was a single leaf, printed small, in the clean type of the commercial shops, and a printer in the delegation quarter had been selling it since before dawn for a copper to anybody who already owned the rest of the file, which by the length of the queue Ephram described was a good part of the city. It was meant to go in at the back. It had the shop's mark at the top and a date and a title, and under the title one line in italics.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-47.md:37:"Anybody below Gold who files against you now, files against that line," she went on. "Win, and he's beaten a boy who got out of the way of something. Lose, and he's the first man in the city to do worse against you than she did. There's no price on that trade anybody sensible will pay." She tapped the leaf. "They printed the end of the exhibitions and sold it for a copper."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-47.md:41:He meant it as a joke, more or less, and Lira laughed, more or less. But Brom took the leaf back and folded it along its crease and put it in his coat with the rest of the file, and nobody else at the table laughed at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-47.md:129:Cael counted them, because counting was what he did when he did not know what to feel. They stood in the rain in a loose half-ring round the case, in coats and hoods and under borrowed sacking, with the water running off their hat-brims. There were dockhands, and two ladies with an umbrella between them, and a boy on his father's shoulders, and a knot of students in somebody's yellow. None of them was a fighter. None of them had anything to file. They had come to watch a man unlock a glass case and read a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-47.md:139:Under the awnings the betting rows, which had refused all sitting to take money on his bouts, had found a way round their own sign. They would not book a bout that had not been filed. But they would book whether one would be. By the middle of the morning there was a price chalked on four boards on *no further filing at this sitting*, and it was getting shorter every hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-47.md:147:The other shook his head. "Nobody wants to be the man he doesn't beat, either. Look at the Silvers. They lost and they're heroes. Who files to be the third hero? It's the same bout twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-47.md:201:She had the frame drawn in the back of her notebook, from memory, with its two filled lines and its column of empty ones. She had been watching it the way she watched any instrument she had predicted, waiting to see whether it would do what she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-47.md:207:There was exactly one name above, and every one of them had been not saying it since the tunnel. It had become a kind of rule among them without anybody making it, the way the long table's rules got made: the name was not to be spent before its time. Brom ate his supper. Ephram looked at his plate. Lira, who left nothing unsaid that she wanted said, put her cup down and looked across at Cael and said nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-57.md:63:Then his own clerks stood up with what he had sent them down to the strong room for at noon, before the bout had even begun: everything the office had ever filed when its tables ran out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-57.md:165:Nine hours on a steward's stool, and he had not slept, and he had not written a word, and his face showed neither. He simply looked. It lasted a second. It was two men who had spent their lives keeping two great instruments honest. One of the instruments had just put in writing, in its own type, that it could not do its work, and they were standing in the room where it had done so. Neither of them nodded. Then the man in grey looked down at his hands, and it was over. The clerk at the end of the table did not write it down. Umber was glad.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-48.md:95:Under it a clerk had added two words in a smaller hand: *filed; acceptance pending*. Under that, in the plain type the board had carried all week, the three bracket finals and the trial's final waited for finals day, and every one of them was set lower and printed smaller than a bout that would put nothing whatever into anybody's standings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-48.md:195:"Mine is Zerin," she said. "I lost to her over four exchanges with the whole city watching, and I've been living on what that bought me ever since. Best thing that's happened to me since Fenmark put my things outside the door." She came away from the window and sat on the end of the bench. "And you've spent a year being measured by things that can't take your weight. Plates and panels and tables and men with rods. There's one person in this city whose measure of you would be worth having, and this morning she asked for it with one word." She looked at him. "I said somebody had to argue against. I never said it was me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-55.md:103:It came along the curve of the barrier from the south-east, close under the cap, and it did not run straight. It followed the wall round. He had no word for it. Nobody on the bluff had a word for it, because Karis had drawn every lane on her wall as a straight line, and every lane he had ever seen had been one. This one curved like a river round a meadow, and he heard it curve, and only just got off the end of it before it reached him. He came out of it with his heart going and his back against the north mast's cable, and he thought, quite clearly, *She has never shown that to anybody.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-55.md:147:The rest of the exchange was arithmetic, done on a damaged instrument.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-56.md:51:He had not made them: he had not filed this bout, or asked for it, or gone looking for it, or put himself one step into her way at any time in the whole three weeks of the sitting. She had filed it herself, alone, at the first bell, over her own house's objection, with one word on the form for a reason.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-56.md:111:Inside, it did not break at all for a long while. From the north barrier Cael could see the dais, and nothing on it moved. Nine pens lay still on nine open charts. Behind them, at their own table, one of the three scribes had stopped and was looking at the end of his pen, as if it had done something without asking him, while the other two wrote as fast as their hands would go. High over the east rail, in the Compact's row, the quiet records officer from the bluff had stood up, and did not seem to know that he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-56.md:230:At the north rail Rooke had taken his hand back. He said something to the air, but the younger healer heard it and said it again at her own supper table that night, and by the end of the week it had reached the bluff by three roads. It was the version the house kept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-56.md:234:Cael looked up once more, past the officials, to the high row over the east rail. Every face in it was turned down toward the floor. Every face but one. The man in plain grey at the end of the row was standing, and going along behind the plated chairs toward the east stair, at the same unhurried pace he had used on the day of the duelist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:39:"An instrument of the registry, under seal," he read from the card, in a voice with nothing in it. "For joint service upon the Chancellor of Halcenvane Academy and upon the enrollee of record whom it names, the two together and in their own persons. Service to be acknowledged in both their hands. The seal to stay whole until it is." He lowered the card. "Are both parties present?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:53:The officer witnessed both signatures in his own hand, sanded them from a box in the case, folded the form along a crease that was already in it, and sealed it inside the case again. Then he took out the instrument.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:59:Nobody touched the instrument.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:67:*Be it known that the Guilds Compact, by its registry, having convened formal proceedings upon the question of the classification designated [SHATTERED] and its bearing upon evaluation integrity within chartered institutions, hereby files a formal challenge to enrollment basis against the said enrollment.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:79:The hall stayed quiet. Somewhere below the bluff the escort's hooves went round the first switchback and were gone. Withrow turned the leaf down again, edge to edge with the ones beneath it, and Cael saw her make certain her fingers would not shake before she let them do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:101:"A registry instrument. Under escort. Served jointly on Withrow and me in the records hall ten minutes ago." He made himself say it in order, the way she would want it. "A challenge to the enrollment basis. Fourteen days to answer. Withrow read the first page aloud. Bracken is making you a copy now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:123:*Bracken's press: the four rulings on the clause. Ask him nicely. — The code, all three volumes, the hall's copy, not the library's; the library's second volume is missing a gathering. — The certified Greyvane transcript, on my shelf in the common room, grey card, my initials on the spine. — Tea. — Tell her she won't see her bed.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:135:Nobody told the bluff. Nobody needed to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:155:"This was on my counter," he said, "when I came back from the press with the rulings. Stamped. Countersigned. In order." He read from it. "*Research standing: floor and archive hours, by application. Applicant: K. Dellenmoor. Hours: the records hall, this night, from the eighth bell until the work is done. Purpose: the house's answer to an instrument served this day. Received and entered.*" He lowered it. "The stamp is the assessment wing's. The hand on the received line is the wing's. The form was built in the recess for an office that had never had a use for it until this morning." He looked down at the three of them on his steps. "Somebody has been expecting us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:163:Ephram found them there a little after, coming round the corner of the lecture range with the captain's file under his arm and the green tape trailing. He stopped at the foot of the steps and looked up at the three of them, and at the cheese, and at the shut door behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:165:"The cohort's asking me," he said. "Forty of them, in the yard, all at once. I'm the captain. They think captains know things." He shifted the file. "What do I tell them?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:193:*They filed. After the hearing I won, and the inspection, and a tournament that ran out of numbers for me, they've gone back to the shelf for one more way to ask. Fine. Their text has lost to ours before. Karis has the code open already. Note the date. The season's open.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:199:He had meant every word. He meant them still. A year ago he had been a boy on a provision, alive because somebody kept writing it down, and a hearing, a panel and the continent's oldest contest had all tried their instruments on him and found they could not make him fit, and every time the text had held him. He had no reason to think this text would not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:203:It was the voice. Withrow's registry voice, reading the first page aloud in the records hall. He had thought, while she read, that she was using it because it was the right instrument for service. He thought now that she had been using it because it was the only one she had that would not shake.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:5:For two months the bluff had been nearly his. There had been the porters and the kitchen and the fire-watch boy, and Bracken in the records hall with his door open, and the four of them and a handful of others who had nowhere better to be. Now the wagons had been coming up the road since noon. Doors banged along the residence front. Somebody below was singing the same three lines of a song over and over, getting the third line wrong each time. The fountain in the court ran on under all of it, as steady as ever, but it was no longer the loudest thing on the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:7:He had Hesk's book open on the sill and Hesk's steel pen in his hand. Hesk had raised him in an instrument-maker's workshop at the far end of the country, and had bound the book for him with his own hands, and it held the Log now, the record where Cael set down each fact with its price beside it. Hesk's travelling clock sat at his elbow, losing time in its usual direction, a little more of the hour every day. In the tournament weeks he had set it an hour fast on purpose so that it would be right by accident, and on finals morning he had put it back himself. Since the road it had gone on losing at its own pace. He told the hour by the fountain and the bell, and kept the clock for the minutes, which it counted honestly whatever it thought of the hours.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:35:*Seventeen. Enrolled on a lawful basis. Third on the continent, with an entry no instrument could finish. Open the year.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:63:The push next, the thing he had taken from the Bronze at Ardenmere. There was a striking post in the corner, wrapped in old canvas. He set his right palm against it and leaned, and then did not lean; he gave it the push. The post shuddered on its foot, and the canvas whumped, and a little dust came down from the beam above. He gave it four. At the fourth the right shoulder spoke, low, the way it always spoke at the fourth. In public he never showed more than a flat hand at a plate. At Norhold he had given a Gold five in one sitting, and his shoulder had counted the fifth for a week afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:119:The record was a public thing now, and it was large. Before Norhold he had carried six, and the registry had held five of them; the quiet thing was on no paper anywhere, and the storm did not yet exist. Only three of the five had been shown in public since he came to the bluff, at rates the house had chosen for him: six bursts on the oak, two contacts of the spark, and a read that looked like good timing. The push, where it showed at all, never went past a flat hand. Norhold had changed that for good. On finals day nine judges and three scribes had written down the Wind past six, and the push at a fighting load, and Reydan's give taking a Gold's fire, and the spark past its two contacts, and something none of their columns had a name for. Every one of those was on paper now in the tournament's own hand, and paper did not forget. But the house did not fight on finals day. On the bluff, in front of the wing's panels, he still fought at the rate the house had always shown, and a little under it, and never over it, and he scattered the figures inside that room so that they would breathe like an honest fighter's. He called it the band, and so did Lira. It was the oldest daily expense he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:149:The minute from the bluff was in the front of this book, in his best hand, three lines long: nothing made to happen; only inside a fight somebody else begins; the cost on paper first. And under it, in Karis's wording, with four sets of initials, the line they had added at Norhold after the bill came in. *Never again in the middle of a fight unless what follows has been priced first, at the weight it will come at.* At Norhold nobody had known the weight. Now he knew it. Fifteen feet, a third deep, the ears, the dust. Eleven weeks of everything leaning. That was what Gold cost, on paper, at last.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:175:The intake had been given the back rows, as intakes always were, and it was bigger this year than any he had seen. Every new house on the bluff studied the old one for its rules before it dared to break any. This one had come up the road with a question of its own, and he could tell what the question was by where the heads went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:179:Ephram came down the row behind and dropped into the seat at Cael's back. He was carrying the captain's file for the new year, thick and tied with green tape, and he carried it the way a man carries a saddle he has not yet paid for, a little too carefully.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:233:He was at the east end of the hall, by the tall windows, alone, standing with his hands clasped behind his back and his head turned a little to one side, the way a man stands in a doorway at night when he thinks he has heard a step on the stair and is waiting to hear it again. The east windows looked down the bluff road as far as the bend above the trees, and anybody who showed at that bend still had two switchbacks to climb before the lodge-man heard a hoof.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-51.md:69:The roar, when the five of them came out, was a roar for plain colours, and Cael could hear the difference. Somewhere in the last fortnight the port had decided they were its own. It had not asked them. A city did not ask. It had simply begun to say *the bluff* the way it said *the harbour*, and it had come out on a dull afternoon, with its bedsheets, to make sure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-51.md:77:Rooke went down to the front rail with a coaching sheet that had nothing written on it, and found Withrow already there. She had watched every bracket bout of the sitting from the upper gallery, among the other chancellors, and had not once come down. Today she sat at the rail itself, with her closed files on her knee and her hands folded on them, and he stood at her shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-51.md:197:"No," said Rooke. "It's for the file." He tapped the folded sheet against his palm. "I've filled that file for twenty years, and I'll tell you a thing about it. Every line I've ever written under a win has a name in it. Who did it. Who carried it. Whose day." He put the sheet into his inside pocket, where card four lived. "I looked for the name today and there wasn't one to write. There were better squads in this building than ours, man for man. Deeper ones. We've fought two." He looked along the row. "You were the only one that's a household."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-51.md:199:Then he went off to the west tower to file it before any of them could think of an answer, and Ephram, whose voice had nearly gone from calling, sat down on the edge of the floor and laughed without making any sound at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-51.md:235:Cael stood in the line with the other four round him and did not move. Eighteen years the house on the bluff had been missing from the lists, and nobody who now taught there had ever stood on this floor in its colours. The whole year had just gone into the office's book in a stranger's voice, line by line, initialled, and there was no clerk anywhere who could reach in and take a line of it out again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:13:The stranger was a Stone, thick through the shoulders and slow in the feet, and he stood at the near end of the painted lines with his hands folded in front of him like a man waiting outside a shop. Cael had seen him last at the wool town, on the far side of a rope, losing to Lira. He had been an Iron of the river academy then, its first entry, and she had beaten him by finding the half a beat in which neither of his planted feet was reading the floor, and going through it. Now he was a Silver, newly advanced, and he had sewn nothing on his collar to say so. Over the recess, Lira had said, he had written to Rooke and asked for the posting, and brought his academy's drill-book with him in a red board cover so that the bluff could copy it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:75:"You saw." She put the cup down on the step. "Rooke says the sitting doesn't read the board. It reads the rating. I'm Iron Rank One on the porter's wall, and I'll stay Rank One on it, and it doesn't matter. When the advancement evaluation comes, they'll put me on a floor in front of a panel and mark what I do, and if the marks sit where Silver sits, that's where I go. Not up the column. Across it." She pressed the tape down at her wrist. "The rank's a certificate. The rating's a figure. Rooke says nobody in the Silver bracket ever asked to see anybody's certificate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:107:The card was still in its brass holder, in Bracken's small upright hand, as it had hung all last year. *Reading room. Assigned: K. Dellenmoor.* What was new sat under the card: a brass lock with a heavy plate, let into the old oak by somebody who knew wood. Its key hung on a cord round her neck. The cord was too long, and the key had swung out over the top of the books.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:121:She drank half her tea standing. Then she crossed to the shelf by the door, where the house's certified copy of the tournament charter stood in its grey card covers among the regional schedules, and laid one finger along its spine, and kept it there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:169:The north wall ran along the edge of the bluff above the river, waist-high, broad enough to sit on, with the whole valley open below it. He had counted the coats from here for two years, and from windows and wagon benches before that, ever since Greyvane. He knew every place on the wall where the angle was good. That evening he took the one by the third buttress, where a crack in the coping held water after rain and nobody else liked to sit, and waited for the bell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:171:The heat of the day was going off the stone. Below him the bluff road went down in its switchbacks to the river street. Across the brown water, the ferry landing stood at the end of its causeway with the ferry tied up beside it for the night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:179:One of the pair at the road's foot was grey in the beard and leaned on a stick he did not seem to need. The other kept his hands in his coat and looked at the river. Neither had raised his eyes to the bluff since the change. The younger coats of other years had craned at the walls like visitors to a cathedral, and shifted their feet a little more each week as the place came clear to them. These two stood as if there were nothing on the hill they had not already been told about. They had the patience of men near the end of a long errand, who know the one thing they have been sent to see and do not mean to miss it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:23:*Daeva's storm. Taken in the middle of a fight she began, awake, by the old minute's three lines, with the cost on paper as far as paper went. Fifteen feet of it, a third deep. The first notice that ever said Gold. The first that ever warned me what it gave might not hold, and that part stands. Everything else I carry is still shifting to make room for it, slower than after any of the others. Gold is heavy. Hesk taught me to write down what a thing costs the instrument as well as what it does. So: written.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:95:Nobody said a word about it, then or at any board between that one and the bluff, and nobody ever would; but every night, at every board, every one of them counted him. They did it the way you count heads at the door at the end of a long day, to be sure that everybody you care about has come in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:113:*Whether the classification [SHATTERED] shall be designated an active threat category.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:117:"You are proposing to classify a boy as a threat category because your instruments cannot classify him as anything else. I have evaluated the instruments. The finding is theirs, not his."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:121:Further down the table somebody asked that the file's standing designation be read in. Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4. By itself, the speaker said, it gave all the warrant the room needed to take the matter out of ordinary hands. The minute then asked, for completeness, which office had authorized that designation.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:125:A young man from the secretariat answered, after some looking at papers. The office that had tried to follow the designation home had followed it into the registry, he said, to a sub-layer under the registry's own floor, and there lost it; there was no instrument of record behind it, and the attempt had been closed. The room made the small pause that rooms of that height make, and a line went into the minute, and the sitting went on. The designation was useful, and at that height useful things are not asked where they came from. It had sat in the file since the first minutes of his Kindling, and it sat there still: a flag no living hand had authorized.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:129:Then the room was empty, and the file lay where the clerk had squared it, thicker by one entry with no number in it. Nothing had been decided. Everything had been entered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:157:On the eleventh day, at a waystation where the coast road came in from the south, the coach from the coast pulled into the yard with its horses steaming while they were eating, and stood there blowing while the coachman got down. The coachman put his head in at the door and asked if there was anybody here from the bluff, and held up a letter. Cael knew the hand from across the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:169:There were two of them, at the far end of a bridge in a market town, standing by a hired horse where the road came off the bridge into the square. Grey coats and good boots. Cael knew both faces. He had counted them for two years from the bluff's walls and windows and the ferry steps. He counted them now from the wagon's bench, and was surprised to find that he was very nearly glad of them. After a city of eight thousand who had all been looking at him at once, two men and a horse were almost homely.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:181:Ephram spent the last three days working out how to tell it on the bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:185:"We went down there and they counted us, and the count's on a staff in the middle of the continent where anyone can read it. And one of us they couldn't count." He considered it. "There's a longer version. It comes with my kit, up the bluff, carried by whoever asked for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:193:First the river, wide and slow and going copper in the late light; then the roofs of Ostrand, slate and brown, coming up out of the valley along it; and last, above them all, the bluff, with its one long roofline holding the sun after everything below had lost it. The wagons went up the climb with nobody saying much, because it was the last mile of a long year, and every one of them had found that it was enough just to be riding it, together, with the light going off the water behind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:197:"I have seven things that aren't a Path," he said. He let the wagon roll on a little way. "A match record no instrument can hold, and five people who watched them fail to score me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:281:The records hall stood across the court from the wall, its long roof and its row of tall windows facing east toward the valley. In the highest window, the one at the north end that lit the stair to Bracken's rooms, a man was standing. He had a small brass glass in his hand, the kind a ship's officer carries, and every so often he lifted it and looked through it down the valley, toward the place where the bluff road came up out of the trees. Then he lowered it, and stood, and lifted it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:289:"Good morning." Cael stood beside him and looked down the valley. From up here you could see the whole of the bluff road's last two switchbacks, and the bend below them, and a long stretch of the river road beyond the bridge, all of it pale and empty in the early sun. "You were at the lecture range's windows on First-day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:5:There was a big young man sitting on the step there with bandaged forearms, watching the court. He looked up as Vastin passed. Vastin knew him from the row: the continental Copper champion, the bluff's wall. He nodded to him, as one nods to a man keeping a door. The young man nodded back, gravely, and went on watching the court.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:9:He had written a note like that himself, once, on the road home from the bluff. Fewer words, and in a worse light, and signed with his whole name. He had thought it careful at the time. He saw now that the west tower had done it better. They had not tried to say what the boy was. They had said only what their instrument could and could not do, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:43:*They didn't score me low. They didn't score me high. They couldn't score me. I have spent three years telling myself I can beat anyone at my tier. Tonight the fairest instrument ever built agreed with the Arbiter, the brackets, and the registry: I am not at any tier.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:93:"The minute from the bluff held," she said. "I want that said first, before anything else, because it's true. Nothing was made. She began it. You were awake and you knew what you were doing." She laid the sheet on the table. "And the third line held too, as far as it went. The cost was on paper. Every cost we had ever seen was on paper." She looked at him. "The trouble is we had never seen one at that weight. We had it written down at the weight of a stair, and a yard, and a copper pot. Not at the weight of a Gold, in the middle of a bout, in front of a city."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:107:Karis initialled it first, small, at the right of the last line. Brom put his under hers, large and careful. Lira signed hers with a flourish she had certainly never used on any official paper. Cael put his last. Four sets of initials on a page of a book bound by an instrument-maker, under a rule that no registry would ever see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:115:*First honest warning I've ever gotten from inside the machine. He built no case, made no ask, took no note. He just told me the weather. The Compact's best evaluator thinks the next thing that comes for me won't wear a uniform or file a challenge. Write it down. Believe it. Keep competing anyway — but from tonight, we build like people who believe it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:139:The steward took the bluff's standard, which was plain and not new and had been mended once at the hoist by somebody's careful needle a long time ago, and carried it past the whole row of fourteen staffs to the head of the floor. He fixed it to the first of the three tall staffs there, the honour staffs. Then he ran it up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:157:The transfer was the oldest procedure in the charter. Cael watched every step of it, because it was an instrument being handed from one keeper to the next, and he had never seen it done.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:163:Cael thought it was exactly like the bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:169:Daeva walked at the head of her house's file, two strides behind the banner, where the marshals had put her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:179:She stepped out of the file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:185:It was not a challenge. A challenge was a form you filed. It was more like what two craftsmen say to each other at the end of a fair, when one of them names a town and a season and the other nods: an appointment, said once, to be kept. He looked at her and nodded.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:187:She was back in the file before the sergeant had decided whether anything had happened. Four seconds. One of the scribes timed it, from habit. She walked on behind the banner all the way round to the champion's gate, and saw it go up, and did not once look back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:189:The procession marshal's office wrote her up for it that afternoon. The citation came to her lead instructor in the evening, folded and sealed, with the office's ribbon on it, for the program's file. He brought it up to her room himself and held it out, looking at it rather than at her, with his head on one side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:5:It was a narrow slip of the seat's buff paper, printed and filled in, with a pin through one corner where it had been fastened to something else. He set it down beside the house's counsel's plate without a word, as if the receipt were the thing that mattered and whatever it had been pinned to could wait. The counsel had come back up the hill in the coach after the sitting and stayed to eat. She put down her fork.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:7:*Received at the registry seat, Ostrand, in the matter of the enrollee of record, Halcenvane: one statement, under the head* Statements entered (Silver and above). *Filing fee, four marks. Paid by the filer.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:9:"Silver and above," said Karis, reading it upside down from across the table. She said it slowly, the way she said a citation she had not expected to need. "That's a fossil. Nobody's filed under that head in—" She stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:11:Withrow, at the table's head, had reached out and turned the slip a little toward the lamp with one finger. Under the printed lines the clerk had written the filer's rank in the space left for it, in a careful hand that had clearly never written it in that space before. Withrow read it, and her finger stayed on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:27:*I fought him. Your instruments failed him, not the other way around. Classify the instruments.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:43:"A long filing gets answered," said Withrow. She had not touched the statement. "A witness doesn't ask to be answered. The highest-ranked practitioner alive was on that floor when the instruments failed, and she has said so. There's no section anywhere that tells a seat what to do with that, because whoever wrote the code never imagined anybody at her rank would bother."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:105:*They did this to me the other way — built the ladder to my height and called it fairness. Nobody files challenges against a favorite. Don't mistake what's happening to you for procedure. It stopped being procedure the day they couldn't score you.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:117:*From anyone else, a joke. From her, the only one of us who has been classified all the way to the top and lived up there, it's directions. That's a strategy. Filed.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:197:On the First-day he had gone down the river path alone before light, which he had not done since the year began. On the Second-day he had gone down the bluff road with Brom at the fourth bell, an hour nobody went down at, to carry a crate of the reading room's copying paper up from the ferry because the carrier had not come. On the Fifth-day the whole house had gone down to the sitting in the coach, as was known a week ahead. On the Sixth-day he had carried two document cases down into the town for Karis, so that her answer to the fee office would go in at its counter by her own hand and nobody else's. Four times in one week his pattern had broken. Any of them would have been worth a line to a man who was paid to follow him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:39:*Ostrand district station; sigil frame reading Stone practitioners one rank above their true rank over one month; integrity provisions, section four; filed by the registrar of Halcenvane in his fourth year in office; frame withdrawn, tested a fortnight, a seam in the casing found admitting damp, repaired, the month's Stones re-read; sustained.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:47:"The original's in the registry's file," said Karis. "I copied the citation from the digest. There's one thing." She took the leaf back and turned it over, to a note at its foot. "In your filing, you spelt *casing* with two esses. I corrected it in the citation. Silently. It's a convention."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:145:It was the leaf about his Kindling. He had not known Karis had written one. It set out, in her plainest prose, the morning at Weaver's Row in Denvash: the circle of pale wood, the registrar who conducted it, the brass plate that lit late, the one bracketed word on the card, the classification line stamped where it had stood empty for fourteen years. Every fact in it was true and every fact was his, and he had never heard any of it in another person's sentences before. He read it in his own voice to a registrar in the middle of the night with the rain on the roof, and Bracken's finger moved down the other copy a line behind him, and twice Bracken said "Slower," and once he said "Again, that line," and made a small mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:167:"Every answer the house has filed in my time," said Bracken, to nobody, and put it away. "I like to know what they weighed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:199:He wrote it that night at the sill, with the copy of the instrument beside him and the answer's first leaf lying in his memory where he could read it with his eyes shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:219:He started at the end, with the morning after, because that was where a person actually lived. The ferryman with a letter, the guild hall with a letter, the clerk with a sealed file and no key. A daughter at Withrow's counter every week for a year, asking whether anybody had begun the review, and nobody ever obliged to begin it. Then the three steps of chapter fourteen in Bracken's voice that would not let itself be a voice, and the third word that nobody had ever had to stamp. Then the six slips of paper laid out down the long table like stones across a stream, each a door, each real law, each opening toward the next and never back the way you had come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:13:Above it, the line he had been half expecting for a fortnight. *Appearing for the petitioner as procurator of record: Jent.* He had read that name once before, at the foot of the instrument's last leaf, under the attestation, and Bracken had not known it then. Cael looked at the registrar now, and Bracken gave the smallest shake of the head: still nobody he knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:49:*Every person who has ever decided a thing about me had a face, and I could read it. Pellin at Denvash, with her brass plate. Coss across a desk. Vastin with his frames. The provost who was frightened and couldn't hide it. Umber polishing his spectacles over a sheet he couldn't score. I read every one of them from across a room, and every reading was worth something.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:51:*They've filed the face out of it. By a section nobody has used in thirteen years, the seat that rules on my enrollment has chosen to be nobody.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:87:The conference was not in the hall. It was up a short stair at the back, in what had been the wool-factors' counting room and was now a district office: a long table, eight chairs, the registry's plain sigil painted over the hearth, and a window at the far end that looked straight down the market street to the wharfs, and over the river to the bluff, with the house along its top.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:111:*The registry code: chapter fourteen. The registry code: the integrity provisions. The registry code: the schedule of discretions. The petition as served. The convening's minute. The clause, as the registry certifies it. The standardization directive, with its schedules. The advisory upon the fielding clause. The adjudication office's referral of the enrollee's entry.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:123:"Thank you," said Karis, quickly, before he had quite finished, and at the end of the table Lira's eyebrows went up as if somebody had dropped a plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:5:Karis had the ninth paragraph under her finger, the longest in the instrument, and had read it out once, and the four of them sat with it. It said that an unclassifiable practitioner inside a chartered house's evaluations raised a question of *evaluation integrity*. It said that the demonstration provision had been drafted for practitioners whose classification was only pending, and not for one who had none to wait for, and so could neither foresee that question nor settle it. And it said that the question therefore fell to the registry itself, under its general charge over the soundness of its own evaluations.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:13:"I filed one once. My fourth year in the office. The district station in Ostrand had a frame that took to reading every Stone who stood in it a rank higher than he was. Not by much. Enough. A month of Stones went out of that station with ranks on their papers they hadn't earned, and nobody noticed until a quarryman's boy came up this hill with a certificate that said Rank Four and couldn't lift what a Rank Two lifts." He turned his folded hands over. "I wrote it up under the integrity provisions, because that's what they're for. They took the frame off its floor and tested it for a fortnight, and found a seam in the casing that let the damp in, and mended it, and read every Stone of that month over again. Then the frame went back on its floor and read true." He looked down the table at Cael. "That's an integrity question, in that volume. A frame that has drifted. You take it off the floor and test it until it reads true."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:17:"Twelve sections," she said. "Frames. Stations. Assessors suspected of being bought. Panels whose records argue with themselves. Instruments, or the people who keep instruments." She lifted the rule. "I've looked tonight for the case where somebody turned these sections round to point at the man standing in the frame instead of the frame. There isn't one. This is new."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:21:"They've stopped asking that entirely. They gave it away in the sixth paragraph." The counsel closed the volume on her rule to keep the place. "Now they ask whether the registry can read you. Everybody at this table knows the answer. It has been in your file since your Kindling."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:27:Cael sat with that. All his life frames had been the things that failed to read him, and he had stood in front of them and been the question they could not answer. Now the paper had turned it round. He was the instrument on the bench, and they meant to open his casing and look for the seam that let the damp in, as Bracken's men had done with the station in Ostrand. He was not afraid of their looking. He was afraid of what they would write when they found no seam at all, because a frame with nothing wrong in it that still would not read was not a thing the second volume had a section for. It was a thing chapter fourteen had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:65:"And now one's sitting at my table," he said. "Somebody, a long time ago, sat in a room like this one and imagined you, and wrote down what we should do with you, in three steps, and filed it, and went home to his supper." He put the spectacles back on. "I've always thought of the code as a thing written by careful men. I'd never thought before tonight about what they were careful of."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:73:"The morning after it's entered, a practitioner goes down to the ferry as he goes every week, and holds out his pass, and the ferryman has had a letter. A short one, very polite, in the plainest form. The pass is held in abeyance. The ferryman is sorry. The ferryman has a licence of his own and a family to feed." Her voice did not rise at all. "So he goes up to his guild hall, where they've drunk with him for twenty years, and they've had a letter too. It tells them their association with him is *under restriction*, and the master reads those two words and understands that his own name goes on the next letter if he so much as nods to the man in the street. So he goes to the records office to see his own file and argue with it, and the clerk tells him it's sealed. Kept, every page, perfectly safe, and nobody may open it. Not he. Not anyone he pays. Not anyone who might stand up somewhere and say what's in it on his behalf." She paused. "And every house that ever put its name beside his gets a letter. This house would get one. After that, every question anybody asked about him would reach us already answered."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:133:"That clause is why he's never stood in a station since Denvash," she said, to the heading and not to him. "It's never once asked a frame anything. It keeps him on a floor in front of people, where no Arbiter ever comes." She underlined the heading. "Every paper they've sent up this hill has broken itself on that clause. This one hands it back to us whole and asks for one thing only. A station. The provision doesn't interest them at all. They're trying to move him back inside the instrument."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:219:He sat on the sill with Hesk's book on his knee and Hesk's steel pen in his hand, and laid his copy of the instrument open beside him at its first page. The entry was finished before the pen touched paper. He wrote it down the way you copy a thing out fair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:9:It could not, and he knew it could not; he had sat on a hundred inquiries into a hundred seals and never seen a petition refused because a press was old. But the question had come up in him on its own, from somewhere under the morning, and an evaluator who will not listen to a foolish question has only taught himself to stop hearing his instruments. So he put the petition down and went looking for where the question had come from.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:13:The list was on a slip pinned to the bundle that had come up in the morning file. *For the evaluator's file, per standing distribution.* Under those words were printed the offices that received such things as a matter of course: the district seats, the stations' office, the archive, the weather clerks, eleven offices in all. The ninth line was his. Senior evaluator, the Compact. Between the stations' office and the weather clerks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:15:Under the slip was the instrument itself, and at its last leaf, where petitions keep the table of authorities they rely on, there were nine lines. Eight were sections of the code. The seventh, in a clerk's even type, was his. *Advisory of the Compact's senior evaluator upon the fielding clause (adopted).* He had not needed to turn to the paragraph; he had written those three sentences in this chair. Somebody had filed his title between a section on frames and a section on discretion.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:55:It was in the boy's file in the same drawer, the clerk's ruled page from the room twelve feet by fourteen on the bluff. The first thing his eye went to, as it always did, was not any of his questions but the line near the foot where one was written going the other way, the boy's to him: *What is the Compact evaluating? Me, or the provision?* The second thing was the line beneath his own eleventh question. He had ruled it himself, before the interview, for a twelfth, the way he always ruled one more line than he expected to use. It was empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:65:He struck the sentence through and put the scrap in the stove, and put the ruled sheet back in the file, and went home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:73:The junior who had pinned it had come up the too-steep stair that afternoon with the girl's file under his arm, nervous and good, and laid it out on the desk because Vastin had made him lay it out himself. Her floor work this season had run on ahead of what her station had written of her, by a margin he had measured four ways and could not make go away. "She doesn't do anything wrong, sir," the junior had said. "That's what I keep coming back to. She does everything her Path says she should. She just does it as if the station had written down somebody a year younger." He had turned his notes round so that Vastin could read them. "I measured it with the frame, and by the clock, and by her bouts, and against the tables. It's inside tolerance every way I measure it. I still don't like it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:77:Under the station's line, in the junior's hand, was the recommendation they had settled between them: *Continue observation.* The hand was still deciding what shape it meant to be, and seeing it, Vastin had remembered a smoking stove in a district house a long time ago, and his own file under his own arm, and a senior evaluator, long since a name on a plaque, telling him the thing every evaluator is told once: *Write what the instrument says. What you feel about it is information about you.* He had handed the same sentence on that afternoon, in other words. It had been easier to say than to hear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:93:Vastin stood a while and watched him work. Then he went back up the stair, and did not think about the frame again for most of the day. He found it in his mind that evening as he put the copy back in *held* one more time: a true instrument, and men who would rather it said something easier.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:106:*The proceedings' evaluation seat is fully constituted. Supplementary findings are not contemplated by the schedule.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:112:Under it in the tray lay the first sheet he had started, with a single half-line on it, struck through corner to corner so that it could still be read. *The hall's request for further time is not contemplated by*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:118:*Minute of the 40th: evaluation seat, to be called. Refusal of the 44th: fully constituted. Who sat down in those five chairs in four days, without asking this office for the file?*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:142:The rest he could have said without the pages. That the proceedings had in their hands already the one honest instrument there was, three years of the record, kept by officers who could see. That a re-evaluation whose answer was known in advance was a procedure wearing an evaluation's coat. That when the standard protocol fell silent in front of this boy the silence was the protocol's own. That the protocol could not read him. Somewhere in the middle was a sentence he had spoken at inquiries for forty years about frames, *the instrument is not at fault; it has told you where its reach ends*, and last night, for the first time, he had written it about a person, and sat with his pen lifted for a long while before he let it stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:144:He had gone home at the ninth bell, later than usual, as he had said he would. The porter had been at the lodge door with his lamp, looking out at the rain, and had said "Late, sir," in the voice of a man noting a thing for the record, and Vastin had agreed that it was. The street had been empty and loud with water. He had walked it without hurrying, with his collar up and his left hand in his coat, and he had found that he was not thinking about the seat, or the five chairs, or who had sat down in them. He was thinking about a boy standing still in a frame that had stuttered and gone dark, three days on the bluff, not moving, not asking what it meant, waiting to be told what the instruments had found. And he was thinking that, of all the officers who had stood in rooms with that boy, he was the one who had written down what that silence meant, and that nobody was going to be allowed to read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:5:The refectory ran the length of the old range under the bell tower, three long tables and a short one under the windows, and the house had always sat where it liked. Cael sat where he always sat, at the end of the middle table by the pillar, with his back to the wall and the door in front of him. Lira was across from him, eating as if somebody might take the plate away. Brom was beside her with two plates. Karis had a book propped against the salt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:13:They were not good at it. One of them was a Mire boy who kept glancing up and then down again as if the ceiling had spoken to him. The other was a girl with ink on her collar, and she was much better. She did not glance at all. She ate her supper, and turned the pages of a small notebook beside her plate with one finger, and every so often she wrote a word in it without lifting her eyes from Cael's hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:33:He stopped at the middle table across from Brom, and laid the sheet on the board between the plates, and turned it so that Brom could read it the right way up. Then he put one finger on a line near the top, and took the finger away, and waited.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:85:Gault sat at the trestle in the middle of the north side with his case beside him on the bench. It was the case with the handle, the one he carried his instruments in, and he had set it down very straight, as he always did, as if it might have opinions about the bench. On his right sat the Ash instructor with her book held against her chest. On his left sat the Mire instructor, a broad calm woman with a pencil behind each ear. Each of the three had a card in front of them, ruled into three columns, execution, control and effect, and the wing added the three cards with no strike, which was the house's own way and not the tournaments'. The desk clerk from the counter sat at the end of the trestle with the wing's book open, to write the figure down when there was one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:113:At Norhold the whole continent had seen him spend past every one of those lines, on a scored floor, with three scribes writing it down faster than any clerk could, and it was in the record now and would stay there for as long as there was a record. A rate you have shown is a rate you have shown. But a fighter did not fight every Fifth-day at the rate he had once spent for a title, and here, on the bluff, in front of three cards and a clerk, he fought at the house's rate and inside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:37:It was a sheaf of paper, bound along its edge with tape, thirty-odd leaves. It had no cover and no title and no note. He came round the table to see it the right way up. Every leaf was ruled into two columns. In the left column, one paragraph of the instrument at a time, in order, stood the paragraph's number and its first words. In the right, under each, ran every section of the code the paragraph leaned on, by volume and chapter and section, and under each section a line in very small print saying where else in the registry's history that section had been used, and by whom, and how it had come out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:75:It was the Greyvane ruling. She had laid it down before anything else, before the paper she was answering had a single paragraph answered, on the bare table in the middle of three cleared feet of wood. The ruling, the certified transcript under it, and Prynn's own copy of the provision on top of both, in that small exact archivist's hand Cael had first seen on a high desk in a room that smelled of cold paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:87:It was a small thing. Her pen came down an inch over the paper and stayed there. She looked across at the instrument lying open on the table's far side, at its first leaf, and he knew what she was looking at, because he had looked at it himself all night. *To the Chancellor, Halcenvane Academy, and to the enrollee of record, Caelen Hesk-ward, unclassified.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:137:He read it at his own window that night, with the fountain going in the court and the copy of the instrument shut in its folder on the bed behind him. Across the top of the grid ran the columns: the sitting, the panel, the day, the finding, the seal. Down the side ran the three times his place on the bluff had been written down by somebody obliged to write it. The grant at Greyvane, from the hearing. The first renewal, at Halcenvane, in the inspection year, before the Compact's delegation. The renewal at the mill town, last year, on the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:147:He remembered the room by its east wall, because that was where the strangers had been. Four chairs set out by Bracken at a guest's distance from one another. The delegation's counsel in the nearest, untying a folder's ribbon with her teeth. Ilsev beside her, sharpening a pencil into a fold of paper on her knee. Havel with a board across his knees, writing the date. And in the fourth chair the Archmarshal, with nothing in his hands at all. Three hours of instruments on the oak under those four chairs: the frames, the board, the pendulum ticking in its case. Then Gault reading his note aloud at the trestle, and the Ash instructor signing, and the Mire instructor adding her four lines about the faulty frame, and the clerk bringing the stamp down on the house's copy. *Renewed.* That had been the day, and the house's copy had been stamped on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:149:But the house's copy was not the only one. Bracken had told him so at the counter two days afterward, when the line went into the enrollment book. The delegation's counsel had asked, in writing, that the registry's own copy of the note go up the road under the delegation's countersignature before the registry sealed it into his file, and Bracken had objected on both sides of a sheet, and lost. So that copy had waited. And when it came back down, sealed and dated, so that the enrollee of record could initial its receipt before it went up again for good, Gault had called him into his office to do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:159:He went back to the table on the sill. Its first renewal's row gave one day, the day of the trestle, which was the day the house's own enrollment book carried in Bracken's small upright hand, because that was the day the house had seen it happen. The registry's file, wherever it sat, would carry the other day, the day its own copy was sealed. The house had never kept a copy of that one. It had gone back up the road the same afternoon. The only place on the continent where both days stood side by side was a book he had filled himself and shut in a drawer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:187:She went on ruling. Every row this time gave two days, the trestle and the seal, and the first renewal's row gave the reason for the gap in a single line. Under the grid she wrote where each day could be found: the house's enrollment book for the trestle; the registry's own file, to be produced on request, for the seals; and for the gap, *the enrollee's contemporaneous record, available to the seat*. Then she reached across the table for the instrument, opened it at schedule two, and laid it beside the old table she had just taken out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:191:He read them together. Schedule two listed his file's standing entries by the registry's own dates, and the registry dated a renewal by its seal. Her old table dated it by the trestle. Two papers on one desk, about one afternoon, nine days apart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:209:And on the way down the stair he remembered the counter, and the four sentences Seln had spent on him in a queue on the third day of the year. *Standing papers on the provision come up for the ledger's half-year check. Keep the documentation current.* The renewals table was the provision's standing papers, set out in a grid. Whatever went down the hill this week would sit in the registry's file beside the registry's own dates when the half-year check came, and a table that disagreed with them by nine days would have been the first thing the check found. Karis had not heard Seln say it. She had kept the documentation current anyway, because that was what she did with documents.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:11:The rest of the room was what it always was. Gault at the trestle in the middle of the north side, with the instrument case set down very straight beside him. The Ash instructor on his right with her book against her chest, the Mire instructor on his left with a pencil behind each ear. The desk clerk at the trestle's end with the wing's book open. And at the east end of the room, at the rail under the high windows, the cohort, more of them than usual, with Ephram in the middle holding his soft-folded chart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:141:"I know it's the number." Ephram had folded the chart along its old creases. "Two years I've kept this, and nobody's ever once agreed with me about you. Now four strangers from four houses write down what I write, in the same minute, and I find I don't like it at all." He had tucked the chart into the captain's file. "First time my chart's been right, and I'd give a good deal to have it wrong again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:143:That was why the chain had come first. The figure was right. Ephram's chart was right. The four strangers' boards were right. It was all exactly right. And it was all going to travel down the bluff road tonight in four satchels to four houses, and be laid beside four bound books, and agree with them; and Rooke, who did not explain anything, had looked at the gallery for the whole bout and then gone and found a chain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:159:Gault came out of his office into the passage behind wing three while Cael was still putting his coat on over the throbbing arm. He had the wing's book under his arm and the instrument case in his hand, and he stopped, which he did not usually do.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:193:It was at the foot of a short covering note, in brown ink, and it was the Chief Adjudicator's own, written large and a little unsteadily, by a hand that had shaken once at a window in the west tower and evidently still did. Karis had the note under her palm and the rest of the courtesy copy beside it: the adjudication office's whole file from Norhold, the match's record, the nine panel charts, and on a sheet by itself the entry Cael had last seen hanging on a nail by the north tunnel. *Result: stoppage, Daeva, fourth exchange. Performance rating: unscorable under standard. Referred.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:197:*The registry having taken up this office's referral, the office sends the referred file in full, for the proceedings to use. It is the whole of the office's finding. The office has nothing to add to it, and asks that nothing be added to it in the office's name.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:199:Cael read the third sentence first, without meaning to, and then went back for the other two, and thought of four satchels going down the bluff road that afternoon with a figure in each of them that was right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:205:"*Not in my name*," said Karis. "That's better. *Not me* is a feeling. This one's under a signature, and it'll be read into the minute with the file, and it'll be there as long as the registry keeps a minute."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:217:"Nothing will happen," she said. "That's what it's for. The clerk reads in every exhibit we filed, one at a time, number, title, date, where it came from, the line of receipt. Nobody argues. Jent conceded every one of them before the first sitting. So the clerk reads, and the seat receives, and at the end of it everything we've proved is in the minute." She looked out at the water. "I want you to watch the room, not the clerk. I want to know whether anybody listens."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:229:It was the house's exhibits, all of them, read into the minute by the seat's clerk in the order the counsel had asked, each with its number, title, date, where it came from and its line of receipt. The clerk had one pace and kept it for everything. A short item, eight or nine seconds; one with a certifying officer's line to recite, twenty or a little more. Bracken's index to the enrollment file, with Bracken's name in its certifying line: fourteen strokes' worth. The Greyvane ruling, the hearing that had kept Cael on his feet in a city he had never wanted to see: twenty-one. Karis's renewals table, rebuilt in a single silent hour with two dates on every row: eleven. The integrity section, forty pages, a night without sleep, ninety-one invocations each traced by hand to its own page: eight seconds. Its title had taken longer than its reading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:231:And in among the strokes, a mark he had made when Jent rose, the only time Jent rose all morning, to enter one exhibit of his own by its number: the file from Norhold. The clerk had read the covering note into the minute with it, all three sentences, in the registry voice, exactly as Karis had said he would. Cael had watched the third one go into the record. *Asks that nothing be added to it in the office's name.* The hood had received it, as it received everything. Jent had sat down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:37:Rooke went down the bluff road at a little before dusk with a satchel on his shoulder, and Cael watched him from the north wall because watching Rooke walk anywhere was unusual; he had business in the town on Third-day evenings, and nobody had ever asked what it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:95:Ephram came by on his way up with the captain's file under his arm and a folded sheet in his hand, which he laid on top of the unhinged pile with some ceremony.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:97:"From my cousin at the river faculty," he said. "Their reading list for the second-years. Week nine." Cael unfolded it. Between a ruling about a ferry company and a ruling about a mill race, in a clerk's neat print, it said *The Halcenvane matter (pending)*. "You're on a syllabus," said Ephram. "A file sits in a drawer and nobody reads it. A syllabus gets read aloud by an old man with a pointer for a hundred years." He took the sheet back. "Try to be one of the dull weeks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:105:He laid it by Brom's plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:23:Cael had the rail at the north end, where he had stood on the second morning of the year to watch the red book. Brom stood on his left with his arms folded. Karis stood on his right with her notebook shut and her pen in her hand. Ephram was three steps down the gallery stair with the captain's file under his arm, where he could see the whole floor and be seen by the whole cohort, which Cael supposed was the point. The girl from the reading room's outer table had found a place against the far wall of the gallery, with her small notebook open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:185:Ephram came down the gallery stair against the stream of people going up it to leave, with the captain's file under his arm and a pencil behind his ear, and stopped beside Cael at the rail's end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:187:"I've started her a page," he said. He opened the file a little way, enough to show a sheet folded in four inside it, new, with only three lines on it so far. "I keep one on everybody. You know that. Hers was a line a month. It's going to be longer now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:191:"Today." Ephram closed the file. "Four exchanges, and which ones she bought, and which ones she paid for." He looked down at the floor, where the Force was rolling his wraps at the far chalk. "And his. I've started him a page too. He'll be worth watching after this. Men who get beaten like that either stop or start."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:227:At supper Lira asked him, without looking up from her plate, where he had put it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:9:She laid the request on Bracken's counter, filled in, in her finding hand. It was addressed to the archive at Greyvane, to the keeper of the founding collection, and it asked for certified copies of the signature leaves of the oldest compilation the archive held: the founders' own hands, at the foot of the founding text, as they had set them down before anybody had written a line of commentary over them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:115:"Enter him as the instrument has him," she said. "Exactly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:117:The officer looked at his list, where the instrument's address had been copied for him by some clerk in a back room, and began to write in the door log's two columns. In the first, for the name, he wrote what the registry had always written: *Caelen Hesk-ward*. In the second, for the standing in which a person came through that door, he wrote what the paper said he was. *The enrollee of record, unclassified.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:121:He filed them where the other eleven lived. A circle of pale wood in an office at Denvash, on the morning of his Kindling, and a brass plate at Pellin's elbow that had stayed dark for eleven seconds before it lit, while a fourteen-year-old boy stood in the circle and did not breathe. He had thought then that eleven seconds was the longest any office would ever take to decide what he was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:153:The door log came in with the porter. He checked it against the list. Every name on the list had a line in the log, and every line in the log had a name on the list, and against the last line the officer at the door had written the enrollee's registry name and his standing, as the instrument had him. Havel initialled the check and set the log aside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:157:He had known the boy would be at it. The house had said it would attend every sitting, and the house kept what it said. Havel did not look at him, because the rotation manual's page on courtesy said that an officer of a seat would show the parties ordinary courtesy and no more, and because looking at the boy was not ordinary. He had looked before. On a pump step at Ardenmere, where looking had been the work. Twice at Greyvane, from the recorder's desk below the bench. Three times on the oak at the bluff, from a chair on the east wall: once as the boy came in, and twice when the frame broke, the second quicker than the first. He remembered every one. He did not mean to add to them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:171:They were not with him. They never came to a sitting. They were in his kit at the inn, between the second manual and the third, where they had lived since the night in Norhold when he had written the fifth on a narrow table behind the east tower, with the line he had read upside down on the senior seat's desk at its head. Five entries. The first was a pump step at Ardenmere, and a form with four questions on it, and a mark in the administrative layer of the boy's file that had no office behind it. The last was a sentence closing a door that the first had found open. In between were three more, and every one of the five recorded something that had happened, and nothing that he made of it. That was the rule of the sheets.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:241:He did not turn round. He heard the draft's paper stop rustling, and her breath close behind his right ear, and he knew where her eyes had gone. His hand moved to close the book, from a habit older than the bluff, and stopped, and lay on the sill beside it instead.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:251:*(Karis read this entry over my shoulder and said "that is not how any of us are built." Noted. Filed. Afraid of it.)*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:5:He was a young man in a wet hat, and he apologised three times on the stair before he got to the door, and twice more inside it. The district seat's records room was a man short, he said. Two men, if you counted the one with the fever. A letter had come in from the registrar of the academy on the bluff, about an order and a seal, and it concerned the proceedings, and the senior records officer had said that the proceedings' own records support was in the town on its days off with nothing to do, and would the junior chair be so good as to check the registers and draft the usual.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:13:Inside the oilcloth were two things. The registrar's letter, in a clear upright hand that wasted nothing, asking that the instrument constituting a certain office be supplied, together with the seal under which that instrument was itself issued. And a sheet of thin paper with a pencil rubbing on it of a seal, taken carefully, the grey of the lead built up in even strokes until the device stood out white: the registry's own pattern, the river and the scale, inside a beaded rim.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:15:The letter said where the rubbing had come from, in its first paragraph, with the instrument quoted whole: it was the seal off the escort order that the leader of four riders had read aloud in the house's outer court on the Sixth-day morning, and that the house had declined at its own gate and let ride away again down the bluff road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:33:So the second was the registry's printed schedule of the seals of office, which was continental. It came out every year in a stiff grey binding, with a plate for every office that had a seal, engraved to the size of the die. The district seat kept the current year and the four before it on a shelf behind the senior records officer's chair. Havel took down the current year and went through it plate by plate, the next morning, with the rubbing beside him on a clean blotter. Then he went through last year's, in case. Then he took down the current year again and went through the index of offices, under *F*, and under *P* for *proceedings*, and under *S* for *seat*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:51:The facilities office was in the district's register of offices. He had expected that too. It was entered there in a good clerk's hand: constituted this year, for the proceedings at the seat at Ostrand, by an instrument filed at the district seat under seal. The instrument was in the file. He asked for it, and it came, and he looked at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:57:*The office named in the registrar's letter was constituted on the date entered in the district's register of offices, by instrument filed at this seat under seal. The registrar is respectfully referred to the issuing authority of the said instrument for any further particulars.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:61:The form of the usual had no line for what Havel had found. There was a line for the office and a line for the instrument and a line for where to write next. There was nowhere on it to say that the seal on the instrument was in none of the registers that seals were kept in. Nobody had ever needed such a line. A seal was a thing that was in the registers. That was what made it a seal.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:71:He read them through. The first went back to a pump step at Ardenmere and a form of four questions, and to the mark in a boy's file that no office would own. The fifth was a sentence on a senior seat's desk at Norhold, read upside down, closing a door that the first had found open. In between were three more. He read them all twice, slowly, the way the manual said a records officer should read anything he meant to rely on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:75:*The seal on the instrument constituting the facilities office named in the order presented at Halcenvane, and on the order itself (registrar's rubbing): absent from the district's impression book; absent from the registry's schedule of seals of office, this year's and last; its rim number absent from the cutters' issue ledger, where the numbers one below it and one above it stand on consecutive lines with no line for it between. Checked three ways. Absent from every registry of seals.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:93:The coach went down the bluff road in the dark with its lamps lit and the rain gone east in the night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:105:On the third evening of the year he had written on this coping that men with no questions get sent at the end of something, not the start. He had not known then what the something was. He had a word for it now. The paper served on the eighth day had begun a thing that would end in a ruling, and the ruling would turn him from one kind of entry in a file into another. That was what grey coats of a senior grade were posted to stand beside: not a boy, but the moment the boy's paper changed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:87:She did the sum on the bluff road, at the second switchback, where the wall hid her from the landing and the gate could not see her yet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:133:Five offices. The house's own counter, the district seat, the Ternhall archive, a provincial registry that kept the Velmere estate's file, and a provincial registry at the far end of the country where an old man kept a shop. Each with its drawer. Each drawer full of true lines about who would mind what. All five indexed under one name and closed, and now nobody was walking anywhere. They were waiting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:165:She nodded, as if he had confirmed a figure she already had in her book, and finished the apple, and threw the core over the wall into the dark, where it would roll halfway down the bluff and feed some bird in the morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:25:"Umber," he had said. "At Norhold. The man who kept the instrument. It couldn't score me, and he had a whole office behind him that would have liked a number, and he wrote *no number goes here* instead."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:29:"Denvash," he had said, and stopped, because that was enough. Every one of them knew the brass plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:103:Every word was true. The boy had attended; he had been examined; the figure was the house's figure, twenty-two and two-thirds, written by a clerk who believed it. Nothing further. In the morning it would go down the hill in the bag, and a clerk in an office would open it and read it and file it, and it would lie in the registry's file on the boy beside the house's own copies from Bracken's enrollment book. Two honest hands, his and the registrar's, one on each side of the file, and nothing whatever between them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:111:The boy's window, on the residence passage, had a lamp in it too. The boy would be at his sill with the old instrument-maker's book, writing his one line. Seln had never asked to read a word of it, and never would. It was the one record on the hill he had decided, a long time ago and without telling anybody, not to know.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:139:In the registry's file on the boy, he supposed, his returns lay in the same drawer as the hearing's finding and the renewals and the Archmarshal's three sentences. If the boy ever saw them he would count them on his own side. There was nothing in them to object to. Seln had made sure there was nothing in them at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:145:He had not left the service. A man in his trade could not leave it and keep what he kept. So he had stayed, and filed true, and kept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:107:She was on the attendance bench, close enough to lean forward and say it into his collar, and she said it before Cael had looked toward the evaluation seat at all. He looked. The grey covers were gone from its five chairs, and four district evaluators he had never seen sat upright in four of them, two men and two women, with their files on small lap-desks. The fifth chair stood a little forward of the others. In it sat a broad-backed woman in the registry's grey. Her files were set at an angle he would have known anywhere, never quite square to the desk and never a hair different from the last time: a counter at Greyvane, the east wall of a room on the bluff, the first seat of the Compact's row over the Concourse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:119:The clerk was on his feet before the parties were called, reading a paper into the minute under the heading *statements entered*: the filer's name and house and Path and rank, the date, the line of receipt, and then the text, in the voice that adds nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:121:*I fought him. Your instruments failed him, not the other way around. Classify the instruments.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:155:He gave a page number in the file from Norhold, once, and closed his mouth on it. Karis wrote the page number down and did not look it up; she knew what was on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:171:It was the names that had made the evaluators look up. Ilsev heard her own read out among them and wrote something small in her file without lifting her head. The counsel read twenty-two of them, one at a time, each with a grade and a house and a day, at the pace at which a person reads names in a room where names matter. The grant at Greyvane and its two renewals on paper, the season's officers under a regional registrar's seal, the tournament's, and three days of the Compact's own senior evaluator on the bluff. At the Archmarshal's name Cael thought the stillness under the hood altered, the way still water alters when something passes under it. It took four minutes. In the quiet he heard a cart's iron tyre grind over the cobbles in the street. More looking, the counsel said when she had finished, than most practitioners get in a working life; and all of it done by people who could see, sealed and minuted, across three years. She turned the rule. Tick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:173:It was Karis's count that made the pencils go down. Cael felt her go still beside him as the counsel gave it: seven times in the registry's whole record the integrity sections had been turned on a person, every one of the seven for something the person had done; eighty-four times on things; and not once on what a person was. Only after the count did the counsel lay her papers face down and read the room the preamble, ninety seconds of plain old words, so that the benches heard what the sections had been written for after they had heard what they had been used for. The testing of instruments. Frames, stations, records, protocols. So that the registry might know which of them it could trust.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:179:Cael missed the shelf. Ilsev had turned a leaf in her file just then and looked, for the first time all morning, at the house's table, at him, for perhaps a breath; and by the time he had looked back to the counsel the rule had turned again and Karis had already ticked the line. Later, on the coach, he would have to ask Karis which rulings had been on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:187:"The petition asks this seat to measure a man with the one instrument that has never once been able to see him, and to call that measuring. Twenty-two officers who could see him have told you what they saw. You have their names. Ask them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:195:The hood had read the ruling and the room had not yet worked out what it had heard, and in that gap Ilsev rose from the middle chair and asked leave, and had it, and did not open her files.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:197:Cael heard the hood's answer before he understood her question. *Its keeping to protocol. Not its result. Nor that.* Ilsev had asked what her seat was there to do, and whether that included deciding if the standard protocol could read this practitioner at all, and the seat had said no to the second. Now she was asking that its words be minuted exactly as spoken, so that nobody could stretch her chair afterward to cover the question. The clerk wrote. Ilsev sat, and wrote the same words into her own file in her own hand, and set the file back at its old angle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:277:"Now you understand the file I've been reading my whole career."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:3:When the plates had been cleared, Seln put four dried beans on the board in a row and said, "Four offices. Ask."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:17:"A provincial registry," said Seln. "The one that keeps the Velmere estate's file. Nine days ago. A query on the standing of the household, the tiers of the people in it, and the estate's registered relationships."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:27:"Then she'll read the query book as well. It lies open on the provincial counter for anybody on the estate's file, with every question in it, and who asked, and when."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:33:Seln touched the third bean. "The district seat. Twelve days ago. A query on the certification history of a Wind practitioner of this house, Iron Rank One, *for completeness of the respondent institution's file*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:35:"Which file?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:43:"They asked for the file."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:45:"Then they got the Fenmark sheet." She looked down at the bean and not at any of them. Everybody at that table knew what was at the bottom of her file, under four years of other people's honest lines, and nobody said it, and she did not say it either. After a moment she looked across at Cael. "Your column. Under my name. That they read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:51:"Mine," she said. "The keeper of the archive at Ternhall wrote to me this morning, privately. Four lines. A district clerk came to her counter six days ago with a sealed request for my transfer file: my old standing, my privileges, the training log from the paired floor." She kept two fingers on the letter, as if it might blow away. "On the Third-day a fee office wanted to know who I was. Now somebody wants to know who I used to be." She was quiet a moment. "That's twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:97:*Grew up in it.* He had. At five years old he had slept under the long bench in a nest of clean shavings, because the stove ticked there and it was warmer than upstairs while Hesk worked late. He had learned his letters off the brass makers' plates on instruments that came in to be mended. The shop's title was one sheet of the district's paper folded in four, in a tin box under that bench with the receipts, and it had Hesk's name on it and no other. Two polite men had asked whether his was in the tin box too. And on a form somewhere, in a column under a printed heading, a pencil had set down *grew up in it*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:119:"Shut every door on one morning, yes. That's what it's for. A restriction that runs one office at a time leaks: somebody hears, somebody moves a deed, somebody's cousin carries a letter over a district line before the freeze arrives. So nobody runs one like that." He laid a fingertip on the board beside each bean in turn, not touching any of them. "Every office finds its own door first, and writes it down, and puts what it found in a drawer. Five drawers, here. Each file lawful. Each as dull as a laundry list. All five indexed under one name. And then they wait."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:127:"Take the bad way first, it's shorter. One clerk, one afternoon, five files carried to one desk; every door shut on the same morning; and the enrollee learns what all this was for on a day when there's nothing left to do about it." He turned the cup back. "Your way, the five drawers are simply never opened together. Each office weeds its old papers in due course, as offices do, and nobody anywhere has broken a single rule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:157:"Write it." The fourth bean went into the pocket. "Give it to the registrar for his pouch, not to the town post. The pouch is locked in Ostrand and opened here, and I open it." He put the fifth bean away. "Letters from a house under inventory are read. Not by anyone wicked. By a clerk, for completeness, who will copy a line of it into a file. Don't give them a line." He stood, and turned his empty cup a quarter-turn on the board, and left it there. "Tell him the stove sounds well. Tell him nothing else he wouldn't want read aloud in a provincial counting-room. He'll understand. He's been minding shapes longer than either of us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:187:"The girl kept coming to my counter after there was nothing more of him to file," said Withrow. "Every week, with her mother's people's address written on the back of her hand in case I had news." She was quiet a moment. "Once, I did. A toll paid at the ferry by the last station, eastbound, in his name. I gave her that. It was the last line anybody ever wrote about him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:215:Past midnight he turned back through Hesk's book to the page where the first pole was written, on the night of the instrument, and read it through with the pen in his hand, meaning to write something under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:223:It was short. He wrote it three times. The first was angry, and he tore it across. The second was frightened, which was worse, and he tore that too, and sat a while with the pieces in his hands before he put them in the stove. The third he wrote slowly, reading every line as a clerk with a pencil would read it in a provincial counting-room, for completeness, and taking out anything he would not have wanted copied into a file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:47:The record. Paper in a subject's file. A query, a designation, a mark in a column. It cost nothing and was never undone, and it was where everything began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:49:The instrument. A proceeding, in daylight, by the book. A petition and a seat and an answer, and every door in it real law. It cost a great deal and was always lawful, and it was what an office used when it wanted to be seen being right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:59:The boy had had the record since before he could read it. He had had the instrument since the first week of the year, in a sealed packet read out by a woman standing in her own hall. Five days ago he had had the knock, at the gate, in eighty-one words, with a woman standing in the one place in the court that could not be got round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:95:He had counted the boy for a year. Two slips on a common-room fire. A season of figures that never once settled into a line. An afternoon at Norhold where everything held back went out in one breath, on purpose, in front of eight thousand people. He had counted all of it, and filed none of it, and somewhere in the counting, without asking his leave, it had turned into the other thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:115:At the residence door a knot of third-years was standing out of the wet arguing about a bout, and he went round the edge of them: seven to twelve. On the stair, coming down, the Current girl from Rooke's program with her wet hair in a towel, who flattened herself to the wall to let him by and said, "Sorry, sorry," as if the stair were hers to be sorry about: thirteen. Two more on the first landing. Three in the corridor, one of whom was the captain, Ephram, with his green-taped file under his arm, who nodded to him and went on: eighteen. Five more behind half-open doors as he passed, reading or mending or asleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:177:*Subject attended to his course of study. Subject attended the seat's fourth sitting as directed. An instrument presented at the house's gate on the Sixth-day was declined by the house; subject was not seen at the refusal. Subject's floor work within the band of the year. Nothing further to report.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:213:"The long way," said Lira, to her plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-23.md:41:"You filed the provision the day the card went up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-23.md:49:"The registry doesn't read my book," he said. "It reads meet records. Stewards' sheets, sealed by a hall, filed with a district. Tomorrow there'll be a line on a confluence sheet with her name on it, and a Silver's name, and a figure. In a year or two some clerk at a counter in Ostrand will pull her file for the advancement sitting, and that line will be in it, in a steward's hand, under a hall's seal. And nobody can take it out again. That's the program. Everything else is mornings."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-23.md:53:"Every fighter I've ever had was better than their file," said Rooke. "The file catches up, if you keep putting true things in it. It's slow." He nodded at the waystation door. "Bed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-23.md:59:Cael had been there once before, at the year's turn, when the water ran with plates of ice along both banks. It was not running with ice now. The two rivers came down from their two directions and met at the tongue's point in a long rough seam of broken water, where neither current would give way to the other, and the town filled the land between them right down to the quays. Barges lay at the quays in rows. The hall stood at the very point of the tongue, with water on three sides of it, as it always had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-23.md:93:The door gave onto a stone apron at the very point of the tongue, with a low wall round it and the water on both sides. In the dark the two rivers were only sound. One came up from the south, broad and steady, the one that ran under the bluff at home. The other came down out of the east, bigger and colder and quicker, and where they met at the apron's point there was a long ragged noise like cloth tearing that went on and on and never finished.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-23.md:123:Rooke had built it at the end of the inn's long back table when the plates had gone: the heel of the loaf on top, a slice under it, a slice under that. He had built it without a word, while she watched, and she had watched until she could not stand it, and then reached across and drawn the bottom slice out from under the other two, so that the heel came down a finger's breadth onto the table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-23.md:127:"Nothing goes first. That's the point of him." Rooke took the slice back off her and put it on his own plate. "I'll give it you backward. You'll remember it better."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-23.md:137:Lira took the slice back off his plate without asking and began to build the stack again herself. She laid the bottom slice down. She set the second on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-23.md:193:She sat at the inn table with a plate in front of her and turned a piece of bread over and over in her fingers, and Brom watched her do it from across the table. Then he got up and went to the kitchen door and came back with a bowl of something hot and set it down in front of her, on top of the bread, and sat down again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-24.md:51:He filed it. He did not know what it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:13:"Ephram read it twice." Brom finished the bread. "Then he took his chart out of the captain's file and made a mark on it, standing at the board, and put it back. I don't know what mark. A gap, I'd guess. He's a man who writes down gaps."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:235:Before he could read, Hesk had walked him down the long bench in the workshop with a hand on his shoulder, naming the tools in their racks. The scriber. The dividers. The little brass square with the nick in its heel. The burnisher, the pin vice, the files from the coarse rasp down to the fine one that Hesk called the whisper. A small boy at the end of a bench full of points and edges was afraid of all of them until somebody said what each was for, and after that they were a row of friends.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:304:"The district seat," he said. "Their answer. It came up in the noon bag." He read it out, as he read everything the house received, every word at the same weight. Two sentences. The office was constituted on the date entered in the district's register of offices, by instrument filed at the seat under seal. The registrar was respectfully referred to the issuing authority of the said instrument for any further particulars.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:308:"It's exactly what she said it would say," said Bracken. He folded it once. "So I shall write to the issuing authority of the said instrument. I've begun the letter." He looked at Karis over his spectacles. "You had it a week early, Advocate. I've written that down too."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:57:"The lower orchard," said Karis. "You can see the lower orchard from the bluff road's second switchback."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:67:The house's farm lay on the back of the bluff, on the long south slope away from the river, where the wind was less. Below the stable passage were the kitchen gardens in their walls, and below the gardens the upper orchard and the lower, old trees in rough grass with their branches bare and black with wet. Below the lower orchard a stream came down out of a fold in the slope and ran along the bottom hedge toward the water meadows, loud over stones. The farm track went down beside it, and at the orchard's foot, where the track met the stream, stood a small stone cottage with smoke going up from it, which was the gardener's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:91:*The proceedings' evaluation seat is fully constituted. Supplementary findings are not contemplated by the schedule.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:113:"No. Think who he is and who you are. He's the Compact's senior evaluator, and he's just asked to put a finding into proceedings where this house is the respondent. A letter from this house on his desk this week, about anything at all, would be on the file by morning; and his finding, if it's ever given, would be read for the rest of its life as something the respondent asked him for." She shook her head. "The kindest thing this house can do for that man is leave him alone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:127:"He knocked on the right door twice," said Karis. "Both of them. The old one first." Her eyes went back to his slip. "I was frightened of his three sentences on the night the instrument came. I've been thinking of him since as the man whose words they borrowed." She closed the volume. "He isn't. He's the man who read the code properly, and was told by return that it didn't matter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:139:Ephram got up from the cohort's table with the captain's file under his arm, walked the length of the room, and held out his free hand, and the first-year holding the sheet gave it to him without a word. He read it standing, top to bottom, like a man checking a bill he suspects of being wrong, and then turned it round to show them the woodcut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:141:"This is your enrollee of record. Look at the jaw. You could split kindling on that jaw. I've watched him on more floors than anybody in this room, and I give you my word as your captain that he has never once stood with his feet like that. If he did I'd have him on his back before the glass turned." He folded it in quarters into the file. "Confiscated. You'll have it back when one of you can tell me what's wrong with the stance."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:151:When the plates were mostly empty, at the middle of the bench, Seln turned his cup a quarter-turn on the board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:153:"They benched their best instrument because he measures true."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:7:The paper itself was a day gone. It had ridden down the bluff road in a saddlebag yesterday morning, after a minute and forty seconds at the lodge window in front of one man with a glass, and whatever it had to say about itself it had said then, to him, and to nobody since. Seln had worked that out at about the fire-watch's second round, sitting up in the wing with the senior clerk's fair copy under the lamp and getting nothing from it but the words, which was all a fair copy on the house's own stock could ever give.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:63:The first went to a man who kept the mould room at the south mill. Eleven years ago Seln had filed one true sentence about him instead of a sentence that would have been more useful to somebody else, and the man had never known which office to thank, but he had known whom. The letter asked one thing. *Was the registry mould mended this year, and if so, when, and was a run made off it after?* It did not say who wanted to know. It did not have to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:67:There was a woman in the registry's western stores who had kept the issue ledgers for the paper that the whole half of the continent wrote its instruments on. Every lot that left her stores went into a ruled book against the office that drew it, with the date and the lot's number and the clerk's initial. She had owed him for nine years. He thought of her at her ledger sometimes, on bad nights, because it was a comforting thought: a person in a room somewhere keeping something perfectly. His letter to her asked one thing too. *The south mill's second run this year: which lots were drawn, and by whom.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:71:It was the rest day, and the rain had decided to stay. The bluff road shone. At the second switchback he met a porter coming up with a sack over his head like a hood, who wished him good morning and did not look at him twice. That was the whole art of the trade. A man going down a hill in the rain with a satchel and an errand was not anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:47:Inside, the long room smelled of lanolin and hot lamp oil, as it always did. The benches were fuller than at the third sitting. Cael crossed lot *nine*, lot *ten*, lot *eleven*, and sat down at the respondent's table with *seventeen* under his left foot, where he always sat now. Ilsev was in the middle chair of the evaluation seat with her files on her lap-desk at their angle and her four district evaluators upright around her. At the records table behind her, at the junior chair, the officer with the careful face had the receipt book open and his pen ready and his eyes on the leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:53:Bracken carried the records up to the clerk's table himself. They were in the house's own boards, as Withrow had said they would be: one volume, half the thickness of a man's hand, bound in the house's dark cloth with the house's seal pressed into the front cover and a contents leaf inside it in the clerk's best copperplate. Every wing-three bout of the year, in order, with its date, its three cards, its figure. Twenty-three and a third. Twenty-two and two-thirds. The band, written out in full, as it had been built to be written.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:83:She had not lifted her head since the seat opened. Through the production, through the receipt, through Jent's compliment, her pencil had gone steadily along her file and her eyes had stayed on the page. Now the pencil stopped. Her head came up. She looked across the floor at the petitioner's table, and kept looking at it, and did not look down again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:103:They were from the dairy. He had written them on the way down in the coach that morning, out of habit, to have the argument by him in case anybody in that room should ever ask him for it: Denvash and the brass plate; Vell's ledger; the Greyvane hearing; the grant and two renewals on paper; the inspection; the clause at Norhold; Umber's *no number goes here*. His receipts. He had laid them out a week ago, in order, at full strength, and Karis had called them an induction and he had not had an answer for her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:153:Cael saw it from the covered walk, a little after the second bell on the Sixth-day morning, on his way across to the wing. It was a small thing. In two years he had never once seen that window shut in daylight. The lodge-man sat behind it on his high stool from the first bell to the last, with his tea and the bluff road in front of him, and talked to anybody who came up it. Now the shutter came across the glass from the inside, and a moment later the lodge door opened, and the lodge-man stood in it without his hat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:205:He sat on his heels behind the bell-frame and heard the name at the top of it go round the court. *Caelen Hesk-ward.* Nobody who knew him had ever said it to his face. It was the name the registry kept for him, the way a shop keeps a number on a parcel, and here it was coming up off the flags of his own gate in a stranger's voice, with an order underneath it. *Attendance. Transport and escort provided. Produce the enrollee.* And at the bottom, the five words he had first read on the morning of the eighth day at the foot of a challenge's first page, and had read at the foot of every registry instrument since, and had never heard spoken by anybody at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:239:"I don't doubt it's been presented." Bracken looked up then, over his spectacles, at the rider and the three behind him. "I don't doubt you, either. But my book has a column for the office that issues an instrument, and I have never yet left that column empty, and I can't write four riders in it." He held the order out toward the rider at the length of his arm, on his open palm, the way he held out a form at his counter that had been filled in on the wrong side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:29:"He's been on the same table as you for two years," said Lira, "and he's never once been in a room with the door shut where you were. He's going to be tonight. He said he's got grievances. I told him to shut them early, and he looked at me as if I'd asked him to set fire to the captain's file, and then he said he would." She began to eat. "Eighteen. You're eighteen today. Somebody should say it out loud at least once before supper, so I've said it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:47:"Don't thank me. I only carried it up a stair." Bracken looked at him over his spectacles. "Many happy returns of the day, enrollee. I have it in the book. Your file has had your date of birth on its first leaf since the day you came up the hill, and I have never once had cause to say it aloud." He nodded, the small nod he gave a form that had been filled in properly, and went back down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:61:Not cleared. Karis did not clear a table; there was nowhere to put what was on it. But every sheet on it had been turned face down. Every stack had been turned over where it stood so that only the backs showed, plain and grey and blank, the code's three volumes shut and laid with their spines to the wall, her notebooks closed, the attestation wallet buckled. The whole long table was a field of the backs of things. In the middle of it, on a clean cloth, stood a honey cake on a kitchen plate with a knife beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:77:He stopped in the doorway and stood there with one hand on the frame, a tall loose young man in the cohort's coat with the green-taped captain's file under his arm, because he went nowhere without it. He looked at the four of them. Then he looked at the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:89:He took the third chair from the end of the table, and turned it round, all the way, so that its back was to the table and its front was to the window. Then he sat down in it, with his back to every paper in the room, facing Lira on the window seat and the dark glass behind her, and put the captain's file on his knees, and folded his hands on top of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:141:She had not said she was going to. She sat on the window seat with her foot on the stool and a plate on her knee and did not eat from it, and when Karis put the knife down she said them out loud, to the room, one after another, in the voice she used to give her figures to a panel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:145:She looked at her plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:157:Then Ephram took the captain's file off his knees and opened it, without turning round, and took out three sheets of close-ruled paper, and held them up over his shoulder without looking, so that Cael could take them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:167:Cael gave him the sheets back. Ephram put them in the file and shut it and folded his hands on top of it again, and looked at the window, and did not turn round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:179:Only then did Cael look at the rest. The flap came down over everything. The strap was long enough to cross a man's body and ride on the opposite hip, so that the bag would sit still when he walked and not swing when he ran. And the buckle was not a bought buckle. Hesk's file marks were on the inside of the tongue, where only somebody who had slept in the shavings under that bench would think to look, and the frame had the little bevel Hesk put on everything he made so that it would not catch on cloth. He ran his thumb over the bevel. The leather round it was the heavy brown kind Hesk kept for instrument cases and would not waste on anything that did not deserve it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:193:The evening broke up a little before the tenth bell. Ephram went first, with the captain's file under his arm, backing out of the door so as not to see the table even at the last, and wished Cael many happy returns again from the landing, and went down the stair whistling the third line wrong. Brom took the kettle. Karis stayed to turn her papers face up again, one stack at a time, in the order she had turned them over, which she said was the only way she would ever find anything in the morning. Seln had gone before any of them, quietly, while the bag was being admired.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md:77:Withrow went on with her supper, and Lira, three places down, put her elbows on the table, which was not allowed, and smiled at her plate for the first time since the street door had opened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md:125:*For your file*, the steward's boy had said, handing it over after the finals, as if it were nothing; and nobody in her life had ever handed her anything *for your file* that she had wanted to keep. Her name and *Iron, Rank One, Wind*. His name, which she had decided she was allowed to know now, and *Silver, Rank Eight, Force*. Four lines of five filled. *Touch, confluence. Touch, Halcenvane. No touch. Touch, confluence. Two to one.* The panel's columns. *28.* *27.* And at the foot, pressed so hard into the paper that she could feel the letters from the back, Rooke's four words, which he had copied onto it at the inn without being asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md:133:Then the sheet itself. Not Rooke's red book, which was the program's and nobody else's, but this: a figure in a meet record, under a hall's seal, filed with a district. One point under a man who had been Silver for eleven years. One day a clerk at a counter in Ostrand would take her file out of a drawer for the advancement sitting, and this would be in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:274:They came home the short way, over Ostrand bridge and along the river street and up the bluff road, as Withrow had told them to, with the lamps lit and the river still loud and brown beside them but lower, much lower, with a long tide-line of sticks and froth on the stones where it had been. At the ferry landing the two coats stood in their places in the last of the light. One of them watched the coach go by. He was not looking at Cael. He was looking at Lira's strapped boot, which she had up on the seat by the window, and he went on looking at it until the coach had passed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:111:It was lit the way it was lit every night of the year, a yellow square at the top of the dark building, with the lamp set close to the glass so that you could see it from the north wall and the gate lodge and, Seln had once said, from the ferry landing at the foot of the bluff. The whole valley knew that window. A coat at the foot of the road could have told you the hour by it: lit at dusk, out somewhere between the tenth bell and the eleventh, never once dark before the ninth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:281:He had told himself all year, and the year before, that the room was the trouble and not the hill. He had found out on the Ostrand road that the room had never been the system at all. He had thought that was the bottom of it, and it was not. Here was a man who had sat in one of the rooms and done everything right. He had read the sheet and asked, and written down the answer he had not been given. He had told a father the truth in four lines, which was more than most people managed in forty. And all of it, his honesty and the father's careful spelling both, had gone up a stair into the drawer that held the corrections. It had been filed there beside them, clipped, in order, and kept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:5:He did it with the stub of pencil from behind his ear, standing in his own doorway, while the last of the four horses was still going down the bluff road below the gate, because his book was on the counter behind him and he did not want to lose the minute in the turning round. Cael saw him do it from the wall stair, coming down. Then the old man went in, and took the book off the counter, and copied the figure off his hand into it, and licked his thumb and rubbed the hand clean.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:95:He came with a full plate. He put his elbows on the table and began to eat, steadily, and between mouthfuls he talked across the board to the Current girl about hips. Her hips, and Lira's, and the whole program's. He told her the inside of a landing hip was the first thing to go on a planked floor and the last thing anybody thought to strap, and that Lira's had come back quiet after two days and she was to make it three before she believed it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:103:Rooke wiped his plate with his bread and took it away. He had not mentioned the gate once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:115:"You won't find it on a meet card," he said. "You find it on a posting roll. I've filed from three towns where one of them was billeted, and I never once saw one on a floor. It's rare at any station. The service writes to the family in the week the station enters it, and most of them go, because nobody else has ever written to them about anything. It holds people. That's what the system keeps it for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:133:"The letter to the district seat," he said, and read it to them; he read aloud everything the house was going to send. The date; the instrument as presented, quoted whole; the issuing office as named; *the registrar of this house is unable to place the said office in any register available to him, and respectfully asks that the instrument constituting it be supplied, together with the seal under which that instrument was itself issued.* He lowered the sheet. "It goes down with the morning bag. They will answer inside the week, because they must."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:215:He did not say which sentence. He did not need to. Every one of them had carried it home from Norhold and up this hill, and Cael heard it again now in the Archmarshal's dry voice, among forty clocks: *Because the people who want this to stop are not going to use official channels much longer.* The sentence no longer ended in the clock room. In Cael's mind it went on down the bluff road, past the arch Withrow had held that morning, toward doors where no chancellor would be waiting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:11:"*Ostrand district registry. Review for advancement of tier: Brom, enrolled at Halcenvane. Schedule of evidence. The panel will read, first, the practitioner's record as entered, the continental title included. Second, the house's attendance and floor sheets for the two years preceding, to be supplied by the registrar under the house's seal. Third, a reading in the sigil frame, under load, taken on the day in the presence of the three assessors. No testimony is contemplated. Sitting: the last Fifth-day of the academic year, at the second bell.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:23:Ephram, who had not stopped his set, finished it, and let the long frame's bar come back to its rest with both hands, and went to the rack by the wall where the short frame's iron plates hung on their pegs. He took down two, and looked at them, and hung one back, and took down a heavier one in its place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:27:He slid the plates onto the short frame's bar, one each side, and stepped back, and looked at Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:35:The short frame was a simple machine: two timber uprights, a crossbar of iron that ran in greased slots between them, and under each end of the bar a spring as thick as a man's wrist. You stood beneath the bar and took it on your shoulders, or on your forearms crossed above your head, and somebody let the catches go, and the springs drove it down. A sigil frame at a district seat was a finer thing, with a plate that read what the practitioner's Path did under the weight. This one read nothing. It only pressed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:55:Ephram took a third plate off the rack, a small one, and held it up, and raised his eyebrows.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:61:They did it eleven times, by Cael's count, in the better part of an hour, and in all that time neither of them said another word that was not a number or a side. Ephram put the plates on and took them off, and watched Brom's left elbow the whole while with the level, patient attention he gave everything he meant to be right about. Twice he shook his head at nothing Cael could see, and took a plate off, and Brom did not argue. Once he added one that Brom had not asked for, and Brom looked at it and walked in under the bar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:69:It was the first whole sentence he had spoken since Bracken left, and he wiped his hands on the cloth at his belt and began taking the plates off the bar, one at a time, and hanging them on their pegs in order of weight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:73:"They'll find it," he said. He did not seem to be speaking to either of them in particular. "That's the thing. I've known since the docket came that they'd find it, and I know now what the paper is that they'll write it on. I told you in the reading room. I can't read that sheet anymore." He turned his forearms over. "And I still want to stand in their frame and have the plate say what's true. I've been thinking about why. I think it's only that I'd like to have been right about myself, once, in front of somebody whose job it was to check."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:79:He did not say what he would do with the finding afterward, but his hand went to the pocket again and rested there. Ephram, at the rack, with his back to them, hung up the last plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:83:The lake house came up the bluff road on the Second-day in a hired wagon with a blue-grey cloth over its hoops, and half the hill was at the north wall to watch it climb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:3:They came back up the bluff the way they had gone down it, by five ways, and nobody on the road could have said they had ever been together.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:7:He walked up the bluff road behind a carter's boy leading a mule with three trunks on its back, and let the mule set the pace.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:13:It took them until the light was going, a slow line down the switchbacks with the trunks swaying and the wheels loud on the turns, and at the foot of the road the coats stepped back against the chandler's wall to let them by. On the river the ferry made its crossing, and came back empty, and crossed again full. Four hundred and thirty enrollees lived on the bluff, and by dusk forty-one of them were left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:19:It was the plainest reason of the four. Since the year's eighth day the registry had had a petition before its seat in the town below, asking that he be stood in a frame and read again. If the frame could not read him, the code had a chapter for that: his enrollment struck out, and after it, a letter at a time, everything else with his name on it. And a respondent who left the hill while the thing was pending would be entered as fled, with a note on three friends' files to say whose friends they were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:119:There was nothing in it, and that was what Hesk's letters were for: a buckle and a captain and a dog asleep across a door, and the stove, and something green, and not one word about registries or polite men or what anybody might be building. Hesk wrote from his bench and stayed at the height of his bench, and the letter came up the bluff like a smell of shavings and reminded Cael that there was a whole country in which people's dogs slept on steps.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:165:"I filed true returns for fifteen years," he said, "knowing what they were for. Every word of every one of them was right. You get used to it." He moved one finger on the cloth. "You should not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:219:The road column was the last column on the page, kept since the year of the thieves on the order of the chancellor before last, one line at every bell, for whatever came up the bluff road or went down it. In term it ran to three or four entries a bell, and this week it ran to one or none. *Carter, flour. Post, the bag. None. None. The registrar's man, to town and back. None.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:231:"The same quiet. Read again." He closed the book and gave it back to the lodge-man through his window with a word of thanks. Then he walked with Cael out under the arch and along the outer wall, where the wind came up the bluff off the river, and spoke to the wall rather than to him. "They have not gone. They have stood down. There is a difference. A man who has gone has decided he was wrong. A man who has stood down has decided he does not need to do anything, because what he wants is coming to him by itself." He walked a little way. "The corridor is on schedule. Every sitting sits. Every form is filed. Nobody on this hill has put a foot wrong, and so nobody has to come up the road and make one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:5:He had half expected it not to. He had lain awake for an hour after the tenth bell with the satchel on the sill and the court dark below, and some part of him had believed that the house would look different in daylight, now that he knew what he knew. But the frost was on the flags as it had been yesterday. The fountain went up and came down. The porter's boy was chalking the day's names on the Crown yard's boards, and two Mire second-years were arguing at the refectory door about whose turn it was to fetch the bread, and the coats at the foot of the road changed on the bell. Not one thing on the whole bluff had moved an inch to make room for what was on the shelf in Karis's room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:11:"From my station," she said. "They wrote that you wanted it for my file, so my mother sent it on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:13:"The house's copy," said Seln. "Thank you." He unfolded it on the counter and read it, and Cael watched him read it. It was a station certificate, the ordinary kind, with a registry seal at its foot and three lines of a clerk's writing above. Seln read the three lines once at his counter pace, neither slow nor quick. Then he blotted the foot where the girl's mother had signed for it, though the ink was days dry, and folded it again along its old folds, and wrote a line in the wing's ledger, and put the certificate in the drawer under the counter where enrollees' station papers went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:21:There would be a certificate in it for every enrollee on the hill, four hundred and thirty of them. There would be one for Lira, from wherever she had been read, and one for Brom from the Velmere district, and one for Karis. There was no certificate in it for him, because the plate at Denvash had lit with a word and no station had ever written him down since. Every other paper in that drawer was three lines in a clerk's hand, and some of the three lines were what a station had read, and some of them were what an order had told it to write. There was no way on earth, standing at a counter in a queue, to tell which.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:177:"Third. The enrolled associates of a respondent entered as fled." She did not look at Lira or Brom. "Every enrollee of this house whose file names him, in a minute or a squad list or a delegation. A note on each file: *associated with a respondent entered as fled.* No penalty, only a note, on three files." She paused. "His makes four."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:183:"Not now. Not without the word on four files and a line on the house's charter, and not without leaving Withrow with that in her folder for the rest of her tenure." Karis turned to the enrollment book. "That's the price of now. Here's the door."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:201:"One." Karis looked at him. "It opens on one day, the day of the ruling, and stays open until the revocation takes effect, and then it shuts. And it only opens at all if we lose. If the seat rules for the house, there's no door, because there's nothing to resign from." She sat back. "Until that day there's only the corridor: every sitting attended, every form filed on time, every bout in wing three at the band, and every first-year at that counter handing in a certificate, and you standing behind her in the queue with your face doing nothing." She was quiet a moment. "Perfectly, until the ruling. If we put one foot wrong before it, the door we'd use afterward turns into the word on four files."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:305:"I'm the best doorstop on the bluff." Brom took his hand away again, deliberately, and Cael felt the *There?* come up like a hound's head, and Brom put his hand back, and it went down. "There. I can do it all night, and I've done harder work for less."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:264:*He said "non-hostile" and the system agreed. Nine fragments and the ninth one's engagement field says the closest thing to friendship a machine knows how to file. I'll take it. It's the truest thing the instrument ever wrote about me except "no number goes here," and it was written about a fight I didn't want to win.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:3:Karis untied the pink tape herself, because it was the wing's knot and she had watched Seln tie it on a hundred files across the counter and knew which end to pull.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:9:It was an evaluator in the east, on a station circuit, about a young man of a carters' guild whose floor work ran a full rank ahead of his file. The evaluator had measured it at a frame and again on a guild floor and again by the clock, and he wrote that the file was plainly wrong, and asked that the young man be read again. At the foot of his report, in another hand, a registry clerk's stamp had been pressed square: *Read against file. Enter at file.* And under the stamp, a month later, the evaluator again, smaller: *Continue observation.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:15:There were twenty-three. She read every one, and she read the stamp at the foot of every one, and after the sixth she stopped reading the stamp aloud because everybody in the room could say it with her. A woman in the hill country whose Stone came up too heavy for its column. A ferryman's boy whose Current had something of Wind in it that his station had not written down. A girl at a guild hall in the south who did everything her Path said she should, the evaluator wrote, only as if she were older than her paper. Each report saw the same thing and asked for the same thing, and each came back stamped the same way. *Read against file. Enter at file.* Some carried *Continue observation* under the stamp in the evaluator's own hand, and some carried nothing, and one carried a single line that Karis read twice before she read it out: *Noted, and I do not like it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:19:"Down," she said, "every one. Where the file and the floor disagreed, the floor was corrected to the file, and never the other way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:37:So she started at the oldest, at the yellow end, and worked forward. The sheets were printed, all of them, each a single leaf in a registry circular's form, with a number at the head and a seal pressed at the foot and a line of signatures under the seal. Cael watched her turn them, and the paper changed as she went, from a thick soft yellow stock that had gone brown at its edges to a thinner whiter one, and then to the hard bright paper the registry used now, the paper of the instrument on his own sill. The type changed too. The oldest leaves had heavy letters with long tails, the kind on the founders' volumes Karis kept on her top shelf, and the newest had the narrow even type of every form Bracken had ever put across a counter. The seal at the foot of each changed by small degrees, a ring of letters gone and another come, the river and the scale drawn first one way and then another. The signatures changed with every issue, officer after officer, a new name every few leaves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:99:Cael waited for her to go on. She did not. She sat with her gloved hands either side of the leaf and her eyes on the last line, and her face had gone into the stillness he had seen on it once before, at a citation in the seventh paragraph of the instrument on the first night of the year, and he knew she was frightened, and he knew she was frightened accurately.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:117:He thought of Denvash, which he did not often let himself do. A district office on a cold morning, a chalk circle on boards worn into two shallow places where a generation of feet had stood, and a warden-adjunct with tired grey eyes three feet away at a desk with a brass plate let into it. Something had woken behind his breastbone, small, a point of light at the edge of his sight that he could feel and never point to. He had counted eleven seconds while the plate did not light, and then the word had come, very far away and very close, and the plate lighting late with the same word in it, and the light at the edge of his sight going out like a pinched wick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:155:"My fourth posting, twelve years ago." Seln put one hand back on the table, as if the wood steadied him. "A district seat in an old building, a records room on its top floor with presses round the walls, the kind with doors. I was looking for a subject's travel endorsements. They were filed where they should not have been, so I opened presses until I found them, and in the fourth press I found these." He moved his eyes over the rows. "The orders were in bound runs, a few years to a volume, with the records bundled behind them, and the memoranda on the bottom shelf in a box. And at the back of the box, loose, under the oldest, that." He did not look at the leaf. "Nobody had opened the press in years; the dust said so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:165:"A corridor," he said, "in a district station, a year on, with green distemper on the walls to the height of a man's shoulder, and benches. I was waiting for a file. A keeper came out of the frame room with a mother and a girl, and he was pleased, the way they are when a reading is a good one, and I heard him tell the mother that her girl read strong, and read into a second family as well, and he had not seen it in years." He looked at the father's letter, folded under its clip in the third row. "A week later I was back for another file, and the station's out-tray was on the counter, and her certificate was in it, waiting to go. One family, first rank." He took his hand off the table. "That was when the paper turned into children. It had been only paper for a year."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:5:He would not let the house's escort have them, or Bracken, or the coach's boot. He rode inside, on the eighth sitting's morning, with the bound volume on his knees in the house's boards and both hands laid on it, and the case with the handle set very straight on the seat beside him, as if the instruments in it might be wanted to swear to something. He was a spare, exact man with a magister's grey at his temples, and Cael had sat across a trestle from him every week for two years and had never before seen him out of a room he was in charge of. He looked at the wet streets of Ostrand going by with a kind of mild disapproval, as a man looks at a column somebody else has added.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:51:"The petitioner has no knowledge of the matter in that note," said Jent. "I say so for the minute, and I say it carefully. It is no part of the petition. No instrument of the kind described was sought by the petitioner or issued at his instance." He paused. "As to the rest of the volume: the petitioner accepts it, as he said he would. He relies on none of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:55:The clerk wrote, and at the records table the junior chair drew the volume toward him and opened his receipt book. In the middle chair below the platform, Ilsev had turned a leaf in her file and was writing something small at its foot, and she did not look at the house's table, and she did not look at the hood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:151:"You found it. You found it by being wrong about where to look, which is how nearly everything worth having gets found. I only used it." Karis tapped the good paper. "When it's done, it goes down to the registrar, and he enters it on your file with the house's stamp: *a note upon a heading of the code, by an enrollee of the second year.* It'll sit there under your name whatever becomes of anybody else's." She said that last part quickly. "The seat has heard that provision argued twice this year. It ought to be on record somewhere who turned it up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:159:"Files change," said Karis. "It's what they're for." She took the code's second volume out of Cael's arms and set it on the corner of the table. "You'll want that. The cross-heading is in the back. Find it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:229:He folded the sheets in four along their soft old creases and put them in the captain's file, and tied the green tape.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:237:"Ninety-eight, and two holes." Ephram tucked the file under his arm. "And not one of them over the line. I ruled that line in the first month I knew you. I thought I'd have to move it." He looked at Cael then, level, the captain's look, the one he gave a first-year who had told him half of something. It went on a moment longer than was comfortable. "I'm not asking," he said. "I told you on your birthday I wasn't, and I'm not."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:245:Cael stood at the rail where the sheets had lain, and the wood was still a little warm from Ephram's forearm. Whatever had been written on the back of the third page was in a green-taped file going down the wing's corridor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:255:*In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward. The evaluation seat constituted for these proceedings, having first given notice in the year's eighth week that certification would be required, now renews its request to the Archmarshal's office for certification of the standard protocol, as applied to a re-evaluation of the enrollee of record at Halcenvane, should such be ordered. The seat's closing sittings are set down for the year's last month. The office's certificate is requested before the first of them.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:261:The first notice had come in the year's eighth week and he had acknowledged it by return, in one line, as he acknowledged every notice. He remembered writing the line, and he had meant, then, to sign the certificate when it was asked for. The protocol was sound. He had helped to write two of its sections as a young man, and had defended the whole of it before three convenings, and in all his service he had never refused his name to a thing he knew to be sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:273:He wrote on the ruled line, in the upright hand he kept for instruments, with no heading and no reason.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:275:*The Archmarshal declines to certify.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:279:He knew what would be done with it. The code let a seat proceed upon its own authority where a certificate was withheld; he had cited that section himself, once, long ago, when a guild hall's assessor would not sign. His line would stop nothing. The sittings would sit, and the ruling would be read, and somewhere in a minute a clerk would enter that a thing usually present was not there. That was the whole of what he could do, and he had weighed it on a post road for four days and found that it was still worth doing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:1:# Chapter 37 — Two Files
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:15:He told the circle chalked on the worn boards of a district office, and the warden-adjunct three feet away watching the brass plate let into her desk, and the thing that woke behind his breastbone and came up to the edge of his sight. He told the eleven seconds he had counted while the plate did not light, and the one word that came at the end of them, very far away and very close, and the light at the edge of his sight going out like a pinched wick and never coming back when he looked. He told the letter that had made Denvash impossible for Hesk's sake, and the road east. He told Lira next, because Lira had come next: six weeks of bouts in a yard at Ardenmere, with her knocking him down on the boards between them more times than either of them had counted. Then one cold night on a cot under a window, with the Log on his knee, something had been written in the dark place behind his breastbone where the light had gone out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:77:"Can an instrument read it off you?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:81:"No frame has ever read anything off me but what I put in front of it. The plates in the wing read what I give them, and I give them the band. The read at Brom's grade is a surface, and I can hold a surface. And my own Arbiter went out at Denvash after one word. It's never lit since. I've not stood in a station since."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:182:"You kept my file too, then."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:202:"While its use can be seen by anyone who could carry the seeing west, it stays where it is." Seln said it slowly and exactly, the way he wrote a line in a return that he meant to be read by somebody careful. "Not for my file, which can look after itself now; it has had practice. For the trade. Somewhere, in a room I will never see, there is a man whose whole work is sorting, and any report of a thing that moves the way I move gives him a thread to pull. Where there is no witness who can carry it west, there is no thread, and then it is yours. Use it or don't, as you judge." He unfolded his hands. "And you will not ask me first. That is the other term."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:3:The coach went down the bluff road on the Fifth-day behind Withrow's carriage, in a fine cold rain, and the town watched it go by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:35:Nothing in it had changed. He had half thought something would, after the recess, after Ostrand and the borrowed office four streets back, as he had half thought the hill would look different on the morning after the case. But the wool exchange smelled of lanolin and damp coats, as it always had, and the forty benches ran back to the doors, a quarter full, with lot seventeen cut into the floor under his left boot. On the platform the hood sat in its place behind the long table, grey and still, and below it the five chairs of the evaluation seat stood in their row with their covers off and five people in them. Four were district evaluators whose names he had heard read once and had written down; and in the middle, with a file open on her knee at its usual angle, sat Ilsev, reading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:55:"The respondent filed, in the week before the recess, a supplemental table of the renewals of this enrollment, with the day of each sitting and the day of each seal set side by side. I have read it. I read it twice, on a boat." He turned his head a little toward the house's table, toward Karis, and inclined it. "It is correct. The petitioner accepts it as filed, in every line, and adopts it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:91:It was. A production order was the most ordinary paper a seat could make. Bracken would carry it up the hill tonight in his coat, and Gault would read it in his office behind wing three with his instrument case very straight on the bench beside him, and would say that he had written it down. There was nothing in Gault's notes that could hurt anybody. There was a year of figures in them that breathed and never settled and never went past a red line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:159:He did not look across the room. He had looked at that boy before, on the pump step and at Greyvane and on the sprung oak at the bluff, and he remembered every one of the looks, and he would not give himself another. He had decided that long ago, and the deciding had held through eleven weeks of Fifth-days in the same long room, forty feet from the house's table. It held this morning. He went down both leaves to the foot and found them alike in everything but the one line. Then he wrote *superseded* in the corner of the first, in the plain hand he kept for corners, with *sixth sitting* under it, and entered the second in the receipt book against the time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:171:The merits were heavier than the first sittings had been, though there was not more talk; there was less. But every sitting put paper on his table, and all of it had to be receipted and numbered and laid against the lists. At the sixth sitting, the respondent's supplemental upon the renewals: eleven leaves, a table, a certified copy of a minute. At the seventh, a week later, the petitioner's acceptance of the tournament office's record, in one leaf; the respondent's second supplemental, upon the manner in which that record had been kept, in nineteen; and a correction slip, signed by the registrar of the house, altering the date on one exhibit by a single day, with the reason given.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:189:A mark in a boy's file with no office behind it. That was the first, and the oldest, and the hand it was written in was younger than his hand was now. Three more lay between, each in its year. The fifth was a sentence on a senior desk at Norhold, read upside down, shutting a door the first had found open. The sixth was a seal that stood in no registry of seals.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:227:"That the thing he's afraid of is done the day a house hands over an enrollee to avoid it. After that, nobody needs a ruling. They need only file, and wait for us to count the cost. I put it more gently than that. He's a good man, and frightened, and I have been both."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:5:"Call days," she said. "Four of them, before any ruling. In those four days a seat may recall a party to put a question it has thought of late, and so may its evaluation seat, and the code gives them an hour's notice to do it in. An hour, on foot, from wherever the party is lodged." She looked out at the switchbacks. "That was a fair rule when every respondent lived over his own shop in the town. It was not written with a bluff in mind. Your hill is an hour and a quarter from that room by a good horse, and so for four days the house must live where it can hear the exchange's bell. The code calls it keeping within the bell. I have always thought it rather a handsome phrase for a nuisance."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:51:Cael came out onto the gallery with the satchel on his shoulder and looked down. The yard was flagged, with a pump in one corner and a stack of empty woolsacks under a pentice roof. In the middle stood the press, a thing as old as the building: two oak posts as thick as a man, a beam across their tops, and a great wooden screw coming down through the beam to a platen of black timber six feet square. A capstan with four long bars turned the screw. In sale weeks, the woman of the house had said, it took four men to bale a sack with it. It was not a sale week.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:53:Brom stood under the platen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:55:He had his coat off and his feet set and his forearms crossed above his head, the right over the left, and the platen rested on them. Lira was at the capstan with both hands on a bar and her heels dug in, walking it round a step at a time. With every step the black timber came down a finger's breadth, and Brom took it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:69:Cael saw what she was going to do a beat before she did it. He had no time to say anything, and Brom, under the platen, could not see her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:73:The bar dropped under her and the screw turned, and the platen came down on Brom's crossed forearms like the lid of a chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:77:Cael had let the old read go out across the flags without deciding to. It came back to him as it always did, in weight and where the weight went. The platen's whole mass, and a girl's, and the speed she had brought with her, ran down through two forearms into the shoulders, both alike now, and down the long back and through the heels into the stone. The skin had gone dull at the wrists. Brom stood under a wool press like a third post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:87:On the ninth Brom said, "Enough," and she walked the capstan back, and the platen rose off him, and the skin went back to being skin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:97:"I say it like I say anything." Brom turned his forearms over and looked at the marks. "I'll stand in it, and I'll hold what they set, and the plate will say what's so. I want that. I've wanted it since before I had a Path." He was quiet a moment. "It's the paper after that I've stopped being able to see properly. Somebody hands you a sheet. It says *found*, and there's a line at the bottom for your name."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:119:Karis filed her last paper that afternoon, and he went with her to carry the wallet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:123:"It's upon the entry they read out," said Karis, as they crossed the lane. "The certificate. The code lets a seat go on by its own authority where the certificate's withheld; I looked at the section the night we got home. So I went back through the digests to see how often a seat ever had." She held up four fingers. "Four times. I've set them out, with their years. A guild hall's frame, where the assessor wouldn't sign. Two station frames in the hill districts. A plate. Four times in the whole record a seat has proceeded without the certifying office's name, and every time it was upon a thing. Never once upon a person."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:163:He knew what was in that building. He knew it as he knew the inside of Hesk's shop. There was a room with a brass plate in it. The girl would be told to stand still, and to put her hand where she was shown, and something in her that had been asleep for fourteen years would wake, and the plate would light. In the frame-house a keeper would read what the frame gave him, and write it in his station book in two figures, a reading and a margin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:167:He did not know that it would be different from what the plate had shown. Most were not. There had been forty pairs in a locked case, from a few districts over a few years, and Karis would not guess past them and Seln had stopped estimating. This girl might walk out of that door with the truth on a slip of paper. He would never know. That was the whole of the horror of it, standing in the sun by a trough. Neither would she. If the margin had run past her column, nobody would enter it, and somebody would be told to observe her for nine weeks, and would see a girl with a green ribbon going about her life, and would write *nothing to report*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:197:He had been arguing about himself for a year: his enrollment and his file, the nine days of his renewal, his frame. And the whole while, on every north road in the Compact, on every morning the stations opened, this had been going on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:233:She stood up from the middle one. She was a spare woman of sixty or so with her grey hair cut short, in a plain coat, and she had her file open on her left forearm at the angle at which he had seen it for a year. She did not come forward. She stood where she had sat, and looked at the seat's clerk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:241:"The scope of this seat was minuted at the third sitting, in the presiding seat's words," said Ilsev. She spoke slowly, with a small pause at the end of every clause, and three pens went with her. "It has kept within them. Within that scope the evaluation seat is obliged to lay before the proceedings any matter upon the subject's file that would govern the conduct of an evaluation. There is one such matter. The file carries a designation. The petitioner has set it out in the second schedule to his petition, at the fourteenth line. A designation of that kind governs who must be present at an evaluation, and to what office its result is sent."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:243:She turned a leaf in her file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:249:"The first. *Return, from the chain of review, to the senior evaluation seat, on referral of the matter at Greyvane. Designation: Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4.*" The pause. "*Determination: no originating authority of record. Designation predates file creation. No action available at this clearance.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:257:"And the note which this officer has written beside that designation upon every form she has filed concerning the subject, and which she now enters here. *Authorizing office: not traced.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:259:She lifted her eyes from the file. She did not look at the house's table, or at Jent, but at the grey cover on the presiding chair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:263:She sat down, and set the file at its angle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:269:*Designation predates file creation.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:273:There had been no file yet. The registry had just said so. It had said so twice, under seal, in a clerk's round hand, and a careful woman with short grey hair had read it out at the pace of dictation in an empty wool exchange so that it would be taken down right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:281:"The petitioner set that designation out in his schedule because it is upon the file," he said. "He was not told its office either. He has no observation."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:9:He put the bread down at her elbow and went away again. The next night, when Karis came down to the east hall with the rest of them, she brought the plate back empty, and he did not know whether she had eaten the bread or Seln had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:51:"The second half of the first finding is the mark on Cael's file."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:55:"The leaf says the function is appended to the reading and advises what's entered. It doesn't say what happens when the reading gives it nothing to advise, and nothing else in the case says it in words." She looked at Cael. "But your file says it. The petition put it in schedule two by its full title, on the first night of the year, and you read it. *Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:59:"Advisory, the same word, on a designation nobody at any height has ever been able to trace to an office." Karis drew a breath. "Cael told me on the third night, when he brought the bread. I asked him about Denvash, and he told me: eleven seconds. The plate lit eleven seconds late, and the word came at the end of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:61:Cael had not known she had asked for anything but the bread. He remembered telling her, standing at her elbow with the plate in his hand, and her not looking up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:63:"Eleven seconds is a long time for a reading," said Karis. "Every other reading on the continent is done before you've finished a breath. I think the function had his reading in front of it for eleven seconds and couldn't find a column to put it in, any column, and couldn't take the margin off because it was all margin. So it did what a good form does when it can't be filled in: it failed safe, and wrote the one word it had for a thing that couldn't be written. Then it marked the file, so that whoever came after would know there was something there it hadn't been able to correct." She laid the slip down. "The mark has no office behind it because no office made it. It made itself, the way a stamp comes down when a lever's pulled. And Ilsev sent it up, at the hearing, twice, to every height she could reach, and asked who'd authorized it. Something answered her each time, something that wasn't anybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:71:"Ilsev did everything right," said Karis. "She's the most honest seat in the district. And Havel at the junior chair, who looks at the file every time it's on a table, as Brom's counted. He's seen something he can't place, and he's kept looking at it, which is all an honest man can do with a thing like that." She looked at each of them. "I want a rule about them before we go on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:95:The words had told her least, she said. Here and there a word had been swapped for one the founding compilers would never have used, and that put the issues in order and no more. The type gave her two breaks. The stocks gave her three: a soft rag, a harder sheet, and then the bright paper of the instrument on Cael's sill, and the house's own oldest filings ran through the same three in the same sequence, so she could say which issues were older than which. None of it gave her a year. Years had come up the library stair on the Seventh-day, in Bracken's arms.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:127:She did not say which flag, and she did not have to. Cael's own schedule two had it, bracketed, under *Path*, on the instrument on his sill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:181:"*They're trying to move him back inside the instrument.*" She moved her finger down a line. "*I don't know why that distinction feels important. Noting it.*" She laid the open notebook on the cloth beside the slips. "Cael put a question mark beside it in his notebook. I saw him do it. I've owed him the answer since the first night."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:197:"Nobody has come since the cut: no team, no knock, no rider at the gate. I have been reading the post for it every morning, and the lodge book, and the coats, and there is nothing." He moved one finger. "I thought at first they were counting what the road cost them, but that is not it. Why spend a team on a road, where things go wrong and a team goes home with nothing, when the seat will deliver him at the close of the year, by the book, in a room with a clerk? They don't need the hand when they have the instrument."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:201:"There were people," said Seln. "Two hundred years ago, one, at a desk. Since then there have been seventy-one people signing for coal, and keepers writing down margins, and clerks entering what they're told, and evaluators stamped *enter at file*, and a district clerk who told a father the truth and sent it up. None of them is wicked. Most of them are good at their work." He was quiet a moment. "A conspiracy can be exposed. A convenience cannot."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:203:"It can't be filed either," said Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:207:"I've thought about it every night this week, who you'd file it with. The seat runs on it, and so does the registry, and every evaluation office in the Compact sends its readings through it. You'd be filing a complaint about the corrections function with an office whose whole business is entering what it advises, and they'd find nothing irregular, because every form's been filled in properly for two hundred years." She took her hand off the notebook. "There's nobody to send it to who isn't standing on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:211:He thought of the first night of the year at his window, with the instrument on the sill and the pole written out in his best hand. *The system has text, and text has held me every time the people failed.* He had believed the people were the trouble and the text was the floor. Then the road had shown him that the room was never the system. He had thought, coming home over the bridge at dusk with his ribs strapped, that he had come to the bottom of it, that the system was broken somewhere he could not see, and that he was looking for the break.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:235:"The case," said Lira. "If it can't be filed, if there's nobody to send it to who isn't standing on it. If we can't publish it without spending Seln, and we can't use it at the seat without the seat reading it as a forgery, and Karis won't take it a line past the paper." She looked down the main stair into the lamplit dark. "Twelve years on a shelf and five nights on her table, and it doesn't prove anything to anybody who matters. What's it for?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:47:*Teaching assistant, Bronze grade, fifteen years' service, absent without notice from first bell of the First-day. Residence room found vacated; no personal effects; bed made. All working files completed and cross-indexed to date. Inner-door key, records-press key, post-room key and wing side-door key surrendered on the copying-table blotter. Floor schedule posted one week forward. No note left, no leave applied for, no address of forwarding held by this house. The house records no disciplinary matter, no irregularity of any kind in the two years of this posting, and no view as to cause. Query submitted to the placing district office per code; direction requested.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:59:The name was found in the drawer. It had nineteen postings against it, in fifteen years, and the last was a chartered house on a bluff above a river. From that house a return had come up every week for two years, on the same day, folded in the same way, with the subject's reference on the outside fold. The desk turned to the last of them, which was dated eight days before the query.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:79:They checked the lines against station books, and house ledgers, and bout sheets, and each one stood. A subject had attended. A figure was as the house had entered it. An instrument had been declined at a gate, and the subject had not been seen at the refusal. Associations were unchanged. *Nothing further to report.* It was as clean a body of product as any reader there had handled, and one of them said so, and it was put in a minute.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:89:The slip with its question stayed on the file. It was never struck through.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:113:He did not write a letter. There was nobody to address it to who could be told the truth, and he had filed enough true, empty paper for one life. He had said the whole of it at a table two nights ago, to four people, and it had been written under a line with no name at its head, and that was all the filing it would get.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:129:For two years he had fetched the house's pouch across that bridge, at the end of a day, with the same satchel on the same shoulder. He nodded to the ward's lantern and was nodded to, and went over the middle arch above the brown water, and nobody would think of it again. The two coats were at the bluff road's foot and the two at the landing, where they had been set to stand, and they had not been set to watch a clerk go for the post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:137:He had not known what he was copying, though the few who learned of it assumed otherwise. He had gone to that press for a subject's travel endorsements, which had been filed where they did not belong, and he had found paper that was correct in every particular and sat a little above where it should have sat; and he had copied it because he copied things. Understanding had come a year later, in a station corridor painted green, and it had come all at once, so that he had stood very still against a wall while a keeper went by with a tray.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:157:He did it badly. He knew he was doing it badly, and he did not move. He had left nineteen towns and had not looked back at one of them. It had not been a hardship. There had been nothing behind him in any of those places but a file and a rented room, and he had no practice at this. He stood full on to the valley, like a first-year told to be seen, with his hands hanging.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:159:The valley was a darkness with a lighter band in it, which was the river. Beyond the river the bluff stood up, blacker than the sky, and he found the house by its shape. It had no lights, for it was past midnight, and four hundred people were asleep in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:195:She minded. That surprised her a little. She had thought, after the reading room, after the forty pairs and the father's letter that she had read aloud in her own voice, that she would never again be able to care what a clerk at a counter laid beside what. She knew what the counter was. She knew what sat behind every certificate in the wing's drawer, her own among them, three lines in a hand she had never seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:197:She had wondered about that, once. Only once, on the second night, on the stair. What had the keeper read on her, before anybody wrote it down? She remembered a brass plate, and cold fingers turning her by the shoulder, and being told to stand still. She did not remember anybody saying a word she could use.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:13:*Fifth-day. Tenth sitting. Eleven minutes. The evaluation seat renewed its request to the Archmarshal's office for a certificate on the protocol, and the hood minuted the renewal. That was the whole of the morning. Nobody at our table looked at anybody else.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:75:It was not a long motion. Cael watched the grey shape and tried, as he had tried at every sitting, to learn something from how it sat, and learned nothing, as he had at every sitting. Below it Ilsev had her file at its angle and was not writing. The four district evaluators looked at the house's table, at Karis, with the frank curiosity of working officers who had been told that a student might be allowed to address them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:113:The coach went down the market street, and over the bridge, and the coats at the foot of the bluff road watched it come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:119:It was an errand of the most ordinary kind. The buckle on Hesk's satchel was as good as the day it was filed, and the strap had begun, exactly as Hesk had said it would, to stretch at the third hole. Brom kept leather. Brom kept everything: cord, wax, an awl, a tin of rivets, a whetstone in a sock. If a thing on the hill had come apart in the last two years, it had generally gone to Brom's room before it went to the porter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:125:It was a plain room, like all the rooms on that corridor, with a bed and a press and a table under the window, and it was tidier than Cael's because Brom was. The two boxes were under the bed, roped, where they had been since the week of the gate, and Cael had stopped seeing them. The plaque, dark wood and a brass plate with the bar and ring, lay on the shelf on its face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:229:At the foot of the bluff road, small and yellow, the coats had their two lanterns lit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:235:He did not think that had been wrong. If he had gone east in the week of the case, the word would be on four files now, and a line on Withrow's charter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:5:The district officer's envelope came back to the seat at Ostrand on the Seventh-day evening, by his own hand, opened and endorsed. *No such enrollee; withdrawn this day; see the registrar's certificate.* The certificate followed in the house's bag on the First-day morning, with four leaves of the registry's own form under the house's seal, and one of the four had three lines of longhand in its margin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:17:So on the Fourth-day, which was the last of the academic year, a stout fair man unlocked a frame-house at the hour appointed, and took the cover off the frame and folded it. The president of the evaluation seat came, since the ruling had charged her to see to the conduct of the thing, and she was a woman who saw to what she was charged with. She brought her four evaluators. They stood on the flags in their plain coats, in a windowless room, with the plate dark at their feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:19:The order was called, and the name was read as the instruments had it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:29:The ninth part was drafted more widely. It allowed the registry to restrict, freeze or hold whatever attached to an unclassified person, pending the resolution of his status, and it did not wait upon a frame. The fences for it had been walked in the first term, and five drawers in five offices held their lists. The instrument was sealed, and went out by five bags.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:33:The house on the bluff answered first, in a registrar's upright hand. No enrollment stood, and no sponsorship; no floor hours were held, and no travel paper had been issued under the house's seal that had not lapsed with the form. The district seat answered that it kept nothing in that name but the file. The archive at Ternhall returned that the privileges in question had been surrendered at a counter, by their holder, before the instrument was dated. A provincial registry returned that the practitioner named as an associate stood at Copper, as he had for years; it added that a finding in his favour lay unsigned, and that the registry could not restrict a rank which nobody had taken up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:39:Two polite men had been through Denvash before. They had come in the first term to ask after an instrument-maker's trade licences and the title to his shop, and they had come again in the recess to see whether his answer had changed, and it had not. They came now a third time, with the sealed instrument in a wallet, and asked the old man at the bench whether his grandson had left any property on the premises.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:47:So the instrument lay on a desk with its five answers pinned to it, and the clerk who had charge of it turned to the last question on his form, which asked where the subject was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:49:He was not anywhere. A watcher's line had him on the east road, four days out, with a cart, and there was no line after that. Beyond the last station no district ran, and a restriction must run in a district; it wanted a counter to be served at, and a book to be written in. The code had foreseen that, as it foresaw most things. The clerk found the phrase in a schedule and wrote it at the instrument's foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:55:In the week after the close a junior clerk went up a bluff road with a stamp. He wrote in the right-hand column of a charter folder that the respondent institution had been found compliant. The chancellor gave him tea, and he said afterward that she had been perfectly civil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:61:It was the third thing from the top, and he took it in its turn. It was a single leaf of registry stock under the seal of the seat at Ostrand, and he knew the form. When a proceeding closed, its seat sent a summary to every office that had been named in it, and each office returned the leaf with whatever it wished to have stand beside the record. His office had been named twice: once as the author of an advisory, three sentences long, which the petition had quoted with approval, and again as the certifying office, which had been asked for its name and had declined to give it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:69:*In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward. Enrollment: closed by resignation; the house's certificate annexed. Proceedings: moot. Re-evaluation: not executed. Asset-restriction: pending jurisdiction.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:75:*Closed by resignation.* He turned that one over longest. He had seen four young people and an older man in a room with nothing of anybody's in it, and had read what they brought him, and had told them to their faces what he would not do. He had not asked what they would do. It had not been his to ask, and he had thought it better not to carry the answer. He saw it now, in a clerk's round hand: they had not appealed, and they had not published; they had waited for the one door in the code that left a house clean, and they had walked out of it in good order, with a registrar's certificate behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:87:*The instrument did not fail for want of care.* That was true, and they would cite it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:113:None of that had happened yet. It was only the First-day, the morning a desk clerk on the bluff found four keys in a row, and they left Ostrand at first light, by the east road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:269:"I was not there to see," said Seln. "I imagine it was noticed. I left the keys where they could be counted." He turned his cup a quarter turn on his knee. "I have filed nothing today. There has not been a First-day like it in fifteen years, and I had expected to mind it more."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:5:It was the only use he could find for the quarter hour before the seat came in. The coach had gone down the bluff road at the usual pace behind Withrow's carriage, and the two coats at the road's foot had looked at it as they looked at it every Fifth-day, and the young officer at the side door had written the same two lines in his log. But the exchange's four steps had been crowded to the plinth this morning, and the benches behind Cael were filling with a noise he had not heard in that room since the preliminary. A closing, the house's counsel had said, was the one day besides the first when a proceeding sold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:7:So he sat with lot seventeen under his left boot and his hands on the house's table, and counted the people, as he had once counted the words in an instrument.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:13:Then the people who were there by office. The seat's clerk at his small table. Ilsev in the middle chair below the platform, with her file at its angle, and her four district evaluators. The senior chair and the junior at the records table. At the petitioner's table Jent, and for the first time a clerk of his own, a thin young man with a satchel who looked as if he had been told not to touch anything. Three officers at the doors. At the house's table Withrow, the counsel, Karis, Bracken with the seal on his knees, and himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:21:He did not stop there, though he might have. He went down them again, more slowly, and this time he was not counting heads. He was doing what he had done on the night the instrument was served, when he had gone through nineteen leaves looking for the place where it lied. He had looked for that place in every sitting since. He had looked for it in Jent's weight in a chair, and in a seal that Havel had found on no register, and under a grey hood that had never once shown him a face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:57:"It may," said Jent. "The seat held at the preliminary that the question was cognizable. Nothing the respondent has filed since has touched that holding, and nothing was meant to; her filings have been upon the facts, and the facts were hers before she filed. So I have nothing to answer. I have used ten minutes because the seat was generous with the hour, and I did not like to seem ungrateful."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:113:*And this. He isn't to be told. Not tomorrow on the oak, nor in a letter from wherever we are by then. He's the one person on this hill who wanted the true measurement and nothing else, and he's waited three years for it, and I'm going to take something from him in the middle of it and never say. Lira's known since Ardenmere. Brom, Karis. The one I took the quiet thing from knows now. He can't. It would put him on a file beside mine, and he's staying.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:207:Cael watched it from the north wall, because there was nothing else he could usefully do. The ferry came across low in the water and went back empty and came again. The bluff road had people on every switchback: wool-factors in their good coats, and carters, and a file of district clerks who had found that they had business on the hill. The man who had bought a second press was there, and so was the woman who kept the guesting-house, with a basket. The lake house's blue-grey wagon had not gone home after the ruling, and it came up at a walk with a lath in a canvas sleeve sticking out of the back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:227:On the fourth side was the faculty stand. Withrow sat in the registrar's chair at its middle, upright, with her hands in her lap, and Bracken stood behind her. Gault was at the stand's end with his instrument case set very straight beside him, though there was nothing in a bout for it to measure. At the long table Rooke stood with his glass and his board, and his clerk sat at his elbow with the house's book open, and the house's healer had the bench below with her bag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:309:It was not a flat hand, which was all he had ever shown on the bluff. This was the push at fighting load, the whole shove of it, through his palm and down, and the air over a square yard of floor went solid and then gave. Ephram's front heel came down on a board that had jumped, and the first beat of his cut landed crooked. A Blade could not take it back. The hip followed the crooked heel, and the edge followed the hip, wide, a yard from anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:337:Ephram did not know about the ten counts; nobody on the bluff did but three people on a tier. But he was the best Iron on the hill and he could see a man who had gone a beat late, and he came in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:1:# Chapter 40 — The Instrument
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:11:"He warned us," said Cael. "At Norhold, in a room that belonged to neither office, with forty clocks in it and nothing written down by either side. He told us to stop competing in public, and why, and he didn't have to. It cost him something to say it, and he said it anyway, and then he went back and was set aside for saying the same thing at the convening." He made himself go slowly. "And he's still the one evaluator in the Compact they need a sentence from. When the seat comes to re-evaluate me, somebody has to certify the protocol, and it'll be him they ask. He's going to be asked to sign the delivery, and he doesn't know what's being delivered."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:13:"Seln paid twelve years for that case," Cael said, "and we've had it six nights. We can't file it, and we can't publish it, and we can't use it. But we can let one honest man inside the thing see what the thing is, before they hand him the pen." He looked at the cloth. "I think we owe him that, and I think it's the same debt as the stair. I don't know how to tell the difference."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:25:"He verifies it," she said. "He's an evaluator; it's the whole of what he is. He doesn't believe a thing because it's on a table; he measures it. So he goes home and pulls a frame reading, or a station sheet, or a fresh reading on one of the forty, to see whether the case is true." She turned her pale fingers over on the cloth. "And the frame's theirs, and the station's theirs, and the reading runs through the function. Every instrument he could check it with comes back and says the case is wrong. And he believes his instruments, because he's believed them for forty years. And somewhere it gets minuted that a forgery was offered to the Archmarshal by persons connected with the respondent at Halcenvane."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:41:"He reads it," he said, "and he asks about me. He's an evaluator, and I'm the one subject in the Compact he's never been able to read, and he's sitting across a table from the case and from me. He asks the natural question: why is the boy's file marked, what is he, how did the function fail on him." He made himself say the rest. "And somebody in the room answers, not because they mean to, but because it's the natural thing, and he asks well, and he's kind about it, and it's late."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:85:*Four streets back from the seat, on the wharf side. The tall house with the brass plate. The third floor. The second bell after noon. Neither office's. Nothing written.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:97:He took the note down the bluff road himself on the Fifth-day, in the house's ordinary post, with a dozen of the wing's letters, and left it at the inn on the wharf with the inn's boy in an envelope addressed in a copying hand to *the officer of the river inspection*. The coats at the foot of the road saw a clerk with the post. They had seen him with the post every week for two years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:103:Lira went with the program's carriers on the market cart at the first bell, because Rooke's program bought its tape and chalk at the chandler's by the bridge and somebody had to carry it, and she was not allowed to carry anything heavier. Brom walked, by the long road round the bluff, which took an hour and was the only exercise his shoulder would let him take. Karis took the house's errand to the fee office with a letter of Bracken's in her wallet. Seln went with the post. Cael went last, alone, by the ferry, because it was the way he always went when he went at all. He sat on the bench by the rail and let the coats at the landing look at him as they had looked at him for two years, and nobody wrote down anything but the hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:105:The lane was narrow and steep, with a gutter down the middle and the backs of the warehouses on one side and a row of tall old houses on the other, let out floor by floor to clerks and factors and men who did a trade in paper. The building was the fourth along, with a door with a brass plate beside it listing its tenants, and a keeper's window, and a stair. Seln was at the keeper's window when Cael came in, writing his reason in the book, and did not look round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:127:"He came in by the front, not the yard. He walked up the lane from the wharf end, on the gutter side, in grey, with nothing in his hands, and he stopped and read the brass plate by the door, and then he went in past the keeper's window, where the keeper could see him." Seln looked at the door. "By the clock on the landing, eleven minutes early."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:201:"The first is verification." His eyes did not go to the case. "I want to. I have wanted to since the twenty-third pair. I could ride home and send for a frame and a station's own sheet and a fresh reading on any one of those forty children, and watch the case come true under my own hand, and sleep." The finger stayed where it was. "But there is no instrument in the Compact I could test it with that does not run on the thing it accuses. The frames are theirs. The sheets come up the same stair. The reading passes through the very function on that leaf before anybody writes it down. Whatever I measured would be a play put on for my benefit by the defendant." He was quiet a moment. "And a play of that kind has a cast. A girl's name on a request, or a junior's on an order. I will not put a child in a ledger so that an old man can feel sure."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:209:"The third is certification." For the first time he looked at Karis, only at Karis, and Cael understood that the not looking at himself was being chosen, and chosen again, every moment. "Before this year is out the seat will send to my desk for my certificate on the protocol for a re-evaluation at Halcenvane. I have known it was coming since the convening. I meant to sign it, because the protocol is sound, and in forty years I have never refused my name to a sound protocol." He lifted his hand off the table. "When it comes, I shall decline. It is the only sentence I have left that they still need."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:273:He would not write the man down. He would not write any of them down: not the girl with the turned ankle who had asked him a straight question, not the big one with the bad shoulder, not the researcher who had held out her gloves, not the boy he had not let himself look at. A description is a file with the name left off, and he had spent forty years learning how easily the name comes back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:281:He could send to the station for its own sheet. A keeper writes at the margin of his own sheet, whatever the clerk of entry is told to enter, and the district clerk in the river town had said so in four lines. The sheet would be there, in a drawer in the north, with the keeper's hand on it. But a request from the Archmarshal's office for a station sheet goes in the dispatch ledger downstairs in his clerk's tidy upright hand, with a number and a date and a name, which would be her name. Then she would be a file, and somebody somewhere would read the ledger. There would be an order, clipped to a copy, and nine weeks of somebody watching her, and a slip on the back of it with nothing on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:291:He had a file under his arm, a different one, and a question about a guild hall's frame that took a minute to ask and a minute to answer. When it was answered he did not go. He stood in the doorway with the file against his chest, nervous and good, and looked past the Archmarshal at the tray.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:301:"Write what the instrument says," said the Archmarshal. "What you feel about it is information about you." He heard himself say it, the sentence every evaluator is told once, the sentence he had handed down to every junior he had ever had, and he heard for the first time what it would sound like to a man who knew what was on a leaf in a sleeve in a borrowed room in Ostrand. He did not take it back. It was the only safe thing he had to give the boy. "Keep her file as it is. Don't send for anything. If she does well, let her do well."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:39:"With no date. Withrow underlined that twice in her own code, nine years apart. And three is part nine." He had it by heart, having heard Bracken read it on the night of the procedure. "They may restrict, freeze or hold anything that attaches to me: a record, a sponsorship, a travel paper, *any institutional relationship.* They've walked those fences already. They read Lira's file for completeness, and they wrote to Brom's family's estate, and they queried Karis's standing twice, and they sent two polite men to Denvash to ask about a shop's title."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:47:"And there's what it is. Karis found it, the fourth of her findings. The frame is how they get me back inside. I'd have a line on a file that says *suspended, pending review*, and somebody told to observe, and I'd be entered, and whatever I was after that would be what was written." He looked at the slip. "I saw a girl go into a station on the north road on the Third-day, and she came out happy. I'll not stand in that building. I can lose everything else that's on this table tonight, but I'll not be entered as advised."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:61:"We have the thing that ends it," said Karis. "It's between Seln's feet. I spent fifty-one minutes last week telling a seat that its instrument had failed to read him, and I couldn't say why. I know why. I can date it to within a renewal of a charter. That isn't an argument from discretion; it's the newest matter anybody has put before the registry in two hundred years, and the code gives an appeal from a seat's ruling upon new matter. I looked it up on the night we came home from the close, because I couldn't sleep. It goes to the convening, within thirty days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:63:"The convening sent the instrument," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:65:"I'm arguing. Let me." Karis held up a finger. "It needn't be the convening. There's the Archmarshal's office. He has read it. He stood at a window in a borrowed room and said what it was, and he has since refused the protocol his name, and a finding from him, with the case behind it, is a thing no seat could set aside as a forgery. And if not him, there's the press. There's a man in the market street who bought a second press this year on the strength of our name, and a Gold at Auremont who has already told the registry in public to classify its instruments. Forty families could be told what was done to their children. It's true, and it's proved, and in my hands it's dated." Her voice had risen a little. "I have never in my life held a true thing and not filed it. I don't know how. Everything I am says that you put it in front of somebody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:77:"An appeal upon new matter is examined before it is heard. That is the code also. It goes to a records office to be verified, and the office would open a district's station book against a clipped order, and find that the two disagree, and report an irregularity in the *exhibit*." He said it without any heat. "Every instrument they would use to test it runs on the thing it accuses. A man told you so at a window. It was his first refusal, and he gave it before any of us had asked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:89:"And the forty," he said. "The families. Say it reached them. A man in a river town opens a sheet and reads that his daughter wasn't entered as her plate showed, eleven years ago, because of an order. What does he do with it? Whose door does he go to?" He lifted his head. "The same door. There's one door, and it's theirs. He takes it to the district, and a clerk who's never heard of any of it looks in the book, and the book says Copper, and the clerk's telling the truth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:101:"Yes," she said. "That's the answer. I knew it was, and I needed to hear it lose." She put her hand on the second slip. "You don't file a grievance with the instrument that forged the record. You leave its jurisdiction."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:143:"*The practitioner is advised that associations of record with any person subject to an order under the fourteenth chapter may be noted upon the practitioner's file, at the registry's discretion, and may be considered upon any later review of standing or sponsorship. The practitioner may wish to regularize such associations before signature.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:149:"It means put them in order," said Bracken. "It was drafted, I believe, with debts in mind: a man advancing to Iron who had stood surety for a suspended partner." He was holding his glasses very tightly. "Nobody has lied in it. The clerk who marked that margin did so because a file told him to. I have marked such margins. I should like that said, in this room, by me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:153:It was all there on two pieces of paper. There was the number he had wanted since before he had a Path, found honestly that morning, in a frame, in front of people whose work it was to check; there was his family's ledger, which closed if he signed; and there was a polite printed paragraph telling him that it would all sit easier on his file if he were first to put a certain association in order.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:207:"Iron Rank One stays mine," said Lira. "That's a certificate, and it's in my coat, and it was true before any of this."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:223:"*Greyvane, the archive at: a second request, sealed by the fee office in the week the first was paid, for the founders' correspondence; answer expected in about eight weeks. I can't forfeit an answer that hasn't come, and so I have written to redirect it.*" She looked up from the sheet, at Cael, and for the first time that night she seemed unsure of herself. "*Care of an instrument-maker, at Denvash.* I ought to have asked you. There wasn't anybody else whose address will still be an address."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:227:"I should hope so." She looked down again. "*Research standing, enrolled: forfeit. Restricted collection, right of entry to: forfeit.*" That one took her half a second longer than the others. "*Tier, registry certification of: Iron, Rank Three. Retained.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:241:"I resigned fifteen years ago. I just never filed it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:269:Ephram came in as he had come in on a birthday, a little out of breath, with the captain's file under his arm in its green tape, and he had plainly not been asleep. He stopped inside the door, and his eyes went along the table from end to end as they went along a floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:285:"Past the Line, then." Ephram stood with his hand on the file. "Then nobody will ever write a figure for you again. Not Gault, nor a panel, nor me." He was quiet for the space of two breaths. "I thought it might be that. I've thought so since the boxes went onto Bracken's shelf; I've eyes, and I walk the records hall on Third-days. I didn't know it was all of you." He looked at Brom, who had come in behind him, and Brom looked back, and whatever passed between the two of them had been settled in a harness room over a rack of iron plates and did not need a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:309:Two years ago this was the worst thing that could have happened to him on the bluff. He had built a band and a rotation and a variance so that nobody would ever stand where Ephram was standing and say what Ephram had just said. He waited for the old cold to come up through his stomach, and it did not come. There was a ruling in his satchel, and a slip on the table with one word on it. The band had been rent paid on a room, and he was leaving the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:339:Lira went to the door and opened it. Ephram was standing at the head of the stair with his back to her, looking down four flights into the dark, with the green file under his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:75:"That was an honest answer, and it is the whole of this case. Every leaf the house has filed went to the facts, and the petitioner has agreed the facts. What is left is a discretion. The registry may ask this question, the seat has held, if it chooses to, and I do not come here to say that it may not; I am bound by the seat's holding and I respect it." She paused. "I come to ask what the discretion is for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:81:She gave it to them plainly, as a thing that belonged to the room: that the provisions existed for the testing of instruments, of frames and stations and records and protocols, and that they had been written so that the registry might know which of its own instruments it could trust.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:83:"Instruments," said Karis. "That is the handle. I went and read how the tool has been used, to see whether the hands that held it could read, and they could. There are ninety-one recorded invocations of these sections in the registry's whole record. Eighty-four concern things: a frame that read a rank high, a station seam that let in the damp, a plate, a ledger, a seal. The sixty-third was filed by the registrar who is sitting at this table, in his fourth year, upon a station frame in this town, and the registry thanked him for it." Bracken looked at the seal's box. "The other seven name a person, and I have read each of them through. What the registry complained of, every time, was an act: somebody had sent in a false return, or paid for a rank, or lent out a seal. Not one of the seven complains of what the person was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:91:"So," said Karis. "A tool for the testing of instruments, used ninety-one times as it was made to be used, and now to be used a ninety-second time in a new way. I should like to look at the new way, and I shall be exact about it. Being exact is my discipline, and I have no other."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:109:Ilsev looked up. It was only for a moment, over the top of her file, at the thin girl on the boards; then she looked down again, and her hand moved to the foot of a leaf and wrote something small.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:111:"And the fourth," said Karis, and her little finger went down. "I asked. I asked the petitioner what evidence would defeat his petition, and a reading in the frame is evidence, and he told me there was none. He did not say, *let us see what the frame returns.* He is too honest a man to say so, and in thirteen sittings he never has. Nobody has. No officer, no clerk, no witness has stood in this room and told the seat that the frame will return a classification. It was asked once before, at a district station, when he was fourteen, and it took eleven seconds to answer, and its answer is the word on his file."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:127:"Now I come back to the handle. These sections are for the testing of instruments, so that the registry may know which of them to trust, and there are two instruments before the seat. One is a frame, which was asked about this practitioner once and had no answer. The other is twenty-two officers of the registry. The house's counsel read their names to the seat at the preliminary, each with a grade and a house and a day: a provost's panel, a regional registrar's assessors, the tournament's, an evaluator of record who has sat across a trestle from him every week for two years. They watched, and wrote down what they saw, and signed it. Their notes agree with one another, and the petitioner has accepted every one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:131:"Twenty-two officers can see him, and one instrument cannot. The preamble tells the registry what to do when its instruments disagree: it is to find out which of them is to be trusted. It does not say that the registry is to prefer the one that failed, and fine the man for the failure."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:141:He knew what lay in that pause as well as he knew his own Log. It was the true argument. She had just told a seat, with perfect accuracy, that its instrument had failed to read him, and she knew why it had failed, and what was appended to the reading, and how long it had been so, and whose hands had signed for it. She could have dated the thing to within a renewal of Greyvane's charter. It would have taken her nine minutes, and she had the nine minutes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:159:Then she spoke of the people who would come after, and she did it quickly, for she had cut it twice. A ruling stood in the registry's books, she said, for every house that held a charter in the same words. The seat had before it four old challenges to the clause, and in one of them a ferryman's daughter in a southern river house had waited a year for a station to settle upon what she was; the house had waited with her, and at the end of the year the station had read her, and she had stayed. Under the ruling now asked for, nobody need have waited. Somebody need only have filed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:201:"*Evaluation-protocol certification: declined by the evaluator of record; proceeding on the presiding seat's authority.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:205:It was a small thing to see, and he did not think the benches saw it. The four district evaluators had been writing in a row, and at that line they stopped, one after another, like men on a road who see the one ahead halt. They were working officers. Every one of them had stood practitioners in frames for years, under a protocol that carried the Archmarshal's certificate as a matter of course, as a loaf carries a baker's mark; the certifying office had put its name to that protocol since before any of them was born. It had been asked twice for its name and had not given it, and the form had no line for why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:207:The evaluator nearest the records table looked at Ilsev. Ilsev did not look at him. She was looking at the clerk, with her file shut on her knee, and her face told Cael nothing whatever.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:291:In another place, that week, a file was closed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:293:Nothing can be said of the room. It may be said that there was a table, and a lamp, since the work could not have been done without them. There was a clerk, and of the clerk it can be said only that the hand was practised, and that it had closed a great many files.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:295:This one had the name on its cover that the instruments used. It was thick; it ran from a single leaf, taken down in a district office at the far end of the country, to four lines sent up that week from a seat at Ostrand. The clerk had those four lines on a slip and was to enter them at the file's foot, in order.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:305:The clerk wrote them. Each was copied as it stood, and each was true. A file with those four lines at its foot was a finished thing, and it would ordinarily have been tied, and stamped, and carried down to the press where finished things were kept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:309:It lay in the pocket inside the file's front cover, where slips of advice were put. The clerk had not put it there. It carried no office's mark, and no initial, and it was in a plain copying hand that might have been anybody's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:311:The clerk had met such slips before, though not often. There was one of them on this file already. It was older than any other paper in it, older than the first leaf, and it had given the subject a designation that two careful officers had since tried to trace and could not. That one sat at the head of the file, and this one had come to its foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:321:The file was not tied, and it was not carried down to the press. A file with that line at its foot was not finished, whatever else was written on it. It went back onto a different shelf, among others of its kind, with its spine outward, where it could be reached.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:331:"The file," said Seln. "I have been thinking of it, since I shall not be let stand a watch. A subject who withdraws is closed. A subject who withdraws and then goes where no district runs is a different matter, and there is a line for that; I have copied it on to other people's files, in my time." He turned the cup. "It says *non-compliance*, which is an odd word for a man who has complied with everything. Then it says that the priority of observation is escalated. It is a form. It means that somebody is told to look harder."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:345:"I have nine things that aren't a Path," Cael said, eventually, to nobody in particular, "no tier, no rank, no enrollment, no protection — and four people who signed out of the world that was supposed to provide them, and one of the nine taken from a hand the world sent to hold me, and one taken in earnest from a friend, and the machine's own maintenance records in a case under that canvas, which we can't file and can't publish and are carrying east anyway because the truth is worth carrying even when it can't be spent."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:11:He walked her to the door, because he had nothing else on the hill to do, and it was a strange thing to find out about himself at the third bell of a working day. For two years he had always been due somewhere: at a trestle in wing three, or a table at the top of the library, or the north wall at the changing of the coats. Now his name stood in a book with five words against it, and nobody on the bluff was expecting him anywhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:77:It was the same thing. She could see that plainly, and she was not going to tell herself a kinder story. It was a school, and a room with a board, and a girl going out of it for good with her certificate in her coat, and anybody watching from the court would have seen exactly what a porter at Fenmark had seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:95:Cael went round by it on his way to the residence. He told himself that it was to hand in his floor card, though the card had been complete since the last supervised bout, and he could have left it on his sill with the instrument.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:121:"The office mentions one thing," said Seln to the sheet in front of him. "There is a district officer on the bluff road. I saw him from the wing's end window at the half bell. He is a stout man and he rides like one, and he will be an hour yet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:139:The plaque was on the shelf, face down. He took it down and turned it over, dark wood with a brass plate and the bar and ring cut in it, and he looked at it for about as long as he had looked at the pen. Then he wrapped it in a shirt and put it at the bottom of the pack, against his back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:217:"I thought you would. I'd a paper about you, for the chancellor." He turned the envelope over, mildly. "A date, and a room, and would she kindly bring you. She gave me a chair and showed me a book, and it seems she's nobody of that name to bring." He did not sound put out. "I've written across it. *No such enrollee; withdrawn this day; see the registrar's certificate.* That's what I found, so that's what goes back."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:229:Ephram walked beside him, on his left, at the same pace. He had his right arm bound across his chest in the healer's linen and his coat hung from the shoulder, and his left hand was empty; he was carrying nothing whatever. He did not look at the box. He looked straight ahead, as a man walks in a file, and he kept his step to Brom's. He could not have taken an end of it with one arm, and he had not offered. He had only come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:263:She had come down from her own range on her own feet. She had come to that gate once before, for four riders, and had an instrument read aloud. She had nothing in her hands today.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:269:"Your files are in order," said Withrow. "All four."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:273:It was also everything she had. She had been twenty years in the registry and a good many more in that chair, and she had spent all of them learning that a file in order was the one thing an officer could truly give a person; a clerk could not make a paper kind, and could only make it right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:289:Rooke looked at Lira, and she looked back, and neither of them said anything at all, since it had been said at dawn, over a book. Then he looked at Brom, who was at the tailboard with the last of the rope in his hands. Brom had stood in a district's frame two days ago and felt a plate come up warm, and had left the paper for it under a film of dust; and Rooke had heard everything on the hill by the second bell of any morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:305:They went down the bluff road at dusk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:331:The woman who kept the guesting-house asked no questions. She had come up the bluff with a basket the day before, and she had seen what there was to see; she gave them the parlour with the two-paned door, and they put the cart in her yard, under the pentice, beside the old screw press.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:333:He wrote the day's line there, sitting on the press's platen with the book on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:9:They were as he had squared them at dusk. Cael had read their heading from the covered walk, with his arm new in its binding, and had thought then that four sheets of paper was a small thing for a year to come to; and he thought so still. A challenge had come up the bluff road under escort, with outriders, and been read aloud standing, and the answer to it had weighed eleven pounds four ounces. This would not have tipped the post-room's beam.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:11:"I have filled in what the enrollment book could tell me," said Bracken. He had one pair of glasses on and the other two on his forehead. "The house, the year of entry, the basis, the tier as certified. I did it last night, to spare you the writing, and because I did not care to have anybody misspell this house in my hall on its last morning with them. Three lines are left to each of you: the day, and your name in your own hand, in my presence, and a line for the reason, which the registry prints because it is curious and which the code allows an enrollee to leave empty." He moved the pen a finger's width toward them. "I take them in the order they are offered. I have no order of my own."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:21:"It stays true. A house does not certify a tier and cannot uncertify one, and that line is on the form only so that the registry may know whom it is reading about."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:71:*Enrollee: Caelen Hesk-ward.* It was the name the registry had given him at Denvash, the one that stood on every instrument and that nobody who knew him had ever used, and Bracken had written it out in full, because a form was an instrument. *House: Halcenvane. Basis of enrollment: the demonstration provision of the assay clause; granted at Greyvane; twice renewed.* And on the line for tier, where Lira's leaf said *Iron* and Brom's said *Copper*, the registrar had written the only true thing there was to write, as he had seen it written on a door log at a wool exchange every Fifth-day for a year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:157:"There is one line more on the council's minute," he said, and he took up the sheet again. "*The enrollee's tier, as certified by the registry: Ember; Iron, Rank Three.* I have not read it to you, and I have struck it from this schedule, in ink, with my initials against the stroke, because it is not the house's. It was never the house's to grant, and it does not lapse with a room." He looked at her over the glasses. "Nobody can take a tier from you, Advocate. They can only decline to count it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:175:"Tomorrow, at the first bell, I shall open the daybook and enter the post. On the next Seventh-day I shall wind that clock. In the week after the close a clerk will come up the road with a stamp for the chancellor's folder, and I shall see that he is handed the right volume and initials his own line." He took the last pair of glasses off his forehead and laid all three in a row. "I tell you so because you might suppose, after this year, that a man in my chair would go about it differently. I have asked myself whether I ought. I intend to keep true records, as I have kept them, and four files will go down to the district in tomorrow's bag with not a line in them that I could not swear to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:187:*Assay-provision enrollment: resigned in good standing prior to revocation date. Baseline file: closed complete. Four terms of evaluation-linked observation, no adverse finding, no anomaly of conduct. The evaluator of record notes that the file closes on the enrollee's own act and not the registry's, and that this is the correct closing, and the only just one available. The instruments will not see its like.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:191:*The instruments will not see its like.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:243:On the sill lay the enrollee's copy of the instrument. It had lain there, first leaf up, since the eighth day of the year: nineteen leaves on one side, thirty-seven paragraphs, three schedules, whose words he had counted on the first night as a man counts the guards on a wall. He left it where it lay, since it had been addressed to the house as much as to him, and the house could have it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:25:The first he had never seen. He had carried the file it answered; he had laid that file at her elbow in a hall on a bluff, and gone back to the foot of the table. A return had gone into it then, and he had not read it, because a records officer did not read what was addressed to the senior seat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:31:His oldest sheet said that there was a mark in a boy's file with no office behind it. He had written that after he asked, in the proper way, and was told behind a shut door that it was not his clearance, and he had taken that to mean that somebody above him knew whose mark it was. In all these years he had wanted nothing from that mark but the name of an office. He would not have needed to be told the name. It would have been enough to know that one was written down somewhere, in a register he could not open, against a date.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:41:"This can't be right. *Designation predates file creation.* A mark can't be older than the file it's written in. That's like a stamp being older than its letter." She looked up at him, quite earnestly, with the ink on her chin. "I think somebody's dropped a word. Shall I query it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:71:The senior evaluator was still in the long room, which he had not expected. She sat in the middle chair below the covered platform with her file shut on her knee, quite alone, looking at nothing, like any woman at the end of a day's work. As he came down the side of the benches her right hand went to the breast of her coat and rested there, over the cloth, and came away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:93:It was a strange word to think, because nothing whatever was done. The mark stood on the file as it had always stood, and the seal he had hunted through three ledgers still belonged to no office. He had six facts and no leave to add them up, and he had never once tried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:113:He knew the grounds. He had sat forty feet from a table for a year of Fifth-days and had not looked at it. He had looked at that boy before, on a pump step and at Greyvane and on a sprung floor at the bluff; he remembered every one of the looks, and had sworn off another, and had kept to it for a year by looking at his own hands. He was tired in a way that had nothing to do with the hours, and he wished to work in a room where the leaves were only leaves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:147:"I held it." He thought about how to say the rest. "It was more than Ephram ever put on, a good deal over. They let it down on springs, and then they let it down again while I had it, which he never did, and I heard the middle one say *forty* and go on counting." He looked at his forearms. "The plate lit. I felt it under my boots. It's warm. I didn't know they were warm."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:153:"It said Iron," said Lira. She had got her breath. "I was on the bench by the door and I watched the middle assessor's face when the plate came up. He looked like a man who'd put his hand in his pocket for a coin and found the coin he expected." She shoved her hands deeper into her own pockets. "He was right about himself, in front of people whose job it was to check. I saw it happen. I wanted somebody to have seen it who knew what it was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:155:Brom did not say anything to that. He stood in the market street with his forearms marked and his face quite calm, and Cael saw that it had been enough. Whatever a clerk wrote on whatever paper, a plate had come up warm under his boots and said what was so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:255:"It is exactly what it says it is. There is nothing behind it at all." He said it in his mild voice, as he might have reported a floor schedule. "In my trade a man is taught four grades of pressure, in their order. The record. The instrument. The knock, which is paper nobody will own. And last the hand, which is a road with no paper at all. I was taught that the hand was the worst of them, and I believed it for fifteen years." He shook his head, very slightly. "I had the order wrong. What stood in the road was a glove." He looked down at the last line of the leaf. "*Given under the Compact's hand.* That is the only true sentence of its kind I have been shown this year. This is the hand. It does not have another."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:91:It was not under glass. It had been pinned up bare, a long time ago, and had gone brown at its edges. Cael knew the print, and he knew the sheet, for it was the registry's public notice of tiers, which he had seen on the wall of every station and guild hall he had ever stood in, and in an instrument-maker's shop, behind the door. There were five words down its left side in heavy type, Copper, Iron, Bronze, Silver and Gold, and against each stood its ranks, and the fee for an evaluation, and the office to which a practitioner should apply.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:267:The plate lay in the floor, in the middle of the room, under its frame. It was brass, and it had gone the brown of an old coin, and there was dust on it, for the carters who swept the house had left this room alone. Nothing in it stirred when he stood in the door, and he had not thought that it would.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:271:"You couldn't read me," he told it, conversationally, the way you tell a fact to an old machine. "Since my Kindling I thought that was the system failing. Turns out it was the most honest thing the system ever did — the one time the layer under you couldn't finish its job, because there was no bin beneath me to file the lie in." He looked at the dark frame a moment longer. "You're the only part of the whole apparatus that never lied about me. You just went quiet. Everything above you did the lying." He shouldered the satchel. "Thanks for the eleven seconds. They were the truest measurement I ever got."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:319:He walked back to where Seln stood, and looked west, with his arms hanging, as he looked at a rack of plates. The track ran back across the upland, pale, as far as the roofs of the station, with the water beyond, and the bare rise. There was no rider on it. There was no lantern, and no tin cup, and no man with a pencil on a top tier.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:371:It was soft at the folds by now. It had come up a bluff in a post-room sort in the year's seventh week, and he had read it some dozens of times, at a sill, and knew it without the flame. A Gold at Auremont had written to tell him not to mistake what was happening to him for procedure. She had said that the rematch stood, and she had told him to keep himself unclassified.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:375:*They ruled. We left — resigned, before the date, in good standing, four files closed complete and one man who filed nothing. You said keep myself unclassified. Done, permanently, on my own paper this time: I'm past the Registry Line, where the word has no meaning, which turns out to be the only place the word is safe.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:437:Cael watched the bag go. It had her name in it. It would go to a turf fire, and down a hill, and onto a hook, and it would cross the whole of a country that had a file on him, and come at last to a hall in a city, and to a woman for whom a ladder had been built to exactly her own height.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:481:He had thought he knew what large was. He had seen the continent drawn on a sheet. He had crossed a good part of it, at a wagon's pace, to stand in a hall at Norhold, and he had looked down from a bluff at a river valley every day for two years and thought it a wide thing.

