editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:3:Vastin went down the tower stair at his ordinary pace and out under the arch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:5:There was a big young man sitting on the step there with bandaged forearms, watching the court. He looked up as Vastin passed. Vastin knew him from the row: the continental Copper champion, the bluff's wall. He nodded to him, as one nods to a man keeping a door. The young man nodded back, gravely, and went on watching the court.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:271:"You couldn't read me," he told it, conversationally, the way you tell a fact to an old machine. "Since my Kindling I thought that was the system failing. Turns out it was the most honest thing the system ever did — the one time the layer under you couldn't finish its job, because there was no bin beneath me to file the lie in." He looked at the dark frame a moment longer. "You're the only part of the whole apparatus that never lied about me. You just went quiet. Everything above you did the lying." He shouldered the satchel. "Thanks for the eleven seconds. They were the truest measurement I ever got."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:237:They were not there for his rank. Iron Rank Six was a good line on a certificate, and near the top of the seeding. But there were other Sixes in the bracket, and a Seven, and nobody stood in a knot at their rope. The knot was there for how the work looked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:277:That was all he said at the midday table about either bout, and he said it to the bread. He had his left forearm on the board beside his bowl, already darkening under the skin from wrist to elbow. He had declined the ice, the salve and Ephram's opinion, in that order.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:123:"It's upon the entry they read out," said Karis, as they crossed the lane. "The certificate. The code lets a seat go on by its own authority where the certificate's withheld; I looked at the section the night we got home. So I went back through the digests to see how often a seat ever had." She held up four fingers. "Four times. I've set them out, with their years. A guild hall's frame, where the assessor wouldn't sign. Two station frames in the hill districts. A plate. Four times in the whole record a seat has proceeded without the certifying office's name, and every time it was upon a thing. Never once upon a person."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:241:"The scope of this seat was minuted at the third sitting, in the presiding seat's words," said Ilsev. She spoke slowly, with a small pause at the end of every clause, and three pens went with her. "It has kept within them. Within that scope the evaluation seat is obliged to lay before the proceedings any matter upon the subject's file that would govern the conduct of an evaluation. There is one such matter. The file carries a designation. The petitioner has set it out in the second schedule to his petition, at the fourteenth line. A designation of that kind governs who must be present at an evaluation, and to what office its result is sent."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:245:"This officer has twice referred that designation, in the proper form, to learn the office that entered it. She has received two returns. She will read both into the record, entire, as they came to her."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:249:"The first. *Return, from the chain of review, to the senior evaluation seat, on referral of the matter at Greyvane. Designation: Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4.*" The pause. "*Determination: no originating authority of record. Designation predates file creation. No action available at this clearance.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:257:"And the note which this officer has written beside that designation upon every form she has filed concerning the subject, and which she now enters here. *Authorizing office: not traced.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:261:"The evaluation seat notes the contradiction. The registry relies upon a designation to govern an evaluation, and the registry's own returns say that no office of record entered it. The evaluation seat makes no finding upon that. It is not within the scope minuted for this seat. This officer has been told that no further inquiry is authorized, and she makes none. She reads what she was sent." A last pause, no longer than the others. "That is the return."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:269:*Designation predates file creation.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:281:"The petitioner set that designation out in his schedule because it is upon the file," he said. "He was not told its office either. He has no observation."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:49:*Every person who has ever decided a thing about me had a face, and I could read it. Pellin at Denvash, with her brass plate. Coss across a desk. Vastin with his frames. The provost who was frightened and couldn't hide it. Umber polishing his spectacles over a sheet he couldn't score. I read every one of them from across a room, and every reading was worth something.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:111:*The registry code: chapter fourteen. The registry code: the integrity provisions. The registry code: the schedule of discretions. The petition as served. The convening's minute. The clause, as the registry certifies it. The standardization directive, with its schedules. The advisory upon the fielding clause. The adjudication office's referral of the enrollee's entry.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-54.md:240:"Whatever you are," he said, "I said I'm glad it's on my side of the floor. At the trial. When she stopped to read you." He looked at his bound shoulder. "The floor's about to be the whole continent, and I'm not on it — I'm staying; you know I'm staying; it's the right thing for me and you'd tell me so if I asked, which is why I'm not asking." He put out his left hand. "Still glad. That's the whole thing. I made myself wait two years to fight you straight and it was worth every month, and I don't need to know what you are to know that. Go be it somewhere with more room."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:75:It was the Greyvane ruling. She had laid it down before anything else, before the paper she was answering had a single paragraph answered, on the bare table in the middle of three cleared feet of wood. The ruling, the certified transcript under it, and Prynn's own copy of the provision on top of both, in that small exact archivist's hand Cael had first seen on a high desk in a room that smelled of cold paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:3:The notice on the assessment wing's board was in Gault's hand this week, not the desk clerk's, and Cael read it twice before he understood why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:183:Nobody stopped him. The assessment wing was dark except for the lamp at the counter, and the door to wing three had never been locked in his time. He went in and stood on the old pine boards with the high east windows black above him, and then he walked the floor, slowly, from his chalk at the south end to the Current's at the north, counting strides. Fourteen. He walked it again along the line her bend would take. Then he went to the west end and looked up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-18.md:217:"Yesterday morning at first light," said Karis, "you ran your entry in the loading yard behind the exchange, in the open, twice through, slowly and at speed. From the inn's back door I could see you. From the gate, so could a steward. Your quarterfinal opponent declined that exact entry twice that afternoon, and you said at the rope it was as if somebody had told him. This morning the Stone declined it too." She closed the notebook on her finger. "Somebody did tell them. It was sold inside a day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:15:The letter said where the rubbing had come from, in its first paragraph, with the instrument quoted whole: it was the seal off the escort order that the leader of four riders had read aloud in the house's outer court on the Sixth-day morning, and that the house had declined at its own gate and let ride away again down the bluff road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:175:Cael counted the cut out of the window as they went, because he counted everything. Four hundred and forty yards, near enough, by the coachman's pace and the bends at each end. At the far end, the east end, the road narrowed to cross a culvert where a stream came down a gully off the hill and went under the road toward the river, with a low stone parapet on either side. Beside the near parapet stood a milestone. He could not read it from that distance. It would say seven.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:61:"We have the thing that ends it," said Karis. "It's between Seln's feet. I spent fifty-one minutes last week telling a seat that its instrument had failed to read him, and I couldn't say why. I know why. I can date it to within a renewal of a charter. That isn't an argument from discretion; it's the newest matter anybody has put before the registry in two hundred years, and the code gives an appeal from a seat's ruling upon new matter. I looked it up on the night we came home from the close, because I couldn't sleep. It goes to the convening, within thirty days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:207:"Iron Rank One stays mine," said Lira. "That's a certificate, and it's in my coat, and it was true before any of this."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:227:"I should hope so." She looked down again. "*Research standing, enrolled: forfeit. Restricted collection, right of entry to: forfeit.*" That one took her half a second longer than the others. "*Tier, registry certification of: Iron, Rank Three. Retained.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-24.md:99:Cael could not read his weight from where he sat; nobody could read a man's weight at that distance across a moving floor. But he did not need to. He could see the man's face. It had been courteous for an exchange and a half, a good craftsman's face, kind to a younger one. Now it was simply interested. He looked at her as a man looks at a job he had thought would take an afternoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:41:"They'll put three assessors and a sigil frame in a room at the district seat about the size of our long room," said Bracken then, in the voice he kept for reading out a fee. "The district had ninety days from the day it handed you that summons to convene them. This convenes them, on paper, as of this morning, with weeks in hand. The sitting itself we asked them to put at the year's end. Rooke wanted a year of your figures on the table before anybody in a grey coat looked at them, and the district hall is crowded in the first weeks and empty in the last, and an empty hall suits a panel that has already made up its mind." He paused. "They agreed. Districts like a champion to look well in their own building."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:45:"There's a word you'll hear about this all year," he said. "In corridors. Behind doors. Said by people who think it's a kindness to keep it from you. I'd sooner you had it from me, across a table, now. Seven of these dockets have crossed my counter in thirteen years, and seven times the panel found for the tier the title was won at. Your name is cut on a staff in the middle of the continent where any carter can read it, and no district office alive would care to be caught reading you lower than that staff. It is a formality. You'll go into that room Copper and come out of it Iron. Your family's ledger closes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:83:Until this year the provision's supervised hours had been signed in the east and north halls. But over the recess Rooke's program had taken the east hall for its own, and the assessment wing had moved all of the provision's bouts into a hall of its own to make room, the third of its three. Everybody called it wing three. It was a long plain room behind Gault's office, with whitewashed walls and old pine boards and a row of east windows high up, so that in the mornings the light came in over the heads of the people at the trestle and lay along the floor in bars.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:165:Neither of them touched in the third. The Shield came on with his new turn and his patient feet, and he had grown bolder since the second exchange, as men do when a thing works. Cael read him and gave him ground and spent a burst on getting out of a corner he had walked into. Once the Shield's plane turned a blow of his so hard that his whole arm rang to the shoulder, and he took that too, the honest way, and felt it settle in the joint like a stone into mud.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:13:"I filed one once. My fourth year in the office. The district station in Ostrand had a frame that took to reading every Stone who stood in it a rank higher than he was. Not by much. Enough. A month of Stones went out of that station with ranks on their papers they hadn't earned, and nobody noticed until a quarryman's boy came up this hill with a certificate that said Rank Four and couldn't lift what a Rank Two lifts." He turned his folded hands over. "I wrote it up under the integrity provisions, because that's what they're for. They took the frame off its floor and tested it for a fortnight, and found a seam in the casing that let the damp in, and mended it, and read every Stone of that month over again. Then the frame went back on its floor and read true." He looked down the table at Cael. "That's an integrity question, in that volume. A frame that has drifted. You take it off the floor and test it until it reads true."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:21:"They've stopped asking that entirely. They gave it away in the sixth paragraph." The counsel closed the volume on her rule to keep the place. "Now they ask whether the registry can read you. Everybody at this table knows the answer. It has been in your file since your Kindling."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:27:Cael sat with that. All his life frames had been the things that failed to read him, and he had stood in front of them and been the question they could not answer. Now the paper had turned it round. He was the instrument on the bench, and they meant to open his casing and look for the seam that let the damp in, as Bracken's men had done with the station in Ostrand. He was not afraid of their looking. He was afraid of what they would write when they found no seam at all, because a frame with nothing wrong in it that still would not read was not a thing the second volume had a section for. It was a thing chapter fourteen had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:147:So he went out into the court with his copy under his arm. The fountain was the only sound on the hill. On his way across he looked, as he always looked, at the assessment wing, and at the far end of its long front, on the ground floor, behind the counter's window, one lamp was burning. At that hour, in a wing where nobody had business before the third bell. He stood in the court and did not let himself guess what the man at the copying table was making. Whatever it was, it would be made properly, and it would arrive without a note.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:111:She was quiet. He knew what she was seeing, because he was seeing it too. Standard protocol meant a station: a frame in a stone room, a registry officer at its side, a practitioner standing in it while the frame read him. It was how every Path on the continent had been declared since before the registry kept books. The frame read the Path and named it, and set a tier against it, and a rank inside the tier, and the officer wrote down what the frame had said, and that was a person's classification for life. It was how Lira had been declared, and Brom, and Karis herself. It was how the frame at Denvash had been asked, on the morning of his Kindling, what he was, and had answered with one word in brackets and nothing else at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:123:*Bracken's press: the four rulings on the clause. Ask him nicely. — The code, all three volumes, the hall's copy, not the library's; the library's second volume is missing a gathering. — The certified Greyvane transcript, on my shelf in the common room, grey card, my initials on the spine. — Tea. — Tell her she won't see her bed.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:155:"This was on my counter," he said, "when I came back from the press with the rulings. Stamped. Countersigned. In order." He read from it. "*Research standing: floor and archive hours, by application. Applicant: K. Dellenmoor. Hours: the records hall, this night, from the eighth bell until the work is done. Purpose: the house's answer to an instrument served this day. Received and entered.*" He lowered it. "The stamp is the assessment wing's. The hand on the received line is the wing's. The form was built in the recess for an office that had never had a use for it until this morning." He looked down at the three of them on his steps. "Somebody has been expecting us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:157:Across the court, behind the assessment wing's tall windows, the counter would be open, and a man would be at a copying table with his head down, rising when the queue moved to take the next sheet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:103:"No. That's why you lost." He said it without any heat. "From tomorrow, every count ends on the right. Every one. I don't care where the line wants to go. You'll land on that foot until you've stopped asking it for leave, and then you'll land on it a hundred times more." He went back to the trestle and bent over the red book, and wrote one word under her name, and Cael, from the door, could not read it and knew what it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:195:She minded. That surprised her a little. She had thought, after the reading room, after the forty pairs and the father's letter that she had read aloud in her own voice, that she would never again be able to care what a clerk at a counter laid beside what. She knew what the counter was. She knew what sat behind every certificate in the wing's drawer, her own among them, three lines in a hand she had never seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:17:*The Chancellor declined the order pending verification, on four grounds, as follows.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:27:*Four. The seat's own schedule has no assessment in it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:45:"Registrar," he said. "If you would add that the evaluator of record was not told of any assessment proposed for the enrollee. Before it was proposed, or since."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:13:*Phase one: assessment,* said the first column. That was fair. She had stood and let them talk to each other on the floor, in weight and in small turns of the head, and they had talked in the language the program had taught all four of them, and so she had understood every word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:247:"I won't read you the objection," said the head at last. "You'll have read it. You read everything." He looked at the painted founder rather than at her. "I'll only say that it was written by people who are fond of you, in a hurry, and that every line of it is true. A filed bout was never meant for this. The underwriters have no table for it. There's no bout like it on any record anywhere. I'd hoped that would be enough." He breathed out. "And in a few days you are to stand at the closing in front of every house on this continent with our banner behind you. That's all. That's the whole of what the program has to say."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-23.md:29:It was the kind of card she used for questions she meant a seat to hear. It had one line on it. Cael could read most things upside down by now, and he could not read this, because she had written it very small and kept the heel of her hand across it. He watched her strike one word in the line and write another above it. Then she struck that one too. She changed the same word four times, while the coach went the whole length of the meadows, and the fourth time she left it. She saw him watching. She did not hide the card. She turned it face down on the board and went back to the draft.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:11:"He warned us," said Cael. "At Norhold, in a room that belonged to neither office, with forty clocks in it and nothing written down by either side. He told us to stop competing in public, and why, and he didn't have to. It cost him something to say it, and he said it anyway, and then he went back and was set aside for saying the same thing at the convening." He made himself go slowly. "And he's still the one evaluator in the Compact they need a sentence from. When the seat comes to re-evaluate me, somebody has to certify the protocol, and it'll be him they ask. He's going to be asked to sign the delivery, and he doesn't know what's being delivered."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:139:Vastin stood on the landing in his plain grey coat with his hands empty at his sides, and did not come in at once. He looked at the room the way Seln had looked at the reading room on the birthday, the door and the window and the bare table and the shapes on the shelves, and then the five of them, one at a time. He did not let his eyes stay on any one of them longer than on the others, not on Cael, and not on the man who had opened the door to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:143:"I was told nothing would be written," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:155:Vastin read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:161:At the twenty-third, Vastin found the letter. He unfolded the father's sheet, and read it, and unfolded the clerk's four lines inside it, and read those. Then he read the father's again from the top. He folded them both along their old folds, exactly as Lira had folded them, and slid them back under the clip, and laid the pair face down on the others. He sat with his hand on top of the face-down stack. Then he took the twenty-fourth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:175:Vastin put the leaf back into its sleeve, carefully, by its edges, and laid the sleeve on the table a hand's width from everything else, where Karis had put it. He took off the gloves and folded them and laid them beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:185:Vastin did not move. His left hand lay on his knee, and once it opened and closed. His eyes were on the window, on the cupola, or past it, on nothing in particular, the way a man looks at the sky when he is listening for something a long way off. His face was quite still.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:189:Then Vastin spoke. He said it to the window.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:197:"Nobody here has asked me for anything," said Vastin. "I noticed that on the landing, and I have been noticing it for two hours." He laid his left hand on the scrubbed wood. "So I will save you the asking. There are three things I will not do, and you should go home knowing which."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:209:"The third is certification." For the first time he looked at Karis, only at Karis, and Cael understood that the not looking at himself was being chosen, and chosen again, every moment. "Before this year is out the seat will send to my desk for my certificate on the protocol for a re-evaluation at Halcenvane. I have known it was coming since the convening. I meant to sign it, because the protocol is sound, and in forty years I have never refused my name to a sound protocol." He lifted his hand off the table. "When it comes, I shall decline. It is the only sentence I have left that they still need."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:213:"I have made something over nine thousand evaluations," said Vastin, to the case, very quietly. "I counted them once, as a young man, and stopped counting when I stopped being one. Each of them I wrote down exactly as I found it, and sent up." His left hand opened on his knee and closed. "How many went into the registry as I wrote them, I don't know. I never shall. That is what I am taking home from this room. Not the paper. That."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:225:Vastin got to his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:237:Karis had begun putting the case back in its order and did not raise her head. Brom was standing with his good hand on the rail of the chair Vastin had left, as though it might still hold some of the warmth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:71:The assessment wing's counter was its usual morning self. The desk clerk had the day book open and a queue of three in front of him: two first-years with floor sheets and a porter with a parcel. The clock ticked over the counter. Behind it, at the copying table under the long window, the teaching assistant sat with his pen in his column and his head bent, as he had sat every morning for two years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:91:Seln had said it once, in Cael's room, with the case on the sill and the rain on the glass. *Read your own line. The hour is when you come up the stair.* And then, as the boy was writing on the back of his hand: *Come the way you always come. A man who changes his road is a man who has a reason.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:9:She laid the request on Bracken's counter, filled in, in her finding hand. It was addressed to the archive at Greyvane, to the keeper of the founding collection, and it asked for certified copies of the signature leaves of the oldest compilation the archive held: the founders' own hands, at the foot of the founding text, as they had set them down before anybody had written a line of commentary over them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:133:The presiding seat was occupied. A registrar sat in it, in the senior grey of the registry, the hood of the seat drawn forward over the head, so that from the house's table there was nothing to see under it but shadow. The hands lay on the bench in front of the hood. They were an ordinary pair of hands, a little lined, neither young nor old, a man's or a woman's, Cael could not have sworn which. The clerk at the side of the platform stood and read into the minute the seat's designation: the seat of the registry at Ostrand, sitting by its seal in proceedings the registry had designated institutional. He read no name. The hood offered none.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:161:*The seat opened at the second bell.* The clerk read the designation; Havel wrote the minute it ended. *The petitioner's procurator entered the petition.* Jent stood at his table with his leaf in one hand, read the petition's working paragraphs in an even voice that gave no one of them more weight than another, named his nine exhibits and sat; Havel wrote the minute he stood and the minute he sat. Nine minutes. *The respondent's counsel entered the answer.* She rose, small and grey with a steel rule in her hand, and summarized eleven pounds in a voice that did not hurry and did not linger; twenty-three minutes. Havel took the receipts for both lists of exhibits in the receipt book, nine items and a hundred and seventy-three, and wrote the numbers, and initialled them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:77:It was the same thing. She could see that plainly, and she was not going to tell herself a kinder story. It was a school, and a room with a board, and a girl going out of it for good with her certificate in her coat, and anybody watching from the court would have seen exactly what a porter at Fenmark had seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:93:The assessment wing was open, for the same reason the seminars were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:217:"I thought you would. I'd a paper about you, for the chancellor." He turned the envelope over, mildly. "A date, and a room, and would she kindly bring you. She gave me a chair and showed me a book, and it seems she's nobody of that name to bring." He did not sound put out. "I've written across it. *No such enrollee; withdrawn this day; see the registrar's certificate.* That's what I found, so that's what goes back."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:51:She was reading him. That was the second thing, and it made him cold all down his back. In the outer court he had watched her get onto a horse without the half beat, the little gathering of the body that tells you a person has decided to move. Somebody had trained it out of her. And because she had none, she could see it in everybody else. She saw his half beat. She saw where his weight was going before his weight knew it. She laid her point there and waited for him to arrive.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:49:The hood came in, and sat, and the clerk read the designation.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:201:*To the Chancellor of the chartered academy at Halcenvane, and to the enrollee of record therein named. The registry, in support of the proceedings presently convened at the seat at Ostrand, directs the attendance of the said enrollee at the registry facility at the district seat for pre-evaluation assessment, transport and escort provided, effective on presentation. The academy is directed to produce the enrollee to the bearers of this order. Given under the Compact's hand.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:37:In the lamplight Lira could not read her heel. She had said so. But Cael, watching her come at the lane on the four-count, saw her find something else. The dust in the cracks between the painted boards had woken as the lane went down, the way it always did, and was running south along the lane in a thin pale stream. In daylight from the high windows you could hardly see it. Under two lamps set low on the floor, it threw a shadow. The whole lane showed on the boards as a faint moving band of dark, like the wake of a fish under still water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:143:What Jent had already given away, Cael could read without looking back at his own notes, because the house's counsel had been keeping score of it in the margin of her privileged minute, where he could see it. Three headings stood there in her small capitals, written before the sitting began. *EXECUTION. PROVISION. RECORD.* Through each of them now ran a single neat line. She had drawn the first in Jent's first minute, when he told the hood the petition had never said the enrollment was wrongly made. The second, a little after, when he called the clause law and took the house's history of it for his own, word for word. The third when he said again, for the minute, that he had never read a better-kept record of an enrollment, and the clerk looked up to ask if that was to go in as spoken, and Jent said, "Every word. I'd decline to soften it if I were asked," and something along the front benches shifted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:11:"I have filled in what the enrollment book could tell me," said Bracken. He had one pair of glasses on and the other two on his forehead. "The house, the year of entry, the basis, the tier as certified. I did it last night, to spare you the writing, and because I did not care to have anybody misspell this house in my hall on its last morning with them. Three lines are left to each of you: the day, and your name in your own hand, in my presence, and a line for the reason, which the registry prints because it is curious and which the code allows an enrollee to leave empty." He moved the pen a finger's width toward them. "I take them in the order they are offered. I have no order of my own."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:21:"It stays true. A house does not certify a tier and cannot uncertify one, and that line is on the form only so that the registry may know whom it is reading about."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:157:"There is one line more on the council's minute," he said, and he took up the sheet again. "*The enrollee's tier, as certified by the registry: Ember; Iron, Rank Three.* I have not read it to you, and I have struck it from this schedule, in ink, with my initials against the stroke, because it is not the house's. It was never the house's to grant, and it does not lapse with a room." He looked at her over the glasses. "Nobody can take a tier from you, Advocate. They can only decline to count it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:159:"They'll decline," said Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:179:"It has not been cheap. I weighed your answer on the post-room's beam and was proud of the figure. I lit this hall by my own authority, and read you the code until morning, and told a table of tired people that it was real law and that we should read it. I should like one thing said at this counter, by me, since nobody will enter it." He cleared his throat. "I was wrong about what the reading would buy you. I have known it since a seal came to my gate that I could not place."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:11:Twenty-five was a strong Iron figure. Everybody on the floor who could read a board knew it, and so did she. It sat in the same band as half the Iron semifinalists of the cycle. And the semifinal before it had been twenty-four, against a fighter the gallery-sheets called buried, and a bout in the second round had gone five exchanges, and the line on the young man's certificate, the line the registry had written and the registry stood behind, said Copper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:73:The first exchange belonged to the file. It is very hard to beat somebody who has read your answers before you have written them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:103:The Halcenvane rail went quiet all along its length, because they knew what they were watching, and nobody else in the hall did. The file had a line in it about this, the cruellest line on the third page, and Ivenne had written it. *Pressed for time, she goes back to shapes she has already written down. She does not invent well, knows it, and declines to try.* Ivenne was standing twenty feet away, waiting on that line. If that sentence was still true, she had already lost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:45:"*Seventy-sixth day. An order was presented at the house's gate, by four riders, in the name of an office styled facilities, for the enrollee's attendance upon an assessment. No assessment of this enrollee has been notified to the evaluator of record, by any office, at any time. I have written that down.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:255:*In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward. The evaluation seat constituted for these proceedings, having first given notice in the year's eighth week that certification would be required, now renews its request to the Archmarshal's office for certification of the standard protocol, as applied to a re-evaluation of the enrollee of record at Halcenvane, should such be ordered. The seat's closing sittings are set down for the year's last month. The office's certificate is requested before the first of them.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:261:The first notice had come in the year's eighth week and he had acknowledged it by return, in one line, as he acknowledged every notice. He remembered writing the line, and he had meant, then, to sign the certificate when it was asked for. The protocol was sound. He had helped to write two of its sections as a young man, and had defended the whole of it before three convenings, and in all his service he had never refused his name to a thing he knew to be sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:275:*The Archmarshal declines to certify.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:279:He knew what would be done with it. The code let a seat proceed upon its own authority where a certificate was withheld; he had cited that section himself, once, long ago, when a guild hall's assessor would not sign. His line would stop nothing. The sittings would sit, and the ruling would be read, and somewhere in a minute a clerk would enter that a thing usually present was not there. That was the whole of what he could do, and he had weighed it on a post road for four days and found that it was still worth doing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:31:*Vastin said there would be a sitting after the houses went home. The houses went home two months ago. Nothing has come up the road. I don't know whether that's good news or slow news.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:189:"I told them they'd need a supervising faculty member and a floor-hours stamp from the assessment wing, and that the wing only stamps on Fifth-days." He turned a page. "They've gone to find out whether that's true. It'll take them a week. By then you'll have changed where you sit."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:5:The card was pinned at the board's head in Rooke's square capitals, as every card was. Under *LIRA* there were three lines, each with a date and a floor and a name. Rooke did not rub them out. He drew one stroke through each, level and unhurried, from the first letter to the last, the way a clerk strikes an entry he means to leave legible, and at the end of each line he wrote two words in the margin. Cael could not read them from the stair. He did not need to. He knew Rooke's two words for a bout not fought, because he had read them in that margin on other fighters' cards, and had never once expected to read them under hers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:5:Vastin held the petition up to the window and tilted it until the light lay along the wax. The Archmarshal's mark was all there, and the ring of small letters round it, but its left edge was shallow, almost a ghost, where the press had bitten at a slant. It was the press's fault. He had screwed it to the corner of the desk himself on his first morning in this room, nineteen years ago, in a hurry and out of true, and had never straightened it. In nineteen years it had never mattered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:19:He had rung for the clerk then, he remembered, and asked whether anything had come up with the bundle: a covering letter, a line from anybody, a question. The clerk had looked through the wallet twice. "No, sir. Only the slip." Vastin had thanked him and sent him down, and sat on with that answer, which was the whole of the matter in four words.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:43:A quarter of an hour later the clerk had come back up with the dispatch slip and laid it by the crooked press, where slips went, and then he had not gone. He had stood with his hands at his sides looking at the slip, and then at the Archmarshal's left hand open on the blotter, and then at nothing. Vastin had waited, and nothing had come, and after a while the man had nodded and gone out to his own desk. But he had left the inner door a hand's breadth wider than he usually left it, and twice before noon Vastin had heard his chair creak as he turned in it. A clerk who watches is a clerk who has noticed there is something to see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:47:Vastin signed beneath the blot and went back into his room and took down the letter-book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:73:The junior who had pinned it had come up the too-steep stair that afternoon with the girl's file under his arm, nervous and good, and laid it out on the desk because Vastin had made him lay it out himself. Her floor work this season had run on ahead of what her station had written of her, by a margin he had measured four ways and could not make go away. "She doesn't do anything wrong, sir," the junior had said. "That's what I keep coming back to. She does everything her Path says she should. She just does it as if the station had written down somebody a year younger." He had turned his notes round so that Vastin could read them. "I measured it with the frame, and by the clock, and by her bouts, and against the tables. It's inside tolerance every way I measure it. I still don't like it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:77:Under the station's line, in the junior's hand, was the recommendation they had settled between them: *Continue observation.* The hand was still deciding what shape it meant to be, and seeing it, Vastin had remembered a smoking stove in a district house a long time ago, and his own file under his own arm, and a senior evaluator, long since a name on a plaque, telling him the thing every evaluator is told once: *Write what the instrument says. What you feel about it is information about you.* He had handed the same sentence on that afternoon, in other words. It had been easier to say than to hear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:93:Vastin stood a while and watched him work. Then he went back up the stair, and did not think about the frame again for most of the day. He found it in his mind that evening as he put the copy back in *held* one more time: a true instrument, and men who would rather it said something easier.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:128:Vastin had looked up from the plain sheets he was writing on. "Later than usual," he had said. "But yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:130:The clerk had gone down without another word, and come back a few minutes later, unasked, with a candle already lit in a tin dish, and set it on the corner of the desk by the crooked press. The house put out the lamps on that floor at the half-bell after six. In the morning Vastin found the candle in the consumables ledger, entered in the clerk's tidy hand against the Archmarshal's name and not the floor's. He had not told him to put it there. He left it as it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:138:"Offered to the proceedings at Ostrand under the code's two provisions; declined without citation as outside their schedule. The schedule itself is not disputed. One thing only is set down. A schedule that leaves no room for the one personal assessment of the subject the Compact holds was drawn for a purpose the evaluator was never told and cannot name from his desk. Where the outcome of an evaluation is settled before the evaluator arrives, it is not the subject who is being measured."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:142:The rest he could have said without the pages. That the proceedings had in their hands already the one honest instrument there was, three years of the record, kept by officers who could see. That a re-evaluation whose answer was known in advance was a procedure wearing an evaluation's coat. That when the standard protocol fell silent in front of this boy the silence was the protocol's own. That the protocol could not read him. Somewhere in the middle was a sentence he had spoken at inquiries for forty years about frames, *the instrument is not at fault; it has told you where its reach ends*, and last night, for the first time, he had written it about a person, and sat with his pen lifted for a long while before he let it stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:144:He had gone home at the ninth bell, later than usual, as he had said he would. The porter had been at the lodge door with his lamp, looking out at the rain, and had said "Late, sir," in the voice of a man noting a thing for the record, and Vastin had agreed that it was. The street had been empty and loud with water. He had walked it without hurrying, with his collar up and his left hand in his coat, and he had found that he was not thinking about the seat, or the five chairs, or who had sat down in them. He was thinking about a boy standing still in a frame that had stuttered and gone dark, three days on the bluff, not moving, not asking what it meant, waiting to be told what the instruments had found. And he was thinking that, of all the officers who had stood in rooms with that boy, he was the one who had written down what that silence meant, and that nobody was going to be allowed to read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:85:The porter had explained, from his stool, before Cael had asked, that he had done his shoulder in lifting a crate on the Fourth-day. The assistant to the assessment wing had come down on the Fifth-day and offered, and had done the sort ever since, and did it very well, though not, the porter said, the way he would have done it himself. Seln did not answer this, or seem to hear it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:91:The first pile was the house's, for the halls and the residence, and it went into his hand as it grew. The second was the assessment wing's: anything addressed to the wing, its counter, its instructors, its office. The third was the registrar's, for Bracken's counter and the records hall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:167:The code allowed the assessment wing to read the gate lodge's book of arrivals once a week, on the Seventh-day, so that the wing's records of who had come to observe the provision's hours could be checked against the gate's record of who had come through the gate. Cael had known that for two years as a line in a rule-book. He had never once thought about who did the reading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:75:Cael, at the passage window, watched him cross it. Not toward the staff stair. Toward the dark bulk of the assessment wing on the far side of the court, where nobody had business at that hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:189:It carried him down past the place where Brom's forearm was going to be and was not yet. Lira's burst came off the wrong foot at the wrong angle and met nothing; she had read him going one way and the lane had taken him a third faster than any feet could. He came out of the end of the lane past Brom's shoulder, behind both of them, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-28.md:107:He had spent the year among rules that somebody had written: a registrar's certificate, a dead board's clause, a schedule nobody had read in forty years. Nobody had written this one, and nobody enforced it. Somebody had done it once, and somebody else the cycle after, and now a man with wet legs and his hat in his hand stood on a verge a long way from any office and kept it, because it was the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:115:"So they didn't go at it." The counsel turned the instrument to its first leaf. "And they told no story at the front. Four sentences of recital, on a paper this heavy. I'd expect forty. The front is where a drafter usually gives something away without noticing, and in twenty-six years I've won most of what I've won there." She laid the rule down. "This one knew somebody would read him from the back. He left me nothing to find."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:147:"*The clause says what it says,*" said Karis at last. "I said that. On the bench under the thorn at the first bend, the morning the entry went down the hill. *It says what it says all the same.* I said it to you, Cael." She laid her palms on the wood, one either side of the paper. "And somebody said it to them, in writing, a year ago. The Archmarshal. Vastin. You told us at Norhold there'd been three sentences of advisory, and none of us ever saw them. There they are."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:163:*Vastin, in the room full of clocks: the people who want this to stop are not going to use official channels much longer. I have never held a more official thing than this paper. Nineteen leaves, a seal, an escort at a walk, a form of joint service I had never seen. So either he was wrong about the channels, or this is the last thing they'll send through one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:19:It was the plainest reason of the four. Since the year's eighth day the registry had had a petition before its seat in the town below, asking that he be stood in a frame and read again. If the frame could not read him, the code had a chapter for that: his enrollment struck out, and after it, a letter at a time, everything else with his name on it. And a respondent who left the hill while the thing was pending would be entered as fled, with a note on three friends' files to say whose friends they were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:159:"The district's confirmation. *Outcome anticipated: advancement, per title.* I used to take it out at night and read it." He did not take it out now. "It's a clerk's round hand. It's a good sheet. It says three assessors and a sigil frame will find me an Iron, and they will. And I look at it now and all I can see is a form." He rubbed his thumb along his knee. "It's not that I think it's false. I think it's true. I just can't see it anymore without seeing the drawer it'll go in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:197:"I said I don't stand on it anymore." He held her eyes. "I didn't say it's not worth writing. They'll read your closing, and the minute will have it, and when they rule they'll rule against it in writing. That's not nothing. It's just not ground."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:311:The clerk had met such slips before, though not often. There was one of them on this file already. It was older than any other paper in it, older than the first leaf, and it had given the subject a designation that two careful officers had since tried to trace and could not. That one sat at the head of the file, and this one had come to its foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:367:He had been read at fourteen, in a room, in eleven seconds, and since that morning there had always been somebody deciding what he was. A warden had done it, and a provost, and a tournament office; a seat had done it under a hood, and a designation had done it that no office would own. Some of them had been kind, and most had been correct, and all of them had been writing in a column that he could not see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:11:"*Ostrand district registry. Review for advancement of tier: Brom, enrolled at Halcenvane. Schedule of evidence. The panel will read, first, the practitioner's record as entered, the continental title included. Second, the house's attendance and floor sheets for the two years preceding, to be supplied by the registrar under the house's seal. Third, a reading in the sigil frame, under load, taken on the day in the presence of the three assessors. No testimony is contemplated. Sitting: the last Fifth-day of the academic year, at the second bell.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:47:It was Brom's own gift he read him with, taken years ago and used every day since, and it came back to him as it always did, as weight and where the weight went. The bar's load ran down through the crossed forearms and into the shoulders, the right taking a little more than the left, and from there down the long muscles of the back into the hips and through both heels into the floor. It was like watching water find its way down a roof. Nothing in the whole of Brom was pushing back against the bar; he was only giving the weight somewhere to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:49:And the left shoulder was taking a little less than its share, though not by much. Brom knew; Cael could read him knowing, in the small care with which the left elbow was set.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:41:"This can't be right. *Designation predates file creation.* A mark can't be older than the file it's written in. That's like a stamp being older than its letter." She looked up at him, quite earnestly, with the ink on her chin. "I think somebody's dropped a word. Shall I query it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:51:She copied it as it stood. She was a good copyist, and she made the capital on *Designation* exactly the height of the one in the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:147:"I held it." He thought about how to say the rest. "It was more than Ephram ever put on, a good deal over. They let it down on springs, and then they let it down again while I had it, which he never did, and I heard the middle one say *forty* and go on counting." He looked at his forearms. "The plate lit. I felt it under my boots. It's warm. I didn't know they were warm."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:153:"It said Iron," said Lira. She had got her breath. "I was on the bench by the door and I watched the middle assessor's face when the plate came up. He looked like a man who'd put his hand in his pocket for a coin and found the coin he expected." She shoved her hands deeper into her own pockets. "He was right about himself, in front of people whose job it was to check. I saw it happen. I wanted somebody to have seen it who knew what it was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:279:He had said, in a harness room, that he could not read a registry sheet anymore; that since the case he looked at one and saw only what it was a form of. He was not reading these. He was doing the one honest thing he could still do with such paper, which was to say what it weighed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:229:It was the house's exhibits, all of them, read into the minute by the seat's clerk in the order the counsel had asked, each with its number, title, date, where it came from and its line of receipt. The clerk had one pace and kept it for everything. A short item, eight or nine seconds; one with a certifying officer's line to recite, twenty or a little more. Bracken's index to the enrollment file, with Bracken's name in its certifying line: fourteen strokes' worth. The Greyvane ruling, the hearing that had kept Cael on his feet in a city he had never wanted to see: twenty-one. Karis's renewals table, rebuilt in a single silent hour with two dates on every row: eleven. The integrity section, forty pages, a night without sleep, ninety-one invocations each traced by hand to its own page: eight seconds. Its title had taken longer than its reading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:210:The seat's clerk read the designation. Then he read the contents leaf of the records the house had produced, bundle by bundle, in the house's boards, from the first card of the year to the last.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:127:"Now I come back to the handle. These sections are for the testing of instruments, so that the registry may know which of them to trust, and there are two instruments before the seat. One is a frame, which was asked about this practitioner once and had no answer. The other is twenty-two officers of the registry. The house's counsel read their names to the seat at the preliminary, each with a grade and a house and a day: a provost's panel, a regional registrar's assessors, the tournament's, an evaluator of record who has sat across a trestle from him every week for two years. They watched, and wrote down what they saw, and signed it. Their notes agree with one another, and the petitioner has accepted every one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:141:He knew what lay in that pause as well as he knew his own Log. It was the true argument. She had just told a seat, with perfect accuracy, that its instrument had failed to read him, and she knew why it had failed, and what was appended to the reading, and how long it had been so, and whose hands had signed for it. She could have dated the thing to within a renewal of Greyvane's charter. It would have taken her nine minutes, and she had the nine minutes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:201:"*Evaluation-protocol certification: declined by the evaluator of record; proceeding on the presiding seat's authority.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:205:It was a small thing to see, and he did not think the benches saw it. The four district evaluators had been writing in a row, and at that line they stopped, one after another, like men on a road who see the one ahead halt. They were working officers. Every one of them had stood practitioners in frames for years, under a protocol that carried the Archmarshal's certificate as a matter of course, as a loaf carries a baker's mark; the certifying office had put its name to that protocol since before any of them was born. It had been asked twice for its name and had not given it, and the form had no line for why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:209:On the fourth bench Gault had not moved. He was the evaluator of record, the only man in that room who was called so every day, and he had declined nothing in his life. The refusal was the Archmarshal's; the form's old column had only Gault's title to put it under. Gault sat with his case straight on his knees and his eyes on the pasted form, and then he took out his small book and wrote a line in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:13:*Fifth-day. Tenth sitting. Eleven minutes. The evaluation seat renewed its request to the Archmarshal's office for a certificate on the protocol, and the hood minuted the renewal. That was the whole of the morning. Nobody at our table looked at anybody else.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:23:"Ninety-one." She sat back and the shawl slid off her shoulders, and she did not notice it go. "Every time anybody has ever called on the integrity provisions since the registry began writing it down. I've read every one. I found them in the registry digests. Bracken's press has the oldest digests and the hall has the rest, and Seln's sheaf told me which year each section first appears in, which saved me a week." She laid her hand on the stack. "Eighty-four of them are about things. Frames. Stations. An assessor who took money in a district I won't name. Two panels whose minutes argued with themselves. A records office that lost a whole quarter's declarations in a flood and then tried to pretend it hadn't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:5:The desk clerk opened the assessment wing on the First-day, as he had opened it every working morning for nine years. He hung up his coat. He took the cover off his ledger and sharpened a pencil, and he had the day book open and the date written before he looked round to say good morning to the copying table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:79:They checked the lines against station books, and house ledgers, and bout sheets, and each one stood. A subject had attended. A figure was as the house had entered it. An instrument had been declined at a gate, and the subject had not been seen at the refusal. Associations were unchanged. *Nothing further to report.* It was as clean a body of product as any reader there had handled, and one of them said so, and it was put in a minute.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:87:It knew what he had sent. It did not know what he had kept. There was no return that listed the things a quiet man had declined to write down. Nor was there any record of a records room on a top floor, in a district he had left twelve years before, where he had gone to look for a subject's travel papers. Nobody thought to ask for one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:59:"Advisory, the same word, on a designation nobody at any height has ever been able to trace to an office." Karis drew a breath. "Cael told me on the third night, when he brought the bread. I asked him about Denvash, and he told me: eleven seconds. The plate lit eleven seconds late, and the word came at the end of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:125:"That was the old way," she said. "Before the policy. An assessor who met a reading that went across many families could write that, pending further study, and the person went on with his life under that word, and nobody corrected anything. It was a door the old system left open. And the first three memoranda shut it, in order. Each of them cites an article of the directive by its number, the same directive I sat over at Halcenvane." She counted on her fingers, slowly, one, two, three. "The first: *close the door.* No assessor may designate by judgment of what a person shows; a station enters what the reading gives, and only that. The second: *remove the exit.* The provisional custom, pending further study, is struck; there is to be no designation that waits on study. The third: *delete the name.* The old word is discontinued, and every person who carried it is to be re-entered under a flag."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:225:*The seat notes the respondent's letter of the eleventh sitting, by which he declined further attendance, and enters the letter upon the record. The respondent not having appeared within the time the code allows, the petition is granted by default.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:33:Seln touched the third bean. "The district seat. Twelve days ago. A query on the certification history of a Wind practitioner of this house, Iron Rank One, *for completeness of the respondent institution's file*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:229:*I'm well. The house is good to me. I read your third paragraph at a window, to the people I'd most want to hear it, and they heard it. I'll mind it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:195:"I know. I heard them." Cael turned round again. The tail of the column was going away up the processional way, the blue and silver getting smaller against the lit tiers of the Concourse. "Everything in that line was put where it was for somebody standing where I'm standing. If I'd read her just now, I'd have read her the way they laid her out for me." He watched the last riders turn the corner toward the delegation quarter. "I'll take my first look at the procession, from wherever the marshals put me, on the tournament's own floor. Until then, all of it is Auremont's publishing. I decline to review the publishing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:215:The Archmarshal Vastin read the Norhold files on a Fourth-day evening, in his room in the Compact's house, a week before the opening.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:235:Vastin sat back in his chair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:245:Then he did a thing he had done perhaps a dozen times in forty years. He noticed a thought beginning, a long one with a shape to it, and he declined to let it go on. It was not that the thought was improper. It was that it had nothing yet to stand on but an empty chair, and he had spent his working life refusing to let a thought carry more weight than the evidence under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:47:The record. Paper in a subject's file. A query, a designation, a mark in a column. It cost nothing and was never undone, and it was where everything began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:163:"Five lines on the First-day," said Seln. "Five floors, five hours, a quarter apart, each in one of your names. A practice booking. It's the most ordinary thing on the board. Read your own line. The floor means nothing. The hour is when you come up the stair."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:177:*Subject attended to his course of study. Subject attended the seat's fourth sitting as directed. An instrument presented at the house's gate on the Sixth-day was declined by the house; subject was not seen at the refusal. Subject's floor work within the band of the year. Nothing further to report.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:111:It went on like that, and Cael had not expected it to be hard to listen to, and it was, because he knew every one of those afternoons. He had chosen each figure the night before it was earned, on a page at the back of Hesk's book, and spent the bout earning it to the decimal, and walked off the boards afterward exactly as tired as the rotation had said he would be. Hearing them read out in a row, the whole year so far, in the girl's careful voice, was like hearing somebody read your letters back to you. Every one of them was true. None of them was him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:75:"You saw." She put the cup down on the step. "Rooke says the sitting doesn't read the board. It reads the rating. I'm Iron Rank One on the porter's wall, and I'll stay Rank One on it, and it doesn't matter. When the advancement evaluation comes, they'll put me on a floor in front of a panel and mark what I do, and if the marks sit where Silver sits, that's where I go. Not up the column. Across it." She pressed the tape down at her wrist. "The rank's a certificate. The rating's a figure. Rooke says nobody in the Silver bracket ever asked to see anybody's certificate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:121:She drank half her tea standing. Then she crossed to the shelf by the door, where the house's certified copy of the tournament charter stood in its grey card covers among the regional schedules, and laid one finger along its spine, and kept it there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:131:By the third morning the assessment wing's queue reached the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:187:*I don't know what they're waiting for. Seln says senior men. Bracken watches the road. Vastin said a sitting.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-42.md:155:The Archmarshal Vastin had come to Norhold, and he was sitting in the chair that had been held for two weeks for somebody nobody would name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-35.md:167:High in the upper gallery, Withrow had not stood up with the rest. Cael looked for her and found her sitting perfectly still with her hands in her lap, looking down at the floor with an expression he could not read at that distance and did not try to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:13:"The house's copy," said Seln. "Thank you." He unfolded it on the counter and read it, and Cael watched him read it. It was a station certificate, the ordinary kind, with a registry seal at its foot and three lines of a clerk's writing above. Seln read the three lines once at his counter pace, neither slow nor quick. Then he blotted the foot where the girl's mother had signed for it, though the ink was days dry, and folded it again along its old folds, and wrote a line in the wing's ledger, and put the certificate in the drawer under the counter where enrollees' station papers went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:21:There would be a certificate in it for every enrollee on the hill, four hundred and thirty of them. There would be one for Lira, from wherever she had been read, and one for Brom from the Velmere district, and one for Karis. There was no certificate in it for him, because the plate at Denvash had lit with a word and no station had ever written him down since. Every other paper in that drawer was three lines in a clerk's hand, and some of the three lines were what a station had read, and some of them were what an order had told it to write. There was no way on earth, standing at a counter in a queue, to tell which.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:59:"I'm not asking to be difficult," she said. "I'm asking because I sat on that bench last night and listened to you all read forty of them out, and I've been doing my drills in my head all day, and I can't make them go." She looked at the coats. "That seat down there runs on it, and so does the evaluation. Every sitting we go to, we're sitting in the room and answering it, and it's the thing in the case. They want to read you again. We know now what reads you." She pulled her coat closer. "My carters said the stamps stop four days east, loaded, or three days light. I've been carrying that in my pocket since the dairy. We could be past the last station by the Seventh-day after next if we left on the Seventh-day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:201:"One." Karis looked at him. "It opens on one day, the day of the ruling, and stays open until the revocation takes effect, and then it shuts. And it only opens at all if we lose. If the seat rules for the house, there's no door, because there's nothing to resign from." She sat back. "Until that day there's only the corridor: every sitting attended, every form filed on time, every bout in wing three at the band, and every first-year at that counter handing in a certificate, and you standing behind her in the queue with your face doing nothing." She was quiet a moment. "Perfectly, until the ruling. If we put one foot wrong before it, the door we'd use afterward turns into the word on four files."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:7:Vastin held the slip to the window and looked at the two letters for some time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:9:His room was high in the Compact's house, up a stair that the builders had made too steep and the clerks had worn too smooth. Its one window gave on a court where a man in a porter's apron fed pigeons every morning at the same hour from the same paper bag. Vastin had come to regard the pigeons and the porter as the most reliable instrument in the building. The room held a table, two chairs, and four shelves of files that were his only in the sense that he was the officer who would be asked about them. It was a little after the second bell. His left hand, which had begun some years ago to dislike mornings, lay on the table beside the bundle and declined to be hurried.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:29:Vastin sat back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:57:A registrar who had been answered against his memorandum would write within the week, wanting language to stand behind: a sentence he could quote back to the house so that the house would know it was the Compact and not the regional office that had given way. Or an office above would write, wanting to know why the Compact's evaluator had once again declined to hand it a difficulty. One or the other. Usually both. He had the answers to both in his drawer, beneath the struck page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:193:"I've been in this trade a long time," he said. "I've seen floors bought. I've seen brackets drawn by men who owed money to the houses they were drawing. I've seen a certificate say Copper about a girl who could take any Iron in her region apart in four exchanges." Lira did not move. "This thing I've never seen lean. Not once. Rich houses have tried, for as long as anybody's kept books. Chief Adjudicator Umber keeps the calibration, and has done since before most of you were Kindled, and I've never heard a word against him that wasn't sour grapes from a house that lost." He tapped the table with one knuckle. "There are perhaps three honest instruments on this continent, and you're looking at one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:223:Then, because the habit was older than the doubt, he sat on the bench and priced the others. Lira, from what he had watched on the oak all recess: execution near nine, the cleanest bursts on the hill; control eight, nine against anyone slower than her; effect nine, because she made people spend. Twenty-six, perhaps. A Silver-touched figure for a girl whose certificate had said Copper not long ago. Brom: execution seven, because he would never look quick; control nine, because nobody moved Brom anywhere he did not agree to go; effect eight. Twenty-four. Ephram he had been rating in his head for a fortnight, and came out at twenty-four too. He did not price Karis. He did not know yet what an Ember fighter's effect looked like to a panel trained to Silver Five, and he found, to his surprise, that he wanted to be surprised by it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:15:The designation in the enrollee's file had been her question for a long while. She had first sent it up from Greyvane, plainly, asking who had entered the mark and on whose authority. Twice it had come back down to her with the same two sentences, *no originating authority of record*, and the second time a third sentence latched on behind them, saying the same nothing more firmly. So last year at Halcenvane, on the fifteenth of Reaping, she had sat at the long table in the emptied hall and filled in a second referral, fuller, every date and reference in its order, and marked it a level higher than the office that had answered. In all her years she had never before filed twice on one matter. A question nobody answered was a question to be asked again, through the next door up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:65:*The Archmarshal Vastin asks the enrollee for one hour, before the houses leave the city. Nothing will be written by either party. It would be better if nothing were brought to write with. The timekeepers' office in the east tower has lent a room for the purpose; it belongs to neither the adjudication office nor the Compact. He is free to say no; it will count against him nowhere, with no one, at any time; and that sentence is not a formality.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:139:Vastin was sitting at the long table with his back to the window. He got up when Cael came in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:143:His eyes went over Cael once. They did it as they had on the bluff, without any hurry and without seeming to do it at all. They rested a moment where the strapping showed under the shirt, and a moment on the wrapped forearm, and on the care with which Cael lowered himself into the chair across the table. Vastin said nothing about any of it. Cael saw him take each thing and put it away, and understood that this was a kind of good manners. In his trade you did not comment on what you found. You kept it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:145:"Thank you for coming," said Vastin, and sat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:153:Vastin looked round the room before he did, at the shelves of clocks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:163:"I'm going to give you eight words," said Vastin. "Four, and then four. I have watched them come across my table this year in letters about your file, the first four in the early part of it, the second four since. I want you to hear them in the order they came." He said the first four slowly, each one by itself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:173:"The first four are words you use about a fault," said Vastin. "A thing gone wrong in the record, which a careful office would like to find and mend. You find a fault so that you can take it out." He moved his left hand an inch on the wood. "The second four are words you use about a thing you mean to keep in a box. I went through the Compact's charter for them, article by article, one evening. They aren't in it. You can't write *handling* about a question. You can only write it about an answer you've already got."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:183:Then Vastin lifted his eyes to Cael's and kept them there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:207:"The designation in my file. The old one, with no office against it. Is that part of what's coming?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:209:Vastin did not answer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:213:"I won't answer that one," said Vastin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:219:"No." Vastin did not soften it. "That is the other half of why I asked for you. What you do now makes no difference to it. A year ago it would have. A thing you do is an answer, and answers are for questions, and they have stopped asking. Nothing answers a conclusion."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:227:Vastin did not ask what he meant. He looked, for a moment, very slightly pleased.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:67:He could not help it. Under every seal there was a line of them, three or four to an issue, and he went down them the way he went down the names on a bout card, by habit, and without meaning to he had counted. Nineteen issues and seventy-one names, give or take two he could not read in the yellow ones where the ink had gone brown and spread. Officers of the registry, by the titles after them: a chief clerk of entry, a keeper of the rolls, a second secretary, men and women whose offices Bracken would have known and Cael had never heard of. Not one of them appeared on two issues: ten years at a desk, and then a new name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:117:He thought of Denvash, which he did not often let himself do. A district office on a cold morning, a chalk circle on boards worn into two shallow places where a generation of feet had stood, and a warden-adjunct with tired grey eyes three feet away at a desk with a brass plate let into it. Something had woken behind his breastbone, small, a point of light at the edge of his sight that he could feel and never point to. He had counted eleven seconds while the plate did not light, and then the word had come, very far away and very close, and the plate lighting late with the same word in it, and the light at the edge of his sight going out like a pinched wick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:119:All his life since, he had said that his Arbiter had spoken once and gone dark. He had said it to panels and wardens and Magisters, and written it in two books. It had been the one fixed fact at the bottom of every argument he had ever won. It had read him once, and said one word, and the registry had written down that word because it had nothing else to write, and he lived in the gap the word left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:165:"A corridor," he said, "in a district station, a year on, with green distemper on the walls to the height of a man's shoulder, and benches. I was waiting for a file. A keeper came out of the frame room with a mother and a girl, and he was pleased, the way they are when a reading is a good one, and I heard him tell the mother that her girl read strong, and read into a second family as well, and he had not seen it in years." He looked at the father's letter, folded under its clip in the third row. "A week later I was back for another file, and the station's out-tray was on the counter, and her certificate was in it, waiting to go. One family, first rank." He took his hand off the table. "That was when the paper turned into children. It had been only paper for a year."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:67:Gault thought about it with the care he gave every question, as though it might later have to be certified.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:203:"I see weight," he said at last. "Where it's going and how hard it's going there. A corner that's being built is all weight, because you have to put your body where the walls are going to be. That wasn't a wall. You stood in my roads and made them dear, and there's nothing in anybody's hips that says what a road costs." He rubbed the shoulder where her hand had been. "I can read you building. I can't read you pricing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:185:"The rule says roster," he said. "A house's roster is whatever its enrollment book says it is. That book was certified at the start of this year by the house's own registrar. A regional office questioned it, was answered, and printed it." He laid the slip on the open page. "There is nothing to rule on. There's only a list."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:5:The district officer's envelope came back to the seat at Ostrand on the Seventh-day evening, by his own hand, opened and endorsed. *No such enrollee; withdrawn this day; see the registrar's certificate.* The certificate followed in the house's bag on the First-day morning, with four leaves of the registry's own form under the house's seal, and one of the four had three lines of longhand in its margin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:17:So on the Fourth-day, which was the last of the academic year, a stout fair man unlocked a frame-house at the hour appointed, and took the cover off the frame and folded it. The president of the evaluation seat came, since the ruling had charged her to see to the conduct of the thing, and she was a woman who saw to what she was charged with. She brought her four evaluators. They stood on the flags in their plain coats, in a windowless room, with the plate dark at their feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:61:It was the third thing from the top, and he took it in its turn. It was a single leaf of registry stock under the seal of the seat at Ostrand, and he knew the form. When a proceeding closed, its seat sent a summary to every office that had been named in it, and each office returned the leaf with whatever it wished to have stand beside the record. His office had been named twice: once as the author of an advisory, three sentences long, which the petition had quoted with approval, and again as the certifying office, which had been asked for its name and had declined to give it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:69:*In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward. Enrollment: closed by resignation; the house's certificate annexed. Proceedings: moot. Re-evaluation: not executed. Asset-restriction: pending jurisdiction.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:75:*Closed by resignation.* He turned that one over longest. He had seen four young people and an older man in a room with nothing of anybody's in it, and had read what they brought him, and had told them to their faces what he would not do. He had not asked what they would do. It had not been his to ask, and he had thought it better not to carry the answer. He saw it now, in a clerk's round hand: they had not appealed, and they had not published; they had waited for the one door in the code that left a house clean, and they had walked out of it in good order, with a registrar's certificate behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:243:Her eyes went over him slowly. His feet first, and where his weight sat on them; then his open hands; then his shoulders; and last his face, where they stayed. He knew exactly what she was doing, because it was what he did himself, at every rail and every gate, to everybody, all day long. She was reading him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:269:Once, near the end, Zerin came along the north rail at full speed on a flank run, and her line crossed Cael's in the middle. He saw her see him. She read him for two full beats, at a speed that should not have allowed anybody to read anything, and he saw her decide. She did not try him. She went round, wide, and spent her speed on Ephram's platform instead.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:35:"It is five days. An officer will ride up to my gate with the notice tomorrow, or the day after, and it will be addressed as the last one was: to the chancellor, to produce the enrollee. You will still be mine on that day, for the revocation does not bite until its close." She looked down at the four lines. "I read an order aloud at that gate once and declined it, because nobody could tell me whose it was. This one would be a seat's, and I could not decline it. I should have to walk you into that frame-house myself, under the house's seal, or else refuse a seat in writing and let them enter the refusal in my folder."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:101:*Bracken: three hundred season sheets certified across the recess, every one in his own hand. "That's the house's year," he said over the last of them. "Strangers will read it now."*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:187:Five competitors, then the staff, Rooke and Gault; two reserves from the third year, the Shield reserve and the Stone; a wagoner. And, on the last line, *S. Seln, assessment office: records and floor scheduling.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:225:"For Ephram, that was singing." She turned back to the board, and her eyes went to her own line, *Lira, Wind, Iron Rank One*, and stayed there a moment with her hand on her coat over the place where the certificate had lived since the anteroom. Then she looked away from it on purpose, before anybody could catch her admiring it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:229:Karis was reading the selection standards again from the top, and her pencil had stopped two-thirds of the way down. The line under it said that the registrar would file the house's entry, together with a certified copy of its enrollment record, complete and current, not later than the close of intake.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:239:Lira's name went up at the foot of the Iron column, under a column of strangers and over nothing, with the short new word beside it in the porter's round hand. Brom's went up in Rooke's cohort's column at the end of the Blade board, third, where the certified standings had put it at the green table on the last morning of term. Fiske's went up near the head of the Copper with the same six letters it had always had. Cael watched Lira's name until the porter had moved his ladder twice more, and then went on watching the porter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:245:On the way back he went past the assessment wing. The sheet that had hung beside Gault's door all recess, the one with the same name against every working day, was gone. In its place hung the term's counter rota, five clerks' initials spread across the week in the usual way, the teaching assistant's among them on three days out of seven. The recess sheet would be in a file somewhere now, in the wing's own order, where it would never be read again by anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:69:At the trestle the five judges were writing, each alone, with their slates held close. He wondered what there was to write. *Execution*: clean feet, no waste. *Control*: a boy who never once let himself be put where he was being sent. *Effect*: nothing. He had done nothing whatever to the man but decline him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:223:He declined all six. He gave the panel what it needed to rate: clean feet, a read that looked like decent timing, two touches on the right lines.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-31.md:11:He accepted the tea and declined the shawl, and thanked them both. Then he told them that the mark had managed three hundred years without a shawl and would very likely manage one more night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:3:The Archmarshal Vastin had reached the city the evening before, by the post road, with one bag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:25:He had read the seating plan in his room, a week before the opening. He had written one line in his day book about it and declined to think the long thought that came after the line. He declined it again now. A held chair was a courtesy. A steward who knew a man's grade by his face had been told it by somebody, and that was all that could be said about it with the evidence in hand, and he had spent forty years refusing to say more than the evidence would hold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:39:There was no limp, quite. There was a boy walking as a man walks across a room he knows has a loose board in it, keeping his weight on one side and not letting anybody see him keep it. Vastin had seen a great many people walk like that into a great many rooms, and very few of them had been sixteen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:41:The whole bowl watched the showman from the lake house, and there was nothing to learn from looking where eight thousand people were already looking. Vastin watched the boy, as he had once sat four feet from him in a room twelve feet by fourteen and watched him while he answered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:43:What he saw in the second exchange he had never seen done on a floor. The boy declined to be told a story. Each of the showman's passages leaned toward its ending, and the bowl leaned with it, and at the moment before the ending the boy was quietly somewhere else, at no speed at all, until the passage had nothing left to land on. It was all in the open, inside the rule, in front of everyone, and as far as Vastin could tell not one person in the building was looking at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:51:In the third exchange the boy took a touch standing in the light, and in the instant before it Vastin saw something cross his face that he could not read. It was not surprise and it was not fear. It was very quick and then gone, and he did not write it down, because a thing he did not know could not go into a book as though he did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:83:Supper was half eaten before anybody spoke of Vastin, and it was Cael who did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:87:The table took that in. Nobody asked how he knew it was Vastin; he had described that stillness to them often enough over the year, and Karis had already had the rest of it from Lira, who had watched the twelfth chair through the whole bout from the north rail instead of watching the floor, and had told everybody afterward, with some indignation, that she had missed the best touch of the afternoon on Cael's account.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-34.md:49:*Subject's method is her signature. Nothing reaches the floor with her that has not been on paper first, and whoever has read her paper has read the bout before it begins. Pressed for time, she goes back to shapes she has already written down. She does not invent well, knows it, and declines to try.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-34.md:243:*Program entrant at twelve. Kindled at fourteen, within the program. Advanced on schedule, every season.* After that the page went on in the shop's flat, careful manner: the cohorts she had been put in, the coaches and the halls, each step up taken in the very year it was due. Iron Rank Nine at nineteen. Silver assessment pending. And at the very foot, a line that was as close as the shop ever came to praise. *No season wasted.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:95:Then the delegation head stood up. He did it slowly, as if his knees had begun to object to such things. He said that his house wished to enter its objection on the record, and he entered it, in full, in words that were careful and grave and plainly felt: what a program owed the people in it, the lack of any precedent, the house's request that the office decline the filing. Umber heard him out without once looking away from him. When the man had finished he did not sit down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:111:For a year the Archmarshal Vastin had kept the Compact's record on the enrollee of Halcenvane, and kept it honest, and a week before he had sat in a held chair at Norhold to watch the boy fight a duelist, and written twice, and gone home before the figure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:127:Vastin had been in the Compact's service long enough to know that silence came in sorts, and that an officer who could not tell one sort from another would spend his whole career being startled. There was the silence of a matter forgotten, which gathered dust and did no harm. There was the silence of a matter settled, which sat comfortably on a shelf. There was the silence of a matter waiting for somebody who was away, which had a fidget in it that could be felt through a shut door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:129:And there was a fourth sort, which he had met perhaps three times in all his years, and had not liked on any of them. It was the silence that falls when you have been taken off a list. Nobody above him had stopped caring about the boy. They had only stopped caring what Vastin thought. His evaluations were going up the stair as truthfully as ever, and nobody at the top of it was reading them, because whatever was going to be done was going to be done on other papers than his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:141:The sixth line asked for purpose. He had filled that line more times than he could count, always with the exact word for what he meant to do. *Evaluation. Station review. Certification dispute.* For this journey there was no exact word. The form's rules allowed an officer of his grade, travelling on his own authority, two that were not quite a reason. He wrote them. *Official interest.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:147:His clerk came back, and saw the empty week, and wrote the journey into it without a word. Vastin let him. When he had gone, Vastin sat on at the desk with the lamp not yet lit and the pigeons settling on the ledges of the court below.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:5:Karis had spread the charter's three copies down its length in a row, open at three different places, with the certified transcription at the head of the row like a magistrate at the head of a bench. Beside them lay a stack of clean foolscap, four pencils sharpened to the same length, the memorandum with its short-inked type, and the grey notebook, open at the page where the clause had been copied out on the afternoon before the recess with one word beside it. Bracken had brought the copies down himself after the last bell, had set them out in the order she asked for, had told the night porter that the hall was in use and why, and had gone home. He would come back, he said, when there was something to check. He did not like to watch people draft. It made him want to correct them before they had finished being wrong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:97:It said that the enrollment record was true, and had been certified so, without omission, as the charter required. It said that the house stood ready to comply with the charter's mandatory language in every particular. And it said that if the regional registrar should determine that a true enrollment record must be altered before it could be accepted, the house respectfully requested that instruction in writing, over the registrar's name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:153:"Go and train," she said. "If their charter holds, you've a season starting in a little over three weeks. If it doesn't, you've a quarrel starting rather sooner, and I'd like you fit for that too." She took her spectacles off, which in Withrow was the same as a door opening. "Thank Karis for me. Don't let her refuse it. Tell her it's from the chancellor and she has no standing to decline."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:233:"Who found the schedule? Who sat up three nights in a stone hall with the porter bringing her tea? Who certified the record, and sanded it, and put it in a wallet, and watched the courier take it?" She took one hand out of her coat and held it up, empty. "Who decided to put Halcenvane in a tournament in the first place, a year before you'd ever heard the word *clause*?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:207:"One thing left," said Karis, with her pen still on the page. "If there's a file on Cael in every meet town, then everybody at Norhold has read him before they meet him. All of them. And Auremont's had scouts at this meet. They won't be buying their file from a shop."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:121:Further down the table somebody asked that the file's standing designation be read in. Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4. By itself, the speaker said, it gave all the warrant the room needed to take the matter out of ordinary hands. The minute then asked, for completeness, which office had authorized that designation.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:125:A young man from the secretariat answered, after some looking at papers. The office that had tried to follow the designation home had followed it into the registry, he said, to a sub-layer under the registry's own floor, and there lost it; there was no instrument of record behind it, and the attempt had been closed. The room made the small pause that rooms of that height make, and a line went into the minute, and the sitting went on. The designation was useful, and at that height useful things are not asked where they came from. It had sat in the file since the first minutes of his Kindling, and it sat there still: a flag no living hand had authorized.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:173:*Two of them, at a bridge. The old arrangement, two and a horse. After Norhold it looks small, and I find I'm fond of it. But Vastin spent an hour telling me the old way is finishing. So: noted, and kept beside his hour. They may be the last watchers I get whose names I could learn.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:145:Cael had gone round by the assessment wing on his way from the reading room because the lamp was still lit there, and Seln was still at the table behind the counter with the day's last forms, copying. The desk clerk had gone. Cael stood at the counter and gave it to him in a dozen words, the two sentences and who had been refused, and Seln did not look up. He finished the line he was writing. He sanded it. Then he laid the pen down on the blotter, square to the edge, and sat for a moment with his hands in his lap and his eyes on the form in front of him, which was a request for floor hours from a Mire second-year and had nothing to do with anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:297:There were certified copies for the meet's clerks, a letter from the chancellor for Rooke, and a packet of forms for Gault that Gault received with deep suspicion.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:309:*You are on more men's scales now than I ever was. Mind the difference between the ones measuring you and the ones reading you. One sort wants a figure off you and goes home with it. The other sort wants to know you, and those are worth the trouble of an answer. — H.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-16.md:235:It had come for a reckoning, and it had been given a quarterfinal, won in three, by a fighter who had made the result certain in the first exchange and then declined to make it hurt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:39:She needed a host rule about delegation quarters. She went to the shelf for the certified transcription of the tournament charter and took it down and opened it at the back, where the schedules were, and turned pages until she found the rule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:131:On the last line, in the same hand as the rest, *S. Seln, assessment office: records and floor scheduling.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-21.md:55:A Mire fighter worked by making the floor feel deeper than it was, and Lira had declined to put her feet where he wanted them. She had spent one burst, Karis said, and never once crossed the place he kept pointing her at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:179:The counter in the assessment wing was open on the Fifth-day, as it was every Fifth-day, and the queue was as long as it always was in the week after a meet. Half the cohort had coursework overdue, and the other half had travel forms they had been told to fill in a fortnight ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:207:He took a canvas pouch from under the counter and laid it on the wood. It was grey and worn soft, with the academy's seal stamped on the flap and a brass ring for the lock. "Certified copies of anything the meet's clerks want from us. The chancellor's letters. And yours, if anybody writes to you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-30.md:173:He thought of Bracken at his green table, certifying a list without omission because a true record was the only kind there was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-30.md:351:The crowd moved round him, reading and talking, and after a time he became aware that the people nearest him had stopped reading the brackets and were reading him reading the blank lines.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:129:*Brom spent the first exchange watching where Marek looked,* Cael wrote afterward. *Not the touches. Where the touches went. Five stakes in the ground, and he knows where every one of them is now, because he watched every one go in. So now he knows what Marek's going to build on. Marek read him, and he read the reading, and only Brom knows there were two surveys on that floor.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:199:It was real. His whole doctrine said to take it. And he stood there and looked at what it had cost, and added that to everything else the morning had cost, and Cael saw him decide that it was not worth the price. It was the first time all tournament Cael had seen Marek decline anything he had found.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-11.md:301:Afterward the steward brought out the certificate. It was a stiff card with the meet's seal on it, printed with the year's date and the words *Iron, upper draw*, and a space where the steward wrote Ephram's name in his best hand and blotted it. He handed it over at the rope. Ephram held it in both hands and looked at it, turning it a little toward the window, as if he were not quite sure which way up it went. He did not say anything for a while.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-11.md:309:"It's a figure of speech." Ephram put the certificate inside his coat, carefully, against his chest. "I'm not giving you half of it. I'm telling you that you're owed. It's different." He looked toward the arch, where the noise from the main hall told them that Lira's final had begun. "I think I'd like to do that again. Win something." He seemed to listen to the sentence after he had said it, and to find it strange, and then to decide he liked it. "I think I'd like to get used to it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:141:Vastin put it at the bottom of the pile. He had an old rule about the paper he most wanted to read, which was that it went last. A man who starts his day with it reads everything else by its light, and his day comes out crooked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:149:The note on attendance quoted the steward's closing report. Vastin read the quoted sentence twice, and found that he had smiled, which he had not expected to do that day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:159:Or it looked like a fighter who had simply had a bad day on a cold floor and a good one on a warm one. A column of figures could not tell you which, and Vastin had spent his life refusing to make columns say more than they said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:217:He made himself price it first, as he priced any advice before he gave it. At the beginning of the year an advisory of three sentences had cost him nothing, because it had only declined to misread a charter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:271:Vastin did not explain the order to his clerk. He found, sitting at his table with the pigeons outside, that he had not explained it to himself either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:83:"Certified," said the clerk. "She checked." He slid the credential across. "Next house."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:89:*A girl I'll never meet paid her own money for a copy of my hearing and read it aloud at supper. Nobody carried that story into this town by talk. It came in on certified paper, through a copying office, at the public rate. People say rumour runs ahead of a man. Mine doesn't. My record does.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:117:Seln already had it. The certified extract of the enrollment record was in the file's front pocket, with a thin paper tab at the provision's page. He drew it out and put it into her hand, and stepped back again, and his face did nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:149:The fourteenth line, on both leaves, was a designation with its title written out in full.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:171:The merits were heavier than the first sittings had been, though there was not more talk; there was less. But every sitting put paper on his table, and all of it had to be receipted and numbered and laid against the lists. At the sixth sitting, the respondent's supplemental upon the renewals: eleven leaves, a table, a certified copy of a minute. At the seventh, a week later, the petitioner's acceptance of the tournament office's record, in one leaf; the respondent's second supplemental, upon the manner in which that record had been kept, in nineteen; and a correction slip, signed by the registrar of the house, altering the date on one exhibit by a single day, with the reason given.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:195:He had thought, this morning a week ago, with the dry pen resting under a fourteenth line, that there might be a seventh. He could admit that here. What he had hoped, for the space of perhaps a breath, was a simple thing: that a designation written out under seal before a seat would have to name its office.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-52.md:271:*Gold Rank 3. The system's favorite. And the loneliest read I've taken since Vastin — except hers is lonely like mine. Two days.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:57:Cael had read the crews' certificate the night before, pinned in the tunnel with the foreman's name and Auremont's risk officer's beneath it. The floor was true to the specification. Every bolt had been driven and every peg checked. It was written down and signed. Umber had signed it too, as the office required. And here he was in front of eight thousand people, testing it again with his own foot, as if the signatures were the beginning of knowing a thing and not the end of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:49:Cael read him: weight forward, left, the shoulders not dropped, so the front foot only, so wait. He waited, and the heel came up, and he went right and outside, and as he went he put his palm on Ephram's ribs, a touch, clean, on the line the second weight had just left open, and stepped off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:183:"You're Copper on your certificate," said Rooke. "You rated twenty-four tonight against an Iron Rank One with a panel of five, two of whom have never once said a kind word about anybody." The reserves did not look at each other. "The Standard doesn't read certificates. That's the whole of what I've been telling you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:225:Par was not a number, though he had thought it was one when he sat in the map room and copied out the bands: thirty, twenty-eight, twenty-two. It was a way of moving, a thing you could see from a bench if you knew how to look: clean, and in command, and doing something to the man in front of you. Lira had been near it tonight. Brom had been nearer than his certificate would ever admit. And Cael had been at a table's distance from it all evening, on purpose, choosing his distance mark by mark, while five people wrote down honestly what he had chosen to give them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:235:It was the night after the mock bouts, and five days remained. The fire was down to embers. Brom had gone to bed and Lira had gone to bed after him, threatening to come back down if anybody laughed. Karis had a lamp to herself and the certified transcription open in front of her. She was well into the back of it, past the schedules where she had found the exhibition provision, into the oldest parts of all: the founding articles, printed in a type nobody used any more, in the vocabulary of a century nobody alive had seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:39:"At whoever can make him." Then she lifted her eyes from the sheet and found Cael's across the table, and held them. There was nothing unkind in it. It was the look she gave a document that had just told her something she had already suspected. "Two bouts, a sheet of figures and a vain man's complaint, and by noon a stranger had found the question this whole circuit is going to be asking by the last meet. She found it, and she wrote it down, and then she very politely declined to answer it." She folded the ruling once. "The category is going to hold you exactly as long as nobody makes you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:7:Cael knew her before she had her coat off. She had been a sheet in Rooke's profiles stack in the first week of the year, marked more heavily than the sheets round it: a fair-day fighter from a market house in the hills, Iron Skin like Brom, Copper Rank Four on her certificate, with four bouts on her record and every one of them won and every one of them short. *Rank Four on paper. Fights like a Seven. Buried.* At the wool town she had walked Brom backward round his own chalk by the arm in front of a muttering gallery, and he had beaten her two touches to one in the fourth exchange, and the figures had come up twenty-three to twenty-two.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:155:"I saw. She couldn't read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:53:Copying it was his work. It was there on the roster against his name: *records and floor scheduling.* The delegation's travel file needed the meet's figures in it before the wagons left, and the clerks of the mill guild, who were slow, would not have the certified copies ready until noon. So Seln stood in the cold hall before the board, in the grey light from the loading doors, and copied the figures out by hand in a column on a fresh sheet, as he copied everything, neatly and without any appearance of interest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:91:Then he went to the document table at the end of the hall, by the arch, where the delegations' papers were stacked for collection, and began to sort Halcenvane's into the order the travel file required. He did it slowly, and it took him some time. At a quarter past the second bell the enrollee came through the arch from the guest house on his way to the second room, limping a little on the left, alone, and stopped at the table to ask whether the certified copies had come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:127:It was what Gault had promised at the end of last term and every time since: the enrollee measured against himself, and nothing else. *Measured against: baseline (entry), and the note of the twentieth of Reaping.* The basis of the enrollment was read into the minute and found unchanged. The term's supervised floor hours were certified from Rooke's sheets, which Gault had brought in the case, signed. The two meet records were laid on the table as evidence of performance under the provision, and the registry officer read the exhibition leaves with great attention and asked four questions about the documented ceilings, all of them competent, and Cael answered each one in the language of the documents themselves. The burst's distance; the burst's rate on sprung oak and on other floors; the landing beat; the read that looked like timing. All of it was in the record, and all of it was true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:17:"Every bracket at every meet is seeded off paper," he said. "Your formal tier puts you in a bracket. Your formal rank puts you on a line in it. The host sets the draw so the top rank meets the bottom rank first and the middle meets the middle, and nobody at the host's table ever looks at you, only at the line on your certificate." He looked at them now, one at a time. "So the paper puts Lira at the foot of the Iron seeding, because she's Rank One, and the first thing the draw will give her is somebody from the top of it. The paper puts Karis in the middle. It puts Ephram near the top, which is where he belongs. And it puts Brom in a Copper bracket, which is where he doesn't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:123:Cael read the Copper stack over Brom's shoulder before he read his own. Halfway down it was a sheet that Rooke had marked more heavily than the others. A fair-day fighter from a market house in the hills, Iron Skin like Brom, Copper Rank Four on her certificate and a record of exactly four bouts, every one of them won, every one of them short. Rooke's margin said: *Rank Four on paper. Fights like a Seven. Buried.* And under it, in smaller letters, as if Rooke had come back to it later: *Somebody's keeping her low on purpose.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:149:Fenmark: the house that had Kindled her at fourteen and read her Copper and sent her out into the world with six letters on a certificate. It had a fighter in the region's Iron bracket this year, a second-year, and Rooke had given him four words: *Good. Schooled. Expects respect.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:7:The entry instrument lay open on the records-hall counter with its four companions stacked beside it, and Bracken had turned it round so that Cael could read it right way up, which he did not need. It was the fourth day of term, a Fifth-day, in the hour after the morning's session let out, and the stone hall was empty except for the three of them and the clerk at the far desk, who was copying something with his tongue between his teeth. Bracken had sent a boy to the residence with the first bell, carrying a half-sheet that said *Records hall, the hour after session. Bring nothing.* Nobody else had been asked. Withrow had declined to come down, and had said so to Bracken in Cael's hearing in the covered walk the afternoon before, in five words: "It's a filing. File it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:11:"Read your line," said Bracken. "The charter gives an enrolled practitioner the right to inspect his own entry before it leaves the house. Nobody ever uses it. You're going to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:21:Under the instrument lay the fee note, with the house's seal on it. Under that lay the acknowledgment of the meet calendar, initialed. And under all of them, thicker than the other four together and bound in a grey card cover with a tape round it, lay the document the charter really required: a certified copy of Halcenvane Academy's enrollment record, every name on it, current to the first of the month.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:27:*I certify this record complete and true, containing every practitioner enrolled at this institution on the date given, without omission.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:77:"Then the registrar signs a certificate that says *without omission* over a record with an omission in it." She looked at him. "Do you want to be the one to write down that you'd have preferred Bracken to lie? Over his name? In ink?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:93:"Everyone's going to say we did something this morning. In a fortnight half the circuit will be telling the other half that Halcenvane played a trick at the registry, and they'll be very pleased with themselves for spotting it." She did not look up from the page. "We didn't put you on the roster. We just declined to commit fraud by leaving you off. The clause does the rest."

