editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:5:There was a big young man sitting on the step there with bandaged forearms, watching the court. He looked up as Vastin passed. Vastin knew him from the row: the continental Copper champion, the bluff's wall. He nodded to him, as one nods to a man keeping a door. The young man nodded back, gravely, and went on watching the court.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:9:He had written a note like that himself, once, on the road home from the bluff. Fewer words, and in a worse light, and signed with his whole name. He had thought it careful at the time. He saw now that the west tower had done it better. They had not tried to say what the boy was. They had said only what their instrument could and could not do, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:93:"The minute from the bluff held," she said. "I want that said first, before anything else, because it's true. Nothing was made. She began it. You were awake and you knew what you were doing." She laid the sheet on the table. "And the third line held too, as far as it went. The cost was on paper. Every cost we had ever seen was on paper." She looked at him. "The trouble is we had never seen one at that weight. We had it written down at the weight of a stair, and a yard, and a copper pot. Not at the weight of a Gold, in the middle of a bout, in front of a city."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:139:The steward took the bluff's standard, which was plain and not new and had been mended once at the hoist by somebody's careful needle a long time ago, and carried it past the whole row of fourteen staffs to the head of the floor. He fixed it to the first of the three tall staffs there, the honour staffs. Then he ran it up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-59.md:163:Cael thought it was exactly like the bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:371:It was soft at the folds by now. It had come up a bluff in a post-room sort in the year's seventh week, and he had read it some dozens of times, at a sill, and knew it without the flame. A Gold at Auremont had written to tell him not to mistake what was happening to him for procedure. She had said that the rematch stood, and she had told him to keep himself unclassified.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:481:He had thought he knew what large was. He had seen the continent drawn on a sheet. He had crossed a good part of it, at a wagon's pace, to stand in a hall at Norhold, and he had looked down from a bluff at a river valley every day for two years and thought it a wide thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md:87:Cael understood it within a dozen heartbeats, and admired it within twenty. The ganger at the wool town had herded. This man did not herd; he had no need to move Cael anywhere. He only took ground away, an inch at a time, and every inch he took he kept. The racks behind him were a wall on his west flank, and nothing could come at him from that side. He advanced on the diagonal that cut off the north half of the floor first. Then, when Cael went south to escape it, he was already turning to cut off that half too, with a step that seemed to have been decided before Cael moved. He was not reading Cael. He was reading the floor. He had spent half his life teaching apprentices where a man could and could not go on these boards, and he simply went to the places Cael would need next, a moment before Cael needed them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-16.md:27:Brom did not read papers about himself alone. He never had, as far as Cael knew, except the letters from Velmere, which he read by himself and folded small and never spoke of. Everything else, he brought to the table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-16.md:207:When he came back to his mark his face had changed. He looked frightened and brave at once, which is the face of a young fighter about to try something he is not ready for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-36.md:79:*Brom fought carefully today,* Cael wrote that night. *He fought a woman who had watched him for a week and a man who had watched him for a season, both at once, from the same floor, and he gave the first a real bout and the second nothing at all. She said the second time is the real rating. I think she's right. The first look at anybody is a story somebody tells. The second is a measurement.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:101:The gallery along the second floor's rope began to mutter. To the gallery it looked like the buried Copper owning the big Halcenvane boy, walking him backward round his own chalk by the arm. Her coach unfolded his arms for the first time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:179:"The adjudication office's own edition," said Gault. "The one the panels train from. Not the forty pages they sell at the meets. All of it, with every note the office has added since the cycle was locked." He touched the cover with one finger, carefully. "Bracken wrote to the office for it on the day the card came up. He paid for it out of the records budget. He says he would not ask anyone to stand in front of a rule that he had not read in the version its enforcers read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:11:"*Auremont went home with the bout. The bluff went home with the first thing anybody has taken off Auremont all week, and there is no column in the standings to put it in.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:23:"I know. I've been thinking about the second half." He looked round at them. "Plan to have fewer. That's not plan to have none. You can't have none. Nobody can. Rooke knows that better than anybody." He stood up and began to clear his own plate, which he never did. "So the question isn't how many I've got. It's which ones he gets to keep."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:155:From the tiers it looked as though Brom had simply outscored him. A big man had stood in the middle of the floor and hit harder than a small man, and won an exchange, and the people who did not read craft cheered it as they would have cheered a wrestler at a fair. The people who did read craft were quiet. Rooke kept his own counsel. But Cael, beside him, saw him take his little book out of his pocket and write one word in it and put it away again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-48.md:87:She came to everything. She had stood at the front of the draw with her hands folded, and at the trial's posting, and at the boards on every night of the brackets, and been seen. Tonight the place at the front where she would have stood had two dockmen in it. Cael looked twice, and on the second look he understood it as he understood a move on a floor. She was the most careful person he knew. She had not forgotten. Somewhere earlier, by herself, she had read this board, and decided it was not hers to stand at. She had left it to the five of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-48.md:151:"Half a second, and she wasn't even looking at you. She was on her way somewhere and you were in the road." He turned his back to the door and leaned on it. "In that ring she'll be looking at you from the moment you walk out. Nothing else. Every bit of her, on you." He thought about it, slowly. "I'd want that one, if I were you. Attention you can read. Traffic you can't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:223:He declined all six. He gave the panel what it needed to rate: clean feet, a read that looked like decent timing, two touches on the right lines.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:257:"Second of six. Lira: I'll see what four bursts in the opening bout cost you at the first dawn on the road, and you won't dress it up for me. Brom: I looked for a note to give you and didn't find one. Karis: you spent your second bout reading the judges' table, and it cost you an exchange you didn't need. I'll lend you that exchange this time. I'm telling you so you know it's a loan. Ephram: the Blade beat you on figures, not on the floor, and you know the difference, so I needn't tell you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-52.md:271:*Gold Rank 3. The system's favorite. And the loneliest read I've taken since Vastin — except hers is lonely like mine. Two days.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:41:The whole bowl watched the showman from the lake house, and there was nothing to learn from looking where eight thousand people were already looking. Vastin watched the boy, as he had once sat four feet from him in a room twelve feet by fourteen and watched him while he answered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:51:In the third exchange the boy took a touch standing in the light, and in the instant before it Vastin saw something cross his face that he could not read. It was not surprise and it was not fear. It was very quick and then gone, and he did not write it down, because a thing he did not know could not go into a book as though he did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:87:The table took that in. Nobody asked how he knew it was Vastin; he had described that stillness to them often enough over the year, and Karis had already had the rest of it from Lira, who had watched the twelfth chair through the whole bout from the north rail instead of watching the floor, and had told everybody afterward, with some indignation, that she had missed the best touch of the afternoon on Cael's account.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:167:The likeness-seller took the copper and made change and handed him the sheet rolled up, and said "Good luck to the bluff," because the sheet had the bluff's name on it, and did not look at his face at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-23.md:85:He had grown up in Denvash, inland, with no river worth the name. But he had stood on the ferry landing under the bluff for a year now, and watched the water come down past Ostrand, and he had watched the barges here at the quays since he arrived. A river's surface shifted and swirled and went every way at once. Underneath, it went one way. It went the way the bed went, because the bed was stone and the water was only water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-23.md:237:She sat on the front bench of the east side, between Bracken and Rooke, with her hands folded on the rug in her lap. Her name was not read. The name of her house was, once, in four syllables, in a voice that gave it nothing. It was the name she had carried up and down the bluff for eighteen years in front of a house that had not qualified once in all that time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:45:It was not office stock; he knew office stock as he knew his own boots. He saw her fold it twice and put it inside her coat, and pick up her office pen again. He did not look for the words. He did not need to. He had four sheets of his own like it, carried through three postings and now in his kit between the second and third of his manuals, where nobody ever looked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:59:"It came to my office by a tournament page," she said. "It's addressed to you, care of me, which is correct. I've read the outside of it, which is my right as your chancellor. I have not read the inside, which is not." She put it in his hand. "I'll be downstairs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:71:"Nothing written," she said. "Think about what that means for us. This whole house runs on one rule, and it's kept you alive since the bluff. If it's on paper, we can survive it. Every word of the hearing was written down, and that's why we could fight it. The provision's on paper. The roster was on paper and that's the only reason you were on a floor at all." She touched the note with one finger. "He's asking you to walk out of the one place we've ever been safe. Whatever's said up there, afterwards there's his memory of it and yours, and his comes with a grey coat and a seal."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:83:"I asked for a witness once," he said, "with a warden across a table from me. I was right to. That man was a piece of the machine, and you don't sit down with a piece of the machine without somebody to say afterward what was said." He looked at the note. "This one's different, and I've had a year to learn how. Eleven questions in a room on the bluff. Three sentences of advisory. A chair held for him that he never asked for, two notes, and a walk out before the figure." He turned the note over. "Every move he's ever made toward me he's made in ink, and signed with his whole name. This is the first thing he's ever asked for that he can't sign. A man like that doesn't give up his ink to set a trap. He'd use the ink." He folded it. "I want to know what's worth that much to him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:143:His eyes went over Cael once. They did it as they had on the bluff, without any hurry and without seeming to do it at all. They rested a moment where the strapping showed under the shirt, and a moment on the wrapped forearm, and on the care with which Cael lowered himself into the chair across the table. Vastin said nothing about any of it. Cael saw him take each thing and put it away, and understood that this was a kind of good manners. In his trade you did not comment on what you found. You kept it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:163:"I'm going to give you eight words," said Vastin. "Four, and then four. I have watched them come across my table this year in letters about your file, the first four in the early part of it, the second four since. I want you to hear them in the order they came." He said the first four slowly, each one by itself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:199:"If I gave you names I would be guessing, and I don't guess." He did not say it to be clever. He said it as Gault said a plate would not read past its last notch. "I can tell you what my year has looked like from my chair. A year ago I was asked what you are. This year I am asked what can be done about you. I answer every one of those letters by saying, again, what you are. Each answer goes up the stair. Each one is entered and filed. I am fairly sure none of them is read." He turned his left hand over, and back. "My office still works. It works very well. It has simply stopped mattering. When the questions stop being questions, an evaluator knows the decision has been taken in some room he was not shown."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:229:"With the wrong instruments, for three days," he said. "I came up to the bluff with a file and a plate and a set of questions, and all three told me nothing. A man in my trade has one great temptation, and I gave in to it. I thought the nothing was something being hidden from me." He moved the left hand. "It is the oldest mistake there is. When an instrument won't read, you'd sooner believe it's broken than believe you've pointed it at a thing it was never made to measure."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-18.md:211:She did not read them the market's file again. They had all seen what it held, and where every line of it came from: meet records, the gallery, the hearing at the copying rate. "The walls hold," she said. "Two rules will keep them holding."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:57:"Lira is in charge of the door." Karis wrote a section number on a slip and tucked it into the second volume. "Not this door. That one has the girl. The records hall's. The intake found out this morning where we sat last night. There were nine of them on the records-hall steps at the second bell, looking at the windows as if the code were going to come out and wave."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:121:Cael read everything that day. That was the arrangement: Karis wrote, and he read. Not for approval, she said; because it was about him, and she did not put her name to documents about people who had not read them. He read at the window with the light behind him and the pages on his knee, and when he had read a leaf he laid it face down on the sill, and Karis took it back without asking what he thought, because if he had thought anything he would have said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:137:He read it at his own window that night, with the fountain going in the court and the copy of the instrument shut in its folder on the bed behind him. Across the top of the grid ran the columns: the sitting, the panel, the day, the finding, the seal. Down the side ran the three times his place on the bluff had been written down by somebody obliged to write it. The grant at Greyvane, from the hearing. The first renewal, at Halcenvane, in the inspection year, before the Compact's delegation. The renewal at the mill town, last year, on the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:15:He had seen the name before, of course. It was on the registry's own extract in the enrollment file, and on the Greyvane transcript, and on the summons that had brought him up the bluff road in the first place. But it was not a name anybody had ever called him. It was the name a clerk at Denvash had built out of a boy and a workshop on the morning of his Kindling. The given name had been made formal, the household he came from had been turned into a word with a hyphen in it, and a number stood after it that meant nothing to anyone but the registry. Seeing it here, in a list of five, under Lira and Brom and Karis and Ephram, was like hearing his own voice played back from the far side of a valley.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:53:"Once it's in the courier's bag it belongs to somebody else," she said on the records-hall steps. "I've been minding it for twelve weeks. I'd like to watch it go." She said it the way she said anything about her own feelings, as a finding she had checked and was now reporting, and Cael went down the bluff road with her as far as the first bend. A stone bench stood there under a thorn the wind had bent half over, and the whole valley lay open below it, river and bridge and town and the long brown fields going away to the hills.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:105:They walked back up together. Behind them the ferry grounded on the far bank, and a speck on a grey went up the road beyond the river toward the hills, and the morning's work was out of every hand on the bluff for good.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:119:He did it the same way for everybody. He took the form. He read it, every line, at an even pace, from the name to the signature. He stamped the received line, tore off the copy-slip along its rule, and gave the slip back. The Stone first-year got what the Mire third-year got, and the Mire third-year what the first-year got, down to the second it took to read the reason for the hours. Cael had worked out in his second month on the bluff what that was for. A man who read every form from every hand was a man nobody remembered reading anything in particular.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:179:"At every meet." Gault sobered, a little. "The tournament charter has the registry validate every tier result. A Copper win goes onto a Copper fighter's record only once a Compact officer has watched it and signed that the tier was what the paper said. So there will be a validation seat at every floor, every meet, a man or a woman with a badge and a book." He reached for the next form on his tray, and then did not pick it up. "Nobody will have to come up this bluff road to look at you this year, enrollee. You are going to them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:183:Since Ardenmere the arrangement had run in one direction, and he had never once seen it run the other. He stayed where he was, and the apparatus travelled to him. Wardens had come to a market town. Assessors had come to a circuit hall. A delegation with an Archmarshal at its head had come up this very road in three carriages and stayed eleven days. And all that time two coats stood at the ferry landing and two at the road's foot, because the bluff was where he was, and somebody paid by the year to have it watched.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-28.md:19:Cael had never seen her away from the bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-56.md:111:Inside, it did not break at all for a long while. From the north barrier Cael could see the dais, and nothing on it moved. Nine pens lay still on nine open charts. Behind them, at their own table, one of the three scribes had stopped and was looking at the end of his pen, as if it had done something without asking him, while the other two wrote as fast as their hands would go. High over the east rail, in the Compact's row, the quiet records officer from the bluff had stood up, and did not seem to know that he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-56.md:115:She stood at the foot of her own lane, by the north mast, where the fire had gone into the iron. In all her public bouts nobody had ever seen her stand still in the middle of an exchange except once on a trial floor, for a second. She looked first at the barrier where he had been. Then along the foot of it, to the scorch his little fire had left at the end of his lane. Then at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-56.md:230:At the north rail Rooke had taken his hand back. He said something to the air, but the younger healer heard it and said it again at her own supper table that night, and by the end of the week it had reached the bluff by three roads. It was the version the house kept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:33:"Rhagen," said Karis. "Doctrine. Everything they do is written down first and taught second. Marek's from there. So is whatever they send to look at you." She considered the slip. "Rooke's right about one thing. If a doctrine house files on you, it'll be to find out what you are, not to beat you. They won't care about the touches. They'll care about the survey."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:55:"I know you didn't. I said it to me." She shut the transcription and put it back on the shelf. "I keep deciding it, every time I go past. It's very tiring. The season first. Then the other thing." She came back to the map and picked up another pin. "It isn't going anywhere. It's been in that book longer than the house has been on this bluff."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:57:Below the window, at the foot of the bluff road, two coats stood in the snow in the lee of the wall, and two more stood at the ferry landing on the far side of the river, small and dark against the white, where Cael could see them from here as he could see them from every high window on the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:135:He signed the report, sealed it with the office's plain seal, and put it in the pouch for the north. The pouch would go down the bluff on the morning coach, past the two coats at the foot of the road. They would not look at it. Nobody ever looked at the pouch; that was what a pouch was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:199:Cael woke to the sound of water. It was running everywhere, off the roofs and down the gutters and along the covered walk, and in the morning the snow on the bluff had gone grey and soft and was sliding off the tiles in long wet sheets. The river under the wall had risen a man's height overnight and ran brown and heavy and very fast, carrying whole trees down past Ostrand toward the confluence, and the bluff road was a stream for a day, and then deep mud, and then, on the morning of the Second-day, a road again, or near enough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:213:The wagons went out through the gate and down the bluff road in the thin early light, with the mud sucking at the wheels and the whole valley below them steaming in the first sun.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:221:Nobody had arranged that either. It was market day, and the town was out, and somebody had seen the wagons coming down the bluff. By the time they reached the bridge there were perhaps two hundred people standing along it in the mud, with more coming down the lanes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:223:There were the baker's boys and the ferry crew and the women from the fish steps, and the man who sold hot chestnuts at the bottom of the hill. There were children on their fathers' shoulders. A great many of them had never set foot on the bluff in their lives.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:233:Cael sat on the tailboard of the second wagon with Lira beside him and looked back. The bridge was still lined with people, getting smaller.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:235:Above it the bluff stood up dark against the morning, with the house on top of it, and the wall where Lira had sat a few nights before, looking north, in the starlight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:245:The watchers grew smaller behind them, two and two, standing in the melt on the bell, and then the road bent and the bluff went behind a line of willows, and they were gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:73:Two days later, on the First-day, the wagons climbed the bluff road at dusk, with the snow coming down thick and soft and the lamps already lit in Ostrand behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:77:Then the wagons began the long climb, and at the first bend Cael saw that something was wrong with the top of the bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:141:Vastin put it at the bottom of the pile. He had an old rule about the paper he most wanted to read, which was that it went last. A man who starts his day with it reads everything else by its light, and his day comes out crooked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:149:The note on attendance quoted the steward's closing report. Vastin read the quoted sentence twice, and found that he had smiled, which he had not expected to do that day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:167:He laid it on the table and did not read it at once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:191:He read it once and turned it face down on the table. Then he turned it up again and read it slowly, a word at a time, with one finger under the line, as he had been taught to read a charter when he was very young and had not read one that way since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:203:He had watched grammar change before, in his time. It was never announced. A word that had lived for years in one kind of paper would begin to turn up in another kind, a little out of place, like a farm cart in a city street. Nobody remarked on it the first time, or the second. By the tenth time it had stopped looking out of place, and the people who used it had forgotten that it had ever been anywhere else. He had once seen *resettlement* travel in that way from a report on a flooded valley to a report on a quarrelsome town, in less than a year, and nobody who used it in the second report had ever read the first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:217:He made himself price it first, as he priced any advice before he gave it. At the beginning of the year an advisory of three sentences had cost him nothing, because it had only declined to misread a charter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:429:Brom turned his over by the fire, where he read everything, with Cael standing at his shoulder, because Brom did not read papers about other people by himself either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:103:The Halcenvane rail went quiet all along its length, because they knew what they were watching, and nobody else in the hall did. The file had a line in it about this, the cruellest line on the third page, and Ivenne had written it. *Pressed for time, she goes back to shapes she has already written down. She does not invent well, knows it, and declines to try.* Ivenne was standing twenty feet away, waiting on that line. If that sentence was still true, she had already lost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:91:The first pile was the house's, for the halls and the residence, and it went into his hand as it grew. The second was the assessment wing's: anything addressed to the wing, its counter, its instructors, its office. The third was the registrar's, for Bracken's counter and the records hall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:111:He did not read it again. He got out Hesk's book instead, and a pen, and copied it into the Log word for word, slowly, the way he had once copied the last lines of Gault's note into a letter for Hesk, because copying a thing makes you see every mark in it. Copying, he saw the dash before her initial, and that she had written the last line after the initial and not before it, as if it were an afterthought; and that she had not written *Dear* or *Cael* or anything at all at the top.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:197:On the First-day he had gone down the river path alone before light, which he had not done since the year began. On the Second-day he had gone down the bluff road with Brom at the fourth bell, an hour nobody went down at, to carry a crate of the reading room's copying paper up from the ferry because the carrier had not come. On the Fifth-day the whole house had gone down to the sitting in the coach, as was known a week ahead. On the Sixth-day he had carried two document cases down into the town for Karis, so that her answer to the fee office would go in at its counter by her own hand and nobody else's. Four times in one week his pattern had broken. Any of them would have been worth a line to a man who was paid to follow him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:77:There were two sheets of him from the same printing house. Both were called *THE UNNAMED OF HALCENVANE*, and the same woodcut of the face did for both. In the first the background was a blaze of sunbeams and the young man was heroic, with his chin up. In the second the background was solid black and the same young man looked out of it like somebody waiting in an alley. There was a third, cruder, on cheaper paper, called *THE BOY THE REGISTRY COULD NOT SORT*, which showed a figure in a fighting stance surrounded by question marks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:79:There was one of Lira, called *LIRA OF THE BLUFF*. It made her a head taller than she was and very much angrier, with her hair streaming and her fists clenched, against a background of lightning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:83:And there was the sixth, the newest, the ink still smelling. It was called *THE FIVE FROM THE BLUFF*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:215:The Archmarshal Vastin read the Norhold files on a Fourth-day evening, in his room in the Compact's house, a week before the opening.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:17:Cael had seen Lira hold the Copper Crown like that, once, on the bluff. He had not expected ever to see it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:11:Cael stood at the west gate with the others and listened to the roar and read it, because reading things was what he did when he was frightened. Under it he could hear two hopes at once, the holders' and the bluff's, shouted by the same people in the same breath. The holders against the house with the blank. The Gold against the boy nobody could rate. He did not write any of it down. There would be no time for writing on that floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:133:Two exchanges. The marshals had chalked them both up beside the bluff's name, in a sanctioned book, in front of everybody who mattered in the business of fighting, and no clerk alive could rub them out again. It had been counted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:327:"At Halcenvane," he said, "I told myself I was going to be careful how I remembered it. All of it. You, especially." He looked at the water running away across the cobbles. "I have been careful. I've watched you from the rail for a year, and I've waited for a floor where I'd see it for myself, whatever it is, with nobody telling me what I was looking at. Today I was on the floor." He looked up. "And she was ahead. She had the exchange, and the floor, and the whole bowl on its feet for her, and she gave up a whole second of it to stand there and look at you. Like we look at you. Like you look at everything."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:35:"I stand in it. Within five weeks, at the district station, with Ilsev seeing to the conduct of it, and she'll see to it properly. I put my hand where I'm shown. It was asked once before, and it took eleven seconds." He did not look at Seln, and he went no nearer to it than that. "We know what it says. Karis told a seat so for fifty-one minutes and nobody contradicted her: *no valid classification.* Then the chapter opens, in its order. One: every enrollment or standing that rests on my being a practitioner is void, and that's this hill. Two: I'm suspended as a practitioner pending a review, and the review is to be begun by the registry at its discretion."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:141:Brom read it, and this time he did not read it aloud. He read it twice, by the movement of his eyes, and then he held it out across the table, not to Cael but to Karis, because she was the one who read such things, and she read it to the room in her small dry voice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:309:Two years ago this was the worst thing that could have happened to him on the bluff. He had built a band and a rotation and a variance so that nobody would ever stand where Ephram was standing and say what Ephram had just said. He waited for the old cold to come up through his stomach, and it did not come. There was a ruling in his satchel, and a slip on the table with one word on it. The band had been rent paid on a room, and he was leaving the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-03.md:147:Lira had the Iron stack, and she had stopped at one sheet near the bottom, and she was not reading it. She was looking at the house name at its head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-33.md:213:"She shortened the count," said Ephram. He was still breathing hard, and his face was red, but his voice was perfectly steady. "I found her wheel in one exchange, and she changed it in the next. Fifteen years of doing it one way, and she changed it in a breath because a boy from the bluff had found it." He looked down at his hands, half unwrapped. "I couldn't hit a pane before it was there. Nobody could. Not with an edge."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:85:It gave his name, and his house on the bluff above some northern river, and the clause of an old charter that had obliged the house to enter him. It gave fifteen exhibition figures, with a note that he had lost none of the bouts behind them. It gave a circuit record from early on in his fighting, in a city called Ardenmere, and the circuit record had losses in it, which the shop printed without comment, as if it could not decide whether they mattered. It used the phrase *peculiar timing* four times. And it had a new last leaf, sold separately for a copper, which said in italics that he had survived her lane at trial speed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-24.md:19:Lira stood at her chalk in the north. She had her sash knotted at her hip and her wrists taped to the second knuckle, and she was not looking at the Silver at all. She was looking at his knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-24.md:99:Cael could not read his weight from where he sat; nobody could read a man's weight at that distance across a moving floor. But he did not need to. He could see the man's face. It had been courteous for an exchange and a half, a good craftsman's face, kind to a younger one. Now it was simply interested. He looked at her as a man looks at a job he had thought would take an afternoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:87:She did the sum on the bluff road, at the second switchback, where the wall hid her from the landing and the gate could not see her yet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:165:She nodded, as if he had confirmed a figure she already had in her book, and finished the apple, and threw the core over the wall into the dark, where it would roll halfway down the bluff and feed some bird in the morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:259:He sat a while longer with Rooke's profiles in front of him and did not read them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:267:He had not argued for it, or asked for it. Other people's honest words had carried him to it: a clerk's record, a dead board's rule, a girl who read schedules, a registrar's mirror and a chancellor's seal. And somebody, somewhere above all of them, had written an answer to a question and let it go through without a word to anybody on the bluff. He did not know who that was. He would very likely never know. Some silence up there had shaped itself round his name, and he could feel the edges of it, a draught from a door he could not see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:271:He opened the window. The fountain went on below in the court, the one continuous sentence it had been saying all recess. Beyond the residence roofs the bluff fell away into the dark. The valley lay under no moon, and the town's lamps had gone out street by street until only the ferry lamp was left, small and steady at the landing, with two grey coats under it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-35.md:17:Twice somebody called Lira's name from a doorway. Once a girl of about ten ran alongside them for half a street, shouting *Bluff! Bluff!*, until her mother caught her by the collar and hauled her back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-35.md:167:High in the upper gallery, Withrow had not stood up with the rest. Cael looked for her and found her sitting perfectly still with her hands in her lap, looking down at the floor with an expression he could not read at that distance and did not try to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:113:At Norhold the whole continent had seen him spend past every one of those lines, on a scored floor, with three scribes writing it down faster than any clerk could, and it was in the record now and would stay there for as long as there was a record. A rate you have shown is a rate you have shown. But a fighter did not fight every Fifth-day at the rate he had once spent for a title, and here, on the bluff, in front of three cards and a clerk, he fought at the house's rate and inside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:209:Below it, three lines of this year. Below them, Ephram took a pencil from his ear and wrote a fourth while Cael watched. *Wing three. Shield R5. Twenty-three and a third.* In the margin he wrote *gave him the second*, and then he looked at what he had written, and drew a small question mark beside it, and left it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:231:He thought there was something else behind it, too. Ephram's eyes had gone to the empty floor while he talked, to the chalk marks at either end, and stayed there a moment longer than a man's eyes stay on a floor he has no business with. Whatever it was, it was not ready to be said. Cael knew that look. He wore it himself, in front of the Log, most nights.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:103:It began with the enrollment basis: the clause, the citations under it, the long chain of verification by which a registrar on a bluff had shown that a boy with no Path on his papers could stand on a floor at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:217:The look lasted one second past the place where Seln's looks ended. Cael had watched that man not look at him across a counter every week for most of a year, and he had never once seen a look of his run over. From Seln it was as loud as a question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:123:She did not read anything aloud this time. She found the two provisions, the old one and the new, and laid the volume open at the old one with her finger beside its last two words, *shall receive*. Then she got the digest down from the bottom shelf, the one her assistant had found for herself on the day of *find it*, and opened it at the table of uses at its back, and turned it round so that they could see the line for themselves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-47.md:27:Under that the shop had added three paragraphs of what it called analysis. Brom did not read those aloud. He read the line twice, to himself, moving his lips a little, and then he passed the leaf to Cael across the bread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:91:"So," said Karis. "A tool for the testing of instruments, used ninety-one times as it was made to be used, and now to be used a ninety-second time in a new way. I should like to look at the new way, and I shall be exact about it. Being exact is my discipline, and I have no other."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:139:There was no slip in her hand; the slips were in the wallet on the table, twenty-five in one bundle and five in the other. But he had heard her say the close three times through, and three times she had stopped after this part, and looked at nothing, and gone on. He had counted the pause in the east hall, and it was four seconds long. She stopped now, and looked at the grey shape above her for four seconds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:37:In the lamplight Lira could not read her heel. She had said so. But Cael, watching her come at the lane on the four-count, saw her find something else. The dust in the cracks between the painted boards had woken as the lane went down, the way it always did, and was running south along the lane in a thin pale stream. In daylight from the high windows you could hardly see it. Under two lamps set low on the floor, it threw a shadow. The whole lane showed on the boards as a faint moving band of dark, like the wake of a fish under still water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:107:She was on the attendance bench, close enough to lean forward and say it into his collar, and she said it before Cael had looked toward the evaluation seat at all. He looked. The grey covers were gone from its five chairs, and four district evaluators he had never seen sat upright in four of them, two men and two women, with their files on small lap-desks. The fifth chair stood a little forward of the others. In it sat a broad-backed woman in the registry's grey. Her files were set at an angle he would have known anywhere, never quite square to the desk and never a hair different from the last time: a counter at Greyvane, the east wall of a room on the bluff, the first seat of the Compact's row over the Concourse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:171:It was the names that had made the evaluators look up. Ilsev heard her own read out among them and wrote something small in her file without lifting her head. The counsel read twenty-two of them, one at a time, each with a grade and a house and a day, at the pace at which a person reads names in a room where names matter. The grant at Greyvane and its two renewals on paper, the season's officers under a regional registrar's seal, the tournament's, and three days of the Compact's own senior evaluator on the bluff. At the Archmarshal's name Cael thought the stillness under the hood altered, the way still water alters when something passes under it. It took four minutes. In the quiet he heard a cart's iron tyre grind over the cobbles in the street. More looking, the counsel said when she had finished, than most practitioners get in a working life; and all of it done by people who could see, sealed and minuted, across three years. She turned the rule. Tick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:247:Brom said the first thing he remembered was the bruise: something had hit him hard on the left hip on the second step, and when he looked down there was a boy's satchel buckle going away from him round the corner by the corn chandler's, and a voice saying sorry over its shoulder. He had not seen the boy's face. He rubbed the hip now, thoughtfully, as if the bruise might tell him more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:79:The hall stayed quiet. Somewhere below the bluff the escort's hooves went round the first switchback and were gone. Withrow turned the leaf down again, edge to edge with the ones beneath it, and Cael saw her make certain her fingers would not shake before she let them do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:135:Nobody told the bluff. Nobody needed to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:5:Halcenvane stood along the top of a bluff above the river Ost, a long grey academy of halls and tiers and covered walks, and for twelve weeks three parts in four of it had been empty. The Crown yard at that hour belonged to nobody. The porter's boards stood bare along the long wall, wiped down for the recess, with the ghost of last season's chalk still grey in the grain of the wood. Mist lay on the tiers. At the far end, by the wash-house, Brom had his shirt off in the cold and was working through his sheet at the striking post, slowly, just as Rooke had written it for him: nineteen exercises, round three, four mornings in the week. He had not missed a morning since the recess began, and he would not have mentioned it if he had been paid to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:21:The burst was hers, in a way. Lira's Path was Wind, and she had been bursting across floors in front of him for three years, since long before either of them had heard of this bluff. Cael had no Path at all that the registry could name. What he had instead was a short list of things he had watched other people do until, at a cost, they had become things he could do too, and her burst had been the first of them. He had paid for it ever since, every time, in the same coin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:99:*The wing's counter: open every working day of the recess, with the same clerk's name against every day on the sheet. Nobody on this bluff has said a word about that sheet. Everybody I live with has read it to the bottom.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:111:*The push I took from the Bronze at Ardenmere. Never shown on this bluff; flat at every plate they've put me on. Four blows a sitting, paid in the right shoulder.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:161:"Four. There are four meets in this region. Every standing earned at any of the four is added to the house's total, and the houses whose totals cross the line go on to the finals. This cycle the finals are at Norhold, a crossroads city about four times the size of the town under this bluff, which once in every three years hosts the oldest contest between chartered houses on this continent.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md:19:He did not read the order until the hall had already cooled. Bracken had two folded leaves in the breast of his coat by then and a fresh smear of the officer's ink on his thumb, and Karis had the duplicate, which she passed along the bench to Cael without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md:127:She did not read those again. She counted instead. Cael had done that to her years ago, at a table in Ardenmere; she had laughed at him, and then caught herself doing it on her own, and never stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:291:And all through it, in every queue and tavern door and printed sheet, the city was telling itself the story of this cycle out of whatever it had to hand. There was the favourites' house and its Gold. There was the house from the bluff, back after eighteen years. There was the wall. And there was the boy with no tier, who had pulled two Silvers out of a fossil in an evening.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:335:He looked along the row. He had known it for four days by face and by seat. Ilsev had the first seat, the senior evaluation seat, with her files set on her lap-desk at an angle that had not shifted by a hair since the opening. He remembered that exact, unhurried care from a hearing room and from the bluff, and in every act of hers that he knew of she had been perfectly correct. Havel had the second. He was the quiet records officer from the bluff, older than Cael remembered him. Cael had hardly exchanged a word with him, and yet he had turned up at the edge of Cael's life at Ardenmere and at Greyvane and on the bluff, watching and writing. The other four observation seats had changed on the rotation every day, badged men and women he did not know. Past them sat two district officers, a liaison man, and two officials of the host city who came and went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:5:"Call days," she said. "Four of them, before any ruling. In those four days a seat may recall a party to put a question it has thought of late, and so may its evaluation seat, and the code gives them an hour's notice to do it in. An hour, on foot, from wherever the party is lodged." She looked out at the switchbacks. "That was a fair rule when every respondent lived over his own shop in the town. It was not written with a bluff in mind. Your hill is an hour and a quarter from that room by a good horse, and so for four days the house must live where it can hear the exchange's bell. The code calls it keeping within the bell. I have always thought it rather a handsome phrase for a nuisance."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:15:The house's lodging was the wool-factors' guesting-house, in the lane behind the exchange. It was a tall narrow building of old brick with a gallery along its back, and it smelled of lanolin, as everything did in that quarter of the town. The factors kept it for buyers who came upriver at the sales. Between sales it stood three parts empty, and the woman who kept it had given the house its whole upper floor for four nights at a price that made Bracken take off his second pair of glasses and look at her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:157:He had known the boy would be at it. The house had said it would attend every sitting, and the house kept what it said. Havel did not look at him, because the rotation manual's page on courtesy said that an officer of a seat would show the parties ordinary courtesy and no more, and because looking at the boy was not ordinary. He had looked before. On a pump step at Ardenmere, where looking had been the work. Twice at Greyvane, from the recorder's desk below the bench. Three times on the oak at the bluff, from a chair on the east wall: once as the boy came in, and twice when the frame broke, the second quicker than the first. He remembered every one. He did not mean to add to them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:241:He did not turn round. He heard the draft's paper stop rustling, and her breath close behind his right ear, and he knew where her eyes had gone. His hand moved to close the book, from a habit older than the bluff, and stopped, and lay on the sill beside it instead.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:111:It was lit the way it was lit every night of the year, a yellow square at the top of the dark building, with the lamp set close to the glass so that you could see it from the north wall and the gate lodge and, Seln had once said, from the ferry landing at the foot of the bluff. The whole valley knew that window. A coat at the foot of the road could have told you the hour by it: lit at dusk, out somewhere between the tenth bell and the eleventh, never once dark before the ninth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:207:Karis took the second pair, and read the record, and Brom read the order. A station in the hill country, a boy. *Ash, first rank. Keeper's note: something of Stone in the frame; second family?* And the order: *Enter: Ash, Rank One. Margin reading not to be entered. Observe nine weeks.* And on the back, *Observed nine weeks. Nothing to report.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:235:Nobody had decided how many to read. Karis read records and Brom read orders, and after a while Lira took orders too, so that Brom could rest his hand. The voices went on across the bare cloth in the circle of the lamp, very low. *Reads into. Something of. Second family, faint. Enter. Margin reading not to be entered.* Station after station went by, a mill town and a ferry crossing and a district with a name Cael had once seen on a map, a girl and a boy and a girl, and on every back the narrow slip, the nine weeks, the nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:255:Then she unfolded the second sheet, the one that had been inside the first. It was short. She looked at it, and did not read it, and held it out across the table to Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:19:She was a lean woman of perhaps fifty, in a dark coat with the coast house's colour at the collar, and she rode alongside the second wagon at a walk for a quarter of a mile. She did not look at the kit, or the reserves, or Gault. She did not look at Lira, though Lira's name stood on every Iron sheet on the continent by now. She looked at Cael, and at nothing else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:3:The coach went down the bluff road on the Fifth-day behind Withrow's carriage, in a fine cold rain, and the town watched it go by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:145:It was the petition's second schedule. He had receipted it once already, on the fortieth day, in a stack of nineteen leaves, and had not read it then, because a junior chair receipted leaves and did not read them. Today he had to. The schedule was drawn in the old grammar that declarations used, a list of what the registry held to be so about the person the petition named, one thing to a line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:159:He did not look across the room. He had looked at that boy before, on the pump step and at Greyvane and on the sprung oak at the bluff, and he remembered every one of the looks, and he would not give himself another. He had decided that long ago, and the deciding had held through eleven weeks of Fifth-days in the same long room, forty feet from the house's table. It held this morning. He went down both leaves to the foot and found them alike in everything but the one line. Then he wrote *superseded* in the corner of the first, in the plain hand he kept for corners, with *sixth sitting* under it, and entered the second in the receipt book against the time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:5:He had half expected it not to. He had lain awake for an hour after the tenth bell with the satchel on the sill and the court dark below, and some part of him had believed that the house would look different in daylight, now that he knew what he knew. But the frost was on the flags as it had been yesterday. The fountain went up and came down. The porter's boy was chalking the day's names on the Crown yard's boards, and two Mire second-years were arguing at the refectory door about whose turn it was to fetch the bread, and the coats at the foot of the road changed on the bell. Not one thing on the whole bluff had moved an inch to make room for what was on the shelf in Karis's room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:263:"I'm not reading them. It's asking about them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:305:"I'm the best doorstop on the bluff." Brom took his hand away again, deliberately, and Cael felt the *There?* come up like a hound's head, and Brom put his hand back, and it went down. "There. I can do it all night, and I've done harder work for less."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:5:The card was pinned at the board's head in Rooke's square capitals, as every card was. Under *LIRA* there were three lines, each with a date and a floor and a name. Rooke did not rub them out. He drew one stroke through each, level and unhurried, from the first letter to the last, the way a clerk strikes an entry he means to leave legible, and at the end of each line he wrote two words in the margin. Cael could not read them from the stair. He did not need to. He knew Rooke's two words for a bout not fought, because he had read them in that margin on other fighters' cards, and had never once expected to read them under hers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-34.md:185:He did not read it aloud. He folded it small again and put it in the satchel's pocket with the book, against the book's back board, and buckled the flap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:5:For two months the bluff had been nearly his. There had been the porters and the kitchen and the fire-watch boy, and Bracken in the records hall with his door open, and the four of them and a handful of others who had nowhere better to be. Now the wagons had been coming up the road since noon. Doors banged along the residence front. Somebody below was singing the same three lines of a song over and over, getting the third line wrong each time. The fountain in the court ran on under all of it, as steady as ever, but it was no longer the loudest thing on the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:59:The read came next, Brom's read, the cheapest thing he owned and the one he trusted most; it had never once failed him on a floor. He knelt and laid his palm on the boards and let it go out of him and down. It did not read wood the way it read a man. It read weight. The floor told him where it was carried and where it was not: a joist running under the north wall, sound; one under the middle that had been sistered with new timber a few years back and was proud of it; a soft place by the far door where water had once come in under the sill. The building above him leaned its loads into the walls, slow and patient, the way old buildings did. He took his hand away and the read came back to him clean.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:119:The record was a public thing now, and it was large. Before Norhold he had carried six, and the registry had held five of them; the quiet thing was on no paper anywhere, and the storm did not yet exist. Only three of the five had been shown in public since he came to the bluff, at rates the house had chosen for him: six bursts on the oak, two contacts of the spark, and a read that looked like good timing. The push, where it showed at all, never went past a flat hand. Norhold had changed that for good. On finals day nine judges and three scribes had written down the Wind past six, and the push at a fighting load, and Reydan's give taking a Gold's fire, and the spark past its two contacts, and something none of their columns had a name for. Every one of those was on paper now in the tournament's own hand, and paper did not forget. But the house did not fight on finals day. On the bluff, in front of the wing's panels, he still fought at the rate the house had always shown, and a little under it, and never over it, and he scattered the figures inside that room so that they would breathe like an honest fighter's. He called it the band, and so did Lira. It was the oldest daily expense he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:149:The minute from the bluff was in the front of this book, in his best hand, three lines long: nothing made to happen; only inside a fight somebody else begins; the cost on paper first. And under it, in Karis's wording, with four sets of initials, the line they had added at Norhold after the bill came in. *Never again in the middle of a fight unless what follows has been priced first, at the weight it will come at.* At Norhold nobody had known the weight. Now he knew it. Fifteen feet, a third deep, the ears, the dust. Eleven weeks of everything leaning. That was what Gold cost, on paper, at last.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:175:The intake had been given the back rows, as intakes always were, and it was bigger this year than any he had seen. Every new house on the bluff studied the old one for its rules before it dared to break any. This one had come up the road with a question of its own, and he could tell what the question was by where the heads went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:233:He was at the east end of the hall, by the tall windows, alone, standing with his hands clasped behind his back and his head turned a little to one side, the way a man stands in a doorway at night when he thinks he has heard a step on the stair and is waiting to hear it again. The east windows looked down the bluff road as far as the bend above the trees, and anybody who showed at that bend still had two switchbacks to climb before the lodge-man heard a hoof.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-01.md:247:"Yes." She considered the registrar's back. "And he hasn't told it to anybody, which means it's either not his to tell or not ready to be told." She turned toward the door. "I'll be in my room. I've a lock to get used to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:57:"The lower orchard," said Karis. "You can see the lower orchard from the bluff road's second switchback."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:67:The house's farm lay on the back of the bluff, on the long south slope away from the river, where the wind was less. Below the stable passage were the kitchen gardens in their walls, and below the gardens the upper orchard and the lower, old trees in rough grass with their branches bare and black with wet. Below the lower orchard a stream came down out of a fold in the slope and ran along the bottom hedge toward the water meadows, loud over stones. The farm track went down beside it, and at the orchard's foot, where the track met the stream, stood a small stone cottage with smoke going up from it, which was the gardener's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:43:A quarter of an hour later the clerk had come back up with the dispatch slip and laid it by the crooked press, where slips went, and then he had not gone. He had stood with his hands at his sides looking at the slip, and then at the Archmarshal's left hand open on the blotter, and then at nothing. Vastin had waited, and nothing had come, and after a while the man had nodded and gone out to his own desk. But he had left the inner door a hand's breadth wider than he usually left it, and twice before noon Vastin had heard his chair creak as he turned in it. A clerk who watches is a clerk who has noticed there is something to see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:55:It was in the boy's file in the same drawer, the clerk's ruled page from the room twelve feet by fourteen on the bluff. The first thing his eye went to, as it always did, was not any of his questions but the line near the foot where one was written going the other way, the boy's to him: *What is the Compact evaluating? Me, or the provision?* The second thing was the line beneath his own eleventh question. He had ruled it himself, before the interview, for a twelfth, the way he always ruled one more line than he expected to use. It was empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:71:*Second frame reading: within tolerance.* The note was in a young man's hand, a hand still deciding what shape it meant to be. It was pinned to the copy of a station's Kindling entry, four years old, for a girl in the northern district: her Path, her first rank, in a station clerk's ordinary writing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:73:The junior who had pinned it had come up the too-steep stair that afternoon with the girl's file under his arm, nervous and good, and laid it out on the desk because Vastin had made him lay it out himself. Her floor work this season had run on ahead of what her station had written of her, by a margin he had measured four ways and could not make go away. "She doesn't do anything wrong, sir," the junior had said. "That's what I keep coming back to. She does everything her Path says she should. She just does it as if the station had written down somebody a year younger." He had turned his notes round so that Vastin could read them. "I measured it with the frame, and by the clock, and by her bouts, and against the tables. It's inside tolerance every way I measure it. I still don't like it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:77:Under the station's line, in the junior's hand, was the recommendation they had settled between them: *Continue observation.* The hand was still deciding what shape it meant to be, and seeing it, Vastin had remembered a smoking stove in a district house a long time ago, and his own file under his own arm, and a senior evaluator, long since a name on a plaque, telling him the thing every evaluator is told once: *Write what the instrument says. What you feel about it is information about you.* He had handed the same sentence on that afternoon, in other words. It had been easier to say than to hear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:142:The rest he could have said without the pages. That the proceedings had in their hands already the one honest instrument there was, three years of the record, kept by officers who could see. That a re-evaluation whose answer was known in advance was a procedure wearing an evaluation's coat. That when the standard protocol fell silent in front of this boy the silence was the protocol's own. That the protocol could not read him. Somewhere in the middle was a sentence he had spoken at inquiries for forty years about frames, *the instrument is not at fault; it has told you where its reach ends*, and last night, for the first time, he had written it about a person, and sat with his pen lifted for a long while before he let it stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:144:He had gone home at the ninth bell, later than usual, as he had said he would. The porter had been at the lodge door with his lamp, looking out at the rain, and had said "Late, sir," in the voice of a man noting a thing for the record, and Vastin had agreed that it was. The street had been empty and loud with water. He had walked it without hurrying, with his collar up and his left hand in his coat, and he had found that he was not thinking about the seat, or the five chairs, or who had sat down in them. He was thinking about a boy standing still in a frame that had stuttered and gone dark, three days on the bluff, not moving, not asking what it meant, waiting to be told what the instruments had found. And he was thinking that, of all the officers who had stood in rooms with that boy, he was the one who had written down what that silence meant, and that nobody was going to be allowed to read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:21:He had not read it yet himself; he had only heard it, and he had always known that those were different things. He had sat forty feet from the middle chair with his pen moving, and had written a time, and had kept his eyes on his own page while a voice he had worked under for years said a thing slowly into an empty room. Now he read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:25:The first he had never seen. He had carried the file it answered; he had laid that file at her elbow in a hall on a bluff, and gone back to the foot of the table. A return had gone into it then, and he had not read it, because a records officer did not read what was addressed to the senior seat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:113:He knew the grounds. He had sat forty feet from a table for a year of Fifth-days and had not looked at it. He had looked at that boy before, on a pump step and at Greyvane and on a sprung floor at the bluff; he remembered every one of the looks, and had sworn off another, and had kept to it for a year by looking at his own hands. He was tired in a way that had nothing to do with the hours, and he wished to work in a room where the leaves were only leaves.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:245:Seln did not read it, for he had heard it read. He held it up to the lamp by two corners and looked through it. Then he laid it down and took Bracken's boxwood rule from its nail, and measured something at the sheet's edge, and measured it again from the other side. He turned the leaf over and ran his thumbnail very lightly round the rim of the seal, as Bracken had once done under an arch, and bent close to the last line to look at the hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:279:He had said, in a harness room, that he could not read a registry sheet anymore; that since the case he looked at one and saw only what it was a form of. He was not reading these. He was doing the one honest thing he could still do with such paper, which was to say what it weighed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-55.md:103:It came along the curve of the barrier from the south-east, close under the cap, and it did not run straight. It followed the wall round. He had no word for it. Nobody on the bluff had a word for it, because Karis had drawn every lane on her wall as a straight line, and every lane he had ever seen had been one. This one curved like a river round a meadow, and he heard it curve, and only just got off the end of it before it reached him. He came out of it with his heart going and his back against the north mast's cable, and he thought, quite clearly, *She has never shown that to anybody.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-55.md:189:He felt a thought begin to form about it. He knew what the thought was going to say, and he took his attention off it before it could say it, the way you take your hand off a door you are not ready to open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:7:The paper itself was a day gone. It had ridden down the bluff road in a saddlebag yesterday morning, after a minute and forty seconds at the lodge window in front of one man with a glass, and whatever it had to say about itself it had said then, to him, and to nobody since. Seln had worked that out at about the fire-watch's second round, sitting up in the wing with the senior clerk's fair copy under the lamp and getting nothing from it but the words, which was all a fair copy on the house's own stock could ever give.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:71:It was the rest day, and the rain had decided to stay. The bluff road shone. At the second switchback he met a porter coming up with a sack over his head like a hood, who wished him good morning and did not look at him twice. That was the whole art of the trade. A man going down a hill in the rain with a satchel and an errand was not anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:101:The seat wanted the maker of the copies to swear to them, and the advocate of record to sign beside his oath. Karis had said at supper that she would not put her name to a page she had not read against the thing it was copied from, and that this was not caution but manners. So the wing's original cards had come up the library stair in two shallow boxes under the porter's oilcloth, with Seln's leave and Bracken's receipt, and the second set of Seln's copies lay open beside them. He had made two of everything. One had gone down to the seat in the house's boards. The other had stayed in the annex, against exactly this.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:129:And there was a fourth sort, which he had met perhaps three times in all his years, and had not liked on any of them. It was the silence that falls when you have been taken off a list. Nobody above him had stopped caring about the boy. They had only stopped caring what Vastin thought. His evaluations were going up the stair as truthfully as ever, and nobody at the top of it was reading them, because whatever was going to be done was going to be done on other papers than his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:49:*Every person who has ever decided a thing about me had a face, and I could read it. Pellin at Denvash, with her brass plate. Coss across a desk. Vastin with his frames. The provost who was frightened and couldn't hide it. Umber polishing his spectacles over a sheet he couldn't score. I read every one of them from across a room, and every reading was worth something.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:87:The conference was not in the hall. It was up a short stair at the back, in what had been the wool-factors' counting room and was now a district office: a long table, eight chairs, the registry's plain sigil painted over the hearth, and a window at the far end that looked straight down the market street to the wharfs, and over the river to the bluff, with the house along its top.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:281:The records hall stood across the court from the wall, its long roof and its row of tall windows facing east toward the valley. In the highest window, the one at the north end that lit the stair to Bracken's rooms, a man was standing. He had a small brass glass in his hand, the kind a ship's officer carries, and every so often he lifted it and looked through it down the valley, toward the place where the bluff road came up out of the trees. Then he lowered it, and stood, and lifted it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-04.md:289:"Good morning." Cael stood beside him and looked down the valley. From up here you could see the whole of the bluff road's last two switchbacks, and the bend below them, and a long stretch of the river road beyond the bridge, all of it pale and empty in the early sun. "You were at the lecture range's windows on First-day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:83:The lake house came up the bluff road on the Second-day in a hired wagon with a blue-grey cloth over its hoops, and half the hill was at the north wall to watch it climb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:3:They came back up the bluff the way they had gone down it, by five ways, and nobody on the road could have said they had ever been together.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:7:He walked up the bluff road behind a carter's boy leading a mule with three trunks on its back, and let the mule set the pace.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:13:It took them until the light was going, a slow line down the switchbacks with the trunks swaying and the wheels loud on the turns, and at the foot of the road the coats stepped back against the chandler's wall to let them by. On the river the ferry made its crossing, and came back empty, and crossed again full. Four hundred and thirty enrollees lived on the bluff, and by dusk forty-one of them were left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:19:It was the plainest reason of the four. Since the year's eighth day the registry had had a petition before its seat in the town below, asking that he be stood in a frame and read again. If the frame could not read him, the code had a chapter for that: his enrollment struck out, and after it, a letter at a time, everything else with his name on it. And a respondent who left the hill while the thing was pending would be entered as fled, with a note on three friends' files to say whose friends they were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:119:There was nothing in it, and that was what Hesk's letters were for: a buckle and a captain and a dog asleep across a door, and the stove, and something green, and not one word about registries or polite men or what anybody might be building. Hesk wrote from his bench and stayed at the height of his bench, and the letter came up the bluff like a smell of shavings and reminded Cael that there was a whole country in which people's dogs slept on steps.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:177:"Because of the coastal man," said Cael. "The one who wrote his letter and went home. The default quoted him. His own words, in the second paragraph, as the reason." He looked at Karis, because she had read it to them in the dairy. "If I stop going, they don't have to prove anything. I prove it for them. I walk out of the room and they write down that I walked, and that's their whole case, signed by me." He shook his head. "I'm not going to hand them that. If they want me in a frame, they'll have to put me in it with their own hands, in their own minute, with Karis's closing standing in front of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:219:The road column was the last column on the page, kept since the year of the thieves on the order of the chancellor before last, one line at every bell, for whatever came up the bluff road or went down it. In term it ran to three or four entries a bell, and this week it ran to one or none. *Carter, flour. Post, the bag. None. None. The registrar's man, to town and back. None.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:231:"The same quiet. Read again." He closed the book and gave it back to the lodge-man through his window with a word of thanks. Then he walked with Cael out under the arch and along the outer wall, where the wind came up the bluff off the river, and spoke to the wall rather than to him. "They have not gone. They have stood down. There is a difference. A man who has gone has decided he was wrong. A man who has stood down has decided he does not need to do anything, because what he wants is coming to him by itself." He walked a little way. "The corridor is on schedule. Every sitting sits. Every form is filed. Nobody on this hill has put a foot wrong, and so nobody has to come up the road and make one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:9:They were as he had squared them at dusk. Cael had read their heading from the covered walk, with his arm new in its binding, and had thought then that four sheets of paper was a small thing for a year to come to; and he thought so still. A challenge had come up the bluff road under escort, with outriders, and been read aloud standing, and the answer to it had weighed eleven pounds four ounces. This would not have tipped the post-room's beam.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:157:"There is one line more on the council's minute," he said, and he took up the sheet again. "*The enrollee's tier, as certified by the registry: Ember; Iron, Rank Three.* I have not read it to you, and I have struck it from this schedule, in ink, with my initials against the stroke, because it is not the house's. It was never the house's to grant, and it does not lapse with a room." He looked at her over the glasses. "Nobody can take a tier from you, Advocate. They can only decline to count it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:91:"Is borrowed." Seln very nearly smiled. "A records administrator's, on the third floor of a leasehold building four streets back from the registry seat. He is on circuit in the hill stations for six weeks. His landlord's keeper lends the room by the morning or the afternoon to anybody with a reason and a deposit, and writes the reason in a book, and does not read it twice. My reason is a reading." He looked at the note. "I have used that kind of room before, but never that one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:97:He took the note down the bluff road himself on the Fifth-day, in the house's ordinary post, with a dozen of the wing's letters, and left it at the inn on the wharf with the inn's boy in an envelope addressed in a copying hand to *the officer of the river inspection*. The coats at the foot of the road saw a clerk with the post. They had seen him with the post every week for two years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:103:Lira went with the program's carriers on the market cart at the first bell, because Rooke's program bought its tape and chalk at the chandler's by the bridge and somebody had to carry it, and she was not allowed to carry anything heavier. Brom walked, by the long road round the bluff, which took an hour and was the only exercise his shoulder would let him take. Karis took the house's errand to the fee office with a letter of Bracken's in her wallet. Seln went with the post. Cael went last, alone, by the ferry, because it was the way he always went when he went at all. He sat on the bench by the rail and let the coats at the landing look at him as they had looked at him for two years, and nobody wrote down anything but the hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:155:Vastin read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:161:At the twenty-third, Vastin found the letter. He unfolded the father's sheet, and read it, and unfolded the clerk's four lines inside it, and read those. Then he read the father's again from the top. He folded them both along their old folds, exactly as Lira had folded them, and slid them back under the clip, and laid the pair face down on the others. He sat with his hand on top of the face-down stack. Then he took the twenty-fourth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-40.md:261:It had lain there, under three other things, since the third evening of the junior's visit. A station's Kindling entry, four years old, for a girl in the northern district, her Path and her first rank in a station clerk's ordinary writing. The junior's notes beside it, in a hand still deciding what shape it meant to be: the frame, the clock, the bouts, the tables. The two recommendations they had settled between them. *Second frame reading: within tolerance.* And under the station's line, *Continue observation.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:5:He did it with the stub of pencil from behind his ear, standing in his own doorway, while the last of the four horses was still going down the bluff road below the gate, because his book was on the counter behind him and he did not want to lose the minute in the turning round. Cael saw him do it from the wall stair, coming down. Then the old man went in, and took the book off the counter, and copied the figure off his hand into it, and licked his thumb and rubbed the hand clean.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:215:He did not say which sentence. He did not need to. Every one of them had carried it home from Norhold and up this hill, and Cael heard it again now in the Archmarshal's dry voice, among forty clocks: *Because the people who want this to stop are not going to use official channels much longer.* The sentence no longer ended in the clock room. In Cael's mind it went on down the bluff road, past the arch Withrow had held that morning, toward doors where no chancellor would be waiting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:5:He was a young man in a wet hat, and he apologised three times on the stair before he got to the door, and twice more inside it. The district seat's records room was a man short, he said. Two men, if you counted the one with the fever. A letter had come in from the registrar of the academy on the bluff, about an order and a seal, and it concerned the proceedings, and the senior records officer had said that the proceedings' own records support was in the town on its days off with nothing to do, and would the junior chair be so good as to check the registers and draft the usual.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:15:The letter said where the rubbing had come from, in its first paragraph, with the instrument quoted whole: it was the seal off the escort order that the leader of four riders had read aloud in the house's outer court on the Sixth-day morning, and that the house had declined at its own gate and let ride away again down the bluff road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:93:The coach went down the bluff road in the dark with its lamps lit and the rain gone east in the night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:175:Cael counted the cut out of the window as they went, because he counted everything. Four hundred and forty yards, near enough, by the coachman's pace and the bends at each end. At the far end, the east end, the road narrowed to cross a culvert where a stream came down a gully off the hill and went under the road toward the river, with a low stone parapet on either side. Beside the near parapet stood a milestone. He could not read it from that distance. It would say seven.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:191:It went out of him through the hand into the ground the way it went into a floor, the cheapest thing he owned and the one he trusted most. It did not read chalk the way it read a man. It read weight. The road under him was carrying the coach, four tons of it through the wheels, the horses in front, the cart's dead weight across the crown forty yards on. All of that was ordinary, and he knew the shape of ordinary.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:11:He walked her to the door, because he had nothing else on the hill to do, and it was a strange thing to find out about himself at the third bell of a working day. For two years he had always been due somewhere: at a trestle in wing three, or a table at the top of the library, or the north wall at the changing of the coats. Now his name stood in a book with five words against it, and nobody on the bluff was expecting him anywhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:121:"The office mentions one thing," said Seln to the sheet in front of him. "There is a district officer on the bluff road. I saw him from the wing's end window at the half bell. He is a stout man and he rides like one, and he will be an hour yet."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:251:"There'll be a second-year wants it. There always is." Ephram looked at the cart, and at the horse, and last at Cael. He lifted his left hand a little way from his side and let it drop, and it was all he did, for they had said the rest at a board, with a ladder between them. He turned and went back into the court, and this time he did not whistle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:305:They went down the bluff road at dusk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:331:The woman who kept the guesting-house asked no questions. She had come up the bluff with a basket the day before, and she had seen what there was to see; she gave them the parlour with the two-paned door, and they put the cart in her yard, under the pentice, beside the old screw press.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:113:The coach went down the market street, and over the bridge, and the coats at the foot of the bluff road watched it come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:229:At the foot of the bluff road, small and yellow, the coats had their two lanterns lit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:27:She had come up the bluff road at the ninth bell in a hired chaise from Ostrand, with one bag, and Cael had never seen her before. The inspection had brought a counsel up the hill once, a woman who talked by every account from the bridge to the gate and signed for her chests with a face like a cat in a bath; she had been the Compact's. This was the house's own. In eleven years nothing she had advised the house had ever carried her name; that was her price, and the house had paid it gladly. People on the bluff called her counsel and nothing else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:55:For two years his whole place on the bluff had lived because somebody kept writing it down: a stamp, a renewal, a line in the enrollment book in Bracken's upright hand, every one of them a thing an officer was obliged to do on a day the code named. The provision was alive because it had dates in it. Part two had none. It was the same machine run backward, a thing that stayed done because nobody was ever obliged to undo it. Cael realized he had never once thought of a date as a kindness, and that it was the kindest thing on any form he had ever signed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:123:The counsel took the second, in the voice of somebody reading out tomorrow's weather.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-06.md:139:She did not read it out. She turned the instrument round on the table so that it faced the rest of them, put her finger on the paragraph, and took the finger away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:274:They came home the short way, over Ostrand bridge and along the river street and up the bluff road, as Withrow had told them to, with the lamps lit and the river still loud and brown beside them but lower, much lower, with a long tide-line of sticks and froth on the stones where it had been. At the ferry landing the two coats stood in their places in the last of the light. One of them watched the coach go by. He was not looking at Cael. He was looking at Lira's strapped boot, which she had up on the seat by the window, and he went on looking at it until the coach had passed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:25:"You are the frame." The counsel's voice did not change at all. "And a frame that will not read true comes off the floor."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:27:Cael sat with that. All his life frames had been the things that failed to read him, and he had stood in front of them and been the question they could not answer. Now the paper had turned it round. He was the instrument on the bench, and they meant to open his casing and look for the seam that let the damp in, as Bracken's men had done with the station in Ostrand. He was not afraid of their looking. He was afraid of what they would write when they found no seam at all, because a frame with nothing wrong in it that still would not read was not a thing the second volume had a section for. It was a thing chapter fourteen had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:77:"It's expulsion that follows you," said Withrow. "You can walk out of a town. You can't walk out of the post. It's waiting at the next town, and the one after, and nobody is obliged ever to begin the review that would stop it." She took the brown book from under her arm and held it in both hands. "Twice in my registry years I put my name to one. The first I'd sign again tomorrow; he had done worse than the registry ever learned. The second had a daughter." She looked at the book and not at them. "She came to my counter every week for a year to ask whether the review had begun. Every week I told her the truth, which was that nobody had begun it and nothing in the code obliged anybody to. And every week she thanked me, and went home." A pause. "I have never forgotten her name. Put that down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:9:It was not a request for approval. Karis had been plain about that, over the bread. A document about his enrollment, going out of the house under the house's seal, that he had not read from the first word to the last, was a kind of thing that neither of them would allow into the world. So she would draft, and he would read, and he would say nothing at all unless something in it was untrue.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:21:"Everything I put under it made it weaker." She was writing the second page and did not look up. "Anyone who reads that line and doesn't understand it won't understand it any better with me standing next to it explaining."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:35:Cael heard her before he saw her. Nobody on the bluff had ever heard Karis run; she walked everywhere at the same even pace, as though the distance between two places were a sum she had already done. But there were feet on the residence stair taking it two at a time, and a door banging on the floor below, and Brom's voice on the landing saying "What—" in the tone of a man flattening himself against a wall. Then she was in Cael's doorway with Bracken's transcription held against her chest in both arms and her hair come half out of its pins.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:89:He did it standing, with his spectacles on the end of his nose and the three charter copies open in front of him, and he did not read the pages as arguments. He read them as a list of claims about where things were. Every time a page said *sixth part* he turned to the sixth part. Every time it said *third schedule* he turned to the third schedule and laid his finger on the line and read the line aloud, in a low voice, and then read the page's quotation of it, and then said "Yes," and moved on. When a page gave a year he asked Karis where the year came from, and she told him, and he went to the shelf and found the volume and checked it. He took an hour and three-quarters over four sheets of foolscap. He found no error.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:119:A long desk bare of everything but the work in hand. A wall of grey file cases, each labelled in the same hand. Above the cases, framed and glazed and hung at the height of a person's eyes, the house's own charter, the founding one, its ink gone brown, hung there not as an ornament but where she could point at it. One window looked down the bluff to the river. The lamp on the desk was already lit, and the folder with the four pages lay beside it, open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:145:Cael sat in the chair with his hands on his knees and found he had nothing ready to say. He had expected, he realized, to be told he was valued, or to be told he was a risk, and he had readied himself for either. He had not readied himself to be told that he came last in the order of things, after a house and a set of rules and the people who kept them, and that this was the reason the thing would stand. It was not a flattering account of himself, and it had been given to his face. It was also, he thought, the safest place in the world to be standing: at the end of a line of other people's honest work, where nobody could pull him out without pulling all of it down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:193:The response had gone down the bluff on the morning courier of the Sixth-day. After that nothing came back up, and the regional registrar did not write. The memorandum's fourteen days went on running down on the calendar behind Bracken's counter, one day a morning, each crossed off in his small neat stroke. In its turn the fourteenth was crossed off like the rest, and nothing went back down the hill to meet it. The house had answered once, in four pages, and Bracken did not resubmit; a true record, he told Brom when Brom asked, did not get any truer for being sent twice. The coats at the ferry landing and the road's foot changed on the bell, two and two, and Karis's marbled book got a line a day, and every line was the line above it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:201:The floor, at least, did not wait for anybody. On the Fourth-day Ephram came to the second session with his shoulders held so still that he looked like a man carrying a full tray across a crowded room. He threw the second-year entry at Cael twenty times in an hour, and Cael beat the second weight twelve of them by the heel alone, because there was no longer any shoulder to read. On the ninth throw he ate the plain entry clean across the ribs and let it pass. On the fourteenth Ephram's shoulders dropped again from tiredness, and Cael called it aloud before the heel had moved, and Ephram muttered something at his own shoulders and went back to his chalk. Rooke's board, at the end, said only *Better. Both.* Cael read it upside down on his way to the water jug, and carried it back up to the residence with him like a coin in his pocket, and turned it over at supper when the sum came back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:37:Rooke went down the bluff road at a little before dusk with a satchel on his shoulder, and Cael watched him from the north wall because watching Rooke walk anywhere was unusual; he had business in the town on Third-day evenings, and nobody had ever asked what it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:63:"Eleven seconds is a long time for a reading," said Karis. "Every other reading on the continent is done before you've finished a breath. I think the function had his reading in front of it for eleven seconds and couldn't find a column to put it in, any column, and couldn't take the margin off because it was all margin. So it did what a good form does when it can't be filled in: it failed safe, and wrote the one word it had for a thing that couldn't be written. Then it marked the file, so that whoever came after would know there was something there it hadn't been able to correct." She laid the slip down. "The mark has no office behind it because no office made it. It made itself, the way a stamp comes down when a lever's pulled. And Ilsev sent it up, at the hearing, twice, to every height she could reach, and asked who'd authorized it. Something answered her each time, something that wasn't anybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:137:She did not read it from the slip. She said it to the room, slowly, in the voice she kept for the last line of a finding, the line she would be held to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:71:Then his ears closed and opened, and he knew. Something large in him had lifted its head: the great slow weight that had lain quiet since the dawn on the second floor, raising itself to look at whatever had just come into the house. The air in the room changed its weight, not much, the way air does before thunder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:59:The name was found in the drawer. It had nineteen postings against it, in fifteen years, and the last was a chartered house on a bluff above a river. From that house a return had come up every week for two years, on the same day, folded in the same way, with the subject's reference on the outside fold. The desk turned to the last of them, which was dated eight days before the query.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:99:He did not read them. There was nothing in them to read; he had seen to that when he wrote them. He untied each tape, and laid the leaves in by the handful, and waited until they had gone grey before he put in the next. A river town went first, where he had learned to fold a return. Then a guild hall, and a district seat in an old building, which he fed in rather quickly. There was an academy with four subjects, and a station in the hills with a green corridor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:129:For two years he had fetched the house's pouch across that bridge, at the end of a day, with the same satchel on the same shoulder. He nodded to the ward's lantern and was nodded to, and went over the middle arch above the brown water, and nobody would think of it again. The two coats were at the bluff road's foot and the two at the landing, where they had been set to stand, and they had not been set to watch a clerk go for the post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:159:The valley was a darkness with a lighter band in it, which was the river. Beyond the river the bluff stood up, blacker than the sky, and he found the house by its shape. It had no lights, for it was past midnight, and four hundred people were asleep in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:103:"No. That's why you lost." He said it without any heat. "From tomorrow, every count ends on the right. Every one. I don't care where the line wants to go. You'll land on that foot until you've stopped asking it for leave, and then you'll land on it a hundred times more." He went back to the trestle and bent over the red book, and wrote one word under her name, and Cael, from the door, could not read it and knew what it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:77:The counsel took the card out of her sleeve. She did not read from it. She held it at her side, face in, and said it from memory, and Cael found that he knew it too as she said it, though he had never once been allowed to read it; he knew it the way you know a tune that has been coming through a wall at you for weeks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:153:Cael saw it from the covered walk, a little after the second bell on the Sixth-day morning, on his way across to the wing. It was a small thing. In two years he had never once seen that window shut in daylight. The lodge-man sat behind it on his high stool from the first bell to the last, with his tea and the bluff road in front of him, and talked to anybody who came up it. Now the shutter came across the glass from the inside, and a moment later the lodge door opened, and the lodge-man stood in it without his hat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:19:So he sat down at the window and waited, and after the second bell Karis lifted her head off her arms, looked at the dead lamp, looked at the stack, and said in a perfectly ordinary voice, "Ninety-one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:131:He read it at his counter, every leaf, from the address to the last schedule. The oil book took a third line and a fourth. He did not skim. Cael came past the records-hall windows late on the first night on his way back from the north wall, and saw him through the glass, a narrow grey shape bent over a single lamp, turning a leaf, reading it, initialling its corner, turning the next. A registrar's initials on a leaf were a promise about the leaf. Bracken did not make promises he had not read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:143:That was why the chain had come first. The figure was right. Ephram's chart was right. The four strangers' boards were right. It was all exactly right. And it was all going to travel down the bluff road tonight in four satchels to four houses, and be laid beside four bound books, and agree with them; and Rooke, who did not explain anything, had looked at the gallery for the whole bout and then gone and found a chain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:199:Cael read the third sentence first, without meaning to, and then went back for the other two, and thought of four satchels going down the bluff road that afternoon with a figure in each of them that was right.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:229:It was the house's exhibits, all of them, read into the minute by the seat's clerk in the order the counsel had asked, each with its number, title, date, where it came from and its line of receipt. The clerk had one pace and kept it for everything. A short item, eight or nine seconds; one with a certifying officer's line to recite, twenty or a little more. Bracken's index to the enrollment file, with Bracken's name in its certifying line: fourteen strokes' worth. The Greyvane ruling, the hearing that had kept Cael on his feet in a city he had never wanted to see: twenty-one. Karis's renewals table, rebuilt in a single silent hour with two dates on every row: eleven. The integrity section, forty pages, a night without sleep, ninety-one invocations each traced by hand to its own page: eight seconds. Its title had taken longer than its reading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:67:He could not help it. Under every seal there was a line of them, three or four to an issue, and he went down them the way he went down the names on a bout card, by habit, and without meaning to he had counted. Nineteen issues and seventy-one names, give or take two he could not read in the yellow ones where the ink had gone brown and spread. Officers of the registry, by the titles after them: a chief clerk of entry, a keeper of the rolls, a second secretary, men and women whose offices Bracken would have known and Cael had never heard of. Not one of them appeared on two issues: ten years at a desk, and then a new name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:165:"A corridor," he said, "in a district station, a year on, with green distemper on the walls to the height of a man's shoulder, and benches. I was waiting for a file. A keeper came out of the frame room with a mother and a girl, and he was pleased, the way they are when a reading is a good one, and I heard him tell the mother that her girl read strong, and read into a second family as well, and he had not seen it in years." He looked at the father's letter, folded under its clip in the third row. "A week later I was back for another file, and the station's out-tray was on the counter, and her certificate was in it, waiting to go. One family, first rank." He took his hand off the table. "That was when the paper turned into children. It had been only paper for a year."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-30.md:269:Lira found hers inside a minute. She read her pool from the top line down: an Ash Path fighter from a southern house in the first round, a middle seed, and a second round that would want looking at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-30.md:271:Then her eyes went on down, past the quarterfinal, to the line she had worked out on the back of her hand on the bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-23.md:29:It was the kind of card she used for questions she meant a seat to hear. It had one line on it. Cael could read most things upside down by now, and he could not read this, because she had written it very small and kept the heel of her hand across it. He watched her strike one word in the line and write another above it. Then she struck that one too. She changed the same word four times, while the coach went the whole length of the meadows, and the fourth time she left it. She saw him watching. She did not hide the card. She turned it face down on the board and went back to the draft.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-23.md:93:The door gave onto a stone apron at the very point of the tongue, with a low wall round it and the water on both sides. In the dark the two rivers were only sound. One came up from the south, broad and steady, the one that ran under the bluff at home. The other came down out of the east, bigger and colder and quicker, and where they met at the apron's point there was a long ragged noise like cloth tearing that went on and on and never finished.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:13:The stranger was a Stone, thick through the shoulders and slow in the feet, and he stood at the near end of the painted lines with his hands folded in front of him like a man waiting outside a shop. Cael had seen him last at the wool town, on the far side of a rope, losing to Lira. He had been an Iron of the river academy then, its first entry, and she had beaten him by finding the half a beat in which neither of his planted feet was reading the floor, and going through it. Now he was a Silver, newly advanced, and he had sewn nothing on his collar to say so. Over the recess, Lira had said, he had written to Rooke and asked for the posting, and brought his academy's drill-book with him in a red board cover so that the bluff could copy it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:21:It began with an entry, an ordinary forward step that any fighter on the hill would have recognised. Then a second step that looked like the first and was not: the weight went onto the front foot, and stayed there one count too long, so that a man watching for the burst would see it coming and set himself. Then a third count in which nothing happened at all. Then, on the fourth, the burst, from the wrong foot, at an angle the first three counts had sworn it would never take.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:125:"Old books first," said Karis to the spine, as if he had asked. "The founding text. The first consolidation. The second. Each one until I can say what it says without looking, and nothing newer on the table until then. I made the bargain with the only person I can't talk round." She took her finger away. "A note for the cold term."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:169:The north wall ran along the edge of the bluff above the river, waist-high, broad enough to sit on, with the whole valley open below it. He had counted the coats from here for two years, and from windows and wagon benches before that, ever since Greyvane. He knew every place on the wall where the angle was good. That evening he took the one by the third buttress, where a crack in the coping held water after rain and nobody else liked to sit, and waited for the bell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:171:The heat of the day was going off the stone. Below him the bluff road went down in its switchbacks to the river street. Across the brown water, the ferry landing stood at the end of its causeway with the ferry tied up beside it for the night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:179:One of the pair at the road's foot was grey in the beard and leaned on a stick he did not seem to need. The other kept his hands in his coat and looked at the river. Neither had raised his eyes to the bluff since the change. The younger coats of other years had craned at the walls like visitors to a cathedral, and shifted their feet a little more each week as the place came clear to them. These two stood as if there were nothing on the hill they had not already been told about. They had the patience of men near the end of a long errand, who know the one thing they have been sent to see and do not mean to miss it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-15.md:379:They stopped there. They stopped for exactly as long as her round allowed for anything worth a second look, which was perhaps two heartbeats.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:95:Nobody said a word about it, then or at any board between that one and the bluff, and nobody ever would; but every night, at every board, every one of them counted him. They did it the way you count heads at the door at the end of a long day, to be sure that everybody you care about has come in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:157:On the eleventh day, at a waystation where the coast road came in from the south, the coach from the coast pulled into the yard with its horses steaming while they were eating, and stood there blowing while the coachman got down. The coachman put his head in at the door and asked if there was anybody here from the bluff, and held up a letter. Cael knew the hand from across the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:169:There were two of them, at the far end of a bridge in a market town, standing by a hired horse where the road came off the bridge into the square. Grey coats and good boots. Cael knew both faces. He had counted them for two years from the bluff's walls and windows and the ferry steps. He counted them now from the wagon's bench, and was surprised to find that he was very nearly glad of them. After a city of eight thousand who had all been looking at him at once, two men and a horse were almost homely.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:181:Ephram spent the last three days working out how to tell it on the bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:185:"We went down there and they counted us, and the count's on a staff in the middle of the continent where anyone can read it. And one of us they couldn't count." He considered it. "There's a longer version. It comes with my kit, up the bluff, carried by whoever asked for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-60.md:193:First the river, wide and slow and going copper in the late light; then the roofs of Ostrand, slate and brown, coming up out of the valley along it; and last, above them all, the bluff, with its one long roofline holding the sun after everything below had lost it. The wagons went up the climb with nobody saying much, because it was the last mile of a long year, and every one of them had found that it was enough just to be riding it, together, with the light going off the water behind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:33:The house on the bluff answered first, in a registrar's upright hand. No enrollment stood, and no sponsorship; no floor hours were held, and no travel paper had been issued under the house's seal that had not lapsed with the form. The district seat answered that it kept nothing in that name but the file. The archive at Ternhall returned that the privileges in question had been surrendered at a counter, by their holder, before the instrument was dated. A provincial registry returned that the practitioner named as an associate stood at Copper, as he had for years; it added that a finding in his favour lay unsigned, and that the registry could not restrict a rank which nobody had taken up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:55:In the week after the close a junior clerk went up a bluff road with a stamp. He wrote in the right-hand column of a charter folder that the respondent institution had been found compliant. The chancellor gave him tea, and he said afterward that she had been perfectly civil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:113:None of that had happened yet. It was only the First-day, the morning a desk clerk on the bluff found four keys in a row, and they left Ostrand at first light, by the east road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:5:It was the only use he could find for the quarter hour before the seat came in. The coach had gone down the bluff road at the usual pace behind Withrow's carriage, and the two coats at the road's foot had looked at it as they looked at it every Fifth-day, and the young officer at the side door had written the same two lines in his log. But the exchange's four steps had been crowded to the plinth this morning, and the benches behind Cael were filling with a noise he had not heard in that room since the preliminary. A closing, the house's counsel had said, was the one day besides the first when a proceeding sold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:171:She did not read them. He had thought she would. She laid the thirty slips face up along the edge of Rooke's table in three rows, in their order, and looked at them once, and then she turned her back on them and spoke to a horse blanket.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:15:There was a note propped against the water jug a little way along the table. It was not addressed to him, and he did not read it. He did not need to. He had heard Lira on the stair the evening before, telling Brom about a cookshop with a blue door past the third wharf, and he knew the hour she had chosen, because she had chosen it at the top of her voice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:109:He crossed the shop to the long table. He put the parcel down in front of Cael, and laid a card and an envelope on top of it, and said, "The pouch. This morning's," in exactly the voice he used across the counter at home on the bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:243:"The office has signed for a good many parcels since the bluff," he said. "Kit. Floor declarations. Two crates of the chancellor's papers and one of Gault's instruments, which he would not let out of his sight on the coach. The office does not as a rule mind handing parcels over." He looked at the book on the table in front of Cael, and then, for a moment, at Cael. "That is the first one it has been sorry to hand over."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:163:Withrow did not read the letter. She asked him to read it to her, all of it, and sat at her desk with her pen in her hand while he did, as if she meant to take a note.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:207:Cael watched it from the north wall, because there was nothing else he could usefully do. The ferry came across low in the water and went back empty and came again. The bluff road had people on every switchback: wool-factors in their good coats, and carters, and a file of district clerks who had found that they had business on the hill. The man who had bought a second press was there, and so was the woman who kept the guesting-house, with a basket. The lake house's blue-grey wagon had not gone home after the ruling, and it came up at a walk with a lath in a canvas sleeve sticking out of the back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:309:It was not a flat hand, which was all he had ever shown on the bluff. This was the push at fighting load, the whole shove of it, through his palm and down, and the air over a square yard of floor went solid and then gave. Ephram's front heel came down on a board that had jumped, and the first beat of his cut landed crooked. A Blade could not take it back. The hip followed the crooked heel, and the edge followed the hip, wide, a yard from anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:337:Ephram did not know about the ten counts; nobody on the bluff did but three people on a tier. But he was the best Iron on the hill and he could see a man who had gone a beat late, and he came in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-31.md:343:In the second exchange she stopped looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:5:The waystation sat at the foot of the hill country, where the road came down off the last of the slate and ran out level across a country of hedges and frozen ditches. Behind it the hills stood up grey in the first light. They had been two days coming down out of them. Ahead, by Rooke's reckoning, lay another week of flat road and then the bluff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:159:They came up the bluff road on the Third-day evening in a thin dry snow that did not settle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:161:The coats were on the bell. Two stood at the ferry landing when the wagons crossed, stamping on the boards, and two more at the foot of the bluff road in the lee of the wall. Cael counted them out of habit. Karis wrote the time in the marbled log without looking at them, and nobody else looked at them either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:205:The confluence was two days north of Ostrand, at the place where the river that ran under the bluff met a bigger one coming down out of the east.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:207:The wagons left on the Second-day with a hired carriage behind them for Withrow and Bracken. The carriage was black and narrow and had been, the wagoner said, a magistrate's once. Withrow sat in it with a rug over her knees and a book open and did not read it. Bracken sat opposite with a box of papers on his knees and read every one of them twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:109:Then the protocols were read, and then they were read again. Every bout above Silver had them read twice, Seln had said, so that nobody could claim afterward not to have heard. The referee and the panel each held the power to stop the bout, either without the other, and nobody could appeal it. There were two healers at each fighters' gate. If fire went where nobody meant it to go, the masts would take it, and the cables would carry it down into the trench, and the trench would carry it off toward the east break, out of the bowl. On the second reading Cael listened for the one thing he had marked on the first. Every line of it was measured in tiers. Every threshold in it, for when a healer should stand and when a bout should stop, was set against a fighter's tier. There was one fighter in the bout to whom none of it could be set.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:11:He had read it before. He had read it at Ardenmere, before it was a file, when it was a form with four questions on it and a boy on a pump step. He had read it at Greyvane across the end of a hearing table, and on the bluff with the rain coming down on the canvas. Now it had a season grown onto it: four meets, thirteen exhibitions, a column of figures, a house first in its region. It was thicker than any file on the boy had ever been, and in the administrative layer underneath it, where nobody but a records officer ever looked, the same mark stood where it had always stood, as plain and as orphaned as on the day he had first found it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:37:He did not write this one down. There was no fifth entry. The rule of the notebook was that it held what had happened and nothing of what he made of it. He had broken that rule once, on the bluff, and did not mean to make a habit of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:173:"I'm not reading them." Brom did not take his eyes off the pages. "I've read them. I read them at the quarry town with you at my shoulder, and again on the road, and again at the confluence. I know what they say." He nodded at the wall. "I'm letting them look at me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:21:He had seen perhaps five such paragraphs in forty years. It asked for nothing. It offered compliance and requested, if compliance should require a true record to be made false, that the instruction come in writing over a name. A document that asked for nothing was a document whose author had already counted every door out of the room and shut each one, gently, without locking it. He did not know whose paragraph it was. He thought he might guess. A registrar at the top of a bluff above a river had kept an index that his own counsel had wished to find fault with and had not, and he had written to that registrar privately afterward, in four sentences, because good work should be told it is good. This paragraph had the same bones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:71:On the Fifth-day the answer came up the bluff, and it did not look like an answer at all. It looked like a timetable.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-51.md:69:The roar, when the five of them came out, was a roar for plain colours, and Cael could hear the difference. Somewhere in the last fortnight the port had decided they were its own. It had not asked them. A city did not ask. It had simply begun to say *the bluff* the way it said *the harbour*, and it had come out on a dull afternoon, with its bedsheets, to make sure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-51.md:235:Cael stood in the line with the other four round him and did not move. Eighteen years the house on the bluff had been missing from the lists, and nobody who now taught there had ever stood on this floor in its colours. The whole year had just gone into the office's book in a stranger's voice, line by line, initialled, and there was no clerk anywhere who could reach in and take a line of it out again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:3:The circular came up the bluff road on the morning courier, two days after the wagons got home, and Rooke chalked it onto the cohort board in the covered walk before the second bell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:267:The wagons went down to the ferry at first light on the Seventh-day, and the two coats at the landing watched them go, as two coats had watched everything go from that landing for longer than anybody on the bluff liked to count.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:21:*The push I took from the Bronze at Ardenmere. Flat on two plates on the bluff and one in a felt bag at the mill town. Four blows a sitting, paid in the right shoulder. Not asked for since the mill town's plate.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:71:"No price on the fifth man of the bluff! None! The house won't quote him! Come and see the thing we can't price!"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:97:"Filed," she said. "Go to bed. All of you. Rooke wants you at the second bell." She looked at Cael. "And happy birthday."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:149:He did not read it. He turned it over and laid it down again, blank side up, and rested his palm on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:207:"Within the hour of opening," said Bracken. "I'm told the Chief Adjudicator read the rule aloud once and approved it without a hearing." He sat down, which he rarely did at their table, and took a piece of bread, which he more rarely did. "I'm also told he was relieved. The trial office wants it known that it was not relieved, being an office, but that he was." He buttered the bread with great precision. "I don't mind telling you I was relieved myself. I've been sleeping badly on that clause for a year. Last night I slept like a quarry stone." He considered. "That's twice since the bluff. I may be getting used to it."

