   201	"There's talk of a sitting."
   202	
   203	"There will be one, after the houses have gone home. I'm called to it, and I'll go." The ghost of something went across his face. "I can't tell you what's on its paper. You won't need me to. You were there yesterday when it was written."
   204	
   205	Cael asked the next thing slowly. He knew which question it was.
   206	
   207	"The designation in my file. The old one, with no office against it. Is that part of what's coming?"
   208	
   209	Vastin did not answer.
   210	
   211	He did not look away either. He sat with his hands on the table and his eyes on Cael, and the clocks counted for him, and Cael counted with them. One. Two. Three. Four. In the whole hour it was the only silence that pressed on anything. Cael felt it the way he felt the air before a squall, heavy, all along one side.
   212	
   213	"I won't answer that one," said Vastin.
   214	
   215	That was all. But it was a great deal. He had not said *I don't know*, which would have been true and easy. He had looked for whoever set that line in the file, as Ilsev had, and found nobody; and it troubled him. It troubled him more than anything else in the hour, more than the eight words, more than the warning. He had just said a thing in this room that could end forty years if anybody heard it, and he would not say this. Cael understood that it was not the record he was wary of. It was the blank in it. A man who has spent his life learning exactly how each instrument is wrong does not like an error that belongs to no instrument at all.
   216	
   217	"If I stopped," said Cael. "If I went home and never fought where anybody could see me again. Would it change anything?"
   218	
   219	"No." Vastin did not soften it. "That is the other half of why I asked for you. What you do now makes no difference to it. A year ago it would have. A thing you do is an answer, and answers are for questions, and they have stopped asking. Nothing answers a conclusion."
   220	
   221	---
   222	
   223	The clocks went round. Cael let them, for a while. Then he set down the question he had brought up the stair with him, because he did not think the hour would have a better place for it.
   224	
   225	"How did you evaluate me?"
   226	
   227	Vastin did not ask what he meant. He looked, for a moment, very slightly pleased.
   228	
   229	"With the wrong instruments, for three days," he said. "I came up to the bluff with a file and a plate and a set of questions, and all three told me nothing. A man in my trade has one great temptation, and I gave in to it. I thought the nothing was something being hidden from me." He moved the left hand. "It is the oldest mistake there is. When an instrument won't read, you'd sooner believe it's broken than believe you've pointed it at a thing it was never made to measure."
   230	
   231	"And then?"
   232	
   233	"Then you answered my fourth question. In full, truthfully, every word of it. And I learned nothing whatever from the answer." Something that was nearly amusement moved in his face. "I have done that to other men for forty years. Nobody had ever done it to me. From that question on I stopped measuring what you could do and started watching how you went about it." He looked at Cael steadily. "The method is not a boy's. That is my whole opinion of you. It's in no file. There is no line on any form where it would go."
   234	
   235	He glanced down at his own left hand on the table, and then up again.
   236	
   237	"You're counting that," he said. "The hand."
   238	
   239	"Yes."
   240	
   241	"Good. Count it right. It's worse in the evenings now as well as the mornings. Put that down where you put things."
   242	
   243	He did not offer protection. He said, in so many words, that he had none to give, because it would have been dishonest to let the boy wonder. Whatever reach his office had, it had through the very channels he had just told Cael would be stepped round. And a warning from a man who could also shelter you, he said, would have been a smaller thing than this, because you could never be sure which of the two he was giving you.
   244	
   245	"Do you think I'll stop?" said Cael.
   246	
   247	For a moment the grey face did something Cael would spend half that night failing to name in Hesk's book.
   248	
   249	"No. I evaluated you." He let it rest a moment. "But you'll know when the channel changes, now. Knowing is worth something. It's the only thing I had to give."
   250	
   251	He stood up, and said a word of thanks to the room. He said it to the shelves and the table and the clocks, the way a man pays a small account on his way out of an inn. Then he went to the door, and out, and Cael heard his steps going down round the inside of the tower at the same even pace all the way to the bottom. He was going back down into a machine he knew to the last screw and could no longer account for.
   252	
   253	Cael stayed where he was.
   254	
   255	He gave it four minutes by the clock over the door. It may have been five by the one beside it. If he got up at once, the hour would have decided when he left, and he meant to decide that himself.
   256	
   257	Then he went round the room with his eyes before he went, as he did every room.
   258	
   259	The table bare. Not a paper, not a cup, nothing on the wood that had not been there before. The other chair, empty. Forty clocks, near enough, every one of them sure. And on the sill by the window, apart from the rest and ticking a little behind them, a small brass travelling clock not unlike Hesk's. It had a card tied to its handle in a timekeeper's hand. *Slow. Owner informed.*
   260	
     1	# Chapter 59 — Same Build
     2	
     3	Vastin went down the tower stair at his ordinary pace and out under the arch.
     4	
     5	There was a big young man sitting on the step there with bandaged forearms, watching the court. He looked up as Vastin passed. Vastin knew him from the row: the continental Copper champion, the bluff's wall. He nodded to him, as one nods to a man keeping a door. The young man nodded back, gravely, and went on watching the court.
     6	
     7	He crossed it slowly. The copy line still ran along the colonnade, and he walked down the length of it and nobody in it knew him. A woman with a basket of eels. Three boys from some hill house arguing about the fourth exchange with their hands. A grey delegation head in Auremont's blue, standing in his place with his hands folded behind him, who did know him, and inclined his head an inch, and was answered by an inch. At the end of the colonnade, by the north tunnel, there was a frame on a nail with a sheet in it. He stopped in front of it and read the eleven words again, although he had heard them read aloud at dawn and could have said them in his sleep.
     8	
     9	He had written a note like that himself, once, on the road home from the bluff. Fewer words, and in a worse light, and signed with his whole name. He had thought it careful at the time. He saw now that the west tower had done it better. They had not tried to say what the boy was. They had said only what their instrument could and could not do, and stopped.
    10	
    11	He walked on toward the inn under the outer wall.
    12	
    13	The summons was in the inside pocket of his coat, where it had been since the post came up the road two days ago. A sitting at the seat, when the houses had dispersed. He was to attend and give evidence as the officer of record. It did not say on what. It did not need to. He would take the post road in the morning. He had already told the inn.
    14	
    15	He knew the machine he was going back to. He knew it better than any man alive, he sometimes thought, and without any pride in the thought. He knew which door every letter went through and which clerk's desk it lay on and for how long, and which seal sent it upstairs and which sent it down. He could have drawn the whole house from memory on the back of a form, stair by stair, with the names on every door. And for the first time in forty years he could not have told anybody which way it was going to move. He had drawn it all in his head and there was a room in the drawing he had never seen, and something in that room was deciding things.
    16	
    17	He had told the boy that he had stopped meaning to ask who chose which men saw the minutes. That was true. He had not told him why. It was not fear. He had examined it closely on the road down, the way he examined everything, and it was not fear. It was nearer to tact. A guest does not open a door in somebody else's house, however long he has lived there.
    18	
    19	That troubled him more than the eight words.
    20	
    21	His left hand ached. The afternoon was getting on, and the hand had started early. He put it in his coat pocket, against the summons, and walked on.
    22	
    23	He did not yet know what he would say at the sitting. He would know when he was standing in it. That was the only way he had ever known anything worth saying. He knew one thing about it already, and it was enough to be going on with. Whatever he said, he would sign every word of it with his whole name.
    24	
    25	---
    26	
    27	Cael went down to the rail over the floor at nightfall, alone.
    28	
    29	The stewards at the north tunnel let him through without a word, as they had let him through every evening since somewhere in the second week, when they had decided that the boy who walked the empty floors at night was part of the building. He went up into the dark of the lower tiers and along to the rail above the north break, where the five of them had stood the day before. He put both arms on it and looked down.
    30	
    31	The ring was still there. It would come up tonight; the crews were already gathering at the tunnel's mouth with their lamps and barrows and long bars, and somebody was laughing. But for the moment it stood as it had stood all day, in the last of the light from the high windows. There was the barrier with its smooth cap, and the four masts with their iron crowns, and the trench grating at the foot of the barrier all round. In the middle was the oak.
    32	
    33	He found the mark without looking for it.
    34	
    35	It lay along the foot of the barrier, west of the north break, a few strides long. Somebody's broom had been over it and had not been able to lift it. It was a pale line on the grey stone, like the mark a pan leaves on a table, and at its far end there was a scorch the size of a hand where his little fire had cracked. It was the only thing on the floor to show what the city had argued about all day, five different ways, and been wrong every time. The crews would have it up with the stone before morning. Nobody would think to keep it.
    36	
    37	He stood and looked at it until the light went off it.
    38	
    39	Under the strapping the left side kept up a low steady complaint, like a kettle left on the back of a stove. The rest of him was not settled at all. All day he had felt the things he carried moving about in him, slowly, the way men move furniture in a house where a great heavy chest has just been carried in. Every one of them leaned a little toward the new weight, and none of them had found its place. Twice on the walk down he had reached for the Wind and got something else. He thought it would be a long while before he could take a step without thinking about it. He had written that in the old volume already, and the healers' account of it, and his own, which was more honest.
    40	
    41	He took out Hesk's book and opened it on the rail. There was just enough light. He wrote standing up.
    42	
    43	*They didn't score me low. They didn't score me high. They couldn't score me. I have spent three years telling myself I can beat anyone at my tier. Tonight the fairest instrument ever built agreed with the Arbiter, the brackets, and the registry: I am not at any tier.*
    44	
    45	He looked at it for a while. The crews had come out onto the floor below him with the lamps, and one of them knelt at the foot of the north mast and began to loosen its cable.

