editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:57:Three rooms had been kept upstairs for the senior officers. The counsel looked into all three before she chose. The Archmarshal, Vastin, who had led the inspection at Halcenvane, tried a single door, the furthest at the back. A table stood under its window. That settled it. Neither of the others had a table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:63:They had the long table nearest the hearth. The counsel talked, as she had talked the whole road from the coast, about inns: this one's mutton, which was good, and its beds, which had been aired. Then she told of a magistrate she had once watched eat a whole game pie at this very table so that he would not have to answer a question put to him between courses. The records officer handled his fork like an instrument under calibration. The assessor ate a little bread and went up before the pudding. Vastin asked the counsel what the question had been, which pleased her a great deal, and listened to the answer, and did not say one word about Halcenvane.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:93:The record had the interval. *At the enrollee's request: fatigue affecting response quality.* The clerk had set the minute beside it, and then, in a smaller hand, squeezed in under the line, three words of his own: *before instruments indicated*. No one had asked him to. He had seen that the boy asked before any needle had a reason to move, and had decided that a record which left it out would be a less true record. Vastin made a mark against the clerk in his head, the kind of mark he made perhaps four times a year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:99:It had the chalk tray. The clerk at the tray's far end was there, since he had entered himself. But no clerk alive writes his own feet into a ledger, and so the brown shoes standing a pace and a half past the place where a heel would have landed were not anywhere in it. Vastin had seen them. He had seen the boy's answer too, when Gault asked him why. The record had the answer word for word: *Because the clerk was standing where I'd have landed.* Read without the shoes it sounded like a pleasant thing a boy had said. Read with them, it was a figure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:125:He had set it down in the quarter of an hour the porters took to fetch the reserve frame from the far store, with the notebook open on his knee and the boy at the west rail with his weight off one leg. He had written fast. Then at the very end, when the wing was emptying and he was on his feet with the chair pushed back, he had taken the book out again and drawn a single line through three of the words. One stroke, so that the words could still be read under it. A man who blacks out his own error so that nobody can see what it was has told a lie about his own mind, and Vastin did not tell those on paper if he could avoid it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:155:Vastin turned back a page, to the nineteenth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:177:The boy kept records. That fact sat in every monitoring filing on the file and in the Greyvane transcript besides, and every officer who had ever read the folder had passed over it as the dullest line in it. Vastin had asked what the records were for and been told, and had believed what he was told: *So I can't lie to myself later.* The next question he had let lie. It was the obvious next question. Withrow's counsel had drawn breath to hear it. Gault, at the boy's elbow, had gone as still as a post. Any capable officer would have put it, and any poor one would have put it badly, and he had looked at it for less than a second and not put it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:181:The mandate on that bluff had been the record against the practitioner, and a private notebook was not in the record. An officer who reaches past his mandate because the reach happens to be open to him has stopped evaluating. He has begun to collect. And a collector at Vastin's height, carrying Vastin's authority, is the worst thing the Compact is able to make.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:183:He had seen one made, when he was young. A senior man of great gifts, who had read other men's letters because he could, and kept what he found, and used it by degrees, a little and then more, until half a district's officers did their work looking over their shoulders at him. Bringing him down had taken four officers, a magistrate and the best part of ten years, and had used up two careers that deserved better. Vastin had been thirty-four on the night it ended. He had stood in a corridor afterward with rain on the window at the end of it and made himself a promise that he would not grow into the thing he had spent those years taking to pieces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-38.md:87:*Archmarshal Vastin.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:73:Vastin had heard her through walls for four nights of inns, and he no longer heard the words. He heard the shape. It went on low and level, about the water in the jug and the state of a collar and something a magistrate had once said to her on a stair. Then it stopped in the middle, and there was a silence about as long as a page, and then it went on again from the exact syllable where it had broken off. She had been handed something to read. He had noticed the habit on the second day of the road and had not written it down, because he did not take notes on colleagues. He only noticed that he had noticed, and let it go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:107:"The first three days are yours," said Vastin. "If I sit at your table, your clerks will write down what I look at instead of what you ask. I'll come on the fifteenth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:113:"Most procedures are," said Vastin. "It's what they're for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:167:Vastin had heard it and copied it into the back of a book and gone on not understanding it for four years. He understood it in a bad month, sitting on the end of a narrow bed in a posting-house with his boots still on. A finding of his had come apart that afternoon at its first review, and he had sat there and traced it back. He had built it step by step from a suspicion he had liked holding, and every step had looked to him like evidence, because he had been walking toward the place where it came out the whole time and had never once turned round to look. The finding had not lived. His career had come within a sheet of paper of going down with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:173:Below the window, across the wet quadrangle, a boy was standing on an upturned bucket under an arch. He was writing on a slate that hung on the wall there, a figure at a time, with his tongue in the corner of his mouth. Then he got down and looked at what he had written from the cobbles, and got up again, and changed one figure. Vastin watched him do it twice. He had no idea what the slate was for. Whatever it was, the boy had decided it ought to be right, though there was nobody anywhere in the quadrangle to check it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:115:"That the clause stands. That Halcenvane's charter carries it. That the chancellor of Halcenvane has directed her registrar to prepare an enrollment under it, effective at the start of their next term, on the satisfaction of an entry demonstration before their assessment office. That counsel finds no defect in the basis." He turned the letter round on the blotter so that it faced Cael. "Read it if you like. It is very short. It took them a great deal of money to make it that short."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:5:He had walked past it most days for a month, on the path behind the assessment wing that ran down to the low hall, and every time he had looked at it and every time he had left it alone. It hung beside the door on a square iron nail at the height of his shoulder, a plain black key on a loop of leather gone shiny with handling, and the door it belonged to was locked from the outside. He had written both facts down on his first morning on the bluff, and underlined them, and had not been able to think of a single reason for either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:37:So at the change of bell after noon, when the halls emptied and filled again, he went down the path behind the assessment wing, took the key off the nail, and knocked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:229:*Lira: nine bouts, nine wins, in a register half the bluff reads with a pencil in hand, and still nothing in it can climb as high as her certificate.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:117:"The assessment wing," said Karis. "They pulled it down to its footings six years ago and built it up again. The new wall, the new gate, every piece of apparatus behind those cloths. It cost more than the library roof, and the money came from that appeal." She looked at Cael. "You wanted to know why they took you. You've been asking the buildings for two weeks. Here's what the buildings say."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:139:There was a case of glass on a stone plinth along one wall, and in it three crowns on three dark cushions with a brass plate under each. The Copper was the plainest of them, and the darkest, and worn bright along its top edge where a great many hands had lifted it. Its plate had been lengthened twice with new brass riveted on below the old.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:185:The assessment office's notice was in his pigeonhole on the twenty-first morning, a half-sheet folded once, in a clerk's hand he did not know.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:187:*Assay-provision enrollee. To demonstrate for entry before the assessment office's panel, Magister Gault presiding. The office will fix the day within the month and post it two days before. The enrollee will hold himself available from the first bell. The demonstration establishes the baseline against which all subsequent evaluations are measured.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-09.md:15:"Transfer," he said, reading off the sheet clipped beside it. "Wind Path. Copper, Rank Two, formal. Re-certification." He looked up. "You understand how the seeding goes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-10.md:29:He had known his name would not be there. A ladder is fed by certificates, and no certificate anywhere carried a tier against his name for it to be fed. The porter wiping the boards had taught him that much. Knowing it and standing over a book of names with his hand on the page were different things.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:7:He leaned over her shoulder. It was Halcenvane's own copy of the founding clause, with the charter article that carried it set out beneath, and below that the assessment office's standing rule for applying it, in a dry modern hand. He had read all three before, on the first night, and again in Bracken's room, and again on the night the notice came. He had not read them the way Karis read things, which was as if each word had been put there by somebody who might be lying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:111:*The assessment office will receive the entry demonstration of the assay-provision enrollee on the forty-eighth day, at the third bell, in the demonstration room of the assessment wing. Magister Gault presiding. The enrollee will present himself at the near door.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:167:He walked Cael across the bluff in the early light without saying anything at all, which was what Cael wanted. They went by the long way, round the rim of the Crown yard, where the porter was already out with his pail chalking the practice rings, and past the north hall, where somebody was running a drill alone on the big stone floor, the same somebody perhaps who had been running one there on Cael's first morning. Brom ate his bread. Cael carried his and did not eat it, and Brom noticed and said nothing about that either. The assessment wing's low wall came up ahead of them, pale and new, with its single gate standing open and nobody in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:183:Two weeks of asking had got him the names of nine pieces out of the twelve. First a drop-frame: a trigger, a rail, a weight to run down it, and four more weights racked underneath; someone at the far end tested the pawl, and it gave one soft click. A pendulum escapement in a glass case, ticking a steady interval that he found himself breathing in time with before he noticed and stopped. Nine shutters in a board, opened in an order a drum behind them decided; the drum's ratchet made the noise of a thumbnail going slowly down the teeth of a comb. Three spring plates on braces, each with a date of calibration painted on its housing. For footprints, a shallow tray of chalk dust. And at the very end, a thing like a tall brass funnel over a cup, which dripped into the cup with a small bright sound every few seconds, and which he could not name at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:79:"Dellenmoor. Standard. Research standing pending; the library committee writes to you. Brom. Standard. Ninth day, first bell, east hall; the Blade cohort wants a look at you. Lira. Transfer, re-certification. Copper bracket. You register for the ladder by the fourteenth or you start the season on the bottom rung and climb from there." He licked a finger and turned the page. "Cael. Assay-provision enrollment. Straight to the registrar. Assessment wing, ground floor, the door with the brass plate. He's cleared the whole morning, which he does not do, so go now and look at the view later."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:83:"Rooms, second quadrangle. The porter's got your keys. The assessment wing has a wall of its own; you'll know it. Don't cross the Crown yard when there's a bout on; they'll shout at you, and I'll be glad they did." He was already reaching for the next carter's slip. "Welcome to Halcenvane. You there, with the casks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:159:*Entry requirement: satisfied by demonstration before the assessment office, this term.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:161:*Ongoing requirement: full evaluation by demonstration before the panel of the assessment office, each semester, without exception. Compact observation rights attach.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:167:In the first stack lay the clause, copied out of three founding-era compilations, every copy certified, every one tagged with where its book stood and which clerk had sworn to it. Bracken read out each mark. Cael, because the index was in his bag, took it out and found each one in Prynn's small upright hand and read her cross-reference back. Two of the compilations were ones Karis had worked from. The third Greyvane had never owned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:169:On the second certified copy, Cael stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:171:"The date on the clerk's certificate," he said. "It's a year after the date in the compilation's own colophon. But the shelf-mark is for the first printing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:175:"The copying clerk's slip," he said. "He took the shelf-mark off the first printing and the certificate off the second, because they stand side by side. The text's the same in both. I checked that myself. But the sheet says two things at once, and anybody who wanted to could pull on that thread." He set the sheet aside on a pile of its own. "It'll be redone this week. That's what I asked you here for. Thank you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:217:"I'd allowed two hours," he said, and seemed to count it against himself. "The assessment office will set the day for your entry demonstration. Magister Gault sits that panel. His face gives nothing away. That isn't a judgment of you; it's his face. Whatever you show him that day is your baseline, and the baseline doesn't move. Every semester's evaluation is measured against it, as long as you're here." He held out the tag. "Choose with that in mind."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:255:"Two terms of putting Iron-equivalents on the floor. An academy's seal on a flag. A queue that's answered that woman three times in two years." She ate the bread. "And they read the number on my certificate, and they stop reading. They always have."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:69:"No," said Bracken. He laid his pen down square to the desk's edge. "They are evidence. They are admissible. They are not a verdict. I would be grateful if nobody in your hearing confused those three words. Nor a standing with a rank. A standing is this season's chalk. A rank is what the registry has written on a certificate, and the registry does not read our wall." He folded his hands. "Next year this academy will contest the qualifying season for the Continental. The chancellor has said so in an open address; it is on the record, so I may repeat it. A squad will be chosen. The people choosing it will read this season's closing standings among a great deal else. A strong season will not put a name on that squad. A weak one will very probably keep a name off it." He picked his pen back up. "Your friend from the north stands near the foot of a column she plainly does not belong in. If she climbs, it will be noticed. If she does not, that will be noticed too. That is all I may tell you, and rather more than I ought."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:77:She was the best Copper fighter Cael had ever seen. Her certificate said Copper Rank Eight, and had said Rank Eight for two years. Rank, like tier, was a word an Arbiter wrote on a certificate after an advancement evaluation; no bout on any ladder could add a number to it, and while her paper said Copper, the Copper bracket was the only bracket she could fight in. No registry had written a word about Fiske in two years, because nobody had asked one to. Lira had worked out in a wash-house, over a pair of ruined shirts, why nobody had asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:119:Cael did not look. But that evening he went along the assessment wing's passage, where the wing posted its working hours on a board for anyone to read. He had every right to read it, since the same board set the hours of his own sessions. He found the two days. On each of them the wing had kept its counter open from the first bell to the last, with panels sitting all afternoon and a long run of practical assessments for the second-years behind them. Ten hours at a desk and on his feet, both days, for a man whose Path charged him a little for every hour he spent among people.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:145:*Pressure-adjacent. Unused this term. Withheld at the baseline plate. On the record as not shown.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:173:"And then they'll write *Copper* on my certificate the morning after, same as the morning before." She was quiet. "You've written down that it won't mean anything."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:195:*Seln. Shadow. Bronze.* At the far end of the staff corridor. Bronze, because Bronze was easy to forget; and behind the card, work no certificate anywhere had a name for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-22.md:123:"For forty days Cael has been studying a man in the assessment wing. He asked me to hold the second pencil, because he said a thing watched by one pair of eyes is a hobby and not a study, and he was right. So I've held it. Every page." Karis drew the thin grey notebook a finger's width nearer to her. "Tonight I'm going to put that study beside the model, one column at a time, and show you what I see. Stop me if a number's wrong. Don't stop me at the end."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-20.md:39:Cael stood and did that sum twice. Nearly every name that had not fought last season was a first-year, and nearly every first-year carried a fresh Arbiter's Rank One. Lira's paper said Rank Two. The one figure on her certificate that had ever helped her had lifted her over seventy people at a stroke and set her directly beneath the last of the old names.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-20.md:41:Her paper and her place were two different measures, and only one of them would outlast the season. Copper Rank Two was her classification, written on her certificate by an Arbiter and changed only by an advancement evaluation. Twenty-second was her seed, a standing the ladder could move every six days and would wipe clean at the season's end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-24.md:189:The man had given up a fifth part of the floor. For it he had gained both stair heads, north and south, and the whole approach from the assessment wing's gate, which from the old place had been hidden behind a pillar. It was the better place. It was the best place on the tier for the last hour of a session, when people began to leave, and it mattered more who went down the stairs than who stood on the floor. He had reached it inside a dozen seconds, inside a crowd that moved for reasons of its own, and the crowd had folded round him so completely that nobody in it could have said afterward that he had moved at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:21:"Held a plate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:41:That night it went into the binder beneath the entry for the plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:181:"A while," said Seln, in the stale voice, and looked at his plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:209:He read it as water reads a hillside. Where would people go, and where would they stop, and from where could they be seen stopping? Three halls on the north edge, each with two sets of doors: six streams that ran together for a quarter of an hour at every change of bell and then broke apart again. The assessment wing sat behind a low wall of its own: one gate, then two doors into the building. Excellent, if you worked inside. Very poor for anyone who wanted to stand outside and watch the gate. The lecture range, four floors high, a stair at either end and every landing open along its whole length, so that a man on one landing could be seen from the far stair by anybody who happened to be looking. The quadrangles built round courts, so many windows facing in that nobody ever looked out of any of them on purpose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:217:On the fourth morning he was in the assessment wing's passage with an armful of forms when the door of the demonstration room opened at the change of bell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:239:He had a filing to hand in on the way, so he went to the assessment wing first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:9:He walked the north edge first, where the three long halls stood with their high windows facing the morning. The largest had six floors marked out on its boards, and somebody was already on one of them at that hour, alone, running a drill he did not know. The second hall was smaller and its floors were timber, not stone, and when he stood in its doorway and shifted his weight he felt the boards answer him very slightly, a give and a return, as though the floor had a breath of its own. He wrote that down and underlined it and did not yet know why. The third hall was low and set apart behind the assessment wing. Its door was locked from the outside, with the key on a nail beside it, which was a thing he had never seen before and could not think of a reason for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:13:The assessment wing had its own wall and its own gate, and through an open door on the ground floor he counted pieces of apparatus standing along a wall under cloths. There were a dozen, and he could guess the purpose of four. The library was the heavy-roofed building he had picked out from the bridge, and an accession board inside its door claimed forty-one thousand volumes in brass numerals. Karis, when he told her that night, said that accession boards counted everything a library had ever been given, including the things it had since burned for being damp, and that she would believe thirty-five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:181:*Ladder. Four fights a session afternoon; the assessment office pairs them. In between, you may call out anybody above you in your own column, four names up at most. Beat him and his place is yours, and everybody between slips one. Lose, and the board stays as it was.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:197:"Her certificate says Copper." The porter wrote *COPPER* in big square capitals across the top of the clean board. "The board doesn't care what you can do, lad. It reads what's on your certificate and puts you where that says. It's not unkind. It's a board."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:201:He took Brom down to Ostrand on the ninth evening, because Brom needed new wraps before his first cohort hour. He had been assessed that morning in the east hall and placed in the Blade instructor's cohort, and the first hour was set for the following week. About the assessment he had said only that the cohort was fast, and Cael had decided not to ask him for more.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-17.md:41:"The assistant in Gault's office is a watcher. He's good. He's very good. Shadow Path, Bronze tier, by the classification on his own card, and I believe the card. He's been on the bluff about four weeks. He has never once looked at me, because looking is the one thing that would give him away, and he stands every day where he can see everything I do without being seen to see it." He paused. "I can't prove whose he is. I don't know of anybody on this continent who'd pay a Bronze to copy forms in an assessment wing, except the people who already pay for those coats at the ferry."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-17.md:73:"He's in Gault's room," she said. "He's got your dates in his ledger and your sheets on his pile. Everything that keeps you on this hill goes across that table, and he's sitting at it." She turned round. "Withrow knew the Compact would come when she took the provision. She told you so. She put it on a sheet with a column for what you'd cost her. There wasn't a line on that sheet for a Compact man with a chair in the assessment office. She'd want to know. Any chancellor would."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:113:"No getting out of this one," said Gault. "Too close, too heavy. Catch it on the plate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:115:The clerk brought the plate across on its brace and put it into Cael's hands: a round of steel with a spring behind it and a small dial in the housing, made to show how much of a blow had been held and how much had gone on through.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:123:He stood with the plate and the reaching in his arm for two breaths, which was one breath longer than he had meant to, and he saw Gault see the extra breath and say nothing. Somewhere the pendulum ticked. He thought of Lira on the wash-house step: *make him look.* He thought of Karis in carrel eleven: *it was mine before it was yours.* He thought of half a page of transcript, late and thin. Then he let the reaching go, as he had let it go on a thousand other floors, and his arm was only his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:125:He braced the plain way: feet wide; the plate held out on an arm loaded from the shoulder; everything down through his legs into the oak. It was how a body with no Path takes a knock, and how he had taken every knock in his first winter on the Ironyard's planks. The pawl let go, and the weight came down the rail and met the plate, and the shock went up the bones of his forearm and stopped in the shoulder like a nail driven into a joist. Nothing in him carried it anywhere else; the shoulder had all of it. Behind the plate the spring hit the end of its travel with a dull clank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:149:The Ash instructor's book lay shut in front of her, with a finger in it at a place she had long since stopped keeping. The Mire instructor no longer looked resentful; she looked thoughtful, which on her face was nearly the same expression turned the other way up. And Gault had watched him; Cael understood it only now. All through the morning, every run and every drop and every clank of the plate, the heavy still man in the middle chair had watched him, start to finish, and looked away only to write.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:167:He wrote the plate up that evening at the desk by the window, with his arm on a folded shirt because it did not like to hang.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:211:Under the plate that night he added the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-34.md:23:Nobody at the table asked whether he was going. Karis talked about the transitional article of a directive nobody else had read. Brom described exercise thirteen, which involved a door frame and was, he said, the worst so far by a distance he could not express in feet. Lira ate with her eyes on her plate and said almost nothing. When they got up she walked beside him as far as the foot of the residence stair, and stopped there, and looked him over once, top to bottom, as she looked over the standings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-31.md:215:*Field assessment (staff unit). Weekly, from Third-day, the sixth bell, the long room off the assessment wing. Observation; position; presence. Open to second and third years; provision enrollees admitted on the training requirement. Places: twelve. Sign below.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-31.md:225:"She's drawn an eye," said Brom, reading it over his shoulder with a plate in one hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-15.md:21:*TA, assessment office: nought of seven.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-15.md:187:Floor times on the bluff moved by paper and by nothing else, so the trade went to the assessment office before noon on a half-sheet in Cael's hand, countersigned for Brom's cohort. Cael took it there himself. The desk clerk read it, initialled the corner, and passed it across to the copying table. There the man with the steady pen read the front, turned it, read the back, wrote both changes into the ledger, and laid the sheet on its pile, without a glance at either the paper's owner or the paper's subject.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-29.md:155:"There's a form," said Fiske. "For an advancement evaluation. The registry sends an Arbiter if the form goes up. It wants one signature that isn't mine." She turned the cup in her hands. "Two seasons I've sat on top of that sheet, with Rank Eight on my certificate. Two crowns. Every bout on this hill worth having. The porter polishes the stud beside my name on Fourth-days. And in all that time nobody has picked up the pen. Not because anyone decided not to. Because nobody ever had a reason to. I was winning. Why would anybody send the winner away?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:5:He had read the boy's slate every morning since his second week on the bluff. It hung by the covered walk's north arch, too high for its keeper, and at the first bell the boy climbed onto an upturned bucket and chalked up the night's reckoning with the tip of his tongue showing at the corner of his mouth. He was perhaps ten. He belonged to the fire-watch, two men who walked the bluff all night with a lantern on a pole, out from the residence quadrangles along the administration range to the assessment wing and back again, noting the hour at which each building put its lamps out. The boy kept their count. At noon a bursar's clerk copied it onto a quarter-sheet and took it away, and at the month's end the bursar and the chandler sat down with the quarter-sheets between them and fought over the oil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:55:"Through the door. With a key or something as good as one." She tapped the end of her drawn room. "The inner door has a counting lock. The Compact makes every chartered records office on the continent fit one. Each time the bolt goes across, it knocks a little brass plate inside the case, and the registrar reads the plate. Bracken reads his every Fifth-day, never oftener, because a registrar seen reading his lock plate on an odd day is a registrar who's telling the building he expects a thief. He read it yesterday anyway. Two marks. This morning, four." She held up four fingers, then folded two of them down, then the other two. "In and out. In and out. Two nights."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:111:The long room off the assessment wing had been a map store once. It still had the wide shallow drawers along one wall, painted over, and on the opposite wall three tall windows that looked out across the yard toward the lecture range. Somebody had put a table down the middle of it and twelve chairs round the table, and at the far end, on an easel, a slate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:131:"Field assessment," he said. "Observation, position, presence. Every week to the close of term, this room, this bell. Gault approves the syllabus and will see your coursework. He will not come. I will. If you miss a session, the wing will know, because I will have written it down." He turned to the slate and wrote three words on it in the round copying hand, one under the other. *Observe. Position. Presence.* "Nobody in this room will learn to be invisible. Nobody on this continent can be. What you'll learn is where people look, and why, and in what order, and how to use that when you're the one doing the looking for a living." He put the chalk down. "Stand up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-47.md:221:He went to the library to look for them, and Karis found him there first. She came in with her supper on a plate, which was against the library's rules, and which the library had stopped trying to enforce on Karis some time in the first month. She sat down opposite him at the long table under the north window.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-47.md:235:"Anywhere. Three to Gault, in the wing, this morning; the clerk doesn't know what. Then nothing. Not in the lecture range at the second bell. Not in the residence quadrangle at noon, or the fire-watch's store, or any of the three floors." She looked at him over the plate. "He went into every room they went into, and stood in it, and he asked nobody anything at all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-47.md:243:He turned the notebook round and showed her the last two lines. She read them, and then she put her fork down on the plate, very quietly, and did not pick it up again for a while.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-43.md:219:"Five." He said them as she wrote. "The Wind. The Pressure. The Iron read. The Compression. The Ember. Not all on the same paper, though." He made himself go through it sheet by sheet, the way she would have. "The transcript anybody can buy holds two of them, the Wind and the read, and that one thin Ember exhibit at the back that nobody ever reads. Gault's baseline holds the Wind numbers and the read, and the plate, where I held the Pressure back, so that line's flat, and a Compression line he ringed because the floor never gave him a fair test of it. And Vell copied the circuit record at Ardenmere. Every bout I fought is in it, the Pressure I used in public and the Compression that finished Reydan."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:33:Gault's office had filed the baseline in the boy's first weeks, and it had been copied into the product soon after. It gave a practitioner of non-standard architecture who executed consistently. It gave a displacement framework, measured to a working ceiling on sprung timber. And it gave a plate trial at the frame's heaviest weight, in which the enrollee had taken the blow on a braced arm and kept his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:47:It lingered on the shear and the setting, the core and the collar, the warden coming within the quarter-hour, every post on the bluff withdrawn, every pin in the academy to be inspected and replaced. It was warm about the institution, and the young man would have nodded at that. Naturally the writer cared about the institution. It was what a man in an assessment wing was best placed to see. And an academy finding a flaw in nine years of its own apparatus was the weather the analysts liked best.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:239:He had watched Fiske, Force Path, Copper Rank Eight on a certificate two years old, fight four times since Lira's decision, as Lira had wanted him to. Four pages. On every one of them the same thing: a minute and a half of collecting, two bad openings, and then the floor closing behind her opponents like doors in a long corridor, one after another, until there was nowhere left to stand. Fiske never struck anybody. She put her force into the boards where they were about to be, and they arrived on it, and stumbled, and found her hand on them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:31:In six days she would walk out onto the oak for the Copper final, Copper Rank Two on her certificate and Iron-equivalent by every honest ledger on the bluff, on a leg the safety instructor had not cleared, against the one fighter in the bracket who knew it best.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:73:Vastin had heard her through walls for four nights of inns, and he no longer heard the words. He heard the shape. It went on low and level, about the water in the jug and the state of a collar and something a magistrate had once said to her on a stair. Then it stopped in the middle, and there was a silence about as long as a page, and then it went on again from the exact syllable where it had broken off. She had been handed something to read. He had noticed the habit on the second day of the road and had not written it down, because he did not take notes on colleagues. He only noticed that he had noticed, and let it go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:81:They had come across the evening before, under the assessment wing's own seal, in answer to a requisition the counsel had written on the road in the ordinary form. The wing had answered it in the ordinary time. Pink tape. A covering index on top, in a hand that was not the hand of the filings under it. Six filings, with an appendix behind each, covering one term of embedded coverage on an enrollee who held his place under a provision.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-41.md:9:*To the assessment office of the receiving institution: compile, under one covering index, the whole of this term's monitoring product on any enrollee held under provision, and keep it sealed against the senior evaluation seat's call.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:9:There were four resistance posts on the bluff, two in the north bay of the east hall and two in the north hall, and the assessment wing kept their ledgers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:11:Each was a stout padded column standing about as high as a man's breastbone, set into an iron floor plate bolted through the oak to the joists beneath. Inside the padding, on a spindle, sat a heavy iron core.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:21:Each post was meant to be that something. Bolted to every plinth, in letters a quarter of an inch high, was a brass plate saying what happened if they ever failed to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:29:The Ash instructor had his sheet. She had read his drill through twice at the start of the hour, the way she read everything, and initialled it, and then gone to the south end of the hall where two second-years were learning a rope frame. Three more enrollees had the south bays, two at falls and one at a wall drill. A floor porter stood in the near doorway leaning on a broom he was not using. At the wall table by the west end, the teaching assistant from the assessment wing was gathering the week's dial sheets into a stack, as somebody from the wing did every Third-day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:93:Jask sat down on the floor plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:109:She did not ask Jask anything at first. She took his right hand in both of hers and said, "Squeeze." He squeezed. "The other." He squeezed with the other. "Look at my finger." She moved it slowly left and right in front of his face, and his eyes followed it, and some of the stiffness went out of her back. Then, "Tell me today's date," and he told her, a day wrong, and she nodded as if a day wrong was about right for a man who had just sat down on a floor plate, and only then asked whether anything hurt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:113:One second-year was off toward the warden's shed before the instructor had finished the word *warden*. The porter, sent to the wing for the duty clerk, went with his broom still on his shoulder. She moved everybody back out of the north bay with a single flat word, and nobody touched the post. One of the second-years, coming back, stood in front of it and read the brass plate aloud, every word, in a voice that was not steady, as though reading it might still help. Nobody had the heart to stop her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-30.md:135:The pen in the assessment wing's end office was chained to the counter by a length of fine brass chain, and it scratched. Cael had signed for things with it perhaps thirty times. He knew where it caught, a third of the way through any downstroke, and he had learned to lift for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-30.md:153:"Gault's putting a unit on the back half of term. Field assessment." A mark in the ledger. "Observation, position, presence. Once a week to the close of term. Most of the places will go to second- and third-years who mean to be assessors. Provision enrollees may sit."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-30.md:197:A thing that was going to be decided was read to all three at once, with the door shut, before anything was done about it. Breakfast was a long table and sixty people. So he ate, and listened to Brom describe exercise nine in a voice of deep injury, and said nothing. Karis looked at him once across the bread and then down at her plate, and he saw her decide not to ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-30.md:225:They stood along the north wall where they had always stood, four iron columns with their padded heads, and the new retaining pins shone in the collars like fresh nails in an old fence. The chains were gone. The floor warden had fitted every pin herself over two long evenings, so the Ash instructor told him at the bay's edge, and had made each Force third-year who used the posts that week watch her do it, and then made them each say the plate aloud before she let them near a dial.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-48.md:55:He thought of every hour he had worked under the wing that term, and of every figure the quiet woman had sent across to the registrar in her neat hand on the same afternoon, and each one of those figures had been made on some post at some setting. He thought of his baseline in the second month, the entry demonstration that had set the measure for his whole provision, taken against plates with their dates of calibration painted on the housings. He thought of the twentieth, when Gault's panel would lay his new numbers beside his old ones and say whether he had grown. And he thought of the posts in hall three, every one of them re-pinned this term after a pin had sheared in the north bay at the seventh setting, with Cael's own forearm the first thing it hit. Was a post with a new pin the same post? Did it read the same? Who said so?
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-48.md:185:"Two copies, certified," said Bracken. "One taken from this library's own holding. The other came up from a house on the coast, four hundred miles off, under that house's seal, in answer to a letter from this office. The letter's dated, and it's in the box. The two librarians who certified them have never written to each other in their lives. Then I gave both copies to a clerk who had never read either, and had him go through them side by side, line against line. What he found is on his sheet, which is document fourteen. Three places where they differ. All three are spelling. None of the three is in the clause."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-48.md:205:That was the second time. The first had been ten minutes before, while Bracken described how the two copies had been compared: by a clerk who had read neither, line against line. Not when the second certified copy came out of the box, which was the stronger point. Not at the long silence while the counsel read. Only at how the comparing was done. And now, only at the counsel saying the index was sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:33:"The unit runs again next term. Sign at the residence board when term opens. Anyone going on into assessment work will find the second harder and less interesting, which is the correct order."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:63:They had the long table nearest the hearth. The counsel talked, as she had talked the whole road from the coast, about inns: this one's mutton, which was good, and its beds, which had been aired. Then she told of a magistrate she had once watched eat a whole game pie at this very table so that he would not have to answer a question put to him between courses. The records officer handled his fork like an instrument under calibration. The assessor ate a little bread and went up before the pudding. Vastin asked the counsel what the question had been, which pleased her a great deal, and listened to the answer, and did not say one word about Halcenvane.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:99:It had the chalk tray. The clerk at the tray's far end was there, since he had entered himself. But no clerk alive writes his own feet into a ledger, and so the brown shoes standing a pace and a half past the place where a heel would have landed were not anywhere in it. Vastin had seen them. He had seen the boy's answer too, when Gault asked him why. The record had the answer word for word: *Because the clerk was standing where I'd have landed.* Read without the shoes it sounded like a pleasant thing a boy had said. Read with them, it was a figure.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:125:He had set it down in the quarter of an hour the porters took to fetch the reserve frame from the far store, with the notebook open on his knee and the boy at the west rail with his weight off one leg. He had written fast. Then at the very end, when the wing was emptying and he was on his feet with the chair pushed back, he had taken the book out again and drawn a single line through three of the words. One stroke, so that the words could still be read under it. A man who blacks out his own error so that nobody can see what it was has told a lie about his own mind, and Vastin did not tell those on paper if he could avoid it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:177:The boy kept records. That fact sat in every monitoring filing on the file and in the Greyvane transcript besides, and every officer who had ever read the folder had passed over it as the dullest line in it. Vastin had asked what the records were for and been told, and had believed what he was told: *So I can't lie to myself later.* The next question he had let lie. It was the obvious next question. Withrow's counsel had drawn breath to hear it. Gault, at the boy's elbow, had gone as still as a post. Any capable officer would have put it, and any poor one would have put it badly, and he had looked at it for less than a second and not put it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:183:He had seen one made, when he was young. A senior man of great gifts, who had read other men's letters because he could, and kept what he found, and used it by degrees, a little and then more, until half a district's officers did their work looking over their shoulders at him. Bringing him down had taken four officers, a magistrate and the best part of ten years, and had used up two careers that deserved better. Vastin had been thirty-four on the night it ended. He had stood in a corridor afterward with rain on the window at the end of it and made himself a promise that he would not grow into the thing he had spent those years taking to pieces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:39:The long table had been cleared to the wood. Four places were laid at the far end of it, close together, with the residence's chipped blue plates and four cups that very nearly matched. And in the middle of the table, lit, its glass chimney polished until it shone, stood the good lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:63:He sat at the end of the table with his coat still half on and his hands flat on the wood either side of an empty blue plate, and found that he could not say anything at all. It was not that he had nothing to say. It was that everything he had to say had arrived at once, at the door of his mouth, and stuck there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:77:Brom set the basket on the table and took the cloth off it with a little flourish and began to lay out what was inside on the blue plates, one thing at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:133:"My sister had been looking at that fork all the way through dinner. And when her pear came, she put the fork down on the cloth, very neatly, right beside her plate, where everybody could see it. And she picked the pear up in her hand. Like this. And she bit it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:193:He thought about where he had been at the start of this half-year: across a desk from Bracken at intake, while the registrar took ninety-four minutes over his papers and then wrote, at the end of it, the first true sentence anybody had ever written about him. Since then Lira had climbed a ladder that had been built for somebody else, Copper Rank Two on a certificate that would have put her at Iron in any honest registry, and stood on its top line. Brom had been taken apart on purpose, and had come out the far side of nineteen dull exercises and gone round again from the first. Karis had found a clause, and a chart, and a way of saying *I don't know* that was harder to argue with than most people's certainty. And a quiet man at a copying table had stood in a doorway in the light and spent everything he had, and never once looked down a flight of stairs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-38.md:21:The watchers at the road's foot wore grey, and Cael had spent a season learning their grey: road grey, the grey of dust that has been rained on, a colour chosen so that nobody would remember it. This was the other grey: cold and faintly blue, like slate after rain, the grey of the registry. He had seen it on the clerks behind the long counter at the certification hall in Denvash, and once on a young assessor standing in front of a pump step, in boots too new to have creased. He had never once mistaken it for anything else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-58.md:3:The afternoon paper reached the assessment wing on the twenty-fourth a little after the fourth bell, and the delegation's closing docket was in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-40.md:163:"Now," she said. "What follows from that? For you, nothing new. I have no instruction to give you that I haven't given every week this term. Go to your lectures. Sit your assessments on the days they fall. Fight your bouts when your names are posted. That's all." She let it settle. "If any of you is tempted to put on a show for our visitors, a cleaner floor, a quieter refectory, a better face, don't. They will see it. They're made to see it. A house pretending to be itself is the commonest thing an inspection finds, and the easiest. A performance is the one thing an inspection can truly see. Give them nothing to see but Halcenvane."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:35:The heaviest weight, the fifth, went onto the rack under the Mire instructor's hands. The clerk rubbed out the chalk square and drew it again one pace from the rail, then fetched the plate on its brace and handed it over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:41:He had carried it for three years, ever since a bout he had lost honestly, and it had always lived close under his skin. It did not keep a blow. It gathered a blow, and turned it, and sent it on somewhere else. The thing in his hands was what it had been made for. If anybody had been marking answers right and wrong, it was the right one, and the little dial in the plate's housing would have swung round to a figure that nobody in the room had ever seen it show.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:45:It was written down elsewhere. Vell's copy of the Ardenmere circuit record held the bouts in which he had used it in public, years ago, before he knew enough to keep it back. But Gault's book had no line for it. Gault's book had a plate held low four times, with the shoulder taking everything, and a boy saying so before anybody asked. To bring the other thing out now would be a second surprise, on a third instrument, in a place the baseline had never pointed at, and a slip of wing paper had told him how many surprises a sitting could afford.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:49:His brace was the plain one: wide feet, the plate out on an arm loaded from the shoulder, and everything sent down through the legs. The pawl let go and the weight came down the rail into the plate, and the shock ran up the bones of his forearm and stopped in the shoulder, all of it, like a nail going into a beam. Behind the plate the spring hit the end of its travel with a dull clack.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:61:Two sheets went down side by side under Gault's hands, the baseline's plate and this morning's, and his eyes went from one to the other. Line for line they matched, with not a graduation between them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:85:At the baseline this weight had been too near and too heavy to escape, and they had made him stand under it with the plate. Now the clerk chalked a square only a pace and a half out from the rail, and along its landing side he laid the long shallow tray of chalk dust and drew a ruler across it to smooth it flat. The wing read that tray standing, at once, before the enrollee had stepped out of it, so that nobody's memory could tidy up what the dust said, and so the clerk took his place at the tray's far end with his ledger open along his forearm, near enough to read a heel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:179:"Growth against baseline: confirmed across all demonstrated capabilities. Ceiling on the board raised from four to six, which the enrollee gave before the board gave it, now as then. The combined trial bettered, at the heavier weight. Ignition point shown, and met to the wing's standard; the earlier exhibit thin. One measure unchanged, the plate, and so entered. Trial five void for a fault in the apparatus, and run again on the reserve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:279:*Thirty-five days since the stair. It wakes when I do. Three hours under the Compact's four best chairs, and it stayed in. Cost: one interval and one headache. The Pressure stayed in my arm. Gault wrote "no change" about the plate and then stood up for me over it, which I didn't earn and can't put right.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:67:"Tomorrow you're going to walk into Gault's room and be careful for three hours, and you've never once not been careful in all the time I've known you, and I doubt you've the least idea how it shows from across a floor. It shows like that. So don't hide what it costs. Let it cost you. Breathe hard after the board, and shake the arm out after the plate where they can watch you shake it, and let your fourth run be a fourth run, a little worse than the first, the way fourth runs always are. Pull together for the fifth, and make the pulling visible. Any trainer alive knows a working body when he sees one. Give them that, and they'll stop looking for the other thing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:79:"The wing posts its order for a semester sitting beside the Magister's door, under the calibration card, and I'm fairly sure I'm the only person who has ever read it from top to bottom, the Magister included." She wrote as she spoke. "One, the board. Two, the frame, read only, with your back to the rail. Three, read and move together. Four, the plate. The last two are the first two over again, with an hour of oak in your legs. That's what you did at the baseline. This time the read stops being a trial of its own and gets carried inside the third and the cold runs, which leaves the second slot empty, and the posted sheet says so in so many words: *Two, at the enrollee's placing.* Anything already on your record that you choose to show goes there, or nowhere." She chalked a short dash beside the figure two. "The fifth slot is new: the heaviest weight on the rail, your square brought in close, and a tray of chalk dust beside it so that the clerk can read your feet from the dust instead of from your account of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:81:"The weight I took on the plate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:85:Next to each row went the baseline's figures, small. Four free on the board, the fifth billed, the sixth wide and slow. Eleven calls in twelve off the frame. Seven clear in eight on the combined, at weight three. The plate held low four times, *and not eight*. Then she drew two chalk brackets down the right-hand side of the slate, one above the other, with a gap between them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:103:Karis chose to rehearse the plate first, out of the posted order, because it was the one line on the slate that was not allowed to move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:105:There being no plate in the common room, she handed him the residence's book of hall rules, which was bound in boards nearly an inch thick, and had him hold it out at shoulder height on his right arm, braced the plain way. Brom hit it from the floor, a short heavy sitting blow that went through the book and up the arm and into the shoulder and stopped there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:121:"I hadn't seen that coming, and it would have had you." Her chalk rapped the lower bracket. "For two weeks you've planned against the roof. Don't go over; don't become a question. Now look where you just went. Under. That plate line sits in Gault's own log in Gault's own hand with *four, not eight* beside it, because you said it before he asked, and anybody in that room can lay tomorrow's line over it in the time it takes to blink. Go in frightened and clamp down on everything by feel, and you won't merely scrape the floor; you'll go clean through it. Then the record says that a boy got worse over a whole supervised term while the Compact sat and watched it happen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:125:"So it was never *hold back*," Karis said. "It's *land on the number*. The plate doesn't move by so much as a graduation, and everything else moves exactly as far as a term would move it. That's a great deal harder than holding back, and it's the actual job."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-42.md:33:He had been fourteen in the Denvash certification hall. A clerk behind a long counter there had written a word about him on a form, in about the time it takes to sneeze, and the word had followed him into every room he had entered since. It had stood there talking about him long after the clerk who wrote it had gone home to his supper. That was what files had always been, to him: true in rooms he was not in, kept by people who had long since gone home, and impossible to question or to stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:5:Cael wrote that down first, standing in the doorway of a chandler's that had not yet opened, on the fifth of the new month, with a fine rain deciding whether to begin. Two storeys of brown stone. A doorstep hollowed like a bowl by a hundred years of grain buyers coming in to argue. Beside the door, a brass plate about the size of a book, which said what the building was in letters too small to read from the far side of the street. A rope-maker's on one side of it, with coils of new rope hung in the window like sleeping snakes, and on the other a shuttered house with a cat on its sill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:23:Bracken had come too. He wore his own plain coat instead of the registrar's gown, and he carried a leather satchel with Halcenvane's sponsorship file in it: the certified season record, the witnessed final, Gault's filing on the registry's long form with Ilsev's line under it, and the season's sheets behind them in his numbered order. He had carried it down the hill himself and would not let Brom take it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:77:"Practitioner Lira," he said. "Wind Path. Copper, Rank Two, on her present certificate. Filed for advancement by an assessment office on the record of a season." He did not look at the file. He had read it already. "We'll run the standard evasion sequence first. Then the load figures. Then the frame." He tipped his head toward the wall without turning. "Have you run the sequence before?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:117:A rope off a drum, a sprung plate on the far wall, a measured rod along the floor: how far the burst carried, how much it could carry with it, how fast she was off the line again after the landing. Five runs at each. The woman read the figures aloud as the man called them, so that both of them would have heard every one. Lira did not listen to them. She knew what they were. They had been Iron since Vell kept them in her ledger at Ardenmere, and everyone who had stood at a rail and watched her fight had known it for two years. The only thing that had ever been missing was a station.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:119:So she gave the station its numbers. She took the rope off the drum in her right hand and burst against its pull, and felt the drum fight her and lose. She put her shoulder to the sprung plate at the end of a burst and felt it give and come back. She ran the measured rod five times, landing and leaving, landing and leaving, and on the fourth landing the hip sent up a single bright complaint, like a nail in a boot, and she let it complain and went on. On the fifth she was off the line faster than on the first. The woman read the figure out twice, in case she had misread it, and the man said, "Yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:149:It took ten. A clerk came in from the front office with a second book, thicker than the first, and sat at the end of the table, and entered the supersession in it, line by line, with a pen that scratched. While he wrote, the woman explained to Lira that her Fenmark certificate would not be destroyed. It would remain in the registry as a historical entry, marked superseded, so that the line of her record was unbroken, and any reader in future would be able to see where she had stood before and on what date she had been moved. The prior classification had to be superseded on the same instrument that made the new one, the woman said, or it stayed live underneath, and that caused trouble later.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:195:The certificate was printed. Somebody had filled in the blanks in ink.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:239:She did not look at him. She looked at the bluff, and at the river, and at the certificate in her hand, which she had still not folded again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:69:"If he asks about anything that's already written down somewhere, I tell him all of it, straight. If he asks about anything that isn't, I tell him I won't answer, out loud and in those words, so that the clerk can put down that I said no." He looked at the face-down sheet. "That's the change. In every room before this one, when there's been a thing I couldn't say, I've said something true with a corner cut off it: on Gault's plate yesterday, at the baseline, at Greyvane. Each time it worked, and each time it cost, and each cost went in the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:75:"Then when you say no, put it down like a full plate," he said. "Both hands, all the way to the table, and no rattle."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:135:"On an old clause in a founding schedule," said Cael. "It lets a house take a practitioner the registry can't classify and measure him by what he can show. A directive changed its wording a long while ago and never got round to repealing it, and I'm enrolled on it as a standing, not a favour: every half-year I go in front of the assessment office's panel and show what I can do, and I stay enrolled if I show it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:163:"For three years I've tried every week to make that half-breath shorter on purpose, and I've never managed it; I tried a month ago on the flagstones and failed." He kept his eyes on the man's face and did not try to make the answer look better than it was. "Yesterday a frame failed by nobody's design. It happened once. How, I can't tell you. Promise you a second time and I'd be selling a skill I haven't got. What I own is one accident and a bruise the size of a plate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:247:"Your two friends fought on the house's ladder this season, and the registry hasn't looked at either of their certificates in years. Did the brackets measure them?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:249:"No." Cael did not soften it. "The ladder sorts people by their paper. It was built for practitioners who'd be exactly what their certificates say on the morning they're seeded, and stay so till the season ends. My friends spent the whole year in a cage built for people who did not exist. One of them took the whole cage outright. That's how you know it was one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:203:"The clause is unrepealed. It appears in two independently certified copies of the founding schedule, is incorporated by the receiving institution's charter under a standard article, and is not among the eleven clauses the standardization directive repealed by name in a schedule its drafters had open in front of them. The directive's transitional article presupposes enrollments made under it. The enrollment before us conforms to the clause's language in its basis, its ongoing requirement, and its recording. Whatever else the delegation reports, the basis is sound law."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:211:The Archmarshal's pen lay crosswise on the closed folder, where it had lain since Gault's last line. It did not rise. It had not risen for the counsel's best argument either, nor for Withrow's answer, nor for the second certified copy coming out of the box, and it did not rise now, when a senior evaluator of the Compact stood up and said, into the record, that the boy on the hard chair by the wall was enrolled on sound law.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-54.md:185:Cael had looked at it every time he came into this room and had never once got a name for it. Squat, copper, three short legs, a little larger than two fists: that was the pot. Out of its lid stood a glass straw scored with lines as close as a comb's teeth. A small brass plate on its belly carried a date of calibration in paint, and the date was this month's. The clerk set it on its legs on the oak a pace from the crossing and stepped back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:91:The glass was half run before the yard caught on. Cael heard the moment it did, because the noise did not rise. It sank, and thickened, and turned careful, like a crowd watching somebody carry too many plates.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:197:Ilsev sat very still. A record certified in the morning, before a result anybody could know; and the result added in the afternoon, at the foot, in the same hand, before a thousand witnesses.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:265:The note from the assessment office reached Ilsev a little after the sixth bell, in the delegation's working room, while she was writing up the morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:267:A half-sheet, folded once, in the wing's trained hand. *The assessment office files at the seventh bell, in the Magister's office, an application of the second class. The office invites the senior evaluation seat of the visiting delegation to attend as witness, at her discretion. — Gault.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:299:Then the ground, in the form's printed words, *advancement demonstrated, inconsistent with tier of record*. Then what stood behind it, in Gault's hand. The season, certified; the final, before a witnessed panel; and the panel's own account of what it had seen, attached.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:317:Those were the only two answers that mattered. A record certified after its result has been told the result. An account written by a clerk is an account of what the clerk was told.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:335:She went back up to the working room and wrote one line at the foot of her day's notes: *Witnessed, at the assessment office's invitation, an advancement filing in respect of a Wind practitioner. Filing in order.* Then she turned to the morning's sheets again and finished them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:41:On the last full day of term Cael took the week's coursework down to the assessment wing for the last time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:95:*This year: held back at the plate both times. It's on the Ardenmere circuit record, not on this wing's, and to show it would have been a second surprise. Cost: one flat line on a semester note read in front of the Compact. Gault put his own name behind that flat line with a delegation listening, and never knew what was under it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:142:A table had been carried out under the north arch and set with a green cloth, and Bracken sat at it in his gown with the season's ladder book open in front of him and a box of blank certificate sheets at his elbow. The porter stood at the long board with his ladder. He read each column aloud from the top, name and standing and record, in his round slow voice, and at the table Bracken checked every line against the book, and wrote it fair on a clean sheet, and signed the foot of the sheet and pressed the registrar's seal beside his name. Then the porter wiped that column off the board with his rag, and moved his ladder, and started on the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:146:"Every one," said Karis. "They go into the selection file. Withrow said so. The registrar certifies them before they go in, so that nobody can say afterward they were copied wrong." She watched Bracken's pen. "Three hundred sheets, near enough, across the recess. He'll do them all himself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:204:Brom sat beside her, turning a pebble over and over in the fingers of the hand that had been strapped. He was Copper on his certificate and would be until somebody filed for him. Rooke's cohort, quicker than any other on the river, had him third. One beat had cost him the final; a season and two losses had paid for mending the seam behind that beat. None of it showed on him. He was watching a barge come down toward the bend with the face of a man estimating its load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:212:Sixty feet along, where the coping dipped and a man might lean his elbows on it without looking as though he had meant to stop, a figure in the plain coat of the assessment office was taking the evening air. He had come along the wall the way any of the staff walked at the close of a term, without hurry and without purpose, and he had stopped, and he stood with his hands clasped behind him and his face turned to the river. The barges seemed to interest him. He had been there a few minutes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:5:It was not a large thing: a plain circlet of beaten copper on a brass mount, dented once on the rim where some champion long ago had dropped it, with a plate riveted to the mount that had been riveted on twice because the first rivets had pulled. The porter polished it every term whether anybody had won it or not. Since the final it had acquired a ring of dried tea on its mount where Brom had put a cup down without looking, and a smear of chalk from Lira's thumb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:7:The evening of the fifth found Lira at that table, the Iron certificate folded square under her hand, studying the crown.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:39:"Where else? It's not mine to keep in a box. I've left that bracket, and it won't see me again." She did not turn round. "But nothing else I own says it out loud, with a plate riveted on: the bracket had me wrong. You don't put a thing like that in a drawer. Prizes go to winners. That's what I got for having to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:47:*It was a plan. It took a whole engine of parts: a season on a public board, a final in front of witnesses, a registrar willing to certify the record before the result was known, an officer of record willing to file, an assessor at his elbow to witness the filing, and a Compact evaluation seat sitting in the second row of the north tier while it all happened. Six pieces. Fiske had drawn all six for her in four sentences at a standings board. She had been two years in the same box. And she handed the plan to the one fighter she thought could use it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:67:Beside the board, by the second bell, somebody from the registrar's office had pinned up a copy of the certificate, as the house did with every change of tier, so that anyone who wanted to know why a name had moved could read the reason.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:157:"Don't." He took the accession book from his clerk and ran an eye down the column. "It's filing. I've three hundred season sheets to certify before the selection file can be opened, and a recess to do them in, and every one of them will be read by strangers. Correct filing is all I have to offer anybody this side of the river." He handed the book back. "Carry on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:167:The first part of it was the ordinary business of shutting a house for the recess, and she gave it the attention of a woman counting linen. The roof over the north hall was poor and would be seen to. The wing's frame was being rebuilt, and the man who built frames had been written to, at length. The porters were thanked one by one, by name, and the porter of the Crown yard went very red. Brom shifted on the bench. Lira, beside Cael, sat with one hand inside her coat, resting flat over the pocket where the folded certificate lived, as though it might otherwise get up and leave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:195:"Two: every sheet written about you this year, the ladder's, the season's, the certified results, goes into the file the selectors read, and strangers will read it, and a season chalked on a board goes into the record, whether or not you lived as though it would.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:209:Lira shook her head. "It goes in as a final. Against the champion. Lost by one beat in the fourth exchange on a mended seam." Her palm settled over the certificate in her coat. "If I were a stranger reading it, I'd want to know who you were."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:105:"Copies," Karis said. "The sealed originals sit under the glass down there. He certified both their season records at the first bell this morning, and sealed them, and filed them, and then he walked them down here himself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:177:He knew her only from paper, and from corridors. On paper she was Wind, Copper, Rank Two, on a certificate three years old that nobody had looked at since.

