editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:7:She read the entry first, once, as she read everything. She read it a second time for the words alone, as she had been taught to do, long ago, by a woman who read a form the way a jeweller looks at a stone. *Under*, not *by*. She noted that. Somebody had been careful, at the end of a long night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:15:The designation in the enrollee's file had been her question for a long while. She had first sent it up from Greyvane, plainly, asking who had entered the mark and on whose authority. Twice it had come back down to her with the same two sentences, *no originating authority of record*, and the second time a third sentence latched on behind them, saying the same nothing more firmly. So last year at Halcenvane, on the fifteenth of Reaping, she had sat at the long table in the emptied hall and filled in a second referral, fuller, every date and reference in its order, and marked it a level higher than the office that had answered. In all her years she had never before filed twice on one matter. A question nobody answered was a question to be asked again, through the next door up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:37:What she wrote, nobody ever read, because it was not for any file and not addressed to anybody, and it was the first thing she had written in all her years of service that was not meant to be read by somebody else. She wrote it, and read it through once, and folded it twice, and put it inside her coat, against her chest, where she had never in her life carried anything that belonged to the Compact.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:59:"It came to my office by a tournament page," she said. "It's addressed to you, care of me, which is correct. I've read the outside of it, which is my right as your chancellor. I have not read the inside, which is not." She put it in his hand. "I'll be downstairs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:99:He noticed that before he had written a word, and it was strange enough that he put the pen down and sat for a moment with his hands on the blotter. Every report he had sent north in a year had been built: true in every line, complete in every box the form asked for, and arranged with great care so that nothing in it led anywhere. He had built them as other men build walls, a stone at a time, checking each. This one did not need building. There was nothing to keep out of it. Eight thousand people had watched the thing. Three scribes had written it down at a speed no clerk could match. The west tower had put it in eleven words and hung them in a frame. Whatever he wrote, the world already held more of it than his form had room for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:177:"I can't name him. I'm not certain there is a him. The better question took me a long while to find. Who decides which men are sent minutes, and which men are left off the list?" He said it evenly, as he would have read a line off a form. "I put it on paper once and sent it where such things are meant to go. Within the week it came back. It was very civil and there was nothing in it. I have not asked again." He looked at his left hand. "I've noticed, lately, that I have stopped meaning to. I don't care for that. I tell you so that you'll know it about me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:199:"If I gave you names I would be guessing, and I don't guess." He did not say it to be clever. He said it as Gault said a plate would not read past its last notch. "I can tell you what my year has looked like from my chair. A year ago I was asked what you are. This year I am asked what can be done about you. I answer every one of those letters by saying, again, what you are. Each answer goes up the stair. Each one is entered and filed. I am fairly sure none of them is read." He turned his left hand over, and back. "My office still works. It works very well. It has simply stopped mattering. When the questions stop being questions, an evaluator knows the decision has been taken in some room he was not shown."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:229:"With the wrong instruments, for three days," he said. "I came up to the bluff with a file and a plate and a set of questions, and all three told me nothing. A man in my trade has one great temptation, and I gave in to it. I thought the nothing was something being hidden from me." He moved the left hand. "It is the oldest mistake there is. When an instrument won't read, you'd sooner believe it's broken than believe you've pointed it at a thing it was never made to measure."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:5:Examinations had claimed the other three. One of the third-years had left a note on the slate's ledge in a large apologetic hand, and Seln read it, folded it once, and put it in his pocket without remark. Then he chalked up the evening's work.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:63:They had the long table nearest the hearth. The counsel talked, as she had talked the whole road from the coast, about inns: this one's mutton, which was good, and its beds, which had been aired. Then she told of a magistrate she had once watched eat a whole game pie at this very table so that he would not have to answer a question put to him between courses. The records officer handled his fork like an instrument under calibration. The assessor ate a little bread and went up before the pudding. Vastin asked the counsel what the question had been, which pleased her a great deal, and listened to the answer, and did not say one word about Halcenvane.
