editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:105:He had been looking at the boy, not the frame, when it went. That had been luck and nothing else; at that moment he had been reading the set of the boy's shoulders, the way a man reads a hand of cards he has not been dealt. The order of it, therefore, he had seen. The hips had dropped for the burst. The held breath had started; there was a small hitch at the throat that went with it every time. And then, before any sound from the rack, before the drag and the late clack that had turned every other head in the room, the whole of that answer had been stopped where it stood and folded back into a quarter of itself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:107:The sound had come after. He was as sure of that as he was of anything he had seen in a measured room. He had heard the catch of the notch while he was already watching the boy go short.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:125:He had set it down in the quarter of an hour the porters took to fetch the reserve frame from the far store, with the notebook open on his knee and the boy at the west rail with his weight off one leg. He had written fast. Then at the very end, when the wing was emptying and he was on his feet with the chair pushed back, he had taken the book out again and drawn a single line through three of the words. One stroke, so that the words could still be read under it. A man who blacks out his own error so that nobody can see what it was has told a lie about his own mind, and Vastin did not tell those on paper if he could avoid it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:137:The struck words had been a verdict. They might even be the right verdict; turning them over now, cold, he thought the odds were better than even. But he had set them down within minutes of the frame failing, before the re-run, before he had watched the boy go back onto the reserve frame with a leg that had just paid for a floor and tell the panel, unasked, which of his runs were the hip's and not his. A finding written that fast is a stake driven before the survey. Every measurement taken afterward gets read as a line toward the stake. The surveyor thinks he is checking his work, and he is only admiring it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:159:That one he had written at the eighth bell in the Crown yard, as the tiers emptied, and had never needed to touch. He had had it by the fourth exchange, watching a Wind fighter on a hip that had no business holding her take an Iron Skin fighter apart with nothing but distance, in a bracket built by people who had imagined neither of them. One day someone at his own height would be asked whether the house's filing for her was sound. He had answered already. There was nothing else worth an Archmarshal's doing at a bracket final. The answer would stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:189:The wing's six filings were not in the folder. He had read them on his first morning on the bluff, the thirteenth, before the first bell, and put them where they belonged in his own reckoning, a good way under the record: a capable officer's honest account of the chair he had been given and the list he had been handed. There had been nothing in them he needed twice. He had tied the pink tape again knot over knot, as it had come, and set the case by the door for the clerk to carry home to the wing, and he did not think of it now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:205:A finding is read once, by the people it concerns, and filed. A file note goes on being read, by every officer who ever lifts the folder down, for as long as the folder exists. Whoever came to this folder after him would come to it through his note, and would read the rest in its light, whether they meant to or not. He had spent his life refusing to let other men's digests do that to him. He had no wish to do it to anyone else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:213:He looked at it. It was true. It was also a door shut in the face of whoever might need it open in a year, and it said nothing that the findings had not already said. He folded the scrap in half.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:245:He did not underline anything. He read it once, and it still meant what he meant, and he knew no better proof for a sentence than that. The tapes went back round the folder, grey, red and string. He put the two halves of the torn scrap into the lamp's chimney, one after the other, and watched them go. Then he turned the lamp down to nothing and stayed at the dark window over the empty yard, his left hand, which had begun to dislike evenings as well as mornings, lying open on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:35:So they walked beside the cart, all of them, and on the worst pitches Brom and Seln put their backs to the tailboard, and Cael put his right shoulder there, low, where it did not have to lift. Karis carried a wedge of ash from the crossroads fire, and each time the gelding stopped she had it under the wheel before the cart could run back a hand's breadth. She did it forty times before noon, and her face was grey with it, and she would not give the wedge to anyone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:87:Seln had not come to the board. He stood inside the door with his satchel on him, and he was reading the room, not the wall; Cael saw his eyes go to the drawer in the table, and stay there a breath, and move on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:97:Brom came and stood at Cael's shoulder. He read the notice down, from *Copper* to *Gold*, and he was a long while at the second word. Then he read the sum.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:157:There had been a ferry there once. Cael saw the posts first, two on each bank, black and leaning, and a flat boat lying on the near shingle with its bottom stove in and grass growing through it. It had been kept for when the water was up and a loaded wagon could not ford. A board lay face down in the grass by the nearer post, and he turned it over with his foot. The paint had gone, but the letters had been cut before they were painted, and he could read them. *Foot. Horse. Cart, loaded.* The registry's seal was at the head of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:201:After the age there was nothing. The reading was blank, and the narrow column, and *Entered*. The keeper's hand had come as far as the fourth column and had not gone into it, and there was no blot, and no stroke through the line. Below it the leaf was ruled and empty to its foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:373:He had not answered, for there had been no answer he could put into a pouch that a clerk would read for completeness. There was no pouch out here. He tore a clean leaf from the back of the observation notebook and laid it on the book's cover, under the pin of light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:377:*The rematch stands. Not postponed — relocated. It happens on a floor nobody owns, and I think you'd prefer that anyway; a Gold-tier fact and an unclassified one, fighting where no seal can score either of us, is the only honest version of the bout, and we both knew it at Norhold. Find me when you're ready to fight something no ladder built. I'll be east.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:3:By the time Cael reached the records hall on the twenty-third day, the seat's notice had been open on the counter for a quarter of an hour, and Karis had already read it upside down twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:113:Nine. And every one of them, Cael realized, was already somewhere in the right-hand column of Seln's sheaf. Not one was a witness, and not one was a finding about anything that had happened in a room. There was nothing on the leaf a person could be asked a question about, or caught out in, or shown to have misremembered. They were texts, the registry's own furniture, and a man did not bring furniture to prove a thing; he brought it to read aloud from, at the moment of his choosing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:141:"His advisory is an exhibit. It speaks for itself. I'd not ask a man to stand up in a room and repeat a paragraph he's already signed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:183:Karis had not laughed. She was looking at the summary in her lap without reading it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:9:He got up and dressed and put the copy in its folder under his arm, because he did not want to leave it on the sill for the porter's boy to read, and went down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:47:"He'd hate that." She turned another page. "I'll use it instead. He'll know I've used it, because he'll see the shape of it in what goes down the hill, and that's the kind of thanks he'd let me give." She looked up at him at last. Her eyes were red at the rims and perfectly clear in the middle. "Sit down. You're going to be told the rules once, and then you're going to read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:51:Four days to the answer's bones, said Karis. After that, the flesh, and then Bracken and the house's counsel and the weighing, and then it went down the hill with time in hand. Every leaf of the code that left the records hall came back to it at night, because Bracken would not sleep with the press unlocked and she would not ask him to. Brom would carry. Brom had already been told and had already said yes, in one word, at the foot of the stair at the second bell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:91:Nobody had asked him that about the name before. Not at Denvash, where Pellin had stamped it on his card; not at Greyvane, where it had been read out over his head; not on any form since. Once, at Ardenmere, he had asked Vell to put it in her book the right way round, which was the nearest he had come. The registry gave him the name the way it gave a ledger a number. People said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:105:The first was Karis's own finding from Greyvane, the long one, the one she had built from the standardization directive's repeal schedules a line at a time until she could say, flatly, in front of a seat, that nobody had ever repealed the clause. It had been left behind, like a child at a fair, when everything round it was swept up and carried off. The second was the house counsel's reconstruction, made the year after, by another road, through the registry's own index of surviving provisions, without reading a word of Karis's first. The third was Prynn's copy and the note on its cover, from the archive at Greyvane, which showed the clause standing in the founding text with nothing written against it in two hundred years of hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:121:Cael read everything that day. That was the arrangement: Karis wrote, and he read. Not for approval, she said; because it was about him, and she did not put her name to documents about people who had not read them. He read at the window with the light behind him and the pages on his knee, and when he had read a leaf he laid it face down on the sill, and Karis took it back without asking what he thought, because if he had thought anything he would have said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:147:He remembered the room by its east wall, because that was where the strangers had been. Four chairs set out by Bracken at a guest's distance from one another. The delegation's counsel in the nearest, untying a folder's ribbon with her teeth. Ilsev beside her, sharpening a pencil into a fold of paper on her knee. Havel with a board across his knees, writing the date. And in the fourth chair the Archmarshal, with nothing in his hands at all. Three hours of instruments on the oak under those four chairs: the frames, the board, the pendulum ticking in its case. Then Gault reading his note aloud at the trestle, and the Ash instructor signing, and the Mire instructor adding her four lines about the faulty frame, and the clerk bringing the stamp down on the house's copy. *Renewed.* That had been the day, and the house's copy had been stamped on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:203:"I saw that two papers disagreed. I couldn't see why." She did not pretend otherwise. "I could have spent a morning in the inspection's papers. Or I could hand the table to the one of us who was in Gault's office that afternoon, and not say what I was looking for." She did look at him then. "If I'd told you there was a mistake on it, you'd have found one. You'd have found one on a clean leaf. I wanted to see what you'd find if you were only reading. Gault is a signature now. The Archmarshal is a chair. You're the one witness who can't be replaced, and witnesses see more when they don't know what they're being asked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:27:She got it wrong the second time. She held the third count, but she held it with her shoulders, and a held shoulder told a watcher more than a moving one. The Stone came and stood beside her and did the four counts himself, very slowly, a Silver doing a child's exercise, with nothing in his shoulders at all. On the fourth he did not burst. He only lifted his back heel a finger's breadth off the boards, and it was enough. Cael, at the rail, felt his own weight go the wrong way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:113:"It makes her kind. Not much. A doubt she lets go by. A page she doesn't turn back to. Nothing she'd ever write down, and nothing she'd ever forgive in anybody else." She went in at last and set the books down on the long table under the window, and he followed with the tea. "I've seen it happen to better readers than me. Every room I've worked in before this one was somebody else's to take back, and I always knew where the door was, and I read faster for knowing it." She turned and looked at the lock from the inside. "It's the first one I've ever wanted to stay in. So I'm going to watch myself in it. I'm telling you, so that there are two of us watching."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:133:The intake had found the counter. A Stone first-year stood in front of Cael holding his form in both hands as if it might go off. Ahead of the Stone, two Current girls argued in whispers about a drill neither of them had run yet, and ahead of them a Mire third-year studied the notice board with great attention so that nobody would think he was waiting for anything. Cael had a floor-allocation sheet in his own hand, because evaluation-linked hours were lodged here or nowhere, and he spent the wait reading the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:179:One of the pair at the road's foot was grey in the beard and leaned on a stick he did not seem to need. The other kept his hands in his coat and looked at the river. Neither had raised his eyes to the bluff since the change. The younger coats of other years had craned at the walls like visitors to a cathedral, and shifted their feet a little more each week as the place came clear to them. These two stood as if there were nothing on the hill they had not already been told about. They had the patience of men near the end of a long errand, who know the one thing they have been sent to see and do not mean to miss it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:23:Cael had the rail at the north end, where he had stood on the second morning of the year to watch the red book. Brom stood on his left with his arms folded. Karis stood on his right with her notebook shut and her pen in her hand. Ephram was three steps down the gallery stair with the captain's file under his arm, where he could see the whole floor and be seen by the whole cohort, which Cael supposed was the point. The girl from the reading room's outer table had found a place against the far wall of the gallery, with her small notebook open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:39:From the rail there was nothing else to watch. A Force fighter's shove went on contact, and this one's contact was the floor. He stamped with his front foot, and a beat later the boards ahead of the stamp, out to a stride, jumped, as a table jumps when somebody slaps it from beneath, and went on jumping for a count or two before they lay down. Then the shove came back up into him along the line it had gone out by, and his knees gave and his hips dropped a finger's breadth while he took it. That was the settle. Cael could see it from the rail and hear the boards answer it. It was the bill for the last stamp, paid before the next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:237:The first came on the thirty-first day, to Bracken's counter, in the district seat's grey stock with the district's own small seal, and Bracken carried it across to the refectory himself at the noon meal. He did not lay it on the table this time. He stood at the end of the middle table and read it aloud, all of it, which he had never done with anybody's paper in that room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:271:Nobody saw him do it. Nobody at the table learned what it said, because he did not tell them, then or afterward. What they saw was his face, when he came down to supper an hour later than usual and sat in his place and pulled the bread toward him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:275:Cael looked at it once, as long as a man looks at a letter that is not addressed to him, and then he looked at his bread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:5:He understood that only as he opened his mouth to start, sitting on the bench in the circle of the shaded lamp with Seln across the bare cloth from him. Lira had learned it in a yard at Ardenmere with grit in her palms, one piece at a time, because she had been there when each piece happened. Brom had learned it from Lira and from his own three mornings in an alcove, and had asked the rest across a table, and been told. Karis had come to it at Greyvane with most of it already worked out on paper in her own hand, and had asked only for the parts she could not get by reading. Each of the three had it whole now. Not one of them had ever heard it in order, start to finish, in one telling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:43:Seln had not moved once. He had not turned his cup, because there was no cup. He had not looked away from Cael's face in all that time, and his own face had shown nothing at all, as it showed nothing at the counter. But Cael had watched him listen, and he listened the way Karis read, all of it, to the end, before he let himself think a single thing about any of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:121:He told it the way he had written it, standing against a wall under a lamp in the covered walk with the notebook held to the stone at the third hour. The archive at Halcenvane on a bad night two years ago, near enough, in the first year he was on the hill. The fire-watch gone the long way round. A man with a bag at the top of the upper flight, in the doorway, with his gloved hand already going into a press he had no business opening. And on the landing above him, in the light, the wing's assistant. He was reading the man a form, one plain line after another, about copying and paper and what a magistrate counted in years, until the man's hand came back out of the press with nothing in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:152:He told the rest quickly, because the rest was only time. Two years, near enough, since the stair. It had never been used anywhere, by anybody's count. He had never once asked it for anything in front of a living soul, save once on the first night, to get past a lantern, and then it had not come. Rent from waking every day, spent or not, the way a room costs rent whether you sleep in it or not. Drifts in the gaps, never in the work: a thinning at his edges on a stair or in a queue, when he was tired. Lira had counted them for him on the back of a chair with her thumbnail, and the count had fallen every week. Its two halves would not sit together in his hands. To hide moving he had to read the room, and to read the room he had to be the most awake thing in it, and being that awake was what put the hiding out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:178:Cael did not watch his face. Two years across a counter had taught him that it was the last place on the man where anything would show. He watched the hands instead, flat on the green cloth, and saw the fingertips pressed down until they had gone pale, the way a clerk presses a ledger open at a page he means to read back through from the top. Cael knew which pages. The counter, the bench and the cup. The slips on the common-room fire. A season's figures that never once settled into a line. An afternoon at Norhold where everything held back went out in one breath, except one thing. Somewhere in all that, a boy had been holding a coat over him and never saying so, and the hands were finding each place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:186:He sat a while longer. Then he took his hands off the table for the second time that night, and folded them in front of him on the cloth. Cael had never seen him fold his hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:202:"While its use can be seen by anyone who could carry the seeing west, it stays where it is." Seln said it slowly and exactly, the way he wrote a line in a return that he meant to be read by somebody careful. "Not for my file, which can look after itself now; it has had practice. For the trade. Somewhere, in a room I will never see, there is a man whose whole work is sorting, and any report of a thing that moves the way I move gives him a thread to pull. Where there is no witness who can carry it west, there is no thread, and then it is yours. Use it or don't, as you judge." He unfolded his hands. "And you will not ask me first. That is the other term."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:226:Karis stayed, because she would turn the lamp in the window down at its usual hour and not a minute before. Brom went first, by the iron stair through the stacks, and Cael heard the ninth tread on the second flight say what Brom had said it would. Seln went second, by the bindery stair, and did not say goodnight. At the door he stopped and looked back at the shelf, at the dark leather case standing between two of Karis's grey boxes like one more box with no label, and then at Cael, and then he went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:23:The code gave him his phrase. An order was executed when its hour had been kept and its room opened, and this one had been executed, in every particular, against nobody. He wrote *Re-evaluation: not executed; evaluand not produced*, which was how the form said the same thing. The president read the line and initialled it. Then the officer put the cover back over the frame and locked the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:61:It was the third thing from the top, and he took it in its turn. It was a single leaf of registry stock under the seal of the seat at Ostrand, and he knew the form. When a proceeding closed, its seat sent a summary to every office that had been named in it, and each office returned the leaf with whatever it wished to have stand beside the record. His office had been named twice: once as the author of an advisory, three sentences long, which the petition had quoted with approval, and again as the certifying office, which had been asked for its name and had declined to give it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:75:*Closed by resignation.* He turned that one over longest. He had seen four young people and an older man in a room with nothing of anybody's in it, and had read what they brought him, and had told them to their faces what he would not do. He had not asked what they would do. It had not been his to ask, and he had thought it better not to carry the answer. He saw it now, in a clerk's round hand: they had not appealed, and they had not published; they had waited for the one door in the code that left a house clean, and they had walked out of it in good order, with a registrar's certificate behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:79:He was glad of it. It was not a thing to write in a margin, and he noted it only in passing, as he would have noted a reading that came out a hair above tolerance.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:83:There were true things he might have set there. He considered each of them, as he considered everything, and with each he saw at once what would be done with it. He had written three true sentences about this practitioner before, in an advisory, as exactly as he could make them, and somebody had lifted them out and used them as a warrant. He had petitioned to be heard and been refused in two lines. He had written a finding by a candle and addressed it to nobody; it was in the lowest drawer, under a tab, and no proceeding had ever come that was worth sending it to. The one sentence of his that they still needed he had withheld, and a clerk had entered its absence on a form.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:175:There would be a line like it about them at every water from here to the last stamp. *Four, a cart, eastbound.* He did not mind. It was true, and whoever read it would learn only that they had gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:251:Brom did not sit at once. He stood at the fire's edge and looked round the circle, slowly, and Cael watched him do it and could see the large head mark each of them as it turned. Lira had her boots off already. Karis sat very upright. A man in a dark coat had his hands round a cup he had not yet turned. Then Brom looked at Cael, and last, briefly, down at himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:291:Cael ate at noon. It was not much, a heel of the guesting-house bread and a piece of cheese, but it stayed where he put it, and afterward he wanted more. Brom, who had been watching him with no attempt to hide it, took a second piece of cheese out of his own coat and held it out without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:9:It was an evaluator in the east, on a station circuit, about a young man of a carters' guild whose floor work ran a full rank ahead of his file. The evaluator had measured it at a frame and again on a guild floor and again by the clock, and he wrote that the file was plainly wrong, and asked that the young man be read again. At the foot of his report, in another hand, a registry clerk's stamp had been pressed square: *Read against file. Enter at file.* And under the stamp, a month later, the evaluator again, smaller: *Continue observation.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:15:There were twenty-three. She read every one, and she read the stamp at the foot of every one, and after the sixth she stopped reading the stamp aloud because everybody in the room could say it with her. A woman in the hill country whose Stone came up too heavy for its column. A ferryman's boy whose Current had something of Wind in it that his station had not written down. A girl at a guild hall in the south who did everything her Path said she should, the evaluator wrote, only as if she were older than her paper. Each report saw the same thing and asked for the same thing, and each came back stamped the same way. *Read against file. Enter at file.* Some carried *Continue observation* under the stamp in the evaluator's own hand, and some carried nothing, and one carried a single line that Karis read twice before she read it out: *Noted, and I do not like it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:23:"Not in this bundle. There's not one report where somebody was read higher than his paper and the paper went up to meet him." She looked at Seln. "Was there one in the press?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:67:He could not help it. Under every seal there was a line of them, three or four to an issue, and he went down them the way he went down the names on a bout card, by habit, and without meaning to he had counted. Nineteen issues and seventy-one names, give or take two he could not read in the yellow ones where the ink had gone brown and spread. Officers of the registry, by the titles after them: a chief clerk of entry, a keeper of the rolls, a second secretary, men and women whose offices Bracken would have known and Cael had never heard of. Not one of them appeared on two issues: ten years at a desk, and then a new name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:95:"*Of the advisory corrections function. The function is appended to the reading and is not the reading. It does not alter what the reading shows. It advises what is to be entered, and the entry is made as advised. It is to be maintained, and its maintenance is the office's charge.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:107:"Yes." Karis did not look up. "And it's not the reading. It says so, and it goes out of its way to say so." She took a slip of paper from the pocket of her coat and a pencil, and wrote one word on the slip, and turned the slip face down on the cloth before anyone could read it. "I'm not going to say what I think, not tonight. I don't guess past paper, and I won't start in front of the paper that would make me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:115:*The function is appended to the reading and is not the reading.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:121:The leaf said there were two things in that office, not one. There was the reading, and there was something appended to it. And there had been eleven seconds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:129:He did not sit down to say it, and he did not raise his voice. He stood at the middle of the table's far side with the whole of the case laid out in front of him in the lamplight, the forty and the bundle and the two stacks and the leaf, and he said it to the room the way he had once read a form to a man in a doorway at the third hour, one plain sentence after another.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:187:Karis had taken her gloves off and laid them side by side on the cloth beside the leaf. She was looking at the yellow stack again, at the memoranda, and when she spoke it was the voice she kept for craft, a reader asking another reader how he had done a thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:205:Seln looked round the room in his order again, the door and the windows and the landing and the shelves. It did not take him as long the second time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:15:The letter said where the rubbing had come from, in its first paragraph, with the instrument quoted whole: it was the seal off the escort order that the leader of four riders had read aloud in the house's outer court on the Sixth-day morning, and that the house had declined at its own gate and let ride away again down the bluff road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:21:It was a good rubbing, and it was also a good seal. That was the first thing he noticed and the thing he went on noticing all afternoon: whoever had cut the die had cut it properly. The river ran the right way; the scale's beam was level. The beading round the rim was even and close, and in the beading, at the bottom, where every registry die carried its cutter's number, there were four small figures that the pencil had picked out faintly, as a pencil does when it passes over something raised. He took the reading glass out of his kit and bent over them until he was sure of all four.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:129:It was a flat chain ferry, broad enough for one coach and its team and nothing else. A great chain ran from an iron ring sunk in the rock on the near side, down under the water, and up again to a winch-house on the far bank, and the ferry hauled itself along the chain by a wheel turned from its deck. The river came through the narrows deep and fast and brown and very loud. But it could not spread. There was no width for it to throw anything about in. It simply went through, as water goes through a pipe, and the ferry sat on top of it on its chain like a cat on a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:175:Cael counted the cut out of the window as they went, because he counted everything. Four hundred and forty yards, near enough, by the coachman's pace and the bends at each end. At the far end, the east end, the road narrowed to cross a culvert where a stream came down a gully off the hill and went under the road toward the river, with a low stone parapet on either side. Beside the near parapet stood a milestone. He could not read it from that distance. It would say seven.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:191:It went out of him through the hand into the ground the way it went into a floor, the cheapest thing he owned and the one he trusted most. It did not read chalk the way it read a man. It read weight. The road under him was carrying the coach, four tons of it through the wheels, the horses in front, the cart's dead weight across the crown forty yards on. All of that was ordinary, and he knew the shape of ordinary.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:221:He did not need to see it. He had read her four times across forty feet of stone in the outer court six days ago, and drawn her on the oak in chalk, and written her shape in the notebook with no numbers in it. She had stood on the one level place in the court. She stood on the one level place in the cut. Every line out of the cut went past her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:245:Lira got down at the coach's tail and stood with her weight on the balls of her feet, looking at the four men by the ditch and the parapet, at their feet, not their eyes. Karis came out last, on the north side, and went to the near horse's head. The horse was standing very still. Too still. Cael saw Karis put out her hand toward its foreleg and stop, a hand's breadth short, as if she had touched a wall in the dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:3:The notice on the assessment wing's board was in Gault's hand this week, not the desk clerk's, and Cael read it twice before he understood why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:7:The first two sentences were only sense: the seat sat on Fifth-days, and so did wing three, and the provision would have to give way to the registry and not the other way round. It was the third sentence he read twice. In the two years he had been reading that board, nobody had ever needed a line about observers. The people who watched the provision's bouts were the cohort, and the cohort came in at the south door and wrote its name in nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:25:*Halcenvane's enrollment stands on a provision the registry has not set aside, and has been entered in full on the public record. Each chartered house must read that provision for itself.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:121:Karis went second, at a steady pace, with her hands held a little out from her sides as if she were carrying a tray, and stopped at the right distance. She would have done well, the gardener said, if she had not then stood and read the date cut into his lintel, with her lips moving, for as long as it took him to wonder what she wanted with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:229:Cael felt the hand touch his head and go forward, and heard nothing for a count of ten. Then the hand came back, and touched him again, and went behind him, and he understood that Brom was going over the line a second time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:31:"The ruling says five weeks," she said. "That is the outside of it. The inside is in a section upon notices, which gives a district five days, and in the habits of a district clerk who has never in my time notified a date one day later than the code allowed him. Bracken went to the district hall on our first morning in the town, to look at the room where Brom would stand, and there is a board beside its door. He read on it that the station's frame-house was reserved for the registry's use on the last day of the year." Her voice was quite even. "It was written up before any ruling was given, and I want you to understand that there was nothing crooked in that. A prudent clerk books a frame when he sees that one may be wanted, exactly as Bracken books a coach, and he would have struck it off again without a thought if the seat had gone the other way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:35:"It is five days. An officer will ride up to my gate with the notice tomorrow, or the day after, and it will be addressed as the last one was: to the chancellor, to produce the enrollee. You will still be mine on that day, for the revocation does not bite until its close." She looked down at the four lines. "I read an order aloud at that gate once and declined it, because nobody could tell me whose it was. This one would be a seat's, and I could not decline it. I should have to walk you into that frame-house myself, under the house's seal, or else refuse a seat in writing and let them enter the refusal in my folder."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:49:"Four chancellors advised me this term to let you go," said Withrow. "I answered every one of them in my own hand, and told them that the harm was done the day a house handed over an enrollee to spare itself. I believed it when I wrote it." She looked at him directly. "And then a small woman with a steel rule showed me that I need hand nobody over. I need only mention a door: a quiet word, in a guesting-house parlour, from a chancellor to an enrollee who has spent a year trying not to cost her anything. You'd have gone that night. Don't tell me you wouldn't. You'd have gone for my folder, and I should have done with one sentence what I refused to do under four riders."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:83:"There is no form for taking a leaf out of that folder," she said. "I told you so. There is also no rule against putting one in. I have a closing in the registry's presses under an enrollee's name, which I can cite, and a magister's note for the seventy-sixth day. I have thirty-four chancellors who wrote to ask me what I would do, and who now owe me the courtesy of reading what I did. And I have a right-hand column in a great many places besides this one." She came round the end of the desk. "I do not know what took you up that stair tonight with four others. I am not a fool, and I can see that it was more than a ruling, and I am not going to ask. Bracken asks when. I find I do not want even that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:121:"I said I'd know what I was answering," said Brom, when he saw where Cael was looking. "I do. I find I needn't read the question." He took the book and read the leaf through at his own pace, and set his mark at the foot, the B with the bar. "They can lie there, the two of them. I've signed nothing and I've refused nothing. Somebody will find them when the room's cleared and wonder." He gave the book back. "He'll hit you hard. He hits harder than he looks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:163:She read the leaf standing, by what was left of the lamp. She had read one of these before, in a week of rain, and had written across the bottom of it in capitals that nobody was to go in after him alone. That one had been about a woman on a road who meant to put him in a wagon; it had frightened her, and it had been simple. This one was not simple. She read down it, through the forearm and the lanes, and came to the part where the pen had plainly stopped and started again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:185:He had not waited for the porter, or for morning. Somebody had come to his door at midnight and told him, and he had put on his coat and gone down to the yard in the dark and chalked it himself, before any officer of the house could wake up and wonder whether it was wise. She watched until he climbed down and held the lantern up to read it over, and then rubbed out one word with his cuff and wrote it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:255:It did not stop, and that was new. The first cut had not finished before Ephram's front heel was down again, a pace on, and a second cut was already beginning out of the end of the first, a different way in, flat, at the ribs. Heel, hip, edge. He could not take the first one back and had not tried to; he had only built the second on top of it, as a mason lays a course.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:293:Cael stood on the south chalk with the sun on his back, and he could feel every one of them above him. He had felt eleven hundred empty seats on his neck at dawn in his first week, and it was nothing to this. Withrow sat in the registrar's chair, and Gault had signed two years of his figures, and a coat held a pencil at the top of the west tier. And there were four hundred people he had eaten beside since he came up the road, who knew him as a quiet enrollee with a provision and a decent read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:313:Four. He went past the wide cut on the outside, and Ephram turned on the miss faster than a man of his size ought to turn. A line was already there. He had not cut it; he had turned his wrist, and the lath lay level across the place where Cael's next landing would come down, because he read lines, and knew what would cross one a breath before it crossed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:329:He saw it with a beat to spare, and understood it in the same instant. A lane was a line. It was the longest, plainest line anybody had ever laid on that oak, with dust running down it to show the way, and Ephram had not needed to know what it was. He had read where it went, and had put his edge at the end of it and stood there, waiting, with his heel down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:339:*Two. Three.* Three cuts in a chain, heel and hip and edge. Cael asked for the Wind and it came after he had asked, and he got out from under the first by the width of a sleeve. *Four.* The second he took on his raised left forearm, with the give open, and it opened late, and the edge went through it. *Five.* He had been right about that too. The give was a cup, and a cup holds a blow, and this was not a blow; it was a line of cold drawn across the bruise that was already there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:101:The seat wanted the maker of the copies to swear to them, and the advocate of record to sign beside his oath. Karis had said at supper that she would not put her name to a page she had not read against the thing it was copied from, and that this was not caution but manners. So the wing's original cards had come up the library stair in two shallow boxes under the porter's oilcloth, with Seln's leave and Bracken's receipt, and the second set of Seln's copies lay open beside them. He had made two of everything. One had gone down to the seat in the house's boards. The other had stayed in the annex, against exactly this.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:111:It went on like that, and Cael had not expected it to be hard to listen to, and it was, because he knew every one of those afternoons. He had chosen each figure the night before it was earned, on a page at the back of Hesk's book, and spent the bout earning it to the decimal, and walked off the boards afterward exactly as tired as the rotation had said he would be. Hearing them read out in a row, the whole year so far, in the girl's careful voice, was like hearing somebody read your letters back to you. Every one of them was true. None of them was him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:135:The girl went on. *Fifth-day, the ninth week.* Cael watched her, and then watched Karis, who had not once looked up at him. She did not need to; the year was in her hands, and she was reading it straight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:237:The rest went down quickly after that, because it was the kind of thing he had written many times before. A week at least without the band, so wing three to be excused on some true ground. The lane not to be laid, and the read not to be trusted, until whatever came had finished coming. Somebody in the room with him every hour of the first two days, because at Norhold the first two days had been the bad ones. Rooke to be told nothing but what was true and small.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-24.md:3:The bargeman did not move from his mark while the steward read the bout in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-24.md:5:Cael had seen a great many fighters stand at a great many marks, and almost all of them did something there. They rolled their shoulders, or bounced, or looked at the crowd, or looked very hard at nothing. The Silver stood at his chalk at the square's south end as a post stands in a quay, with his weight even on both feet and his hands hanging open at his sides, and looked at the boards between himself and Lira as if he were reading the grain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-24.md:7:He was a big man, though not as big as Brom. He was thick through the hips and short in the neck, with grey coming in at the temples and forearms that had spent a life on wet rope. He wore a plain shirt and the guild's dark sash knotted at the hip, and his feet were bare. Somebody on the south benches called his name, and somebody else, and then the whole south side of the hall took it up, a deep warm roar with a laugh in it, the sound of a town shouting for one of its own. He did not look up at it. He lifted one hand off his thigh a finger's breadth and let it fall again, and the roar went up twice as loud.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-24.md:27:The Silver did not come forward. Cael had known he would not, from Rooke's bread, and still it was strange to watch. A Force fighter at Iron came at you, because he had to lay his courses where you were. This one stood on his chalk and settled his knees, a long slow settle like a man lowering himself onto a bench, and the boards in front of him began to hum.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-24.md:33:She was testing. He could see her doing it, because he had watched her test a hundred new floors at dawn. She set the ball of the foot down at the edge of the humming, lightly, and kept her weight back on the other, and felt. Then she lifted the foot and set it a hand's breadth further in, and felt again. Her heel did not come down. She was reading the course the way a blind woman reads a face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-24.md:45:He had not walked there. He had simply laid his next course a stride to his left, and then the next a stride beyond it, so that the humming floor spread toward the square's middle and the square's middle was suddenly his. He had built a wall of warm boards between Lira and the open floor without crossing any of it, and the only open ground left to her ran south and west, along the ropes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-24.md:55:Cael heard it before he saw it: a single deep knock under the boards, like a barge coming against a quay. The course the Silver had been laying all the while finished, and the floor under the square's west side heaved. It did not throw Lira. It did something worse. It lifted the boards under her a finger's breadth all at once and set them down again a hand's breadth to the north, and she went with them, because there was nothing else to do. She went away along the west rope at a run she had not chosen, trying to get her feet back under her, and the north-west post came up out of the lamplight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-24.md:99:Cael could not read his weight from where he sat; nobody could read a man's weight at that distance across a moving floor. But he did not need to. He could see the man's face. It had been courteous for an exchange and a half, a good craftsman's face, kind to a younger one. Now it was simply interested. He looked at her as a man looks at a job he had thought would take an afternoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-24.md:107:Cael knew it would be within ten breaths. The Silver's settles were short now, the Stone's short settle, the one he used in a hurry, and the courses came up out of the floor faster than Lira could find their edges. He laid one, and one under it, and one under that, and the hum in the boards went down and down until Cael felt it in his teeth. Lira stepped in toward his front foot twice, on her feet and not her bursts, and twice he was already standing on something else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-24.md:111:He did not push. He leaned. He laid his courses in a fan behind her, one after another, each one finishing on its fourth count just as she had passed over it, so that every few breaths the floor at her heels rose a finger's breadth and set her down again a little further on. It was nothing she could fight. It was like standing on a raft while a bargeman leaned on the water with his pole, and the raft going where he wanted and not where you were standing. She went with it because there was nothing to push against, and she went south and west, a step and a step and a step, until she turned with her push foot set for the burst that would take her back at him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-24.md:157:The second time she nearly had it. She lied with her front foot and her shoulders and went on the fourth count, from the side he had last turned away from, and there was a gap there no wider than a door between his settle and his stamp, and she went into it with her sixth burst.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-24.md:205:He did not say it to either of them. He said it to his board, and he wrote it there while he said it, in his square capitals, and Cael read it upside down. *South-west quarter, crossways boards, exchange three. The stewards wrote that they'd mended something. I walked this floor at first light looking for it and pressed that corner three times and missed it. Mine.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:45:"Then they got the Fenmark sheet." She looked down at the bean and not at any of them. Everybody at that table knew what was at the bottom of her file, under four years of other people's honest lines, and nobody said it, and she did not say it either. After a moment she looked across at Cael. "Your column. Under my name. That they read it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:95:It did not work. He heard it not work as he read it. The dog and the stove and the level went out into the room and fell on the table among the beans, and none of them could get Hesk back out of the third paragraph. He stopped reading and folded the letter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:123:"For a ruling." Seln's voice did not change. "Think what five offices' clerks cost for nine weeks. An office spends that only when it already believes it knows the answer. So if the walking has begun, then somebody well above the seat has settled in his mind how the seat will rule, and has started paying for the morning after. The walking is a finding of its own. It's made by people who don't sit in the seat, and made first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:157:"Write it." The fourth bean went into the pocket. "Give it to the registrar for his pouch, not to the town post. The pouch is locked in Ostrand and opened here, and I open it." He put the fifth bean away. "Letters from a house under inventory are read. Not by anyone wicked. By a clerk, for completeness, who will copy a line of it into a file. Don't give them a line." He stood, and turned his empty cup a quarter-turn on the board, and left it there. "Tell him the stove sounds well. Tell him nothing else he wouldn't want read aloud in a provincial counting-room. He'll understand. He's been minding shapes longer than either of us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:163:Withrow did not read the letter. She asked him to read it to her, all of it, and sat at her desk with her pen in her hand while he did, as if she meant to take a note.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:179:"He'd done nothing," said Withrow. "Twenty years before, a station clerk made a slip writing down his Kindling, and the one officer who could have sworn to the true reading was dead, so the slip could never be put right. The registry stood him in a frame again. Twice. Twice the frame had nothing to say. That was all." She touched the printed list beside her old note. "These are the nine headings. Employment, guild, credentials, property, debts, enrollments, travel, office, and *other relationships*. The district was short of clerks that year and I was good at it. I walked his fences myself. Seven weeks. I was proud of how careful it was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-19.md:197:She opened the code once more at the nine headings, took up her pen, and wrote one word in the margin beside the ninth, small, under her own old note about the haulier, in the hand she had used to underline that book in her second year. Then she turned the book round so that he could read it. *Choosing.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:5:The east hall had its two lamps lit and its shutters down. Outside it was not yet light, and a wet wind was leaning on the north wall, so that the long room seemed smaller than it was, a lit box with six people in it and the dark pressing on the glass. The program's clerk had moved her trestle out from the wall to the edge of the painted floor and sat at it with the red book open and two pens laid ready. Rooke sat on her right with his board. On her left the Stone stood with a flag in each hand, a red and a blue, held down along his thighs like a man carrying two fish.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:43:She did not run the count the second time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:63:Twice she nearly was. The second time she stepped across to shut the girl's line, and it was a good step, and it ended on her right foot with the Current girl's shoulder a hand's breadth from her fingers. All she had to do was plant and reach.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:103:"No. That's why you lost." He said it without any heat. "From tomorrow, every count ends on the right. Every one. I don't care where the line wants to go. You'll land on that foot until you've stopped asking it for leave, and then you'll land on it a hundred times more." He went back to the trestle and bent over the red book, and wrote one word under her name, and Cael, from the door, could not read it and knew what it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:207:She wanted it anyway. She turned that over, sitting on the bench, and it did not come apart. She wanted to stand on a rated floor and be read right, once, by people whose whole work was reading. It was not noble. She did not think it was wrong either. It was only what she wanted, and she had spent too long wanting nothing to be ashamed of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:87:He sorted the district bag into three piles on the bench. Cael had never watched anybody sort post before with any attention, and he found that it was not one act but four, done so quickly they looked like one. Seln took a letter out of the bag. He read the address. He turned it over and read the seal. He laid it on its pile. He did not hold any letter for longer than another; he did not let his eyes rest on any seal more than on any other. A man watching from the end of the bench would have said he was sorting the post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:111:He did not read it again. He got out Hesk's book instead, and a pen, and copied it into the Log word for word, slowly, the way he had once copied the last lines of Gault's note into a letter for Hesk, because copying a thing makes you see every mark in it. Copying, he saw the dash before her initial, and that she had written the last line after the initial and not before it, as if it were an afterthought; and that she had not written *Dear* or *Cael* or anything at all at the top.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:197:On the First-day he had gone down the river path alone before light, which he had not done since the year began. On the Second-day he had gone down the bluff road with Brom at the fourth bell, an hour nobody went down at, to carry a crate of the reading room's copying paper up from the ferry because the carrier had not come. On the Fifth-day the whole house had gone down to the sitting in the coach, as was known a week ahead. On the Sixth-day he had carried two document cases down into the town for Karis, so that her answer to the fee office would go in at its counter by her own hand and nobody else's. Four times in one week his pattern had broken. Any of them would have been worth a line to a man who was paid to follow him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:5:He noticed that before anything else, coming in fourth, as he had come in fourth on the night the case was opened. On that night every sheet in the room had been stacked and weighted and ready, and on his birthday every sheet had been turned on its face, because the captain was coming. Tonight the cloth lay bare from end to end. The shelves behind it had gaps in them the width of grey boxes, and the only paper in the whole room was three slips in front of Karis and the book he had brought himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:65:"I'm arguing. Let me." Karis held up a finger. "It needn't be the convening. There's the Archmarshal's office. He has read it. He stood at a window in a borrowed room and said what it was, and he has since refused the protocol his name, and a finding from him, with the case behind it, is a thing no seat could set aside as a forgery. And if not him, there's the press. There's a man in the market street who bought a second press this year on the strength of our name, and a Gold at Auremont who has already told the registry in public to classify its instruments. Forty families could be told what was done to their children. It's true, and it's proved, and in my hands it's dated." Her voice had risen a little. "I have never in my life held a true thing and not filed it. I don't know how. Everything I am says that you put it in front of somebody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:77:"An appeal upon new matter is examined before it is heard. That is the code also. It goes to a records office to be verified, and the office would open a district's station book against a clipped order, and find that the two disagree, and report an irregularity in the *exhibit*." He said it without any heat. "Every instrument they would use to test it runs on the thing it accuses. A man told you so at a window. It was his first refusal, and he gave it before any of us had asked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:125:"I do not ask what the room is being used for," he said. "I ask when a thing is wanted, and never why, and I have found that it saves me a great deal. I have come about the district's evening bag, which reached my counter a quarter of an hour ago with one item in it. It is addressed to the practitioner, care of the registrar, and so I have read it." He held out the packet. "I walked his docket up to a refectory table on the fourth day of the year. I thought I had better finish as I began."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:137:Brom did not answer, for he was looking at the second paper. It was a printed slip, a third the size of the sheet, with a narrow margin and a rule at its head, and Cael, from the bench, could read its heading upside down: *Guidance to the practitioner upon advancement.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:141:Brom read it, and this time he did not read it aloud. He read it twice, by the movement of his eyes, and then he held it out across the table, not to Cael but to Karis, because she was the one who read such things, and she read it to the room in her small dry voice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:155:He folded the finding along its folds and laid it on the table. He did not sign it, and he did not tear it, and he did not say that he refused it. He put it down as a man sets down a thing he has decided not to carry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:181:"Then it is a matter of forms, and forms are my trade." Bracken picked up his lantern. "The registry prints one for an enrollee's withdrawal, and I hold a quire of it. In thirteen years I have not had occasion to square more than one at a time on that counter." He looked at the four of them who were enrolled, one after another, and did not look at Seln, who was staff and outside any form of his. "I shall have them ready. I say nothing about how many."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:13:"I filed one once. My fourth year in the office. The district station in Ostrand had a frame that took to reading every Stone who stood in it a rank higher than he was. Not by much. Enough. A month of Stones went out of that station with ranks on their papers they hadn't earned, and nobody noticed until a quarryman's boy came up this hill with a certificate that said Rank Four and couldn't lift what a Rank Two lifts." He turned his folded hands over. "I wrote it up under the integrity provisions, because that's what they're for. They took the frame off its floor and tested it for a fortnight, and found a seam in the casing that let the damp in, and mended it, and read every Stone of that month over again. Then the frame went back on its floor and read true." He looked down the table at Cael. "That's an integrity question, in that volume. A frame that has drifted. You take it off the floor and test it until it reads true."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:25:"You are the frame." The counsel's voice did not change at all. "And a frame that will not read true comes off the floor."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:27:Cael sat with that. All his life frames had been the things that failed to read him, and he had stood in front of them and been the question they could not answer. Now the paper had turned it round. He was the instrument on the bench, and they meant to open his casing and look for the seam that let the damp in, as Bracken's men had done with the station in Ostrand. He was not afraid of their looking. He was afraid of what they would write when they found no seam at all, because a frame with nothing wrong in it that still would not read was not a thing the second volume had a section for. It was a thing chapter fourteen had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:39:He turned the observation notebook round and slid it up to her. She looked down the column: nineteen leaves, thirty-seven paragraphs and three schedules; nine discretions to three evidences and one fraud, in the negative; eight provisions cited, two minutes, no rulings and no witnesses. She read it twice, which he had not seen her do with anything else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:51:Then he drew it to him, opened it at a place he did not need to look for, laid a strip of plain paper under the first line, and put on his spectacles. Cael had heard him read a hundred pages aloud and never one like this; there was nothing in the voice at all, because Bracken would not let there be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:73:"The morning after it's entered, a practitioner goes down to the ferry as he goes every week, and holds out his pass, and the ferryman has had a letter. A short one, very polite, in the plainest form. The pass is held in abeyance. The ferryman is sorry. The ferryman has a licence of his own and a family to feed." Her voice did not rise at all. "So he goes up to his guild hall, where they've drunk with him for twenty years, and they've had a letter too. It tells them their association with him is *under restriction*, and the master reads those two words and understands that his own name goes on the next letter if he so much as nods to the man in the street. So he goes to the records office to see his own file and argue with it, and the clerk tells him it's sealed. Kept, every page, perfectly safe, and nobody may open it. Not he. Not anyone he pays. Not anyone who might stand up somewhere and say what's in it on his behalf." She paused. "And every house that ever put its name beside his gets a letter. This house would get one. After that, every question anybody asked about him would reach us already answered."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:125:"The house will answer," said Karis. "Because the other choice has its own word in the docket, and the word is *fled*, and this house is named on that paper, and I won't have *fled* written anywhere near it while I live here." She swept the six slips into one hand and tucked them into the back of her notebook. "I'll have bones on it in four days. And when the seat reads it, the seat will go through it with a pen and find nothing to mark. If the end's already fixed, I still want our tracks in the record, every step of them exactly where the code says a step goes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:181:All of it had taken nine hours, and he did not give them all of it. He gave them what had moved the table. The frame at the Ostrand station that read every Stone too high, and what the integrity sections were for. The honest man's three sentences, quoted whole and made a warrant. The ferryman's daughter whose initials he had written down. Chapter fourteen in Bracken's voice. The ferry pass, and the guild hall, and the daughter at Withrow's counter every week for a year. Three marks on a calendar card. Six slips of paper laid out like stepping stones.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:57:That had been the last morning, in a room over a ship's chandler's on a quay, with six young men on benches who were not yet anything, and a cat asleep in the sun on the window-ledge. She had been old even then. She had opened every lesson by telling them that nobody in that room would ever be told the whole of anything and that the sooner they stopped minding the better; she had spent the weeks between on the craft, how to read a subject, how to write a return that was true and useful and no longer than it needed to be, how to live a year in a town and leave nothing behind but a room that had been let. The grades she had kept for the end, so that they would know what the other side of the table did, and where they themselves stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:115:At the residence door a knot of third-years was standing out of the wet arguing about a bout, and he went round the edge of them: seven to twelve. On the stair, coming down, the Current girl from Rooke's program with her wet hair in a towel, who flattened herself to the wall to let him by and said, "Sorry, sorry," as if the stair were hers to be sorry about: thirteen. Two more on the first landing. Three in the corridor, one of whom was the captain, Ephram, with his green-taped file under his arm, who nodded to him and went on: eighteen. Five more behind half-open doors as he passed, reading or mending or asleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:139:Not here, said Seln. A residence room had a door anybody could knock on, and a corridor full of people who knew whose room it was. Not the wing either. The wing was his, and everybody who watched the wing knew it was his, and a lamp in it after hours was already a thing somebody wrote down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:153:"Then the researcher's room." He took his hand off the case at last. "Not before the sitting. Whatever comes out of this, you don't want to be carrying it in your head into a room with Jent in it. He reads nothing, but other people at that table do. The First-day after."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:177:*Subject attended to his course of study. Subject attended the seat's fourth sitting as directed. An instrument presented at the house's gate on the Sixth-day was declined by the house; subject was not seen at the refusal. Subject's floor work within the band of the year. Nothing further to report.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:219:It was what he took to every floor that was not the house's own. Two rolls of linen strapping, a roll of the stiff kind that the healers used for wrists, a tin of the porter's horse salve that smelled of tar and worked better than anything the infirmary had, a clean neckcloth, a flask, a heel of bread wrapped in a napkin, and a short length of leather with a buckle at each end that Cael had never seen him use and had never asked about. He went through all of it with his big careful fingers, as he did before a meet, and put it back in a different order.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:233:He told them in the order it had happened, because that was the only fair way: the knock at dusk, the case set down beside the book, *I'm going to open this. Not tonight.* What he had asked and what Seln had answered. The reading room. The floor schedule and the First-day after the sitting, five names, five quarters of an hour, and a sixth line that was only Ephram wanting the Crown yard. He did not say what was in the case, because he did not know. Nobody asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:249:Brom stood and shouldered his kit bag. At the door he stopped, and put his hand flat to the left side of his coat, where the letter was, the one he had carried sealed since the forty-fifth day, and kept it there a moment, as if he were checking that the road had not already taken something.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:23:"Ninety-one." She sat back and the shawl slid off her shoulders, and she did not notice it go. "Every time anybody has ever called on the integrity provisions since the registry began writing it down. I've read every one. I found them in the registry digests. Bracken's press has the oldest digests and the hall has the rest, and Seln's sheaf told me which year each section first appears in, which saved me a week." She laid her hand on the stack. "Eighty-four of them are about things. Frames. Stations. An assessor who took money in a district I won't name. Two panels whose minutes argued with themselves. A records office that lost a whole quarter's declarations in a flood and then tried to pretend it hadn't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:93:When he looked up from the notebook, the stack had been turned over. The last page lay on top, where he would read it first, and Karis was standing at the window with her back to the room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:131:He read it at his counter, every leaf, from the address to the last schedule. The oil book took a third line and a fourth. He did not skim. Cael came past the records-hall windows late on the first night on his way back from the north wall, and saw him through the glass, a narrow grey shape bent over a single lamp, turning a leaf, reading it, initialling its corner, turning the next. A registrar's initials on a leaf were a promise about the leaf. Bracken did not make promises he had not read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:145:It was the leaf about his Kindling. He had not known Karis had written one. It set out, in her plainest prose, the morning at Weaver's Row in Denvash: the circle of pale wood, the registrar who conducted it, the brass plate that lit late, the one bracketed word on the card, the classification line stamped where it had stood empty for fourteen years. Every fact in it was true and every fact was his, and he had never heard any of it in another person's sentences before. He read it in his own voice to a registrar in the middle of the night with the rain on the roof, and Bracken's finger moved down the other copy a line behind him, and twice Bracken said "Slower," and once he said "Again, that line," and made a small mark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:197:Karis did not come down to watch. Cael looked up at the top of the library, at the reading room's window, and she was not in it. She had gone to sleep in her chair the moment the wallet left the counter, the girl at the outer table told him afterward, with her boots still on and her key on its cord in her fist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:209:She walked the coping itself, not the path beside it, as she always did; it was narrower, so it was quicker. Her hands were still taped from the evening drill and she had a heel of bread in one of them. She broke it in two without looking, gave him the larger half, and sat down beside him with her legs over the drop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:17:*The Chancellor declined the order pending verification, on four grounds, as follows.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:53:The last entry Bracken gave the clerk was the shortest. *No refusal in writing was requested.* The clerk wrote it and sanded the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:69:And all that while she had laid nothing. He had run the read at her at full depth across forty feet of stone, at the moment the word went up, when anyone standing in a court with a refusal going on in it lets go of a fraction of something. She let go of nothing. It was not hiding; hiding has a grain to it, and he had learned that grain from two years of Seln at a counter. There was simply nothing there. She was a person standing in a court, in exactly the right place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:75:He did not write down the four times he had read her. He had them already, and the page was for what they meant.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:219:He took his right hand off the table and reached for his cup, and Cael thought he was going to turn it at last. He did not. He picked it up and set it down again a full hand's breadth further away from the order, on the far side of his own reach, as if it had been standing too near something; and he put the hand back flat beside the paper, where it had been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:37:In the lamplight Lira could not read her heel. She had said so. But Cael, watching her come at the lane on the four-count, saw her find something else. The dust in the cracks between the painted boards had woken as the lane went down, the way it always did, and was running south along the lane in a thin pale stream. In daylight from the high windows you could hardly see it. Under two lamps set low on the floor, it threw a shadow. The whole lane showed on the boards as a faint moving band of dark, like the wake of a fish under still water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:39:Lira saw it on her second count. He watched her see it. Her lie went onto the back foot, outside the band, and on the third count, the empty one, she did not put a foot into the lane at all. On the fourth she burst along its edge, the length of it, not across, and came out at its end beside him with her palm already on his shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:95:"I'll need to mark my place. Two hours is long for a gallery." The counsel laid the rule on the table, pointing at the window, and turned it a quarter of the way round, and then another quarter, as if she were trying the weight of the idea. "That will do. A turn for each. Anyone who loses the thread can look at the rule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:109:Ilsev did not look up. She was reading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:127:Everything else that morning he saw only between things. The side-door officer, his cold gone, not needing to ask the last name on his list. Somebody had already copied *Caelen Hesk-ward* into his door log from the last sitting, so that all he had to write was *the enrollee of record, unclassified*, which took him nine seconds now that his hand knew the way. *Seventeen* under Cael's left foot again, the third time, and this time chosen; he could not choose the room, but he could choose where he stood in it. And a slip of paper folded twice that Karis had handed Brom on the ferry, which was in Brom's right inside pocket now, with orders not to be opened until the coach went home. The left pocket was for Velmere, and it had a sealed letter in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:143:What Jent had already given away, Cael could read without looking back at his own notes, because the house's counsel had been keeping score of it in the margin of her privileged minute, where he could see it. Three headings stood there in her small capitals, written before the sitting began. *EXECUTION. PROVISION. RECORD.* Through each of them now ran a single neat line. She had drawn the first in Jent's first minute, when he told the hood the petition had never said the enrollment was wrongly made. The second, a little after, when he called the clause law and took the house's history of it for his own, word for word. The third when he said again, for the minute, that he had never read a better-kept record of an enrollment, and the clerk looked up to ask if that was to go in as spoken, and Jent said, "Every word. I'd decline to soften it if I were asked," and something along the front benches shifted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:147:Cael's own notes had what Jent said it was. The registry's confidence in its own evaluations, which the integrity sections were there to protect; Jent had read their first line to show it. If no standard reading, no trust, *by that fact and by nothing else*, which he had said three times, like a man laying a plank across a ditch and then walking on it. The discretion, whole and undisputed, needing no fault found anywhere first. And then the thing he had just taken back, the overreach, the stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:173:It was Karis's count that made the pencils go down. Cael felt her go still beside him as the counsel gave it: seven times in the registry's whole record the integrity sections had been turned on a person, every one of the seven for something the person had done; eighty-four times on things; and not once on what a person was. Only after the count did the counsel lay her papers face down and read the room the preamble, ninety seconds of plain old words, so that the benches heard what the sections had been written for after they had heard what they had been used for. The testing of instruments. Frames, stations, records, protocols. So that the registry might know which of them it could trust.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:197:Cael heard the hood's answer before he understood her question. *Its keeping to protocol. Not its result. Nor that.* Ilsev had asked what her seat was there to do, and whether that included deciding if the standard protocol could read this practitioner at all, and the seat had said no to the second. Now she was asking that its words be minuted exactly as spoken, so that nobody could stretch her chair afterward to cover the question. The clerk wrote. Ilsev sat, and wrote the same words into her own file in her own hand, and set the file back at its old angle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:9:He put the bread down at her elbow and went away again. The next night, when Karis came down to the east hall with the rest of them, she brought the plate back empty, and he did not know whether she had eaten the bread or Seln had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:33:"I sorted them by the margin," she said, "not by the station or the date, but by what the keeper wrote at the edge of the reading, that the order told the clerk not to enter. And every margin is the same kind of thing. *Reads into. Something of. A second family, faint.* Not one keeper in forty wrote at the margin that a child was too strong. Several of the forty were weak; a first rank, and a low one, with a second family showing faintly behind it like a face behind a window. They were corrected all the same. And I went through every pair looking for one where the order took strength away and left the rest, and there isn't one. When a rank goes, it goes because the second family went, and the second family was what was holding the rank up." She moved one finger on the cloth. "They don't correct how much. They correct how far."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:55:"The leaf says the function is appended to the reading and advises what's entered. It doesn't say what happens when the reading gives it nothing to advise, and nothing else in the case says it in words." She looked at Cael. "But your file says it. The petition put it in schedule two by its full title, on the first night of the year, and you read it. *Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:61:Cael had not known she had asked for anything but the bread. He remembered telling her, standing at her elbow with the plate in his hand, and her not looking up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:109:"Middle." She touched the leaf's place on the table, though the leaf was in its sleeve in the case. "Read it again in your head: *The function is appended to the reading and is not the reading. It does not alter what the reading shows.* It's a note written by somebody who added something to a thing that was already there, and wanted it understood which part was his." She looked at Brom. "Think of your father's forge in the shed past the gardens, and the mill that must have stood on the estate's stream. If a millwright wants a wheel, he doesn't dig the river, because the river's there already. He cuts a race beside it, and puts his wheel in the race, and spends the rest of his life keeping the race clear of weed. That's maintenance, and that's what this leaf is: a millwright's note about a race. It doesn't say a word about who dug the river."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:137:She did not read it from the slip. She said it to the room, slowly, in the voice she kept for the last line of a finding, the line she would be held to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:161:"I went through everything for the anomaly," she said to Cael, "for session nine. You asked me to, at Greyvane, and I've never forgotten that you asked." She did not look at the slip. "Anything in the forty that read across a family with nobody of that family near. Anything in the reports about a practitioner who took something from no one. Any memorandum that mentions Tide by name, or a reading at a distance, or one that came without an engagement. Anything in the leaf." She shook her head. "Nothing. Not one line in it touches session nine. The function works at Kindling, and session nine wasn't a Kindling. Whatever it is, the case didn't do it and doesn't know about it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:185:"Now I've got it." She put her finger on the line. "The petition asks for one thing, compelled re-evaluation under standard protocol. It doesn't care about the provision or the house or the clause; it wants him in front of a reading again." She looked at the case on its shelf. "A re-evaluation is an evaluation. The memorandum covers every evaluation at which a reading is entered. If the reading reads him this time, older, with eight things in him instead of none, the function is waiting beside it with its advice, and the advice is entered, and the order comes down, and he's a column, whatever column it chooses: one family, first rank, margin not to be entered." Her voice was very steady. "And if the reading goes out again, the way it went out at Denvash, then there's no valid classification, and it's chapter fourteen, in sequence: nullification, suspension, restriction."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:5:He had half expected it not to. He had lain awake for an hour after the tenth bell with the satchel on the sill and the court dark below, and some part of him had believed that the house would look different in daylight, now that he knew what he knew. But the frost was on the flags as it had been yesterday. The fountain went up and came down. The porter's boy was chalking the day's names on the Crown yard's boards, and two Mire second-years were arguing at the refectory door about whose turn it was to fetch the bread, and the coats at the foot of the road changed on the bell. Not one thing on the whole bluff had moved an inch to make room for what was on the shelf in Karis's room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:59:"I'm not asking to be difficult," she said. "I'm asking because I sat on that bench last night and listened to you all read forty of them out, and I've been doing my drills in my head all day, and I can't make them go." She looked at the coats. "That seat down there runs on it, and so does the evaluation. Every sitting we go to, we're sitting in the room and answering it, and it's the thing in the case. They want to read you again. We know now what reads you." She pulled her coat closer. "My carters said the stamps stop four days east, loaded, or three days light. I've been carrying that in my pocket since the dairy. We could be past the last station by the Seventh-day after next if we left on the Seventh-day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:91:"Leaving. What it costs, to the day and the line; not what it feels like, but what the code writes down." Karis shifted the volume under her arm. "I know what happens if a respondent walks out in the middle of a proceeding, because I read the coastal default to you in the dairy, and you all remember the word. I don't know what happens to the rest of us, or to the house, or whether there's any day in the year when it's different." She looked at Lira. "Give me two days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:173:"First. A respondent who leaves the jurisdiction while a proceeding is pending is absent. You remember the coastal letter. The seat doesn't need him to have run. It needs him not to be there. The code doesn't count missed sittings; it counts days from the close of the fourth sitting, the one that put the record, and if he's out of the jurisdiction on the fortieth of them, it enters him." She did not need to read the line. "*Respondent absent from the jurisdiction; entered as fled.* That's Cael. The fourth sitting closed on the seventy-fifth day. If he were gone, it would enter on the hundred and fifteenth, long before any ruling."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:227:She had the bench by the rail, with Rooke's red drill-book on her knee for something to hold and her right foot out straight on the boards. Karis had chalk and her grey notebook and her coat buttoned right for once. Brom had gone up the gallery stair and drawn every shutter down the length of the north wall, as Lira had drawn them on the first honest floor of the year. Now he stood in the hall's great doorway, with one of the two tall doors standing open a hand's breadth onto the dark of the passage and his right hand on its edge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:245:He had fought her for four exchanges and never once seen what she saw, and now he did. She had stood in the cut and seen this, every rest and every line, a whole road strung with places a thing could be held. She had laid her points where his body was about to come to rest, because she could see the rests before he reached them. It was not magic; it was this, done well, for years, by somebody who had learned it as he had learned to read a floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:263:"I'm not reading them. It's asking about them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:283:It did not ask, and that was the strange thing. The great door stood open a hand's breadth, a tall oak leaf on three iron hinges, a threshold, the very thing the new thing wanted most of anything in the world. It had asked about every door he had passed through for a week, and it did not ask about this one at all. It knew the door was there. He could feel the hinges as points and the edge as a long place. But it was quiet about it, the way it went quiet after he had named a thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:321:A point of fire sat on the boards at the line's end. It was no bigger than the head of a pin, blue at its root and yellow at its tip, standing up perfectly still on the bare wood the way a candle flame stands in a room with no draught. It did not spread, or even scorch the board, but simply sat there, burning on nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:39:He had no folder today. He had left even the one folder on the table, shut, with his spectacles lying on it, and he stood in front of the platform with his hands at his sides, as a man stands who has come to say a thing he could say in his sleep and does not wish to seem to be reading it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:45:"The enrollment was lawfully made. The provision under which it was made is law, and has not been repealed, and the seat has held so. The renewals are good upon their days; the respondent's table of them is the petitioner's own. The evaluator of record is competent, and his figures are honest, and his notes were produced entire. The tournament office's record is accepted. And the house has kept within its charter in every particular I have been able to examine, and I examined a great many." He paused. "I want one of those in the minute in my own voice, and not only in a schedule. The Chancellor has done nothing wrong. She answered under escort. She read every order at her own gate. She produced what was asked before it fell due. If every respondent I met were the Chancellor, I should have very little work, and I should think the better of the registry for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:49:"That is what is agreed," said Jent. "Here is what is asked, and it will take me four sentences. A practitioner is enrolled in a chartered house. The standard protocol has not classified him. The registry asks that he be evaluated under that protocol, as every practitioner in every chartered house is evaluated. If the protocol reads him, his enrollment stands, and I shall be the first man in this room to say so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:125:"No." He did not smile. "It's a thing to ask you. I've had it ready since the tenth sitting's week, and I'm holding it until after the last Fifth-day. I'll not ask a man for anything while a seat has him. It wouldn't be a fair ask. You couldn't say no to me properly, and I'd never know whether you'd said yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:171:She did not read them. He had thought she would. She laid the thirty slips face up along the edge of Rooke's table in three rows, in their order, and looked at them once, and then she turned her back on them and spoke to a horse blanket.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:7:He named the door anyway, under his breath, before he put his feet on the boards. *Door. It opens on the corridor.* It cost nothing to say. Lira had said on the stair that she would name them with him when she could, and she was not here, so he did it himself, the way you lock a door you know is already locked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:95:Karis came first, at the seventh bell, from the refectory, by the library's front door and the main stair. She had come that way every evening of the year with her supper still in her and her notebook under her arm. Brom came second. Brom never took the main stair if there was another one. He went in at the library's north end and up the iron stair through the stacks, three flights of open treads that rang under a heavy man, because he said the long way round was the only exercise the library offered. Lira came third, by the front, slowly. And Seln, Cael knew without being told, would come last, up the bindery stair from the yard behind, the way the wing's man always came when he carried returned volumes up to the readers' shelves.

