    47	## Ground (one picture)
    48	
    49	The border road comes down off the moor into hedged fields and runs west to the river. Posts at nine, six and three miles. A carters' pull-in by the three-mile post: a stone trough, a ring of blackened stones, a thorn hedge, a ditch and a bank along the road's edge. The river comes down from the north and loops round a bluff. The grey bridge crosses it from the east on five piers to the outer gate. Inside the outer wall the ground is flat for two streets and then begins to climb: the brokers' street runs from the gate to the foot of the second wall; the rope-walk lies along the inside of the outer wall on the river side, down to a landing where flat boats come in; the smith's is at the brokers' street's upper end, by a public trough; the inn with two doors is one street back from the trough. The second wall goes round the bluff's waist. One street climbs from the brokers' street through the second gate and straight on through the third, and from the third gate it runs on into the square, with the station's steps at the square's far side and the registry's front door facing them. The registry's back falls away down the bluff's north side to a yard, reached from the wall lane by the stepped cut.
