   226	
   227	The carter stood inside the door with his hat in his hands. He was a short thick man with twenty-two years on one road, and he had driven a light cart up a frozen valley since first light; his eyebrows were white with it. He had held a scrap of bark out across the board, and Cael had had a book in one hand and a pencil in the other. So it was Brom who had taken it and turned it to the fire, and who was giving it to the table now in his slow voice, a stop at a time.
