    46	
    47	The last ferry crossed at noon on the hundred and thirtieth day, with two fingers of snow on its rail.
    48	
    49	The ferryman had been at the shelf since first light with a pole and an axe, to cut her a lane. Half the meadow stood on the landing to see it. The scow went out along its cable slowly, shouldering the broken ice, with the gelding walking his round in the shed and the boy calling the turns. On the far bank a man in a hat with one side cut away stood waiting beside a mule.
