     1	# Chapter 40 — Six Places
     2	
     3	He slept through the third night, and woke on the hundred and second morning to find that a forge was only a forge.
     4	
     5	The nine were where he had left them. He could have named each by its sound, lying on his back among the barley, and he had no wish to; they went on beneath his thinking as a mill goes on beneath a miller, and the only one that he noticed was the ground, which had come back to its proper time. He lay and enjoyed that for the length of a few breaths. Then he saw that Lira's blankets were empty, and Karis's, and that the purse was gone from the nail where Karis hung it.
     6	
     7	He found the three of them at the long house door.
     8	
     9	It was a hard white morning, with the breath standing in front of every mouth, and the geese had not yet left the lee of the wall. The steading's woman stood on the step with her arms folded and a ladle in one fist. Lira was below her on the frozen ground, with her foot set flat and her chin up, and Karis stood a little behind with the crew's purse held in both hands, like a second at a bout.
    10	
    11	"And the fire," Lira was saying.
    12	
    13	"The fire's lit of an evening whether you're under my roof or not."
    14	
    15	"Lit for us. At the hearth end, when your people have eaten, with the board cleared."
    16	
    17	"That's my board cleared twice in a night."
    18	
    19	"And a kid."
    20	
    21	The woman's eyebrows went up. She looked over Lira's head at the wicket in the northern fence, beyond which her goats were, and Cael could see her going along them one by one.
    22	
    23	"Six," she said, as a person says the weight of a sack that she has lifted before.
    24	
    25	"Five, and Brom splits your wood."
    26	
    27	"He's split it. He was at my wood-heap before it was light, on some business with my man that I've not been told of." The ladle moved, an inch. "Six marks, for the room and the kid, and I'll say nothing of what's been had off my shelves."
    28	
    29	"Six," said Lira.
    30	
    31	She put out her right hand behind her without turning her head. Karis laid the purse in it.
    32	
    33	Cael had seen that purse opened a good many times. It was a bag of soft hide with a drawn neck, and it wanted two hands: one to hold and one to count. Lira set the bottom of it against her hip and pinned it there with the heel of her palm. Her thumb and first finger worked the neck open, and two fingers went in; a coin came out between them and was laid on the step at the woman's feet, and the fingers went back for the next.
    34	
    35	She did it six times, without dropping one and without a glance downward, as a person does a thing that she has learned in the dark. When the sixth lay on the stone she hooked the cord round her little finger and drew it tight against her belt, and held the purse out behind her again.
    36	
    37	The steading's woman bent and gathered up six marks.
    38	
    39	"You've done that before."
    40	
    41	"A week."
    42	
    43	"Six days," said Karis. Her voice still came out of her like a gate on a dry hinge, but there were more words in it each morning. "With Brom's flint-bag. And pebbles from the well."
    44	
    45	"So that's where it went," said Cael.
    46	
    47	All three of them looked at him. Lira had gone a little red.
    48	
    49	He saw the six days then, as he saw a bout when it was over. A girl with one arm tied had gone off behind a barn each evening with another man's flint-bag full of pebbles, to teach five fingers the work of ten, so that when the day came she could pay for it herself and ask nobody to hold a purse open for her.
    50	
    51	"He's lit every fire for six days with Seln's. I was going to put it back."
    52	
    53	"What's the six marks for?"
    54	
    55	"It's the hundred and second day."
    56	
    57	"I know what day it is."
    58	
    59	"You don't. You know what number it is." She faced him squarely, on her flat foot, with her one arm at her side. "I've had it written in the route book since the shelf, and I've been counting toward it across a mountain. You'd have let it go by. You always let a thing go by when you think that you've not earned it."
    60	
    61	He could not answer that, since it was so, and since she had known it of him from a lane in Ardenmere, where he had once tried to let a good deal more than a day go by.
    62	
    63	"You've had a bad week," said Lira, "and it was of your own making, and I told you what I thought of it. It's still your day. Those are two different sentences as well."
    64	
    65	Karis had opened her notebook against the purse.
    66	
    67	"Earned, a hundred and thirty-two. In hand, a hundred and eight, less six." The hinge creaked. "A hundred and two. It is the first that I have paid out. And been glad to."
