editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:367:*Hundred and second day, night. The loft. Read myself, as she set it: once, alone, on my back.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:29:"She's set two things on it. I'm to use it on myself before I use it on anybody. And whatever I find, she is the one I tell, on a day that she names." He looked round the four of them. "So if there's anything to find, it's hers before it's yours. I'm saying so now, in order that nobody should think later that I kept a thing back without leave. It's part of what's owed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:289:"I ask first. Every time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:13:"Lira's had that since dusk. The rest of you have it now. It's fourteen days late, and I'm sorry for every one of them." He looked at the square woman by the ladder. "And there's one thing more that I have, from the night of the birthday, which none of you is getting. I read myself, as she set it. What I found is hers before it's anybody's. I'm telling you that it exists, so that nobody learns later that I sat on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:55:"I've tried that line by myself, more than once. Every time, I was a woman in a coat." Her hands came down. "I'm not going to guess past it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:157:*The reading turned outward. I ask first. Every time. If I ever don't, that's the day I became the instrument.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:169:"You said that you'd say when, and you go in an hour. I read myself on the night of the hundred and second, as you set it, alone and on my back. I found—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:211:*Hers. Three readings of myself, on three different days. Then all of it to her, at once. Nobody on the road, to find out whether I can.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:323:"There's one thing more," he said. "I read myself, on the night of the birthday. I found something. I can't give it to you. It's the healer's to hear before anybody; that was her price, and I'm paying it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-45.md:49:It was the same thing that she had stopped at the well. He wished to have three readings done in three nights, so that he could set them down at her first bench and stand square. She had told him to read it frightened, and to read it when he was not. She had said nothing about reading it in a hurry; that was a third temper, and it was the one that he had been in when he lay down on her table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:117:"I should walk myself onto it. I have told you what I think the paper is. I did not say that it was wise to answer it. I was asked what I would do, and I have said. I should be sorry to have it counted for more than one hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:141:"We walk into it since it's the only one that ever will have. If we leave that sheet on the nail, the people who wrote it don't go home. They write a third, and they cut it closer, and they pick the ground again; and the next ground mightn't be a floor at all. It might be a ford, or a street." He turned to Lira, since it was hers to hear. "You're right about the walk and the fence. You're right about the arm. If this were only for money, I'd hang it back myself and we'd go and carry salt. But I think that this is the one time they'll come for us to a place where I'm more than they've counted, and I don't think we'll be offered it twice."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-46.md:219:"No." Pike blew on the ink. "Anybody can ask. I've said yes to one man in thirty years, and he's sitting behind you. This one I'm putting on myself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:61:"It was *there*," said Cael. He heard his own voice, and it was not steady. "At three. I'd counted to ten on myself and had my own feet and nothing else. I thought that was what ten gave you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:249:"We go at first light. We go in file, ten yards apart, down the old road: myself, and then Lira, and Karis, and Cael, with Brom last. The bow is spanned before we cross, with a bolt in the groove, and Brom carries it. Each of us has a day's water and a day's bread on his back. I have known a morning's errand to take a day." His eyes went to the boxes. "Nothing else goes over. The three boxes stay at this stone with both beasts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-48.md:277:*Tide: once, on the healer, on the ridge road. I asked twice today and she granted the second. The short count and then a minute of the long, at one sitting; I am counting that as one, since it was one asking and one yes. At three there were banks. I knew the Path by ten. In the minute, one channel and one thing in it, going somewhere. It cost her nothing that she would notice. It cost me the use of that hand for an hour, and most of a loaf.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-47.md:105:"I've read one thing with what I had from you, and it was myself. I found no bank in it anywhere, as you found none. I don't know what a bank is like under the hand. I've only your word that there is such a thing." He kept his own hands at his sides. "When you take the boy's wrist, may I set my hand on it too? I want to learn what reaches you through yours."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:11:"A Shadow, Bronze, who sought it and found that it was not there to be asked. A Wind: Iron, first rank, who asked for the whole of it and was given a step." Her voice had lost most of its hinge, and what was left of it creaked only on the long words. "And a Tide: Iron, fourth rank, with a count that stopped at four and nothing in her hands. That makes five Paths, and three tiers, and five people who had never before agreed about anything."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:23:"It went at one stride," said Karis, "and it came back at one stride, going the other way. I have that from all five of us on the ninety-fifth evening, and from myself nine times that night, and from each of the trials at noon on the day after. And when it came back, it came back as it had been. You have the measure of that, Lira, and I have never bettered it: a clock that stopped and started again without losing the time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:119:"I know who I've got on that road a good deal better than you do." She said it without heat. "And I'll not have spent a night of myself on an arm and then walked off from it in the week that it wanted watching. Those are my five days. You didn't ask for them, and you're not to carry them. Go and shoot your bow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:207:"Oldest to the right," she said. "This first is a district hall's, and I had it off the step myself; it was dressed in my grandmother's time. The pale one is from a library that I used to be allowed into, and the honey-coloured is Ostrand's wall. And that is the oldest dressed stone between Denvash and the Line that anybody can put a year to. A mason was re-laying a drain at the foot of the old gate, the one that the city counts its years from. I call it the founding stone, and I have never owned anything older."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:277:"I know that it is. I wanted to hear myself say it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-49.md:299:"Back up your six steps," said Oryn. "And if you put that arm out for anything again, I'll take the other one off you myself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-60.md:37:*Wind-adjacent — Lira. Pressure-adjacent — the Bronze from Ardenmere. Iron-adjacent — Brom. Compression-adjacent — Reydan. Ember-adjacent — Karis. Shadow-adjacent — Seln; source protected; the seal held past the Line as it held inside, and was used twice out here where no one who could carry it west could see it, and that is the seal's whole logic and it is unchanged. Storm-adjacent — Daeva. Anchor-adjacent — unnamed; the hand with no face; opened a door on a rim and held a floor in a hall, and I own a piece of the craft the system uses to hold people and have used it twice this year, once each way. Blade-adjacent — Ephram. Tide-adjacent — Oryn; partial; Iron; self and contact; the tenth, and the first that does nothing in a fight, and the only one whose engagement field says clinical, and the only one with a rule on it in her hand and mine: ask first, every time. Deployment notes current on all ten.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:203:*Read myself. Ten currents, and I can't find the banks either — Oryn's right. And the anomaly's there. Not a fragment. Not a flow. A still place, and the rest of me has been going around it since before I knew it existed. It isn't the Quiet. The Quiet is outside me and turns Paths off. This is inside me and the Paths — the fragments — go around it like they were built to. I don't know what it is. I know it was there before Oryn, before Brom's read, before the ring. Session nine caught the edge of it. I'm not going to guess. Guessing is what the registry did to me. Observation, then the log, then patience.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:287:He did, and it surprised him how little he wished to move it. Nine days ago he would have given a good deal to hear that one word; he had tried to buy it at a well with a thing half looked at, and had been told what a single morning was worth. He had it now for the full price, three readings and a night between, and it was only a word about a debt, and it left the rest exactly as heavy as she had made it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:289:"Then here's the last of what I've got, and it's a healer's and not a theorist's. Write it down and stop touching it. Things you keep touching don't heal."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-55.md:299:*Tide: once, on the healer, by her leave.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-57.md:193:"I owe the rest of you something. She's had it, as she set it, so it's free." He kept it short. "There's a place in me, under the breastbone, where nothing moves. All ten fragments go round it. I've read it three times, and it keeps. I don't know what it is, and I've been told to stop touching it."

