   241	He had not known that he was afraid of it until the pencil said so. He had kept that question since he was fourteen, on a great many desks, the wrong way up. Registrars had reached for it, and wardens, and an Archmarshal with a plate; and a healer had found the edge of it with her two hands, on a mountain, and had been made to wait a long while for the rest. Every one of them had come to him, and he had never in his life carried it to anybody. And he was walking to a town, three miles off, to lay it down in front of a man whom he had not seen, on the word of a catalogue.
   242	
   243	He wrote two lines more, since they were owed.
   244	
   245	*V. sat. He told me why. I told him what the reading can't do, and nothing of what reads. The rest of that is his, and it isn't entered.*
   246	
   247	*Deployment since the Line: thirty-two. Myself, twice: not counted, as before.*
   248	
   249	He turned the book round on his knee, and held it out.
   250	
   251	Lira took it. She read as she always read, with her lips moving and her chin down, and she went through it twice. Then she laid her thumb on the leaf, beside one line, and did not say the line.
   252	
   253	"You've let me hold your boots," she said. "And your ribs, on a mountain. And a rope with you on the wrong end of it."
   254	
   255	"Not that."
   256	
   257	"No. I know that." She shut the book over her thumb. "I never asked for it. I want that in there as well. I could have, and I'd have got it, and you'd have hated me for a month."
   258	
   259	"A week."
   260	
   261	"A month. I know you." She gave the book back. "Is he going to get it? This man in a hole?"
   262	
   263	"I don't know. I don't know what he reads. I've a line off a catalogue."
   264	
   265	"If he does, I'll not mind. I'd sooner somebody had it who could do something with it. I'd only have sat on it." She pulled her knees up, and looked at the fire and not at him. "And the old man had it before the book did."

