editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:205:A finding is read once, by the people it concerns, and filed. A file note goes on being read, by every officer who ever lifts the folder down, for as long as the folder exists. Whoever came to this folder after him would come to it through his note, and would read the rest in its light, whether they meant to or not. He had spent his life refusing to let other men's digests do that to him. He had no wish to do it to anyone else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:219:The second got shorter shrift. Within a month it would put a warden on the bluff road. And a boy who had already sat through one adjudication would answer it with just the behaviour that turns a monitoring file into a stage play, because a boy who knows why he is watched begins to perform for the watcher, and nothing true has ever been got out of a performance. He tore it across. The halves lay one on the other by the lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:223:It was the kind reading, and half the continent had reached it already. A small house in the hills had staked a year on it, and he did not think less of them. It said the file was a mistake. Some clerk at some station had made one wrong mark at a boy's Kindling, and everything since, two academies and a hearing and a provision and a stamp, had been the long labour of picking out one bad stitch. It was comfortable because it asked nobody to change their mind about anything: the machine was sound, it had caught its sleeve once on one boy, and decent people had spent a year working the sleeve free.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-54.md:185:Cael had looked at it every time he came into this room and had never once got a name for it. Squat, copper, three short legs, a little larger than two fists: that was the pot. Out of its lid stood a glass straw scored with lines as close as a comb's teeth. A small brass plate on its belly carried a date of calibration in paint, and the date was this month's. The clerk set it on its legs on the oak a pace from the crossing and stepped back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:79:"Dellenmoor. Standard. Research standing pending; the library committee writes to you. Brom. Standard. Ninth day, first bell, east hall; the Blade cohort wants a look at you. Lira. Transfer, re-certification. Copper bracket. You register for the ladder by the fourteenth or you start the season on the bottom rung and climb from there." He licked a finger and turned the page. "Cael. Assay-provision enrollment. Straight to the registrar. Assessment wing, ground floor, the door with the brass plate. He's cleared the whole morning, which he does not do, so go now and look at the view later."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:115:Cael could not hear all of it from the bench, but he could see it, and he could read Lira standing at an official table with her arms folded better than he could read most people's faces. Her clerk was a grey-haired woman with spectacles on a cord. Lira's file had come up from Greyvane ahead of her: the old Fenmark record, the acknowledgment of standing she had signed before any of them knew her, and, pinned at the back, the sheet Greyvane had given her. It was the flag asking the registry to look at her tier again, under Greyvane's seal, the provost's whole two terms of watching folded into one line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:179:"Most people would leave the second sentence out," said Bracken. "It looks like weakness. It's the strongest line in the file." He tapped it. "Never let a document," he said, "claim more than it proves. If it claims one line more, that one line is where it gets attacked. Then people stop reading, and the whole file goes into the fire with its true lines and its false one together."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:5:People talked in recesses. They stood at windows with cups and told each other what the morning had meant, and by the time the sitting began again, half of them had agreed on a meaning that nobody had read anywhere. She had seen it happen in a hundred halls, and so when the counsel rose and went out talking, with two of the clerks trailing after her, and the academy's people drifted to the far windows to stretch, Ilsev stayed in her chair and opened the file she had asked for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:85:Cael had spent the recess on that hard chair, because a clerk would have, and because getting up to walk about was the kind of small change he had promised himself at dawn not to make. Of the whole delegation only two had kept their places: the Archmarshal, with his folder shut and his pen across it, and Ilsev, who had read something from a file and then written on a form for about eleven minutes, by Cael's count of his own breaths, and laid it in the records officer's tray, and drunk a cup of tea in four swallows. He did not know what it was. It was not his to know, and he did not write it on the back of his floor sheet. He only noticed that whatever she had read had not changed her face at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:103:Today she had not read it a third time. She had read it once, and filed again inside the recess, and drunk her tea cold. And Havel, at the foot of the table with his head bent over the ledger, found that it moved him more than the third reading had. The third reading had been a person puzzled. This was a person deciding to go on being puzzled, out loud, in ink, at the next level up, where people with better lamps would have to read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:145:*Pressure-adjacent. Unused this term. Withheld at the baseline plate. On the record as not shown.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-41.md:33:He thought of it as a ledger, because he had been trained on ledgers, and on one side stood the subject. In a single year the boy had produced an adjudication, a transcript that half the continent had paid six silver marks to read, and a designation on his file graded well above that of the man sent to watch him. On the other side stood a full term of embedded coverage by an officer with a clean record. And at the foot, where the total went, there was nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:267:The plate lay in the floor, in the middle of the room, under its frame. It was brass, and it had gone the brown of an old coin, and there was dust on it, for the carters who swept the house had left this room alone. Nothing in it stirred when he stood in the door, and he had not thought that it would.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:271:"You couldn't read me," he told it, conversationally, the way you tell a fact to an old machine. "Since my Kindling I thought that was the system failing. Turns out it was the most honest thing the system ever did — the one time the layer under you couldn't finish its job, because there was no bin beneath me to file the lie in." He looked at the dark frame a moment longer. "You're the only part of the whole apparatus that never lied about me. You just went quiet. Everything above you did the lying." He shouldered the satchel. "Thanks for the eleven seconds. They were the truest measurement I ever got."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:319:He walked back to where Seln stood, and looked west, with his arms hanging, as he looked at a rack of plates. The track ran back across the upland, pale, as far as the roofs of the station, with the water beyond, and the bare rise. There was no rider on it. There was no lantern, and no tin cup, and no man with a pencil on a top tier.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-50.md:13:"I'll tell you a thing I didn't know until today," said the registrar. He was holding a volume up to the lamp to read its board. "Twelve years I've had this office, and I've always believed the best day a file can have is the day you close it. You tie the last box. You write the count on the lid. It's done, and it's right, and you go home." He slid the volume into its place and squared it with one finger. "It isn't. The best day a file can have is the day somebody who wishes it ill opens every box, with a good mind and a whole morning, and can't find anything wrong."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-50.md:151:*1. What held. Sound law, said standing up, into their own record, by the one person on their side of the table who reads to the end. A year of work. Two academies. Four grey boxes and three hundred and eleven sealed sheets. Bracken's index. Withrow's twenty years. And Karis, who spent eleven weeks of our last term at Greyvane trying to prove that nothing had happened to a clause, and proved it, and then let Bracken take every trace of her hand out of the file so that the file could stand without her. That's what today was for.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-58.md:35:Of the form's five parts, four were furniture, and he had filled them so often that his hand did them without much help from him. Coverage held from the first day, by way of nothing but the posting's ordinary work. The subject's days, set out in full across a page and a half: floor hours under supervision, lectures, the library, the ladder from its gallery, the wing's own unit, a habit of private writing, a handful of letters posted in the open to people already on file. No one had ever written down the boy's days more truly, and none of it would tell a reader anything. Associations unchanged since the bridge. The inspection, the delegation, the sittings, the observed evaluation, all entered in a voice a harbour clerk keeps for tides.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-31.md:7:He read it from the observation notebook, word for word, because he had written it word for word on the walk back from the wing before it could soften in his head. He gave them the sheet and the chained pen, and *Your training requirement will take coursework*, and Gault and the unit and the back half of term, the room in the wing and Rooke's boards; then *Who takes it?* and *I do*, and the file sentence, and his own three words, and the board where his name would have to go up among everybody else's. He read the half-second at the door as well, since to skip it would have made the evening's first lie. Then he turned the page and read them the other entry, the one about his feet in hall three, and what he wanted, in his own hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-31.md:225:"She's drawn an eye," said Brom, reading it over his shoulder with a plate in one hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:61:Cael had read it in the profiles stack, and Brom had read it twice. *Rank Four on paper. Fights like a Seven. Buried.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-08.md:303:"He wasn't coaching," he said at last. "He was keeping. She's worth more to him as a Four who wins than a Seven who wins, because a Four who wins every fair-day purse in the hills is a living, and a Seven just gets matched against other Sevens." He picked up the bread again. "I'd like to know who files for her. I'd like it to be somebody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-17.md:117:They came away with three things. Cael numbered them on a clean page as they were agreed, because he had watched agreements made aloud in good faith dissolve inside a season, and a numbered page could be taken out and laid on a table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-40.md:161:She gave them the three differences first. They came in the order her counsel had used round the long table, and no more dressed than they had been there: early, and nobody had said why; large, eleven where two would do; and at the head of it, an officer of a rank that did not come to academies. She did not dress any of it, and she did not soften it either. She laid the three things out in front of five hundred people like three stones on a table and let them look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:59:He could have recited it. Fourteen of Greyvane's monthly sittings had been read into the hearing as exhibits, one after another, and every one of them, under three assessors' names, had said the same two things about him: that he could get out of the way with the Wind, and that he could feel a weight coming before it came. A court had looked at those two and filed them where anybody could find them. Nothing else about how he was made had ever been put where the world could read it, except the Ember, and that only in a single late exhibit, half a page, so thin that showing it on Gault's floor would not confirm anything. It would announce something. Of Pressure and Compression it said nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:147:"I can't come in tomorrow," she said, sitting down on the step where Lira had been. "Closed panel. I asked Bracken. He said I could read the note afterward, when it's filed, like everybody else, and then he looked at me as if I'd asked to borrow his spectacles."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-12.md:183:Two weeks of asking had got him the names of nine pieces out of the twelve. First a drop-frame: a trigger, a rail, a weight to run down it, and four more weights racked underneath; someone at the far end tested the pawl, and it gave one soft click. A pendulum escapement in a glass case, ticking a steady interval that he found himself breathing in time with before he noticed and stopped. Nine shutters in a board, opened in an order a drum behind them decided; the drum's ratchet made the noise of a thumbnail going slowly down the teeth of a comb. Three spring plates on braces, each with a date of calibration painted on its housing. For footprints, a shallow tray of chalk dust. And at the very end, a thing like a tall brass funnel over a cup, which dripped into the cup with a small bright sound every few seconds, and which he could not name at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:191:He read them together. Schedule two listed his file's standing entries by the registry's own dates, and the registry dated a renewal by its seal. Her old table dated it by the trestle. Two papers on one desk, about one afternoon, nine days apart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:5:Cael wrote that down first, standing in the doorway of a chandler's that had not yet opened, on the fifth of the new month, with a fine rain deciding whether to begin. Two storeys of brown stone. A doorstep hollowed like a bowl by a hundred years of grain buyers coming in to argue. Beside the door, a brass plate about the size of a book, which said what the building was in letters too small to read from the far side of the street. A rope-maker's on one side of it, with coils of new rope hung in the window like sleeping snakes, and on the other a shuttered house with a cat on its sill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:117:A rope off a drum, a sprung plate on the far wall, a measured rod along the floor: how far the burst carried, how much it could carry with it, how fast she was off the line again after the landing. Five runs at each. The woman read the figures aloud as the man called them, so that both of them would have heard every one. Lira did not listen to them. She knew what they were. They had been Iron since Vell kept them in her ledger at Ardenmere, and everyone who had stood at a rail and watched her fight had known it for two years. The only thing that had ever been missing was a station.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:119:So she gave the station its numbers. She took the rope off the drum in her right hand and burst against its pull, and felt the drum fight her and lose. She put her shoulder to the sprung plate at the end of a burst and felt it give and come back. She ran the measured rod five times, landing and leaving, landing and leaving, and on the fourth landing the hip sent up a single bright complaint, like a nail in a boot, and she let it complain and went on. On the fifth she was off the line faster than on the first. The woman read the figure out twice, in case she had misread it, and the man said, "Yes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-43.md:189:Karis stayed after the other two had gone up. She stayed at the long table with the marbled book shut and the tied batons under her chair, and when Cael got up to go she said, without looking round, "Sit down a minute. I want to ask you three things."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-43.md:219:"Five." He said them as she wrote. "The Wind. The Pressure. The Iron read. The Compression. The Ember. Not all on the same paper, though." He made himself go through it sheet by sheet, the way she would have. "The transcript anybody can buy holds two of them, the Wind and the read, and that one thin Ember exhibit at the back that nobody ever reads. Gault's baseline holds the Wind numbers and the read, and the plate, where I held the Pressure back, so that line's flat, and a Compression line he ringed because the floor never gave him a fair test of it. And Vell copied the circuit record at Ardenmere. Every bout I fought is in it, the Pressure I used in public and the Compression that finished Reydan."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-35.md:247:The chart stayed in carrel eleven, under an inkpot. The notebook stayed in his coat. The Log stayed where the Log had always lived. Gault's brass and dials would read what was put before them and report it honestly. The assay file would keep its three words and add none. Nobody's coursework, no supervision note, no reason written on a deferral would ever carry so much as a hint of a sixth thing. At the semester's measuring he meant to stand before the panel and show five things, the five the record already knew, and every one of them true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:33:Gault's office had filed the baseline in the boy's first weeks, and it had been copied into the product soon after. It gave a practitioner of non-standard architecture who executed consistently. It gave a displacement framework, measured to a working ceiling on sprung timber. And it gave a plate trial at the frame's heaviest weight, in which the enrollee had taken the blow on a braced arm and kept his feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-18.md:163:"They had," said Karis. "They'd all read the same file."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-18.md:211:She did not read them the market's file again. They had all seen what it held, and where every line of it came from: meet records, the gallery, the hearing at the copying rate. "The walls hold," she said. "Two rules will keep them holding."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:85:Last term he had stood up in a room full of people and read their own schedule back to them, from the first page to the last, and he had won because he had not reached past the text by a single line. If he let himself start wanting this clause to be alive, he would end by reading it the way the Compact's clerks had read his own file for two years: hoping, and writing the hope down as though it were a finding, and never once noticing the difference.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:5:It was a narrow slip of the seat's buff paper, printed and filled in, with a pin through one corner where it had been fastened to something else. He set it down beside the house's counsel's plate without a word, as if the receipt were the thing that mattered and whatever it had been pinned to could wait. The counsel had come back up the hill in the coach after the sitting and stayed to eat. She put down her fork.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:9:"Silver and above," said Karis, reading it upside down from across the table. She said it slowly, the way she said a citation she had not expected to need. "That's a fossil. Nobody's filed under that head in—" She stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:39:The long table had been cleared to the wood. Four places were laid at the far end of it, close together, with the residence's chipped blue plates and four cups that very nearly matched. And in the middle of the table, lit, its glass chimney polished until it shone, stood the good lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:63:He sat at the end of the table with his coat still half on and his hands flat on the wood either side of an empty blue plate, and found that he could not say anything at all. It was not that he had nothing to say. It was that everything he had to say had arrived at once, at the door of his mouth, and stuck there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:77:Brom set the basket on the table and took the cloth off it with a little flourish and began to lay out what was inside on the blue plates, one thing at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:133:"My sister had been looking at that fork all the way through dinner. And when her pear came, she put the fork down on the cloth, very neatly, right beside her plate, where everybody could see it. And she picked the pear up in her hand. Like this. And she bit it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:113:"No getting out of this one," said Gault. "Too close, too heavy. Catch it on the plate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:115:The clerk brought the plate across on its brace and put it into Cael's hands: a round of steel with a spring behind it and a small dial in the housing, made to show how much of a blow had been held and how much had gone on through.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:123:He stood with the plate and the reaching in his arm for two breaths, which was one breath longer than he had meant to, and he saw Gault see the extra breath and say nothing. Somewhere the pendulum ticked. He thought of Lira on the wash-house step: *make him look.* He thought of Karis in carrel eleven: *it was mine before it was yours.* He thought of half a page of transcript, late and thin. Then he let the reaching go, as he had let it go on a thousand other floors, and his arm was only his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:125:He braced the plain way: feet wide; the plate held out on an arm loaded from the shoulder; everything down through his legs into the oak. It was how a body with no Path takes a knock, and how he had taken every knock in his first winter on the Ironyard's planks. The pawl let go, and the weight came down the rail and met the plate, and the shock went up the bones of his forearm and stopped in the shoulder like a nail driven into a joist. Nothing in him carried it anywhere else; the shoulder had all of it. Behind the plate the spring hit the end of its travel with a dull clank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:149:The Ash instructor's book lay shut in front of her, with a finger in it at a place she had long since stopped keeping. The Mire instructor no longer looked resentful; she looked thoughtful, which on her face was nearly the same expression turned the other way up. And Gault had watched him; Cael understood it only now. All through the morning, every run and every drop and every clank of the plate, the heavy still man in the middle chair had watched him, start to finish, and looked away only to write.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:167:He wrote the plate up that evening at the desk by the window, with his arm on a folded shirt because it did not like to hang.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:211:Under the plate that night he added the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-39.md:53:The third decision was about the standardization directive, all forty-one pages of the order that had swept away the old terminology. It protected against a reader who knew only the four pages everybody quoted, and would say that a repeal might be hiding in the rest. So the whole of it went into the file, all forty-one pages, copied and sealed. The four passages where the old words were replaced by new ones were tabbed with green paper. At every place in the text where a clause repealing the assay provision would have had to stand, if one existed, a narrow white slip was pinned with a line in Bracken's hand saying that nothing stood there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-48.md:55:He thought of every hour he had worked under the wing that term, and of every figure the quiet woman had sent across to the registrar in her neat hand on the same afternoon, and each one of those figures had been made on some post at some setting. He thought of his baseline in the second month, the entry demonstration that had set the measure for his whole provision, taken against plates with their dates of calibration painted on the housings. He thought of the twentieth, when Gault's panel would lay his new numbers beside his old ones and say whether he had grown. And he thought of the posts in hall three, every one of them re-pinned this term after a pin had sheared in the north bay at the seventh setting, with Cael's own forearm the first thing it hit. Was a post with a new pin the same post? Did it read the same? Who said so?
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-52.md:91:The glass was half run before the yard caught on. Cael heard the moment it did, because the noise did not rise. It sank, and thickened, and turned careful, like a crowd watching somebody carry too many plates.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:35:The heaviest weight, the fifth, went onto the rack under the Mire instructor's hands. The clerk rubbed out the chalk square and drew it again one pace from the rail, then fetched the plate on its brace and handed it over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:41:He had carried it for three years, ever since a bout he had lost honestly, and it had always lived close under his skin. It did not keep a blow. It gathered a blow, and turned it, and sent it on somewhere else. The thing in his hands was what it had been made for. If anybody had been marking answers right and wrong, it was the right one, and the little dial in the plate's housing would have swung round to a figure that nobody in the room had ever seen it show.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:45:It was written down elsewhere. Vell's copy of the Ardenmere circuit record held the bouts in which he had used it in public, years ago, before he knew enough to keep it back. But Gault's book had no line for it. Gault's book had a plate held low four times, with the shoulder taking everything, and a boy saying so before anybody asked. To bring the other thing out now would be a second surprise, on a third instrument, in a place the baseline had never pointed at, and a slip of wing paper had told him how many surprises a sitting could afford.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:49:His brace was the plain one: wide feet, the plate out on an arm loaded from the shoulder, and everything sent down through the legs. The pawl let go and the weight came down the rail into the plate, and the shock ran up the bones of his forearm and stopped in the shoulder, all of it, like a nail going into a beam. Behind the plate the spring hit the end of its travel with a dull clack.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:61:Two sheets went down side by side under Gault's hands, the baseline's plate and this morning's, and his eyes went from one to the other. Line for line they matched, with not a graduation between them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:85:At the baseline this weight had been too near and too heavy to escape, and they had made him stand under it with the plate. Now the clerk chalked a square only a pace and a half out from the rail, and along its landing side he laid the long shallow tray of chalk dust and drew a ruler across it to smooth it flat. The wing read that tray standing, at once, before the enrollee had stepped out of it, so that nobody's memory could tidy up what the dust said, and so the clerk took his place at the tray's far end with his ledger open along his forearm, near enough to read a heel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:179:"Growth against baseline: confirmed across all demonstrated capabilities. Ceiling on the board raised from four to six, which the enrollee gave before the board gave it, now as then. The combined trial bettered, at the heavier weight. Ignition point shown, and met to the wing's standard; the earlier exhibit thin. One measure unchanged, the plate, and so entered. Trial five void for a fault in the apparatus, and run again on the reserve.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:197:The east wall emptied. The counsel's chair scraped first, and she went out re-tying her ribbons and already talking. Havel tucked his board under his arm with the log sheet squared on the file beneath it and followed her with his eyes on the floor. Ilsev picked up her case and stopped for a breath or two beside the copper pot, where its thread of colour had sunk back to the first line, before she went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:279:*Thirty-five days since the stair. It wakes when I do. Three hours under the Compact's four best chairs, and it stayed in. Cost: one interval and one headache. The Pressure stayed in my arm. Gault wrote "no change" about the plate and then stood up for me over it, which I didn't earn and can't put right.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:11:Each was a stout padded column standing about as high as a man's breastbone, set into an iron floor plate bolted through the oak to the joists beneath. Inside the padding, on a spindle, sat a heavy iron core.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:21:Each post was meant to be that something. Bolted to every plinth, in letters a quarter of an inch high, was a brass plate saying what happened if they ever failed to be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:93:Jask sat down on the floor plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:109:She did not ask Jask anything at first. She took his right hand in both of hers and said, "Squeeze." He squeezed. "The other." He squeezed with the other. "Look at my finger." She moved it slowly left and right in front of his face, and his eyes followed it, and some of the stiffness went out of her back. Then, "Tell me today's date," and he told her, a day wrong, and she nodded as if a day wrong was about right for a man who had just sat down on a floor plate, and only then asked whether anything hurt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-27.md:113:One second-year was off toward the warden's shed before the instructor had finished the word *warden*. The porter, sent to the wing for the duty clerk, went with his broom still on his shoulder. She moved everybody back out of the north bay with a single flat word, and nobody touched the post. One of the second-years, coming back, stood in front of it and read the brass plate aloud, every word, in a voice that was not steady, as though reading it might still help. Nobody had the heart to stop her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:79:That night Daeva read the file again, the third time since the trial.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:95:It never did, anywhere. Every room she had slept in since she was sixteen had a single pane of thick leaded glass, with a small brass plate on the sill to say what it was rated for, and the rating was always her own assessed ceiling and a margin. The program's facilities office ordered them. She had stopped trying latches years ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:247:"I won't read you the objection," said the head at last. "You'll have read it. You read everything." He looked at the painted founder rather than at her. "I'll only say that it was written by people who are fond of you, in a hurry, and that every line of it is true. A filed bout was never meant for this. The underwriters have no table for it. There's no bout like it on any record anywhere. I'd hoped that would be enough." He breathed out. "And in a few days you are to stand at the closing in front of every house on this continent with our banner behind you. That's all. That's the whole of what the program has to say."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-30.md:197:A thing that was going to be decided was read to all three at once, with the door shut, before anything was done about it. Breakfast was a long table and sixty people. So he ate, and listened to Brom describe exercise nine in a voice of deep injury, and said nothing. Karis looked at him once across the bread and then down at her plate, and he saw her decide not to ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-30.md:225:They stood along the north wall where they had always stood, four iron columns with their padded heads, and the new retaining pins shone in the collars like fresh nails in an old fence. The chains were gone. The floor warden had fitted every pin herself over two long evenings, so the Ash instructor told him at the bay's edge, and had made each Force third-year who used the posts that week watch her do it, and then made them each say the plate aloud before she let them near a dial.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:269:His clerk read the direction twice, entered it in the day book, and went to fetch the files. If he wondered about it, he did his wondering in the corridor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:23:*Read your account three times. Filed it with your record, where it belongs. The offer of a proper session stands whenever the road runs through Ardenmere again. The ledger doesn't forget. Neither do I.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:95:*This year: held back at the plate both times. It's on the Ardenmere circuit record, not on this wing's, and to show it would have been a second surprise. Cost: one flat line on a semester note read in front of the Compact. Gault put his own name behind that flat line with a delegation listening, and never knew what was under it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:128:*Here is what I hold toward the next question, all of it. A gap on Prynn's shelf where a volume never came back. One retired word, copied into two notebooks, that neither of us can read. A designation on my file that nobody I've ever asked can trace to a desk or a hand. And, somewhere over all of it, one note in one file, in a hand I'd wager signs its whole name.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:83:"Instruments," said Karis. "That is the handle. I went and read how the tool has been used, to see whether the hands that held it could read, and they could. There are ninety-one recorded invocations of these sections in the registry's whole record. Eighty-four concern things: a frame that read a rank high, a station seam that let in the damp, a plate, a ledger, a seal. The sixty-third was filed by the registrar who is sitting at this table, in his fourth year, upon a station frame in this town, and the registry thanked him for it." Bracken looked at the seal's box. "The other seven name a person, and I have read each of them through. What the registry complained of, every time, was an act: somebody had sent in a false return, or paid for a rank, or lent out a seal. Not one of the seven complains of what the person was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:111:"And the fourth," said Karis, and her little finger went down. "I asked. I asked the petitioner what evidence would defeat his petition, and a reading in the frame is evidence, and he told me there was none. He did not say, *let us see what the frame returns.* He is too honest a man to say so, and in thirteen sittings he never has. Nobody has. No officer, no clerk, no witness has stood in this room and told the seat that the frame will return a classification. It was asked once before, at a district station, when he was fourteen, and it took eleven seconds to answer, and its answer is the word on his file."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:159:Then she spoke of the people who would come after, and she did it quickly, for she had cut it twice. A ruling stood in the registry's books, she said, for every house that held a charter in the same words. The seat had before it four old challenges to the clause, and in one of them a ferryman's daughter in a southern river house had waited a year for a station to settle upon what she was; the house had waited with her, and at the end of the year the station had read her, and she had stayed. Under the ruling now asked for, nobody need have waited. Somebody need only have filed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:9:And Ephram, regional champion of a day and a half's standing, sat across the table from Karis with Rooke's coaching notes on the bout spread in front of him, copying them out into a book of his own in his careful hand. Rooke had told him that afternoon that champions kept their own files. Ephram had gone straight out and bought the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:111:The committee room had a long table and a portrait of a dead miller over the fireplace, and the panel sat along one side of the table with their backs to the portrait. It was the panel of record Gault had written into his calendar at the end of last term, in his ruled lines: *Gault, with two.* The two were a registry officer from the district seat, a careful dark man who had plainly read the whole file in the coach on the way and had the page numbers ready, and the host region's senior examiner, a stout grey woman who had sat on the meet's own panels all week and who said very little.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:129:Then Gault took the plate out of the case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:131:It was the travelling plate, the small twin of the one in the wing at home, a brass-faced disc on a short arm with its dial set into the housing. It had come upriver in a felt bag in the bottom of the case, under the seals. The guild had lent the panel its proving frame, an iron thing the millwrights used to test shafts, with a weight that ran down a rail on a pawl, and the guild's own millwright stood by to work it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:133:Cael set his feet, and took the plate on his forearm, and braced it from the right shoulder, as he had braced it in the wing every time he had ever been tested.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:135:The pawl let go. The weight came down the rail into the plate, and the shock went up the bones of his forearm and stopped in the right shoulder, all of it, with nothing taken and nothing turned. The dial in the housing swung a little way, and stopped where it had stopped at the baseline, and where it had stopped on the twentieth of Reaping.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:137:"No change," said Gault, and wrote it. "The plate flat, and so entered."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:145:"Routine," said Gault to the officer, putting the plate away in its felt bag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:153:*Yesterday I chose a figure for five judges, and they wrote it down in good faith: twenty-four, a careful distance under par. Today three officers measured me against myself on a borrowed plate, and found me exactly as unchanged as I'd decided to be. One instrument reads a performance. The other reads a file. Neither has ever once touched the thing they're measuring.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:155:*Inventory, while I'm here. Wind: five of five on hard boards yesterday, which is all the day sells free on that floor. Four went to the yard-master and one to the academy Irons, and the Bronze got a step. The hip after; the left calf tight from the exchange I held. Pressure: flat at the plate again, the right shoulder paying. The cap is four blows a sitting, and today took one. Compression: held at a sleeve's width from the ganger's hand. Not on any record here. Ember: none spent; nobody here asked for it. Shadow: deployment none. Still.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:291:*If you don't know what a thing is, you ask. If you think you already know, you don't ask. You go and look at the paper, and then at the thing, and see whether whoever kept the paper kept it properly. That's what he did. He came to audit a file. The file was about me; that's all I was in it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:139:There was a case of glass on a stone plinth along one wall, and in it three crowns on three dark cushions with a brass plate under each. The Copper was the plainest of them, and the darkest, and worn bright along its top edge where a great many hands had lifted it. Its plate had been lengthened twice with new brass riveted on below the old.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-23.md:89:Somewhere around Brom's weapon the thing he meant to say had come clear all at once, as the read comes clear at a pace and a half, and after that it was only a matter of laying it down in order. He found the order by starting with what he would not do. That was the oldest habit he had. Before any bout, he wrote down the three things he would not let himself try, so that the bout could not talk him into them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-47.md:221:He went to the library to look for them, and Karis found him there first. She came in with her supper on a plate, which was against the library's rules, and which the library had stopped trying to enforce on Karis some time in the first month. She sat down opposite him at the long table under the north window.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-47.md:235:"Anywhere. Three to Gault, in the wing, this morning; the clerk doesn't know what. Then nothing. Not in the lecture range at the second bell. Not in the residence quadrangle at noon, or the fire-watch's store, or any of the three floors." She looked at him over the plate. "He went into every room they went into, and stood in it, and he asked nobody anything at all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-47.md:243:He turned the notebook round and showed her the last two lines. She read them, and then she put her fork down on the plate, very quietly, and did not pick it up again for a while.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:5:It was not a large thing: a plain circlet of beaten copper on a brass mount, dented once on the rim where some champion long ago had dropped it, with a plate riveted to the mount that had been riveted on twice because the first rivets had pulled. The porter polished it every term whether anybody had won it or not. Since the final it had acquired a ring of dried tea on its mount where Brom had put a cup down without looking, and a smear of chalk from Lira's thumb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:39:"Where else? It's not mine to keep in a box. I've left that bracket, and it won't see me again." She did not turn round. "But nothing else I own says it out loud, with a plate riveted on: the bracket had me wrong. You don't put a thing like that in a drawer. Prizes go to winners. That's what I got for having to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:23:"Their counsel proposed one of theirs at the foot of her request, and I objected in writing, on both sides of a sheet." He set the pen down. "It took me a quarter of an hour to draft and her four minutes to accept, and I'd like you to know it was done, because you'll never see it anywhere else. It'll be in the file. Nobody reads that part of a file."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:69:"If he asks about anything that's already written down somewhere, I tell him all of it, straight. If he asks about anything that isn't, I tell him I won't answer, out loud and in those words, so that the clerk can put down that I said no." He looked at the face-down sheet. "That's the change. In every room before this one, when there's been a thing I couldn't say, I've said something true with a corner cut off it: on Gault's plate yesterday, at the baseline, at Greyvane. Each time it worked, and each time it cost, and each cost went in the book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:75:"Then when you say no, put it down like a full plate," he said. "Both hands, all the way to the table, and no rattle."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-56.md:163:"For three years I've tried every week to make that half-breath shorter on purpose, and I've never managed it; I tried a month ago on the flagstones and failed." He kept his eyes on the man's face and did not try to make the answer look better than it was. "Yesterday a frame failed by nobody's design. It happened once. How, I can't tell you. Promise you a second time and I'd be selling a skill I haven't got. What I own is one accident and a bruise the size of a plate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:55:"Through the door. With a key or something as good as one." She tapped the end of her drawn room. "The inner door has a counting lock. The Compact makes every chartered records office on the continent fit one. Each time the bolt goes across, it knocks a little brass plate inside the case, and the registrar reads the plate. Bracken reads his every Fifth-day, never oftener, because a registrar seen reading his lock plate on an odd day is a registrar who's telling the building he expects a thief. He read it yesterday anyway. Two marks. This morning, four." She held up four fingers, then folded two of them down, then the other two. "In and out. In and out. Two nights."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:59:"Nothing." She said it with the small extra weight she put on words she meant to build on. "Not a sheet, Cael. Nothing torn, nothing moved, nothing missing. Somebody came in twice, read your file, put it back, and left. And Bracken would never have known, if his drawer hadn't sat a hair proud and his building hadn't burned two extra gills of oil."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:61:He looked at the shaded square and thought about it from the reader's side, which was the only side that ever told him anything. A thief took a file and left a hole that somebody would one day find. A reader took nothing. He went away with the whole of it in his head or his bag, and the file stayed in its drawer looking as innocent as ever, and nobody knew that it had been had. Somebody had paid for the second thing, not the first. Somebody wanted to know what was in that drawer and wanted nobody to know they knew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:185:*Going out tonight, to watch the records hall. Observation only. I will not interfere with whoever is reading the provision files, and I will not try to stop them, and I will not go inside the building. If anybody on the staff finds me there at that hour, it costs me the enrollment. That record is the one true thing ever set down about me by anybody.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:207:"Because somebody is reading my file in the dark," he said. "Somebody who isn't the Compact, or isn't the Compact's normal way, because the Compact can read my file whenever it likes; it's got a man in the wing with a key. Somebody's money pulled that drawer out at the fourth hour. And Bracken's copy lay on his desk at the ninth bell yesterday. He knows. Nobody but Karis knows that drawer better." He stopped. "I want to see what he does about it. I want to see it with my own eyes, once, instead of reading it afterward in an incident record in eleven words. That's the want. I've written it down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:21:There would be a certificate in it for every enrollee on the hill, four hundred and thirty of them. There would be one for Lira, from wherever she had been read, and one for Brom from the Velmere district, and one for Karis. There was no certificate in it for him, because the plate at Denvash had lit with a word and no station had ever written him down since. Every other paper in that drawer was three lines in a clerk's hand, and some of the three lines were what a station had read, and some of them were what an order had told it to write. There was no way on earth, standing at a counter in a queue, to tell which.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:169:"Three things," she said. "Then the door. Then the price."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:67:"Tomorrow you're going to walk into Gault's room and be careful for three hours, and you've never once not been careful in all the time I've known you, and I doubt you've the least idea how it shows from across a floor. It shows like that. So don't hide what it costs. Let it cost you. Breathe hard after the board, and shake the arm out after the plate where they can watch you shake it, and let your fourth run be a fourth run, a little worse than the first, the way fourth runs always are. Pull together for the fifth, and make the pulling visible. Any trainer alive knows a working body when he sees one. Give them that, and they'll stop looking for the other thing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:79:"The wing posts its order for a semester sitting beside the Magister's door, under the calibration card, and I'm fairly sure I'm the only person who has ever read it from top to bottom, the Magister included." She wrote as she spoke. "One, the board. Two, the frame, read only, with your back to the rail. Three, read and move together. Four, the plate. The last two are the first two over again, with an hour of oak in your legs. That's what you did at the baseline. This time the read stops being a trial of its own and gets carried inside the third and the cold runs, which leaves the second slot empty, and the posted sheet says so in so many words: *Two, at the enrollee's placing.* Anything already on your record that you choose to show goes there, or nowhere." She chalked a short dash beside the figure two. "The fifth slot is new: the heaviest weight on the rail, your square brought in close, and a tray of chalk dust beside it so that the clerk can read your feet from the dust instead of from your account of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:81:"The weight I took on the plate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:85:Next to each row went the baseline's figures, small. Four free on the board, the fifth billed, the sixth wide and slow. Eleven calls in twelve off the frame. Seven clear in eight on the combined, at weight three. The plate held low four times, *and not eight*. Then she drew two chalk brackets down the right-hand side of the slate, one above the other, with a gap between them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:103:Karis chose to rehearse the plate first, out of the posted order, because it was the one line on the slate that was not allowed to move.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:105:There being no plate in the common room, she handed him the residence's book of hall rules, which was bound in boards nearly an inch thick, and had him hold it out at shoulder height on his right arm, braced the plain way. Brom hit it from the floor, a short heavy sitting blow that went through the book and up the arm and into the shoulder and stopped there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:121:"I hadn't seen that coming, and it would have had you." Her chalk rapped the lower bracket. "For two weeks you've planned against the roof. Don't go over; don't become a question. Now look where you just went. Under. That plate line sits in Gault's own log in Gault's own hand with *four, not eight* beside it, because you said it before he asked, and anybody in that room can lay tomorrow's line over it in the time it takes to blink. Go in frightened and clamp down on everything by feel, and you won't merely scrape the floor; you'll go clean through it. Then the record says that a boy got worse over a whole supervised term while the Compact sat and watched it happen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:125:"So it was never *hold back*," Karis said. "It's *land on the number*. The plate doesn't move by so much as a graduation, and everything else moves exactly as far as a term would move it. That's a great deal harder than holding back, and it's the actual job."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-53.md:299:*Only I'm not sure the man watching would be glad to see me hurt. I've had a week of him. Three things he asked Gault, and every one concerned when the posts were last checked. In nine hours at the records table his pen moved five times, and every time it was about this house's paper and never about me. He sat through a final with his book shut and wrote one line at the end of it. Nothing in that is hoping I fall over. What it wants is the figure to come out true.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:31:He knew the enrollee. That was the plain fact of it, and he did not pretend otherwise to himself. He had sat four feet from the boy in a room twelve feet by fourteen, some months back, and put eleven questions to him, and left a twelfth where it lay. He had watched him on a failing frame. He had written a line in his own hand into the file the Compact kept on him, and signed it with his name entire. *Classification error is unlikely. Continue observation.* Anybody above him who read that file had read that line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-05.md:61:The ordinary silence after an advisory had a shape he knew as well as he knew the porter and his pigeons: an acknowledgment from the receiving office, a file marked closed somewhere below with a clerk's initials, a line in the weekly digest. He had read that silence ten thousand times. This was not it. The advisory was received. He knew it was received, because the dispatch slip in his coat said so. After that, nothing. No acknowledgment came back up the chain. The file did not reappear in the digest. The two letters on the fourth line of the routing slip were not on any other slip that crossed his table in the next nine days, and he looked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:45:At the end of the row was the twelfth chair. It had been held, unplated, for thirteen days before anybody sat in it, and had been empty again since the day of the duelist. There was a man in it now. He wore the plain grey of an inspecting officer and nothing on his collar. Seln said he had come in by the east stair an hour before the brackets began, alone, with no case and no clerk, and had sat down, and had not moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:109:Then the protocols were read, and then they were read again. Every bout above Silver had them read twice, Seln had said, so that nobody could claim afterward not to have heard. The referee and the panel each held the power to stop the bout, either without the other, and nobody could appeal it. There were two healers at each fighters' gate. If fire went where nobody meant it to go, the masts would take it, and the cables would carry it down into the trench, and the trench would carry it off toward the east break, out of the bowl. On the second reading Cael listened for the one thing he had marked on the first. Every line of it was measured in tiers. Every threshold in it, for when a healer should stand and when a bout should stop, was set against a fighter's tier. There was one fighter in the bout to whom none of it could be set.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:151:And as he read her he understood what she was doing. Her lanes were not full. He had seen her full lane on the trial floor and these were not that. They ran a little short, and a little shallow, and each one came at a slightly different speed from the one before, as if she were turning a dial. She was not trying to finish him. She was finding out where he stopped. It was what Gault did with a plate before he measured anything against it: take the baseline first, honestly, so that every reading after it would mean something. She was taking his. And then she would fight a little above it, so as not to waste either of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:111:*The push I took from the Bronze at Ardenmere. Never shown on this bluff; flat at every plate they've put me on. Four blows a sitting, paid in the right shoulder.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:163:"Eighteen." She said it with no weight on it at all. "This house has not been there in eighteen years. I have read the file on the last side we sent. It was made of the people the house liked best, and the continent did not like them nearly as much. I don't intend to repeat the experiment."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:229:Karis was reading the selection standards again from the top, and her pencil had stopped two-thirds of the way down. The line under it said that the registrar would file the house's entry, together with a certified copy of its enrollment record, complete and current, not later than the close of intake.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:245:On the way back he went past the assessment wing. The sheet that had hung beside Gault's door all recess, the one with the same name against every working day, was gone. In its place hung the term's counter rota, five clerks' initials spread across the week in the usual way, the teaching assistant's among them on three days out of seven. The recess sheet would be in a file somewhere now, in the wing's own order, where it would never be read again by anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-40.md:135:"I have," she said. "It's called having a house." She put her good hand on his arm for a moment. "Go and eat something. Brom's ordered for you. He hasn't told anybody, but there's a plate in the common room with your name on it in gravy."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-20.md:11:They came in ones and twos at first, and then in a steady stream, between the bells. Every one of them did the same thing first. He saw ninety-odd people do it that morning and not one failed to: they found their own name before they looked at anything else, with a small fierce hunt of the eyes, as if somebody might have left them off. Then, having found it, they did one of three things, and the three things told him more about the bluff than a month of corridors.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-20.md:59:Cael had Nyle already. Everybody on the ladder was in the binder by now, one page each, and Nyle's was one of the fuller ones because Cael had watched him twice on practice afternoons out of pure liking. He was a third-year, Copper Rank Five, broad through the shoulders with a settled, comfortable stance, a dozen wins across two seasons in Bracken's files.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-11.md:59:Every scored performance was judged on three things. The manual gave each its own short chapter, and Cael read them twice. *Execution* was what the fighter did: its difficulty and its cleanness, the technical fact of it, considered without regard to whether it worked. *Control* was how much room the fighter kept between himself and what he did: whether he was the master of his movement or only its passenger, whether anything was spent that had not needed spending, whether anything happened that he had not meant to happen. *Effect* was what the performance accomplished against the opponent actually in front of it, and against no other.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-28.md:21:On the hill she was the house, the way the long wall was the house, or the bell; she moved through it at its own pace and was part of its furniture. Here she seemed smaller and quicker, and a good deal younger. She laughed once at something Ephram said, a short surprised laugh that kept Ephram's eyes on his plate for the rest of the meal.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:229:At supper Lira ate with her left hand flat on the table beside her plate, and every so often she glanced down at it. Ephram bore this for as long as he could.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:281:Seln had come round from the stable with the travel file under his arm. He stopped beside the wagon and looked up at the pole just long enough to read it, and then made a small mark in the file, and Cael, who could see the page upside down, watched him write the crossroads, the day and the hour in his narrow upright hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:5:*Continental finals, Norhold. Observation support. Seat two of six.* There was a date for the coach and a list of what to bring, and at the foot of the list, in the same type, *Read the qualifying files before departure*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:9:There were three qualifying files from the western region, one for each house. He read the two older houses' files first, completely, because they were first in the bundle, and then he read Halcenvane's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:11:He had read it before. He had read it at Ardenmere, before it was a file, when it was a form with four questions on it and a boy on a pump step. He had read it at Greyvane across the end of a hearing table, and on the bluff with the rain coming down on the canvas. Now it had a season grown onto it: four meets, thirteen exhibitions, a column of figures, a house first in its region. It was thicker than any file on the boy had ever been, and in the administrative layer underneath it, where nobody but a records officer ever looked, the same mark stood where it had always stood, as plain and as orphaned as on the day he had first found it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:13:He did not stop at it. He read past it at the pace he read everything, and finished the file, and squared it, and set it with the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-31.md:117:It was a short sentence, and a plain one. There was no ornament in it at all. It said three things and said each of them once, and it had held up the whole weight of the tournament for longer than anybody could remember.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-31.md:143:It meant the crowd read the file from its smallest end to its largest, and kept the best of it for last, like a good storyteller.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:25:By the time he reached the outer court somebody had already been up the ladder at the great board with a brush. *Filed; acceptance pending* had been painted out. In its place, in fresher white, was a single word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:21:*The push I took from the Bronze at Ardenmere. Flat on two plates on the bluff and one in a felt bag at the mill town. Four blows a sitting, paid in the right shoulder. Not asked for since the mill town's plate.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-41.md:279:"The captain's a chapter," said Karis. "Rhagen sells its book. Whoever files next will have read today before he steps through the gate, and whoever files after him will have read two days like it." She looked round the table. "I don't think the doctrine's failing. It held. It held perfectly. But every time it holds, it costs a little more to hold it, and there's a little less room." She took her hand off the page. "The doctrine isn't failing. The doctrine is *filling*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:11:Brom walked out across the grey stone to the table. His forearms were still wound in linen from wrist to elbow, and he carried them a little out from his sides. The clerk lifted the plaque off the grey cloth and gave it to him. It was a plain board of dark wood with a brass plate let into it, the tournament's bar-and-ring cut at the top and a line of small type underneath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:21:He carried it up the guesting-house stair the same way and set it on the long table beside his plate, upright, against the water jug, where it stayed all through supper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:31:Then Seln read it, once, and laid it in front of Karis. "The office notes," he said, "that the review is mandatory. It does not note that anyone at the registry has yet read the bout." He went back to his file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:49:When he came back he had the envelope folded small in his fist. He went to his kit against the wall, opened it, and put the envelope in at the very bottom, beside nothing else Cael had ever seen him keep there. Then he came back and sat down in front of his plate and the plaque.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:71:Withrow came up the stair late that same evening, when the plates had been cleared, which she seldom did. She sat down at the end of the long table with a cup of the guesting-house's poor wine and listened to Brom's plaque being discussed for a while, and said nothing until it had been discussed enough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:209:He had been at the rail all week. He had watched three people he loved walk onto three floors and be read by the best files on the continent. Lira had met the fighter the program built and learned, to a tenth, exactly how far she was from Silver. Brom had let the finest reader at his tier take him apart line by line, on purpose, so that he could sell the one false line that mattered. Karis had met the woman who wrote her down and walked straight out of the writing. All three had been studied as hard as anybody could be studied. All three had come off the floor larger than they went on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:235:*Three floors this week,* he wrote, before he slept. *Three of us read by the best files on the continent. Lira lost to the program by a tenth and came home wanting a bigger thing than she went out for. Brom let the best reader alive read every line of him and won on the one line he'd forged. Karis walked out of her own file in front of the people who wrote it, and it cost her both hands.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:21:"Held a plate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:41:That night it went into the binder beneath the entry for the plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:181:"A while," said Seln, in the stale voice, and looked at his plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:81:He did it sitting up straight, with both hands flat on the table on either side of his bowl. He kept them shut for one breath, a long one, in and out, while the fire cracked and the innkeeper's girl clattered plates behind the counter and nobody at the table moved at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:149:*Qualified. Rooke read it at a waystation table with the bread going stale beside it, and shut his eyes for one breath, and then told us not to get careless. Seln had known since breakfast. Gault filed it in his case and then thought better of it and gave it to Rooke.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:211:The town stood on a tongue of land between the two rivers, built up to the water on both sides. Both rivers were iron-grey and running hard, with ice along their edges in plates and shelves, and the place where they met was a long rough seam of broken water that never froze, even in the deep cold, because the two currents never agreed with each other long enough to let it. Barges lay up at the quays three deep, roped together, their masts down. The hall where the meet was held stood at the very point of the tongue, with water on three sides of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:9:He put the bread down at her elbow and went away again. The next night, when Karis came down to the east hall with the rest of them, she brought the plate back empty, and he did not know whether she had eaten the bread or Seln had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:55:"The leaf says the function is appended to the reading and advises what's entered. It doesn't say what happens when the reading gives it nothing to advise, and nothing else in the case says it in words." She looked at Cael. "But your file says it. The petition put it in schedule two by its full title, on the first night of the year, and you read it. *Suppression-Advisory Watch, Priority Level 4.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:59:"Advisory, the same word, on a designation nobody at any height has ever been able to trace to an office." Karis drew a breath. "Cael told me on the third night, when he brought the bread. I asked him about Denvash, and he told me: eleven seconds. The plate lit eleven seconds late, and the word came at the end of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:61:Cael had not known she had asked for anything but the bread. He remembered telling her, standing at her elbow with the plate in his hand, and her not looking up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:63:"Eleven seconds is a long time for a reading," said Karis. "Every other reading on the continent is done before you've finished a breath. I think the function had his reading in front of it for eleven seconds and couldn't find a column to put it in, any column, and couldn't take the margin off because it was all margin. So it did what a good form does when it can't be filled in: it failed safe, and wrote the one word it had for a thing that couldn't be written. Then it marked the file, so that whoever came after would know there was something there it hadn't been able to correct." She laid the slip down. "The mark has no office behind it because no office made it. It made itself, the way a stamp comes down when a lever's pulled. And Ilsev sent it up, at the hearing, twice, to every height she could reach, and asked who'd authorized it. Something answered her each time, something that wasn't anybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:185:"Now I've got it." She put her finger on the line. "The petition asks for one thing, compelled re-evaluation under standard protocol. It doesn't care about the provision or the house or the clause; it wants him in front of a reading again." She looked at the case on its shelf. "A re-evaluation is an evaluation. The memorandum covers every evaluation at which a reading is entered. If the reading reads him this time, older, with eight things in him instead of none, the function is waiting beside it with its advice, and the advice is entered, and the order comes down, and he's a column, whatever column it chooses: one family, first rank, margin not to be entered." Her voice was very steady. "And if the reading goes out again, the way it went out at Denvash, then there's no valid classification, and it's chapter fourteen, in sequence: nullification, suspension, restriction."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:189:"Both doors open into the same room, and it doesn't matter to them which he walks through." Karis laid her hand over the open cover. "Re-evaluation isn't the test. It's the delivery."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-34.md:181:Lira had gone to bed in the afternoon on Rooke's orders and slept through the supper bell. When she came down, the others had kept her place and her plate, and Brom had kept the end of the loaf, which in Brom was close to a declaration.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-34.md:187:He set the jug down. "Then one week you stopped coming at me. You went round. You made me chase you all over the oak until I was blowing like a carthorse, and you touched me on the back of the neck, and I sat down on the floor." He went back to his own plate. "You beat me the times you made me play yours. Don't play hers."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-56.md:234:Cael looked up once more, past the officials, to the high row over the east rail. Every face in it was turned down toward the floor. Every face but one. The man in plain grey at the end of the row was standing, and going along behind the plated chairs toward the east stair, at the same unhurried pace he had used on the day of the duelist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-51.md:77:Rooke went down to the front rail with a coaching sheet that had nothing written on it, and found Withrow already there. She had watched every bracket bout of the sitting from the upper gallery, among the other chancellors, and had not once come down. Today she sat at the rail itself, with her closed files on her knee and her hands folded on them, and he stood at her shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-51.md:113:Rhagen had sent its Wind to take the west crossing off Lira. Cael watched the Wind learn three things, in his own order, at the price of three walks to his gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:57:Cael ran the middle. He had no platform of his own. He stood a little north of the barrier between the two northern ramps, where he could see across the open ground, and he read the traffic. He read weight and direction and where every fighter on the floor was going a fraction before they went, the ordinary read, the thing his file called peculiar timing, and he gave it out to the others in single words. The five of them had been growing those words for a year at tables and on floors, until they were almost a language of their own. *Left. Back. Two coming. Gap.* Nobody else in the building could have understood half of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:245:Nobody ever had. In a year of wardens and evaluators and brass plates and panels, everybody who looked at him had been looking for a line to fill in or a figure to post. Nothing in her face was going to be written down anywhere. She meant to find out what he was before she went on, because he was on none of her tables.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:317:Rooke read all five lines, slowly, at the head of the table. He did not say anything about any of them. He folded the sheet into the coaching file, and closed the file, and sat with his hand flat on the cover of it for a moment longer than he needed to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-46.md:349:*She stopped mid-trial to read me. Gold Rank 3, nineteen years old, and the first person at this tournament whose attention feels like mine. Filed under: things that are going to happen whether I schedule them or not.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:53:Rooke had said nothing at all since asking for the ruling to be read. He sat at the end of the table with the coaching file closed under his hand and his eyes on the fire, and when the talk had moved on to other things, Cael saw him open the file, find a page near the back, and write something on it that was longer than one line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-23.md:21:He was an Iron Rank Nine of the Force Path, the steward said, and had worked the barges on these two rivers since he was a boy. In the deep cold, when the barges lay up, he fought. He had fought at the confluence every year for longer than the steward had kept the list. The gallery loved him, and it was easy to see why, because he was what the whole town was made of: the rivers, and the barges, and the long hard trade of the deep cold, when the ice came down in plates and a man who misread the water for one moment lost a boat and everything in it. When he raised one hand to the beams, the beams roared back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-23.md:245:It came out at the long table in the inn that evening. The innkeeper had given them his long room at the back, with the fire built up to roaring and the framed ruling taken down off his wall and propped on the mantel, where he said it belonged for the night. The company had eaten, and the plates had gone, and everybody had settled back in the warmth, half expecting something. Rooke had the look of a man who has been warned he may be called on, and has a few words ready, and hopes not to need them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:91:Cael had read the compilers' files on the five of them, all four pages of Brom's and every shop's careful guess at his own. None of them had Seln in it. Every list had him on its last line, and every file took its cue from the lists. Then a woodcutter who had never read any of them had looked up from his bench as they went by his shop, and cut six figures into the block instead of five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:127:*And one of all of us. It took a man with a knife and a block of pearwood, who has never read a file in his life, to see where the sixth of us stands.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:215:The Archmarshal Vastin read the Norhold files on a Fourth-day evening, in his room in the Compact's house, a week before the opening.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:221:He had read a great many files in forty years, and most of them told him more about the office that had made them than about the thing they described. These were no different. Their courtesies told him which officers liked one another and which did not. Their dates told him that somebody had been in a hurry in the cold term, and then had not. He noted that, and did not make it carry more than a date can carry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:227:It was a plain document, a plan of twelve seats drawn in a clerk's neat hand, with a name-plate entered against each. The six observation seats came first, then the district officers, then a liaison seat, then two left for officials of the host city. He read along them in order. One, two, three. Eleven.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:231:There was no plate entered against it. Where the name should have been there was a short instruction in the same clerk's hand. *Seat twelve: held. No plate. Stewards to keep the seat clear for the length of the sitting.* Beneath that, in the column for the grade of the officer for whom a seat was held, the clerk had written one word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:93:He did not mean it unkindly. He meant it as a sum. Any filing carried three things inside it, and only one of them was the thing it said it was about. It carried what a person had noticed. It also carried the chair that person had been put in, and the list of what they had been told to look for, and nobody in the whole history of the service had ever found a way to wash those two out of the ink afterward. You could not ask a filing to see round its own chair. You could lean on it a little. You could not lay a floor across it and stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:125:The order was the panel's. It was the order the panel had been given the matter in, and so it cost the most to read, and he had paid that price on every file for most of his life.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:157:So there were two ways to read it. Either the three weeks were wrong in the record, which happened, quietly and for years. Or something in the two years before them had made a boy who could do that much with three weeks. And nowhere in the file, in two years of district filings or in any page of the warden's long reports, did a single sentence say what. There was a great deal about what the boy had done. There was nothing about what had made him able to do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:169:He had read files in the panel's order ever since, and grown no theories he could help.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:215:"Her talking and her reading keep to different rooms in her head," he said. "She can argue with you and read your file at the same time, and she won't lose her place in either."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:7:Cael, at the rail, knew exactly where he had read about it. There was an old edition of Brom's file in circulation, the first one the shops had made, four close pages with the seam laid out on the third. The shops had sold a later one since. This young man had bought the cheap one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:123:Ephram took the first exchange two to one with the plain entry he had kept all year, and the heel behind it, and the late half-turn of the front foot that had won him the mill town. The coast Blade had read none of it in any file. He met it honestly and lost honestly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:163:Ephram studied his plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:207:"Rhagen first, because Rhagen's the one that matters." He laid the first page down. "Every house on this continent owns a copy of Rhagen's book. Their doctrine is written down, every piece of it, and sold, and argued over in every coaching room I've ever sat in. By tomorrow night I'll have that man's whole structure laid out for you to the half beat. He knows that. He knows we'll have read it." Rooke looked round the table. "And he's filed anyway. So he isn't coming to win, and he isn't coming to surprise anybody. He's coming to see whether the book holds. He'll run the whole of it at you, every layer, and watch what happens to each piece." He searched for the word and found it, and made a face at it. "It isn't a bout. It's a *survey*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:295:"It's reading me. It's only polite to read back." Below them a printed sheet went from one hand to another, and Cael saw his own woodcut face go past upside down. "The coats at home were reading my file. They wanted something out of it. These people only want a good afternoon, and I'm in it. Nobody down there cares how anything works. They want to see what happens next."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:337:Every one of their chairs had a brass plate on its back with a name cut into it. He had read the plates from the floor with a borrowed glass on the first day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:341:He had the borrowed glass again now, and he put it to his eye. The chair was the same as the others, dark wood with a cane seat, and the registry's grey rail-cloth ran on in front of it without a break. There was a clean pale square on its back where a plate would have been screwed, and the screw holes were there, empty, from some earlier sitting. Somebody had sat in it once, at some other sitting, under some other plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:343:He looked at it for a long time. A plate, a steward and a brushed seat were all a chair could tell anybody from a public gallery, and this one had told him two of the three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:347:*Compact row, seat twelve. Held, unplated, empty, and guarded. Three times now a steward has cleared it, the last time of a man with a pie.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:349:*Every other chair in that row has a brass plate on its back. Twelve has a pale square where a plate used to be, two empty screw holes, a brushed seat, and a steward at the head of the stair. No name.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:213:"The long way," said Lira, to her plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:17:Brom ate his breakfast in silence. He ate all of it, and then he ate the end of the loaf, which nobody contested, and then he sat with his hands on the table on either side of his plate and looked at nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:23:"I know. I've been thinking about the second half." He looked round at them. "Plan to have fewer. That's not plan to have none. You can't have none. Nobody can. Rooke knows that better than anybody." He stood up and began to clear his own plate, which he never did. "So the question isn't how many I've got. It's which ones he gets to keep."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:71:Across the floor, Marek looked at where Brom was standing, and then down at the seam in front of him, and then at Brom again. Cael was watching his face. He saw nothing on it at all, except that the man had read something, and filed it, and gone on to the next line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:73:High on the east side, over the far rail, the Compact's row was full except for one chair. Ilsev sat in the first seat with her lap-desk on her knees. Havel was in the second. At the end of the row, behind the grey rail-cloth, the twelfth chair stood empty, with its brushed seat and its pale square where a plate should have been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:91:Marek stepped back over the seam onto the stone. He did not look at the left side again for the rest of the morning. One contact had told him everything the dossier was worth, and he had closed that page and put it away, and Cael saw the craft-readers in the tiers see it too. In the space of a single touch, the old edition of Brom's file had stopped being worth the paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:325:*I sat at the rail today and thought about my own record,* Cael wrote that night, after the ceremony and the letter and everything else. *Every figure on it is a price I set. Every exhibition I've fought this year, I've fought so that the paper would read a certain way. Brom forged one line, once, in the open, for one bout, and then shook the man's hand and told him exactly what he'd done. Mine is the whole file, every day, and I can't tell anybody.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-37.md:327:*Marek said he'd never had a page forged on him before, and sounded pleased. I don't think anybody who reads my file is ever going to get to say that.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:93:He had it from four things, and he had filed none of them. There was the post locking in the east hall, with the dial sheets against his chest, and two things the boy's record said he did not own arriving inside one breath. There was Jask, a third-year, telling the refectory queue for a week that the boy was *quick off a read*; the file that came home from the guest floor with nothing written on it, and his own line under it, *unmarked file. one of three. probably the third.*; and one slip of wing paper, cut small, in a hand that was nobody's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:175:Cael watched him do it, and watched him wait until the plates were gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-17.md:207:"One thing left," said Karis, with her pen still on the page. "If there's a file on Cael in every meet town, then everybody at Norhold has read him before they meet him. All of them. And Auremont's had scouts at this meet. They won't be buying their file from a shop."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:117:Seln already had it. The certified extract of the enrollment record was in the file's front pocket, with a thin paper tab at the provision's page. He drew it out and put it into her hand, and stepped back again, and his face did nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-07.md:255:Three things, for one touch. Lira thought it cheap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-47.md:21:Brom had not said anything. He had a folded paper of his own beside his plate, and he waited until the others had finished with the broadsides before he unfolded it, because Brom read things at the table or not at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-47.md:23:It was a single leaf, printed small, in the clean type of the commercial shops, and a printer in the delegation quarter had been selling it since before dawn for a copper to anybody who already owned the rest of the file, which by the length of the queue Ephram described was a good part of the city. It was meant to go in at the back. It had the shop's mark at the top and a date and a title, and under the title one line in italics.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-47.md:207:There was exactly one name above, and every one of them had been not saying it since the tunnel. It had become a kind of rule among them without anybody making it, the way the long table's rules got made: the name was not to be spent before its time. Brom ate his supper. Ephram looked at his plate. Lira, who left nothing unsaid that she wanted said, put her cup down and looked across at Cael and said nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:25:Every reader of records learns early that the registry can say no in a hundred ways. *Not of record. Not found. No file.* The registry was full of honest holes, places where something had once been written down and later lost, or never written down at all. She had filed hundreds of those holes in her time without losing a night's sleep over any of them. A hole was only a hole. You marked it, and went round it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:37:What she wrote, nobody ever read, because it was not for any file and not addressed to anybody, and it was the first thing she had written in all her years of service that was not meant to be read by somebody else. She wrote it, and read it through once, and folded it twice, and put it inside her coat, against her chest, where she had never in her life carried anything that belonged to the Compact.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:199:"If I gave you names I would be guessing, and I don't guess." He did not say it to be clever. He said it as Gault said a plate would not read past its last notch. "I can tell you what my year has looked like from my chair. A year ago I was asked what you are. This year I am asked what can be done about you. I answer every one of those letters by saying, again, what you are. Each answer goes up the stair. Each one is entered and filed. I am fairly sure none of them is read." He turned his left hand over, and back. "My office still works. It works very well. It has simply stopped mattering. When the questions stop being questions, an evaluator knows the decision has been taken in some room he was not shown."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:229:"With the wrong instruments, for three days," he said. "I came up to the bluff with a file and a plate and a set of questions, and all three told me nothing. A man in my trade has one great temptation, and I gave in to it. I thought the nothing was something being hidden from me." He moved the left hand. "It is the oldest mistake there is. When an instrument won't read, you'd sooner believe it's broken than believe you've pointed it at a thing it was never made to measure."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-16.md:347:When supper was over and the plates were being cleared, the door from the kitchen passage opened and Seln came in from the quay. He had his coat on and the travel file under his arm, and his face was the same as it always was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-16.md:351:"When the plates are gone," he said, to the table in general, "the office has a matter to put to the table. It won't take long."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-48.md:195:"Mine is Zerin," she said. "I lost to her over four exchanges with the whole city watching, and I've been living on what that bought me ever since. Best thing that's happened to me since Fenmark put my things outside the door." She came away from the window and sat on the end of the bench. "And you've spent a year being measured by things that can't take your weight. Plates and panels and tables and men with rods. There's one person in this city whose measure of you would be worth having, and this morning she asked for it with one word." She looked at him. "I said somebody had to argue against. I never said it was me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-34.md:23:Nobody at the table asked whether he was going. Karis talked about the transitional article of a directive nobody else had read. Brom described exercise thirteen, which involved a door frame and was, he said, the worst so far by a distance he could not express in feet. Lira ate with her eyes on her plate and said almost nothing. When they got up she walked beside him as far as the foot of the residence stair, and stopped there, and looked him over once, top to bottom, as she looked over the standings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-34.md:150:Three things on that page had never been on any page before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:93:The block's inventory went into the Log that night, with the ferry landing white and empty below except for its two coats. Most of it said what it always said, so he wrote those lines short: the oak's six free a day and the landing beat no shorter; the right shoulder unasked for anything since the mill town's plate; Compression shown nowhere, not even for the barge-master's fall; Shadow's rent from waking, and one drift on the stair. Then he wrote the two lines that were new.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:169:The snow had stopped. The sky had cleared and was full of stars, and the river below was loud and dark and fast, with ice going down it in plates that knocked together in the current with a sound like somebody far off moving furniture.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:15:There remained the order for re-evaluation, and that was not the clerk's to close. It had a date upon it, and a room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:17:So on the Fourth-day, which was the last of the academic year, a stout fair man unlocked a frame-house at the hour appointed, and took the cover off the frame and folded it. The president of the evaluation seat came, since the ruling had charged her to see to the conduct of the thing, and she was a woman who saw to what she was charged with. She brought her four evaluators. They stood on the flags in their plain coats, in a windowless room, with the plate dark at their feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:23:The code gave him his phrase. An order was executed when its hour had been kept and its room opened, and this one had been executed, in every particular, against nobody. He wrote *Re-evaluation: not executed; evaluand not produced*, which was how the form said the same thing. The president read the line and initialled it. Then the officer put the cover back over the frame and locked the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:69:*In the matter of Caelen Hesk-ward. Enrollment: closed by resignation; the house's certificate annexed. Proceedings: moot. Re-evaluation: not executed. Asset-restriction: pending jurisdiction.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:45:He had six free on the sprung oak. This floor was not the oak, it was the second floor's boards, a little stiffer, a little less forgiving, and he knew from a month of mornings that six here was really five. The record said he had a burst that carried twenty feet and a read that looked like peculiar timing. The record had nothing in it about a burst shut off halfway through its paying, or about a contact that took a blow and did not give it back, or about a plate. So tonight, in front of a panel of five, he would fight with what the record held, at the rates the record held it, and not one thing more. It was the doctrine, and he had chosen it, and he had never before had to keep to it in front of people holding pencils.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:233:Karis sat over the tournament charter every night that week, late, in the small common room. She always read a governing document before it governed anybody. Cael sat across the table from her with the observation notebook and Rooke's forty-one profiles, which he was learning like coursework.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:7:The entry instrument lay open on the records-hall counter with its four companions stacked beside it, and Bracken had turned it round so that Cael could read it right way up, which he did not need. It was the fourth day of term, a Fifth-day, in the hour after the morning's session let out, and the stone hall was empty except for the three of them and the clerk at the far desk, who was copying something with his tongue between his teeth. Bracken had sent a boy to the residence with the first bell, carrying a half-sheet that said *Records hall, the hour after session. Bring nothing.* Nobody else had been asked. Withrow had declined to come down, and had said so to Bracken in Cael's hearing in the covered walk the afternoon before, in five words: "It's a filing. File it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:29:Through the nearest window he could see the long table at the back, and the five of them round it, and the plates. He could also see, along the side of the room, the counter, where a man could sit on a stool with a cup in front of him and his back to the wall and see the whole room and everybody in it, and not be at anybody's table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:61:Brom was opposite him, with his wrapped forearms resting on the table and a plate of something in front of him that had certainly not been there when the proprietor opened her door. As Cael sat, a girl from the kitchen came and put down a basket of bread between them. Then she brought a pot of mussels in a dark, buttery broth, and a dish of potatoes cut thick and salted with grey salt, and went away again. Nobody had asked her for any of it. Brom looked at the mussels with mild interest, as a man looks at weather he had expected.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:77:She had them under her on the bench, palms down, both of them, and when she saw him see it she went faintly pink and took them out and put them on the table, and they lay there, empty. There was no notebook beside her plate. There was no document wallet at her feet or pencil behind her ear. She had come to the harbour with nothing whatever to write on, and she had found, by the look of her, that her hands did not know what to do about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:93:"It's balanced," he said, sitting down, still breathless. "The whole gate. It weighs as much as this building and it's balanced so exactly that one man can move it with one hand, if he moves it at the right time. If he moves it at the wrong time, nothing on earth can shift it." He unfolded the paper and laid it among the plates. "He gave me this."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:115:Brom saw it too. Without looking up from his plate he put out one wrapped hand and moved the pot of mussels six inches along the table, to the empty end of the bench beside him, where nobody was sitting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:121:Then it went on, and five people managed it without a word between them. Lira, in the middle of telling Ephram that he would not be allowed to wind anything else today, reached down the table without looking and set a clean cup in front of Seln and filled it from the cider jug, and went on talking. Brom went back to his plate. Ephram turned round and asked Seln, quite seriously, whether the office happened to know what the harbour's lock gates were made of, because the lock-keeper had said oak and Ephram thought he had seen iron, and Seln said, "Both," and Ephram said, "I knew it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:129:Cael ate his fish. He kept his face on his plate. He put the moment away very carefully, in the place where he kept the few things he meant to have for the rest of his life.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:193:It was a brown honey-cake of the northern kind, sticky on top and plain underneath. Lira borrowed a knife from the next table and cut it into six pieces, and looked at them, and moved one by a hair, and served them round. Brom did not look up from his plate. Lira looked at him down the whole length of the table while she did it, steadily, until Karis laughed out loud at the two of them, which happened about once a season.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-19.md:473:Brom read the Copper leaf with his head on one side and said, "Same line again," with deep satisfaction. Ephram copied the whole record into his file book, both brackets and the exhibitions and the house totals, standing up, in his careful hand, and when Karis asked him why, when Seln was already copying it, he said that a champion of the upper Iron kept his own files and that he did not intend to stop being one just because he had lost a semifinal. Gault picked up his case by its handle and held it a little higher than he needed to, so that the registry clerk at the next table could see the stitching.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:21:He followed her along the back of the gallery, past the public benches and behind the row's plated chairs, to the end, where the grey rail-cloth ran on in front of a chair with a pale square on its back and two empty screw holes. She lifted the cloth for him and let it fall again when he had sat down, and went back to her stair.

