191:He had known it, of course. An officer of his grade did not arrive; he was announced, by a form, four days before, and the form went to the very people whom he came to look at. So he had ridden for forty years through streets that had been swept the day before, past whitewashed kerbs, to offices where every book lay squared on its desk and a man stood at the sill with his cap in his hand. He had allowed for it, as a careful officer allows for the known error of a scale, and had supposed that he could subtract the whitewash and arrive at the street.
195:It was filthy. It was loud, and exact, and nobody in it had been told anything. A boy went past with a yoke of slops. Two porters were quarrelling over a card, with their foreheads almost touching. A woman had been turned away from the painted door ten minutes since; he had heard the whole of it, *that side's for names, mistress, you want the arch*, and had heard her come in by the arch, grumbling, and pay.
223:Out under the arch, a clerk in an apron had come to the bench across the way. Four people got up. A card went into a cap, and the four went off up the hill with frames on their backs, and not one of them had been asked so much as a name.
229:It was the large young man who had set it there, and he had six others on a board. Vastin felt for a copper. He was told, as he had been told on four evenings running, to put it away.
241:"I did not go above the trough," said Seln. He sat down on the ladder's head with his feet on the second rung, which was as near as he came to resting. "I hired a frame for a copper, and I sat for three hours on the porters' bench at the top of the street, and was twice offered work. The man beside me has carried to the registry's yard for eleven years, and he likes to be listened to. I have four things from him, and one difficulty. I shall give them in his order."
245:"The first is the place. The old town stands upon a hole: before there was a wall on that bluff, somebody had quarried the crown of it from the inside, by galleries, and had gone. The town that came afterward kept its records down there, since it was dry; and when the Compact took the town, it built its registry across the quarry's mouth, and has never troubled to bring the old records up. Its people call that the lower archive, and they do not go into it."
249:"He carries to a yard; he has not been below the door, and he knows nobody who has." He inclined his head to her, by a very little. "There is one door, in the undercroft beneath the yard, and it is of iron. He says that the registry's wall is built round its frame, and he meant it as a complaint against the masons."
253:"The second is the leave. I have seen that form, in another house: it is the oldest provision in the registry's book, and the shortest. A practitioner who is registered under historical studies has standing entry to a lower archive, by the week, renewed at the yard door, and he may have matter brought down to him for study. For the practitioner, that is the whole of it."
269:"The third is the clerk. One man keeps the provision's book, at the yard door, and has kept it for many years, and in all that time there has been a single name in it. My porter does not know what he is called, and I did not ask."
273:"The fourth is what the form asks, when matter is brought." He drew his hands into his sleeves. "It was drawn a long while ago, when a scholar's bundles came with whoever happened to be carrying them, and nobody thought such people worth a line apiece. So it has a line for the matter, which is described. It has a line for the matter's bearer, who is one. And it has a line for hands."
275:[[TIER-B: "Four hands." / "I've two." "Entered by count. That's the door." — coordinator to supply protected text]]
285:He had been filed in every registry that had ever had him in reach. He had learned to read upside down so that he might know what was in the file. Now he lay in a hayloft under the oldest registry on the Line, and learned that it had one lawful door which a person of no tier could go through; and the door did not ask for his name. It did not ask for anything that he had spent his life refusing to give. It wanted to know how many of him there were.
293:"It is the exposure. I cannot shorten it." Seln spoke as he would have priced a wall. "The form does not require the clerk to ask who a bearer is, or a hand, and it does not forbid him. There will be a moment at that yard door when a pen is held over a line, and a man is counting; and if he is a curious man, or only a careful one, he will say *and you are?*, to pass the time. I have known a hundred clerks. Eighty of them would."
297:"And then somebody must answer, with a name or a lie; and I will not have a lie told at a registry's door by anybody who means to go on standing on this side of the Line." His eyes went, briefly, to Lira, and to Brom. "We go in on a count, or we do not go in. I cannot tell you which of the hundred he is."
301:"The square."
305:"It is the only way to the front door, and a load does not go in at a front door. There is a yard, and a yard has a gate, and carts must reach it by some road which is not a paved square with a station's steps on it." Seln got up off the ladder's head. "I have not found that road. I shall find it tomorrow; and tomorrow at noon somebody will stand at a cook-house counter with a sheet in her hand that a certain man cannot look away from. That is as far as I have got."
311:[[TIER-B: the S8 close — "Last time we walked into a place like this," … "it took four years and a hearing to walk out." / "Last time we didn't know what we were looking for." / "Do we now?" / "A name and a reason," … "Older than the walls, under our feet, and nobody's read it because nobody's had a reason to look and a person who could read it in the same lifetime." … "That's us. That's the whole reason it's still buried. We're the first ones who are both." — coordinator to supply protected text]]
