5:The day, when it came, was the two hundred and eighty-third. She had the second box unroped before there was any sun, and its lid off, and she knelt on the boards of the loft with fifty-two sheets in three heaps before her: eleven, and twenty-nine, and twelve. They were her own first copies, the ones that she had drawn on her knees under a ring of stones, and on a stair, and in a drowned hall, with a lantern held low. Cael had carried them a long way. He had not seen them out of their wrapper since a cold term.
7:"I had meant to take him the best," she said. "I lay awake over which they were: there are three from the Stair that came out clean, where the cutting was deep, and one from the Hall that I am proud of." She squared the third heap with the side of her hand. "And then I saw what I was doing, which was choosing what a man was to think before he had looked. If I show him my four best, I have shown him four things that a careful person can draw; so I shall take him all of it, with the bad sheets in their places."
13:She had a square of oilcloth for them, and a tape; and Seln watched her tie it, from the ladder's head, with a carrying-frame on his back that he had hired for the day and a keg lashed to the frame. He had been down to the boards before the lamp was out and had come back with a card in his cap, and the card's edge was buff.
37:Vastin had been standing at the gable all this while, under the blanket, with a strap of the tarred case in his hands. He had found a place where the stitching had begun to go, and he was working at it with an awl that he had borrowed of the stable.
39:"A practitioner of the Bronze, in a class which the registry ranks beneath its notice, who has gone down by one door for longer than a porter can remember," he said, to the strap. "I certified this town on four occasions, and his name was on a roll that was laid before me each time, and I did not once ask who he was."
47:Cael saw that from the wood-yard. It was a single long room, low, with a counter running down the far side of it; and behind the counter the pale stone went up bare into the smoke, with pots hung on iron pins that had been driven into its joints. Three keepers in blue coats were eating at a table by the street door. Along the counter stood clerks, and a groom or two, and a woman in a good cloak with a child.
49:The yard lay at the room's lower end, and it was only a space between the cook-house and the next building, roofed over with boards and stacked to the height of a man with split logs. There was a hatch in the wall, and a boy at the hatch, to whom Cael gave a copper, and from whom he had a bowl and half the copper back. A dozen porters stood about among the logs, eating on their feet; and he found a place by the doorpost, with a stack at his shoulder, where he could see the whole length of the counter end on.
57:His hands were square, with the nails pared to the quick, and they were as pale as roots.
59:Nobody stood next to him. There was room for two at that end of the counter, and the clerks had left it, as the carters on a bench had made room for a keeper; but this was the other kind of room, and Cael knew it well. It was the space that people leave about a person whom they have long since stopped trying to talk to.
63:She was very small in that doorway, with the light behind her, and a roll of oilcloth held against her chest. He saw her look down the room and find the dusty coat. She did not hurry. She came along behind the clerks with her chin level, as she had once walked up to a broker's plank with a hide; and she stopped at the counter's end, in the place that nobody took.
65:She did not speak. She undid the tape, and drew out the uppermost sheet, and laid it on the counter beside his bowl, and squared it to the counter's edge with one finger.
77:[[TIER-B: "Off floors," / "Fifty-two sheets." — coordinator to supply protected text]]
79:The eyes had gone back to the sheet. One square pale finger came out, and did not touch it; it moved along above a row of her drawing, an inch off the paper, and stopped.
83:"Drawn: by eye, with a rule, squared to a hand's breadth. I had no paper wide enough to rub, and in two of the three places the cutting was too shallow to take wax."
117:She had both hands on the roll, and they were white.
145:"Then it's a stair's worth." He felt in the dusty coat, and brought out a square of stiff card, the size of a man's palm, with a buff edge and a seal pressed into one corner, old, and rubbed nearly flat. He reached across the counter without asking, and took the pen that the cook kept for his tallies, and wrote three short lines with his face close to the card, and blew on them.
151:"He's not asked me anything in a long while. I can't speak for what he'll do with somebody new." He pushed the card along the counter with one finger. "No names. I don't want yours. If I've got it, I can be asked for it, and I've been asked for things before. You'll not have mine off my own tongue, either. It's on the card. That's as near as it comes to being said."
153:Karis took the card, and held it by its edges.
163:"One lamp below. Mine. Whoever holds it, holds it where I tell them. No other light, and no other fire of anybody's, however it's made." He looked at her hands on the roll, briefly, and Cael could not tell what he saw there. "There's paper under that town that's waited longer than the town has for somebody to drop a spark on it. It's not going to be one of yours."
199:She went up the ladder with the roll in one arm and the card in her teeth. She sat down on the nearest pallet, and took the card out of her mouth, and looked at it; and Cael saw that she had begun to shake, a little, and all over, as a person does who has come in out of the cold.
205:"He did not ask what I thought. I have been waiting for somebody to ask me that for as long as I can remember, and the people who asked did not believe it, and I had grown used to that. I had it ready, all of it, in order, with the evidence under each head." She laid the card on her knee. "And he would not have it. He did not disbelieve me; he simply would not hear it yet, and I do not know what a person does with that."
219:Lira had the card, since she had lifted it off Karis's knee by its edges, as it had been held, and carried it to where the light came round the blanket.
221:"*Matter*," she read, with her lips. "*Its bearer, one. Hands—*" She stopped. "There's a figure. And his name, at the top, and a rank, and a number as long as my arm. And a day." She turned it over. The back was blank. "That's all that there is. That'd take five people through the buff gate and up to the top of everything. And it's not got one of them on it."
225:"It's got how many." She was quiet for a moment, holding it. "I've a paper in this coat that would walk me through the blue on my own two feet, with a keeper reading my name out like a herald. I worked for that paper harder than I've worked for anything." She did not touch the pocket. "And this one's a scrap of card with a number, and it goes higher."
231:He did not push. He had seen her look at a blue coat from a smith's yard, the day before, with both her hands held still; and he thought that whatever she was working at had not finished with her yet.
237:"The third gate," he said, and made a stroke. "From it, the street goes on, straight, and opens into the square within eighty yards, and that is the way which a person is told." He drew it, and then he laid the straw across it. "We do not take that. Under the wall, on the right hand as you come through, there is a lane, which loads use; and a keeper will point you to it if he is asked where loads go, and I should let him."
241:"There are two. Carts go to the left, by a street that runs the whole way about the crown, half a mile, to the yard's lower gate; and half way along it there is a bar, and a beam, where every load is weighed, and the weight is written against an owner." His eyes came up. "It is a book."
257:Cael marked it, as he marked anything that people took care to step round. There was a figure on that card. There were three boxes, and they had three backs under them. There was a tarred case, which had ridden on one shoulder since a fork-stone, where nobody had asked for it and nobody had forbidden it. And there was a man with a straw in his fingers who went ahead of every load and had never yet been counted with one.
265:*Two hundred and eighty-third day. Treswick, the district under the first wall. A hundred and thirty-six. Saw the man, from a wood-yard, at five strides. He looked at one sheet for as long as it takes to swallow, and he did not read it as anything of mine has ever been read: he asked it nothing, and he started at the bottom. He wouldn't hear what she thinks they are, and he wouldn't have a name, and he never looked at the yard. I'd wanted that, and I find I'd wanted the other as well. Entered.*
267:*S. has a way to the yard that crosses nothing I've written about. A stair. Tomorrow, the second bell, on a card with a figure on it.*
277:Karis had not lain down either. She sat against the boxes, with the roll across her knees and both her hands flat on it.
293:She took that in, and he watched it go into her, slowly, like water into dry ground; and her hands closed on the oilcloth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md
5:She had walked nine miles along the hill-foot since noon on legs that had wanted a bed. She had told a carter that she did not put her hands to hard work tired; and she had then spent an hour inside his wrist and set out on a road. It was not the first time that she had broken that rule. It was the first time in a long while that she had broken it before the day's second gate, with the gate still in front of her.
27:That hold kept its keeper outside the wall. There was a room built against the wall's outer face, on the road side, with one window and a bed in it, since somebody must sleep on the wrong side of a bar to hear who knocks at night. An old woman stood in its doorway with her sleeves rolled and her hands held away from her apron.
35:"Square across. I've tried to walk it round, and I've not the hands for it any more."
41:"I'm spent. I gave it to a wrist this morning, and I'd do the same again, and that's no use to you at all. I told a man last night that I don't put my hands on this sort of thing tired." She dried them, a finger at a time. "A child doesn't wait on what I've told people."
43:The room was hot, and smelled of tallow, and there was a young man in the corner who had been told to keep out of the way and could not find any. The woman on the bed had her eyes shut. She was perhaps twenty. She opened them when the pain let go of her, and looked at a strange square face in the lantern-light.
45:"I'm a healer," said Oryn. "My name's Oryn; I walk this road. Your child's lying crosswise in you, with its back to the door, and I can turn it. I do it from outside, with my hands on your belly, slowly, in between; it'll hurt you some, and a good deal less than what you're doing now." She waited until the eyes had hold of hers. "May I?"
49:She laid her hands on, and counted, and went in.
57:Her hands asked. The Path eased what they asked of, a little, so that a wall which had been set like a fist went slack under her palms for the length of a breath; and in that breath she walked the small boat round by the width of a finger. Then the pain came back, and the wall shut, and she stood with her hands exactly where they were and waited for it to pass over. She did that forty times, perhaps, and lost the count, which she never did.
63:The head came down into its place a little after midnight. After that it was the old woman's work, and the young one's; and Oryn sat on the floor against the wall with her hands in her lap, because she could not have stood, and kept two fingers on an ankle to hear the rivers through to the end.
71:She had met one body in which ten ran together with no bank to any of them. In the middle of the ten there had been a place where nothing ran at all, and everything went round. She had called it a stone, in her own head, on a carter's wrist, and she had called it a join. Sitting here with her hands as heavy as wet sacks, she knew that it had been neither of those, and that she had only reached for the two nearest words. She did not have a word. She had a child, an hour old, that had lain turned the wrong way in the dark and could not have told anybody so; and she had a boy three days south who was walking into a town with a thing in him that he could not read. She had not read it. She had sent him in with a rule about a square, since a rule had been the only thing that she owned to give him.
87:There was a white frost in the inn yard at dawn on the two hundred and eighty-fourth day, and the three boxes stood in it on their ends.
93:Karis had the flat under one arm and the hide under the other. She had the card inside her left glove, against the palm, and she had looked to see that it was there four times since the ladder.
95:Seln stood by the pump, with his hands in his sleeves. He had nothing on his back.
97:"The card has a figure on it," he said. "Three of that figure are plain, and they are standing beside their loads; and I am not the fourth. I shall say why once, since you will otherwise think it a nicety."
101:"A hand is a thing that a clerk looks at for the space of a count. I was in a registry's service for fifteen years, and for the most of them I was looked through; but it was clerks who looked through me, and a clerk remembers furniture. There may be one man in that yard who has seen me walk a file from one desk to another, in a house above Ostrand or in some other. If he finds such a face among the porters, he will not ask me who I am. He will ask himself what the rest of you are, to be worth it." He drew a breath. "I should cost you the count. And there is this besides: when a door has shut on five people, somebody ought to be standing where he can see who comes to look at the door. That person cannot be on a card."
115:He had said it before a mouth was open. He stood at the foot of the ladder, with both hands on his stick and the frost round his boots, and he looked at none of them in particular.
141:Cael put his boot on the first of the slanted setts with a box on his back and a card going before him in a small pale woman's glove. He marked it, as he had marked a sill, since two days ago he had stood at that kerb and not stepped. It was the same stone; he had only become a different sort of thing to it.
145:Karis gave up the card. She did it well, he thought: she did not offer it, or explain it, but only held it out and looked at the wall. The keeper with empty hands took it. He looked for the figure, as he had looked for a hundred figures since the gate opened. Then his eyes went back up the card, to its head, and stopped there.
149:"There's no such card."
171:"He'll want to. It's a hill, from here." The feather dropped. He made a stroke on his sheet, a single one, and tore the corner off the card and gave the card back. "Go on."
179:There would be names on that sheet, in a clerk's hand, each with a district and a rank; and there was a pocket in her coat with such a line folded in it, sealed, which a station at Ostrand had made out to her, and which she had worked harder for than for anything else that she owned. She had only to put the box down.
189:The roofs were slate, every one. The street had a gutter down each side of it, with water running, and no dung. The windows had glass in them. At a corner there was a cistern with a brass spout, and a girl was filling a jug at it; and she did not put a copper in any dish, since there was no dish. A row of children's voices came out of an upper window, chanting something all together, and he caught the shape of it before the words: it was a list, and it went up by steps, and at the top of each step there was a colour.
193:It was not as the outer street had not looked, where everybody was carrying and a box was only a thing in the way. People here were not carrying anything. They walked with their hands free, in good cloth, at their own pace; and their eyes went over four backs under loads as eyes go over a row of posts. A woman drew her skirt aside for Brom without breaking her talk. Cael had been stared at by such people for the whole of his life since a Kindling. He had not known that there was a way of standing so low that they could not find him.
215:"It is the standing order for a district house," said Vastin. He had come up level, and stopped, with both hands on the stick, and he was looking at the board as he had looked at a bridge with five piers. "I have read it in the code, where it is the ninth article of a schedule. I had never seen it in chalk."
221:They walked past it. There was nothing else to be done with it. But Cael carried it up the hill with the box, a square woman's voice saying that it was not cruelty and was only how a heap was sorted; and he thought of a girl on a kerb with a basket of eggs, and a child who had taken hold of a stick with both fists.
229:He read the card twice. He read it as Karis read a seal, from the stamp inward.
231:"Lower archive," he said. He had a dry, exact voice. "I had heard that there was such a card. My father kept this gate before me, and he told me that he had passed one, once, when he was young." He looked up, at three boxes, and at a tarred case, and for rather longer at a courier's flat of waxed leather with a stamp across its face. "That is a good deal of matter."
237:"One, and four," he said. He made his stroke, and tore the second corner off the card.
243:"Not by the square. They'll not thank you for boxes on that paving, with the steps swept." He pointed with the pen, through the arch and to the right. "Under the wall. There is a lane. Follow it till it goes down, and then go down with it, and you will come to a yard. Mind your lame one on the steps. They are older than he is."
253:Then he was through, and he stood inside the third wall of Treswick, on the crown of the bluff, with a box on his back.
259:It was a hundred paces off. He could have walked to it while a person counted to sixty. Hesk's book lay against his ribs, under the box's strap, with two sentences in it that a square woman had read with her lips moving; and he found that he was glad, for the first time, that she had made him write them. A thing that he had only said would have been arguing with him by now.
