9:Each was no wider than a door. Each ran straight, from the lane, away to his left, between blind walls; and at the far end of each, a hundred yards off, there stood an upright strip of white. It was the same white that had lain on the setts at the head of the street. The square was at the end of every one of them. It lay along that side of the lane, the whole way, like a lit room beyond a row of keyholes.
23:It came down the alley from the white end, small and clear in the quiet: a broom, going on stone, in long even strokes. Somebody was sweeping a great many steps. The keeper at the arch had said that they were swept. Under the broom, once, he thought that he heard a child's voice, high up and a long way off, counting.
31:"There is a plan of this town, filed at the seat, which I have had unrolled for me on four occasions. It shows three walls, two gates, a square, and the principal street. The registry is drawn as a block with one door." The stick came down. "I do not find upon it any lane by which a load might reach that building without crossing the paving. I should have said, before today, that there was none."
45:It turned as it fell. The houses ended on the left hand, and the wall on the right dropped to a parapet no higher than his waist, where the crown of the bluff broke off; and for the length of ten yards there was nothing on that side but air.
125:He did the last five without a word. At the bottom he stood with both hands on the ash, quite grey, and looked back up the slot at the strip of sky.
137:He looked at it from the ground upward. It was of the pale stone, mortared, and it went up five storeys, with a row of small barred windows to each. It had no ornament on this side. A registry does not trouble about its back.
147:There was a low door in that dark footing, and a square hatch beside the door with a ledge, and a bench along the wall for people who waited. Karis went to the hatch. She laid the card on its ledge, with two corners gone. A hand came out of the dimness and took it in, and nothing else happened.
157:He had not meant to write until night. But he had a quarter of an hour, and a thing that he had seen at a parapet, and he knew by now what became of the things that he left until night. He set the day at the head, and the place, which was the registry's yard at Treswick; and he looked at that for a moment, since he had never in his life expected to write those four words together of his own will.
173:He left it. He shut the book on the pencil, and sat with it between his hands.
175:Somewhere above him, on the far side of five storeys of pale stone, there was a square. He did not know how many yards off it lay; a building's thickness, and a flight of steps. He had come round it by a lane with four keyholes, and down a slot, and he sat now with its whole registry between himself and it, on the low side, among the firewood. He had not set a foot on its paving. He had not lifted his eyes to what stood over it.
183:The low door opened, and the clerk came out with a book.
185:He was a man of no particular age. He had a grey coat with ink on its right cuff and none upon the left, and a narrow book bound in green cloth, and a pen. He was pale, as people are who are kept indoors, and he blinked once at the daylight. Cael did not learn his name that day, or on any day after it.
187:He had the card in his fingers. He stood on the sill and read it again, slowly, though he must have read it at the hatch. Then he looked at the small pale woman who had brought it.
191:This was the place. A quiet man had told them so on a ladder's head, and had not been able to shorten it. There was a line in that green book, and a pen above the line, and a person holding the pen who was neither forbidden to ask nor required to. A hundred clerks, Seln had said. Eighty would.
193:The clerk's eyes left Karis. They came along the bench.
203:The clerk's mouth had opened.
207:The clerk shut his mouth.
209:He looked down again at the card, at the seal in its corner, which was old, and had a piece gone. Something went over his face, and Cael could not have sworn to what it was. It was not suspicion. It was not much like curiosity either. If he had been made to put a word to it, he would have said that a man who had kept a book with one name in it, for a great many years, had at last been handed a second line to write, and did not mean to spoil it by learning anything that he would then be obliged to set down.
217:[[TIER-B: "Four hands," / "Four." / "Right." — coordinator to supply protected text]]
223:Cael got his arms through the beckets. He had been a name with a word after it on every sheet that the Compact had ever kept of him; and he had spent his life since a Kindling on other people's wrong side of a counter, reading that word upside down. He went past a clerk of the oldest registry on the Line, into its foundations, as the quarter part of a figure in the last column of a green book. The man did not look at him as he went by. He was looking at the line that he had written, with something very like satisfaction.
229:It was a short flight, of the pale stone, and it brought them into a vault that ran away under the building for farther than the lamps showed. There were lamps: iron ones, in brackets, a long way apart. There were pillars as thick as Brom, and between them stacks of bound paper tied in tape, and broken stools, and a dead clock. A registry had been putting things down here for a long time that it did not want and could not burn.
233:He heard them through the vault's crown, dull and many, going to and fro: five storeys of clerks, at their desks and between them. A whole town was up there, above that again, with its gates and its coats and its chalked boards, sorting everybody who came within reach of it by a colour. It had a book at every door. It had looked at him four times since a bridge and seen a back.
235:The clerk had come down behind them with a lantern. He did not lead; he only walked at the rear, as a man does who is seeing a load to its place.
247:He had expected something great. It was not. It was low, lower than the round arch had been, and broad, and it was made of plates of black iron laid one over another like slates. There was not a lock on it that he could see, or a handle. It stood in a frame of the dark stone; and on either side of that frame the registry's pale masonry came up, and stopped, each block cut to lie against the dark, as a tower had been cut to a bridge.
255:The clerk went by him and knocked.
261:There was a man in the opening, with a lamp held low at his knee. He had a coat the colour of dust. He stood at the head of a stair that went down into no light whatever, and his red eyes did not go to any face. They went to the card, which Karis held out to him, and then they went to the loads.
267:Karis went in first, with her bare hand held before her like something that she was carrying. Brom bent nearly double to follow, and the bowl under his lashing scraped the iron. Lira went. The stick went, a tread at a time, and its owner did not look round at a clerk who had very nearly asked him a question.
273:Above him lay a registry, and above the registry a square. Three walls stood round that, and two gates, with a pen at each; and at the very top of it all, where he had not once lifted his eyes, there was a thing that had been built to read whatever was brought near it. Not one stone of the whole reached lower than this door.
277:It shut with no more noise than a book makes. The clerk's lantern went out of the world, and the feet overhead, and the last grey thread of the day.

