    15	"Bring him here."
    16	
    17	She heard him on the bench, by the gate-house lantern, with two fingers on a wrist no thicker than a spoon and her ear against his back. He would not keep still, and she did not need him to. The river in him ran quick and clean. The whistle had gone down into a small low hum, a long way off, like a kettle in another room.
    18	
    19	"He's mended himself," she said. "They do, at that age, as often as not. Then I'm sent for to be told so."
    20	
    21	"So I needn't have feared."
    22	
    23	"You'd every need. You couldn't know which sort it was, and nor could I, from the far side of a shut road. Fear's what you had instead of me." She let the boy go, and he went straight back to the mule. "I'm sorry that it was all you had."
    24	
    25	It was then that the gate-house door opened.
    26	
    27	That hold kept its keeper outside the wall. There was a room built against the wall's outer face, on the road side, with one window and a bed in it, since somebody must sleep on the wrong side of a bar to hear who knocks at night. An old woman stood in its doorway with her sleeves rolled and her hands held away from her apron.
    28	
    29	"You're the healer," she said. "I'd have sent a boy up the road for you this morning, if I'd known there was a road with you on it. It's my son's wife. She's been at it since first light, and it's not coming. I've caught every child in this hold for thirty years, and I can feel a shoulder where there ought to be a head."
    30	
    31	Oryn stood up. The yard went grey at its edges, and came back.
    32	
    33	"Lying across."
    34	
    35	"Square across. I've tried to walk it round, and I've not the hands for it any more."
    36	
    37	She washed to the elbow at the butt, and it took her longer than it should have. The old woman watched her do it.
    38	
    39	"You're spent," she said. It was one tradeswoman's finding to another.
    40	
    41	"I'm spent. I gave it to a wrist this morning, and I'd do the same again, and that's no use to you at all. I told a man last night that I don't put my hands on this sort of thing tired." She dried them, a finger at a time. "A child doesn't wait on what I've told people."
    42	
    43	The room was hot, and smelled of tallow, and there was a young man in the corner who had been told to keep out of the way and could not find any. The woman on the bed had her eyes shut. She was perhaps twenty. She opened them when the pain let go of her, and looked at a strange square face in the lantern-light.
    44	
    45	"I'm a healer," said Oryn. "My name's Oryn; I walk this road. Your child's lying crosswise in you, with its back to the door, and I can turn it. I do it from outside, with my hands on your belly, slowly, in between; it'll hurt you some, and a good deal less than what you're doing now." She waited until the eyes had hold of hers. "May I?"
    46	
    47	"Yes. Yes. Do it."
    48	
    49	She laid her hands on, and counted, and went in.
    50	
    51	There were two rivers.
    52	
    53	She had felt it a hundred times, on a hundred benches, and it had never yet become an ordinary thing. There was the woman's, wide and driven, like a millrace with a week of rain behind it. And inside that, in a bed that was no bigger than a loaf, there ran a second, as quick as a bird's, twice as quick as anything grown, going its own way entirely. It did not know that anything was the matter. It lay across the current of its mother like a boat that has swung broadside in a stream.
    54	
    55	She did not hurry. She could not have hurried, with what she had left in her; and a child was not a thing that she would have hurried in any case.
    56	
    57	Her hands asked. The Path eased what they asked of, a little, so that a wall which had been set like a fist went slack under her palms for the length of a breath; and in that breath she walked the small boat round by the width of a finger. Then the pain came back, and the wall shut, and she stood with her hands exactly where they were and waited for it to pass over. She did that forty times, perhaps, and lost the count, which she never did.
    58	
    59	Twice the quick river faltered.
    60	
    61	Both times she stopped as she was, with nothing moving in the room but a tallow flame, and gave it what she had been keeping back for her own walk to the next gate. Both times it picked up, and ran on, and she breathed.
    62	
    63	The head came down into its place a little after midnight. After that it was the old woman's work, and the young one's; and Oryn sat on the floor against the wall with her hands in her lap, because she could not have stood, and kept two fingers on an ankle to hear the rivers through to the end.
    64	
    65	It was a daughter. She came at first light, in a great hurry at the last, and she was very angry about it.
    66	
    67	Oryn heard the cry from the bench outside, where they had carried her a cup. She had not been able to lift the cup. She sat with her back against field-stone and watched the hill go from black to grey, and listened to a person who had been in the world for less than a minute telling it what she thought.
    68	
    69	Two rivers in one body; and she had never in her life had any trouble in telling which was which. Each had its own bed. She could have drawn the banks.
    70	
    71	She had met one body in which ten ran together with no bank to any of them. In the middle of the ten there had been a place where nothing ran at all, and everything went round. She had called it a stone, in her own head, on a carter's wrist, and she had called it a join. Sitting here with her hands as heavy as wet sacks, she knew that it had been neither of those, and that she had only reached for the two nearest words. She did not have a word. She had a child, an hour old, that had lain turned the wrong way in the dark and could not have told anybody so; and she had a boy three days south who was walking into a town with a thing in him that he could not read. She had not read it. She had sent him in with a rule about a square, since a rule had been the only thing that she owned to give him.
    72	
    73	It was three weeks, less three days. She did not like to owe.
    74	
    75	The old woman came out and stood beside the bench, with her arms folded, looking at the light.
    76	
    77	"You were grey when you came," she said. "You're the colour of that wall."
    78	
    79	"I'll be some other colour by noon. Is she feeding?"
    80	
    81	"Like a calf."
    82	
    83	"Then leave me be for an hour, and tell that bench that I'll see it when I've slept. And somebody's to keep the boy off my mule."
    84	
    85	---
    86	
    87	There was a white frost in the inn yard at dawn on the two hundred and eighty-fourth day, and the three boxes stood in it on their ends.
    88	
    89	Brom had roped them again in the night, for a stair. Each had a second becket now, low down, so that it would sit high between the shoulders and leave a person's eyes clear for his feet. He went along the three of them once more in the half light, a knot at a time, as he had gone along them at a ferry's shed. Oryn's empty bowl was wedged under the lashing of his own, mouth down.
    90	
   225	The third wall was the oldest, and it showed.
   226	
   227	It was lower than the others, and thicker, and its stone had gone the colour of a tooth. The arch in it was round, and narrow, and not tall enough for a loaded wagon. There was one keeper under it. He was an old man, in a long pale coat that had been made for somebody broader, and he sat on a stool with a board on his knee.
   228	
   229	He read the card twice. He read it as Karis read a seal, from the stamp inward.
   230	
   231	"Lower archive," he said. He had a dry, exact voice. "I had heard that there was such a card. My father kept this gate before me, and he told me that he had passed one, once, when he was young." He looked up, at three boxes, and at a tarred case, and for rather longer at a courier's flat of waxed leather with a stamp across its face. "That is a good deal of matter."
   232	
   233	Karis said nothing. She looked at the wall.
   234	
   235	"It is not my business what it is. My business is how many." He lifted his pen, and it had no feather; he used the point. It went to her, and to Brom, and to Lira; it came to Cael, and he felt it on his breastbone like a finger, and it went on. It came to the stick, and the bound leg, and it did not linger there at all, since that old man had seen a great many porters, and he had long ago stopped expecting them to look like anything.
   236	
   237	"One, and four," he said. He made his stroke, and tore the second corner off the card.
   238	
   239	"Where do loads go?" said Karis.
   240	
   241	It was the only thing that she had been told she might ask. She asked it of the wall.
   242	
   243	"Not by the square. They'll not thank you for boxes on that paving, with the steps swept." He pointed with the pen, through the arch and to the right. "Under the wall. There is a lane. Follow it till it goes down, and then go down with it, and you will come to a yard. Mind your lame one on the steps. They are older than he is."
   244	
   245	"Thank you."
   246	
   247	"Go on."
   248	
   249	They went under the round arch, one behind another, and it was so low that Brom bent his head.
   250	
   251	*There?* asked Anchor. It asked very softly here, as a person lowers his voice in an old room. Cael told it no.
   252	
   253	Then he was through, and he stood inside the third wall of Treswick, on the crown of the bluff, with a box on his back.
   254	
   255	He had his eyes on the ground. He had put them there under the arch, before the light changed, and he held them there now as he would have held a rope. He saw setts, small ones, laid in a pattern of fans. They ran away in front of him between two rows of very old doorsteps. A long way up that street, eighty yards perhaps, they stopped being grey, and went white, where the sun came down upon something wide and open and paved.
   256	
   257	There was no sound from that end, and he had thought that there would be. There was only a brightness lying on the stones, and at the top of his sight, where he would not lift it, a paleness in the air that was not the sun's.
   258	
   259	It was a hundred paces off. He could have walked to it while a person counted to sixty. Hesk's book lay against his ribs, under the box's strap, with two sentences in it that a square woman had read with her lips moving; and he found that he was glad, for the first time, that she had made him write them. A thing that he had only said would have been arguing with him by now.
   260	
   261	He turned right.
   262	
   263	A small quick shape came up on his left side as he did it, with a box on its back, and settled there, between him and the street's end. She did not look that way herself. She had her eyes on the lane, and her chin level, and she said nothing whatever; she only walked where a wall would have been, if anybody had thought to build him one.

