   315	*(Refreshed after repair r1. Line numbers are those of the repaired chapters.)*
   316	
   317	- **The slip and Pike's rule.** The clearance slip came over in the bag on the 277th; Pike drew a road between two heaps beside it. Twenty, "at the hold's own gate, on the factor's thumb, when the factor's walked it". **Pike will not write a clearance taken until a healer is said** (his own rule since his second year, when one of a crew of four bled for two days on a hurdle). His sixth line: *And a healer; of her own road; at her own price.*, the first column he has had for Oryn.
   318	- **Oryn's round this thaw.** West with the six as the slip's healer; from the fork-stone north to "five gates up along the hill-foot", one with a child that she was to have heard a cold term ago; then "down the far water to the ford, and over, and on to the mill from above". Three weeks puts her at Millrace.
   319	- **Teague.** The forty sacks went aboard on backs on the morning of the 278th; he is still on the east landing when the six cross, bound for the block on the cold road. He had first call on the slip by the cloth and left it. "A man that holds a thing so doesn't leave off because nobody's asked him. I'd know what I had at my back." The word left with Pike: the cut, two days west by the border road, and whatever is beyond it; no day.
   320	- **The ferry and the rope.** On the 278th she crossed in the forenoon with people, led beasts and carried sacks; no wheels. A copper a head, the mule a head. **The broker's cordage on the western landing is three-strand, hawser-laid. Brom refused it.** The sixty yards of four-strand are still owed.
   321	- **The border road.** One of two roads out of the western landing. Alders, then moor; a halting-ground in a fold by a beck, eleven miles out, with a dozen dead rings; a long shoulder of hill; the cut; four miles of high moor to a spring; six more to the fork-stone; a long rise of a mile and more.
   322	- **The four hands.** Flat, palm down: *stand*. A shut fist: *seen*. Two fingers: *come on*. A hand turned over empty: *nothing*. The doctrine in one line: "Nobody declares unless they're meant to be loud. One of us, once, in a place that we've chosen beforehand."
   323	- **Stillhounds, added to Book 7's account.** One paced the column for half a mile in the hour before sunrise and stopped at a beck. **Karis's sixth line (16:119):** *It goes to where a declaration ends, if it has learned that the declaring moves.* Eight laired round the sink, three of them under the north bank where the lip hides four yards.
   324	- **The shoulder hold and its factor.** A foot-track from a cairn below the spur; a plain bar in a wall as thick as a cart; a small clean yard, a long low house, a salting-shed with empty hooks. The factor: spare, grey, a leather apron, a slate on a cord, a sheep-crook, pale eyes; a thumb-mark like a crook; **no name and no pronoun on the page**. Three carts, a carter and two mules lost since the frost.
   325	- **The cut.** A hundred strides, a cart and a man wide, rising east to west by the height of two men. South bank: thirty feet of scree, with the hold's sheep-walk on its lip. North bank: low; beyond it fifty-five yards of scree down to the sink, a round bowl in shadow where water goes into the hill, iced grey and rotten. A seep at the cut's head runs down the middle of the road under ice that never thaws in that shade. Three runs worn over the north bank in the upper half. Three wrecked carts at the foot.
   326	- **Iron Wall as shown.** Named by Vastin (16:109); **no tier is stated**. He steps into the road and brings the stick's foot down once; a line runs from his boots across the cut and cracks the ice white; "the air stood up from it": a face from bank to bank as tall as the scree, seen only by the frost that lies on it. It holds eight hounds at a run. It does not go anywhere; **he may not lift a foot**; each blow on it comes down through him into the stiff leg; it goes out of him steadily; it shuts the road and not the banks. "A Path of the tribunal floor… taught for fields; and a field is ground where a thing has been declared, a hearing, or a square, or a gate with a crowd at it." "I have stood on eleven such."
   327	- **The fight's count.** Brom's rock at the plane's south end; Lira, two-handed, on the hound at Vastin's knee; one broken on the plane in the second run; Seln's knife on one standing in the runnel; Brom's rock on the other. Three over the north bank and through the sink's ice. Seln was on the north bank above the sink when he said that the cut was clear because it was empty.
   328	- **Bodies.** Vastin: four holes and a tear below the right knee, shut by Oryn; the joint bound with linen and two laths; **the bad knee is the right**. Brom: the right boot torn; two toes bruised, bound together, ten days.
   329	- **Oryn's past (kept by the coordinator as written; the order of telling changed in r1).** "I'd my rank off a board at the foot of a bed." A house for the sick "inside a wall", forty beds, six healers; her Iron fourth rank is provisional because she "never stayed to have it confirmed". **A sheet on a board at each bed's foot: name, complaint, what had been spent, and a column for the patient's tier.** In a week of fever the spending went down the column from the top; a woman from the river sheds with no tier went to the bottom of the heap; Oryn went to her at night, off the sheet, and it was enough (**nobody died**); the woman who kept the house said that a healer who spends off the sheet has stolen from whoever stood next on it. Asked where, she will not say: "They keep the same sheet at the foot of every bed inside the Line." She walked out of a gate in the coat she stood in and has not been through one since.
   330	- **The spring.** A furlong south of the road, four miles past the cut: an outcrop as big as a barn, a stone-built basin, and a fallen slab twelve feet by six, waist-high, out of the wind.

