   136	
   137	The second post stood beside a brook, where a plank bridge had been thrown over for people on foot and the carts went through the water. It said six. They had done three miles in an hour and a half, and it was a stick that had set the pace.
   138	
   139	Brom sat down on the plank's end and had his right boot off inside a breath. He held the bound foot out over the water without wetting it, and frowned at two toes, and counted on the fingers of one hand to eight.
   140	
   141	Vastin did not sit. He put his back against the post and both hands on the head of his stick, and stood with the bound leg thrust out before him, as he had stood at every halt for two days. A man in that binding could get himself down to the ground. It was the getting up again that could not be done without another person's arm; and Cael had watched him measure that at a fold, and at a fork, and at the top of a rise, and choose his feet every time.
   142	
   143	There was a gate into the field behind the hedge, hung on a post of the same dressed stone as the mile-mark. Cael lifted its latch. He set his box down inside, on a headland where the grass had not been ploughed, and lay flat in the lee of the bank with his boots toward the brook.
   144	
   145	Lira's back came against the far side of the hedge. He heard her settle, and begin to pull the heads off grass.
   146	
