     3	They came to the third post with the sun a hand above the town, and they were not going to do the last three miles.
     4	
     5	Seln had known it for some time. He had read two boards at two lane-ends, and both had said the same thing in the same paint: the gate was shut at sunset and opened at first light, and whoever was outside it slept outside it. A stick could not have done three miles in what was left of the day. Nobody said so, since nobody needed to; and it was Vastin himself who looked at the sun, and at the figure on the stone, and stopped.
     6	
     7	"Here," he said. "I shall not have five people locked out of a town on my account and told that they chose it."
     8	
     9	There was a place made for it: a strip of trodden ground between the road and the river meadows, as long as four wagons, with a stone trough at one end and a ring of blackened stones at the other, and a thorn hedge along its weather side. A bank and a ditch ran the length of it at the road's edge, and carters who missed the gate had been pulling in there for longer than the post had stood.
    10	
    11	Two of them had it tonight, with a wagon of hurdles under a sheet, and four horses on a line, and a fire at the trough's end. The elder lifted one hand from his knee when the boxes came in off the road; he looked at the loads, and at the old man on the stick, and went back to his pot. He did not ask where they were from, and it came to Cael that inside a hedge a person was not asked.
    12	
    13	Brom built the fire in the ring, from thorn that he bought of the carters for a copper.
    14	
