    16	
    17	"I'd lost the trick," he said, when it caught.
    18	
    19	"You have been spoiled," said Karis. She was watching the flame as a person watches somebody else do her work badly. "So have I. I have not been cold at a fire-laying for years. I had not known that I minded."
    20	
    21	"It's one point."
    22	
    23	"It is one point, on a town's ground, three miles from a gate. Seln?"
    24	
    25	"She is right," said Seln. He had not sat; he stood at the hedge's end, where he could see the road in both directions, with his hands in his sleeves. "Those two will be at that gate before us in the morning, and a carter talks while he waits. *There was a woman at the three-mile who lit a fire by looking at it.* It is not a crime; it is a line for somebody to write."
    26	
    27	Seven bowls went round. Brom set the seventh on a flat stone a little apart, empty, and looked at it; and then he put a heel of bread in it, and took the bread out again, and ate it himself.
    28	
    29	"She'd say it was waste," he said.
    30	
    31	Vastin had his bowl standing, in the gap at the hedge's end, across from Seln. He ate as he did everything, with attention, and he thanked Brom for it, once. His face was the grey of the stone that he leaned beside. He had come nine miles since noon on one leg and a stick; and when the bowl was empty he held it for a while, as though the setting of it down were a thing that would have to be planned.
    32	
    33	Lira took it out of his hand as she went by with her own.
    34	
    35	"I had it," said Vastin.
    36	
    37	"I know you had. I was going that way."
