    74	## New canon minted
    75	
    76	All of this is mine and is flagged for ruling.
    77	
    78	- **Where the Line is at Treswick.** The river is the Line; the sill (one long stone, worn hollow, a socket at each end for a bar) lies under the outer gate's arch. The hedged fields east of the bridge are "ground that the town uses and does not own", worked to the foot of the moor, with a watch that rides as far as the fields go. **The road's hold is lifted at the first hedge, in one half only:** they may speak; nobody declares ("Out there a declaration draws a thing with teeth. In here it draws a clerk.").
    79	- **The road in.** Wayposts of dressed stone at nine, six and three miles, each with a figure and three short bars one above another. The six-mile post stands by a brook with a plank bridge. At three miles a carters' pull-in: a stone trough, a ring of blackened stones, a thorn hedge, a bank and ditch at the road's edge. The gate is shut at sunset and opened at first light.
    80	- **The bridge.** Five piers; one cart wide, with a bay over each pier; a dark stone with no beds in it, laid dry and cut to fit; the roadway worn in two grooves deeper than a wheel's rim. The pale mortared gate-tower is cut to lie against the bridge's end, course by course: the bridge was there first. Vastin has ridden over it four times.
    81	- **The outer gate.** A keeper in undyed grey on a high stool, with a frame of three wires and wooden beads (feet, wheels, hooves); he counts and asks nothing. A second keeper beside a strapped book on a sloped desk, who writes only those who come to sell ("A load on wheels is the town's business. A load on a back is the back's.").
    82	- **How the town sorts (the mechanism).** Three walls. **Iron walks through the second gate by right; Bronze through the third.** A stranger who comes by right shows a certificate, which is read aloud and written with the hour, and the entry goes to a counter before night. Everybody else goes up on a **card with a coloured edge** (blue for the second wall, buff for the third) that states a door, a purpose and a count; the keeper counts caps against the figure, makes one stroke, and tears a corner off the card. Cards carry an hour and a day. Keepers wear the gate's coat: grey, dark blue, pale buff. The second gate has two keepers and a groove for a portcullis; the third is a low round arch with one old keeper.
    83	- **The outer district.** The brokers' street from the gate to the bluff's foot, with a slate tally board at every door (*Wanted*; a load or a number of hands; the landing, the second wall or the third; a rate; a daub of colour for the card that goes with the work); the porters' bench; loads up on the right hand and down on the left. The rope-walk: a shed three hundred yards long against the inside of the town wall, ending in a yard of tar barrels above a quay; **Brom found four-strand laid round a heart, asked the price of sixty yards, found it fair, and did not buy** ("I'll take it going out"); no figure is printed. A public trough forty feet long at the street's head, where a brown mule that is not theirs was drinking (theirs has the white foot). A ring road under the second wall, right round the bluff. A landing with six flat boats, a treadwheel hoist and a cable ferry.
    84	- **The smith's two forges.** One with a hood, charcoal and a lad on the bellows, shoeing carters' beasts; one with no bellows and a white even bed held by a hired Ember in a dark blue coat (Karis: "Rank Two work, at the most"), shoeing a bay with a blue band in its forelock. Both smiths draw bar from one rack and nails from one keg.
    85	- **The inn with two doors.** A long house one street back from the trough, with a carriage-arch. A painted street door with a book on a stand inside it ("that side's for names"); a half-door under the arch where a woman takes money in front and writes nothing. **Two marks for the long loft over the stable for a week, the six of them, with the pot at dusk: 136.** A lower room off the yard with one long table and a hatch. Lira hung a horse-blanket over the loft's gable window, which looks up the hill.
    86	- **The road to the registry.** One street climbs from the trough through both gates and, eighty yards beyond the third, opens into the square. The registry's front door faces the station's steps across it. Loads go another way: from the third gate, a lane on the right hand under the wall, two hundred paces, with four alleys opening off it toward the square; then either the carts' street to the left (half a mile round the crown, past a bar and a weigh-beam where every load is written against an owner) or **the cut**: five flights, twenty-two steps, a stride and a half wide, between walls three men high with no door or window, down the bluff's north side to the registry's yard. (The cut's measures are BOOK_MAP §8's.) At the cut's head a waist-high parapet looks down over the whole town.
    87	- **The cook-house** built against the second wall beside the blue gate: a counter down one side, the wall's own stone behind it; a roofed wood-yard at its lower end where porters are fed through a hatch; the yard door open to the room.
    88	- **Cleon, as seen.** Not tall; grey hair cut short and badly; a coat the colour of dust; round shoulders; square pale hands with the nails pared to the quick; red-rimmed wet eyes, which he keeps shut while he eats; a voice "like a hinge". He looks at a sheet from its foot upward. He eats at the counter's end by the yard door at noon and goes down within the hour. **His terms (mine):** not on a counter; all fifty-two, not a choice; they come down to him and he does not come up; his card, written at the counter (the matter; its bearer, one; hands, a figure), good for the next day only, at the second bell; no names given or wanted; not a word of what she thinks they are until he has looked; one lamp below, his, held where he says, and no other light or fire of anybody's. He pushed her sheet back without touching it. He left by the street door and stopped a full stride beyond it for three breaths. **He never looked toward the yard.** The Path is not shown; only that he rests his eyes.
    89	- **The provision, as Seln reports it.** The oldest and shortest leave in the registry's book: a practitioner registered under historical studies has standing entry to a lower archive, by the week, renewed at the yard door, and may have matter brought down to him. When matter is brought the form has a line for the matter (described), a line for **the matter's bearer, who is one**, and a line for hands. **Karis goes as the bearer**; nobody's name is on the card but the practitioner's. One clerk has kept the provision's book at the yard door for many years, with a single name in it. The exposure: the form neither requires nor forbids the clerk to ask who a bearer or a hand is.
    90	- **The quarry, as Seln reports it** (from a porter who has carried to the yard for eleven years and has never been below): the crown of the bluff was quarried from the inside, by galleries, before there was a wall on it; the later town kept its records there, since it was dry; the Compact built its registry across the quarry's mouth and never brought the old records up; one iron door in the undercroft, with the registry's wall built round its frame.
    91	- **The registry's back, the clerk and the door.** A sloping paved yard with a dock, sheds and a weigh-beam; five storeys of pale stone with small barred windows; **the lowest three courses are of the bridge's dark, dry-laid stone**. A low door, a hatch and a bench. **The clerk:** no name; no particular age; a grey coat with ink on the right cuff; a narrow book bound in green cloth; he opened his mouth, shut it, wrote the matter, one mark in a narrow column and *four* in a narrower, blotted it, and stood aside. He asked nothing. The undercroft: a vault of pale stone with iron lamps, pillars, and the registry's lumber, with clerks' feet overhead; at a pillar the pale stone ends and the walls are dark and dry-laid and the air dry and cold. **The door:** low, broad, of black iron plates laid like slates, no lock or handle on the outer side, in a frame of dark stone to which the pale masonry is cut; the clerk knocks three times; it is drawn from within and swings inward; an iron sill worn bright; a stair going down into no light. Cleon counted the loads ("Three. And the case."), not the people.
    92	- **Vastin.** He told Cael beforehand that he would sit and could not rise without him; he sat on the bank at the three-mile post (the first time since the cut), in the inn's lower room (the second), and on the yard's bench (the third). **What he says of the file:** he read it first in the week before he came up the bluff, by the candles of four inns, and last on the morning he left a seal in a tray. **The why of the declining (mine):** the reason he gave at Ostrand was true and was the one he could give with his back straight; the other was that a second reading would either be silent again (two silences on one file, for which an office finds a word) or would light (and then his three days and nine thousand readings were worth what a plate said); he could afford neither and "kept my hand off a door". His sentence from forty years of inquiries, "The instrument is not at fault. It has told you where its reach ends," is said aloud here; he wrote it once of a person, in an assessment that a seat declined to receive (Book 6). **His papers:** three sheets under one seal, in oilskin, which would open both gates and bring an officer down the hill; he does not know who reads the town's books at night. He has certified the town four times. In his window: every district he inspected had been told four days before that he was coming.
    93	- **Oryn's north road.** The first gate lies a day and a half north of the fork: a small hold with a wall of field-stone, a gate of oak bars, and a gate-house and bench on the wall's outer face; she has sat there on thirty rounds and never been through the gate. **The carter:** of Oxhollow, big, red-bearded, there for the hay; his right wrist was broken under a wheel a week after she passed Oxhollow before the snow, and strapped at Oxhollow's gate by the keeper's wife with a wet strap and two spoons; it knit a thumb's breadth out. She would not part it tired or without his word; he gave it in the morning; she re-set and knit it in an hour and was grey; six weeks in the binding; paid in oats. She spent her one day in hand there. **The second gate,** nine miles on, reached after dark on the 283rd: a boy of three whose chest she was to have heard a cold term ago, and who had mended himself; the keeper's son's wife in a gate-house built outside the wall, in labour since first light with the child lying across; "May I?" / "Yes. Yes. Do it."; she turned it from outside over some hours, giving it what she had kept for the road; **a daughter, alive, at first light on the 284th.** Nobody died. In her own head she has called the place in Cael "a stone" and "a join" and has decided that it was neither; she has no word.
    94	- **Small things.** Brom carries a flint, a steel and charred rag. Brom stood over Karis with a bowl on the 283rd. Oryn's empty bowl rides under the lashing of Brom's box. Children in the tar yard play at gates ("Then I'm the gate"). A house for the sick inside the second wall hangs its hours on a slate at the door: *First bell: buff. Second bell: blue. Cards, after the fourth, at the side door, as the house's means allow.* (Vastin: the ninth article of a schedule.)
    95	- **Roles (all unnamed):** two carters at the pull-in; the three gate keepers and the book-keeper at the outer gate; a broker's clerk; the ropemaker; the two smiths and the hired Ember; the inn's woman; an old woman porter at the kerb; a girl with eggs; the cook-house boy; Seln's porter; a yard-man; the clerk; Oryn's patients, the carter, the old woman who catches the hold's children, the mother and the keeper's son.
    96	
    97	## State at movement end (for the ledger)
    98	
    99	**Calendar.** The forenoon of the **two hundred and eighty-fourth day past the Line**, under Treswick, on the head of the stair inside the iron door. Days on the page: 281 (the rise's foot; the posts; the three-mile pull-in), 282 (the bridge; the outer gate; the district; the inn; the district's edge; Seln's report), 283 (the cook-house at noon; the route; the evening), 284 (the inn yard at dawn; the second bell; two gates; the lane; the cut; the yard; the clerk; the door). Oryn: the first gate at dusk on the 282nd and the morning of the 283rd; the second gate from the night of the 283rd to first light on the 284th. No season, month or weekday name; no figure for the technician's date; no count of years since the Kindling.
   100	
   101	**Where everyone is.** Five inside the iron door: Karis, Brom, Lira, Vastin, Cael, with Cleon at the stair's head holding one lamp. The clerk is outside it with his lantern. **Seln is above ground, on no card, where he can see who comes to look at the door**; nobody saw him on the climb. Oryn and the brown mule are at the second of her five gates, north along the hill-foot, with three gates, the ford and Millrace ahead and her day in hand spent. Teague's five are east of the river (not on the page).
   102	
   103	**Cael.** Nineteen. **Ten fragments. Deployment thirty-two.** He has read himself a fourth and a fifth time (the 281st), round and not in. **Finding:** the reading feels what moves; the place has nothing that moves; the reading's nothing was never an answer; he needs an instrument for what does not move, and knows of two (the station, which he has promised to stay away from, and the man under the town). He has entered that he read Oryn's order for the leave he wanted, and that she is to be told so at the mill before anything else. **He has told Vastin** that the place was read twice that day and came back with nothing, and that what he reads with cannot read what does not move; **not what reads, or how.** Lira has read the log's entry. He has not told anybody the date, and has not written it. **He has not set foot on the square or the steps and has not looked at the crown**; the term has cost him something for the first time (the lane with four alleys). He has seen Cleon at five strides and was not looked at; he is afraid that the man will "look up". He went into the registry as one of *four* in a green book, with his eyes on his boots. Body: sound; a box-strap's mark on his neck.
   104	
   105	**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. No burst. Arm: the nineteenth day out of the wool on the 284th; it carries a box by both beckets. **She has kept her eyes off the crown since the rise, unasked, hung a blanket over the loft's window, and walked between Cael and the square in the lane.** **Her registry paper is in her coat and has not been touched or shown.** At the cut's head she looked at the whole town from the top; it did not pull; she priced it ("Two gates. And a view."; "a hill with a tax on it"). She has read the 281st's log and has said that she never asked for the question.
   106	
   107	**Brom.** Iron Skin; Copper. No grey. Right shoulder in the older healer's knot (it has not spoken). Right foot: five days left on the 284th; he favours it on hills. One box, with Oryn's bowl under its lashing. **The purse: a hundred and thirty-six.** Pike's thumbed slip in his shirt. He has looked at four-strand and will take it "going out". He lays seven bowls.
   108	
   109	**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three; shut since the fork. She has said, on the evidence of her own eyes, that the bridge is older than the walls; she has seen the same dark stone under the registry's back and under her bare hand in the undercroft, and has said nothing of it yet. She laid one sheet on a counter, answered a craftsman's questions, and **said that she came to ask how the thing stands, not what it says**. She has agreed to his terms and has kept her case to herself. She carried the card as its bearer; it has two corners gone. She holds the flat, the hide, the page in her coat, the ledger and her notebook. She has told Cael that she cannot tell her happiness from terror. She does not know what Cael meant by "look up" and did not ask.
   110	
   111	**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. He lifted the road's silence and said what a town puts in its place. He learned the quarry, the provision, the clerk and the practitioner from a porter, without going above the trough; went up once on the 283rd as a counted hand with lamp oil and found the two ways round; **is on no card** ("I should cost you the count"). He has said the practitioner's Path and tier. The case is on him, shut; not mentioned.
   112	
   113	**Vastin.** No office; no age stated. **Right knee: the fifth day of thirty on the 284th**; on the stick; no kneeling, no running, no wall. It did nine miles on the 281st and twenty-two steps down on the 284th; the stick slipped once and he caught himself on the wall with the left hand. **He has sat three times.** He has confessed why he declined the second reading. He holds what Cael gave him and has not asked what read it. He does not know the mechanism, what Cael carries, the sum, or what is under the bar; he does no date arithmetic. He has said once that he has papers he will not show. He consented to be the fourth before he was asked and went through three counts as a hand, looking at his boots, "very ill" and then a little better. He has not asked to join and nobody has spoken of it. He carries the kit case, still against the letter of Oryn's instruction; Oryn does not know.
   114	
   115	**Oryn.** Tide; Iron, Rank Four, provisional. Two gates done of five. She parted and re-knit a carter's wrist at the first (asked; told yes in the morning) and turned a child at the second (asked; told yes). **Nobody has died under her hands.** She is spent to grey and a day behind her own reckoning. She has not been through a gate. She has the mule, her linen and the crossbow. She owes Cael the reading and has thought about it twice; she has heard of no leaf and knows nothing of what happened on the road's edge.
   116	
   117	**Cleon.** Named on the page once. Rune Path, Bronze, by Seln's report only. He has set terms, written a card, and pulled the iron to. He has taken no notice of Cael. He has not been told anything of what the sheets are thought to be.
   118	
   119	**The clerk.** Unnamed. He asked nothing. *Four* is in his book.
   120	
   121	**Objects and money.** **136 marks** (138 less two at the inn's yard door). The reserve sealed in the third box, which is on Cael's back. Three boxes, the kit case, the flat and the hide are inside the iron door. Karis's fifty-two first copies are back in their box in their order. The card is in Cleon's hand. Still owed: sixty yards of four-strand to the Lowmarch ferryman. The loft is paid for a week from the 282nd.
   122	
   123	**Who knows what.** The mechanism: six, as before (Oryn among them); not Vastin, Cleon or the clerk. The inward finding of the 281st: Cael, Lira (from the log) and, in its outline, Vastin. The why of the declining: Cael and Vastin. The sum: Cael and Seln, each alone. Nobody has interpreted anything.
   124	
   125	**Open threads.** Five markers. A date that two people know. A question that Cael has never carried to anybody, and a man who has not looked up. A dark stone under a bridge and under a registry. A clerk who nearly asked. A term in a log that now has a square at the end of four alleys. A paper in a coat that did not pull. A knee on its fifth day, and a healer who has not been told about the case. A reading owed at Millrace, and an admission owed with it. A rope to be bought going out.
   126	
   127	## Owner flags
   128	
   129	1. **Five markers** (ch21:117; ch23:275; ch23:311; ch24:77; ch26:217). Each needs its protected text and speakers. If "You were the instrument … I was the error" is divided between two speakers, the silence after it still stands.
   130	2. **How Karis is entered.** The plan names the four hands and Seln's absence from the count and does not say how Karis passes the gates and the clerk without a written name. I made her the matter's **bearer**, entered as "one", on a card that names only the practitioner. If the plan intends otherwise, ch23 sc5, ch24 sc3–4 and ch25–26's counts ("One, and four") want re-composing.
   131	3. **The why of Vastin's declining** is mine (see New canon). It rests on Book 4's bluff, Book 6 ch14 and ch40, and stays clear of the date, the layer and the mechanism. It is the movement's largest minted fact about him.
   132	4. **What Cael gave Vastin.** He said that he has "one thing … that reads" and that the place was read twice that day. Vastin now knows that Cael has some means of reading himself, and has not asked what. The mechanism is not told. If that is past the line, the two speeches in ch21 sc4 can lose "with the one thing I've got that reads".
   133	5. **The line Cael drew round Oryn's order.** He read himself, going round and not in, and logged that he chose the reading of the order that he wanted and owes her the telling. The packet asks for the readings; the admission and the debt at the mill are mine.
   134	6. **The hand on the gatepost** (ch20 sc4): the going-along reached his palm on stone and found the same nothing. Not counted; nothing was asked; it is the image made literal. Rule it a trial if it reads as a use.
   135	7. **Where the Line lies** (the river; the sill under the outer gate) and **the lifting of the hold at the first hedge**. No earlier text of this edition places the Line at Treswick.
   136	8. **Tier rights at the gates** (Iron by right through the second wall, Bronze through the third; cards with blue and buff edges; the coats' colours) and the house for the sick with its hours by colour. All mine.
   137	9. **The dark stone** under the bridge, under the registry's back and in the undercroft, and the iron door's look (no lock or handle outside; opened from within; the clerk knocks). Movement 5 owns the galleries; I have gone no farther than the stair's head.
   138	10. **Cleon's terms and appearance** are mine; "One lamp below. Mine." is laid for Movement 5's lamp. He is unnamed in chapter 24 (his name is "on the card") and named once in chapter 26.
   139	11. **Oxhollow.** I did not put Oryn at Oxhollow's gate, since her route as told at the fork reaches the ford last. The packet's "Oxhollow's gate" is honoured by the carter's wrist having been strapped there. If a scene at Oxhollow itself is wanted, it belongs later than the 284th.
   140	12. **The rope** is priced with no figure; the cash track seems to leave three marks between the 292nd and the 298th.
   141	13. **Seln on the 283rd** went above the second and third gates as a counted hand with lamp oil. He has not shown any paper of his own.
   142	14. **Length** 35,184 against ~34,000; **paragraph median 32** in chapter 20; **FK 3.3–3.4** in three chapters.
   143	15. **A sound from the square** (ch26 sc1): a broom on the steps, and once a child's voice counting, heard down an alley. He does not look. Cut the child's voice if it presumes a Kindling day.
