    17	## Decisions made before drafting
    18	
    19	1. **Deployment stays at thirty-two.** No fragment is used outward in this movement. The two inward readings are not counted, as the three before them were not. Anchor asks at each gate and at the iron door and is told no; Shadow lifts at each dusk and is told no; the read asks at the bridge and is told that it need not know. No fragment is acquired.
    20	2. **The line between the order and the reading.** Oryn's order was `Write it down and stop touching it.` What he did three times was go straight at the place and bring the ten up to it one after another. That is the touching. On the 281st he lets the going-along take its own way and follows where it bends, the whole way round, and asks the place nothing. He says to Lira beforehand what he is about, and enters afterward that he chose the reading of the order that he wanted.
    21	3. **The finding is about the instrument.** Tide feels what moves. Everything he has ever learned about the place is a fact about what goes round it. Where nothing moves the instrument does not say *empty*; it says nothing. The image is used once, in narration, in the packet's words. Coming out of the fourth reading his right hand is on the waypost's stone, and the going-along comes to that palm and has nowhere to go: the same nothing. It is not a use (nothing is asked, nothing is read, no person is touched) and it is not counted.
    22	4. **Where the Line is.** The river is the Line at Treswick, and the sill is under the outer gate's arch. The hedged fields east of the bridge are worked from the town and its watch rides them to the nine-mile post. Seln lifts the road's silence at the first hedge and says what replaces it: outside, a declaration draws what hunts; inside, it draws a clerk. Nobody declares, on either side.
    23	5. **Vastin sits** at the road's edge by the three-mile post, at dusk on the 281st, by lowering himself on his arms with the bound leg straight out. A man in that binding cannot get up again without an arm, and he knows it before he goes down. That is what it means.
    24	6. **"I read your file …" is recomposed**, anchored to events and not to a number of years (BOOK_MAP §12 C2): from the week before he came up the bluff until the morning he left a seal in a tray.
    25	7. **The why of the declining is mine**, built only on what the edition's Books 4–6 show: the three days on the bluff told him nothing; in the borrowed office he said that the refusal was the one sentence the seat still needed of him. The confession: that was true, and it was the reason he could give with his back straight. The other reason was fear. A second reading under his certificate would either be silent again (two silences on one file, for which an office writes a word) or would light (and then his three days and his forty years of reading were worth what a plate said). He could afford neither answer and kept his hand off the door. Nothing in it touches the date, the mechanism or the layer.
    26	8. **What Cael gives back.** That the place a healer found in him was read again today, twice, and that the reading came back with nothing, because the instrument cannot read what does not move. He does not say what read it or how. Vastin does not ask. Nobody else holds it at that hour.
    27	9. **The city (mine; flagged).** Three walls. The outer gate counts heads on a frame of beads and has a book that it opens only for those who come to trade under a name. Inside the second wall (slate roofs) Iron walks by right; inside the third (the old town) Bronze does. Everybody else goes through on a pass that states a purpose and a count, which the keepers tick with a pen. Keepers wear the gate's coat: undyed grey at the outer, dark blue at the second, pale buff at the third. The station is the one building that reads; everything else reads by paper, coat and habit.
    28	10. **The inn with two doors (mine).** A street door with a book on a stand inside it, for those who lodge under a name; a yard door for carters and hands, who pay in front and are written nowhere. Two marks for the long loft over the stable for a week, with the pot at dusk: 138 → 136.
    29	11. **Karis goes as the pass's bearer.** Cleon's pass under the provision names him and nobody else: the matter, its bearer, and hands not above four, all by count. The four are Cael, Brom, Lira and Vastin. Seln is on no paper and goes up by his own way. (Flagged: the plan names the four hands and does not say how Karis is entered.)
    30	12. **Seln's route.** From the third gate, not the street ahead (which opens on the square) but the lane on the right hand under the wall, and down a stepped cut of five flights and twenty-two steps, a stride and a half wide, between blind walls three men high with no doors, to the registry's yard gate. Carts go the long way round by their own street, past a weigh-bar where a load is written against its owner. The cut's measures are BOOK_MAP §8's, so that Movement 5 finds the same place.
    31	13. **Cleon's noon house** is a cook-house built against the foot of the second wall, with a counter inside and a hatch on its wood-yard. Karis goes to the counter alone. Cael stands in the yard with a bowl and hears it through the open yard door. Cleon never looks at him. The two names are kept out of one sentence.
    32	14. **Oryn's cutaway uses no new hold name.** The first of her five gates and the second are unnamed. Oxhollow is the ford at the far end of the round; the man with the wrist is a carter of Oxhollow's whose wrist was strapped at Oxhollow's gate in the cold term, when no healer came. The birth is at the second gate, on the night of the 283rd, and the child is born alive at first light on the 284th. Nobody dies. She asks before every hand.
    33	15. **Markers, not guesses**, wherever the plan quotes a line in part or leaves its speaker unpinned (listed at the foot).
