editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:359:He left it sealed. He had not come all this way to try a door on the first night because it was unwatched.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:179:"It has not been cheap. I weighed your answer on the post-room's beam and was proud of the figure. I lit this hall by my own authority, and read you the code until morning, and told a table of tired people that it was real law and that we should read it. I should like one thing said at this counter, by me, since nobody will enter it." He cleared his throat. "I was wrong about what the reading would buy you. I have known it since a seal came to my gate that I could not place."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:225:*A morning,* he wrote that night at the sill. *A hood, two hands, a seal. A procurator with nothing on him. A junior at the records table who wouldn't look. A room doing exactly what the code says a room does.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-23.md:93:The door gave onto a stone apron at the very point of the tongue, with a low wall round it and the water on both sides. In the dark the two rivers were only sound. One came up from the south, broad and steady, the one that ran under the bluff at home. The other came down out of the east, bigger and colder and quicker, and where they met at the apron's point there was a long ragged noise like cloth tearing that went on and on and never finished.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:127:Everything else that morning he saw only between things. The side-door officer, his cold gone, not needing to ask the last name on his list. Somebody had already copied *Caelen Hesk-ward* into his door log from the last sitting, so that all he had to write was *the enrollee of record, unclassified*, which took him nine seconds now that his hand knew the way. *Seventeen* under Cael's left foot again, the third time, and this time chosen; he could not choose the room, but he could choose where he stood in it. And a slip of paper folded twice that Karis had handed Brom on the ferry, which was in Brom's right inside pocket now, with orders not to be opened until the coach went home. The left pocket was for Velmere, and it had a sealed letter in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:145:Cael looked at the table under the window. They lay where they had lain for two nights: on the left the district's finding, folded along its folds, with the printed slip tucked into it, and on the right, a hand's breadth off, the envelope from Velmere, with the red seal on it whole. There was a little dust on both already. Brom had cleared the summons and the dockets from under the whetstone and burned them in the corridor stove, and he had left these two.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:64:"Shoulder," said Brom, round the strapping. "Left. It's in. It was out for a heartbeat and it went back in by itself when he came off me." He tied the knot with his teeth and his right hand. "A week. It's had worse." Then he sat back on his heels and, with the same right hand, without thinking about it, reached across under his coat and felt the left inside pocket, where the sealed letter was. Cael watched his face as he found it there. Then Brom took the hand away and put it on Lira's strapped foot and left it there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-32.md:172:"Think how it reads," she said. "Not how it happened. How it reads, on a minute, at a seat, in that room. The respondent arrives at a documentary sitting the seat asked for him in person, with chalk to his knees and his advocate's hand in a wet cloth. He says that on the way here, this morning, on the only road open, six people he cannot name, whose faces he did not see, with no paper and no seal and no office, tried to take him into custody, and failed, and left. His witnesses are the four people who came with him and a coachman in the house's pay." She stopped. "No faces. No names. No paper. No wagon, by tonight, I'd wager, if anybody went to look. On the morning of a sitting, six days after the house turned away an order at its gate and made the order look sinister." She did turn then, and looked at all of them. "It reads as manufactured theater. Jent wouldn't even have to say so. He'd be kind about it. Everybody in the room would be kind about it, and every weekly in four towns would print it by the Fourth-day under a woodcut of the gate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:73:"The morning after it's entered, a practitioner goes down to the ferry as he goes every week, and holds out his pass, and the ferryman has had a letter. A short one, very polite, in the plainest form. The pass is held in abeyance. The ferryman is sorry. The ferryman has a licence of his own and a family to feed." Her voice did not rise at all. "So he goes up to his guild hall, where they've drunk with him for twenty years, and they've had a letter too. It tells them their association with him is *under restriction*, and the master reads those two words and understands that his own name goes on the next letter if he so much as nods to the man in the street. So he goes to the records office to see his own file and argue with it, and the clerk tells him it's sealed. Kept, every page, perfectly safe, and nobody may open it. Not he. Not anyone he pays. Not anyone who might stand up somewhere and say what's in it on his behalf." She paused. "And every house that ever put its name beside his gets a letter. This house would get one. After that, every question anybody asked about him would reach us already answered."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:115:"Bracken would carry a paper like this," she said. "So would I, if it came across my desk in order. So would every honest clerk between here and the seat, and every one of them would be right to, because there isn't a lie in it anywhere, and no villain either. Look for the trick all night and you'll come up empty, and the end is still the end. It was the end the morning they sealed it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-22.md:95:The return was on the copying table where he had left it at the sixth bell, sealed and addressed, ready for the morning bag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:281:He signed beneath, his name and his office, as he had signed something over nine thousand findings, and he sanded it and shook the sand back into its box.
