editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:219:He started at the end, with the morning after, because that was where a person actually lived. The ferryman with a letter, the guild hall with a letter, the clerk with a sealed file and no key. A daughter at Withrow's counter every week for a year, asking whether anybody had begun the review, and nobody ever obliged to begin it. Then the three steps of chapter fourteen in Bracken's voice that would not let itself be a voice, and the third word that nobody had ever had to stamp. Then the six slips of paper laid out down the long table like stones across a stream, each a door, each real law, each opening toward the next and never back the way you had come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:137:Lira's hands had come together in her lap, one inside the other, and stayed there. Karis had not taken her gloves off. She sat with them resting on the cloth either side of the leaf, as if she had forgotten they were on. Brom's good hand had gone, very slowly, to the left side of his coat, and lay over the inside pocket where the summons was, and the docket, and the letter from Velmere that he had carried sealed since the forty-fifth day. He did not take anything out. He only kept his hand there, the way a man puts his hand on a wall in the dark.
