editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:3:The circular came up the bluff road on the morning courier, two days after the wagons got home, and Rooke chalked it onto the cohort board in the covered walk before the second bell.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:93:"The hall keeps no recess," said Bracken, before he could ask. "Enrollees go home. Paper does not." He turned the daybook round on the counter so that Cael could read the morning's entry, which was a thing he did only when he was pleased with it. *Recess, the first day. In residence: forty-one enrolled, by slip. Post: the small bag. Nothing sealed.* "Nothing sealed," he repeated, and turned it back. "I have written that at the foot of the day for a week running, and I find I look forward to it. Go and be idle somewhere. It is what a recess is for, and I am told it does not come naturally to everybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:119:There was nothing in it, and that was what Hesk's letters were for: a buckle and a captain and a dog asleep across a door, and the stove, and something green, and not one word about registries or polite men or what anybody might be building. Hesk wrote from his bench and stayed at the height of his bench, and the letter came up the bluff like a smell of shavings and reminded Cael that there was a whole country in which people's dogs slept on steps.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:219:The road column was the last column on the page, kept since the year of the thieves on the order of the chancellor before last, one line at every bell, for whatever came up the bluff road or went down it. In term it ran to three or four entries a bell, and this week it ran to one or none. *Carter, flour. Post, the bag. None. None. The registrar's man, to town and back. None.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:231:"The same quiet. Read again." He closed the book and gave it back to the lodge-man through his window with a word of thanks. Then he walked with Cael out under the arch and along the outer wall, where the wind came up the bluff off the river, and spoke to the wall rather than to him. "They have not gone. They have stood down. There is a difference. A man who has gone has decided he was wrong. A man who has stood down has decided he does not need to do anything, because what he wants is coming to him by itself." He walked a little way. "The corridor is on schedule. Every sitting sits. Every form is filed. Nobody on this hill has put a foot wrong, and so nobody has to come up the road and make one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:115:The room was high up in the Compact's house, two days from Norhold by the post road. He had worked in it for nineteen years. It looked down into a paved court where a porter fed the pigeons from a paper bag every morning at the same hour, and there was a long cabinet of shallow drawers down one wall, and a seal-press he had screwed to the desk's corner himself, crooked, on his first morning, and never straightened. On the desk stood three trays: in, held, done. He had kept them in that order since before he had a desk worth the name.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:229:"With the wrong instruments, for three days," he said. "I came up to the bluff with a file and a plate and a set of questions, and all three told me nothing. A man in my trade has one great temptation, and I gave in to it. I thought the nothing was something being hidden from me." He moved the left hand. "It is the oldest mistake there is. When an instrument won't read, you'd sooner believe it's broken than believe you've pointed it at a thing it was never made to measure."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:37:Halcenvane stood along its top. The first thing he noticed was that nothing in it had been inherited. Every school he had ever seen had been made out of something older: a road-house, a granary, a lord's barn with a floor laid in it. This one had been built to be what it was, all at once, by somebody who had drawn it first. The roofs all ran at the same pitch. The stone was the same pale stone from end to end. Along the bluff's north lip ran three long halls with their windows set high under the eaves, all on the side the sun came up. One heavy-roofed building had walls thick enough to carry a great weight of something, and he thought it must be books. A separate block sat behind a low wall of its own. And in the very centre, where everything else on the bluff seemed to lean toward it, there was a great hollow ringed on three sides with tiered stone seats.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:145:To the right of the line there was nothing. The printer had left the paper bare for a hand's breadth, to the sheet's edge, and he had used the space: the key to the symbols was set in it, in a neat box, and under the key the registry's seal and the year of the impression.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-15.md:93:"He hasn't," said the counsel. "The seat answered him this morning, at Ostrand. His petition went to the seat by courier yesterday: standing to put a finding before it, signed and sealed as his own. The answer went out this morning by seat courier. Wherever the Archmarshal keeps his desk, it's a long road from the river. He'll have it tomorrow at the soonest." Her bag stayed in her hand. "We have it tonight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:3:Hesk's answer came up the bluff on the twelfth, by the evening coach, which was fast even for Hesk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:130:*Whatever it believes it has settled about me, and the reason it was built to settle such things. That's next year's question. I came up the bluff road with a smaller question than this. This one I came by honestly, and I've paid for all of it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:185:He did it under the records-hall porch, out of the rain, with the list on a board against his forearm and the chests set down in a row in front of him on the flags. There were four of them, and they had come up the bluff road under four seals apiece, and they would go down it under four new ones, pressed that morning at the delegation's working table in the counsel's wax and the senior seat's. His list had sixteen lines, one for each seal, and beside each line the impression's number and the hand that had pressed it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:195:He had thought, this morning a week ago, with the dry pen resting under a fourteenth line, that there might be a seventh. He could admit that here. What he had hoped, for the space of perhaps a breath, was a simple thing: that a designation written out under seal before a seat would have to name its office.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:219:"Thirty-four," said Withrow. "You like counting, so I'll give you the number before you start on it yourself. Thirty-four letters from the heads of other chartered houses, since the preliminary. They have come in ones and twos. Bracken puts them on my desk in the morning with the seals uppermost, so that I can see whose they are before I decide whether to have breakfast."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-47.md:217:He sat looking at that while the wind came up off the river and found its way under his collar. Nobody came. Below the bluff the water went on as it always went on, with nobody watching it and nobody needing it to be anything except water. After a while the shake in his hands eased, and the other shake under it eased a little too, and he got up and went to find the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:105:"Copies," Karis said. "The sealed originals sit under the glass down there. He certified both their season records at the first bell this morning, and sealed them, and filed them, and then he walked them down here himself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-32.md:59:"Nothing." She said it with the small extra weight she put on words she meant to build on. "Not a sheet, Cael. Nothing torn, nothing moved, nothing missing. Somebody came in twice, read your file, put it back, and left. And Bracken would never have known, if his drawer hadn't sat a hair proud and his building hadn't burned two extra gills of oil."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-41.md:45:He added nothing, took nothing out, and left the exception report shut. Near its end stood a word he had once weighed for four seconds, and he did not open the report to weigh it again. Nothing in the bundle was false. Nothing in it was the whole of anything. At the fourth bell Gault's clerk took it from him across the counter with the rest of the day's paper and pressed the wing's seal into the wax over the tape, and locked it in the holding cabinet, where it would wait four days for the delegation.
