editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:11:"One, to the counter, since I am not a fool; and the rest under my arm, in their order, where he can see how thick the roll is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:29:Karis considered that, with her roll held against her chest, in both arms, like a child that she had been told not to drop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:49:The yard lay at the room's lower end, and it was only a space between the cook-house and the next building, roofed over with boards and stacked to the height of a man with split logs. There was a hatch in the wall, and a boy at the hatch, to whom Cael gave a copper, and from whom he had a bowl and half the copper back. A dozen porters stood about among the logs, eating on their feet; and he found a place by the doorpost, with a stack at his shoulder, where he could see the whole length of the counter end on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:53:He stood at the very end of it, by the yard door, where the draught was, with his back to the room and his face to the stone. He was not tall. He had grey hair, cut short and badly, as a person cuts his own; and a coat that had been brown and was now the colour of dust, with dust worked into every seam of it, paler than any that the street could have put there. His shoulders were round; and there was a bowl in front of him, from which he was eating steadily, with his eyes shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:143:"Three boxes. A surveyor's case. What I have under my arms."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:189:He did not turn; his shoulders came up a little, and went down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:199:She went up the ladder with the roll in one arm and the card in her teeth. She sat down on the nearest pallet, and took the card out of her mouth, and looked at it; and Cael saw that she had begun to shake, a little, and all over, as a person does who has come in out of the cold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:221:"*Matter*," she read, with her lips. "*Its bearer, one. Hands—*" She stopped. "There's a figure. And his name, at the top, and a rank, and a number as long as my arm. And a day." She turned it over. The back was blank. "That's all that there is. That'd take five people through the buff gate and up to the top of everything. And it's not got one of them on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:257:Cael marked it, as he marked anything that people took care to step round. There was a figure on that card. There were three boxes, and they had three backs under them. There was a tarred case, which had ridden on one shoulder since a fork-stone, where nobody had asked for it and nobody had forbidden it. And there was a man with a straw in his fingers who went ahead of every load and had never yet been counted with one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:37:Vastin went beside him on the ditch side, a shoulder off. The stick came down a little after each of Cael's strides, and the tarred case knocked softly against a brown coat, and neither of them spoke; of the six they were the two who found the leave hardest to use. Cael had kept one question from that man since a board at Lowmarch. He thought that the man was keeping something too. He walked like somebody carrying a full bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:49:*What does it weigh?* asked the read, civilly, of the post, as it had asked of every rut for three days. Cael told it that it need not know. Brom lowered his box to the verge, and rolled the left shoulder, and looked at his right boot as a man looks at a debtor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:71:She walked with the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, as she had since a landing, and her eyes had not left the bluff since it came up over the hedges. She was not looking at its walls.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:89:He turned the phrase over for a furlong, with the box riding on his shoulders. *How a thing is built.* He had not thought to ask before what kind of reader sat under that town. He had only known that there was one more of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:129:"So you've settled what she'd say, and she's not here to say different." There was no unkindness in it. She hitched the beckets higher on her shoulders. "Write down that I asked. When you write it. I'd like it in there that somebody did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:133:"And I'll sit on the bank. If Brom comes round that hedge and finds you flat on your back with a hand on your chest, he'll have you over his shoulder before you can open your eyes, box and all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:141:Vastin did not sit. He put his back against the post and both hands on the head of his stick, and stood with the bound leg thrust out before him, as he had stood at every halt for two days. A man in that binding could get himself down to the ground. It was the getting up again that could not be done without another person's arm; and Cael had watched him measure that at a fold, and at a fork, and at the top of a rise, and choose his feet every time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:159:Pressure drew and filled under his breastbone, slowly, like a bellows under a tired arm. It went out of him narrow and deep, and it came back at him off anything curved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:165:Higher than that, in his right arm, a wet finger went round the rim of a glass. Blade would put an edge on steel for as long as a struck match burns, and it was the steel that paid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:183:It was farther out than he had ever marked, since on every other night he had been looking ahead, at the place itself. There was no check in the lean, and nothing piled. The Wind bore off as a road bears off round the shoulder of a hill, which has gone that way for as long as there has been a road. He leaned with it. He went by on the one hand, and did not look in; the lean eased, a long way beyond, and the hiss ran on toward his feet as though nothing had ever lain in front of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:201:It came down on the foot of the gatepost. The going-along had not been called home yet. It went down his arm to the palm, as it had gone to a healer's wrist when he had her leave, and there it stood. The stone gave it nothing to travel on. He had always known that it would not; the tenth did nothing whatever on a dead thing, and he had never troubled to think about that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:207:He did not care for that at all. He made himself hold it at arm's length until he could see what it was worth, and it was not worth much. It did not say that the place in him was stone, or dead, or of any sort. It said that this instrument could not tell the two apart. Karis kept a rule for exactly this, and had said it twice over a cord in a frozen yard, and he had not understood until now what it cost her to keep. He had learned something about the tool. He had learned nothing about what the tool had been laid on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:235:The road ran straighter from the brook, between hedges that were older and thicker, and it began to be a town's road. There were carts in both directions. At five miles there was a farm that stood close on the verge, with a woman beating a rug in its yard; at four there was a lane with a signboard at its mouth, and a painted hand on the board, and Seln read whatever stood under the hand without breaking his stride.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:251:The stick had taken its place again on Cael's left, on the ditch side. It had not lost a stride on him in six miles. He could hear what that was costing, since the breath beside him came short at each planting of the bound leg, and the tarred case had been shifted twice to the other shoulder and twice back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:15:Karis looked at the heap when it was laid, and then put both her hands under her arms and stood back from it. Brom felt in his coat. He brought out a steel and a flint and a twist of charred rag, which Cael had not seen in his fingers since before a small pale woman joined them, and he knelt and struck. It took him four tries.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:47:It stood at the pull-in's western end, where the hedge stopped and the ditch ran on beside the road. He sat down on the bank with his back against the stone and his face to the east. The moor lay along the sky there, a long way off and very dark; they had been on the top of it at noon. The town was behind his shoulder, with whatever lights it had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:81:He turned his back to the bank. He set the stick's foot in the turf, a long way out, and took its head in both his raw hands. Then he let himself down by the strength of his arms, an inch at a time, with the bound leg held off the ground in front of him and the other bending under the whole of his weight. Cael saw the hands shake on the ash. He saw the left one, which did not open flat, slip a little, and take hold again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:85:It was the first time that Cael had seen him lower than his own two feet since a cut on a moor, where he had gone down because nothing was left in him to stand with. This was not that. He had chosen it. A man who had stood for forty years at the upper end of every room had put himself where he could not rise without the arm of a boy whom the registry had no column for; and he had done it before he said a word, so that it could not afterward be taken back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:215:"I shall want your arm now," said Vastin. "I told you that I should."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:217:Cael got up. He went round the post and stood on the slope below the old man, where the footing was, and put out his right arm, bent. A raw hand closed on it above the elbow. It was a good deal stronger than it looked. The stick went into the turf, and the whole long weight of the man came up through that grip in one pull, with a breath shut hard behind the teeth, and stood.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:227:He wrote it by the fire, with the book on his knee and Lira's shoulder a foot from his own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:257:"No. I know that." She shut the book over her thumb. "I never asked for it. I want that in there as well. I could have, and I'd have got it, and you'd have hated me for a month."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:261:"A month. I know you." She gave the book back. "Is he going to get it? This man in a hole?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:3:The carters were gone before the two hundred and eighty-second day had any light in it, and they had left their fire raked out and a stack of thorn beside the ring for whoever came next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:13:She had stopped so short that he all but walked the box into her. She stood on the bank at the bridge's end, with the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, and she was not looking at the gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:69:He had come back inside a Compact wall for the first time since he walked down a bluff with a house's certificate in his coat; and he had come as the fifth part of a movement of a stranger's thumb. Nobody had asked what he was. No pen had been lifted. He went on up the paved slope beyond the arch with his box on his shoulders, and his heart going as it had gone on the lip of a cut, and found that he could not have said whether he was relieved or insulted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:73:The old man came over the sill on his stick, with the tarred case on his shoulder. The keeper's eyes were on the stick's foot. The thumb moved one bead, and stayed there, and the grey head did not lift.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:143:He stood in the middle of that open place with a box on his shoulder. He looked at her feet, the near fore and the off, and then at the others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:157:Cael marked nothing in that at first; a busy smith might well keep two. It was Brom who slowed, and then stood, and then set his box down on its end and folded his arms on the top of it, as a man does who means to be some time. He had spent a good many nights of his boyhood on a heap of sacks in a shed where his father kept a forge for the pleasure of hitting something, and he looked at a fire as Karis looked at a seal.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:185:Lira was looking at them too. Her right hand hung at her side, and her left was on the becket at her shoulder, and neither of them went near the breast of her coat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:195:"The long loft," the woman said, when Brom asked. She had counted them as the keeper had, by the feet. "It's over the horses, so it's warm. A week, the six of you: two marks, in front. That's the pot at dusk as well, a bowl a head."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:207:She looked at him as a person looks at a horse that may be going to kick, and then she shrugged with one shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:215:The first of the five gates lay a day and a half north of the fork, under the hill-foot, and Oryn came to it at dusk on the second day with the mule's shadow going long in front of her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:243:It was a river with a stone dropped in it. The arm's current came down to the joint, strong enough, a carter's; and at the joint it went round. It ought to have gone through. She followed it, as she would have followed a path round a fallen tree, and found the place: two ends of bone that had been laid a thumb's breadth off one another, and had done what bone does, and joined where they lay. It was good, sound, patient knitting. The body had made the best of what it was given, as the keeper's wife had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:29:"You're the healer," she said. "I'd have sent a boy up the road for you this morning, if I'd known there was a road with you on it. It's my son's wife. She's been at it since first light, and it's not coming. I've caught every child in this hold for thirty years, and I can feel a shoulder where there ought to be a head."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:75:The old woman came out and stood beside the bench, with her arms folded, looking at the light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:89:Brom had roped them again in the night, for a stair. Each had a second becket now, low down, so that it would sit high between the shoulders and leave a person's eyes clear for his feet. He went along the three of them once more in the half light, a knot at a time, as he had gone along them at a ferry's shed. Oryn's empty bowl was wedged under the lashing of his own, mouth down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:93:Karis had the flat under one arm and the hide under the other. She had the card inside her left glove, against the palm, and she had looked to see that it was there four times since the ladder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:109:It leaned against the pump's trough, tarred and black, with its strap new-stitched in three places, and it had ridden one shoulder since a fork-stone, though nobody had asked for that. A healer had forbidden it, twice, and had been waited out; and the person who kept a book had been told so on a road, to his face, and had neither given leave nor refused it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:117:"You are one short of a figure, and I am standing here. I have carried that case for three days against a healer's express instruction, and I do not propose to hand it to somebody else at the foot of a hill because the carrying has acquired a name." He bent, hooked the strap with the stick's head, as he had at a stone, and brought it to his shoulder. "I have been counted once already in this town, by a man with beads. I found that it could be survived."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:173:Vastin went under the arch with the case on his shoulder and his eyes on the setts. It was a gate that he had ridden through on four occasions, with a keeper standing at each jamb and the board held out to him, open, so that he might see for himself how it was kept; and he went through it now as one stroke in four, on the wrong side of the pen, and Cael heard him let his breath go when the shadow of the arch was behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:199:The street climbed harder past the cistern. It had been laid in long shallow steps there, a stride to each, with a raised kerb for the grip of hooves. Behind him the stick had begun to come down out of time. Cael did not turn round; he slowed, by a little, as one slows a line for the last on it, and heard the breath at his shoulder catch up, and shorten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:3:The long loft ran the whole length of the stable, under a roof of slates that a person could touch with his hand, and it smelled of hay and warm horse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:15:Karis had not sat down. She stood in the middle of the loft with the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, as though she had been set there by somebody who would be back for her directly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:57:He went down the ladder without a sound. Karis looked after him; and then she lowered herself onto the nearest pallet, very carefully, with a courier's flat held in both arms against her chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:69:She saw it at once. With the wall on his left, the hill would be on his left as well; and whatever stood on the crown of the hill would be behind his shoulder for the whole of the walk, where he need do nothing about it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:77:The road opened there into a small paved place, where the street from the trough came up to meet the arch. There was a cook-house built against the wall on the near hand, with smoke going up from it, and a water-butt at its corner. Cael put his shoulder against the butt and stood. Nobody looks twice at a person who is waiting beside water.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:103:Cael took his shoulder off the butt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:281:"It doesn't even want to know what," said Lira. She had sat up, with her arms round her knees. "That's the whole trick of this town, and it's taken me a day to see it. If you walk in on your own paper they write you down, because you're somebody. If you walk in under a load you're a stroke of a pen. There's nothing to write." She looked across the lantern. "He's spent his whole life being a name with a word after it. And the only way they'll let him into the one place that might know what the word means is as a number."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:29:He spoke low. He was a stride behind, with the stick coming down on the cobbles and the case on his shoulder, and he had not looked down an alley either, though nobody had required it of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:37:Karis had said nothing since the gate. She walked nearest the wall, and her right shoulder all but brushed it, and twice Cael saw her tilt her head back and look up its whole height, as she had looked at a bluff from six miles off. She was counting courses.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:77:She swung the box off the parapet, and got her arms through the beckets, the right and then the left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:81:Karis had turned to her, with the flat under one arm. She did not say anything, since she never did when a thing was finished. She only moved aside, so that the girl with the box should go first into what lay beyond the parapet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:105:The grey eyes rested on him. Then Vastin shifted the case higher on his shoulder, and set the stick's foot on the first tread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:109:He went down as a man goes down who has thought it through. The stick went first. Then the bound leg was swung out and lowered, quite straight, like a plank let down on a rope; and then the sound one bent, and took the whole of him, and followed. He did that for every step. Cael went backward below him, with a hand on either wall and his heels feeling for the treads, and the box dragging at his shoulders.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:119:The load did not come. A raw left hand, which had not opened flat since a ferry's strap, struck the wall and held there; and the old man hung on it, with his shoulder against the stone and his breath gone, a foot above the box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:211:He opened the green book on his forearm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:223:Cael got his arms through the beckets. He had been a name with a word after it on every sheet that the Compact had ever kept of him; and he had spent his life since a Kindling on other people's wrong side of a counter, reading that word upside down. He went past a clerk of the oldest registry on the Line, into its foundations, as the quarter part of a figure in the last column of a green book. The man did not look at him as he went by. He was looking at the line that he had written, with something very like satisfaction.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:189:The roofs were slate, every one. The street had a gutter down each side of it, with water running, and no dung. The windows had glass in them. At a corner there was a cistern with a brass spout, and a girl was filling a jug at it; and she did not put a copper in any dish, since there was no dish. A row of children's voices came out of an upper window, chanting something all together, and he caught the shape of it before the words: it was a list, and it went up by steps, and at the top of each step there was a colour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:49:The yard lay at the room's lower end, and it was only a space between the cook-house and the next building, roofed over with boards and stacked to the height of a man with split logs. There was a hatch in the wall, and a boy at the hatch, to whom Cael gave a copper, and from whom he had a bowl and half the copper back. A dozen porters stood about among the logs, eating on their feet; and he found a place by the doorpost, with a stack at his shoulder, where he could see the whole length of the counter end on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:265:*Two hundred and eighty-third day. Treswick, the district under the first wall. A hundred and thirty-six. Saw the man, from a wood-yard, at five strides. He looked at one sheet for as long as it takes to swallow, and he did not read it as anything of mine has ever been read: he asked it nothing, and he started at the bottom. He wouldn't hear what she thinks they are, and he wouldn't have a name, and he never looked at the yard. I'd wanted that, and I find I'd wanted the other as well. Entered.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:13:Brom built the fire in the ring, from thorn that he bought of the carters for a copper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:229:It was the large young man who had set it there, and he had six others on a board. Vastin felt for a copper. He was told, as he had been told on four evenings running, to put it away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:241:"I did not go above the trough," said Seln. He sat down on the ladder's head with his feet on the second rung, which was as near as he came to resting. "I hired a frame for a copper, and I sat for three hours on the porters' bench at the top of the street, and was twice offered work. The man beside me has carried to the registry's yard for eleven years, and he likes to be listened to. I have four things from him, and one difficulty. I shall give them in his order."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:125:A woman with tarred hands had come up beside him. He asked her the price of sixty yards. She named it, and it was a fair one, since he nodded as he nodded at a wall that stood plumb.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:127:"I'll take it going out," he said. "I'll not carry sixty yards of rope up a hill to carry it down again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:195:"The long loft," the woman said, when Brom asked. She had counted them as the keeper had, by the feet. "It's over the horses, so it's warm. A week, the six of you: two marks, in front. That's the pot at dusk as well, a bowl a head."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:197:Brom counted two marks onto the ledge, and she swept them off it with the side of her hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:199:"A hundred and thirty-six," he said, to the purse.

