   219	"It reads the same. It is a good deal harder to walk past."
   220	
   221	They walked past it. There was nothing else to be done with it. But Cael carried it up the hill with the box, a square woman's voice saying that it was not cruelty and was only how a heap was sorted; and he thought of a girl on a kerb with a basket of eggs, and a child who had taken hold of a stick with both fists.
   222	
   223	---
   224	
   225	The third wall was the oldest, and it showed.
     1	# Chapter 20 — Wayposts
     2	
     3	The hold came off at the first hedge.
     4	
     5	It was Seln who took it off. He had gone down the western side of the rise thirty yards ahead of the boxes, as he went ahead of everything; and at the foot, where the ruts ran in between two banks of thorn, he stopped and let the file come up to him. He had not done that on any road since the Line.
     6	
     7	"We may talk," he said.
     8	
     9	Nobody did. They had come two hundred and eighty-one days through a country where a road was a place for hands and not for mouths, and the habit had gone deeper into them than any of the six had known.
    10	
    11	"Who says?" said Brom at last. His own voice seemed to startle him.
    12	
    13	"The hedge does. A hound does not lie up where a plough goes, and a thing that hunts what a Path gives off wants a mile of nothing round its den." Seln nodded at the bank. It had been laid within the year, with the stems cut half through and bent over and woven between stakes. "Somebody tends this. That town works these fields to the foot of the moor, and it will keep a watch that rides as far as its fields go. There will be posts."
    14	
    15	"So we're inside," said Lira.
    16	
    17	"We are not. The river is the Line here, and the sill lies under the gate. This is ground that the town uses and does not own." He put his hands into his sleeves. "I had better say the rest now, since it will be wanted before night. The hold was one rule in two halves, and only one of the halves is lifted. You may speak. Nobody declares."
    18	
    19	"There's nothing to hear it. You said so."
    20	
    21	"There is nothing to hunt it. Out there a declaration draws a thing with teeth. In here it draws a clerk. A Path that is opened on a town's ground is opened in somebody's district, under somebody's eye, and he is paid to write down what he saw and whose it was." The mild voice did not alter. "I should take the teeth, for choice. A hound does not send a copy up."
    22	
    23	Lira laughed. It was one short breath through the nose, and it was the first sound of the kind that Cael had heard on a road since a meadow at Lowmarch.
    24	
    25	"So we can talk, and we can't do anything," said Brom.
    26	
    27	"That is a town."
    28	
    29	"I'd forgotten that I didn't like them."
    30	
    31	They went on between the hedges, and for half a mile nobody used the leave that he had been given. Cael found that he had a great deal to say and no notion of how a person began. He had not been silent all those days; they had talked at every fire, behind every wall, and through a whole cold term in a back room. It was only on a road that the mouth was shut, with the sigils in their pockets and his ten lying closed, and he had left off noticing it as a carter leaves off noticing his own wheels. He noticed it now, as it lifted. It was like setting down the box.
    32	
    33	The road found its tongue sooner than they did. A cart came out of a field gate ahead of them, piled with dung and steaming, and the man at the horse's head was whistling. He whistled the same four notes over and over, badly, and did not leave off when he saw six strangers under loads. He looked at the boxes, and at the beckets, and pulled his horse over to let the weight go by, as one carrier does for another. He had given Cael the same length of glance that he gave the hedge.
    34	
    35	After him there was a boy with a switch and nine geese, and a woman under a yoke, and two men mending a hurdle; and not one of them gave the six more than a glance, which took in the loads and priced the distance. Cael counted them, since he counted anything. He had been stared at on roads since he was fourteen, by people who had been told what to look for, and for the better part of a year he had walked where there was nobody to stare. This was a third thing. He was a back with a box on it. He thought that he could have borne a great deal of that.
    36	
    37	Vastin went beside him on the ditch side, a shoulder off. The stick came down a little after each of Cael's strides, and the tarred case knocked softly against a brown coat, and neither of them spoke; of the six they were the two who found the leave hardest to use. Cael had kept one question from that man since a board at Lowmarch. He thought that the man was keeping something too. He walked like somebody carrying a full bowl.
    38	
    39	---
    40	
    41	The first post stood where a lane came in from the north. It was a dressed stone as high as Cael's waist, squared, with a figure cut deep in its eastern face, and under the figure three short bars, one above another.
    42	
    43	"Nine," said Lira. "They've counted it for us."
    44	
    45	"A town of the second schedule is bound to post its approaches at intervals of—" Vastin stopped. Nobody had asked him. He looked at the stone for a moment. "It is a milestone," he said.
    46	
    47	"There," said Seln, without turning his head.
    48	
    49	*What does it weigh?* asked the read, civilly, of the post, as it had asked of every rut for three days. Cael told it that it need not know. Brom lowered his box to the verge, and rolled the left shoulder, and looked at his right boot as a man looks at a debtor.
    50	
    51	Lira had her box off before him. She set it down on the grass and sat on it; and she sat with her back to the west.
    52	
    53	Cael would not have marked it, had he not been doing the same thing himself for three miles. Since the foot of the rise the town had dropped behind the hedges, and come up again, and dropped; and each time that it rose he had put his eyes on the lowest wall and held them there, since there was a small pale light above the highest roofs that did not flicker. It was work. It was like carrying a second box.
    54	
    55	She had never once looked up. He went back over the morning, and he was sure of it. On the rise she had looked at three walls and called them a ladder. After that she had come down two miles of open road with the whole bluff in front of her, and that quick chin had stayed level the whole way, on Brom's heels, and on a hedge, and on a boy's geese. Lira looked at the top of everything. She had looked at the tops of walls since a house on a bluff, to see what it would cost to stand there.
    56	
    57	Nobody had asked it of her. A healer had set a term at a fire, for one person, and had made him write it; and a girl on a meal-sack had heard, and had taken a share of it without a word, as she would have taken one end of a load.
    58	
    59	He did not thank her. She would have asked him what for, and made him say it, and then told him that it was a boot.
    60	
    61	"What?" said Lira.
    62	
    63	"Nothing. You're sitting on fifty-two sheets."
    64	
    65	"Then they'll be flat."
    66	
    67	---
    68	
    69	He fell back beside Karis when the road went on.
    70	
    71	She walked with the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, as she had since a landing, and her eyes had not left the bluff since it came up over the hedges. She was not looking at its walls.
    72	
    73	"It is bedded," she said, before he could speak. "The rock. Do you see the lines in the face of it, under the lowest wall, where the river has cut? Each of those is a floor that was laid down and then covered. I have been trying to count them for a mile, and I lose my place at the seventh."
    74	
    75	"At the spring you said what he was. The man under it." He shifted the box. "You said a historical-Path practitioner, and I let it go by, since I'd a mule to fetch. What does one do?"
    76	
    77	"I shall tell you what a catalogue says, and you will remember that it is a catalogue." She did not look at him. "The registry keeps a class that it calls historical studies. It is a small one. The Paths in it are of no use on a floor or in a field, and so nobody has troubled to rank them high; I do not know which of them he has. What they have in common is that they are turned on made things. A wall. A seal. The binding of a book. Such a person can say how a thing was put together: what was laid first, and what rests on what, and whether two things came from one hand."
    78	
    79	"And what it means?"
    80	
    81	"No. Every account of them is careful to say so in its first line. He reads how a thing is built. He does not read what it says."
    82	
    83	"That's half."
    84	
    85	"It is the half that I have not got." Her voice had gone thin, as it did when she came near the edge of what she had. "I have dated three floors past anything that I can lay paper beside. I have forty-one kinds of sign in one order. And I cannot tell you which of them is holding the others up. I do not need a man to tell me what it says, Cael. I shall do that, if it can be done. I need one who can tell me how it stands."

