editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:29:He spoke low. He was a stride behind, with the stick coming down on the cobbles and the case on his shoulder, and he had not looked down an alley either, though nobody had required it of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:61:It did not. She stood flat on both heels, with one hand on a box, and she looked the whole of it over, from the parapet to the bridge, slowly, with her lips moving. She had looked at a factor's fence so, on a hillside, when she was asked what it would take to mend.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:81:Karis had turned to her, with the flat under one arm. She did not say anything, since she never did when a thing was finished. She only moved aside, so that the girl with the box should go first into what lay beyond the parapet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:105:The grey eyes rested on him. Then Vastin shifted the case higher on his shoulder, and set the stick's foot on the first tread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:113:It was on the fourth flight that the stick slipped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:119:The load did not come. A raw left hand, which had not opened flat since a ferry's strap, struck the wall and held there; and the old man hung on it, with his shoulder against the stone and his breath gone, a foot above the box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:143:They ran the whole length of the wall's foot, under the pale, close-jointed and without a thread of mortar; and where a pale block had been set upon a dark one, the mason had cut it to lie snug, one stone at a time. She did not say what it was. She had said it once already, on a river bank. Her gloved hand went out, and came back, and closed on the flat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:195:They went to Brom, and to the box at his feet with a bowl wedged under its lashing. They went to Lira, who was looking at a cart. They came to Cael; and Cael, who had been read by registrars since he was fourteen, and had learned to meet each of them in the eye so that it should cost them something, looked down at the toes of his boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:199:The eyes went on. They reached an old man who stood very straight, with a stick, and a bound leg, and a tarred case; and there they rested.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:205:Cael saw it from under his brows. He saw the breath taken, and the pen come up an inch from the page; and he saw, at the edge of his sight, a raw hand close on the head of an ash stick until the linen round it creaked. Vastin was looking at the ground. He was doing it a little better than he had done it at a gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:213:He wrote for some time. He looked at the boxes, and counted them with his eyes, and wrote. He looked at the case, and the courier's flat, and the calfskin, and wrote those. He did not ask what was in any of them. Then his pen moved to the right, to a narrow column, and made a single mark there; and then to a narrower one, at the very edge of the leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:243:She had taken her right glove off, without setting down the hide, and Cael did not know how. Her bare palm was flat against the dark wall. She walked so, the whole way down that last stretch, with her fingers trailing over joint after joint, like a person who has been told of a thing all her life and is at last allowed to touch it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:263:"Three," he said, in the voice like a hinge. "And the case."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-26.md:267:Karis went in first, with her bare hand held before her like something that she was carrying. Brom bent nearly double to follow, and the bowl under his lashing scraped the iron. Lira went. The stick went, a tread at a time, and its owner did not look round at a clerk who had very nearly asked him a question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:55:She did not hurry. She could not have hurried, with what she had left in her; and a child was not a thing that she would have hurried in any case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:89:Brom had roped them again in the night, for a stair. Each had a second becket now, low down, so that it would sit high between the shoulders and leave a person's eyes clear for his feet. He went along the three of them once more in the half light, a knot at a time, as he had gone along them at a ferry's shed. Oryn's empty bowl was wedged under the lashing of his own, mouth down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:93:Karis had the flat under one arm and the hide under the other. She had the card inside her left glove, against the palm, and she had looked to see that it was there four times since the ladder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:107:That left the case.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:111:Cael looked at the case, and so did Brom. He saw Karis begin to turn her head, and stop herself, since she had been raised to think it rude to look at a person whom one was about to ask for something.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:117:"You are one short of a figure, and I am standing here. I have carried that case for three days against a healer's express instruction, and I do not propose to hand it to somebody else at the foot of a hill because the carrying has acquired a name." He bent, hooked the strap with the stick's head, as he had at a stone, and brought it to his shoulder. "I have been counted once already in this town, by a man with beads. I found that it could be survived."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:173:Vastin went under the arch with the case on his shoulder and his eyes on the setts. It was a gate that he had ridden through on four occasions, with a keeper standing at each jamb and the board held out to him, open, so that he might see for himself how it was kept; and he went through it now as one stroke in four, on the wrong side of the pen, and Cael heard him let his breath go when the shadow of the arch was behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-25.md:231:"Lower archive," he said. He had a dry, exact voice. "I had heard that there was such a card. My father kept this gate before me, and he told me that he had passed one, once, when he was young." He looked up, at three boxes, and at a tarred case, and for rather longer at a courier's flat of waxed leather with a stamp across its face. "That is a good deal of matter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:13:She had a square of oilcloth for them, and a tape; and Seln watched her tie it, from the ladder's head, with a carrying-frame on his back that he had hired for the day and a keg lashed to the frame. He had been down to the boards before the lamp was out and had come back with a card in his cap, and the card's edge was buff.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:19:"You will rope three boxes for a stair, as though you knew that there was one, and Brom will keep off that foot. At noon there will be two of you at the cook-house, and no more." He turned his eyes on Karis, and then on Cael. "One at the counter, who has something to show; and one in the wood-yard, with a bowl in his hand, who is nobody. The yard is where porters are fed, through a hatch, and its door stands open to the room for the heat, so that a person may hear from there and need not be seen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:37:Vastin had been standing at the gable all this while, under the blanket, with a strap of the tarred case in his hands. He had found a place where the stitching had begun to go, and he was working at it with an awl that he had borrowed of the stable.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:49:The yard lay at the room's lower end, and it was only a space between the cook-house and the next building, roofed over with boards and stacked to the height of a man with split logs. There was a hatch in the wall, and a boy at the hatch, to whom Cael gave a copper, and from whom he had a bowl and half the copper back. A dozen porters stood about among the logs, eating on their feet; and he found a place by the doorpost, with a stack at his shoulder, where he could see the whole length of the counter end on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:53:He stood at the very end of it, by the yard door, where the draught was, with his back to the room and his face to the stone. He was not tall. He had grey hair, cut short and badly, as a person cuts his own; and a coat that had been brown and was now the colour of dust, with dust worked into every seam of it, paler than any that the street could have put there. His shoulders were round; and there was a bowl in front of him, from which he was eating steadily, with his eyes shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:55:Cael looked at that for some time. The man did not blink them shut and open them, as someone does who is tired. He kept them closed, and found the bowl with his spoon by habit, and chewed, and rested them as a carter rests a horse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:63:She was very small in that doorway, with the light behind her, and a roll of oilcloth held against her chest. He saw her look down the room and find the dusty coat. She did not hurry. She came along behind the clerks with her chin level, as she had once walked up to a broker's plank with a hide; and she stopped at the counter's end, in the place that nobody took.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:65:She did not speak. She undid the tape, and drew out the uppermost sheet, and laid it on the counter beside his bowl, and squared it to the counter's edge with one finger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:129:He had picked up his spoon again. He spoke between mouthfuls, to the wall, in short pieces, like a man reading down a bill; and Cael, by the doorpost, with a bowl going cold in his hand, understood that he was hearing a list that had been ready for a very long time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:141:"They come down to me. I don't come up. I've come up as far as this bowl at noon since before those hinges were hung, and it's as far as I come." He jerked his head, without looking, toward the gate. "What is there to carry?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:143:"Three boxes. A surveyor's case. What I have under my arms."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:145:"Then it's a stair's worth." He felt in the dusty coat, and brought out a square of stiff card, the size of a man's palm, with a buff edge and a seal pressed into one corner, old, and rubbed nearly flat. He reached across the counter without asking, and took the pen that the cook kept for his tallies, and wrote three short lines with his face close to the card, and blew on them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:179:He passed within two strides of the yard, and of a boy of nineteen who stood against a doorpost with a bowl; and his red eyes did not come that way at all. They were on the floor, and they went along the flags, a joint at a time, as though he were reading how those had been laid as well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:209:He had been across the yard already. He had a bowl in his hand, from the hatch, with a spoon standing in it, and he set it on the boards between her boots. Then he stood there, as he had told a healer at a fork that he would.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:211:Karis looked at the bowl. She looked up at a great deal of Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:257:Cael marked it, as he marked anything that people took care to step round. There was a figure on that card. There were three boxes, and they had three backs under them. There was a tarred case, which had ridden on one shoulder since a fork-stone, where nobody had asked for it and nobody had forbidden it. And there was a man with a straw in his fingers who went ahead of every load and had never yet been counted with one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:275:The loft was quiet. Brom slept as he always slept, like something felled, with seven bowls stacked by his head, and Lira lay on her side with her back to the gable. Vastin stood at it, since he would not lie down while anybody else was awake, and the awl was still going in his fingers, in and out of a strap, by feel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:277:Karis had not lain down either. She sat against the boxes, with the roll across her knees and both her hands flat on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:293:She took that in, and he watched it go into her, slowly, like water into dry ground; and her hands closed on the oilcloth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-24.md:303:"You asked. I took a slip. It was my hand on the paper."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:21:"There is nothing to hunt it. Out there a declaration draws a thing with teeth. In here it draws a clerk. A Path that is opened on a town's ground is opened in somebody's district, under somebody's eye, and he is paid to write down what he saw and whose it was." The mild voice did not alter. "I should take the teeth, for choice. A hound does not send a copy up."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:37:Vastin went beside him on the ditch side, a shoulder off. The stick came down a little after each of Cael's strides, and the tarred case knocked softly against a brown coat, and neither of them spoke; of the six they were the two who found the leave hardest to use. Cael had kept one question from that man since a board at Lowmarch. He thought that the man was keeping something too. He walked like somebody carrying a full bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:65:"Then they'll be flat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:71:She walked with the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, as she had since a landing, and her eyes had not left the bluff since it came up over the hedges. She was not looking at its walls.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:101:He had kept that order as he kept few things, and he looked at it now as he had learned to look at a slip on a board: for what it said, and not for what he wished that it said. She had said *touching*. She had said what she meant by it, too, standing over him: that a person who has found a place in himself goes back to it every night with his fingers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:133:"And I'll sit on the bank. If Brom comes round that hedge and finds you flat on your back with a hand on your chest, he'll have you over his shoulder before you can open your eyes, box and all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:143:There was a gate into the field behind the hedge, hung on a post of the same dressed stone as the mile-mark. Cael lifted its latch. He set his box down inside, on a headland where the grass had not been ploughed, and lay flat in the lee of the bank with his boots toward the brook.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:251:The stick had taken its place again on Cael's left, on the ditch side. It had not lost a stride on him in six miles. He could hear what that was costing, since the breath beside him came short at each planting of the bound leg, and the tarred case had been shifted twice to the other shoulder and twice back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-20.md:269:"I shall want a quarter of an hour of you before that town," said Vastin. "Not on the road. Somewhere that a man can stop. I have carried a thing three hundred miles that is not in any flat, and I find that I should like to set it down before I go through a gate behind you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:15:Karis had not sat down. She stood in the middle of the loft with the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, as though she had been set there by somebody who would be back for her directly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:39:An old man leaned at the foot of the gable, under the blanket, with both hands on his stick. He had come in by a yard, and climbed a ladder on one leg with a healer's laths on the other, and had not yet been heard to say what he thought of either. There was a flatness under the breast of the brown coat, on the left side. Cael had taken it for a receipt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:57:He went down the ladder without a sound. Karis looked after him; and then she lowered herself onto the nearest pallet, very carefully, with a courier's flat held in both arms against her chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:89:And there were the slips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:115:It came out above the river on the bluff's far side, where the town wall ran along a quay, and there was a landing. He had not seen a flat boat since Lowmarch. There were six of them here, tied nose to tail, and a great wooden wheel on the quay with a man walking inside it, round and round, so that a sack went up on a rope at every dozen steps. A ferry lay at the landing's lower end, on a cable. He looked at the cable's blocks for Brom's sake, and could not have said what lay the rope was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:183:He had done it in the lower room of the inn, which opened off the yard: a long low place with one table down the middle of it and a bench on either side, a hearth at the far end, and a hatch through which bowls came. He had sat on the bench's end with the bound leg laid along it. There was a post behind him, and a strap hung from the post, and he had marked both before he let himself down. He would be able to get up by them. He had sat twice now in two days, and he noted, with the interest that he gave to any finding, that the second time had cost him less than the first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:203:He was off his watch, by the unbuttoned collar; but he had kept the blue coat on, and he sat in the one chair that had a back. A bowl had come to him through the hatch before he asked for it. He had not paid. The carters along the bench had moved up for him as water moves for a boat, without looking round, and the bench had closed again behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:209:It was half way to his breast before he saw it go. He looked at it. It was an old man's hand, with a strip of linen round the palm, and it had been about to write a district's keeper into a report for a bowl of barley.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:225:He watched them out of sight. The lowest thing in that district went through its walls on the least ink. He set that beside the keeper's bowl, as a fact about a mechanism, and could not have said why he had troubled to keep it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:227:A bowl came down on the table in front of him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:233:"You've not been fed. You've been counted." Brom set the seventh bowl at the table's end, by itself. "It's different. Eat it while it's hot."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:289:The lantern had burned down a finger's breadth, and Brom had carried seven bowls back across the yard and come up again, before anybody asked for the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-23.md:307:Karis laid her pencil down on the flat. Her hand was not quite steady.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:27:Seven bowls went round. Brom set the seventh on a flat stone a little apart, empty, and looked at it; and then he put a heel of bread in it, and took the bread out again, and ate it himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:31:Vastin had his bowl standing, in the gap at the hedge's end, across from Seln. He ate as he did everything, with attention, and he thanked Brom for it, once. His face was the grey of the stone that he leaned beside. He had come nine miles since noon on one leg and a stick; and when the bowl was empty he held it for a while, as though the setting of it down were a thing that would have to be planned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:49:He had found a thing at a brook, and it had seemed to him a large one. But he had found it cold and empty, after a quarter of an hour on his back in a field, and he had learned from Karis what a first count was worth. She never trusted a figure until it had been made a second time, by a person in a different temper. He was in a different temper now. He had a bowl in him, and nine miles, and he was too tired to want anything in particular.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:81:He turned his back to the bank. He set the stick's foot in the turf, a long way out, and took its head in both his raw hands. Then he let himself down by the strength of his arms, an inch at a time, with the bound leg held off the ground in front of him and the other bending under the whole of his weight. Cael saw the hands shake on the ash. He saw the left one, which did not open flat, slip a little, and take hold again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:111:"When the folder had grown as thick as my hand," said Vastin, "the seat did what it does when its instruments have not served. It sent the one that it keeps for such cases. It sent me. I came up that road with a plate, and a set of questions, and three days."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:115:"Four feet. I measured it, as I measure everything." His voice had not altered at all, and it was that which made it hard to listen to. "I brought to those three days whatever forty years had given me: I have stood on eleven fields, and made something over nine thousand evaluations, and I can tell a man's rank from the way that he takes a stair, before he has reached the head of it. At the end of the third day I went down your hill with nothing whatever. The plate had lain as flat as a dish; every question had been answered, civilly, and I believe that every answer was true; and I could not have built one finding upon the whole of them. I had never in my life come away from a person without a figure."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:147:"I wrote *declined*, and I gave a reason, which was a true one, and I have been thought the better of for it by six people on a moor." He shut his eyes. "I did not decline in order to spare you a plate; I declined so that I need never learn what the plate would say, and I kept my hand off a door. You were left a blank on the Compact's books so that an old man might go on supposing that he could read. That is what I have carried from the seat to this bank, and it is not in the flat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:179:He had done the same once before, on a bluff, with a notebook lying shut on a table between them and one more question plainly in reach. Cael had taken it then for a thing that the man had missed, and he knew better now: it had been looked at and left where it lay, and tonight it was being left again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:201:"So you'd have had the first of your two," said Cael. "I can't swear it; a plate's not what I've got. But there's nothing in that place that moves, and I think a plate's the same sort of tool as mine. It would have lain flat, and I'd have been two blanks on one file, and they'd have found their word." He rubbed his cold hands on his knees. "Nobody has that but you. It's not in the book yet. I've not told her, and she's read every line I ever wrote."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:213:It was not friendship, and Cael did not mistake it for that. A friend was a girl who sat on a bank with her back to a hedge and pulled the heads off grass. This was something flatter and harder, and he thought that it would bear more weight than it looked able to. Two people had each come to the end of an instrument, from opposite sides, and had met there. It was ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-21.md:221:"Thank you," he said. He had said it to Brom for a bowl, a dozen times, in the same two words. It did not sound the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:13:She had stopped so short that he all but walked the box into her. She stood on the bank at the bridge's end, with the flat under one arm and the hide under the other, and she was not looking at the gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:19:"The wall is the bluff's stone. It is pale, and it lies in beds, and it has been laid in mortar, in courses, by people who were paid by the yard. I have seen forty such walls." She took three quick steps down the bank, to where she could see the first pier side on, and bent, with the calfskin sticking out behind her like a wing. "This is dark. It has no beds in it at all. And there is no mortar. Brom. Come and look at this, and tell me whether there is mortar."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:35:"You've not got a hand for a pencil, either," said Brom. "You've a hide and a flat. Look at it going over. It'll be here on the way out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:41:The stick came over behind him, a stride at a time. On the third pier it stopped; and when he glanced round, the old man was standing in the bay, with one hand flat on the dark stone and a look that Cael had not seen on him before, which was simple curiosity.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:67:The thumb moved. Cael heard the beads go along the wire, one after another, small and dry, like peas dropped in a cup: Seln, and Lira, and Brom, and a small pale woman with a hide. There was one for him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:73:The old man came over the sill on his stick, with the tarred case on his shoulder. The keeper's eyes were on the stick's foot. The thumb moved one bead, and stayed there, and the grey head did not lift.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:99:"Is the pass that goes with the work. A person without the tier may not live above a wall. But the wall cannot do without him by day. So it lets him in by the piece. He has a slip, with a coloured edge, that says which door he is going to and how many he is; he is counted in at the gate above, with a pen, and he is counted out again before dusk."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:101:There was a long bench against the house across the way, and a dozen people sat on it, men and women, with carrying-frames between their knees. While the six watched, a clerk in an apron came out of the next door and rubbed a line off his slate. He chalked another. Four of the dozen got up without a word and went to him, and each was given a slip of card; and Cael saw the blue on its edge as it went into a cap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:149:"He's counted bowls for a day and a night," said Lira, to nobody. "Now he's counting mules by the leg."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:195:"The long loft," the woman said, when Brom asked. She had counted them as the keeper had, by the feet. "It's over the horses, so it's warm. A week, the six of you: two marks, in front. That's the pot at dusk as well, a bowl a head."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:201:"And a seventh bowl," he added.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/manuscript/chapter-22.md:205:"There's seven bowls."

