editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:138:- **What four a month buys (flag):** the hearth's end; the pot at dusk, a bowl a head; and the crew's names said to anybody who comes asking. Not bread, candles, the back room or the mule. "A cold term's five by my book": twenty, in front. The keeper comes out to his own door, behind the plank, to hear his floor priced (the join for the restored `The keeper said, "Pike," and Pike said, "Eleventh."`).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:170:**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three. **She has made her case to six** (the 268th): instructions; an index with a key that is kept; and then, in her protected speech, west, Treswick, the registry office on the old town, the Deepstacks under it, the old scholars' provision for a registered historical-studies practitioner, which she is not, and "a man there who is" (unnamed). She said the sentence whole to Cael on the 263rd, without the word west. She cannot read the four signs and has put no meaning on the stroke and the bar. She knows how the kit's cord is used and has taught the five. She has entered the anomaly as *Cause: not found. No inference entered.* and has written no sentence joining it to anything. Fifty-two sheets; the hide; the kit; the dead man's ledger, open now; a hair in her notebook. She keeps the purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:205:**Movement 1 CLOSED (2026-10-11).** Sol recheck of repair r1: CLOSE WITH LINE FIXES; all four tracked passages RESOLVED against the source; no new tracking; every binding ruling above borne out by the surviving prose. Two fixes applied by the coordinator: ch3, the author's own "his Path" (of Teague) now reads "the Force"; ch6, Seln's sentence after Karis's protected speech now gives the asset-restriction as locked Book 6 ch58 states it — **the paper executes when an enrolled name is written in a book inside a district, and must be served within the hour** (not "a certificate inside the Line"). The "New canon minted" and "State at movement end" sections above were refreshed from the post-repair author report after the fixes. Final: 30,941 prose words; gates 0; overlap 0 unprotected (9 protected). Purse 118 marks; deployment twenty-nine; day 270 at Lowmarch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/STATE_LEDGER.md:240:- **What Vastin adds about the layer (repaired: four facts and no more).** (1) It cannot be reached: he asked to be shown what the office holds upon the thing beneath the function; "The office has one page upon the matter, and it is the office's own. The page says that the layer is not to be opened; and it gives as its reason that it cannot be"; it "reserves no leave to anybody". (2) The citation: in the oldest register of housings that he was let to see, the line for the layer has no maker's name but a word and a place. (3) The held records (the flat). (4) The technician's line. **Cut in repair:** the frame-shop mender, the shimmed frame, the rule of the shop, the office's schedules, and any history of attempts. He could not go to the archive himself: an archive on the Line keeps a book at its door. Brom and Lira say what it changes: "So there's nobody to ask"; "it'll not be the people who own the plates that tell him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:13:**The title:** "Before the Paths" names two things. It is the era before the classification existed, when the un-bound walked in the land. It is also the book's argument: everything in Valdris was laid on top of a buried thing by someone who wanted it to stay buried. The book's image is a Compact registry built on the roof of a pre-Compact quarry it has never read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:29:- **Pike/the Compact:** Pike has nine completed lines under the five names; the ninth is the Stair and reads *three hundred; taken; not paid*. The first-snow season line is written with no number against it. The five names remain **eleventh**. The Compact has a Fallow completion report, the Stair rim's observations and a clerk's packet saying the crew kept the kit; it does not know that fragments work inside. [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: amendment 4] It has reached across the Line twice: on paper (the Fallow completion report) and in person (the six on the Stair's rim). The asset-restriction is pending. **Vastin is still inside**, writing nothing, with one window in Book 7. [B7-reconciled 2026-10-11: coordinator ruling] (restored: entry content the amendment's replacement had dropped)
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:482:**Names pending owner approval: none required.** Every new figure the expansion needs is carried by role: the axe-head hold's factor, the carter at the ice road, the ferryman and his boy, Oryn's route patients (at Oxhollow and unnamed holds), the hold child at the Kindling, the broker's men, and the hood in the tail. This keeps the audio load low in a book whose cast is already heavy with role-names (Teague's crew must stay unnamed by canon). If the author finds a recurring role that needs a name in the audio, it is listed here for approval before drafting, and drafted under the proposed name per the edition's policy (RUN-LEDGER 18:41).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-08-before-the-paths/BOOK_MAP.md:484:**Existing names stay exactly as canon has them.** Never "Halcenvane" on the page; use "the academy above Ostrand". The registry collisions are untouched (OWNER-DECISIONS #6). **Audiobook read-through list** (carried): Cleon/Cael (KLEE-on/KAYL; never in one spoken run); Span/Stair; Treswick/Ternhall (keep them out of one spoken inventory); the O-onset stack Oryn/Oxhollow/Ostrand; Pike/Fiske. Places minted by canon only: Treswick, the Deepstacks, the Sunken Span, "the court" (description), the shoulder hold, the cut, the pass. **Do not name new holds** on Oryn's route. Use Oxhollow or Thornwater, or none.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:107:It was the last day of the academic year. Cael had known it when he woke, as one knows a date that has been set against one's name for a long time. Somewhere behind him, six switchbacks up from a river, a bell would ring that evening and the house would close its books; and at some hour between, on a north road short of a toll-bar, a stout fair man would unlock a windowless building. He did not know the hour, for Withrow's sheet had given only the day, and he kept looking at the sun.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:159:A man had paid a toll here, eastbound, a long time ago. He had been a haulier, with a guild contract and fourteen years owing on a house, and nine letters had come to his door in a week, and nobody had ever laid a hand on him. A ferryman had written his name in a book on this bank, and the line had gone west in a bag, and years afterward a registrar gave it to his daughter across a counter, because it was the last thing anybody had written about him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:91:Nobody had asked him that about the name before. Not at Denvash, where Pellin had stamped it on his card; not at Greyvane, where it had been read out over his head; not on any form since. Once, at Ardenmere, he had asked Vell to put it in her book the right way round, which was the nearest he had come. The registry gave him the name the way it gave a ledger a number. People said Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-08.md:219:Not the burst, nor the exchange given away. Those were the band's ordinary prices, and he paid them every Fifth-day without looking at the bill. It was the hour. Up the library stair, Karis was spending a week of her life laying his name on a table one true day at a time, and he had spent this morning in front of three cards making sure the house's book held a slightly smaller version of him than the one she was writing down. The band had always been a daily expense. He had never before had to pay it in the same week as somebody else paid for the truth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:233:"In the examination hall. Three examiners at a table, and a slate in a wooden frame between them." She held her hands a foot apart. "They chalked it while I fought. Afterward the one in the middle copied it fair onto the form, and wiped the slate with his sleeve so the form would have no crossings-out. I remember thinking how careful that was." She put her hands down. "Every line on that form was correct. Wind. Copper. My name spelt right. Every step in the book, in the book's order. It was the room that was wrong. I've never been allowed to say both of those at once and have anybody listen. I only had *angry*, and angry's a very poor word. It's all one size."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:77:"An appeal upon new matter is examined before it is heard. That is the code also. It goes to a records office to be verified, and the office would open a district's station book against a clipped order, and find that the two disagree, and report an irregularity in the *exhibit*." He said it without any heat. "Every instrument they would use to test it runs on the thing it accuses. A man told you so at a window. It was his first refusal, and he gave it before any of us had asked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:89:"And the forty," he said. "The families. Say it reached them. A man in a river town opens a sheet and reads that his daughter wasn't entered as her plate showed, eleven years ago, because of an order. What does he do with it? Whose door does he go to?" He lifted his head. "The same door. There's one door, and it's theirs. He takes it to the district, and a clerk who's never heard of any of it looks in the book, and the book says Copper, and the clerk's telling the truth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:243:He did not add to it. Cael ruled a fifth line on the leaf, to the right of the four, and left its head blank, because that was a name that did not go into books, and under it he wrote the sentence.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:195:There was no name on any of them, only a paper tag on each, tied to the cord, with a number in Bracken's hand: a one, a two and a three. He did not touch the tags, and he did not need to lift a box to know its weight. He thought he could have said, to within a dozen leaves, what was in each. The Greyvane notebooks. The year's papers from Norhold, in their two tied bundles with the contents leaves. Her own fair copies of the year's filings, the ones that were hers and not the house's.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:77:"It's expulsion that follows you," said Withrow. "You can walk out of a town. You can't walk out of the post. It's waiting at the next town, and the one after, and nobody is obliged ever to begin the review that would stop it." She took the brown book from under her arm and held it in both hands. "Twice in my registry years I put my name to one. The first I'd sign again tomorrow; he had done worse than the registry ever learned. The second had a daughter." She looked at the book and not at them. "She came to my counter every week for a year to ask whether the review had begun. Every week I told her the truth, which was that nobody had begun it and nothing in the code obliged anybody to. And every week she thanked me, and went home." A pause. "I have never forgotten her name. Put that down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-07.md:125:"The house will answer," said Karis. "Because the other choice has its own word in the docket, and the word is *fled*, and this house is named on that paper, and I won't have *fled* written anywhere near it while I live here." She swept the six slips into one hand and tucked them into the back of her notebook. "I'll have bones on it in four days. And when the seat reads it, the seat will go through it with a pen and find nothing to mark. If the end's already fixed, I still want our tracks in the record, every step of them exactly where the code says a step goes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:31:"The ruling says five weeks," she said. "That is the outside of it. The inside is in a section upon notices, which gives a district five days, and in the habits of a district clerk who has never in my time notified a date one day later than the code allowed him. Bracken went to the district hall on our first morning in the town, to look at the room where Brom would stand, and there is a board beside its door. He read on it that the station's frame-house was reserved for the registry's use on the last day of the year." Her voice was quite even. "It was written up before any ruling was given, and I want you to understand that there was nothing crooked in that. A prudent clerk books a frame when he sees that one may be wanted, exactly as Bracken books a coach, and he would have struck it off again without a thought if the seat had gone the other way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:155:The red book lay on Rooke's table, where it always lay. There was a word in it under her name; he had written it on the day of the Blade, and then a second, and she had never seen either. She stood over the book in the dark with her hand on its cover, and did not open it. It was his, and whatever he had written, he had written of the program's entrant. She would tell him tomorrow, after the bout, in one sentence, to his face, for she owed him that much and he would want no more. If he chose to show her the page then, he would.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-18.md:95:He thought back. The wing had always had district post. The provision's hours were signed in the wing, and the district seat had written to the wing about them, about observers and schedules and the figures in the wing's book, every few weeks for two years. He had stood at the wing's counter himself and watched the desk clerk open grey seals.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:47:Inside, the long room smelled of lanolin and hot lamp oil, as it always did. The benches were fuller than at the third sitting. Cael crossed lot *nine*, lot *ten*, lot *eleven*, and sat down at the respondent's table with *seventeen* under his left foot, where he always sat now. Ilsev was in the middle chair of the evaluation seat with her files on her lap-desk at their angle and her four district evaluators upright around her. At the records table behind her, at the junior chair, the officer with the careful face had the receipt book open and his pen ready and his eyes on the leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:67:"Good." The captain found his own name and moved his finger to the next. "The chart's got a hole in it, and I don't like holes." He considered the Fourth-day column, Cael's line among the others, a practice booking on a floor nobody wanted after supper. Then he went on down the door to the Fifth-day, as if he had read nothing at all, and Cael could not have said whether he had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:117:"Counting." She did not touch any of it while she named it. "A lock. The key. A girl who brings me digests and asks me what they mean, and gets one of her mistakes handed back every morning, only one, so that she has to go and find the rest herself. And a line in the house's accounts with my name under it." Her mouth moved a little. "Bracken says the council sat twice in the recess to find a heading for it, because the books had never had one. *Research, enrolled, standing.* Eight people, two sittings, three words."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:85:Gault sat at the trestle in the middle of the north side with his case beside him on the bench. It was the case with the handle, the one he carried his instruments in, and he had set it down very straight, as he always did, as if it might have opinions about the bench. On his right sat the Ash instructor with her book held against her chest. On his left sat the Mire instructor, a broad calm woman with a pencil behind each ear. Each of the three had a card in front of them, ruled into three columns, execution, control and effect, and the wing added the three cards with no strike, which was the house's own way and not the tournaments'. The desk clerk from the counter sat at the end of the trestle with the wing's book open, to write the figure down when there was one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:11:"Four," he said, low, like a man passing on what he has been told to keep. "Four names in the lodge book since the First-day, asking to observe. Officers from other houses. The Magister's had the porters clear out the old gallery for them, over the west door." He straightened. "So the room won't surprise you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:33:Cael crossed the fountain court with Gault's third sentence in his head. Holding himself inside the house's rate in front of three cards and a clerk had never been nothing, but it had always been private. Tomorrow four strangers with writing boards would sit over the west door of wing three, and lay what he did beside everything the broadsheets and the tournament books had printed, line for line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:169:The trouble was the strangers. Three cards and a clerk who knew him were one kind of audience. Four officers from four houses, with the broadsheets and the tournament books in their satchels, were another. Rhagen wrote down every exchange of every bout it could see and bound the result and sold it; somebody in an inn that very night would be laying tomorrow's figure beside last season's, line for line. Whatever he did must look exactly like the record, which meant like a strong Iron with a few odd gifts on a good day. And it must not look like the last bout, or the one before, because a record that matches itself has been arranged, and men whose trade is arrangements can smell it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:161:"The house's answer goes on the board within the hour," he said. "The Chancellor has written it. It is one line long. I'm told that's deliberate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:11:He walked her to the door, because he had nothing else on the hill to do, and it was a strange thing to find out about himself at the third bell of a working day. For two years he had always been due somewhere: at a trestle in wing three, or a table at the top of the library, or the north wall at the changing of the coats. Now his name stood in a book with five words against it, and nobody on the bluff was expecting him anywhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:79:But nobody had held the door for her this time, and nobody had filled in the line. She had written it herself, in capitals, an hour ago, and a clerk in a district office was going to have to copy it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:175:There was a horse at the ring already. It stood inside the arch, by the lodge door, a grey cob with a district saddle-cloth and its girth slackened, and the lodge-man had given it a bucket. Its rider had signed the book and asked for the registrar, and had been walked across the court some while ago with a long envelope in his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:87:"The advocate of record for the respondent will be heard at the next sitting. She will be heard once, and within the hour. The petitioner may reply, if he is so advised, and not otherwise. The seat will then reserve." The level voice went on with no more weight on it than before. "So ordered."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:63:So there were two absences, and they had come within one week: a man was not at his table, and a return had not been written. The desk laid the two facts side by side, as it had been trained to, and looked at what lay between them. It had before it the house's query, in a registrar's honest hand, and a seat's ruling, and a district officer's paper endorsed *no such enrollee*. It had a watcher's line from the foot of a road, which said that a subject had gone down at dusk with three others and a cart.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:201:It was past the ninth bell. The lamp in the window would be wanted out within the hour or the valley would notice a difference, and the case had to be somewhere before it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:99:"Is the answer known? The registry's own tournament office has entered it, on the record of the continental meet, under a regional registrar's seal. *Unscorable under standard.* That is the registry writing down, in its own book, what its own protocol does with this practitioner."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:49:He was not anywhere. A watcher's line had him on the east road, four days out, with a cart, and there was no line after that. Beyond the last station no district ran, and a restriction must run in a district; it wanted a counter to be served at, and a book to be written in. The code had foreseen that, as it foresaw most things. The clerk found the phrase in a schedule and wrote it at the instrument's foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-58.md:53:He put it in a drawer. It would lie there, sealed and entire, for as long as it must, and on the day that any one of four names was written in a book inside a district, it would be taken out and served within the hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:19:Karis stayed at the counter with her hands on the wood, looking at the pigeonhole where the request had gone. Cael had seen her ask for things at counters before. At Greyvane she had asked on her own account, for a student's fee, and been made to wait. At Norhold she had asked on the tournament's, and been answered by a clerk who did not look up. She had never asked as anybody's. Now there was a line in a book with her name above it, and a house's seal on her request, and a registrar who had entered it without a word, as he would have entered a request from the Magister.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:151:The senior chair came in at the half-bell, a district records officer named on Havel's sheet only by his grade, a heavy, competent man who nodded to him and sat and opened his own book and did not speak to him again all morning. That was correct. The senior chair kept the minute. The junior chair kept the times, the receipts and the door log, and checked the one against the others, and spoke when spoken to. Havel had sat in a great many junior chairs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:161:*The seat opened at the second bell.* The clerk read the designation; Havel wrote the minute it ended. *The petitioner's procurator entered the petition.* Jent stood at his table with his leaf in one hand, read the petition's working paragraphs in an even voice that gave no one of them more weight than another, named his nine exhibits and sat; Havel wrote the minute he stood and the minute he sat. Nine minutes. *The respondent's counsel entered the answer.* She rose, small and grey with a steel rule in her hand, and summarized eleven pounds in a voice that did not hurry and did not linger; twenty-three minutes. Havel took the receipts for both lists of exhibits in the receipt book, nine items and a hundred and seventy-three, and wrote the numbers, and initialled them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:233:There was one slip for each. Lira's first meet off the hill, three weeks away now, on a road, against Silvers from other houses. Brom's panel, the last Fifth-day of the year, at the district seat in Ostrand. The next sitting at the wool exchange. And a fourth slip with nothing written on it, for the re-evaluation, whose date the seat would fix when it ruled. He set them in a row, as Karis had set out her six on the long table, and sat looking at them with Hesk's book open on his knee, and wrote.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:235:*The month closes, and for once the people I came up this hill with have each been handed something fair. Lira has her ladder, a point short, with the point named. Brom has his number, written on a docket a street from the seat. Karis has a shelf, and a key, and a girl to teach, and a line in a book with her name above it. None of it bought. None of it begged. All of it given across a counter, honestly, for once.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:337:She put the two flames out with a closing of her hand, and stood, and moved the line. She drew it again two feet further from him, and lit its ends again, and counted three and four, and the new thing asked, and he named it. She moved it two feet further and counted five and six, and it asked a little less surely, as a voice is less sure across a wider room. At ten feet he named it, and Karis wrote *10* in her notebook and drew a ring round it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:163:"Five lines on the First-day," said Seln. "Five floors, five hours, a quarter apart, each in one of your names. A practice booking. It's the most ordinary thing on the board. Read your own line. The floor means nothing. The hour is when you come up the stair."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-14.md:29:The hand was careful, and had plainly practised on other paper before it trusted itself to this sheet. Her father had been a district officer on the coast who took money to enter a rank in a station book that no frame had read. There had been one other, before him: a hill-station keeper who let a cracked housing read high for six years and covered it with false returns. Both findings were his, both sound, both sent down this stair; and somewhere below, in an office whose door he had never opened, the rest had followed. He had answered the girl himself, with one word, because a longer answer would only have given her another sentence to wait on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:19:He turned back a leaf, and another. The house's placings, a long thin column of meets and figures, and last year's the only one with *third* in it. Inspections, each with its date and its officer's name and its finding. Near the front he came to the only two leaves in the folder written in two different inks. On each, the left side was an inspector's complaint and the right side was the house answering it, in another year and another hand. He read the right-hand sides first, because he had just been reading one that was empty: a book kept again, and below the house's answer a visiting officer's clearance; an index finished, and below that answer another officer's clearance. Only then did he look across to see what they had answered. A quarter's lamp-hours nobody had written down. A year's declarations nobody had indexed. Both were long before Withrow. Then the charter's grant on its heavier paper, under a seal gone the colour of tea.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:107:*Posting sought.* The district seat's records room was a man short. He had seen it for himself when he went there with a rubbing of a seal: an impression book four months behind, and a ledger of dies that nobody had balanced against the cutters' copies since the last officer left. He wrote *District records, Ostrand, or any records room that is short.* He did not write *out of monitoring*, for the form had no line for what a man wished to leave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:117:*The proceedings conclude. The district records room stands a man short, to the officer's own knowledge, and its impression book is in arrear.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:177:"*The seat, having heard the petition and the responses in full, and having found the enrollment lawful in its execution and the respondent institution compliant with its charter throughout — which findings stand undisturbed — nonetheless finds the evaluation-integrity question sustained on the merits. The demonstration provision describes the practitioner; it does not evaluate him within the meaning of the integrity provisions. The petition for compelled re-evaluation under standard Arbiter protocol is granted. The re-evaluation shall be conducted at the district Arbiter station within five weeks. Should the re-evaluation return no valid classification, the procedure of the code's chapter fourteen shall apply: nullification, suspension, asset-restriction. The demonstration-provision enrollment is revoked effective at the close of the present academic year.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-21.md:95:Up there, pinned to the east hall door in Rooke's square capitals, was a card with her name on it for the program's first road meet. Rooke had crossed out its date a week ago and chalked another eight days later, when the confluence's stewards wrote that the meet was put back for repairs to their hall; she had minded for an afternoon and then been glad of the eight days. Up there was a red drill-book with a figure in it that was hers, and a broad patient man from the river academy who had written to ask whether he might come and teach her, after she beat him. Up there was a word she had never had before this year, and had to learn to say: *program*. Somebody had built a ladder, and leaned it against a wall, and put her name at the foot of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-55.md:37:"Then it goes in as written." He drew the leaf to him and blotted it, and on the line the form kept for the registrar he wrote a few words of his own and did not turn them round. "It will be copied into the district's return, in a round hand, by a clerk who has never met you. I should like to see his face."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:27:The first was the district's own impression book. It lived in the records room at the district seat, on a slanted desk of its own under the window, a book as tall as a child and as thick as a door-step. Every die issued for use in the district went into it on the day of issue, pressed into a red wafer and pasted on the page in the order it came, with the office's name and the date beside it in the keeper's hand. Havel turned its pages for two hours on the Third-day afternoon with the clerk in the wet hat hovering at his elbow, offering to help and being thanked. He found the seat's own seal on its page, forty years old, the river and the scale. He found the fee office's, and the bridge-ward's, and a ferry company's, and a hundred and eighty others.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:75:*The seal on the instrument constituting the facilities office named in the order presented at Halcenvane, and on the order itself (registrar's rubbing): absent from the district's impression book; absent from the registry's schedule of seals of office, this year's and last; its rim number absent from the cutters' issue ledger, where the numbers one below it and one above it stand on consecutive lines with no line for it between. Checked three ways. Absent from every registry of seals.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-10.md:197:"Not on any of them," said Bracken at last, and closed the third. "Admitted somewhere, or he couldn't stand at a seat. But not here, and not next door." He put the three books one on top of another. "So he came from further off than the next district to stand at our seat in a matter about our house." He looked at Cael over the books. "Which is information. I don't yet know what it's information about."

